export default {"format":"cadcraft-help","version":1,"app":"cadcraft","name":"CadCraft","common":"basics","sections":{"start":"Getting started","tasks":"Walkthroughs","switching":"Coming from other software","reference":"Reference","trouble":"Troubleshooting and limits"},"variants":{"os":{"mac":"Mac","windows":"Windows","linux":"Linux"},"browser":{"chromium":"Chrome or Edge","firefox":"Firefox","safari":"Safari"},"shell":{"web":"In the browser","desktop":"Desktop app"},"net":{"online":"Online","offline":"Offline"},"input":{"mouse":"Mouse","touch":"Touch"}},"topics":[{"slug":"whats-new","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Nytt i 0.7","summary":"PDF-underlag og PDF-import, skannede tegninger med automatisk trasering, ekte splines med SPLINE, SPLINEDIT og PEDIT, en behandler for måltilsettingsstiler, tekst som beholder formateringen, WBLOCK og egne målestokker, 3D-volumer fra andre programmer, registrering av skanninger, et Laser-panel, USD-eksport, Historikk og utskrift i CadCraft Studio, en veiledet første tegning, verktøylinjen på hvilken som helst side eller i færre rader, og mer for AI-assistenter.","section":"start","order":5,"keywords":["nytt","hva er nytt","utgivelsesnotater","endringslogg","endringer","0.7","versjon","nye funksjoner","pdf","pdfattach","pdfimport","underlag","skanning","scanattach","scantrace","trasering","raster","rastersnap","spline","splinedit","pedit","dimstyle","dimedit","dimtedit","mtext","formatering","wblock","scalelistedit","målestokkliste","acis","3d-volumer","registerscans","registrer skanninger","icp","e57","las","laser","grbl","g-kode","usd","usdout","historikk","versjoner","sammenlign","skriv ut","skriver","png","svg","plottstil","linjetype","omvisning","jukselapp","kommandokart","verktøylinjens plassering","verktøylinjerader","toolbardock","regen","regenall","styreflate","sveip","kodesider","kyrillisk","koreansk","kinesisk","japansk","elektro","rcd","p&id","pid","acad.lsp","acaddoc.lsp","støttemappe","assistenter","mcp","enheter","portugisisk"],"headings":[{"id":"nytt","text":"Nytt","level":2},{"id":"pdf-er-og-skannede-tegninger","text":"PDF-er og skannede tegninger","level":3},{"id":"tegning-og-redigering","text":"Tegning og redigering","level":3},{"id":"tekst-og-m-ltilsettinger","text":"Tekst og måltilsettinger","level":3},{"id":"plotting-og-utskrift","text":"Plotting og utskrift","level":3},{"id":"3d-og-punktskyer","text":"3D og punktskyer","level":3},{"id":"lage-ting","text":"Lage ting","level":3},{"id":"komme-i-gang-og-skjermen","text":"Komme i gang og skjermen","level":3},{"id":"i-cadcraft-studio","text":"I CadCraft Studio","level":3},{"id":"for-ai-assistenter","text":"For AI-assistenter","level":3},{"id":"endret","text":"Endret","level":2},{"id":"rettet","text":"Rettet","level":2},{"id":"i-alle-apper","text":"I alle apper","level":2},{"id":"ikke-i-denne-versjonen-enn","text":"Ikke i denne versjonen ennå","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EDette er det som er endret i CadCraft 0.7 sammenlignet med 0.6. Mye av det ble etterspurt på Discord. Listen er gruppert etter område, med det viktigste først.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"nytt\"\u003ENytt\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"pdf-er-og-skannede-tegninger\"\u003EPDF-er og skannede tegninger\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELegg en PDF-side under tegningen.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPDFATTACH\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EPDFATT\u003C/code\u003E) plasserer en side i sann størrelse eller en målestokk du skriver. Fang til linjene, tone den ned, gjør den monokrom (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPDFADJUST\u003C/code\u003E), klipp den (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPDFCLIP\u003C/code\u003E) og slå PDF-ens egne layer av og på (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPDFLAYERS\u003C/code\u003E). Den plottes som linjer, fyll og tekst, og en DWG eller DXF 2000 beholder den som en lenke til filen. Å åpne en PDF med \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EÅpne\u003C/span\u003E spør om den skal legges ved eller importeres. Se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/pdf-underlay\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/pdf-underlay\"\u003EPDF-underlag og PDF-import\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EGjør en PDF-side om til tegneobjekter.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPDFIMPORT\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EPDFIM\u003C/code\u003E) lager linjer, polylinjer, sirkler, buer, heldekkende skraveringer og tekst på layer oppkalt etter PDF-en, i sann størrelse, en innskrevet målestokk, eller skalert fra to punkter og en ekte avstand, i ett angretrinn. Se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/pdf-underlay#import-a-page\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/pdf-underlay\" data-hash=\"import-a-page\"\u003EImporter en side\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESkannede tegninger.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESCANATTACH\u003C/code\u003E henter inn en PNG, JPEG eller TIFF (én side eller alle) eller en PDF-side som er et bilde. Deretter kan du skalere den fra to punkter (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESCANSCALE\u003C/code\u003E), justere den (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESCANALIGN\u003C/code\u003E), rette den opp etter sine egne lange linjer (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESCANDESKEW\u003C/code\u003E), gjøre den svart-hvit og fjerne flekker (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESCANCLEAN\u003C/code\u003E), beskjære den og viske ut deler av den. Hvert trinn er ett angretrinn, og originalfilen beholdes. Se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/scanned-drawings\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/scanned-drawings\"\u003ESkannede tegninger og traseringen\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETrasér en skanning til linjer og buer.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESCANTRACE\u003C/code\u003E tegner skanningens linjer som linjer og buer på et nytt layer, SCAN-TRACE, med en toleranse du setter, og lar tekst og skravur stå som de er. Rapporten sier hva den laget og hva den lot ligge, og den hevder ikke at resultatet er riktig: kontroller det mot arket. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ERASTERSNAP\u003C/code\u003E lar objektfangene finne skanningens ender, hjørner og kryssinger. Ingenting sendes noe sted, og ingen trent modell brukes. Se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/scanned-drawings#the-trace\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/scanned-drawings\" data-hash=\"the-trace\"\u003ETraseringen\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"tegning-og-redigering\"\u003ETegning og redigering\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEkte splines.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESPLINE\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003ESPL\u003C/code\u003E) tegner en kurve gjennom punkter eller fra kontrollpunkter (grad 1 til 5), med endetangenter; \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESPLINEDIT\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003ESPE\u003C/code\u003E) flytter, legger til og sletter tilpasnings- eller kontrollpunkter, setter vekter, åpner, lukker, snur, eller gjør en spline om til en polylinje. Splines fra DWG- og DXF-filer åpnes som splines, og DWG og DXF 2000 lagrer dem som splines. Se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/splines\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/splines\"\u003ESplines og polylinjer\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPEDIT.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPEDIT\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EPE\u003C/code\u003E) lukker og åpner polylinjer, slår linjer og buer sammen med dem, setter én bredde, tilpasser en kurve gjennom hjørnepunktene eller en spline langs dem og tar den tilbake med Decurve, snur, og redigerer flere samtidig. Se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/splines#edit-a-polyline\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/splines\" data-hash=\"edit-a-polyline\"\u003ERediger en polylinje\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWBLOCK.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EWBLOCK\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EW\u003C/code\u003E) skriver en blokk, noen objekter eller hele tegningen til en ny DWG eller DXF som bare inneholder layerne, stilene og blokkene de bruker. Se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/blocks#write-a-block-or-objects-to-a-file-of-their-own\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/blocks\" data-hash=\"write-a-block-or-objects-to-a-file-of-their-own\"\u003ESkriv en blokk eller objekter til en egen fil\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EREGEN og REGENALL.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EREGEN\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003ERE\u003C/code\u003E) tegner fanen du er på om fra tegningen, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EREGENALL\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EREA\u003C/code\u003E) hver fane, rute og åpne tegning. Se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/commands#regen-and-regenall\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/commands\" data-hash=\"regen-and-regenall\"\u003EREGEN og REGENALL\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"tekst-og-m-ltilsettinger\"\u003ETekst og måltilsettinger\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETekst beholder formateringen.\u003C/strong\u003E Avsnittstekst fra andre programmer beholder fargene per ord, fet og kursiv, understreking, stablede brøker og høyder: de tegnes på skjermen og i PDF-er, beholdes når du redigerer ordene, og lagres tilbake. Se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/text#formatting-inside-paragraph-text-from-other-programs\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/text\" data-hash=\"formatting-inside-paragraph-text-from-other-programs\"\u003EFormatering i avsnittstekst fra andre programmer\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEn behandler for måltilsettingsstiler.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMSTYLE\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003ED\u003C/code\u003E) lager, endrer, sammenligner og bruker navngitte måltilsettingsstiler; å endre en stil endrer hver måltilsetting som bruker den. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMEDIT\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EDED\u003C/code\u003E) setter tilbake tekst, skriver ny tekst, dreier den eller skrår målhjelpelinjene; \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMTEDIT\u003C/code\u003E flytter teksten til venstre, til høyre eller til midten. Se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/dimension-settings#named-dimension-styles\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/dimension-settings\" data-hash=\"named-dimension-styles\"\u003ENavngitte måltilsettingsstiler\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEgne målestokker.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESCALELISTEDIT\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003ESCALELIST\u003C/code\u003E) legger til målestokker som 1:75 i listene for visningsområde og plott; de lagres i tegningen (DWG og DXF 2000), og en tegning fra et annet program bringer med sin egen liste. Se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/units-papers-scales#scales\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/units-papers-scales\" data-hash=\"scales\"\u003EMålestokker\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETekst i andre alfabeter.\u003C/strong\u003E DXF-filer lagret i kyrilliske, koreanske, kinesiske, japanske eller vietnamesiske kodesider åpnes nå med teksten sin, og kinesisk, japansk og koreansk tekst skrives ut i PDF-er (plottet henter en større font første gang den trengs). Se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/text#font\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/text\" data-hash=\"font\"\u003EFont\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"plotting-og-utskrift\"\u003EPlotting og utskrift\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMer i plottstiltabeller.\u003C/strong\u003E For hver farge kan en tabell nå også sette en linjetype, linjeende og linjesammenføyning, og en fyllstil som skriver ut heldekkende fyll som skravurlinjer; tabellen har sin egen liste over linjevekter og en linjetypeskala. Se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/plot-pdf#plot-style-tables\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/plot-pdf\" data-hash=\"plot-style-tables\"\u003EPlottstiltabeller\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESkriv ut, og PNG- og SVG-filer.\u003C/strong\u003E Plott-dialogen har \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESkriv ut…\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELast ned PNG\u003C/span\u003E (300 dpi) og \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELast ned SVG\u003C/span\u003E. I CadCraft Studio lister den datamaskinens skrivere, papiret deres og antall kopier. Se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/plot-pdf#print\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/plot-pdf\" data-hash=\"print\"\u003ESkriv ut\u003C/a\u003E og \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/plot-pdf#picture-and-svg-files\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/plot-pdf\" data-hash=\"picture-and-svg-files\"\u003EBilde- og SVG-filer\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"3d-og-punktskyer\"\u003E3D og punktskyer\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E3D-volumer fra andre programmer.\u003C/strong\u003E Volumer, regioner og legemer i en tegnings modellrom vises på 3D-fanen, skrivebeskyttet, og å lagre som DWG skriver dem tilbake uendret. Flater på overflater CadCraft ikke tegner ennå, listes, ikke gjettes på. Se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/3d-solids#solids-from-other-programs\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/3d-solids\" data-hash=\"solids-from-other-programs\"\u003EVolumer fra andre programmer\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERegistrer flere skanninger.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EREGISTERSCANS\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EREGSCAN\u003C/code\u003E) plasserer skanninger tatt fra ulike posisjoner i én ramme, ved punktpar eller innskrevne målkoordinater, finjusterer dem med ICP, lagrer registreringen som en liten prosjektfil, og skriver den registrerte skyen som én LAS- eller E57-fil. Se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/register-scans\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/register-scans\"\u003ERegistrer flere skanninger til én\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUSD-eksport.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EUSDOUT\u003C/code\u003E, eller \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXPORT3D\u003C/code\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUSD\u003C/span\u003E, skriver 3D-fanens volumer som \u003Ccode\u003E.usda\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.usdc\u003C/code\u003E eller \u003Ccode\u003E.usdz\u003C/code\u003E. Se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/usd\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/usd\"\u003EEksporter 3D-modellen som USD\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBeregninger på 3D-fanen.\u003C/strong\u003E Der 3D-fanens verktøy ber om en avstand, kan du skrive en beregning, som på kommandolinjen. Se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/coordinate-entry#arithmetic-in-a-typed-value\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/coordinate-entry\" data-hash=\"arithmetic-in-a-typed-value\"\u003ERegning i en innskrevet verdi\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"lage-ting\"\u003ELage ting\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESend en tegning til en laser.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELASER\u003C/code\u003E, eller \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EVisning\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMer\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELaser…\u003C/span\u003E, åpner et panel over tegningen: hvert layer skjærer, ripes, graverer linjer, fyller former, graverer et bilde eller sendes ikke, med effekt, hastighet og antall gjennomganger. Forhåndsvisningen følger redigeringene dine, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELagre G-kode…\u003C/span\u003E skriver GRBL-G-kode i lasermodus, og i Chrome eller Edge kan panelet ramme inn jobben og sende den over USB. Innstillingene lagres i tegningen (DWG og DXF). Se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/laser-cutting\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/laser-cutting\"\u003ESend en tegning til en laser\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESymboler for bygningsinstallasjoner i tillegget Electrical.\u003C/strong\u003E Data-, telefon- og TV-uttak, nøkkel- og tidsbrytere, en termostat, nødutgangsskilt, detektorer, brannmelder og sirene, en vifte, varmtvannsbereder, komfyr, ladepunkt for elbil, energimåler og underfordeling, og symboler for RCD og RCBO. En kurs kan ha en RCD, tavlelisten har en RCD-kolonne, og kontrollene navngir stikkontaktkurser uten RCD på 30 mA eller mindre. Det er en planleggingspåminnelse, ikke en forskriftskontroll.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEt P&amp;ID-tillegg.\u003C/strong\u003E P&amp;ID tegner prosessdiagrammer: symboler med tilkoblingspunkter, prosess- og signallinjer, merker, linjenumre, lister over linjer, utstyr, instrumenter og ventiler (CSV eller en arbeidsbok) og kontroller. Det følger med CadCraft og er avslått til du slår det på i \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInnstillinger\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGenerelt\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETillegg\u003C/span\u003E. Det er et tegnehjelpemiddel, ikke en ingeniørgodkjenning, og teksten er foreløpig bare på engelsk.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"komme-i-gang-og-skjermen\"\u003EKomme i gang og skjermen\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEn veiledet første tegning.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ETOUR\u003C/code\u003E (eller \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETa omvisningen\u003C/span\u003E på startkortet) tar deg gjennom seks trinn i den virkelige tegningen: en linje, en sirkel, en flytting, en trimming, en sletting og en måltilsetting. Den starter av seg selv første gang CadCraft åpnes med ingenting i. Se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/getting-started#prefer-to-be-guided\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/getting-started\" data-hash=\"prefer-to-be-guided\"\u003EForetrekker du veiledning?\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEn jukselapp og et kommandokart.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/cheat-sheet\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/cheat-sheet\"\u003EJukselappen\u003C/a\u003E har de 40 daglige kommandoene på én side, også som PDF til utskrift, og \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/command-map\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/command-map\"\u003Ekommandokartet\u003C/a\u003E lister kommandoer med kortnavnene deres, A til Å.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EVerktøylinjen på hvilken som helst side.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInnstillinger\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EVerktøylinjens plassering\u003C/span\u003E: topp (som før), bunn, venstre eller høyre, eller skriv \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ETOOLBARDOCK\u003C/code\u003E. Se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/settings#toolbar-position\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/settings\" data-hash=\"toolbar-position\"\u003EVerktøylinjens plassering\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EVerktøylinjerader.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInnstillinger\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EVerktøylinjerader\u003C/span\u003E: bryt for å passe, 2 rader eller 1 rad, for CadCraft-verktøylinjen og stilene Klassisk tegning og Bånd. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F1\"\u003E⌃⇧ F1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+Ctrl+F1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E går gjennom dem, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control F1\"\u003E⌃ F1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+F1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E gjennom det knappene viser, og \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ETOOLBARROWS\u003C/code\u003E og \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ETOOLBAR\u003C/code\u003E setter dem. Starthintet har en lukkeknapp. Takk til GJoe2, som sendte dette som en pull request. Se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/settings#toolbar-rows\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/settings\" data-hash=\"toolbar-rows\"\u003EVerktøylinjerader\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"i-cadcraft-studio\"\u003EI CadCraft Studio\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EHistorikk.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EHISTORY\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EHIST\u003C/code\u003E), eller \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EVisning\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMer\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHistorikk…\u003C/span\u003E: kopiene Studio beholder når du åpner og lagrer en tegning, og tegningens skyversjoner når du åpnet den fra skyen. Åpne eller gjenopprett én, eller sammenlign to, med det som er lagt til, fjernet og endret vist i farger. Se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/history\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/history\"\u003EHistorikk\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eacad.lsp og acaddoc.lsp.\u003C/strong\u003E Velg en støttemappe i \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EAPPLOAD\u003C/code\u003E: de to filene lastes når CadCraft starter (acaddoc.lsp på nytt for hver nye tegningsfane), og \u003Ccode\u003ES::STARTUP\u003C/code\u003E kjører etter dem. Se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/load-applications#load-acadlsp-and-acaddoclsp-from-a-support-folder-studio\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/load-applications\" data-hash=\"load-acadlsp-and-acaddoclsp-from-a-support-folder-studio\"\u003ELast inn applikasjoner\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPlott-dialogen i et eget vindu.\u003C/strong\u003E En knapp i tittellinjen flytter den til et vindu du kan legge på en annen skjerm. Se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/arrange-panels#move-a-dialog\" data-topic=\"basics/arrange-panels\" data-hash=\"move-a-dialog\"\u003EFlytt en dialog\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"for-ai-assistenter\"\u003EFor AI-assistenter\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFiler ved navn, i mapper du velger.\u003C/strong\u003E I CadCraft Studio kan en assistent åpne, lagre og plotte filer ved navn, og gi en kommando filen den ber om (INSERT fra en fil, COUNTs eksport, SAVEAS, PLOT), men bare inne i mappene du legger til under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInnstillinger\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAssistenter\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMapper den kan bruke\u003C/span\u003E. Se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/use-with-ai-assistants#let-it-use-files-in-folders-you-choose\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/use-with-ai-assistants\" data-hash=\"let-it-use-files-in-folders-you-choose\"\u003ELa den bruke filer i mapper du velger\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFærre kall.\u003C/strong\u003E En assistent kan skrive en hel kommandolinje på én gang, holde en kommando åpen mellom kallene sine, telle objekter etter type og layer uten å liste dem, og plassere en blokk med attributtverdiene sine i ett trinn.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUten vindu.\u003C/strong\u003E Kommandolinjen \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft\u003C/code\u003E skriver SVG-plott og konverterer, plotter, kontrollerer eller beskriver mange filer på én gang. MCP-pakken er ikke publisert ennå. Se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/use-with-ai-assistants#work-on-files-without-opening-cadcraft\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/use-with-ai-assistants\" data-hash=\"work-on-files-without-opening-cadcraft\"\u003EArbeid på filer uten å åpne CadCraft\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"endret\"\u003EEndret\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEn styreflate panorerer.\u003C/strong\u003E Flytt to fingre på en styreflate, og 2D-visningen følger dem; knip og musehjulet zoomer. Se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/mouse-trackpad-touch\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/mouse-trackpad-touch\"\u003EMus, styreflate og berøring\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOLE-objekter beholdes.\u003C/strong\u003E En DXFs innebygde objekter vises som bildet sitt eller som en merket ramme og lagres tilbake av DXF 2000. En DWGs beholdes og skrives tilbake når du lagrer som DWG, men vises ikke ennå. Før gikk de tapt ved lagring. Se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/pictures#objects-from-other-programs-ole\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/pictures\" data-hash=\"objects-from-other-programs-ole\"\u003EObjekter fra andre programmer (OLE)\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEnhetene i en ny tegning\u003C/strong\u003E følger enheten du setter stjerne på i \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInnstillinger\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGenerelt\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEnheter\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"rettet\"\u003ERettet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELayouter som åpnet tomme, eller mistet et visningsområde og viste hele modellen i stedet, åpnes nå med de virkelige visningsområdene sine. En layout uten lenke til innholdet beholdes, og åpningsrapporten sier det.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDXF-filer i eldre kodesider viste forvansket kyrillisk og koreansk tekst. De leses riktig nå.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAvsnittstekst fra andre programmer mistet formateringen da den ble åpnet og ble lagret tilbake som ren tekst.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"i-alle-apper\"\u003EI alle apper\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEnheter.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInnstillinger\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGenerelt\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEnheter\u003C/span\u003E har én liste hver for lengde, areal, volum, vinkel og helling, med de eksakte størrelsene enhetenes definisjoner gir (den amerikanske målefoten er sin egen enhet). Slå av enhetene du aldri bruker, så forlater de enhetslistene; sett stjerne på én, så starter nytt arbeid i den. En fil beholder alltid sin egen enhet. I CadCraft setter stjernen enhetene i en ny tegning når det er en enhet en tegning kan bære; CadCrafts egen boks \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEnheter\u003C/span\u003E følger ikke bryterne ennå. Når en tegning har enheter, kan en lengde du skriver, bære merket til en hvilken som helst enhet. Se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/unit-lists\" data-topic=\"basics/unit-lists\"\u003EEnheter, innskrevne verdier og enhetslistene\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEt tettere oppsett og paneler som ryddes bort.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInnstillinger\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGenerelt\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayouttetthet\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKompakt\u003C/span\u003E får plass til mer på skjermen (berøringsskjermer og telefoner beholder de store knappene). Hvert sidepanel har en smal tittellinje: dra den for å feste eller frigjøre panelet, slipp den på et annet panels linje for å dele én ramme som faner, fest det for å gli bort til kanten, eller skjul det og hent det tilbake fra kanten. Se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/arrange-panels#make-the-layout-denser\" data-topic=\"basics/arrange-panels\" data-hash=\"make-the-layout-denser\"\u003EOrdne panelene\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDialoger flyttes.\u003C/strong\u003E Dra en dialog etter tittellinjen ut av veien for tegningen; den kommer tilbake der du lot den stå. Dobbeltklikk tittellinjen for å sentrere den igjen. Se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/arrange-panels#move-a-dialog\" data-topic=\"basics/arrange-panels\" data-hash=\"move-a-dialog\"\u003EFlytt en dialog\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOppskrifter for AI-assistenter.\u003C/strong\u003E Ti gjennomarbeidede forespørsler til din egen assistent (rydd en tegning, plott et sett, kontroller tegninger, still en modell spørsmål, summer et mengdeuttak, prøv en delvariant …), hver med verktøyene den kaller og tillatelsene den trenger, og hver avspilt av testene våre. Appene inneholder ingen AI. Se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/assistants-recipes\" data-topic=\"basics/assistants-recipes\"\u003EOppskrifter for AI-assistenter\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESpråk.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cstrong\u003EPortuguês (Portugal)\u003C/strong\u003E er nytt i \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInnstillinger\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGenerelt\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpråk\u003C/span\u003E. Det, \u003Cstrong\u003EPortuguês (Brasil)\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EEspañol (México)\u003C/strong\u003E og \u003Cstrong\u003EBahasa Indonesia\u003C/strong\u003E har nå hvert ord i appene, som utkast som ennå ikke er gjennomgått av en morsmålsbruker. Hjelpeemnene er på engelsk.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECadCraft Studio\u003C/strong\u003E sier hvorfor en oppdateringskontroll feilet (ingen fil for systemet ditt ennå, ingen nettverk, en ugyldig signatur) i stedet for bare «Ferdig», og slutter å hente tilbake et Nytt-kort du lukket før det finnes en nyere utgivelse.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"ikke-i-denne-versjonen-enn\"\u003EIkke i denne versjonen ennå\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E0.7 er ikke kodesignert.\u003C/strong\u003E Signering, og Microsoft Store-pakken, ble flyttet til 0.8 fordi sertifikatvalideringen ikke har kommet gjennom ennå. Kontroller SHA-256 ved siden av hver fil på getcadcraft.com/download. På Windows, hvis SmartScreen stopper installasjonsprogrammet, velg \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMer informasjon\u003C/span\u003E, deretter \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKjør likevel\u003C/span\u003E. På en Mac høyreklikker du CadCraft Studio første gang og velger \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EÅpne\u003C/span\u003E. CadCraft Studio er ikke i en appbutikk ennå, og det finnes ingen iPad- eller iPhone-versjon ennå.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EVinkel- og hellingslistene i \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEnheter\u003C/span\u003E lagres, men ingen boks leser dem ennå.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEn DWGs OLE-objekter beholdes, men vises ikke, og metafilbilder inne i OLE-objekter tegnes ikke.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EÅ lese plottstiltabeller (CTB-filer) fra andre programmer venter på en eksempelfil.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEldre asiatisk tekst skrevet som \u003Ccode\u003E\\M+\u003C/code\u003E-koder leses, men er ikke kontrollert mot en fil fra et annet program ennå.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPDF-underlag: bilder inne i siden vises på skjermen, men plottes ikke, en assistents plott utelater underlag, og hvordan andre programmer leser det lagrede klippet og målestokken er ikke kontrollert i dem.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHistorikk: CadCraft sender ikke lagringene sine til skyen ennå, så skyversjonene er de som er lagret fra andre enheter og nettet. Historikk finnes bare i CadCraft Studio.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOm Studio beholder støttemappen for acad.lsp etter en omstart, er ikke kontrollert ennå; hvis den spør, velg mappen igjen i APPLOAD.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELaser-panelet er ikke prøvd på en ekte laser ennå: test hver innstilling på skrap. CadCraft Studio har ingen Web Serial, så i Studio lagrer panelet bare G-kode.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERegistrering av skanninger er testet på oppdiktede skanninger, ennå ikke på et ekte skannersett.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPanorering med to fingre er prøvd med simulerte styreflatehendelser, ennå ikke på en ekte styreflate.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPEDIT\u003C/code\u003E har ikke alternativet Rediger hjørnepunkt (bruk håndtak eller Egenskaper). Forskyv, trim, forleng, avrund, bryt og slå sammen tar ikke splines ennå; en splines høyde (Z) beholdes ikke.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENy avsnittstekst kan ikke gis formateringskoder ennå, og SVG-plott viser formatert tekst i én farge.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWBLOCKs Hele tegningen skriver bare modellen, ikke arkene. Den gjeldende måltilsettingsstilen lagres ikke i filen: en tegning åpnes med Standard som gjeldende.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EBånd-menystilene har ingen lister over layer, farger eller linjetyper ennå: bruk panelet \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayer\u003C/span\u003E og \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEgenskaper\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPunktskyer lagres fortsatt ikke i tegningen eller plottes, og E57-filer åpnes opp til 6 millioner punkter og 512 MB.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECadCrafts lagrede DWG-filer er ikke kontrollert i AutoCAD selv ennå.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAlt annet står i \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003EIkke bygget ennå\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Dette er det som er endret i CadCraft 0.7 sammenlignet med 0.6. Mye av det ble etterspurt på Discord. Listen er gruppert etter område, med det viktigste først. Nytt PDF-er og skannede tegninger Legg en PDF-side under tegningen. PDFATTACH (PDFATT) plasserer en side i sann størrelse eller en målestokk du skriver. Fang til linjene, tone den ned, gjør den monokrom (PDFADJUST), klipp den (PDFCLIP) og slå PDF-ens egne layer av og på (PDFLAYERS). Den plottes som linjer, fyll og tekst, og en DWG eller DXF 2000 beholder den som en lenke til filen. Å åpne en PDF med Åpne spør om den skal legges ved eller importeres. Se PDF-underlag og PDF-import. Gjør en PDF-side om til tegneobjekter. PDFIMPORT (PDFIM) lager linjer, polylinjer, sirkler, buer, heldekkende skraveringer og tekst på layer oppkalt etter PDF-en, i sann størrelse, en innskrevet målestokk, eller skalert fra to punkter og en ekte avstand, i ett angretrinn. Se Importer en side. Skannede tegninger. SCANATTACH henter inn en PNG, JPEG eller TIFF (én side eller alle) eller en PDF-side som er et bilde. Deretter kan du skalere den fra to punkter (SCANSCALE), justere den (SCANALIGN), rette den opp etter sine egne lange linjer (SCANDESKEW), gjøre den svart-hvit og fjerne flekker (SCANCLEAN), beskjære den og viske ut deler av den. Hvert trinn er ett angretrinn, og originalfilen beholdes. Se Skannede tegninger og traseringen. Trasér en skanning til linjer og buer. SCANTRACE tegner skanningens linjer som linjer og buer på et nytt layer, SCAN-TRACE, med en toleranse du setter, og lar tekst og skravur stå som de er. Rapporten sier hva den laget og hva den lot ligge, og den hevder ikke at resultatet er riktig: kontroller det mot arket. RASTERSNAP lar objektfangene finne skanningens ender, hjørner og kryssinger. Ingenting sendes noe sted, og ingen trent modell brukes. Se Traseringen. Tegning og redigering Ekte splines. SPLINE (SPL) tegner en kurve gjennom punkter eller fra kontrollpunkter (grad 1 til 5), med endetangenter; SPLINEDIT (SPE) flytter, legger til og sletter tilpasnings- eller kontrollpunkter, setter vekter, åpner, lukker, snur, eller gjør en spline om til en polylinje. Splines fra DWG- og DXF-filer åpnes som splines, og DWG og DXF 2000 lagrer dem som splines. Se Splines og polylinjer. PEDIT. PEDIT (PE) lukker og åpner polylinjer, slår linjer og buer sammen med dem, setter én bredde, tilpasser en kurve gjennom hjørnepunktene eller en spline langs dem og tar den tilbake med Decurve, snur, og redigerer flere samtidig. Se Rediger en polylinje. WBLOCK. WBLOCK (W) skriver en blokk, noen objekter eller hele tegningen til en ny DWG eller DXF som bare inneholder layerne, stilene og blokkene de bruker. Se Skriv en blokk eller objekter til en egen fil. REGEN og REGENALL. REGEN (RE) tegner fanen du er på om fra tegningen, REGENALL (REA) hver fane, rute og åpne tegning. Se REGEN og REGENALL. Tekst og måltilsettinger Tekst beholder formateringen. Avsnittstekst fra andre programmer beholder fargene per ord, fet og kursiv, understreking, stablede brøker og høyder: de tegnes på skjermen og i PDF-er, beholdes når du redigerer ordene, og lagres tilbake. Se Formatering i avsnittstekst fra andre programmer. En behandler for måltilsettingsstiler. DIMSTYLE (D) lager, endrer, sammenligner og bruker navngitte måltilsettingsstiler; å endre en stil endrer hver måltilsetting som bruker den. DIMEDIT (DED) setter tilbake tekst, skriver ny tekst, dreier den eller skrår målhjelpelinjene; DIMTEDIT flytter teksten til venstre, til høyre eller til midten. Se Navngitte måltilsettingsstiler. Egne målestokker. SCALELISTEDIT (SCALELIST) legger til målestokker som 1:75 i listene for visningsområde og plott; de lagres i tegningen (DWG og DXF 2000), og en tegning fra et annet program bringer med sin egen liste. Se Målestokker. Tekst i andre alfabeter. DXF-filer lagret i kyrilliske, koreanske, kinesiske, japanske eller vietnamesiske kodesider åpnes nå med teksten sin, og kinesisk, japansk og koreansk tekst skrives ut i PDF-er (plottet henter en større font første gang den trengs). Se Font. Plotting og utskrift Mer i plottstiltabeller. For hver farge kan en tabell nå også sette en linjetype, linjeende og linjesammenføyning, og en fyllstil som skriver ut heldekkende fyll som skravurlinjer; tabellen har sin egen liste over linjevekter og en linjetypeskala. Se Plottstiltabeller. Skriv ut, og PNG- og SVG-filer. Plott-dialogen har Skriv ut…, Last ned PNG (300 dpi) og Last ned SVG. I CadCraft Studio lister den datamaskinens skrivere, papiret deres og antall kopier. Se Skriv ut og Bilde- og SVG-filer. 3D og punktskyer 3D-volumer fra andre programmer. Volumer, regioner og legemer i en tegnings modellrom vises på 3D-fanen, skrivebeskyttet, og å lagre som DWG skriver dem tilbake uendret. Flater på overflater CadCraft ikke tegner ennå, listes, ikke gjettes på. Se Volumer fra andre programmer. Registrer flere skanninger. REGISTERSCANS (REGSCAN) plasserer skanninger tatt fra ulike posisjoner i én ramme, ved punktpar eller innskrevne målkoordinater, finjusterer dem med ICP, lagrer registreringen som en liten prosjektfil, og skriver den registrerte skyen som én LAS- eller E57-fil. Se Registrer flere skanninger til én. USD-eksport. USDOUT, eller EXPORT3D › USD, skriver 3D-fanens volumer som .usda, .usdc eller .usdz. Se Eksporter 3D-modellen som USD. Beregninger på 3D-fanen. Der 3D-fanens verktøy ber om en avstand, kan du skrive en beregning, som på kommandolinjen. Se Regning i en innskrevet verdi. Lage ting Send en tegning til en laser. LASER, eller Visning › Mer › Laser…, åpner et panel over tegningen: hvert layer skjærer, ripes, graverer linjer, fyller former, graverer et bilde eller sendes ikke, med effekt, hastighet og antall gjennomganger. Forhåndsvisningen følger redigeringene dine, Lagre G-kode… skriver GRBL-G-kode i lasermodus, og i Chrome eller Edge kan panelet ramme inn jobben og sende den over USB. Innstillingene lagres i tegningen (DWG og DXF). Se Send en tegning til en laser. Symboler for bygningsinstallasjoner i tillegget Electrical. Data-, telefon- og TV-uttak, nøkkel- og tidsbrytere, en termostat, nødutgangsskilt, detektorer, brannmelder og sirene, en vifte, varmtvannsbereder, komfyr, ladepunkt for elbil, energimåler og underfordeling, og symboler for RCD og RCBO. En kurs kan ha en RCD, tavlelisten har en RCD-kolonne, og kontrollene navngir stikkontaktkurser uten RCD på 30 mA eller mindre. Det er en planleggingspåminnelse, ikke en forskriftskontroll. Et P&ID-tillegg. P&ID tegner prosessdiagrammer: symboler med tilkoblingspunkter, prosess- og signallinjer, merker, linjenumre, lister over linjer, utstyr, instrumenter og ventiler (CSV eller en arbeidsbok) og kontroller. Det følger med CadCraft og er avslått til du slår det på i Innstillinger › Generelt › Tillegg. Det er et tegnehjelpemiddel, ikke en ingeniørgodkjenning, og teksten er foreløpig bare på engelsk. Komme i gang og skjermen En veiledet første tegning. TOUR (eller Ta omvisningen på startkortet) tar deg gjennom seks trinn i den virkelige tegningen: en linje, en sirkel, en flytting, en trimming, en sletting og en måltilsetting. Den starter av seg selv første gang CadCraft åpnes med ingenting i. Se Foretrekker du veiledning? En jukselapp og et kommandokart. Jukselappen har de 40 daglige kommandoene på én side, også som PDF til utskrift, og kommandokartet lister kommandoer med kortnavnene deres, A til Å. Verktøylinjen på hvilken som helst side. Innstillinger › CadCraft › Verktøylinjens plassering: topp (som før), bunn, venstre eller høyre, eller skriv TOOLBARDOCK. Se Verktøylinjens plassering. Verktøylinjerader. Innstillinger › CadCraft › Verktøylinjerader: bryt for å passe, 2 rader eller 1 rad, for CadCraft-verktøylinjen og stilene Klassisk tegning og Bånd. Shift+Ctrl+F1 går gjennom dem, Ctrl+F1 gjennom det knappene viser, og TOOLBARROWS og TOOLBAR setter dem. Starthintet har en lukkeknapp. Takk til GJoe2, som sendte dette som en pull request. Se Verktøylinjerader. I CadCraft Studio Historikk. HISTORY (HIST), eller Visning › Mer › Historikk…: kopiene Studio beholder når du åpner og lagrer en tegning, og tegningens skyversjoner når du åpnet den fra skyen. Åpne eller gjenopprett én, eller sammenlign to, med det som er lagt til, fjernet og endret vist i farger. Se Historikk. acad.lsp og acaddoc.lsp. Velg en støttemappe i APPLOAD: de to filene lastes når CadCraft starter (acaddoc.lsp på nytt for hver nye tegningsfane), og S::STARTUP kjører etter dem. Se Last inn applikasjoner. Plott-dialogen i et eget vindu. En knapp i tittellinjen flytter den til et vindu du kan legge på en annen skjerm. Se Flytt en dialog. For AI-assistenter Filer ved navn, i mapper du velger. I CadCraft Studio kan en assistent åpne, lagre og plotte filer ved navn, og gi en kommando filen den ber om (INSERT fra en fil, COUNTs eksport, SAVEAS, PLOT), men bare inne i mappene du legger til under Innstillinger › CadCraft › Assistenter › Mapper den kan bruke. Se La den bruke filer i mapper du velger. Færre kall. En assistent kan skrive en hel kommandolinje på én gang, holde en kommando åpen mellom kallene sine, telle objekter etter type og layer uten å liste dem, og plassere en blokk med attributtverdiene sine i ett trinn. Uten vindu. Kommandolinjen cadcraft skriver SVG-plott og konverterer, plotter, kontrollerer eller beskriver mange filer på én gang. MCP-pakken er ikke publisert ennå. Se Arbeid på filer uten å åpne CadCraft. Endret En styreflate panorerer. Flytt to fingre på en styreflate, og 2D-visningen følger dem; knip og musehjulet zoomer. Se Mus, styreflate og berøring. OLE-objekter beholdes. En DXFs innebygde objekter vises som bildet sitt eller som en merket ramme og lagres tilbake av DXF 2000. En DWGs beholdes og skrives tilbake når du lagrer som DWG, men vises ikke ennå. Før gikk de tapt ved lagring. Se Objekter fra andre programmer (OLE). Enhetene i en ny tegning følger enheten du setter stjerne på i Innstillinger › Generelt › Enheter. Rettet Layouter som åpnet tomme, eller mistet et visningsområde og viste hele modellen i stedet, åpnes nå med de virkelige visningsområdene sine. En layout uten lenke til innholdet beholdes, og åpningsrapporten sier det. DXF-filer i eldre kodesider viste forvansket kyrillisk og koreansk tekst. De leses riktig nå. Avsnittstekst fra andre programmer mistet formateringen da den ble åpnet og ble lagret tilbake som ren tekst. I alle apper Enheter. Innstillinger › Generelt › Enheter har én liste hver for lengde, areal, volum, vinkel og helling, med de eksakte størrelsene enhetenes definisjoner gir (den amerikanske målefoten er sin egen enhet). Slå av enhetene du aldri bruker, så forlater de enhetslistene; sett stjerne på én, så starter nytt arbeid i den. En fil beholder alltid sin egen enhet. I CadCraft setter stjernen enhetene i en ny tegning når det er en enhet en tegning kan bære; CadCrafts egen boks Enheter følger ikke bryterne ennå. Når en tegning har enheter, kan en lengde du skriver, bære merket til en hvilken som helst enhet. Se Enheter, innskrevne verdier og enhetslistene. Et tettere oppsett og paneler som ryddes bort. Innstillinger › Generelt › Layouttetthet › Kompakt får plass til mer på skjermen (berøringsskjermer og telefoner beholder de store knappene). Hvert sidepanel har en smal tittellinje: dra den for å feste eller frigjøre panelet, slipp den på et annet panels linje for å dele én ramme som faner, fest det for å gli bort til kanten, eller skjul det og hent det tilbake fra kanten. Se Ordne panelene. Dialoger flyttes. Dra en dialog etter tittellinjen ut av veien for tegningen; den kommer tilbake der du lot den stå. Dobbeltklikk tittellinjen for å sentrere den igjen. Se Flytt en dialog. Oppskrifter for AI-assistenter. Ti gjennomarbeidede forespørsler til din egen assistent (rydd en tegning, plott et sett, kontroller tegninger, still en modell spørsmål, summer et mengdeuttak, prøv en delvariant …), hver med verktøyene den kaller og tillatelsene den trenger, og hver avspilt av testene våre. Appene inneholder ingen AI. Se Oppskrifter for AI-assistenter. Språk. Português (Portugal) er nytt i Innstillinger › Generelt › Språk. Det, Português (Brasil), Español (México) og Bahasa Indonesia har nå hvert ord i appene, som utkast som ennå ikke er gjennomgått av en morsmålsbruker. Hjelpeemnene er på engelsk. CadCraft Studio sier hvorfor en oppdateringskontroll feilet (ingen fil for systemet ditt ennå, ingen nettverk, en ugyldig signatur) i stedet for bare «Ferdig», og slutter å hente tilbake et Nytt-kort du lukket før det finnes en nyere utgivelse. Ikke i denne versjonen ennå 0.7 er ikke kodesignert. Signering, og Microsoft Store-pakken, ble flyttet til 0.8 fordi sertifikatvalideringen ikke har kommet gjennom ennå. Kontroller SHA-256 ved siden av hver fil på getcadcraft.com/download. På Windows, hvis SmartScreen stopper installasjonsprogrammet, velg Mer informasjon, deretter Kjør likevel. På en Mac høyreklikker du CadCraft Studio første gang og velger Åpne. CadCraft Studio er ikke i en appbutikk ennå, og det finnes ingen iPad- eller iPhone-versjon ennå. Vinkel- og hellingslistene i Enheter lagres, men ingen boks leser dem ennå. En DWGs OLE-objekter beholdes, men vises ikke, og metafilbilder inne i OLE-objekter tegnes ikke. Å lese plottstiltabeller (CTB-filer) fra andre programmer venter på en eksempelfil. Eldre asiatisk tekst skrevet som \\M+-koder leses, men er ikke kontrollert mot en fil fra et annet program ennå. PDF-underlag: bilder inne i siden vises på skjermen, men plottes ikke, en assistents plott utelater underlag, og hvordan andre programmer leser det lagrede klippet og målestokken er ikke kontrollert i dem. Historikk: CadCraft sender ikke lagringene sine til skyen ennå, så skyversjonene er de som er lagret fra andre enheter og nettet. Historikk finnes bare i CadCraft Studio. Om Studio beholder støttemappen for acad.lsp etter en omstart, er ikke kontrollert ennå; hvis den spør, velg mappen igjen i APPLOAD. Laser-panelet er ikke prøvd på en ekte laser ennå: test hver innstilling på skrap. CadCraft Studio har ingen Web Serial, så i Studio lagrer panelet bare G-kode. Registrering av skanninger er testet på oppdiktede skanninger, ennå ikke på et ekte skannersett. Panorering med to fingre er prøvd med simulerte styreflatehendelser, ennå ikke på en ekte styreflate. PEDIT har ikke alternativet Rediger hjørnepunkt (bruk håndtak eller Egenskaper). Forskyv, trim, forleng, avrund, bryt og slå sammen tar ikke splines ennå; en splines høyde (Z) beholdes ikke. Ny avsnittstekst kan ikke gis formateringskoder ennå, og SVG-plott viser formatert tekst i én farge. WBLOCKs Hele tegningen skriver bare modellen, ikke arkene. Den gjeldende måltilsettingsstilen lagres ikke i filen: en tegning åpnes med Standard som gjeldende. Bånd-menystilene har ingen lister over layer, farger eller linjetyper ennå: bruk panelet Layer og Egenskaper. Punktskyer lagres fortsatt ikke i tegningen eller plottes, og E57-filer åpnes opp til 6 millioner punkter og 512 MB. CadCrafts lagrede DWG-filer er ikke kontrollert i AutoCAD selv ennå. Alt annet står i Ikke bygget ennå.","lang":"nb"},{"slug":"getting-started","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Kom i gang med CadCraft","summary":"Hva tegneappen CadCraft gjør, hvor den kjører, og den første tegningen din fra en tom skjerm til en lagret fil.","section":"start","order":10,"keywords":["start","første tegning","ny","åpne","lagre","lagre som","begynne","veiledning","innføring","hurtigstart"],"headings":[{"id":"foretrekker-du-veiledning","text":"Foretrekker du veiledning?","level":2},{"id":"dette-trenger-du","text":"Dette trenger du","level":2},{"id":"ideen-i-ett-avsnitt","text":"Ideen i ett avsnitt","level":2},{"id":"din-f-rste-tegning","text":"Din første tegning","level":2},{"id":"pne-en-eksisterende-tegning","text":"Åpne en eksisterende tegning","level":2},{"id":"lagring-hva-du-kan-vente","text":"Lagring: hva du kan vente","level":2},{"id":"veien-videre","text":"Veien videre","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ECadCraft er en 2D-tegneapp. Du tegner og redigerer linjer, buer, polylinjer, tekst, måltilsettinger og blokker, setter opp ark med tegningshoder og visningsområder, og plotter dem til PDF. Den åpner DWG- og DXF-tegninger og lagrer dem som DWG eller DXF.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDen kjører i nettleseren din. Det er ingenting å installere: nettstedet gir deg adressen, og hele appen lastes inn i siden. Tegningene dine åpnes fra din egen datamaskin og lagres tilbake dit. \u003Cstrong\u003EIngenting lastes opp.\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHvis du har tegnet før, les \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/coming-from\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/coming-from\"\u003EHvis du kommer fra annen tegneprogramvare\u003C/a\u003E etter denne siden. De fleste vanene følger med, og de få som ikke gjør det, er listet der.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"foretrekker-du-veiledning\"\u003EForetrekker du veiledning?\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESkriv \u003Ccode\u003ETOUR\u003C/code\u003E (eller klikk \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETa omvisningen\u003C/span\u003E på startkortet, eller \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInnstillinger\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETa omvisningen igjen\u003C/span\u003E). Seks korte trinn i en boks over tegningen: tegn en linje og en sirkel, flytt den, trim den, slett noe og måltilsett det. Hvert trinn er ferdig når du gjør det, og du kan hoppe over et trinn eller hele omvisningen. Den starter av seg selv bare første gang. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/cheat-sheet\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/cheat-sheet\"\u003EJukselappen\u003C/a\u003E har de 40 kommandoene du bruker mest, og \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/command-map\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/command-map\"\u003Ekommandokartet\u003C/a\u003E hver eneste kommando.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"dette-trenger-du\"\u003EDette trenger du\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEn oppdatert Chrome, Edge, Firefox eller Safari på en Mac-, Windows- eller Linux-datamaskin. Se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/browsers\" data-topic=\"basics/browsers\"\u003ENettlesere og plattformer\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEn mus med hjul er mest behagelig. En styreflate fungerer; se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/mouse-trackpad-touch\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/mouse-trackpad-touch\"\u003EMus, styreflate og berøring\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEt tastatur. CadCraft styres med innskrevne kommandoer, som de fleste tegneprogrammer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"ideen-i-ett-avsnitt\"\u003EIdeen i ett avsnitt\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDu skriver en kommando (eller klikker knappen), og kommandolinjen nederst stiller deg spørsmål ett om gangen: «Angi første punkt», «Angi neste punkt». Du svarer på hvert spørsmål ved å klikke i tegningen eller skrive en verdi, og så trykke \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E avbryter. Det er hele samspillsmodellen; alt annet er detaljer.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"din-f-rste-tegning\"\u003EDin første tegning\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDu skal tegne et rektangel på 4000 ganger 3000 av linjer, sette en sirkel i midten og lagre filen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EÅpne CadCraft. Kommandolinjen nederst viser \u003Ccode\u003EKommando:\u003C/code\u003E og tegneområdet viser et kort «Begynn å tegne».\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESkriv \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E og trykk \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Du trenger ikke klikke kommandolinjen først: det du skriver hvor som helst, havner der.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESpørsmålet endres til \u003Ccode\u003EAngi første punkt:\u003C/code\u003E. Skriv \u003Ccode\u003E0,0\u003C/code\u003E og trykk \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESkriv \u003Ccode\u003E@4000,0\u003C/code\u003E og trykk \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. En linje dukker opp og løper mot høyre. \u003Ccode\u003E@\u003C/code\u003E betyr «fra siste punkt».\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESkriv \u003Ccode\u003E@0,3000\u003C/code\u003E og trykk \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESkriv \u003Ccode\u003E@-4000,0\u003C/code\u003E og trykk \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESkriv \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E og trykk \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E er alternativet Close: den siste linjen går tilbake til første punkt, og kommandoen avsluttes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EKlikk \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUtstrekning\u003C/span\u003E i gruppen Visning på verktøylinjen. Rektangelet fyller skjermen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDu skal nå se fire linjer som danner et rektangel, og historikken over kommandolinjen lister det du skrev.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESkriv \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E og trykk \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for å starte sirkelkommandoen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESkriv \u003Ccode\u003E2000,1500\u003C/code\u003E og trykk \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for sentrum.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESkriv \u003Ccode\u003E500\u003C/code\u003E og trykk \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for radius.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEn sirkel dukker opp midt i rektangelet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETrykk \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, eller klikk \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELagre\u003C/span\u003E i gruppen Fil.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENettleseren laster ned \u003Ccode\u003EDrawing1.dwg\u003C/code\u003E til den vanlige nedlastingsmappen din, og historikken sier at den lagret en kopi som DWG. Den filen åpnes i CadCraft igjen, og i de fleste andre CAD-programmer. (Hvis du har valgt DXF i \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELagre som\u003C/span\u003E tidligere, i denne nettleseren, lagres en ny tegning som \u003Ccode\u003EDrawing1.dxf\u003C/code\u003E i stedet.)\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETrykk \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E på en tom kommandolinje for å gjenta forrige kommando. Trykk \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E i stedet for \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E hvis du foretrekker det: utenfor tekst gjør den det samme.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"pne-en-eksisterende-tegning\"\u003EÅpne en eksisterende tegning\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETrykk \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, eller klikk \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EÅpne\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EVelg en \u003Ccode\u003E.dwg\u003C/code\u003E- eller \u003Ccode\u003E.dxf\u003C/code\u003E-fil i filvelgeren.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDu kan også dra en DWG- eller DXF-fil fra \u003Cspan class=\"hp\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:mac\" title=\"Mac\"\u003EFinder\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:windows\" title=\"Windows\"\u003EFilutforsker\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:linux\" title=\"Linux\"\u003Efilbehandleren\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/span\u003E og slippe den på tegneområdet. Filen leses i siden; den forlater aldri datamaskinen din.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESe \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/files\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/files\"\u003EFiler: hva som åpnes, hva som lagres\u003C/a\u003E for hva som beholdes og hva som konverteres.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"lagring-hva-du-kan-vente\"\u003ELagring: hva du kan vente\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELagring i en nettleser fungerer annerledes enn i et skrivebordsprogram:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELagre\u003C/span\u003E \u003Cstrong\u003Elaster alltid ned en ny kopi\u003C/strong\u003E. Den skriver ikke over filen du åpnet. Hvis en fil med samme navn allerede ligger i nedlastingsmappen din, legger nettleseren vanligvis til et tall, for eksempel \u003Ccode\u003EDrawing1 (1).dwg\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELagre\u003C/span\u003E bruker tegningens format: DWG hvis du åpnet en DWG, DXF hvis du åpnet en DXF. For å velge trykker du \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command S\"\u003E⇧⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E eller klikker \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELagre som\u003C/span\u003E, og velger \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDWG\u003C/span\u003E (og versjonen), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDXF R12\u003C/span\u003E eller \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDXF 2000\u003C/span\u003E. Se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/files#saving\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/files\" data-hash=\"saving\"\u003ELagring\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFilen du åpnet blir aldri endret.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EÅ åpne en annen fil, eller \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENy\u003C/span\u003E, legger den i en egen fane over tegningen, så ingenting du har åpent lukkes. Hvis du lukker en tegnings fane eller forlater siden med ulagrede endringer, spør CadCraft først: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELagre en kopi først\u003C/span\u003E, forkast eller \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAvbryt\u003C/span\u003E. Å lukke nettleserfanen viser i stedet nettleserens eget spørsmål «Forlate nettstedet?».\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESkylagring finnes ikke ennå. Hvor filene dine havner og hvorfor de forblir private, forklares i \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/your-files\" data-topic=\"basics/your-files\"\u003EFilene dine og personvern\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"veien-videre\"\u003EVeien videre\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETrykk \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E eller klikk \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHjelp\u003C/span\u003E i linjen øverst for å åpne denne hjelpen ved siden av tegningen når som helst. Den fungerer uten forbindelse.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/workspace\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/workspace\"\u003ECadCraft-skjermen\u003C/a\u003E navngir hver linje og hvert panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/draw-with-coordinates\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/draw-with-coordinates\"\u003ETegn med koordinater\u003C/a\u003E er den beste første gjennomgangen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/keyboard\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/keyboard\"\u003ETastatur og mus\u003C/a\u003E lister hver tast.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESkriv \u003Ccode\u003EHELP\u003C/code\u003E på kommandolinjen for en liste over alle kommandoer, eller les \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/commands\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/commands\"\u003EKommandoreferansen\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"CadCraft er en 2D-tegneapp. Du tegner og redigerer linjer, buer, polylinjer, tekst, måltilsettinger og blokker, setter opp ark med tegningshoder og visningsområder, og plotter dem til PDF. Den åpner DWG- og DXF-tegninger og lagrer dem som DWG eller DXF. Den kjører i nettleseren din. Det er ingenting å installere: nettstedet gir deg adressen, og hele appen lastes inn i siden. Tegningene dine åpnes fra din egen datamaskin og lagres tilbake dit. Ingenting lastes opp. Hvis du har tegnet før, les Hvis du kommer fra annen tegneprogramvare etter denne siden. De fleste vanene følger med, og de få som ikke gjør det, er listet der. Foretrekker du veiledning? Skriv TOUR (eller klikk Ta omvisningen på startkortet, eller Innstillinger › CadCraft › Ta omvisningen igjen). Seks korte trinn i en boks over tegningen: tegn en linje og en sirkel, flytt den, trim den, slett noe og måltilsett det. Hvert trinn er ferdig når du gjør det, og du kan hoppe over et trinn eller hele omvisningen. Den starter av seg selv bare første gang. Jukselappen har de 40 kommandoene du bruker mest, og kommandokartet hver eneste kommando. Dette trenger du En oppdatert Chrome, Edge, Firefox eller Safari på en Mac-, Windows- eller Linux-datamaskin. Se Nettlesere og plattformer. En mus med hjul er mest behagelig. En styreflate fungerer; se Mus, styreflate og berøring. Et tastatur. CadCraft styres med innskrevne kommandoer, som de fleste tegneprogrammer. Ideen i ett avsnitt Du skriver en kommando (eller klikker knappen), og kommandolinjen nederst stiller deg spørsmål ett om gangen: «Angi første punkt», «Angi neste punkt». Du svarer på hvert spørsmål ved å klikke i tegningen eller skrive en verdi, og så trykke Enter. Esc avbryter. Det er hele samspillsmodellen; alt annet er detaljer. Din første tegning Du skal tegne et rektangel på 4000 ganger 3000 av linjer, sette en sirkel i midten og lagre filen. Åpne CadCraft. Kommandolinjen nederst viser Kommando: og tegneområdet viser et kort «Begynn å tegne». Skriv L og trykk Enter. Du trenger ikke klikke kommandolinjen først: det du skriver hvor som helst, havner der. Spørsmålet endres til Angi første punkt:. Skriv 0,0 og trykk Enter. Skriv @4000,0 og trykk Enter. En linje dukker opp og løper mot høyre. @ betyr «fra siste punkt». Skriv @0,3000 og trykk Enter. Skriv @-4000,0 og trykk Enter. Skriv C og trykk Enter. C er alternativet Close: den siste linjen går tilbake til første punkt, og kommandoen avsluttes. Klikk Utstrekning i gruppen Visning på verktøylinjen. Rektangelet fyller skjermen. Du skal nå se fire linjer som danner et rektangel, og historikken over kommandolinjen lister det du skrev. Skriv C og trykk Enter for å starte sirkelkommandoen. Skriv 2000,1500 og trykk Enter for sentrum. Skriv 500 og trykk Enter for radius. En sirkel dukker opp midt i rektangelet. Trykk Ctrl+S, eller klikk Lagre i gruppen Fil. Nettleseren laster ned Drawing1.dwg til den vanlige nedlastingsmappen din, og historikken sier at den lagret en kopi som DWG. Den filen åpnes i CadCraft igjen, og i de fleste andre CAD-programmer. (Hvis du har valgt DXF i Lagre som tidligere, i denne nettleseren, lagres en ny tegning som Drawing1.dxf i stedet.) Trykk Enter på en tom kommandolinje for å gjenta forrige kommando. Trykk Space i stedet for Enter hvis du foretrekker det: utenfor tekst gjør den det samme. Åpne en eksisterende tegning Trykk Ctrl+O, eller klikk Åpne. Velg en .dwg- eller .dxf-fil i filvelgeren. Du kan også dra en DWG- eller DXF-fil fra Finder / Filutforsker / filbehandleren og slippe den på tegneområdet. Filen leses i siden; den forlater aldri datamaskinen din. Se Filer: hva som åpnes, hva som lagres for hva som beholdes og hva som konverteres. Lagring: hva du kan vente Lagring i en nettleser fungerer annerledes enn i et skrivebordsprogram: Lagre laster alltid ned en ny kopi. Den skriver ikke over filen du åpnet. Hvis en fil med samme navn allerede ligger i nedlastingsmappen din, legger nettleseren vanligvis til et tall, for eksempel Drawing1 (1).dwg. Lagre bruker tegningens format: DWG hvis du åpnet en DWG, DXF hvis du åpnet en DXF. For å velge trykker du Ctrl+Shift+S eller klikker Lagre som, og velger DWG (og versjonen), DXF R12 eller DXF 2000. Se Lagring. Filen du åpnet blir aldri endret. Å åpne en annen fil, eller Ny, legger den i en egen fane over tegningen, så ingenting du har åpent lukkes. Hvis du lukker en tegnings fane eller forlater siden med ulagrede endringer, spør CadCraft først: Lagre en kopi først, forkast eller Avbryt. Å lukke nettleserfanen viser i stedet nettleserens eget spørsmål «Forlate nettstedet?». Skylagring finnes ikke ennå. Hvor filene dine havner og hvorfor de forblir private, forklares i Filene dine og personvern. Veien videre Trykk F1 eller klikk Hjelp i linjen øverst for å åpne denne hjelpen ved siden av tegningen når som helst. Den fungerer uten forbindelse. CadCraft-skjermen navngir hver linje og hvert panel. Tegn med koordinater er den beste første gjennomgangen. Tastatur og mus lister hver tast. Skriv HELP på kommandolinjen for en liste over alle kommandoer, eller les Kommandoreferansen.","lang":"nb"},{"slug":"cheat-sheet","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Jukselapp: de 40 daglige kommandoene","summary":"Én side med de 40 kommandoene du bruker mest, kortnavnene deres og tastene som sparer tid. Last den ned som PDF for å skrive den ut.","section":"start","order":12,"keywords":["jukselapp","hurtigreferanse","kommandoer","aliaser","kortnavn","nybegynner","slett","trim","skriv ut","pdf","snarveier"],"headings":[{"id":"tegn","text":"Tegn","level":2},{"id":"endre","text":"Endre","level":2},{"id":"m-ltilsetting","text":"Måltilsetting","level":2},{"id":"blokker-og-layer","text":"Blokker og layer","level":2},{"id":"visning-og-foresp-rsler","text":"Visning og forespørsler","level":2},{"id":"fil","text":"Fil","level":2},{"id":"taster","text":"Taster","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ESkriv kortnavnet (aliaset) og trykk Enter, eller klikk verktøyet. Esc avbryter. Enter på en tom linje gjentar forrige kommando.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESkriv den ut:\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ca href=\"https://getcadcraft.com/assets/apps/cadcraft/current/cheat-sheet.pdf\" class=\"hx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"\u003Echeat-sheet.pdf\u003C/a\u003E (én A4-side; på Letter-papir skriver du den ut med «tilpass til side»).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"tegn\"\u003ETegn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESkriv\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKommando\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EHva den gjør\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELINE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETegne rette linjer\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPL\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPLINE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETegne en sammenhengende kjede av linjer og buer\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECIRCLE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETegne en sirkel (sentrum og radius)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EARC\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETegne en bue\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EREC\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ERECTANG\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETegne et rektangel fra to hjørner\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPOL\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPOLYGON\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETegne en regulær mangekant\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EEL\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EELLIPSE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETegne en ellipse\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ETEXT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESkrive én tekstlinje\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ET\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMTEXT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESkrive tekst som brytes (avsnitt)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EH\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EHATCH\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFylle et område med et mønster\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"endre\"\u003EEndre\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESkriv\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKommando\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EHva den gjør\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EM\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMOVE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFlytte objekter\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ECO\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECOPY\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EKopiere objekter\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ERO\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EROTATE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDreie objekter rundt et punkt\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESC\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESCALE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGjøre objekter større eller mindre\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EMI\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMIRROR\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESpeile objekter om en linje\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EO\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EOFFSET\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETegne en parallell kopi i en avstand\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ETR\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ETRIM\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EKutte objekter tilbake til der andre krysser dem\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EEX\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXTEND\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EForlenge objekter fram til andre\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EF\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EFILLET\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERunde av et hjørne med en bue\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ECHA\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECHAMFER\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EKutte et hjørne med en rett kant\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EERASE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESlette objekter\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EX\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXPLODE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBryte en blokk eller polylinje i delene sine\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESTRETCH\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFlytte endene inne i et kryssingsvindu\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EAR\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EARRAY\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EKopiere i et rutenett eller en sirkel\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EJ\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EJOIN\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESlå sammen linjer og buer til ett objekt\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EBR\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EBREAK\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDele et objekt i to, eller ta ut et stykke\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"m-ltilsetting\"\u003EMåltilsetting\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESkriv\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKommando\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EHva den gjør\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDLI\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMLINEAR\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMåltilsette horisontalt eller vertikalt\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDAL\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMALIGNED\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMåltilsette langs en skrå linje\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDRA\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMRADIUS\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMåltilsette en radius\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDAN\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMANGULAR\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMåltilsette en vinkel\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"blokker-og-layer\"\u003EBlokker og layer\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESkriv\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKommando\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EHva den gjør\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EB\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EBLOCK\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELage en blokk av objekter\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EI\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EINSERT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlassere en blokk\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ELA\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYER\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EÅpne layerne\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"visning-og-foresp-rsler\"\u003EVisning og forespørsler\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESkriv\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKommando\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EHva den gjør\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EZ\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EZOOM\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoome inn, ut eller til alt (E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDI\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIST\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMåle en avstand og en vinkel\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ELI\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELIST\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EVise detaljene til et objekt\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EMA\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMATCHPROP\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EKopiere utseendet til ett objekt over på andre\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"fil\"\u003EFil\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESkriv\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKommando\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EHva den gjør\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EU\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EUNDO\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETa tilbake siste kommando\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESAVE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESAVE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELagre tegningen\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPLOT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPLOT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlotte til PDF\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"taster\"\u003ETaster\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECtrl (Cmd på en Mac): Z angre · Y gjør om · S lagre · P plott.  F3 objektfang · F8 ortho · F10 polar.  Space eller Enter gjentar forrige kommando · hjulet zoomer · å dra hjulet panorerer\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENy i tegning? Skriv \u003Ccode\u003ETOUR\u003C/code\u003E for en veiledet første tegning, les \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/getting-started\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/getting-started\"\u003EKom i gang\u003C/a\u003E, eller se alle kommandoer i \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/commands\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/commands\"\u003EKommandoer\u003C/a\u003E. Hvis du kommer fra annen tegneprogramvare: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/coming-from\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/coming-from\"\u003EHvis du kommer fra annen tegneprogramvare\u003C/a\u003E og \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/command-map\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/command-map\"\u003Ekommandokartet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Skriv kortnavnet (aliaset) og trykk Enter, eller klikk verktøyet. Esc avbryter. Enter på en tom linje gjentar forrige kommando. Skriv den ut: cheat-sheet.pdf (én A4-side; på Letter-papir skriver du den ut med «tilpass til side»). Tegn Skriv Kommando Hva den gjør L LINE Tegne rette linjer PL PLINE Tegne en sammenhengende kjede av linjer og buer C CIRCLE Tegne en sirkel (sentrum og radius) A ARC Tegne en bue REC RECTANG Tegne et rektangel fra to hjørner POL POLYGON Tegne en regulær mangekant EL ELLIPSE Tegne en ellipse DT TEXT Skrive én tekstlinje T MTEXT Skrive tekst som brytes (avsnitt) H HATCH Fylle et område med et mønster Endre Skriv Kommando Hva den gjør M MOVE Flytte objekter CO COPY Kopiere objekter RO ROTATE Dreie objekter rundt et punkt SC SCALE Gjøre objekter større eller mindre MI MIRROR Speile objekter om en linje O OFFSET Tegne en parallell kopi i en avstand TR TRIM Kutte objekter tilbake til der andre krysser dem EX EXTEND Forlenge objekter fram til andre F FILLET Runde av et hjørne med en bue CHA CHAMFER Kutte et hjørne med en rett kant E ERASE Slette objekter X EXPLODE Bryte en blokk eller polylinje i delene sine S STRETCH Flytte endene inne i et kryssingsvindu AR ARRAY Kopiere i et rutenett eller en sirkel J JOIN Slå sammen linjer og buer til ett objekt BR BREAK Dele et objekt i to, eller ta ut et stykke Måltilsetting Skriv Kommando Hva den gjør DLI DIMLINEAR Måltilsette horisontalt eller vertikalt DAL DIMALIGNED Måltilsette langs en skrå linje DRA DIMRADIUS Måltilsette en radius DAN DIMANGULAR Måltilsette en vinkel Blokker og layer Skriv Kommando Hva den gjør B BLOCK Lage en blokk av objekter I INSERT Plassere en blokk LA LAYER Åpne layerne Visning og forespørsler Skriv Kommando Hva den gjør Z ZOOM Zoome inn, ut eller til alt (E) DI DIST Måle en avstand og en vinkel LI LIST Vise detaljene til et objekt MA MATCHPROP Kopiere utseendet til ett objekt over på andre Fil Skriv Kommando Hva den gjør U UNDO Ta tilbake siste kommando SAVE SAVE Lagre tegningen PLOT PLOT Plotte til PDF Taster Ctrl (Cmd på en Mac): Z angre · Y gjør om · S lagre · P plott. F3 objektfang · F8 ortho · F10 polar. Space eller Enter gjentar forrige kommando · hjulet zoomer · å dra hjulet panorerer Ny i tegning? Skriv TOUR for en veiledet første tegning, les Kom i gang, eller se alle kommandoer i Kommandoer. Hvis du kommer fra annen tegneprogramvare: Hvis du kommer fra annen tegneprogramvare og kommandokartet.","lang":"nb"},{"slug":"learn-from-a-sample","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Lær av en eksempeltegning","summary":"Åpne CadCrafts eksempelhus eller eksempel som bygget og gå gjennom arkene, tegningshodet, måltilsettingene og layerne.","section":"start","order":15,"keywords":["eksempel","eksempler","eksempeltegning","lære","veiledning","gjennomgang","tegningshode","ark","som bygget","arkitektsett","åpne et eksempel"],"headings":[{"id":"dette-l-rer-du","text":"Dette lærer du","level":2},{"id":"pne-et-eksempel","text":"Åpne et eksempel","level":2},{"id":"se-deg-rundt-i-modellen","text":"Se deg rundt i modellen","level":2},{"id":"arkene","text":"Arkene","level":2},{"id":"tegningshodet","text":"Tegningshodet","level":2},{"id":"som-bygget","text":"Som bygget","level":2},{"id":"neste","text":"Neste","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"dette-l-rer-du\"\u003EDette lærer du\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHvordan et ferdig sett er bygget: modellrom, ark med visningsområder i en målestokk, et tegningshode med attributter, måltilsettinger i fot og tommer og i millimeter, layer. Eksemplene er CadCrafts egne og kan brukes fritt til hva som helst (CC0).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"pne-et-eksempel\"\u003EÅpne et eksempel\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EI panelet til venstre, under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETegning\u003C/span\u003E, klikker du \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EÅpne et eksempel\u003C/span\u003E (eller skriver \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESAMPLES\u003C/code\u003E; i bånd-stilene finner du det i menyen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFil\u003C/span\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EKlikk \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EÅpne\u003C/span\u003E ved siden av \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEksempelhus\u003C/span\u003E. Det åpnes i en egen fane, så arbeidet ditt blir åpent.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"se-deg-rundt-i-modellen\"\u003ESe deg rundt i modellen\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EKlikk \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUtstrekning\u003C/span\u003E (eller skriv \u003Ccode\u003EZOOM\u003C/code\u003E, deretter \u003Ccode\u003EE\u003C/code\u003E): planen, frontfasaden, et snitt og en veggdetalj.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EI \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayer\u003C/span\u003E klikker du øyet ved siden av \u003Ccode\u003EA-ANNO-DIMS\u003C/code\u003E for å skjule måltilsettingene, og igjen for å vise dem.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EKlikk en dør: Egenskaper viser en blokkreferanse til \u003Ccode\u003EDOOR-32\u003C/code\u003E. Dører og vinduer er blokker, plassert dreid og speilet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"arkene\"\u003EArkene\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EKlikk \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EA-101 FLOOR PLAN\u003C/span\u003E under tegningen: et ARCH D-ark med ett visningsområde i 1/4&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDobbeltklikk inne i visningsområdet for å arbeide på modellen gjennom det; dobbeltklikk utenfor for å komme tilbake.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EA-501 DETAILS\u003C/span\u003E viser veggdetaljen i 1 1/2&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;: samme modell, en annen målestokk.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"tegningshodet\"\u003ETegningshodet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPå et hvilket som helst ark dobbeltklikker du tegningshodet: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERediger attributter\u003C/span\u003E lister PROJECT, TITLE, SCALE, DATE, DRAWN og SHEET.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEndre DRAWN til initialene dine og klikk \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOK\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFor å endre selve tegningshodet på alle ark velger du det og velger \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBlokker\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMer\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERediger på stedet\u003C/span\u003E. Se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/attributes\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/attributes\"\u003EAttributter\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"som-bygget\"\u003ESom bygget\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EÅpne \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEksempel som bygget\u003C/span\u003E på samme måte. De grå prikkene på \u003Ccode\u003ESCAN-SLICE\u003C/code\u003E er et snitt av en skanning; veggene på \u003Ccode\u003EAB-WALL\u003C/code\u003E ble tracet over dem. Østveggen er 35 mm ute av vinkel: måltilsettingene sier det to ganger, i millimeter (\u003Ccode\u003EAB-DIMS-MM\u003C/code\u003E) og i fot og tommer (\u003Ccode\u003EAB-DIMS-FTIN\u003C/code\u003E, de samme lengdene gjennom en målestokk). De røde skyene på \u003Ccode\u003EREV-1\u003C/code\u003E markerer det oppmålingen fant.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"neste\"\u003ENeste\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/change-dimension-text-size\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/change-dimension-text-size\"\u003EEndre størrelsen på måltilsettingstekst\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/plot-pdf\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/plot-pdf\"\u003EPlott til PDF\u003C/a\u003E: plott \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EA-101 FLOOR PLAN\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Dette lærer du Hvordan et ferdig sett er bygget: modellrom, ark med visningsområder i en målestokk, et tegningshode med attributter, måltilsettinger i fot og tommer og i millimeter, layer. Eksemplene er CadCrafts egne og kan brukes fritt til hva som helst (CC0). Åpne et eksempel I panelet til venstre, under Tegning, klikker du Åpne et eksempel (eller skriver SAMPLES; i bånd-stilene finner du det i menyen Fil). Klikk Åpne ved siden av Eksempelhus. Det åpnes i en egen fane, så arbeidet ditt blir åpent. Se deg rundt i modellen Klikk Utstrekning (eller skriv ZOOM, deretter E): planen, frontfasaden, et snitt og en veggdetalj. I Layer klikker du øyet ved siden av A-ANNO-DIMS for å skjule måltilsettingene, og igjen for å vise dem. Klikk en dør: Egenskaper viser en blokkreferanse til DOOR-32. Dører og vinduer er blokker, plassert dreid og speilet. Arkene Klikk A-101 FLOOR PLAN under tegningen: et ARCH D-ark med ett visningsområde i 1/4\" = 1'-0\". Dobbeltklikk inne i visningsområdet for å arbeide på modellen gjennom det; dobbeltklikk utenfor for å komme tilbake. A-501 DETAILS viser veggdetaljen i 1 1/2\" = 1'-0\": samme modell, en annen målestokk. Tegningshodet På et hvilket som helst ark dobbeltklikker du tegningshodet: Rediger attributter lister PROJECT, TITLE, SCALE, DATE, DRAWN og SHEET. Endre DRAWN til initialene dine og klikk OK. For å endre selve tegningshodet på alle ark velger du det og velger Blokker › Mer › Rediger på stedet. Se Attributter. Som bygget Åpne Eksempel som bygget på samme måte. De grå prikkene på SCAN-SLICE er et snitt av en skanning; veggene på AB-WALL ble tracet over dem. Østveggen er 35 mm ute av vinkel: måltilsettingene sier det to ganger, i millimeter (AB-DIMS-MM) og i fot og tommer (AB-DIMS-FTIN, de samme lengdene gjennom en målestokk). De røde skyene på REV-1 markerer det oppmålingen fant. Neste Endre størrelsen på måltilsettingstekst Plott til PDF: plott A-101 FLOOR PLAN.","lang":"nb"},{"slug":"workspace","scope":"cadcraft","title":"CadCraft-skjermen","summary":"En omvisning i hver linje, hvert panel og hver fane i tegneappen, med navnet den har på skjermen.","section":"start","order":20,"keywords":["skjerm","grensesnitt","verktøylinje","bånd","paneler","kommandolinje","statuslinje","faner","layoutfaner","egenskaper","layerpanel","omvisning"],"headings":[{"id":"appfeltet","text":"Appfeltet","level":2},{"id":"verkt-ylinjen","text":"Verktøylinjen","level":2},{"id":"panelet-til-venstre","text":"Panelet til venstre","level":2},{"id":"tegneomr-det","text":"Tegneområdet","level":2},{"id":"fanene-under-tegningen","text":"Fanene under tegningen","level":2},{"id":"panelet-til-h-yre-egenskaper","text":"Panelet til høyre: Egenskaper","level":2},{"id":"kommandolinjen","text":"Kommandolinjen","level":2},{"id":"statuslinjen","text":"Statuslinjen","level":2},{"id":"dialogen-plott-til-pdf","text":"Dialogen Plott til PDF","level":2},{"id":"hjelpen","text":"Hjelpen","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ESkjermen har sju deler. Ovenfra og ned: appfeltet, verktøylinjen, tegneområdet med et panel på hver side, fanene under tegningen, kommandolinjen og statuslinjen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"appfeltet\"\u003EAppfeltet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDen tynne linjen helt øverst tilhører nettstedet, ikke tegningen. Den viser CadCraft-merket, appnavnet, hvilken versjon du kjører, en lenke til appens side, en knapp \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHjelp\u003C/span\u003E (eller \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), en knapp \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELagre for frakoblet bruk\u003C/span\u003E, en knapp \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFullskjerm\u003C/span\u003E og to temaknapper, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPapir\u003C/span\u003E og \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModell\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EKnappen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInnstillinger\u003C/span\u003E (tannhjul) har CadCrafts egne innstillinger og de som alle apper deler: se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/settings\"\u003ECadCraft-innstillinger\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELagre for frakoblet bruk\u003C/span\u003E beholder en kopi av CadCraft, med DWG-leseren, i denne nettleseren, slik at den åpnes uten forbindelse. Etiketten viser tilstanden: «Lagrer N av M», «Klar frakoblet», «Kunne ikke lagre» eller «Frakoblet bruk er ikke tilgjengelig». Se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/offline\" data-topic=\"basics/offline\"\u003EArbeide frakoblet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EKnappene \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPapir\u003C/span\u003E og \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModell\u003C/span\u003E i appfeltet endrer bare fargene på skjermen: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPapir\u003C/span\u003E er et lyst tema, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModell\u003C/span\u003E et mørkt. De har ingenting å gjøre med fanen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModell\u003C/span\u003E under tegningen, som bytter mellom tegningen og arkene.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EKnappen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFullskjerm\u003C/span\u003E, eller \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (Control-tasten på alle datamaskiner, også Mac), bytter mellom tre skjermmoduser: normal, fullskjerm og fullskjerm med linjene skjult. Med linjene skjult flytter du pekeren til toppkanten av skjermen for å få dem tilbake.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"verkt-ylinjen\"\u003EVerktøylinjen\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnder appfeltet står verktøylinjen. Hver knapp kjører en kommando, akkurat som om du hadde skrevet den, så kommandolinjen stiller de samme spørsmålene begge veier. Hold pekeren over en knapp for å se kommandoaliaset. Gruppene er:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EGruppe\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKnapper\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFil\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENy\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EÅpne\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELagre\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELagre som\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlott\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETegn\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinje\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPolylinje\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESirkel\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBue\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERektangel\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMangekant\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEllipse\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETekst\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAvsnitt\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESkravur\u003C/span\u003E, og \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMer\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPunkt\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKonstruksjonslinje\u003C/span\u003E og \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStråle\u003C/span\u003E (se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/construction-lines\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/construction-lines\"\u003EKonstruksjonslinjer og stråler\u003C/a\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEndre\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFlytt\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKopier\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERoter\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESkaler\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpeil\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStrekk\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EForskyv\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETrim\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EForleng\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAvrund\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFas\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEksploder\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESlett\u003C/span\u003E, og \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMer\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMatrise\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESlå sammen\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMatch egenskaper\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBryt\u003C/span\u003E og \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EForleng\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMerknad\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELineært\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInnrettet\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EVinkel\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERadius\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDiameter\u003C/span\u003E (mål), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHjelpelinje\u003C/span\u003E (flerhjelpelinje), og \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMer\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFortsett måltilsetting\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGrunnlinjemål\u003C/span\u003E og \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERevisjonssky\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EParametrisk\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAuto\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBetingelse\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMåltilsetting\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EParametere\u003C/span\u003E, og \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMer\u003C/span\u003E med hver betingelse (se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/constraints\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/constraints\"\u003ESett betingelser på en tegning\u003C/a\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBlokker\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELag\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESett inn\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBibliotek\u003C/span\u003E, og \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMer\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERediger blokk\u003C/span\u003E og \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDefiner attributt\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArk\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENytt ark\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EVisningsområde\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERediger\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAngre\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGjør om\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKopier\u003C/span\u003E (til utklippstavlen), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELim inn\u003C/span\u003E, og \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMer\u003C/span\u003E: en meny med \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKlipp ut\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKopier\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELim inn\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELim inn på samme sted\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDupliser og flytt\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESlett\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EVelg alle\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFjern utvalg\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHurtigvalg\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EVelg lignende\u003C/span\u003E og \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFinn og erstatt\u003C/span\u003E, hver med sin tast\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EVisning\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUtstrekning\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EVindu\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EForrige\u003C/span\u003E, og \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMer\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMål avstand\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAreal\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMål (radius, vinkel …)\u003C/span\u003E, og delt skjerm: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDel side ved side\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDel over hverandre\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFire ruter\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EÉn rute\u003C/span\u003E (se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/split-screen\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/split-screen\"\u003EDel skjermen\u003C/a\u003E), og \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrei visningen 90° til venstre\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrei visningen 90° til høyre\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETilbake til verden\u003C/span\u003E (se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/ucs-and-turned-view\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/ucs-and-turned-view\"\u003EDrei visningen\u003C/a\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKopier\u003C/span\u003E i gruppen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEndre\u003C/span\u003E er kommandoen \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECOPY\u003C/code\u003E (basispunkt, deretter hvor hver kopi går); \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKopier\u003C/span\u003E i gruppen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERediger\u003C/span\u003E legger objekter på utklippstavlen for \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELim inn\u003C/span\u003E. Se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/copy-paste\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/copy-paste\"\u003EKopier, lim inn og dupliser\u003C/a\u003E. Knappen til kommandoen som kjører, forblir uthevet til kommandoen er ferdig. Verktøytipsene nevner Windows-snarveiene («Ctrl+O»); på en Mac fungerer \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E også.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EI et lavt vindu (for eksempel en bærbar med skjermen skalert til 125 % eller mer) tar verktøylinjen aldri tegningens plass: når tegningen ville få for lite høyde, mister knappene ordene under ikonene og gruppenavnene (hold pekeren over en knapp for navnet), og hvis det fortsatt ikke er nok, blir verktøylinjen én rad som ruller sidelengs. Et høyere vindu henter ordene tilbake.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"panelet-til-venstre\"\u003EPanelet til venstre\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPanelet til venstre har tre deler.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayer\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/strong\u003E Hvert layer i tegningen, med en fylt prikk på det aktive. Klikk et navn for å gjøre det layeret aktivt. Hver rad har en fargeprøve (klikk for å velge en farge) og fire knapper: på/av (øye), frys (snøflake), lås (hengelås) og plott (skriver). \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENytt layer\u003C/span\u003E åpner en boks for å legge til ett. Se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/layers\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/layers\"\u003EOrganiser en tegning med layer\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBlokker\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/strong\u003E Blokkene som er definert i tegningen, hvor mange ganger hver er brukt, og en knapp \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESett inn\u003C/span\u003E for hver. Se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/blocks\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/blocks\"\u003ELag og sett inn blokker\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETegning\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/strong\u003E Fakta om den åpne filen: navn (med «(endret)» når det finnes ulagrede endringer), format, størrelse, enheter, antall objekter, layer, blokker og ark. Etter at du har åpnet en fil, lister en boks «Om denne filen» alt CadCraft ikke kunne vise eller måtte konvertere.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"tegneomr-det\"\u003ETegneområdet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMidten av skjermen er tegningen. Pekeren er et siktekors. Når CadCraft vil ha et punkt, viser en liten markør og en etikett («Endepunkt», «Midtpunkt») hvor en objektfang vil lande. Når den vil ha objekter, har siktekorset en velgerboks, og objektene under lyser opp.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENår tegningen er tom, viser et kort «Begynn å tegne» noen første kommandoer. Det forsvinner så snart du kjører en kommando eller åpner en fil. Du kan slippe en DWG- eller DXF-fil hvor som helst på dette området for å åpne den.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"fanene-under-tegningen\"\u003EFanene under tegningen\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnder tegningen står fanene: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModell\u003C/span\u003E først, deretter \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E, deretter én fane per ark (layout), og så en knapp \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E+\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModell\u003C/span\u003E er selve tegningen, i full størrelse, i tegneenheter.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E viser tegningen liggende på bakken og volumlegemene laget av den. Mens den vises, har verktøylinjen en gruppe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E i stedet for tegnegruppene. Se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/3d-solids\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/3d-solids\"\u003EModeller i 3D\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHver arkfane er et ark med et tegningshode og visningsområder mot modellen. Det du tegner der, er i arkenheter (millimeter eller tommer). Dobbeltklikk inne i et visningsområde for å arbeide på modellen gjennom det (se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/sheets-and-viewports#work-on-the-model-through-a-viewport\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/sheets-and-viewports\" data-hash=\"work-on-the-model-through-a-viewport\"\u003EArbeid på modellen gjennom et visningsområde\u003C/a\u003E). Et langt arknavn kuttes på fanen; hold pekeren over fanen for hele navnet. Med mange ark viser \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInnstillinger\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArkfaner\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBare arknumre\u003C/span\u003E bare hvert arks nummer (A-101) på fanen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E+\u003C/span\u003E lager et nytt ark med ramme, tegningshode og ett visningsområde. Det er det samme som kommandoen \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYOUT\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDobbeltklikk en arkfane for å gi den nytt navn. Med en fane i fokus flytter \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"←\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E og \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"→\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E mellom fanene.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHver fane beholder sin egen zoom og panorering. Se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/sheets-and-viewports\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/sheets-and-viewports\"\u003ESett opp et ark\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"panelet-til-h-yre-egenskaper\"\u003EPanelet til høyre: Egenskaper\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPanelet til høyre endres med det som er valgt.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIngenting valgt, på fanen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModell\u003C/span\u003E:\u003C/strong\u003E overskriften er \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGjeldende innstillinger\u003C/span\u003E. Her setter du \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayer\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFarge\u003C/span\u003E og \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETeksthøyde\u003C/span\u003E for nye objekter, tegningens \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEnheter\u003C/span\u003E og størrelsene for nye måltilsettinger (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETeksthøyde\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPilstørrelse\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDesimaler\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESamlet skala\u003C/span\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIngenting valgt, på et ark:\u003C/strong\u003E overskriften er «Ark:» og arknavnet. Du kan gi arket nytt navn, endre \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPapir\u003C/span\u003E og \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrientering\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlott dette arket\u003C/span\u003E eller \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESlett dette arket\u003C/span\u003E. Innstillingene for nye objekter følger under.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEtt objekt valgt:\u003C/strong\u003E typen som overskrift (Linje, Sirkel, Tekst, Måltilsetting, Visningsområde …), deretter layer, farge og geometri. Skriv en ny verdi og trykk \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for å endre den.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFlere objekter valgt:\u003C/strong\u003E hvor mange, og felles \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayer\u003C/span\u003E og \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFarge\u003C/span\u003E. Å endre en av dem endrer alle. «Varierer» betyr at de er ulike.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESe \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/grips-and-properties\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/grips-and-properties\"\u003ERediger med håndtak og Egenskaper\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnder det åpnes panelet \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EParametere\u003C/span\u003E når du velger \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EParametere\u003C/span\u003E eller skriver \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPARAMETERS\u003C/code\u003E: alle parametere og målbetingelser i én tabell. Se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/parameters\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/parameters\"\u003EBruk parametere og ligninger\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"kommandolinjen\"\u003EKommandolinjen\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENederst er kommandolinjen: historikken over det du gjorde, og under den spørsmålslinjen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESpørsmålsetiketten er \u003Ccode\u003EKommando:\u003C/code\u003E når ingenting kjører. Mens en kommando kjører, viser den spørsmålet, alternativene i hakeparenteser og standardverdien i vinkelparenteser, for eksempel \u003Ccode\u003EAngi sirkelens radius [Diameter] &lt;500&gt;:\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMens du skriver et kommandonavn, foreslår et hint til høyre kommandoer som passer. Trykk \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for å godta den første. «Ingen slik kommando» betyr at ingenting passer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↑\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E og \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↓\"\u003E↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E henter tilbake linjer du skrev tidligere.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHistorikken beholder de siste 400 linjene. Meldinger fra kommandoer, målinger fra \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIST\u003C/code\u003E og \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELIST\u003C/code\u003E, og lagrings- og plottrapporter vises her.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"statuslinjen\"\u003EStatuslinjen\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDen nederste raden er statuslinjen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EKoordinater\u003C/strong\u003E til venstre: X og Y for siktekorset. Mens en kommando har et basispunkt, viser den også avstanden og vinkelen fra det, for eksempel \u003Ccode\u003EΔ 1200 &lt; 90°\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMeldinger\u003C/strong\u003E i midten, som «Ortho på» eller «Aktivt layer: Walls». De toner bort etter noen sekunder.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERutenett\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrtho\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPolar\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFang\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpor\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDyn\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/strong\u003E og \u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELWT\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/strong\u003E til høyre. Hver er en bryter; en nedtrykt knapp er på. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELWT\u003C/span\u003E viser linjevekter på skjermen (se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/units-papers-scales#line-weights-and-linetypes\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/units-papers-scales\" data-hash=\"line-weights-and-linetypes\"\u003ELinjevekter og linjetyper\u003C/a\u003E). Den lille pilen ved siden av \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFang\u003C/span\u003E åpner \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EObjektfang\u003C/span\u003E, der du krysser av hvilke fanger som er aktive. Se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/object-snaps\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/object-snaps\"\u003EFang til punkter på objekter\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"dialogen-plott-til-pdf\"\u003EDialogen Plott til PDF\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlott\u003C/span\u003E (eller \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command P\"\u003E⌘P\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+P\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) åpner dialogen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlott til PDF\u003C/span\u003E, med innstillingene til venstre og en levende forhåndsvisning til høyre. Se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/plot-pdf\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/plot-pdf\"\u003EPlott til PDF\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"hjelpen\"\u003EHjelpen\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E eller knappen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHjelp\u003C/span\u003E åpner denne hjelpen som et sidepanel; tegningen kan fortsatt brukes ved siden av. Den fungerer uten forbindelse. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E igjen, eller \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E i panelet, lukker den. Se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/help\" data-topic=\"basics/help\"\u003EBruke denne hjelpen\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Skjermen har sju deler. Ovenfra og ned: appfeltet, verktøylinjen, tegneområdet med et panel på hver side, fanene under tegningen, kommandolinjen og statuslinjen. Appfeltet Den tynne linjen helt øverst tilhører nettstedet, ikke tegningen. Den viser CadCraft-merket, appnavnet, hvilken versjon du kjører, en lenke til appens side, en knapp Hjelp (eller F1), en knapp Lagre for frakoblet bruk, en knapp Fullskjerm og to temaknapper, Papir og Modell. Knappen Innstillinger (tannhjul) har CadCrafts egne innstillinger og de som alle apper deler: se CadCraft-innstillinger. Lagre for frakoblet bruk beholder en kopi av CadCraft, med DWG-leseren, i denne nettleseren, slik at den åpnes uten forbindelse. Etiketten viser tilstanden: «Lagrer N av M», «Klar frakoblet», «Kunne ikke lagre» eller «Frakoblet bruk er ikke tilgjengelig». Se Arbeide frakoblet. Knappene Papir og Modell i appfeltet endrer bare fargene på skjermen: Papir er et lyst tema, Modell et mørkt. De har ingenting å gjøre med fanen Modell under tegningen, som bytter mellom tegningen og arkene. Knappen Fullskjerm, eller Ctrl+Shift+F (Control-tasten på alle datamaskiner, også Mac), bytter mellom tre skjermmoduser: normal, fullskjerm og fullskjerm med linjene skjult. Med linjene skjult flytter du pekeren til toppkanten av skjermen for å få dem tilbake. Verktøylinjen Under appfeltet står verktøylinjen. Hver knapp kjører en kommando, akkurat som om du hadde skrevet den, så kommandolinjen stiller de samme spørsmålene begge veier. Hold pekeren over en knapp for å se kommandoaliaset. Gruppene er: Gruppe Knapper Fil Ny, Åpne, Lagre, Lagre som, Plott Tegn Linje, Polylinje, Sirkel, Bue, Rektangel, Mangekant, Ellipse, Tekst, Avsnitt, Skravur, og Mer: Punkt, Konstruksjonslinje og Stråle (se Konstruksjonslinjer og stråler) Endre Flytt, Kopier, Roter, Skaler, Speil, Strekk, Forskyv, Trim, Forleng, Avrund, Fas, Eksploder, Slett, og Mer: Matrise, Slå sammen, Match egenskaper, Bryt og Forleng Merknad Lineært, Innrettet, Vinkel, Radius, Diameter (mål), Hjelpelinje (flerhjelpelinje), og Mer: Fortsett måltilsetting, Grunnlinjemål og Revisjonssky Parametrisk Auto, Betingelse, Måltilsetting, Parametere, og Mer med hver betingelse (se Sett betingelser på en tegning) Blokker Lag, Sett inn, Bibliotek, og Mer: Rediger blokk og Definer attributt Ark Nytt ark, Visningsområde Rediger Angre, Gjør om, Kopier (til utklippstavlen), Lim inn, og Mer: en meny med Klipp ut, Kopier, Lim inn, Lim inn på samme sted, Dupliser og flytt, Slett, Velg alle, Fjern utvalg, Hurtigvalg, Velg lignende og Finn og erstatt, hver med sin tast Visning Utstrekning, Vindu, Forrige, og Mer: Mål avstand, Areal, Mål (radius, vinkel …), og delt skjerm: Del side ved side, Del over hverandre, Fire ruter, Én rute (se Del skjermen), og Drei visningen 90° til venstre, Drei visningen 90° til høyre, Tilbake til verden (se Drei visningen) Kopier i gruppen Endre er kommandoen COPY (basispunkt, deretter hvor hver kopi går); Kopier i gruppen Rediger legger objekter på utklippstavlen for Lim inn. Se Kopier, lim inn og dupliser. Knappen til kommandoen som kjører, forblir uthevet til kommandoen er ferdig. Verktøytipsene nevner Windows-snarveiene («Ctrl+O»); på en Mac fungerer Ctrl+O også. I et lavt vindu (for eksempel en bærbar med skjermen skalert til 125 % eller mer) tar verktøylinjen aldri tegningens plass: når tegningen ville få for lite høyde, mister knappene ordene under ikonene og gruppenavnene (hold pekeren over en knapp for navnet), og hvis det fortsatt ikke er nok, blir verktøylinjen én rad som ruller sidelengs. Et høyere vindu henter ordene tilbake. Panelet til venstre Panelet til venstre har tre deler. Layer. Hvert layer i tegningen, med en fylt prikk på det aktive. Klikk et navn for å gjøre det layeret aktivt. Hver rad har en fargeprøve (klikk for å velge en farge) og fire knapper: på/av (øye), frys (snøflake), lås (hengelås) og plott (skriver). Nytt layer åpner en boks for å legge til ett. Se Organiser en tegning med layer. Blokker. Blokkene som er definert i tegningen, hvor mange ganger hver er brukt, og en knapp Sett inn for hver. Se Lag og sett inn blokker. Tegning. Fakta om den åpne filen: navn (med «(endret)» når det finnes ulagrede endringer), format, størrelse, enheter, antall objekter, layer, blokker og ark. Etter at du har åpnet en fil, lister en boks «Om denne filen» alt CadCraft ikke kunne vise eller måtte konvertere. Tegneområdet Midten av skjermen er tegningen. Pekeren er et siktekors. Når CadCraft vil ha et punkt, viser en liten markør og en etikett («Endepunkt», «Midtpunkt») hvor en objektfang vil lande. Når den vil ha objekter, har siktekorset en velgerboks, og objektene under lyser opp. Når tegningen er tom, viser et kort «Begynn å tegne» noen første kommandoer. Det forsvinner så snart du kjører en kommando eller åpner en fil. Du kan slippe en DWG- eller DXF-fil hvor som helst på dette området for å åpne den. Fanene under tegningen Under tegningen står fanene: Modell først, deretter 3D, deretter én fane per ark (layout), og så en knapp +. Modell er selve tegningen, i full størrelse, i tegneenheter. 3D viser tegningen liggende på bakken og volumlegemene laget av den. Mens den vises, har verktøylinjen en gruppe 3D i stedet for tegnegruppene. Se Modeller i 3D. Hver arkfane er et ark med et tegningshode og visningsområder mot modellen. Det du tegner der, er i arkenheter (millimeter eller tommer). Dobbeltklikk inne i et visningsområde for å arbeide på modellen gjennom det (se Arbeid på modellen gjennom et visningsområde). Et langt arknavn kuttes på fanen; hold pekeren over fanen for hele navnet. Med mange ark viser Innstillinger › CadCraft › Arkfaner › Bare arknumre bare hvert arks nummer (A-101) på fanen. + lager et nytt ark med ramme, tegningshode og ett visningsområde. Det er det samme som kommandoen LAYOUT. Dobbeltklikk en arkfane for å gi den nytt navn. Med en fane i fokus flytter ← og → mellom fanene. Hver fane beholder sin egen zoom og panorering. Se Sett opp et ark. Panelet til høyre: Egenskaper Panelet til høyre endres med det som er valgt. Ingenting valgt, på fanen Modell: overskriften er Gjeldende innstillinger. Her setter du Layer, Farge og Teksthøyde for nye objekter, tegningens Enheter og størrelsene for nye måltilsettinger (Teksthøyde, Pilstørrelse, Desimaler, Samlet skala). Ingenting valgt, på et ark: overskriften er «Ark:» og arknavnet. Du kan gi arket nytt navn, endre Papir og Orientering, Plott dette arket eller Slett dette arket. Innstillingene for nye objekter følger under. Ett objekt valgt: typen som overskrift (Linje, Sirkel, Tekst, Måltilsetting, Visningsområde …), deretter layer, farge og geometri. Skriv en ny verdi og trykk Enter for å endre den. Flere objekter valgt: hvor mange, og felles Layer og Farge. Å endre en av dem endrer alle. «Varierer» betyr at de er ulike. Se Rediger med håndtak og Egenskaper. Under det åpnes panelet Parametere når du velger Parametere eller skriver PARAMETERS: alle parametere og målbetingelser i én tabell. Se Bruk parametere og ligninger. Kommandolinjen Nederst er kommandolinjen: historikken over det du gjorde, og under den spørsmålslinjen. Spørsmålsetiketten er Kommando: når ingenting kjører. Mens en kommando kjører, viser den spørsmålet, alternativene i hakeparenteser og standardverdien i vinkelparenteser, for eksempel Angi sirkelens radius [Diameter] \u003C500\u003E:. Mens du skriver et kommandonavn, foreslår et hint til høyre kommandoer som passer. Trykk Tab for å godta den første. «Ingen slik kommando» betyr at ingenting passer. ↑ og ↓ henter tilbake linjer du skrev tidligere. Historikken beholder de siste 400 linjene. Meldinger fra kommandoer, målinger fra DIST og LIST, og lagrings- og plottrapporter vises her. Statuslinjen Den nederste raden er statuslinjen. Koordinater til venstre: X og Y for siktekorset. Mens en kommando har et basispunkt, viser den også avstanden og vinkelen fra det, for eksempel Δ 1200 \u003C 90°. Meldinger i midten, som «Ortho på» eller «Aktivt layer: Walls». De toner bort etter noen sekunder. Rutenett, Ortho, Polar, Fang, Spor, Dyn og LWT til høyre. Hver er en bryter; en nedtrykt knapp er på. LWT viser linjevekter på skjermen (se Linjevekter og linjetyper). Den lille pilen ved siden av Fang åpner Objektfang, der du krysser av hvilke fanger som er aktive. Se Fang til punkter på objekter. Dialogen Plott til PDF Plott (eller Ctrl+P) åpner dialogen Plott til PDF, med innstillingene til venstre og en levende forhåndsvisning til høyre. Se Plott til PDF. Hjelpen F1 eller knappen Hjelp åpner denne hjelpen som et sidepanel; tegningen kan fortsatt brukes ved siden av. Den fungerer uten forbindelse. F1 igjen, eller Esc i panelet, lukker den. Se Bruke denne hjelpen.","lang":"nb"},{"slug":"files","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Filer: hva som åpnes, hva som lagres","summary":"Formatene CadCraft åpner, lagrer og eksporterer, hva som beholdes eller konverteres, og hvor filene havner.","section":"start","order":30,"keywords":["xclip","klipp","xclip-avgrensning","bind klippede","dwg","dxf","pdf","r12","åpne","lagre","eksporter","importer","formater","konvertering","last ned","saveas","dxfout","lagre som","dwg-lagring","format","qsave","xref","xattach","lenket tegning","ekstern referanse","bind","last på nytt","løsne"],"headings":[{"id":"med-et-blikk","text":"Med et blikk","level":2},{"id":"pne-en-tegning","text":"Åpne en tegning","level":2},{"id":"hva-som-kommer-inn","text":"Hva som kommer inn","level":3},{"id":"hva-som-ikke-kommer-inn-enn","text":"Hva som ikke kommer inn ennå","level":3},{"id":"lagring","text":"Lagring","level":2},{"id":"hva-en-dwg-beholder-og-endrer","text":"Hva en DWG beholder og endrer","level":2},{"id":"hva-en-dwg-beholder","text":"Hva en DWG beholder","level":3},{"id":"hva-en-dwg-endrer","text":"Hva en DWG endrer","level":3},{"id":"hvorfor-r12-og-hva-som-konverteres","text":"Hvorfor R12, og hva som konverteres","level":3},{"id":"lenkede-tegninger-eksterne-referanser","text":"Lenkede tegninger (eksterne referanser)","level":2},{"id":"pne-i-3d","text":"Åpne i 3D","level":2},{"id":"plotte-en-pdf","text":"Plotte en PDF","level":2},{"id":"hvor-filene-havner","text":"Hvor filene havner","level":2},{"id":"pln-og-pla-prosjektfiler","text":"PLN- og PLA-prosjektfiler","level":2},{"id":"hvis-siden-lukkes-f-r-du-lagrer","text":"Hvis siden lukkes før du lagrer","level":2},{"id":"tilkoblet-og-frakoblet","text":"Tilkoblet og frakoblet","level":2},{"id":"betingelser-i-filer","text":"Betingelser i filer","level":2},{"id":"ikke-tilgjengelig-enn","text":"Ikke tilgjengelig ennå","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"med-et-blikk\"\u003EMed et blikk\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFormat\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EÅpne\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ELagre\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMerknader\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDXF (tekst, alle versjoner)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EJa\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EJa, som DXF R12 eller DXF 2000\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBinær DXF avvises: lagre den som tekst-DXF først.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDWG\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EJa\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EJa, som DWG (AutoCAD 2000, 2004, 2007, 2010, 2013 eller 2018)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELeses og skrives av CadCrafts eget DWG-bibliotek (AutoCAD R13- til 2018-filer). DWG-en din blir aldri endret. Skadde filer, eller versjoner leseren ikke håndterer, avvises med en melding.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPDF\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHenter inn en side: som underlag eller som objekter (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/pdf-underlay\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/pdf-underlay\"\u003EPDF-underlag\u003C/a\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EJa, ved plotting\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EVektor-PDF av et ark, eller av modellen i en målestokk.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHver tegning du åpner (eller starter med \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENy\u003C/span\u003E) får sin egen fane over tegneområdet; en ny tegning ingen har rørt ennå, brukes i stedet for å legge til en fane. Klikk en fane for å bytte, dens \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E×\u003C/span\u003E for å lukke den (CadCraft spør først hvis det finnes ulagrede endringer), eller flis de åpne tegningene side ved side: se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/split-screen\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/split-screen\"\u003EDel skjermen\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"pne-en-tegning\"\u003EÅpne en tegning\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETrykk \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, klikk \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EÅpne\u003C/span\u003E, eller skriv \u003Ccode\u003EOPEN\u003C/code\u003E, og velg deretter filen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEller dra en \u003Ccode\u003E.dwg\u003C/code\u003E- eller \u003Ccode\u003E.dxf\u003C/code\u003E-fil til tegneområdet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFilen leses inne i nettleseren din. Den lastes ikke opp noe sted. Historikken melder hvor mange objekter og layer som kom inn og hvor lang tid det tok, og delen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETegning\u003C/span\u003E i panelet til venstre viser formatet, for eksempel «DWG 2018 (AC1032)».\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDWG-leseren er en del av CadCraft og hentes med den første DWG-en du åpner etter at siden er lastet (noen hundre KB), så det er ingen egen nedlasting å vente på. Den leser filen i bakgrunnen, så siden fortsetter å svare mens en stor DWG åpnes.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"hva-som-kommer-inn\"\u003EHva som kommer inn\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELinjer, buer, sirkler, polylinjer (med buesegmenter), ellipser, punkter, tekst og flerlinjetekst, volumer, skraveringer (standardmønstrene tegnet som sine linjer, andre som en flat tone), blokkreferanser med attributtene sine (verdier du kan redigere; se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/attributes\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/attributes\"\u003EBlokkattributter\u003C/a\u003E), måltilsettinger og layouter med visningsområdene sine.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESplines kommer inn som splines (grad, kontrollpunkter, knuter, vekter, tilpasningspunkter); se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/splines\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/splines\"\u003ESplines og polylinjer\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFlerhjelpelinjer med én hjelpelinje og tekst kommer inn som CadCraft-flerhjelpelinjer du kan redigere (se \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMLEADER\u003C/code\u003E). Andre (med en blokk, flere hjelpelinjer eller buede linjer) tegnes slik det andre programmet tegnet dem og flyttes som ett; historikken sier hvor mange.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EBilder vises som en ramme med bildets filnavn til du gir CadCraft filen med \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EIMAGE\u003C/code\u003E: se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/pictures#show-the-pictures-a-dwg-uses\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/pictures\" data-hash=\"show-the-pictures-a-dwg-uses\"\u003EBilder i en tegning\u003C/a\u003E. Maskeringer vises som rammen sin, og det som er under dem, forblir synlig.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETabeller i en DWG kommer inn slik det andre programmet tegnet dem.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E3D-flater i modellrommet kommer inn på fanen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E, ikke som 2D-objekter; det samme gjelder de inne i blokker, én gang per blokkreferanse i modellen. I en blokk og på et ark tegnes de også flate, som omriss. Se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/3d-solids\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/3d-solids\"\u003EModeller i 3D\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELayer beholder farge-, på-, frosset-, låst- og plottinnstillingene sine. DEFPOINTS plottes aldri.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMåltilsettinger blir ekte, redigerbare CadCraft-måltilsettinger der punktene deres kan leses. De vises først nøyaktig slik det andre programmet tegnet dem; så snart du endrer en, tegner CadCraft den på nytt i sin egen stil.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHver layout blir en arkfane. I det andre programmet har hver layout et skjult første visningsområde som er selve arket; CadCraft utelater det.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"hva-som-ikke-kommer-inn-enn\"\u003EHva som ikke kommer inn ennå\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAlt CadCraft ikke kan tegne, telles, ikke gjettes på. Boksen «Om denne filen» i panelet til venstre lister det som «Vises ikke ennå:» etterfulgt av hver objekttype og hvor mange. Typiske tilfeller er tabeller i en DXF, nett og 3D-volumer inne i blokker. 3D-volumer, regioner og legemer i modellrommet vises på 3D-fanen (se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/3d-solids#solids-from-other-programs\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/3d-solids\" data-hash=\"solids-from-other-programs\"\u003EModeller i 3D\u003C/a\u003E). Visningsområder som er vridd i filen, vises vridd som der, og beholder vridningen når du lagrer; det samme gjelder gjeldende BKS, de navngitte BKS-ene og en dreid visning.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETekst tegnes i én Helvetica-lignende font. En etikett som får plass på én linje i det opprinnelige programmets smalere font, kan brytes over to linjer i CadCraft.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"lagring\"\u003ELagring\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDet er tre måter å lagre på. Hver laster ned en kopi.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELagre\u003C/strong\u003E: trykk \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, klikk \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELagre\u003C/span\u003E, eller skriv \u003Ccode\u003ESAVE\u003C/code\u003E (også \u003Ccode\u003EQSAVE\u003C/code\u003E). Den lagrer i tegningens format: DWG hvis du åpnet en DWG, DXF hvis du åpnet en DXF. En DWG beholder versjonen den kom inn i (en 2018-fil lagres som 2018). En ny tegning bruker formatet og DWG-versjonen du sist valgte i Lagre som i denne nettleseren; hvis du aldri valgte noe, DWG i AutoCAD 2000-formatet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELagre som\u003C/strong\u003E: trykk \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command S\"\u003E⇧⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, klikk \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELagre som\u003C/span\u003E, eller skriv \u003Ccode\u003ESAVEAS\u003C/code\u003E. Dialogen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELagre en kopi som\u003C/span\u003E tilbyr \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDWG\u003C/span\u003E, med en liste \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EVersjon\u003C/span\u003E (AutoCAD 2018, 2013, 2010, 2007, 2004 eller 2000), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDXF R12\u003C/span\u003E og \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDXF 2000\u003C/span\u003E, hver med filnavnet den vil gi, og tegningens gjeldende format og versjon allerede valgt. Klikk \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELagre\u003C/span\u003E for å laste ned, eller \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAvbryt\u003C/span\u003E (eller \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) for å lagre ingenting; historikken sier da «Ikke lagret.» Det du velger, blir stående: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E bruker det for denne tegningen fra da av, og nye tegninger i denne nettleseren starter med det.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDXFOUT\u003C/strong\u003E: skriv \u003Ccode\u003EDXFOUT\u003C/code\u003E. Den laster alltid ned en DXF R12-kopi, og neste \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E lagrer fortsatt i tegningens eget format.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENettleseren laster ned \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;tegningsnavn&gt;.dwg\u003C/code\u003E eller \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;tegningsnavn&gt;.dxf\u003C/code\u003E. Hver lagring er en \u003Cstrong\u003Eny nedlasting\u003C/strong\u003E; filen du åpnet blir aldri overskrevet eller endret. Historikken bekrefter det, for eksempel «Lagret en kopi som Plan.dwg (DWG (AutoCAD 2018)). Originalen Plan.dxf er uendret.» For en DXF står det «(DXF R12, åpnes i alle CAD-programmer)». Linjene under lister alt som endret seg på veien.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENår CadCraft kjører i skrivebordsappen, fungerer lagring slik det gjør i andre skrivebordsprogrammer: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E skriver tegningen tilbake til filen du åpnet, i sitt eget format og sin egen versjon, og historikken sier hvor den ble lagret. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELagre som\u003C/span\u003E og eksportene viser systemets lagringsdialog, og en \u003Ccode\u003E.dwg\u003C/code\u003E-, \u003Ccode\u003E.dxf\u003C/code\u003E- eller \u003Ccode\u003E.ccsheets\u003C/code\u003E-fil du dobbeltklikker, åpnes i CadCraft.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor en PDF plotter du i stedet (se nedenfor).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"hva-en-dwg-beholder-og-endrer\"\u003EHva en DWG beholder og endrer\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECadCraft skriver DWG-en selv, inne i nettleseren din, med det samme DWG-biblioteket den leser med. Velg versjonen i Lagre som: AutoCAD 2000-formatet (filversjon AC1015) åpnes i flest programmer, også gamle; de nyere formatene (2004, AC1018, opp til 2018, AC1032) beholder også ekte farger. Ingenting lastes opp, og å lagre en DWG fungerer frakoblet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHver versjon er kontrollert ved å lese de lagrede filene tilbake med tre andre programmers DWG-lesere og med CadCraft selv. En CadCraft-DWG er ikke åpnet i AutoCAD selv ennå.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"hva-en-dwg-beholder\"\u003EHva en DWG beholder\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELinjer, buer, sirkler, ellipser, punkter og polylinjer med buesegmentene sine.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEnlinjetekst med justering og breddefaktor.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFlerlinjetekst og brutt tekst, som flerlinjetekst. Det andre programmet bryter den på nytt fra samme bredde.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EObjektfarger: etter layer, etter blokk, de 255 standardfargene, og ekte farger i 2004-formatet og senere.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELayer med farge, linjetype, linjevekt og på-, frosset-, låst- og plottinnstillingene sine; objektenes egne linjetyper, linjevekter og linjetypeskalaer, linjetypemønstrene og tegningens linjetypeskala.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETegneenhetene og lengdeformatet (desimal, fot og tommer, brøker …).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EBlokker med basispunktet og attributtdefinisjonene sine, også usynlige, konstante og forhåndsinnstilte.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EBlokkreferanser med skala, rotasjon og attributtverdier.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHeldekkende fyll og mønsterskraveringer med mønster, skala og vinkel (mønsterets egne linjer, så andre programmer tegner de samme linjene som CadCraft).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMåltilsettinger, som ekte måltilsettinger: andre programmer måler dem på nytt når de strekkes. Hver beholder sine egne innstillinger (teksthøyde, pilstørrelse, desimaler, lengdeformat) og teksten sin, og har den tegnede kopien hver DWG-måltilsetting har.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EArk (layouter): hvert ark med papirstørrelse og enheter, det som er tegnet på det, og visningsområdene med visning og skala.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENavngitte visninger.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFlerhjelpelinjer, som flerhjelpelinjer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELayer- og blokknavn slik du skrev dem, med store og små bokstaver. (DXF R12 skriver dem med store bokstaver.)\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"hva-en-dwg-endrer\"\u003EHva en DWG endrer\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHver av disse listes i historikken etter at du har lagret, når det gjelder tegningen din.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EI CadCraft\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EI den lagrede DWG-en\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEkte farger (valgt med fargevelgeren), i 2000-formatet\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENærmeste standardfarge: 2000-formatet har bare de 255. Lagre som 2004 eller senere for å beholde dem\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENavngitte visninger på et ark\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPapirromvisninger: en DWG sier ikke hvilket ark, så når CadCraft åpner filen igjen, tilhører de første ark\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFlerlinjetekst med breddefaktor\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENormal bokstavbredde: i en DWG gjelder breddefaktoren bare enlinjetekst\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELayer- eller blokknavn med tegn DWG ikke tillater\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEndret: et navn fra en lenket tegning som \u003Ccode\u003EBase|Walls\u003C/code\u003E blir \u003Ccode\u003EBase$0$Walls\u003C/code\u003E (slik binding av en lenket tegning navngir det), og andre tegn blir \u003Ccode\u003E_\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"hvorfor-r12-og-hva-som-konverteres\"\u003EHvorfor R12, og hva som konverteres\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENår du lagrer som DXF, skriver CadCraft DXF R12, versjonen alle CAD-programmer kan åpne. Den er eldre enn noen funksjoner, så noen ting skrives i en enklere form:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EI CadCraft\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EI den lagrede R12-filen\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFlerlinjetekst\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEtt tekstobjekt per linje\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEllipser\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPolylinjer som følger ellipsen\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESkraveringer (fra åpnede filer)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBare omrissene\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEkte farger (valgt med fargevelgeren)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENærmeste standardfarge\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinjevekter, og objektenes egne linjetypeskalaer\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUtelatt: R12 har ingen av dem (linjetyper beholdes)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELayer- og blokknavn\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESkrevet med store bokstaver; mellomrom og andre tegn blir \u003Ccode\u003E_\u003C/code\u003E, og navn kuttes til 31 tegn («Title block» blir \u003Ccode\u003ETITLE_BLOCK\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELayer satt til ikke å plottes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAndre programmer vil plotte dem (CadCraft husker innstillingen når tegningen har et ark)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMåltilsettinger\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEkte måltilsettinger med sin egen tegnede blokk, så andre programmer viser dem slik du ser dem\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFlerhjelpelinjer\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinjene, pilspissen og teksten de tegner\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENavngitte visninger\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBeholdt, med navn med store bokstaver; et arks visninger kommer tilbake på det første arket\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFlere ark\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDet første arket er papirrommet alle programmer viser; de andre beholdes som blokker kalt \u003Ccode\u003ECCLAYOUT_1\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ECCLAYOUT_2\u003C/code\u003E … som CadCraft gjør om til ark igjen når den åpner filen\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHistorikken lister hver av disse som gjaldt tegningen din.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor å fortsette å arbeide på en tegning over flere dager lagrer du som DXF og åpner den DXF-en neste gang. Alt CadCraft kan tegne, overlever rundturen, også måltilsettinger, blokker og alle ark.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"lenkede-tegninger-eksterne-referanser\"\u003ELenkede tegninger (eksterne referanser)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EXATTACH\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EXA\u003C/code\u003E, eller \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBlokker\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMer\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKoble en tegning (xref)\u003C/span\u003E) lenker en annen DWG eller DXF: velg filen, og klikk deretter hvor den skal stå (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E legger den på 0,0). Den kommer inn som én blokk oppkalt etter filen, i riktig skala når de to tegningene har ulike enheter. Layerne og blokkene beholder navnene sine med lenkens navn foran — \u003Ccode\u003EPlan|Walls\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EPlan|Door\u003C/code\u003E — så de aldri blandes med dine: slå de lenkede layerne av og på, frys eller lås dem som dine egne. En lenke er skrivebeskyttet: endre den andre tegningen og last den på nytt. Det du setter på en lenkes layer (farge, linjetype, linjevekt, på/av, frys, lås, plott), beholdes når du laster den på nytt, når du lagrer tegningen som DWG mens lenken fortsatt er en lenke, og når en annen tegning lenker til denne.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EXREF\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EXR\u003C/code\u003E): \u003Ccode\u003E?\u003C/code\u003E lister lenkene (lastet eller ikke, hvilken fil, hvor mange referanser); \u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E kobler til; \u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E laster én på nytt fra filen sin (velg filen igjen: nettleseren kan ikke nå mappene dine av seg selv); \u003Ccode\u003EB\u003C/code\u003E binder én (den blir vanlige blokker og layer kalt \u003Ccode\u003EPlan$0$Walls\u003C/code\u003E); \u003Ccode\u003ED\u003C/code\u003E løsner én (referansene og layerne forsvinner). Hver er ett angretrinn.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EXCLIP\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EXC\u003C/code\u003E) klipper blokkreferanser og lenker: velg dem, \u003Ccode\u003EN\u003C/code\u003E for en ny avgrensning, deretter \u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E og to hjørner eller \u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E og punktene til en mangekant (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E lukker den); \u003Ccode\u003ED\u003C/code\u003E fjerner klippet. Bare det som ligger innenfor, vises og plottes, og klippet flyttes med referansen. DWG og DXF 2000 beholder klipp; DXF R12 lagrer referansen hel og sier det. Når du binder en lenke som har klippede referanser, spør \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EXREF\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003EB\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E binder alt, \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E beholder bare hver referanses klippede del (linjer og buer kuttes ved avgrensningen; tekster og nøstede blokker blir stående når innsettingspunktet er innenfor).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEn tegning du åpner, kan lenke til filer CadCraft ikke kan se: den sier hvor mange som ikke er lastet. Bruk \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EXREF\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E og velg hver fil for å se dem.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENår du lagrer, \u003Cstrong\u003Ebindes\u003C/strong\u003E en lastet lenke inn i filen (andre programmer ser vanlige blokker og layer kalt \u003Ccode\u003EPlan$0$…\u003C/code\u003E), og historikken sier det. En lenke som ikke er lastet, forblir en lenke til filen sin, i DWG og DXF.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"pne-i-3d\"\u003EÅpne i 3D\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EVisning\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMer\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EÅpne i ModelCraft\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EOPENIN3D\u003C/code\u003E) åpner tegningen i 3D-modelleringsappen i en ny fane, som en DWG, uten å laste den opp. Endringer gjort der, blir der; lagre dem der. Menyvalget vises bare der den appen kan ta imot en overlevert tegning.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"plotte-en-pdf\"\u003EPlotte en PDF\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETrykk \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command P\"\u003E⌘P\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+P\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, klikk \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlott\u003C/span\u003E, eller skriv \u003Ccode\u003EPLOT\u003C/code\u003E. PDF-en lages inne i nettleseren og lastes ned, eller åpnes i en ny fane for utskrift. Se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/plot-pdf\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/plot-pdf\"\u003EPlott til PDF\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"hvor-filene-havner\"\u003EHvor filene havner\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHver lagring og hvert plott er en nedlasting, så filene havner der nettleseren legger nedlastinger: vanligvis mappen Nedlastinger. For å velge en mappe hver gang endrer du nedlastingsinnstillingen i nettleseren; \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/browsers\" data-topic=\"basics/browsers\"\u003ENettlesere og datamaskiner\u003C/a\u003E viser hvor.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIngenting du åpner eller lagrer, sendes til oss. Se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/your-files\" data-topic=\"basics/your-files\"\u003EHvor filene dine havner\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"pln-og-pla-prosjektfiler\"\u003EPLN- og PLA-prosjektfiler\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECadCraft åpner DWG- og DXF-tegninger, ikke Archicads egne filer (\u003Ccode\u003E.pln\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.pla\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.tpl\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.bpn\u003C/code\u003E). Å åpne en forteller deg hva den er. Lagre visningene eller layoutene du trenger som DWG i Archicad og åpne dem her, eller eksporter modellen som IFC og åpne den i BimCraft. Der BimCraft kan overta filen, tilbyr CadCraft \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EÅpne i BimCraft\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"hvis-siden-lukkes-f-r-du-lagrer\"\u003EHvis siden lukkes før du lagrer\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECadCraft beholder en kopi av hver tegning du endrer, bare i denne nettleseren, et øyeblikk etter at du slutter å redigere. Hvis fanen lukkes, nettleseren stopper eller datamaskinen starter på nytt før du lagrer, spør CadCraft \u003Cstrong\u003EGjenopprette arbeidet ditt?\u003C/strong\u003E neste gang den starter: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGjenopprett\u003C/span\u003E åpner tegningen igjen, ulagret, i en egen fane. Å lagre tegningen fjerner kopien. Se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/recover\" data-topic=\"basics/recover\"\u003EGjenopprett arbeidet ditt\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"tilkoblet-og-frakoblet\"\u003ETilkoblet og frakoblet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EÅ åpne, tegne, lagre (DWG og DXF) og plotte skjer alt på datamaskinen din, så det fungerer uten forbindelse når CadCraft er lastet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor å bruke CadCraft helt uten forbindelse klikker du \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELagre for frakoblet bruk\u003C/span\u003E i linjen øverst mens du er tilkoblet. Etiketten teller opp («Lagrer 40 av 212») og ender på «Klar frakoblet». Den lagrede kopien inneholder DWG-leseren, omtrent 5 MB til sammen, så \u003Cstrong\u003EDWG-filer åpnes også frakoblet\u003C/strong\u003E når den er lagret. Klikk knappen igjen for \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELagre på nytt (hent nyeste versjon)\u003C/span\u003E eller \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFjern den frakoblede kopien\u003C/span\u003E. «Kunne ikke lagre» betyr at noen filer ikke ble lastet ned: sjekk forbindelsen og klikk for å prøve igjen. «Frakoblet bruk er ikke tilgjengelig» betyr at dette nettleservinduet ikke kan beholde en kopi (private vinduer og noen administrerte datamaskiner).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUten en lagret kopi hentes DWG-leseren første gang du åpner en DWG etter at siden er lastet, så å åpne en DWG frakoblet kan mislykkes; DXF-filer trenger den aldri. Ikke last siden på nytt frakoblet med mindre CadCraft er lagret.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESe \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/offline\" data-topic=\"basics/offline\"\u003EArbeide frakoblet\u003C/a\u003E for detaljene.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"net:offline\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EOffline\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDu er frakoblet nå. Tegninger lagres som DWG eller DXF og plottes som vanlig, og DXF-filer åpnes. DWG-filer åpnes hvis CadCraft viser «Klar frakoblet», eller hvis DWG-leseren allerede er lastet i denne fanen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"betingelser-i-filer\"\u003EBetingelser i filer\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBetingelser og parametere (se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/constraints\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/constraints\"\u003ESett betingelser på en tegning\u003C/a\u003E) lagres i både DWG og DXF. Hvert objekt med betingelser bærer et lite CadCraft-notat (utvidede data), og en ubrukt blokk kalt \u003Ccode\u003ECADCRAFT_PARAMETRIC\u003C/code\u003E holder betingelsene og parametertabellen. Andre programmer åpner tegningen som vanlig og ignorerer begge; de kan slette den ubrukte blokken når de renser. Notatene viser til objekter med sin egen nøkkel, ikke filens håndtak, så et program som nummererer håndtakene på nytt, ødelegger dem ikke. Når et annet program har slettet eller endret et objekt med betingelser, fjerner CadCraft betingelsene på det når den åpner filen og sier hvor mange. En fil uten dem åpnes uten betingelser.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEn skisse eksportert fra en delmodellerer (\u003Ccode\u003E.json\u003C/code\u003E) åpnes også: se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/solidworks-sketches\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/solidworks-sketches\"\u003EFlytt skisser til og fra andre delmodellerere\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"ikke-tilgjengelig-enn\"\u003EIkke tilgjengelig ennå\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFlerhjelpelinjer som flerhjelpelinje-objekter i en lagret DXF (R12 har ingen: de lagres som det de tegner).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EBilder i en lagret DXF (de lagres som rammene sine; DWG beholder dem som bilder), og maskeringer som skjuler det som er under dem.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EÅ lagre en nyere DXF-versjon enn 2000.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESkylagring og synkronisering mellom datamaskiner.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESe \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003EIkke bygget ennå\u003C/a\u003E for hele listen.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Med et blikk Format Åpne Lagre Merknader DXF (tekst, alle versjoner) Ja Ja, som DXF R12 eller DXF 2000 Binær DXF avvises: lagre den som tekst-DXF først. DWG Ja Ja, som DWG (AutoCAD 2000, 2004, 2007, 2010, 2013 eller 2018) Leses og skrives av CadCrafts eget DWG-bibliotek (AutoCAD R13- til 2018-filer). DWG-en din blir aldri endret. Skadde filer, eller versjoner leseren ikke håndterer, avvises med en melding. PDF Henter inn en side: som underlag eller som objekter (PDF-underlag) Ja, ved plotting Vektor-PDF av et ark, eller av modellen i en målestokk. Hver tegning du åpner (eller starter med Ny) får sin egen fane over tegneområdet; en ny tegning ingen har rørt ennå, brukes i stedet for å legge til en fane. Klikk en fane for å bytte, dens × for å lukke den (CadCraft spør først hvis det finnes ulagrede endringer), eller flis de åpne tegningene side ved side: se Del skjermen. Åpne en tegning Trykk Ctrl+O, klikk Åpne, eller skriv OPEN, og velg deretter filen. Eller dra en .dwg- eller .dxf-fil til tegneområdet. Filen leses inne i nettleseren din. Den lastes ikke opp noe sted. Historikken melder hvor mange objekter og layer som kom inn og hvor lang tid det tok, og delen Tegning i panelet til venstre viser formatet, for eksempel «DWG 2018 (AC1032)». DWG-leseren er en del av CadCraft og hentes med den første DWG-en du åpner etter at siden er lastet (noen hundre KB), så det er ingen egen nedlasting å vente på. Den leser filen i bakgrunnen, så siden fortsetter å svare mens en stor DWG åpnes. Hva som kommer inn Linjer, buer, sirkler, polylinjer (med buesegmenter), ellipser, punkter, tekst og flerlinjetekst, volumer, skraveringer (standardmønstrene tegnet som sine linjer, andre som en flat tone), blokkreferanser med attributtene sine (verdier du kan redigere; se Blokkattributter), måltilsettinger og layouter med visningsområdene sine. Splines kommer inn som splines (grad, kontrollpunkter, knuter, vekter, tilpasningspunkter); se Splines og polylinjer. Flerhjelpelinjer med én hjelpelinje og tekst kommer inn som CadCraft-flerhjelpelinjer du kan redigere (se MLEADER). Andre (med en blokk, flere hjelpelinjer eller buede linjer) tegnes slik det andre programmet tegnet dem og flyttes som ett; historikken sier hvor mange. Bilder vises som en ramme med bildets filnavn til du gir CadCraft filen med IMAGE: se Bilder i en tegning. Maskeringer vises som rammen sin, og det som er under dem, forblir synlig. Tabeller i en DWG kommer inn slik det andre programmet tegnet dem. 3D-flater i modellrommet kommer inn på fanen 3D, ikke som 2D-objekter; det samme gjelder de inne i blokker, én gang per blokkreferanse i modellen. I en blokk og på et ark tegnes de også flate, som omriss. Se Modeller i 3D. Layer beholder farge-, på-, frosset-, låst- og plottinnstillingene sine. DEFPOINTS plottes aldri. Måltilsettinger blir ekte, redigerbare CadCraft-måltilsettinger der punktene deres kan leses. De vises først nøyaktig slik det andre programmet tegnet dem; så snart du endrer en, tegner CadCraft den på nytt i sin egen stil. Hver layout blir en arkfane. I det andre programmet har hver layout et skjult første visningsområde som er selve arket; CadCraft utelater det. Hva som ikke kommer inn ennå Alt CadCraft ikke kan tegne, telles, ikke gjettes på. Boksen «Om denne filen» i panelet til venstre lister det som «Vises ikke ennå:» etterfulgt av hver objekttype og hvor mange. Typiske tilfeller er tabeller i en DXF, nett og 3D-volumer inne i blokker. 3D-volumer, regioner og legemer i modellrommet vises på 3D-fanen (se Modeller i 3D). Visningsområder som er vridd i filen, vises vridd som der, og beholder vridningen når du lagrer; det samme gjelder gjeldende BKS, de navngitte BKS-ene og en dreid visning. Tekst tegnes i én Helvetica-lignende font. En etikett som får plass på én linje i det opprinnelige programmets smalere font, kan brytes over to linjer i CadCraft. Lagring Det er tre måter å lagre på. Hver laster ned en kopi. Lagre: trykk Ctrl+S, klikk Lagre, eller skriv SAVE (også QSAVE). Den lagrer i tegningens format: DWG hvis du åpnet en DWG, DXF hvis du åpnet en DXF. En DWG beholder versjonen den kom inn i (en 2018-fil lagres som 2018). En ny tegning bruker formatet og DWG-versjonen du sist valgte i Lagre som i denne nettleseren; hvis du aldri valgte noe, DWG i AutoCAD 2000-formatet. Lagre som: trykk Ctrl+Shift+S, klikk Lagre som, eller skriv SAVEAS. Dialogen Lagre en kopi som tilbyr DWG, med en liste Versjon (AutoCAD 2018, 2013, 2010, 2007, 2004 eller 2000), DXF R12 og DXF 2000, hver med filnavnet den vil gi, og tegningens gjeldende format og versjon allerede valgt. Klikk Lagre for å laste ned, eller Avbryt (eller Esc) for å lagre ingenting; historikken sier da «Ikke lagret.» Det du velger, blir stående: Ctrl+S bruker det for denne tegningen fra da av, og nye tegninger i denne nettleseren starter med det. DXFOUT: skriv DXFOUT. Den laster alltid ned en DXF R12-kopi, og neste Ctrl+S lagrer fortsatt i tegningens eget format. Nettleseren laster ned \u003Ctegningsnavn\u003E.dwg eller \u003Ctegningsnavn\u003E.dxf. Hver lagring er en ny nedlasting; filen du åpnet blir aldri overskrevet eller endret. Historikken bekrefter det, for eksempel «Lagret en kopi som Plan.dwg (DWG (AutoCAD 2018)). Originalen Plan.dxf er uendret.» For en DXF står det «(DXF R12, åpnes i alle CAD-programmer)». Linjene under lister alt som endret seg på veien. Når CadCraft kjører i skrivebordsappen, fungerer lagring slik det gjør i andre skrivebordsprogrammer: Ctrl+S skriver tegningen tilbake til filen du åpnet, i sitt eget format og sin egen versjon, og historikken sier hvor den ble lagret. Lagre som og eksportene viser systemets lagringsdialog, og en .dwg-, .dxf- eller .ccsheets-fil du dobbeltklikker, åpnes i CadCraft. For en PDF plotter du i stedet (se nedenfor). Hva en DWG beholder og endrer CadCraft skriver DWG-en selv, inne i nettleseren din, med det samme DWG-biblioteket den leser med. Velg versjonen i Lagre som: AutoCAD 2000-formatet (filversjon AC1015) åpnes i flest programmer, også gamle; de nyere formatene (2004, AC1018, opp til 2018, AC1032) beholder også ekte farger. Ingenting lastes opp, og å lagre en DWG fungerer frakoblet. Hver versjon er kontrollert ved å lese de lagrede filene tilbake med tre andre programmers DWG-lesere og med CadCraft selv. En CadCraft-DWG er ikke åpnet i AutoCAD selv ennå. Hva en DWG beholder Linjer, buer, sirkler, ellipser, punkter og polylinjer med buesegmentene sine. Enlinjetekst med justering og breddefaktor. Flerlinjetekst og brutt tekst, som flerlinjetekst. Det andre programmet bryter den på nytt fra samme bredde. Objektfarger: etter layer, etter blokk, de 255 standardfargene, og ekte farger i 2004-formatet og senere. Layer med farge, linjetype, linjevekt og på-, frosset-, låst- og plottinnstillingene sine; objektenes egne linjetyper, linjevekter og linjetypeskalaer, linjetypemønstrene og tegningens linjetypeskala. Tegneenhetene og lengdeformatet (desimal, fot og tommer, brøker …). Blokker med basispunktet og attributtdefinisjonene sine, også usynlige, konstante og forhåndsinnstilte. Blokkreferanser med skala, rotasjon og attributtverdier. Heldekkende fyll og mønsterskraveringer med mønster, skala og vinkel (mønsterets egne linjer, så andre programmer tegner de samme linjene som CadCraft). Måltilsettinger, som ekte måltilsettinger: andre programmer måler dem på nytt når de strekkes. Hver beholder sine egne innstillinger (teksthøyde, pilstørrelse, desimaler, lengdeformat) og teksten sin, og har den tegnede kopien hver DWG-måltilsetting har. Ark (layouter): hvert ark med papirstørrelse og enheter, det som er tegnet på det, og visningsområdene med visning og skala. Navngitte visninger. Flerhjelpelinjer, som flerhjelpelinjer. Layer- og blokknavn slik du skrev dem, med store og små bokstaver. (DXF R12 skriver dem med store bokstaver.) Hva en DWG endrer Hver av disse listes i historikken etter at du har lagret, når det gjelder tegningen din. I CadCraft I den lagrede DWG-en Ekte farger (valgt med fargevelgeren), i 2000-formatet Nærmeste standardfarge: 2000-formatet har bare de 255. Lagre som 2004 eller senere for å beholde dem Navngitte visninger på et ark Papirromvisninger: en DWG sier ikke hvilket ark, så når CadCraft åpner filen igjen, tilhører de første ark Flerlinjetekst med breddefaktor Normal bokstavbredde: i en DWG gjelder breddefaktoren bare enlinjetekst Layer- eller blokknavn med tegn DWG ikke tillater Endret: et navn fra en lenket tegning som Base|Walls blir Base$0$Walls (slik binding av en lenket tegning navngir det), og andre tegn blir _ Hvorfor R12, og hva som konverteres Når du lagrer som DXF, skriver CadCraft DXF R12, versjonen alle CAD-programmer kan åpne. Den er eldre enn noen funksjoner, så noen ting skrives i en enklere form: I CadCraft I den lagrede R12-filen Flerlinjetekst Ett tekstobjekt per linje Ellipser Polylinjer som følger ellipsen Skraveringer (fra åpnede filer) Bare omrissene Ekte farger (valgt med fargevelgeren) Nærmeste standardfarge Linjevekter, og objektenes egne linjetypeskalaer Utelatt: R12 har ingen av dem (linjetyper beholdes) Layer- og blokknavn Skrevet med store bokstaver; mellomrom og andre tegn blir _, og navn kuttes til 31 tegn («Title block» blir TITLE_BLOCK) Layer satt til ikke å plottes Andre programmer vil plotte dem (CadCraft husker innstillingen når tegningen har et ark) Måltilsettinger Ekte måltilsettinger med sin egen tegnede blokk, så andre programmer viser dem slik du ser dem Flerhjelpelinjer Linjene, pilspissen og teksten de tegner Navngitte visninger Beholdt, med navn med store bokstaver; et arks visninger kommer tilbake på det første arket Flere ark Det første arket er papirrommet alle programmer viser; de andre beholdes som blokker kalt CCLAYOUT_1, CCLAYOUT_2 … som CadCraft gjør om til ark igjen når den åpner filen Historikken lister hver av disse som gjaldt tegningen din. For å fortsette å arbeide på en tegning over flere dager lagrer du som DXF og åpner den DXF-en neste gang. Alt CadCraft kan tegne, overlever rundturen, også måltilsettinger, blokker og alle ark. Lenkede tegninger (eksterne referanser) XATTACH (XA, eller Blokker › Mer › Koble en tegning (xref)) lenker en annen DWG eller DXF: velg filen, og klikk deretter hvor den skal stå (Enter legger den på 0,0). Den kommer inn som én blokk oppkalt etter filen, i riktig skala når de to tegningene har ulike enheter. Layerne og blokkene beholder navnene sine med lenkens navn foran — Plan|Walls, Plan|Door — så de aldri blandes med dine: slå de lenkede layerne av og på, frys eller lås dem som dine egne. En lenke er skrivebeskyttet: endre den andre tegningen og last den på nytt. Det du setter på en lenkes layer (farge, linjetype, linjevekt, på/av, frys, lås, plott), beholdes når du laster den på nytt, når du lagrer tegningen som DWG mens lenken fortsatt er en lenke, og når en annen tegning lenker til denne. XREF (XR): ? lister lenkene (lastet eller ikke, hvilken fil, hvor mange referanser); A kobler til; R laster én på nytt fra filen sin (velg filen igjen: nettleseren kan ikke nå mappene dine av seg selv); B binder én (den blir vanlige blokker og layer kalt Plan$0$Walls); D løsner én (referansene og layerne forsvinner). Hver er ett angretrinn. XCLIP (XC) klipper blokkreferanser og lenker: velg dem, N for en ny avgrensning, deretter R og to hjørner eller P og punktene til en mangekant (Enter lukker den); D fjerner klippet. Bare det som ligger innenfor, vises og plottes, og klippet flyttes med referansen. DWG og DXF 2000 beholder klipp; DXF R12 lagrer referansen hel og sier det. Når du binder en lenke som har klippede referanser, spør XREF B: A binder alt, C beholder bare hver referanses klippede del (linjer og buer kuttes ved avgrensningen; tekster og nøstede blokker blir stående når innsettingspunktet er innenfor). En tegning du åpner, kan lenke til filer CadCraft ikke kan se: den sier hvor mange som ikke er lastet. Bruk XREF, R og velg hver fil for å se dem. Når du lagrer, bindes en lastet lenke inn i filen (andre programmer ser vanlige blokker og layer kalt Plan$0$…), og historikken sier det. En lenke som ikke er lastet, forblir en lenke til filen sin, i DWG og DXF. Åpne i 3D Visning › Mer › Åpne i ModelCraft (OPENIN3D) åpner tegningen i 3D-modelleringsappen i en ny fane, som en DWG, uten å laste den opp. Endringer gjort der, blir der; lagre dem der. Menyvalget vises bare der den appen kan ta imot en overlevert tegning. Plotte en PDF Trykk Ctrl+P, klikk Plott, eller skriv PLOT. PDF-en lages inne i nettleseren og lastes ned, eller åpnes i en ny fane for utskrift. Se Plott til PDF. Hvor filene havner Hver lagring og hvert plott er en nedlasting, så filene havner der nettleseren legger nedlastinger: vanligvis mappen Nedlastinger. For å velge en mappe hver gang endrer du nedlastingsinnstillingen i nettleseren; Nettlesere og datamaskiner viser hvor. Ingenting du åpner eller lagrer, sendes til oss. Se Hvor filene dine havner. PLN- og PLA-prosjektfiler CadCraft åpner DWG- og DXF-tegninger, ikke Archicads egne filer (.pln, .pla, .tpl, .bpn). Å åpne en forteller deg hva den er. Lagre visningene eller layoutene du trenger som DWG i Archicad og åpne dem her, eller eksporter modellen som IFC og åpne den i BimCraft. Der BimCraft kan overta filen, tilbyr CadCraft Åpne i BimCraft. Hvis siden lukkes før du lagrer CadCraft beholder en kopi av hver tegning du endrer, bare i denne nettleseren, et øyeblikk etter at du slutter å redigere. Hvis fanen lukkes, nettleseren stopper eller datamaskinen starter på nytt før du lagrer, spør CadCraft Gjenopprette arbeidet ditt? neste gang den starter: Gjenopprett åpner tegningen igjen, ulagret, i en egen fane. Å lagre tegningen fjerner kopien. Se Gjenopprett arbeidet ditt. Tilkoblet og frakoblet Å åpne, tegne, lagre (DWG og DXF) og plotte skjer alt på datamaskinen din, så det fungerer uten forbindelse når CadCraft er lastet. For å bruke CadCraft helt uten forbindelse klikker du Lagre for frakoblet bruk i linjen øverst mens du er tilkoblet. Etiketten teller opp («Lagrer 40 av 212») og ender på «Klar frakoblet». Den lagrede kopien inneholder DWG-leseren, omtrent 5 MB til sammen, så DWG-filer åpnes også frakoblet når den er lagret. Klikk knappen igjen for Lagre på nytt (hent nyeste versjon) eller Fjern den frakoblede kopien. «Kunne ikke lagre» betyr at noen filer ikke ble lastet ned: sjekk forbindelsen og klikk for å prøve igjen. «Frakoblet bruk er ikke tilgjengelig» betyr at dette nettleservinduet ikke kan beholde en kopi (private vinduer og noen administrerte datamaskiner). Uten en lagret kopi hentes DWG-leseren første gang du åpner en DWG etter at siden er lastet, så å åpne en DWG frakoblet kan mislykkes; DXF-filer trenger den aldri. Ikke last siden på nytt frakoblet med mindre CadCraft er lagret. Se Arbeide frakoblet for detaljene. Du er frakoblet nå. Tegninger lagres som DWG eller DXF og plottes som vanlig, og DXF-filer åpnes. DWG-filer åpnes hvis CadCraft viser «Klar frakoblet», eller hvis DWG-leseren allerede er lastet i denne fanen. Betingelser i filer Betingelser og parametere (se Sett betingelser på en tegning) lagres i både DWG og DXF. Hvert objekt med betingelser bærer et lite CadCraft-notat (utvidede data), og en ubrukt blokk kalt CADCRAFT_PARAMETRIC holder betingelsene og parametertabellen. Andre programmer åpner tegningen som vanlig og ignorerer begge; de kan slette den ubrukte blokken når de renser. Notatene viser til objekter med sin egen nøkkel, ikke filens håndtak, så et program som nummererer håndtakene på nytt, ødelegger dem ikke. Når et annet program har slettet eller endret et objekt med betingelser, fjerner CadCraft betingelsene på det når den åpner filen og sier hvor mange. En fil uten dem åpnes uten betingelser. En skisse eksportert fra en delmodellerer (.json) åpnes også: se Flytt skisser til og fra andre delmodellerere. Ikke tilgjengelig ennå Flerhjelpelinjer som flerhjelpelinje-objekter i en lagret DXF (R12 har ingen: de lagres som det de tegner). Bilder i en lagret DXF (de lagres som rammene sine; DWG beholder dem som bilder), og maskeringer som skjuler det som er under dem. Å lagre en nyere DXF-versjon enn 2000. Skylagring og synkronisering mellom datamaskiner. Se Ikke bygget ennå for hele listen.","lang":"nb"},{"slug":"draw-with-coordinates","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Draw with coordinates","summary":"Draw an exact floor outline by typing absolute, relative and polar coordinates, and by direct distance entry.","section":"tasks","order":10,"keywords":["line","pline","polyline","coordinates","relative","polar","absolute","direct distance","@","typing points","exact","ortho"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-make","text":"What you will make","level":2},{"id":"before-you-start","text":"Before you start","level":2},{"id":"absolute-coordinates-the-first-point","text":"Absolute coordinates: the first point","level":2},{"id":"relative-coordinates-dx-dy","text":"Relative coordinates: @dx,dy","level":2},{"id":"direct-distance-entry-a-bare-number","text":"Direct distance entry: a bare number","level":2},{"id":"polar-coordinates-distance-angle","text":"Polar coordinates: @distance\u003Cangle","level":2},{"id":"the-same-outline-as-one-polyline","text":"The same outline as one polyline","level":2},{"id":"options-while-drawing","text":"Options while drawing","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-make\"\u003EWhat you will make\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe outline of an L-shaped room, 6000 by 4500 with a 2000 by 1500 notch, drawn exactly, plus a wall at 30 degrees. You will use all four ways of typing a point. Work in millimeters: one drawing unit stands for one millimeter.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe quick reference for every format is in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/coordinate-entry\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/coordinate-entry\"\u003ECoordinate and distance entry\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"before-you-start\"\u003EBefore you start\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen CadCraft. If a drawing is open, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew\u003C/span\u003E first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E so that nothing is selected. The right panel reads \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECurrent settings\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnits\u003C/span\u003E to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMillimetres\u003C/span\u003E. Nothing is resized; this only tells sheets and plots what a unit means.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"absolute-coordinates-the-first-point\"\u003EAbsolute coordinates: the first point\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn absolute coordinate is a position measured from the origin, typed as \u003Ccode\u003Ex,y\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The prompt reads \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify first point:\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E0,0\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe line is now rubber-banding from the origin to the crosshair. The coordinates in the status bar show the distance and angle from the start point as you move.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"relative-coordinates-dx-dy\"\u003ERelative coordinates: @dx,dy\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA relative coordinate is typed as \u003Ccode\u003E@dx,dy\u003C/code\u003E: so far across, so far up, from the last point. Negative numbers go left or down.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E@6000,0\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. A 6000 line runs to the right.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E@0,3000\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E@-2000,0\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E@0,1500\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou should see four connected lines climbing up the right side of the room.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"direct-distance-entry-a-bare-number\"\u003EDirect distance entry: a bare number\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen you type a single number, CadCraft goes that distance in the direction of the crosshair. Turn on Ortho so the direction snaps to horizontal or vertical.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F8\"\u003EF8\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF8\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrtho\u003C/span\u003E in the status bar. The status bar says &quot;Ortho on&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the crosshair to the left of the last point. The rubber band stays horizontal.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E4000\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The line goes 4000 to the left.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the crosshair below the last point.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E4500\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. You are back at the start point.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E on the empty prompt to end the command.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EF8 may control media playback on a Mac keyboard. Hold \u003Cstrong\u003Efn\u003C/strong\u003E and press F8, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrtho\u003C/span\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/keys\" data-topic=\"basics/keys\"\u003EKeys, mice and trackpads\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou now have a closed L-shaped outline made of six separate lines.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExtents\u003C/span\u003E to see all of it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"polar-coordinates-distance-angle\"\u003EPolar coordinates: @distance&lt;angle\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA polar coordinate is typed as \u003Ccode\u003E@distance&lt;angle\u003C/code\u003E. Angles are in degrees, measured counterclockwise from the positive X direction: 0 is right, 90 is up, 180 is left, 270 (or -90) is down.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E6000,0\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to start at the lower-right corner.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E@2500&lt;-30\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. A 2500 line leaves the corner, heading down and to the right at 30 degrees below horizontal.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to end the command.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA polar coordinate without \u003Ccode\u003E@\u003C/code\u003E, such as \u003Ccode\u003E2500&lt;30\u003C/code\u003E, is measured from the origin instead of from the last point.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-same-outline-as-one-polyline\"\u003EThe same outline as one polyline\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESix separate lines are fine for many jobs, but a polyline is one object: it offsets, measures and selects as a whole. Use \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPLINE\u003C/code\u003E with the same input.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EPL\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E0,-6000\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to start below the first outline.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E@6000,0\u003C/code\u003E, then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E@0,3000\u003C/code\u003E, then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E@-2000,0\u003C/code\u003E, then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E@0,1500\u003C/code\u003E, then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E@-4000,0\u003C/code\u003E, then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The polyline closes and the command ends.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the new outline. The right panel shows &quot;Polyline&quot;, its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EVertices\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength\u003C/span\u003E and, because it is closed, its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArea\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe area should read 24000000 (square millimeters, which is 24 square meters).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"options-while-drawing\"\u003EOptions while drawing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELINE\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPLINE\u003C/code\u003E, type \u003Ccode\u003EU\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to take back the last segment without leaving the command.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELINE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E closes back to the first point once there are at least two segments.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPLINE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E switches to arc segments (each arc continues smoothly from the segment before), and \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E switches back to lines. In arc mode, the close option is \u003Ccode\u003ECL\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPressing \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E at \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify first point:\u003C/code\u003E in \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELINE\u003C/code\u003E starts from the last point you gave, so you can carry on where you stopped.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETyping \u003Ccode\u003E6000,0\u003C/code\u003E when you meant \u003Ccode\u003E@6000,0\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/strong\u003E Without \u003Ccode\u003E@\u003C/code\u003E, the point is measured from the origin, not from the last point, so the line shoots off somewhere unexpected. In CadCraft, \u003Ccode\u003Ex,y\u003C/code\u003E is always absolute. Press \u003Ccode\u003EU\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to take it back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA comma typed as a decimal point.\u003C/strong\u003E Decimals use a period: \u003Ccode\u003E1250.5\u003C/code\u003E. A comma always separates X from Y.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe direct distance went the wrong way.\u003C/strong\u003E A bare number follows the crosshair. Move the crosshair to the side you want before you press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, and turn on \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrtho\u003C/span\u003E for straight lines.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;Point or option keyword required.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E CadCraft did not understand what you typed. Check for spaces inside the number or a missing comma, and type it again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFeet and inches.\u003C/strong\u003E CadCraft reads plain numbers only. Type \u003Ccode\u003E150\u003C/code\u003E for 12&#039;-6&quot; in an inch drawing, or \u003Ccode\u003E12.5\u003C/code\u003E in a foot drawing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will make The outline of an L-shaped room, 6000 by 4500 with a 2000 by 1500 notch, drawn exactly, plus a wall at 30 degrees. You will use all four ways of typing a point. Work in millimeters: one drawing unit stands for one millimeter. The quick reference for every format is in Coordinate and distance entry. Before you start Open CadCraft. If a drawing is open, click New first. Press Esc so that nothing is selected. The right panel reads Current settings. Set Units to Millimetres. Nothing is resized; this only tells sheets and plots what a unit means. Absolute coordinates: the first point An absolute coordinate is a position measured from the origin, typed as x,y. Type L and press Enter. The prompt reads Specify first point:. Type 0,0 and press Enter. The line is now rubber-banding from the origin to the crosshair. The coordinates in the status bar show the distance and angle from the start point as you move. Relative coordinates: @dx,dy A relative coordinate is typed as @dx,dy: so far across, so far up, from the last point. Negative numbers go left or down. Type @6000,0 and press Enter. A 6000 line runs to the right. Type @0,3000 and press Enter. Type @-2000,0 and press Enter. Type @0,1500 and press Enter. You should see four connected lines climbing up the right side of the room. Direct distance entry: a bare number When you type a single number, CadCraft goes that distance in the direction of the crosshair. Turn on Ortho so the direction snaps to horizontal or vertical. Press F8, or click Ortho in the status bar. The status bar says \"Ortho on\". Move the crosshair to the left of the last point. The rubber band stays horizontal. Type 4000 and press Enter. The line goes 4000 to the left. Move the crosshair below the last point. Type 4500 and press Enter. You are back at the start point. Press Enter on the empty prompt to end the command. F8 may control media playback on a Mac keyboard. Hold fn and press F8, or click Ortho. See Keys, mice and trackpads. You now have a closed L-shaped outline made of six separate lines. Click Extents to see all of it. Polar coordinates: @distance\u003Cangle A polar coordinate is typed as @distance\u003Cangle. Angles are in degrees, measured counterclockwise from the positive X direction: 0 is right, 90 is up, 180 is left, 270 (or -90) is down. Type L and press Enter. Type 6000,0 and press Enter to start at the lower-right corner. Type @2500\u003C-30 and press Enter. A 2500 line leaves the corner, heading down and to the right at 30 degrees below horizontal. Press Enter to end the command. A polar coordinate without @, such as 2500\u003C30, is measured from the origin instead of from the last point. The same outline as one polyline Six separate lines are fine for many jobs, but a polyline is one object: it offsets, measures and selects as a whole. Use PLINE with the same input. Type PL and press Enter. Type 0,-6000 and press Enter to start below the first outline. Type @6000,0, then press Enter. Type @0,3000, then press Enter. Type @-2000,0, then press Enter. Type @0,1500, then press Enter. Type @-4000,0, then press Enter. Type C and press Enter. The polyline closes and the command ends. Click the new outline. The right panel shows \"Polyline\", its Vertices, Length and, because it is closed, its Area. The area should read 24000000 (square millimeters, which is 24 square meters). Options while drawing In LINE and PLINE, type U and press Enter to take back the last segment without leaving the command. In LINE, C closes back to the first point once there are at least two segments. In PLINE, A switches to arc segments (each arc continues smoothly from the segment before), and L switches back to lines. In arc mode, the close option is CL. Pressing Enter at Specify first point: in LINE starts from the last point you gave, so you can carry on where you stopped. Common mistakes Typing 6000,0 when you meant @6000,0. Without @, the point is measured from the origin, not from the last point, so the line shoots off somewhere unexpected. In CadCraft, x,y is always absolute. Press U and Enter to take it back. A comma typed as a decimal point. Decimals use a period: 1250.5. A comma always separates X from Y. The direct distance went the wrong way. A bare number follows the crosshair. Move the crosshair to the side you want before you press Enter, and turn on Ortho for straight lines. \"Point or option keyword required.\" CadCraft did not understand what you typed. Check for spaces inside the number or a missing comma, and type it again. Feet and inches. CadCraft reads plain numbers only. Type 150 for 12'-6\" in an inch drawing, or 12.5 in a foot drawing.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"object-snaps","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Snap to points on objects","summary":"Use object snaps, Ortho and the grid to pick exact endpoints, midpoints, centers and intersections with the mouse.","section":"tasks","order":20,"keywords":["osnap","object snap","endpoint","midpoint","center","intersection","perpendicular","quadrant","nearest","node","insertion","ortho","grid","f3","f8","f7","polar tracking","polar","polarang","object snap tracking","otrack","tracking","f10","f11"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-make","text":"What you will make","level":2},{"id":"how-snapping-works","text":"How snapping works","level":2},{"id":"choose-your-snaps","text":"Choose your snaps","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"skip-a-snap-for-one-click","text":"Skip a snap for one click","level":2},{"id":"ortho","text":"Ortho","level":2},{"id":"polar-tracking","text":"Polar tracking","level":2},{"id":"object-snap-tracking","text":"Object snap tracking","level":2},{"id":"grid","text":"Grid","level":2},{"id":"function-keys-on-your-computer","text":"Function keys on your computer","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-make\"\u003EWhat you will make\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA rectangle with its two diagonals, a circle centered where they cross, and a line from the middle of one side square onto the circle. Every point is picked with the mouse, and every point is exact.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-snapping-works\"\u003EHow snapping works\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile a command asks for a point, CadCraft looks for snap points within about 10 pixels of the crosshair. When it finds one, a marker and a label appear (&quot;Endpoint&quot;, &quot;Intersection&quot;), and a click lands \u003Cstrong\u003Eexactly\u003C/strong\u003E on that point, not where the crosshair is.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESnaps are on when \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESnap\u003C/span\u003E in the status bar is pressed. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F3\"\u003EF3\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF3\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E turns them on and off.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe snaps you can use are:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESnap\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ELands on\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEndpoint\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe ends of lines, arcs and polyline segments\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMidpoint\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe middle of lines, arcs and polyline segments\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECenter\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe center of circles and arcs, and of polyline arc segments\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EQuadrant\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe 0, 90, 180 and 270 degree points of circles and arcs\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIntersection\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWhere two objects cross\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPerpendicular\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe point on an object square to the last point (needs a base point)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETangent\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe point on a circle or arc where a line from the last point just touches it. With no last point yet (a line&#039;s first point), the marker says &quot;deferred&quot;: the touch point is worked out once the next point is known, so a line from Tangent to Tangent touches both circles\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENearest\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe nearest point on any object\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENode\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPoint objects\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EInsertion\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe insertion point of text and block references\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAll of them are on by default except Nearest, which tends to win when you want something else.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"choose-your-snaps\"\u003EChoose your snaps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the small arrow next to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESnap\u003C/span\u003E in the status bar. The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EObject snaps\u003C/span\u003E list opens.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETick or untick snaps. Changes apply at once.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the arrow again to close the list.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECadCraft remembers your choice, and the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGrid\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrtho\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESnap\u003C/span\u003E settings, in this browser.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EREC\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E0,0\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E@4000,3000\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. A rectangle appears.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExtents\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the crosshair to the lower-left corner. The marker shows &quot;Endpoint&quot;. Click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove to the upper-right corner until &quot;Endpoint&quot; shows. Click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to end the line.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again to repeat \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELINE\u003C/code\u003E, and draw the other diagonal from the lower-right corner to the upper-left corner the same way.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to start a circle.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the crosshair to where the diagonals cross. The marker shows &quot;Intersection&quot;. Click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E800\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou should see a circle centered exactly on the crossing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove to the middle of the left side of the rectangle until &quot;Midpoint&quot; shows. Click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the crosshair onto the circle, on its left side. The marker shows &quot;Perpendicular&quot; or &quot;Quadrant&quot;; both are the same point here. Click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe new line runs from the middle of the left side and stops exactly on the circle.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"skip-a-snap-for-one-click\"\u003ESkip a snap for one click\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while you click. Object snaps are ignored for that click, and the point goes exactly where the crosshair is (or along the Ortho direction, if Ortho is on).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"ortho\"\u003EOrtho\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrtho\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F8\"\u003EF8\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF8\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) keeps the next point straight across or straight up and down from the base point. It applies to clicks and to direct distance entry (a bare number typed at the prompt). Object snaps win over Ortho: if a snap marker shows, the click goes to the snap.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"polar-tracking\"\u003EPolar tracking\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPolar\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F10\"\u003EF10\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF10\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) is Ortho at more angles. While a command has a base point (the last point of a line, a move&#039;s base point), the pointer lines up whenever it comes near a ray from it at a multiple of the polar step: a dotted line shows, and a label by the crosshair reads \u003Ccode\u003EPolar: 1200 &lt; 30°\u003C/code\u003E. Click to use that point, or type a distance: \u003Ccode\u003E1200\u003C/code\u003E goes exactly 1200 along the ray.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe step is 90° until you change it: type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPOLARANG\u003C/code\u003E, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, and type \u003Ccode\u003E30\u003C/code\u003E (or 45, 22.5, 15 …). Polar and Ortho exclude each other: turning one on turns the other off.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"object-snap-tracking\"\u003EObject snap tracking\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETrack\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F11\"\u003EF11\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF11\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) lines the pointer up with snap points you have rested on, so you can pick a point level with one corner and above another without drawing helper lines.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETurn on \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESnap\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETrack\u003C/span\u003E, and start a command that asks for a point (\u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERest the pointer on a snap point, such as a corner, for half a second, without clicking. A small cross marks it: it is acquired. Rest on it again to drop it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the pointer straight away from it, horizontally or vertically. A dotted line follows the pointer and the label reads \u003Ccode\u003ETrack: 600 &lt; 90°\u003C/code\u003E. Click, or type a distance.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAcquire a second point, and move to where the vertical through one meets the horizontal through the other: the pointer snaps to that crossing (\u003Ccode\u003ETrack: where two lines meet\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPolar\u003C/span\u003E on as well, acquired points track at every polar angle. Up to seven points are kept, until the command ends. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E turns tracking off for one click, like snaps.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"grid\"\u003EGrid\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGrid\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F7\"\u003EF7\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF7\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) shows a grid of dots behind the drawing, spaced in powers of ten that change as you zoom. It is a visual guide only: points do not snap to the grid.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"function-keys-on-your-computer\"\u003EFunction keys on your computer\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F10\"\u003EF10\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF10\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E is Polar and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F11\"\u003EF11\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF11\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E is Track.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a Mac, F3, F7 and F8 usually control Mission Control and media. Hold \u003Cstrong\u003Efn\u003C/strong\u003E with the key, or turn on standard function keys, as described in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/keys\" data-topic=\"basics/keys\"\u003EKeys, mice and trackpads\u003C/a\u003E. The status bar buttons always work.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:firefox\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EFirefox\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn Firefox, F7 is also the key for caret browsing. If Firefox asks whether to turn caret browsing on, choose \u003Cstrong\u003ENo\u003C/strong\u003E. Use the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGrid\u003C/span\u003E button if the question keeps coming back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:touch\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ETouch\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a touch screen there is no hover, so the snap marker only appears where your finger touches. Zoom in close for small targets, or type coordinates instead.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe click went to the wrong point.\u003C/strong\u003E Look at the marker label before you click: Endpoint wins over Midpoint when both are near. Zoom in, or untick snaps you do not need.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENo marker appears.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESnap\u003C/span\u003E is off, or the snap you want is unticked, or the object is on a layer that is off or frozen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESnaps grab the wrong thing in a busy drawing.\u003C/strong\u003E Zoom in. The snap distance is measured in screen pixels, so zooming in makes it smaller on the drawing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EExpecting Shift to give a straight line.\u003C/strong\u003E In CadCraft, Shift turns snaps off for one click. Use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrtho\u003C/span\u003E for straight lines.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EExpecting points to land on grid dots.\u003C/strong\u003E The grid does not snap. Type coordinates for round numbers.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will make A rectangle with its two diagonals, a circle centered where they cross, and a line from the middle of one side square onto the circle. Every point is picked with the mouse, and every point is exact. How snapping works While a command asks for a point, CadCraft looks for snap points within about 10 pixels of the crosshair. When it finds one, a marker and a label appear (\"Endpoint\", \"Intersection\"), and a click lands exactly on that point, not where the crosshair is. Snaps are on when Snap in the status bar is pressed. F3 turns them on and off. The snaps you can use are: Snap Lands on Endpoint The ends of lines, arcs and polyline segments Midpoint The middle of lines, arcs and polyline segments Center The center of circles and arcs, and of polyline arc segments Quadrant The 0, 90, 180 and 270 degree points of circles and arcs Intersection Where two objects cross Perpendicular The point on an object square to the last point (needs a base point) Tangent The point on a circle or arc where a line from the last point just touches it. With no last point yet (a line's first point), the marker says \"deferred\": the touch point is worked out once the next point is known, so a line from Tangent to Tangent touches both circles Nearest The nearest point on any object Node Point objects Insertion The insertion point of text and block references All of them are on by default except Nearest, which tends to win when you want something else. Choose your snaps Click the small arrow next to Snap in the status bar. The Object snaps list opens. Tick or untick snaps. Changes apply at once. Click the arrow again to close the list. CadCraft remembers your choice, and the Grid, Ortho and Snap settings, in this browser. Steps Type REC and press Enter. Type 0,0 and press Enter. Type @4000,3000 and press Enter. A rectangle appears. Click Extents. Type L and press Enter. Move the crosshair to the lower-left corner. The marker shows \"Endpoint\". Click. Move to the upper-right corner until \"Endpoint\" shows. Click. Press Enter to end the line. Press Enter again to repeat LINE, and draw the other diagonal from the lower-right corner to the upper-left corner the same way. Type C and press Enter to start a circle. Move the crosshair to where the diagonals cross. The marker shows \"Intersection\". Click. Type 800 and press Enter. You should see a circle centered exactly on the crossing. Type L and press Enter. Move to the middle of the left side of the rectangle until \"Midpoint\" shows. Click. Move the crosshair onto the circle, on its left side. The marker shows \"Perpendicular\" or \"Quadrant\"; both are the same point here. Click. Press Enter. The new line runs from the middle of the left side and stops exactly on the circle. Skip a snap for one click Hold Shift while you click. Object snaps are ignored for that click, and the point goes exactly where the crosshair is (or along the Ortho direction, if Ortho is on). Ortho Ortho (F8) keeps the next point straight across or straight up and down from the base point. It applies to clicks and to direct distance entry (a bare number typed at the prompt). Object snaps win over Ortho: if a snap marker shows, the click goes to the snap. Polar tracking Polar (F10) is Ortho at more angles. While a command has a base point (the last point of a line, a move's base point), the pointer lines up whenever it comes near a ray from it at a multiple of the polar step: a dotted line shows, and a label by the crosshair reads Polar: 1200 \u003C 30°. Click to use that point, or type a distance: 1200 goes exactly 1200 along the ray. The step is 90° until you change it: type POLARANG, press Enter, and type 30 (or 45, 22.5, 15 …). Polar and Ortho exclude each other: turning one on turns the other off. Object snap tracking Track (F11) lines the pointer up with snap points you have rested on, so you can pick a point level with one corner and above another without drawing helper lines. Turn on Snap and Track, and start a command that asks for a point (L). Rest the pointer on a snap point, such as a corner, for half a second, without clicking. A small cross marks it: it is acquired. Rest on it again to drop it. Move the pointer straight away from it, horizontally or vertically. A dotted line follows the pointer and the label reads Track: 600 \u003C 90°. Click, or type a distance. Acquire a second point, and move to where the vertical through one meets the horizontal through the other: the pointer snaps to that crossing (Track: where two lines meet). With Polar on as well, acquired points track at every polar angle. Up to seven points are kept, until the command ends. Shift turns tracking off for one click, like snaps. Grid Grid (F7) shows a grid of dots behind the drawing, spaced in powers of ten that change as you zoom. It is a visual guide only: points do not snap to the grid. Function keys on your computer F10 is Polar and F11 is Track. On a Mac, F3, F7 and F8 usually control Mission Control and media. Hold fn with the key, or turn on standard function keys, as described in Keys, mice and trackpads. The status bar buttons always work. In Firefox, F7 is also the key for caret browsing. If Firefox asks whether to turn caret browsing on, choose No. Use the Grid button if the question keeps coming back. On a touch screen there is no hover, so the snap marker only appears where your finger touches. Zoom in close for small targets, or type coordinates instead. Common mistakes The click went to the wrong point. Look at the marker label before you click: Endpoint wins over Midpoint when both are near. Zoom in, or untick snaps you do not need. No marker appears. Snap is off, or the snap you want is unticked, or the object is on a layer that is off or frozen. Snaps grab the wrong thing in a busy drawing. Zoom in. The snap distance is measured in screen pixels, so zooming in makes it smaller on the drawing. Expecting Shift to give a straight line. In CadCraft, Shift turns snaps off for one click. Use Ortho for straight lines. Expecting points to land on grid dots. The grid does not snap. Type coordinates for round numbers.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"register-scans","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Register several scans into one","summary":"Place scans taken from different positions in one frame by picked point pairs or typed targets, refine them with ICP, save the registration as a project, and export the registered cloud as LAS or E57. The command is REGISTERSCANS.","section":"tasks","order":29,"keywords":["register","registration","scans","point cloud","icp","iterative closest point","targets","point pairs","rigid transform","align","merge","e57","las","REGISTERSCANS","REGSCAN"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"place-the-scans","text":"Place the scans","level":2},{"id":"refine-with-icp","text":"Refine with ICP","level":2},{"id":"save-and-export","text":"Save and export","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESeveral scans of one place (each from its own scanner position) start in their own coordinates. Register them: give each scan one placement so they all sit in one frame, then save that as a project and write the registered cloud as one LAS or E57 file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"place-the-scans\"\u003EPlace the scans\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the dialog (type \u003Ccode\u003EREGISTERSCANS\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003EREGSCAN\u003C/code\u003E)). \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd scans…\u003C/span\u003E takes LAS, LAZ, E57, PLY, PCD, PTX, PTS or XYZ files, or drop them on the dialog. Scans that use different units are refused with a note: convert one first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScan to move\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlace against\u003C/span\u003E. The picture shows every scan in the frame as it is now, one color each: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlan\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFront\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESide\u003C/span\u003E; drag to pan, use the wheel to zoom.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBy point pairs.\u003C/strong\u003E Click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPick a pair\u003C/span\u003E, then a point on the scan to move, then the same place on another scan (a corner, a target, the foot of a column). Do this at least three times, with points that are not in one line and are spread apart. Click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlace by pairs\u003C/span\u003E: the scan is placed by a least-squares fit, and \u003Cstrong\u003ELeft over\u003C/strong\u003E shows how far each pair still misses. A pair with a large number is a bad pick: remove it with the cross and place again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBy targets.\u003C/strong\u003E If a point&#039;s true coordinates are known (surveyed targets), click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPick, then type target\u003C/span\u003E, click the point on its scan, and type the target&#039;s X, Y and Z in the row. Pairs and typed targets mix freely.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"refine-with-icp\"\u003ERefine with ICP\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPairs give a rough placement. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERefine\u003C/span\u003E moves the scan until its surfaces sit on the other scan&#039;s: first \u003Cstrong\u003Epoint to point\u003C/strong\u003E, then \u003Cstrong\u003Epoint to plane\u003C/strong\u003E. The table lists every step with its error and how many points matched; the line above it draws the error falling. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStop\u003C/span\u003E ends the run after the step in progress and keeps what it reached. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E takes the whole run (or the last placement) back; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset\u003C/span\u003E puts the scan back at its file coordinates.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo keep it quick on a laptop, ICP works on a decimated share of the points (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPoints used\u003C/span\u003E, 5,000 to 60,000) and ignores matches farther than \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELargest match distance\u003C/span\u003E (by default 5% of the scan&#039;s size, shrinking as it settles). It needs a rough placement first: scans that barely overlap report that they cannot be matched. Surfaces that are only flat or only straight cannot fix a position along themselves; add pairs in that case.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"save-and-export\"\u003ESave and export\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave project\u003C/span\u003E writes a small \u003Ccode\u003E.ccscans.json\u003C/code\u003E file: each scan&#039;s name, size and a fingerprint of its first 64 KB, with its transform. It holds no points. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen project…\u003C/span\u003E finds the scan files by those references (the files must be added first) and puts every transform back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport LAS\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport E57\u003C/span\u003E write one file with every scan in its registered place. Points are never rewritten in the originals; the transform is applied on the way out. With \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERead every point from the files\u003C/span\u003E on, each file is read again for every point (up to 20 million per scan); off, only the points shown in the viewer are written.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow registered cloud\u003C/span\u003E (CadCraft) writes the LAS and attaches it under the drawing at its own coordinates, like any cloud.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENothing is uploaded. Registration works in the files&#039; own units and keeps millimeters: a LAS is written with a scale that fits (down to 0.1 mm), an E57 with doubles. A coordinate system is carried into the LAS only when every scan names the same EPSG code. The E57 writer is our own; it is checked by reading its files back with this reader and with libE57Format.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"What you'll do Several scans of one place (each from its own scanner position) start in their own coordinates. Register them: give each scan one placement so they all sit in one frame, then save that as a project and write the registered cloud as one LAS or E57 file. Place the scans Open the dialog (type REGISTERSCANS (or REGSCAN)). Add scans… takes LAS, LAZ, E57, PLY, PCD, PTX, PTS or XYZ files, or drop them on the dialog. Scans that use different units are refused with a note: convert one first. Choose Scan to move and Place against. The picture shows every scan in the frame as it is now, one color each: Plan, Front or Side; drag to pan, use the wheel to zoom. By point pairs. Click Pick a pair, then a point on the scan to move, then the same place on another scan (a corner, a target, the foot of a column). Do this at least three times, with points that are not in one line and are spread apart. Click Place by pairs: the scan is placed by a least-squares fit, and Left over shows how far each pair still misses. A pair with a large number is a bad pick: remove it with the cross and place again. By targets. If a point's true coordinates are known (surveyed targets), click Pick, then type target, click the point on its scan, and type the target's X, Y and Z in the row. Pairs and typed targets mix freely. Refine with ICP Pairs give a rough placement. Refine moves the scan until its surfaces sit on the other scan's: first point to point, then point to plane. The table lists every step with its error and how many points matched; the line above it draws the error falling. Stop ends the run after the step in progress and keeps what it reached. Undo takes the whole run (or the last placement) back; Reset puts the scan back at its file coordinates. To keep it quick on a laptop, ICP works on a decimated share of the points (Points used, 5,000 to 60,000) and ignores matches farther than Largest match distance (by default 5% of the scan's size, shrinking as it settles). It needs a rough placement first: scans that barely overlap report that they cannot be matched. Surfaces that are only flat or only straight cannot fix a position along themselves; add pairs in that case. Save and export Save project writes a small .ccscans.json file: each scan's name, size and a fingerprint of its first 64 KB, with its transform. It holds no points. Open project… finds the scan files by those references (the files must be added first) and puts every transform back. Export LAS and Export E57 write one file with every scan in its registered place. Points are never rewritten in the originals; the transform is applied on the way out. With Read every point from the files on, each file is read again for every point (up to 20 million per scan); off, only the points shown in the viewer are written. Show registered cloud (CadCraft) writes the LAS and attaches it under the drawing at its own coordinates, like any cloud. Nothing is uploaded. Registration works in the files' own units and keeps millimeters: a LAS is written with a scale that fits (down to 0.1 mm), an E57 with doubles. A coordinate system is carried into the LAS only when every scan names the same EPSG code. The E57 writer is our own; it is checked by reading its files back with this reader and with libE57Format.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"select-objects","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Select objects","summary":"Pick objects one by one, with a window or a crossing, before or during a command, and take objects back out of a selection.","section":"tasks","order":30,"keywords":["select","selection","window","crossing","noun-verb","pick","all","last","previous","fence","remove","add","tab","cycle","overlapping","qselect","quick select","filter","select similar","selectsimilar","shift","deselect","ctrl+a","pickfirst"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"pick-one-object-at-a-time","text":"Pick one object at a time","level":2},{"id":"window-and-crossing","text":"Window and crossing","level":2},{"id":"select-then-run-a-command","text":"Select, then run a command","level":2},{"id":"run-a-command-then-select","text":"Run a command, then select","level":2},{"id":"pick-one-of-several-objects-on-top-of-each-other","text":"Pick one of several objects on top of each other","level":2},{"id":"select-by-a-rule-quick-select","text":"Select by a rule: Quick select","level":2},{"id":"select-similar","text":"Select similar","level":2},{"id":"select-now-use-later","text":"Select now, use later","level":2},{"id":"select-everything","text":"Select everything","level":2},{"id":"what-cannot-be-selected","text":"What cannot be selected","level":2},{"id":"erase-quickly","text":"Erase quickly","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to choose the objects a command works on. CadCraft has two ways to work, and both are fine:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESelect first, then the command\u003C/strong\u003E (noun-verb). Select objects, then type \u003Ccode\u003EM\u003C/code\u003E for Move. The command takes your selection and goes straight on to its next question.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECommand first, then select\u003C/strong\u003E (verb-noun). Type \u003Ccode\u003EM\u003C/code\u003E; the command asks \u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects:\u003C/code\u003E; you select, then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"pick-one-object-at-a-time\"\u003EPick one object at a time\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith no command running, click a line. It is highlighted and its grips (small squares) appear. The right panel shows its properties.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick another object. It is added to the selection.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and click a selected object. It is taken out of the selection.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Nothing is selected.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"window-and-crossing\"\u003EWindow and crossing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick once on empty space, move, and click again: a rectangle. Pressing and dragging draws a freehand lasso instead, with the same two kinds (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/lasso-selection\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/lasso-selection\"\u003ELasso selection\u003C/a\u003E; Settings can make a drag draw the rectangle). Which objects you get depends on the direction:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EYou move\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EBox\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESelects\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELeft to right\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWindow\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOnly objects \u003Cstrong\u003Efully inside\u003C/strong\u003E the box\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERight to left\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECrossing\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EObjects inside the box \u003Cstrong\u003Eand\u003C/strong\u003E objects it touches\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw a few overlapping lines and circles.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick empty space to the left of some objects, move right, and click again. Only the objects completely inside are selected.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick empty space to the right, move left across some objects, and click again. Everything the box touches is selected.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and draw a box or a lasso over some selected objects. They come out of the selection.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"select-then-run-a-command\"\u003ESelect, then run a command\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect two circles.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EM\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe history says \u003Ccode\u003E2 found\u003C/code\u003E, and the prompt goes straight to \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify base point\u003C/code\u003E. Finish the move (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/modify-objects\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/modify-objects\"\u003EMove, copy, rotate, scale and mirror\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen a command ends, the selection is cleared, so the next command starts fresh.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECommands that take a selection this way: \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EERASE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMOVE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECOPY\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EROTATE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESCALE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMIRROR\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXPLODE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EBLOCK\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELIST\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EOFFSET\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EFILLET\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECHAMFER\u003C/code\u003E always ask you to pick objects one at a time instead; \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ETRIM\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXTEND\u003C/code\u003E take a pick or a path you drag through the pieces (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/trim-and-extend\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/trim-and-extend\"\u003ETrim and extend\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"run-a-command-then-select\"\u003ERun a command, then select\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMake sure nothing is selected (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EE\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The prompt reads \u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects:\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick objects, or drag windows and crossings. The history counts each pick (\u003Ccode\u003E1 found\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E3 found\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or right-click to finish selecting.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe objects are erased and the history says how many.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAt a \u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects:\u003C/code\u003E prompt you can also type:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EALL\u003C/code\u003E, then \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: every object you can edit in the open tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003ELAST\u003C/code\u003E), then \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: the most recently drawn object.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E (Previous): the objects the last command worked on, if they are still there.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EF\u003C/code\u003E (Fence): click or type points along a line; every object the line crosses is added. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to end the fence; \u003Ccode\u003EU\u003C/code\u003E takes back its last point.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EW\u003C/code\u003E (Window) or \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E (Crossing): two corners, whichever way you drag.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E (Remove): from now on, clicks, windows and fences take objects \u003Cstrong\u003Eout\u003C/strong\u003E. The prompt reads \u003Ccode\u003ERemove objects:\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E (Add) switches back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E?\u003C/code\u003E: the list of these in the history.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThese options are not shown in the prompt, as in AutoCAD.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"pick-one-of-several-objects-on-top-of-each-other\"\u003EPick one of several objects on top of each other\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhere objects overlap or touch, a click takes the nearest one. To get another:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHold the pointer still over the spot.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The next object there is highlighted, and the status bar says which one it is (&quot;2 of 3: Line on layer Walls&quot;).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again until the one you want is highlighted, then click without moving.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMoving the pointer starts over. This works with no command running, at \u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects:\u003C/code\u003E and wherever a command asks you to pick one object.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"select-by-a-rule-quick-select\"\u003ESelect by a rule: Quick select\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EQSELECT\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EQS\u003C/code\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuick select\u003C/span\u003E) selects every object that matches a rule, such as every circle with a radius under 50, or every block reference called DOOR-900.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuick select\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EObject type\u003C/span\u003E (the list counts each type), then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperty\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayer\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EColor\u003C/span\u003E, and for some types \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERadius\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArea\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EText height\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EText contents\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBlock name\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHatch pattern\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOperator\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EValue\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003E*\u003C/code\u003E matches with wildcards (\u003Ccode\u003EDOOR-*\u003C/code\u003E); \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect all\u003C/span\u003E takes every object of the type. The count at the bottom shows how many match as you choose.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHow to apply\u003C/span\u003E: include the matches, or everything else. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EApply to\u003C/span\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECurrent selection\u003C/span\u003E narrows what you had selected; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAppend to current selection\u003C/span\u003E adds to it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E. Then run the command you want: it uses the selection.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"select-similar\"\u003ESelect similar\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect one or more objects and type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESELECTSIMILAR\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003ESIM\u003C/code\u003E), or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect similar\u003C/span\u003E. Every object of the same type on the same layer and with the same color is selected; block references also need the same block, hatches the same pattern. Type \u003Ccode\u003ESE\u003C/code\u003E at its \u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects\u003C/code\u003E prompt to choose what must match.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"select-now-use-later\"\u003ESelect now, use later\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003ESELECT\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, choose objects with any of the ways above, and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. They stay selected for the next command, and \u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E picks them again at a later \u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects:\u003C/code\u003E prompt.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"select-everything\"\u003ESelect everything\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith no command running, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect all\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E menu, to select every object you can edit on the open tab. If you have typed something at the command line, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E selects that text instead: press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to clear it first. At a \u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects:\u003C/code\u003E prompt, type \u003Ccode\u003EALL\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect none\u003C/span\u003E in the same menu, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, clears the selection.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf you would rather have each click replace the selection (and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E add to it), set \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClicking objects\u003C/span\u003E to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReplaces the selection\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/settings\"\u003ECadCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-cannot-be-selected\"\u003EWhat cannot be selected\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EObjects on a layer that is \u003Cstrong\u003Eoff\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003Efrozen\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003Elocked\u003C/strong\u003E. Locked objects stay visible, and you can still snap to them, but you cannot select or change them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn a sheet tab, the model shown inside a viewport. You select the sheet&#039;s own objects (the title block, notes, the viewport frames). To edit the model, go to the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"erase-quickly\"\u003EErase quickly\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect objects, then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E when the command line does not have the cursor). On a Mac keyboard, the key labeled delete works once you have clicked in the drawing; while the command line has the cursor, press fn and delete together. This runs \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EERASE\u003C/code\u003E on the selection. If nothing happens, you have typed something at the command line: press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or type \u003Ccode\u003EE\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E instead.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe window took too little.\u003C/strong\u003E You dragged left to right, which only takes what is fully inside. Drag right to left for a crossing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA click on empty space started a box.\u003C/strong\u003E One click on empty space starts a window; the second click finishes it. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or right-click to drop it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA click on empty space let go of the selection.\u003C/strong\u003E It does, unless you hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. To keep the selection on such a click and clear it with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E only, set \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClicking an empty spot\u003C/span\u003E to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeeps the selection\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/settings#clicking-an-empty-spot\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/settings\" data-hash=\"clicking-an-empty-spot\"\u003ECadCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe command used objects you did not mean.\u003C/strong\u003E Something was still selected when you started it. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E before a command to start with nothing selected.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENothing is selected after a command.\u003C/strong\u003E That is on purpose: a finished command clears the selection.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to choose the objects a command works on. CadCraft has two ways to work, and both are fine: Select first, then the command (noun-verb). Select objects, then type M for Move. The command takes your selection and goes straight on to its next question. Command first, then select (verb-noun). Type M; the command asks Select objects:; you select, then press Enter. Pick one object at a time With no command running, click a line. It is highlighted and its grips (small squares) appear. The right panel shows its properties. Click another object. It is added to the selection. Hold Shift and click a selected object. It is taken out of the selection. Press Esc. Nothing is selected. Window and crossing Click once on empty space, move, and click again: a rectangle. Pressing and dragging draws a freehand lasso instead, with the same two kinds (see Lasso selection; Settings can make a drag draw the rectangle). Which objects you get depends on the direction: You move Box Selects Left to right Window Only objects fully inside the box Right to left Crossing Objects inside the box and objects it touches Draw a few overlapping lines and circles. Click empty space to the left of some objects, move right, and click again. Only the objects completely inside are selected. Press Esc. Click empty space to the right, move left across some objects, and click again. Everything the box touches is selected. Hold Shift and draw a box or a lasso over some selected objects. They come out of the selection. Select, then run a command Select two circles. Type M and press Enter. The history says 2 found, and the prompt goes straight to Specify base point. Finish the move (see Move, copy, rotate, scale and mirror). When a command ends, the selection is cleared, so the next command starts fresh. Commands that take a selection this way: ERASE, MOVE, COPY, ROTATE, SCALE, MIRROR, EXPLODE, BLOCK and LIST. OFFSET, FILLET and CHAMFER always ask you to pick objects one at a time instead; TRIM and EXTEND take a pick or a path you drag through the pieces (see Trim and extend). Run a command, then select Make sure nothing is selected (Esc). Type E and press Enter. The prompt reads Select objects:. Click objects, or drag windows and crossings. The history counts each pick (1 found, 3 found). Press Enter, press Space or right-click to finish selecting. The objects are erased and the history says how many. At a Select objects: prompt you can also type: ALL, then Enter: every object you can edit in the open tab. L (or LAST), then Enter: the most recently drawn object. P (Previous): the objects the last command worked on, if they are still there. F (Fence): click or type points along a line; every object the line crosses is added. Press Enter to end the fence; U takes back its last point. W (Window) or C (Crossing): two corners, whichever way you drag. R (Remove): from now on, clicks, windows and fences take objects out. The prompt reads Remove objects:. A (Add) switches back. ?: the list of these in the history. These options are not shown in the prompt, as in AutoCAD. Pick one of several objects on top of each other Where objects overlap or touch, a click takes the nearest one. To get another: Hold the pointer still over the spot. Press Tab. The next object there is highlighted, and the status bar says which one it is (\"2 of 3: Line on layer Walls\"). Press Tab again until the one you want is highlighted, then click without moving. Moving the pointer starts over. This works with no command running, at Select objects: and wherever a command asks you to pick one object. Select by a rule: Quick select QSELECT (QS, or Edit › More › Quick select) selects every object that matches a rule, such as every circle with a radius under 50, or every block reference called DOOR-900. Open Quick select. Choose Object type (the list counts each type), then Property: Layer, Color, and for some types Length, Radius, Area, Text height, Text contents, Block name or Hatch pattern. Choose Operator and Value. * matches with wildcards (DOOR-*); Select all takes every object of the type. The count at the bottom shows how many match as you choose. How to apply: include the matches, or everything else. Apply to Current selection narrows what you had selected; Append to current selection adds to it. Click Select. Then run the command you want: it uses the selection. Select similar Select one or more objects and type SELECTSIMILAR (SIM), or choose Edit › More › Select similar. Every object of the same type on the same layer and with the same color is selected; block references also need the same block, hatches the same pattern. Type SE at its Select objects prompt to choose what must match. Select now, use later Type SELECT and press Enter, choose objects with any of the ways above, and press Enter. They stay selected for the next command, and P picks them again at a later Select objects: prompt. Select everything With no command running, press Ctrl+A, or choose Select all in the toolbar's Edit › More menu, to select every object you can edit on the open tab. If you have typed something at the command line, Ctrl+A selects that text instead: press Esc to clear it first. At a Select objects: prompt, type ALL. Select none in the same menu, or Esc, clears the selection. If you would rather have each click replace the selection (and Shift add to it), set Clicking objects to Replaces the selection in CadCraft settings. What cannot be selected Objects on a layer that is off, frozen or locked. Locked objects stay visible, and you can still snap to them, but you cannot select or change them. On a sheet tab, the model shown inside a viewport. You select the sheet's own objects (the title block, notes, the viewport frames). To edit the model, go to the Model tab. Erase quickly Select objects, then press Delete (or Backspace when the command line does not have the cursor). On a Mac keyboard, the key labeled delete works once you have clicked in the drawing; while the command line has the cursor, press fn and delete together. This runs ERASE on the selection. If nothing happens, you have typed something at the command line: press Esc, or type E and press Enter instead. Common mistakes The window took too little. You dragged left to right, which only takes what is fully inside. Drag right to left for a crossing. A click on empty space started a box. One click on empty space starts a window; the second click finishes it. Press Esc or right-click to drop it. A click on empty space let go of the selection. It does, unless you hold Shift. To keep the selection on such a click and clear it with Esc only, set Clicking an empty spot to Keeps the selection in CadCraft settings. The command used objects you did not mean. Something was still selected when you started it. Press Esc before a command to start with nothing selected. Nothing is selected after a command. That is on purpose: a finished command clears the selection.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"lasso-selection","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Lasso selection","summary":"Press and drag a freehand outline round objects to select them, as a window, a crossing or a fence; switch with Space while you drag.","section":"tasks","order":31,"keywords":["lasso","lassomode","drag","freehand","select","selection","window","crossing","fence","space","polygon","touch","pen","rectangle"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"draw-a-lasso","text":"Draw a lasso","level":2},{"id":"window-crossing-and-fence","text":"Window, crossing and fence","level":2},{"id":"inside-a-command","text":"Inside a command","level":2},{"id":"the-rectangle","text":"The rectangle","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to select objects by drawing round them, and how to get the rectangle back when you want it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-a-lasso\"\u003EDraw a lasso\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw a few lines and circles close together.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith no command running, press the mouse button on an empty spot, and drag round some of them, moving \u003Cstrong\u003Eright\u003C/strong\u003E first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELet go.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOnly the objects wholly inside the outline are selected. The outline is drawn solid while you drag: this is a window lasso.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"window-crossing-and-fence\"\u003EWindow, crossing and fence\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhich objects you get depends on the first move of your drag, and on \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EYou drag\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ELasso\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESelects\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERight first\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWindow (solid outline)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOnly objects \u003Cstrong\u003Ewholly inside\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELeft first\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECrossing (dashed, green)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EObjects inside \u003Cstrong\u003Eand\u003C/strong\u003E objects the outline touches\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while dragging\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWindow, then crossing, then fence\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFence (a dashed path, not closed): only objects the path crosses\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress on an empty spot and drag round some objects, moving \u003Cstrong\u003Eleft\u003C/strong\u003E first. Everything the outline touches is selected too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress and drag again; while still holding the button, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E once. The outline turns into a crossing; press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again for a fence.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while you let go takes the objects out of the selection instead (with &quot;Clicking objects: Adds&quot; in Settings).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"inside-a-command\"\u003EInside a command\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA lasso works wherever CadCraft asks \u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects\u003C/code\u003E: draw it, then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to finish. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESTRETCH\u003C/code\u003E takes a crossing lasso as its window, so you can stretch an L-shaped part of a drawing in one go.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ETRIM\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXTEND\u003C/code\u003E use a drag differently: there it trims (or extends) every piece the path crosses. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/trim-and-extend\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/trim-and-extend\"\u003ETrim and extend\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-rectangle\"\u003EThe rectangle\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA click on empty space, a move, and a second click always draws the rectangle window (left to right) or crossing (right to left).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo make a drag draw the rectangle again, open \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDragging to select\u003C/span\u003E and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERectangle\u003C/span\u003E, or type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELASSOMODE\u003C/code\u003E, then \u003Ccode\u003E0\u003C/code\u003E. Type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELASSOMODE\u003C/code\u003E, then \u003Ccode\u003E1\u003C/code\u003E for the lasso. The choice is kept in this browser.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:touch\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ETouch\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDrag a finger or a pen the same way. A tap still picks one object.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENothing was selected.\u003C/strong\u003E The lasso was a window and nothing lay wholly inside. Start the drag to the left, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while dragging.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe drag moved a grip.\u003C/strong\u003E Pressing on a selected object&#039;s grip moves the grip. Start the lasso on an empty spot.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESpace ended the command.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E switches the lasso only while the button is held down; otherwise it is Enter.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to select objects by drawing round them, and how to get the rectangle back when you want it. Draw a lasso Draw a few lines and circles close together. With no command running, press the mouse button on an empty spot, and drag round some of them, moving right first. Let go. Only the objects wholly inside the outline are selected. The outline is drawn solid while you drag: this is a window lasso. Window, crossing and fence Which objects you get depends on the first move of your drag, and on Space: You drag Lasso Selects Right first Window (solid outline) Only objects wholly inside Left first Crossing (dashed, green) Objects inside and objects the outline touches Press Space while dragging Window, then crossing, then fence Fence (a dashed path, not closed): only objects the path crosses Press on an empty spot and drag round some objects, moving left first. Everything the outline touches is selected too. Press Esc. Press and drag again; while still holding the button, press Space once. The outline turns into a crossing; press Space again for a fence. Shift while you let go takes the objects out of the selection instead (with \"Clicking objects: Adds\" in Settings). Inside a command A lasso works wherever CadCraft asks Select objects: draw it, then press Enter to finish. STRETCH takes a crossing lasso as its window, so you can stretch an L-shaped part of a drawing in one go. TRIM and EXTEND use a drag differently: there it trims (or extends) every piece the path crosses. See Trim and extend. The rectangle A click on empty space, a move, and a second click always draws the rectangle window (left to right) or crossing (right to left). To make a drag draw the rectangle again, open Settings › CadCraft › Dragging to select and choose Rectangle, or type LASSOMODE, then 0. Type LASSOMODE, then 1 for the lasso. The choice is kept in this browser. Drag a finger or a pen the same way. A tap still picks one object. Common mistakes Nothing was selected. The lasso was a window and nothing lay wholly inside. Start the drag to the left, or press Space while dragging. The drag moved a grip. Pressing on a selected object's grip moves the grip. Start the lasso on an empty spot. Space ended the command. Space switches the lasso only while the button is held down; otherwise it is Enter.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"modify-objects","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Move, copy, rotate, scale, mirror, stretch and array","summary":"Lay out furniture in a room with the basic modify commands, using base points, typed distances and undo.","section":"tasks","order":40,"keywords":["move","copy","rotate","scale","mirror","stretch","array","join","matchprop","match properties","painter","arrayrect","arraypolar","polar array","erase","explode","undo","redo","base point","displacement","modify","align","distribute","line up","space evenly"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-make","text":"What you will make","level":2},{"id":"draw-the-pieces","text":"Draw the pieces","level":2},{"id":"move-base-point-then-second-point","text":"Move: base point, then second point","level":2},{"id":"copy-as-many-times-as-you-like","text":"Copy, as many times as you like","level":2},{"id":"mirror-the-row-to-the-other-side","text":"Mirror the row to the other side","level":2},{"id":"rotate-a-chair","text":"Rotate a chair","level":2},{"id":"scale-the-table","text":"Scale the table","level":2},{"id":"undo-and-redo","text":"Undo and redo","level":2},{"id":"stretch-a-room-wider","text":"Stretch a room wider","level":2},{"id":"array-rows-and-columns-or-round-a-center","text":"Array: rows and columns, or round a center","level":2},{"id":"join-pieces-into-one-polyline","text":"Join pieces into one polyline","level":2},{"id":"match-properties","text":"Match properties","level":2},{"id":"align-objects","text":"Align objects","level":2},{"id":"erase-and-explode","text":"Erase and explode","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-make\"\u003EWhat you will make\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA table with one chair, copied round the table, turned, mirrored and resized. Along the way you use \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMOVE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECOPY\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EROTATE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMIRROR\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESCALE\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EUNDO\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-the-pieces\"\u003EDraw the pieces\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EREC\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, type \u003Ccode\u003E0,0\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, then type \u003Ccode\u003E@1800,900\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. This is the table.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EREC\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, type \u003Ccode\u003E300,-700\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, then type \u003Ccode\u003E@450,450\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. This is a chair, below the table.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExtents\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"move-base-point-then-second-point\"\u003EMove: base point, then second point\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery move, copy, rotate and scale starts from a \u003Cstrong\u003Ebase point\u003C/strong\u003E: the point that is carried along, or turned about.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the chair to select it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EM\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The history says \u003Ccode\u003E1 found\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify base point\u003C/code\u003E, snap to the chair&#039;s upper-left corner (Endpoint) and click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify second point\u003C/code\u003E, type \u003Ccode\u003E@0,200\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe chair moves up 200, closer to the table.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003ED\u003C/code\u003E at \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify base point\u003C/code\u003E to give a displacement instead: CadCraft then asks \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify displacement\u003C/code\u003E, and you type how far across and up, such as \u003Ccode\u003E0,200\u003C/code\u003E (no \u003Ccode\u003E@\u003C/code\u003E needed).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"copy-as-many-times-as-you-like\"\u003ECopy, as many times as you like\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the chair.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003ECO\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESnap to the chair&#039;s lower-left corner and click as the base point.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E@600,0\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. A copy appears 600 to the right.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E@600,0\u003C/code\u003E again and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. A second copy appears 1200 to the right of the original.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to end the command.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECOPY\u003C/code\u003E keeps going until you press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Each copy is placed by the distance from the base point to the point you give. An \u003Ccode\u003E@\u003C/code\u003E point counts from the last point you entered, which after the first copy is that copy&#039;s point, so \u003Ccode\u003E@600,0\u003C/code\u003E twice gives copies at 600 and 1200. Type \u003Ccode\u003EU\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to take back the last copy without leaving the command.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou should see three chairs in a row below the table.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECOPY\u003C/code\u003E is for copies inside one drawing, placed by a base point. To carry objects to another drawing or tab, or to paste them later, use the clipboard: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, then \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E makes one copy that moves with the pointer. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/copy-paste\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/copy-paste\"\u003ECopy, paste and duplicate\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mirror-the-row-to-the-other-side\"\u003EMirror the row to the other side\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag a crossing (right to left) over the three chairs, without touching the table.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EMI\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify first point of mirror line\u003C/code\u003E, snap to the Midpoint of the table&#039;s left side and click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify second point of mirror line\u003C/code\u003E, snap to the Midpoint of the table&#039;s right side and click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003EErase source objects? [Yes/No] &lt;N&gt;\u003C/code\u003E, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to keep them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou should see three chairs below the table and three mirrored chairs above it. Text in a mirrored selection stays readable: it is placed mirrored but not written backwards.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"rotate-a-chair\"\u003ERotate a chair\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the rightmost chair below the table.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003ERO\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESnap to the chair&#039;s lower-right corner and click as the base point.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E90\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe chair turns 90 degrees counterclockwise about the base point. A negative angle turns clockwise. Instead of typing, you can click a second point: the angle is measured from the base point to that point.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"scale-the-table\"\u003EScale the table\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the table.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003ESC\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESnap to the table&#039;s lower-left corner and click as the base point.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E1.2\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe table is 20% bigger, growing away from the base point. A factor below 1 makes it smaller.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf you click a point instead of typing the scale factor, the factor is the distance from the base point to that point. Clicking one drawing unit away gives a factor of 1. Typing the number is usually what you want.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"undo-and-redo\"\u003EUndo and redo\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E button, or \u003Ccode\u003EU\u003C/code\u003E then \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes the last command. A whole command is one step: undoing a \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECOPY\u003C/code\u003E that made five copies removes all five.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command Z\"\u003E⇧⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedo\u003C/span\u003E button, or \u003Ccode\u003EREDO\u003C/code\u003E brings it back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe history says what was undone (&quot;Undid COPY&quot;).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECadCraft keeps the last 500 steps. Layer settings (on, freeze, lock, color) are settings, not edits, and are not undone.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"stretch-a-room-wider\"\u003EStretch a room wider\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESTRETCH\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStretch\u003C/span\u003E) moves only what lies inside a crossing window: corners and line ends inside it move, everything else stays put, so the room gets wider instead of moving.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag a window from right to left over the right-hand wall, so it covers the wall&#039;s corners and the ends of anything meeting it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a base point, then type \u003Ccode\u003E@600,0\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe rectangle is 600 wider; lines that ended on the wall still end on it. An arc with one end inside keeps its height, circles and text move when their center or insertion point is inside, and a dimension measures its new length. Objects picked one by one, without a window, simply move.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou can also select first: drag the crossing window (or a crossing lasso) over the wall with no command running, then type \u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E. STRETCH takes that selection and stretches what its windows hold, just as if you had dragged them at its own prompt.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"array-rows-and-columns-or-round-a-center\"\u003EArray: rows and columns, or round a center\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EARRAY\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EAR\u003C/code\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArray\u003C/span\u003E) copies the selection in a grid or in a circle.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect a chair and type \u003Ccode\u003EAR\u003C/code\u003E, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for Rectangular.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA preview of 4 columns and 3 rows appears. Type \u003Ccode\u003ECOU\u003C/code\u003E, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, then \u003Ccode\u003E5\u003C/code\u003E for columns and \u003Ccode\u003E2\u003C/code\u003E for rows.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, then \u003Ccode\u003E600\u003C/code\u003E between columns and \u003Ccode\u003E-900\u003C/code\u003E between rows (negative goes down).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to make the copies.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor a ring of chairs round a table, choose \u003Ccode\u003EPO\u003C/code\u003E (Polar) instead, click the table&#039;s center, and set \u003Ccode\u003EI\u003C/code\u003E (items), \u003Ccode\u003EF\u003C/code\u003E (fill angle: 360 for all the way round, 180 for half) and \u003Ccode\u003EROT\u003C/code\u003E (whether the copies turn to face the center). \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EARRAYRECT\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EARRAYPOLAR\u003C/code\u003E skip the question.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"join-pieces-into-one-polyline\"\u003EJoin pieces into one polyline\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EJOIN\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EJ\u003C/code\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModify\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJoin\u003C/span\u003E): select lines, arcs and open polylines whose ends meet, and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. They become one polyline, closed when they go all the way round, which you can then offset in one go. Lines that lie on one line become one line even with gaps between them; arcs of one circle become one arc, or the circle.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"match-properties\"\u003EMatch properties\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMATCHPROP\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EMA\u003C/code\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModify\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMatch properties\u003C/span\u003E): click the object whose look you want, then select the objects to change and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. They take its layer and color; a text takes its height, a dimension its sizes, a hatch its pattern. Type \u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E (Settings) at the second question to turn any of these off.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"align-objects\"\u003EAlign objects\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBy points.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EALIGN\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EAL\u003C/code\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModify\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAlign\u003C/span\u003E): select the objects and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, then click a point on them (the source) and where it should go (the destination).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOne pair moves them. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E at \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify second source point\u003C/code\u003E to stop there.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETwo pairs also turn them, so the line between the two source points lies along the line between the two destination points. CadCraft then asks \u003Ccode\u003EScale objects based on alignment points?\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003EY\u003C/code\u003E makes them that much bigger or smaller too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA third pair mirrors them when its two points are on opposite sides of that line.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEdges and spacing.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModify\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAlign and distribute\u003C/span\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003EALIGNPANEL\u003C/code\u003E) opens a small panel over the drawing. Select objects, then:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Epick what to line up with in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAlign to\u003C/span\u003E (the whole selection, the first or the last object you picked, or a point you click) and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELeft\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECenter\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERight\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMiddle\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBottom\u003C/span\u003E;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eor pick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEqual gaps\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEqual centres\u003C/span\u003E and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAcross\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUp and down\u003C/span\u003E: the outer two objects stay, the rest are spaced evenly between them (three objects at least).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach click is one undo step, and the selection stays, so you can try another. Each object counts by its bounding box, in the UCS when one is set. On the command line the same is \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EALIGNTO\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDISTRIBUTE\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"erase-and-explode\"\u003EErase and explode\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EERASE\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EE\u003C/code\u003E, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) removes the selected objects.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXPLODE\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EX\u003C/code\u003E) breaks a polyline into lines and arcs, a block reference into its objects, and a dimension into lines, arrowheads and text. Exploded dimensions no longer measure themselves.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETyping a distance without \u003Ccode\u003E@\u003C/code\u003E at the second point.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E600,0\u003C/code\u003E is the absolute point 600,0, not &quot;600 to the right&quot;. Use \u003Ccode\u003E@600,0\u003C/code\u003E, or a bare \u003Ccode\u003E600\u003C/code\u003E with the crosshair pointing the right way.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe rotation went the wrong way.\u003C/strong\u003E Positive angles turn counterclockwise.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo removed more than expected.\u003C/strong\u003E One undo takes back the whole last command, including every copy it made.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe command did not ask to select objects.\u003C/strong\u003E Objects were already selected, so it used them. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E first to start with nothing selected.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will make A table with one chair, copied round the table, turned, mirrored and resized. Along the way you use MOVE, COPY, ROTATE, MIRROR, SCALE and UNDO. Draw the pieces Type REC and press Enter, type 0,0 and press Enter, then type @1800,900 and press Enter. This is the table. Type REC and press Enter, type 300,-700 and press Enter, then type @450,450 and press Enter. This is a chair, below the table. Click Extents. Move: base point, then second point Every move, copy, rotate and scale starts from a base point: the point that is carried along, or turned about. Click the chair to select it. Type M and press Enter. The history says 1 found. At Specify base point, snap to the chair's upper-left corner (Endpoint) and click. At Specify second point, type @0,200 and press Enter. The chair moves up 200, closer to the table. Type D at Specify base point to give a displacement instead: CadCraft then asks Specify displacement, and you type how far across and up, such as 0,200 (no @ needed). Copy, as many times as you like Click the chair. Type CO and press Enter. Snap to the chair's lower-left corner and click as the base point. Type @600,0 and press Enter. A copy appears 600 to the right. Type @600,0 again and press Enter. A second copy appears 1200 to the right of the original. Press Enter to end the command. COPY keeps going until you press Enter. Each copy is placed by the distance from the base point to the point you give. An @ point counts from the last point you entered, which after the first copy is that copy's point, so @600,0 twice gives copies at 600 and 1200. Type U and press Enter to take back the last copy without leaving the command. You should see three chairs in a row below the table. COPY is for copies inside one drawing, placed by a base point. To carry objects to another drawing or tab, or to paste them later, use the clipboard: Ctrl+C, then Ctrl+V. Ctrl+D makes one copy that moves with the pointer. See Copy, paste and duplicate. Mirror the row to the other side Drag a crossing (right to left) over the three chairs, without touching the table. Type MI and press Enter. At Specify first point of mirror line, snap to the Midpoint of the table's left side and click. At Specify second point of mirror line, snap to the Midpoint of the table's right side and click. At Erase source objects? [Yes/No] \u003CN\u003E, press Enter to keep them. You should see three chairs below the table and three mirrored chairs above it. Text in a mirrored selection stays readable: it is placed mirrored but not written backwards. Rotate a chair Click the rightmost chair below the table. Type RO and press Enter. Snap to the chair's lower-right corner and click as the base point. Type 90 and press Enter. The chair turns 90 degrees counterclockwise about the base point. A negative angle turns clockwise. Instead of typing, you can click a second point: the angle is measured from the base point to that point. Scale the table Select the table. Type SC and press Enter. Snap to the table's lower-left corner and click as the base point. Type 1.2 and press Enter. The table is 20% bigger, growing away from the base point. A factor below 1 makes it smaller. If you click a point instead of typing the scale factor, the factor is the distance from the base point to that point. Clicking one drawing unit away gives a factor of 1. Typing the number is usually what you want. Undo and redo Ctrl+Z, the Undo button, or U then Enter undoes the last command. A whole command is one step: undoing a COPY that made five copies removes all five. Ctrl+Y, Ctrl+Shift+Z, the Redo button, or REDO brings it back. The history says what was undone (\"Undid COPY\"). CadCraft keeps the last 500 steps. Layer settings (on, freeze, lock, color) are settings, not edits, and are not undone. Stretch a room wider STRETCH (S, or Stretch) moves only what lies inside a crossing window: corners and line ends inside it move, everything else stays put, so the room gets wider instead of moving. Type S and press Enter. Drag a window from right to left over the right-hand wall, so it covers the wall's corners and the ends of anything meeting it. Press Enter. Click a base point, then type @600,0 and press Enter. The rectangle is 600 wider; lines that ended on the wall still end on it. An arc with one end inside keeps its height, circles and text move when their center or insertion point is inside, and a dimension measures its new length. Objects picked one by one, without a window, simply move. You can also select first: drag the crossing window (or a crossing lasso) over the wall with no command running, then type S. STRETCH takes that selection and stretches what its windows hold, just as if you had dragged them at its own prompt. Array: rows and columns, or round a center ARRAY (AR, or Array) copies the selection in a grid or in a circle. Select a chair and type AR, press Enter. Press Enter for Rectangular. A preview of 4 columns and 3 rows appears. Type COU, press Enter, then 5 for columns and 2 for rows. Type S, press Enter, then 600 between columns and -900 between rows (negative goes down). Press Enter to make the copies. For a ring of chairs round a table, choose PO (Polar) instead, click the table's center, and set I (items), F (fill angle: 360 for all the way round, 180 for half) and ROT (whether the copies turn to face the center). ARRAYRECT and ARRAYPOLAR skip the question. Join pieces into one polyline JOIN (J, or Modify › More › Join): select lines, arcs and open polylines whose ends meet, and press Enter. They become one polyline, closed when they go all the way round, which you can then offset in one go. Lines that lie on one line become one line even with gaps between them; arcs of one circle become one arc, or the circle. Match properties MATCHPROP (MA, or Modify › More › Match properties): click the object whose look you want, then select the objects to change and press Enter. They take its layer and color; a text takes its height, a dimension its sizes, a hatch its pattern. Type S (Settings) at the second question to turn any of these off. Align objects By points. ALIGN (AL, or Modify › More › Align): select the objects and press Enter, then click a point on them (the source) and where it should go (the destination). One pair moves them. Press Enter at Specify second source point to stop there. Two pairs also turn them, so the line between the two source points lies along the line between the two destination points. CadCraft then asks Scale objects based on alignment points?: Y makes them that much bigger or smaller too. A third pair mirrors them when its two points are on opposite sides of that line. Edges and spacing. Modify › More › Align and distribute (or ALIGNPANEL) opens a small panel over the drawing. Select objects, then: pick what to line up with in Align to (the whole selection, the first or the last object you picked, or a point you click) and click Left, Center, Right, Top, Middle or Bottom; or pick Equal gaps or Equal centres and click Across or Up and down: the outer two objects stay, the rest are spaced evenly between them (three objects at least). Each click is one undo step, and the selection stays, so you can try another. Each object counts by its bounding box, in the UCS when one is set. On the command line the same is ALIGNTO and DISTRIBUTE. Erase and explode ERASE (E, or Delete) removes the selected objects. EXPLODE (X) breaks a polyline into lines and arcs, a block reference into its objects, and a dimension into lines, arrowheads and text. Exploded dimensions no longer measure themselves. Common mistakes Typing a distance without @ at the second point. 600,0 is the absolute point 600,0, not \"600 to the right\". Use @600,0, or a bare 600 with the crosshair pointing the right way. The rotation went the wrong way. Positive angles turn counterclockwise. Undo removed more than expected. One undo takes back the whole last command, including every copy it made. The command did not ask to select objects. Objects were already selected, so it used them. Press Esc first to start with nothing selected.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"copy-paste","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Copy, paste and duplicate","summary":"Copy objects to CadCraft's clipboard, paste them where you click or in place, duplicate with the pointer, and bring work across from another drawing or tab.","section":"tasks","order":45,"keywords":["copy","cut","paste","paste in place","duplicate","clipboard","copyclip","cutclip","pasteclip","pasteorig","copybase","ctrl c","ctrl v","ctrl x","ctrl d","between drawings","another tab","base point"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"copy-and-paste-where-you-click","text":"Copy and paste where you click","level":2},{"id":"paste-in-place","text":"Paste in place","level":2},{"id":"duplicate-and-move","text":"Duplicate and move","level":2},{"id":"cut","text":"Cut","level":2},{"id":"between-drawings-and-tabs","text":"Between drawings and tabs","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to copy objects to the clipboard and paste them where you click, paste them at their own coordinates, duplicate them with the pointer, cut them, and carry them into another drawing. Everything here is also in the toolbar&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E group: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E and the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E menu.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"copy-and-paste-where-you-click\"\u003ECopy and paste where you click\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the objects: click them, or drag a window.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E group. The command line says how many objects were copied. They stay selected.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E. The copy appears on the cursor, hanging from the lower-left corner of the copied objects.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick where that corner goes, or type a point such as \u003Ccode\u003E2000,500\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Object snaps and Ortho work as usual.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe pasted objects are selected, so you can move or change them straight away. One \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes the whole paste away.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWant the copy to hang from a point you choose, such as the corner of a wall? Type \u003Ccode\u003ECOPYBASE\u003C/code\u003E, click the base point, select the objects, and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Pasting then places that point where you click. To hang every copy from its center instead of its lower-left corner, change \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste point\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/settings\"\u003ECadCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"paste-in-place\"\u003EPaste in place\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command V\"\u003E⇧⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste in place\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E menu. The copy goes in at exactly the coordinates it was copied from, with no click. Use it to carry objects into another drawing at the same place, or from a sheet into another sheet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"duplicate-and-move\"\u003EDuplicate and move\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the objects.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDuplicate and move\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E menu.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA copy sits on top of the original and moves with the pointer. Click where it goes. You can also type a displacement such as \u003Ccode\u003E@0,-600\u003C/code\u003E, or a distance toward the cursor.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E before the click leaves nothing behind. The duplicate is one undo step and does not touch the clipboard.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"cut\"\u003ECut\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command X\"\u003E⌘X\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+X\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E menu. The objects go on the clipboard and are erased from the drawing, in one undo step. Paste them back with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command V\"\u003E⇧⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"between-drawings-and-tabs\"\u003EBetween drawings and tabs\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe clipboard keeps its copy when you open another drawing, and it is shared by every CadCraft tab in this browser. Copy in one tab, then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E in another.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EBlocks the objects use come with them. If the drawing you paste into already has a block of the same name, its own definition is used, as AutoCAD does.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELayers the objects use are added, with the color and settings they had.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe copy is not rescaled when the two drawings use different units: CadCraft says so, and you can use \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESCALE\u003C/code\u003E afterwards.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EViewports stay on their sheet; they are left out of a copy.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe clipboard is CadCraft&#039;s own. You cannot paste CadCraft objects into another program, or paste from another program into CadCraft: save as DWG or DXF instead. A very large copy may be too big to share between tabs; the command line then says only this tab can paste it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E pasted text into the command line.\u003C/strong\u003E You had typed something there, so the keys worked on the text. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to clear it, then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENothing happened, and the status bar says to finish the current command.\u003C/strong\u003E Another command is running. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe paste landed far away.\u003C/strong\u003E It hangs from the lower-left corner of what you copied, which may be a distant object you selected by accident. Use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste in place\u003C/span\u003E, or copy with \u003Ccode\u003ECOPYBASE\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;Nothing pasted&quot; with a reason.\u003C/strong\u003E What another tab left on the clipboard could not be read (it was damaged, or changed by hand). Copy the objects again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee the full list of keys in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/keyboard\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/keyboard\"\u003EKeyboard and mouse\u003C/a\u003E, and every prompt in the \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/commands#clipboard\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/commands\" data-hash=\"clipboard\"\u003ECommand reference\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to copy objects to the clipboard and paste them where you click, paste them at their own coordinates, duplicate them with the pointer, cut them, and carry them into another drawing. Everything here is also in the toolbar's Edit group: Copy, Paste and the More menu. Copy and paste where you click Select the objects: click them, or drag a window. Press Ctrl+C, or click Copy in the Edit group. The command line says how many objects were copied. They stay selected. Press Ctrl+V, or click Paste. The copy appears on the cursor, hanging from the lower-left corner of the copied objects. Click where that corner goes, or type a point such as 2000,500 and press Enter. Object snaps and Ortho work as usual. The pasted objects are selected, so you can move or change them straight away. One Ctrl+Z takes the whole paste away. Want the copy to hang from a point you choose, such as the corner of a wall? Type COPYBASE, click the base point, select the objects, and press Enter. Pasting then places that point where you click. To hang every copy from its center instead of its lower-left corner, change Paste point in CadCraft settings. Paste in place Press Ctrl+Shift+V, or choose Paste in place in the More menu. The copy goes in at exactly the coordinates it was copied from, with no click. Use it to carry objects into another drawing at the same place, or from a sheet into another sheet. Duplicate and move Select the objects. Press Ctrl+D, or choose Duplicate and move in the More menu. A copy sits on top of the original and moves with the pointer. Click where it goes. You can also type a displacement such as @0,-600, or a distance toward the cursor. Esc before the click leaves nothing behind. The duplicate is one undo step and does not touch the clipboard. Cut Press Ctrl+X, or choose Cut in the More menu. The objects go on the clipboard and are erased from the drawing, in one undo step. Paste them back with Ctrl+V or Ctrl+Shift+V. Between drawings and tabs The clipboard keeps its copy when you open another drawing, and it is shared by every CadCraft tab in this browser. Copy in one tab, then press Ctrl+V in another. Blocks the objects use come with them. If the drawing you paste into already has a block of the same name, its own definition is used, as AutoCAD does. Layers the objects use are added, with the color and settings they had. The copy is not rescaled when the two drawings use different units: CadCraft says so, and you can use SCALE afterwards. Viewports stay on their sheet; they are left out of a copy. The clipboard is CadCraft's own. You cannot paste CadCraft objects into another program, or paste from another program into CadCraft: save as DWG or DXF instead. A very large copy may be too big to share between tabs; the command line then says only this tab can paste it. Common mistakes Ctrl+V pasted text into the command line. You had typed something there, so the keys worked on the text. Press Esc to clear it, then press Ctrl+V again. Nothing happened, and the status bar says to finish the current command. Another command is running. Press Esc first. The paste landed far away. It hangs from the lower-left corner of what you copied, which may be a distant object you selected by accident. Use Paste in place, or copy with COPYBASE. \"Nothing pasted\" with a reason. What another tab left on the clipboard could not be read (it was damaged, or changed by hand). Copy the objects again. See the full list of keys in Keyboard and mouse, and every prompt in the Command reference.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"split-screen","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Split the screen","summary":"Show the drawing in two, three or four panes with their own zoom, put a sheet beside the model, and open several drawings side by side.","section":"tasks","order":46,"keywords":["split","split screen","panes","vports","viewports","tiled","tile","side by side","stacked","two drawings","tabs","document tabs","compare","divider","model and sheet","several drawings","open drawings"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"split-one-drawing-into-panes","text":"Split one drawing into panes","level":2},{"id":"draw-from-one-pane-into-another","text":"Draw from one pane into another","level":2},{"id":"the-model-beside-a-sheet","text":"The model beside a sheet","level":2},{"id":"several-drawings-in-one-window","text":"Several drawings in one window","level":2},{"id":"pan-and-zoom-together","text":"Pan and zoom together","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to look at one drawing in several panes at once, each with its own zoom, and how to open several drawings and see them side by side.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"split-one-drawing-into-panes\"\u003ESplit one drawing into panes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen a drawing, or draw a few lines and circles.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESplit side by side\u003C/span\u003E (or type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EVPORTS\u003C/code\u003E, then \u003Ccode\u003E2\u003C/code\u003E, then \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe drawing area splits into two panes, both showing the same view. The active pane has a blue frame and gets the crosshair.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick in the other pane. It becomes active. (That first click only switches panes; click again to pick.)\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EZoom with the wheel. Only that pane zooms.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag the line between the panes to make one wider. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ←\"\u003Efn ←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EHome\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E on a focused divider puts it back in the middle.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EVPORTS\u003C/code\u003E also takes \u003Ccode\u003E3\u003C/code\u003E (two panes on one side and a big one on the other: \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E), \u003Ccode\u003E4\u003C/code\u003E, and \u003Ccode\u003ESI\u003C/code\u003E for one pane again. The active pane&#039;s view then fills the area. On a sheet, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EVPORTS\u003C/code\u003E adds a viewport to the sheet instead (as AutoCAD); type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESPLIT\u003C/code\u003E there, or use the View menu.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-from-one-pane-into-another\"\u003EDraw from one pane into another\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESplit side by side, and zoom each pane onto a different corner of the drawing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, and click the first point in the left pane.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick once in the right pane to make it active, then click the second point there.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe line runs between the two places. A command goes on as you change panes, and the rubber band shows in every pane.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-model-beside-a-sheet\"\u003EThe model beside a sheet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESplit side by side.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the right pane, then click a sheet&#039;s tab under the drawing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe right pane shows the sheet; the left one still shows the model. Each pane keeps its own space, so you can draw on the model and watch the sheet&#039;s viewport follow.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"several-drawings-in-one-window\"\u003ESeveral drawings in one window\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen a drawing. Then open another (or press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew\u003C/span\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach drawing opens in its own tab over the drawing area. Click a tab to switch; the command line, the panels and undo follow the drawing you switch to. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E in one drawing and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E in another copies objects between them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETile the open drawings side by side\u003C/span\u003E (the split icon at the end of the tabs), or the stacked icon beside it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach pane now shows one of the open drawings, up to four. Click a pane to work in that drawing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E×\u003C/span\u003E on a tab to close that drawing. If it has unsaved changes, CadCraft asks first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpening a drawing while the window holds only a new, untouched drawing uses that tab instead of adding one.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"pan-and-zoom-together\"\u003EPan and zoom together\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo compare two revisions, tile them side by side, then click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELink the panes&#039; views\u003C/span\u003E (the link icon at the end of the tabs), or choose it in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E, or type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELINKVIEWS\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EON\u003C/code\u003E. Now a pan or zoom in one pane moves every pane showing the model (or every pane showing a sheet) to the same place, so the two drawings stay lined up. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELINKVIEWS\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003EOFF\u003C/code\u003E lets each pane move on its own again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMy click did nothing.\u003C/strong\u003E It made that pane active. Click again to pick or draw.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe wheel zoomed the wrong pane.\u003C/strong\u003E The wheel zooms the pane under the pointer and makes it active.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe panes went back to one.\u003C/strong\u003E Opening a drawing shows it as it was saved; a drawing saved without panes opens in one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELeaving the page warns about unsaved changes I can&#039;t see.\u003C/strong\u003E Another tab holds a drawing with changes. Switch to it and save, or leave without saving.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to look at one drawing in several panes at once, each with its own zoom, and how to open several drawings and see them side by side. Split one drawing into panes Open a drawing, or draw a few lines and circles. Open View › More and choose Split side by side (or type VPORTS, then 2, then Enter). The drawing area splits into two panes, both showing the same view. The active pane has a blue frame and gets the crosshair. Click in the other pane. It becomes active. (That first click only switches panes; click again to pick.) Zoom with the wheel. Only that pane zooms. Drag the line between the panes to make one wider. Home on a focused divider puts it back in the middle. VPORTS also takes 3 (two panes on one side and a big one on the other: L or R), 4, and SI for one pane again. The active pane's view then fills the area. On a sheet, VPORTS adds a viewport to the sheet instead (as AutoCAD); type SPLIT there, or use the View menu. Draw from one pane into another Split side by side, and zoom each pane onto a different corner of the drawing. Type L and press Enter, and click the first point in the left pane. Click once in the right pane to make it active, then click the second point there. The line runs between the two places. A command goes on as you change panes, and the rubber band shows in every pane. The model beside a sheet Split side by side. Click the right pane, then click a sheet's tab under the drawing. The right pane shows the sheet; the left one still shows the model. Each pane keeps its own space, so you can draw on the model and watch the sheet's viewport follow. Several drawings in one window Open a drawing. Then open another (or press New). Each drawing opens in its own tab over the drawing area. Click a tab to switch; the command line, the panels and undo follow the drawing you switch to. Ctrl+C in one drawing and Ctrl+V in another copies objects between them. Click Tile the open drawings side by side (the split icon at the end of the tabs), or the stacked icon beside it. Each pane now shows one of the open drawings, up to four. Click a pane to work in that drawing. Click the × on a tab to close that drawing. If it has unsaved changes, CadCraft asks first. Opening a drawing while the window holds only a new, untouched drawing uses that tab instead of adding one. Pan and zoom together To compare two revisions, tile them side by side, then click Link the panes' views (the link icon at the end of the tabs), or choose it in View › More, or type LINKVIEWS and ON. Now a pan or zoom in one pane moves every pane showing the model (or every pane showing a sheet) to the same place, so the two drawings stay lined up. LINKVIEWS OFF lets each pane move on its own again. Common mistakes My click did nothing. It made that pane active. Click again to pick or draw. The wheel zoomed the wrong pane. The wheel zooms the pane under the pointer and makes it active. The panes went back to one. Opening a drawing shows it as it was saved; a drawing saved without panes opens in one. Leaving the page warns about unsaved changes I can't see. Another tab holds a drawing with changes. Switch to it and save, or leave without saving.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"construction-lines","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Construction lines and rays","summary":"Draw lines that run on without end (XLINE) or from a point one way (RAY) to line things up, and trim them into ordinary lines.","section":"tasks","order":47,"keywords":["xline","ray","construction line","construction lines","infinite line","guide line","reference line","bisect","offset line","layout lines","xl"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"two-kinds","text":"Two kinds","level":2},{"id":"draw-construction-lines","text":"Draw construction lines","level":2},{"id":"draw-rays","text":"Draw rays","level":2},{"id":"snap-to-them-and-trim-them","text":"Snap to them and trim them","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to draw construction lines to line things up while you draft: through two points, level or upright, at an angle, halfway between two directions, or a set distance from a line. And how to trim them into ordinary lines when they have done their job.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"two-kinds\"\u003ETwo kinds\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003Econstruction line\u003C/strong\u003E (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EXLINE\u003C/code\u003E) runs on without end both ways.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003Eray\u003C/strong\u003E (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ERAY\u003C/code\u003E) starts at a point and runs on without end one way.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBoth draw across the whole drawing area whatever the zoom, in split screen and in viewports on a sheet. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExtents\u003C/span\u003E leaves them out, so zooming to the drawing still fits what you drew.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-construction-lines\"\u003EDraw construction lines\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraw\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConstruction line\u003C/span\u003E, or type \u003Ccode\u003EXL\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a point. Every click after it adds a construction line through the first point and the one you click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to finish.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EInstead of the first point you can type an option:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EOption\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EH\u003C/code\u003E (Hor)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELevel lines, one through each point you click.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EV\u003C/code\u003E (Ver)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUpright lines through each point.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E (Ang)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELines at an angle you type (degrees, counter-clockwise from the X axis), through each point. \u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E (Reference) measures the angle from a line you pick.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EB\u003C/code\u003E (Bisect)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick the corner, then a point along one side: each point you click along the other side adds the line halfway between the two.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EO\u003C/code\u003E (Offset)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EType a distance, pick a line, then click the side: a construction line that far from it, parallel. \u003Ccode\u003ET\u003C/code\u003E (Through) puts it through a point you click instead. The distance is remembered for the next time.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-rays\"\u003EDraw rays\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraw\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERay\u003C/span\u003E, or type \u003Ccode\u003ERAY\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick where the rays start.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a point for each ray to pass through. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to finish.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"snap-to-them-and-trim-them\"\u003ESnap to them and trim them\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EConstruction lines and rays take the Nearest, Perpendicular and Intersection snaps. They have no ends and no middle, so Endpoint and Midpoint don&#039;t find anything on them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThey cut and get cut like other lines in \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ETRIM\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EBREAK\u003C/code\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETrim one end off a construction line and it becomes a ray starting at the cut.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETrim the far part off a ray and it becomes an ordinary line.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EBreak a construction line at one point and you get two rays.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMOVE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECOPY\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EROTATE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMIRROR\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EOFFSET\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESTRETCH\u003C/code\u003E work on them too. A window selection never picks them, because no window can hold all of an endless line; a crossing window or a click does.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA DWG (every version) and a DXF 2000 keep them as construction lines. DXF R12 has none, so there they become lines across the drawing, and the save says so. A plot or PDF draws them only across what it prints.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to draw construction lines to line things up while you draft: through two points, level or upright, at an angle, halfway between two directions, or a set distance from a line. And how to trim them into ordinary lines when they have done their job. Two kinds A construction line (XLINE) runs on without end both ways. A ray (RAY) starts at a point and runs on without end one way. Both draw across the whole drawing area whatever the zoom, in split screen and in viewports on a sheet. Extents leaves them out, so zooming to the drawing still fits what you drew. Draw construction lines Click Draw › More › Construction line, or type XL and press Enter. Click a point. Every click after it adds a construction line through the first point and the one you click. Press Enter to finish. Instead of the first point you can type an option: Option What it does H (Hor) Level lines, one through each point you click. V (Ver) Upright lines through each point. A (Ang) Lines at an angle you type (degrees, counter-clockwise from the X axis), through each point. R (Reference) measures the angle from a line you pick. B (Bisect) Click the corner, then a point along one side: each point you click along the other side adds the line halfway between the two. O (Offset) Type a distance, pick a line, then click the side: a construction line that far from it, parallel. T (Through) puts it through a point you click instead. The distance is remembered for the next time. Draw rays Click Draw › More › Ray, or type RAY and press Enter. Click where the rays start. Click a point for each ray to pass through. Press Enter to finish. Snap to them and trim them Construction lines and rays take the Nearest, Perpendicular and Intersection snaps. They have no ends and no middle, so Endpoint and Midpoint don't find anything on them. They cut and get cut like other lines in TRIM and BREAK: Trim one end off a construction line and it becomes a ray starting at the cut. Trim the far part off a ray and it becomes an ordinary line. Break a construction line at one point and you get two rays. MOVE, COPY, ROTATE, MIRROR, OFFSET and STRETCH work on them too. A window selection never picks them, because no window can hold all of an endless line; a crossing window or a click does. A DWG (every version) and a DXF 2000 keep them as construction lines. DXF R12 has none, so there they become lines across the drawing, and the save says so. A plot or PDF draws them only across what it prints.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"load-applications","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Load applications (APPLOAD)","summary":"Load LISP routines and their dialog files, unload them, load them again from the history, and keep the ones you use in the Startup Suite.","section":"tasks","order":47,"keywords":["appload","ap","load application","lisp","lsp","dcl","routine","startup suite","unload","history","drop","load","customize","manage"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"load-a-routine","text":"Load a routine","level":2},{"id":"unload-a-routine","text":"Unload a routine","level":2},{"id":"load-a-routine-every-time-cadcraft-starts","text":"Load a routine every time CadCraft starts","level":2},{"id":"load-acad-lsp-and-acaddoc-lsp-from-a-support-folder-studio","text":"Load acad.lsp and acaddoc.lsp from a support folder (Studio)","level":2},{"id":"load-a-file-again-from-the-history","text":"Load a file again from the history","level":2},{"id":"files-cadcraft-can-t-load","text":"Files CadCraft can't load","level":2},{"id":"stop-a-routine","text":"Stop a routine","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to load a LISP routine (a \u003Ccode\u003E.lsp\u003C/code\u003E file) so its commands work in CadCraft, how to unload it, and how to have it load every time CadCraft starts.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"load-a-routine\"\u003ELoad a routine\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELoad application…\u003C/span\u003E, or type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EAPPLOAD\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003EAP\u003C/code\u003E) and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELoad a file…\u003C/span\u003E and choose one or more \u003Ccode\u003E.lsp\u003C/code\u003E files. A routine that opens a dialog usually comes with a \u003Ccode\u003E.dcl\u003C/code\u003E file: choose it too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe dialog says what each file made, for example &quot;Loaded delblocks.lsp: 1 command, DELBLOCKS.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClose\u003C/span\u003E, type the routine&#039;s command and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou can also drop \u003Ccode\u003E.lsp\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E.dcl\u003C/code\u003E files on the drawing, or on the Load applications dialog: they load at once.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe file is read here, in your browser. Nothing is uploaded.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E at an empty command line to run the routine&#039;s command again, the same as any other command. Your command aliases can reach it too: see &quot;Edit command aliases&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"unload-a-routine\"\u003EUnload a routine\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELoad applications\u003C/span\u003E, under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELoaded applications\u003C/span\u003E, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnload\u003C/span\u003E beside the file. The commands it made are gone until you load it again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"load-a-routine-every-time-cadcraft-starts\"\u003ELoad a routine every time CadCraft starts\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELoad the routine.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELoaded applications\u003C/span\u003E, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd to Startup Suite\u003C/span\u003E beside it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFrom now on it loads by itself whenever CadCraft starts in this browser. To stop that, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove\u003C/span\u003E beside it under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStartup Suite\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe Startup Suite keeps a copy of each file in this browser&#039;s storage. If you change the routine, load the new file and add it again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"load-acad-lsp-and-acaddoc-lsp-from-a-support-folder-studio\"\u003ELoad acad.lsp and acaddoc.lsp from a support folder (Studio)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn CadCraft Studio, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESupport folder\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELoad applications\u003C/span\u003E dialog takes a folder on your computer. Click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChoose folder…\u003C/span\u003E. When CadCraft starts it reads \u003Ccode\u003Eacad.lsp\u003C/code\u003E and then \u003Ccode\u003Eacaddoc.lsp\u003C/code\u003E from that folder (whatever the case of the names), loads them, and then loads the Startup Suite. \u003Ccode\u003Eacaddoc.lsp\u003C/code\u003E is read again each time a new drawing tab opens. Nothing else in the folder is read, and a file over 2 MB is skipped with a message.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf a file defines a function named \u003Ccode\u003ES::STARTUP\u003C/code\u003E, it runs once after the files are loaded (also after the Startup Suite). \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELoad now\u003C/span\u003E does the same on request; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClear\u003C/span\u003E forgets the folder.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn the browser a page cannot keep a folder, so there is no support folder: use the Startup Suite above. Compiled \u003Ccode\u003E.fas\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E.vlx\u003C/code\u003E files and native programs stay refused.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"load-a-file-again-from-the-history\"\u003ELoad a file again from the history\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHistory\u003C/span\u003E lists the files you loaded in this browser, newest first. Click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELoad\u003C/span\u003E beside one to load it again, without looking for it on your computer.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"files-cadcraft-can-t-load\"\u003EFiles CadCraft can&#039;t load\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFile\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it is\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat to do\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.fas\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.vlx\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ecompiled or protected LISP\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eload the \u003Ccode\u003E.lsp\u003C/code\u003E source instead\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.arx\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.crx\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.dbx\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Enative Windows programs\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ethere is no way to run these in CadCraft\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.dvb\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ea VBA project\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ethere is no way to run these in CadCraft\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.craft\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.lua\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.py\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECraftScript scripts\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eopen them in PartCraft&#039;s Script workspace\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe Load applications dialog says which of these a file is when you choose it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"stop-a-routine\"\u003EStop a routine\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. At one of the routine&#039;s prompts, Esc cancels it the way the routine expects (its own clean-up runs). If it is busy and not asking anything, Esc stops it outright and says so; your loaded routines stay loaded.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEverything a routine changes in the drawing is one step: one \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes it all back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to load a LISP routine (a .lsp file) so its commands work in CadCraft, how to unload it, and how to have it load every time CadCraft starts. Load a routine Open View › More › Load application…, or type APPLOAD (or AP) and press Enter. Click Load a file… and choose one or more .lsp files. A routine that opens a dialog usually comes with a .dcl file: choose it too. The dialog says what each file made, for example \"Loaded delblocks.lsp: 1 command, DELBLOCKS.\" Click Close, type the routine's command and press Enter. You can also drop .lsp and .dcl files on the drawing, or on the Load applications dialog: they load at once. The file is read here, in your browser. Nothing is uploaded. Press Enter at an empty command line to run the routine's command again, the same as any other command. Your command aliases can reach it too: see \"Edit command aliases\". Unload a routine In Load applications, under Loaded applications, click Unload beside the file. The commands it made are gone until you load it again. Load a routine every time CadCraft starts Load the routine. Under Loaded applications, click Add to Startup Suite beside it. From now on it loads by itself whenever CadCraft starts in this browser. To stop that, click Remove beside it under Startup Suite. The Startup Suite keeps a copy of each file in this browser's storage. If you change the routine, load the new file and add it again. Load acad.lsp and acaddoc.lsp from a support folder (Studio) In CadCraft Studio, Support folder in the Load applications dialog takes a folder on your computer. Click Choose folder…. When CadCraft starts it reads acad.lsp and then acaddoc.lsp from that folder (whatever the case of the names), loads them, and then loads the Startup Suite. acaddoc.lsp is read again each time a new drawing tab opens. Nothing else in the folder is read, and a file over 2 MB is skipped with a message. If a file defines a function named S::STARTUP, it runs once after the files are loaded (also after the Startup Suite). Load now does the same on request; Clear forgets the folder. In the browser a page cannot keep a folder, so there is no support folder: use the Startup Suite above. Compiled .fas and .vlx files and native programs stay refused. Load a file again from the history History lists the files you loaded in this browser, newest first. Click Load beside one to load it again, without looking for it on your computer. Files CadCraft can't load File What it is What to do .fas, .vlx compiled or protected LISP load the .lsp source instead .arx, .crx, .dbx native Windows programs there is no way to run these in CadCraft .dvb a VBA project there is no way to run these in CadCraft .craft, .lua, .py CraftScript scripts open them in PartCraft's Script workspace The Load applications dialog says which of these a file is when you choose it. Stop a routine Press Esc. At one of the routine's prompts, Esc cancels it the way the routine expects (its own clean-up runs). If it is busy and not asking anything, Esc stops it outright and says so; your loaded routines stay loaded. Everything a routine changes in the drawing is one step: one Ctrl+Z takes it all back.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"sheet-sets","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Sheet sets","summary":"Bring sheets from many drawings together in one numbered list, fill their title blocks from the set, publish them as one PDF and archive the set as a zip.","section":"tasks","order":47,"keywords":["sheet set","sheet set manager","ssm","sheetset","ccsheets","dst","sheet list","sheet index","title block","fields","publish","renumber","archive","etransmit","transmittal","subsets","cover sheet","drawing list"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"start-a-set","text":"Start a set","level":2},{"id":"add-sheets","text":"Add sheets","level":2},{"id":"arrange-the-set","text":"Arrange the set","level":2},{"id":"number-sheets-from-their-fields","text":"Number sheets from their fields","level":2},{"id":"fill-title-blocks-from-the-set","text":"Fill title blocks from the set","level":2},{"id":"a-sheet-list-on-the-cover-sheet","text":"A sheet list on the cover sheet","level":2},{"id":"publish-the-set","text":"Publish the set","level":2},{"id":"archive-the-set","text":"Archive the set","level":2},{"id":"the-set-file","text":"The set file","level":2},{"id":"troubleshooting","text":"Troubleshooting","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to collect sheets (layouts) from several drawing files into a \u003Cstrong\u003Esheet set\u003C/strong\u003E, number and title them, have their title blocks filled in for you, publish the whole set as one PDF, and pack everything into one zip to send.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA sheet set lives in a small file of its own, \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;name&gt;.ccsheets\u003C/code\u003E, in the folder that holds the drawings. The set file lists the sheets with paths from that folder, so you can move or copy the whole folder.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpening a folder to read and write needs Chrome or Edge (they can let a page work in a folder you choose), or the desktop app. Other browsers open a set read only: you can look at it and publish it, and the set file downloads instead of saving.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"start-a-set\"\u003EStart a set\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFind the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESheet set\u003C/span\u003E section in the left panel, and open its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESet\u003C/span\u003E menu. Typing \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESHEETSET\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003ESSM\u003C/code\u003E) also brings you there.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew set…\u003C/span\u003E. Give the set a name, and if you like the project name and number.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick the folder that holds the drawings. The set file is saved there.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo open a set later, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen set…\u003C/span\u003E and pick the same folder. If the folder holds more than one set, CadCraft asks which.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-sheets\"\u003EAdd sheets\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd sheets from a drawing…\u003C/span\u003E and pick one of the drawings in the folder.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECadCraft reads that drawing in the background (it does not open on screen) and lists its sheets. Tick the ones you want and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOK\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect a sheet and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F2\"\u003EF2\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF2\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERename and renumber…\u003C/span\u003E) to give it a number and a title, such as \u003Ccode\u003EA-101\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EFloor plan\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew sheet from template…\u003C/span\u003E makes a new drawing from another drawing&#039;s sheet (its title block and viewports), named after the new sheet, and adds it. The set remembers the drawing you used as its template.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"arrange-the-set\"\u003EArrange the set\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESubsets\u003C/strong\u003E group sheets, such as Architectural › Plans: select where it goes and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew subset…\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOrder\u003C/strong\u003E: drag a sheet to another place in the list, or onto a subset to move it there. With the keyboard, select it and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option ↑\"\u003E⌥↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option ↓\"\u003E⌥↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOpen a sheet\u003C/strong\u003E: double-click it (or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E). Its drawing opens in its own tab, on that sheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERemove\u003C/strong\u003E: select it and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The drawing itself is not changed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo set change\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedo set change\u003C/span\u003E step through what you changed in the set.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERenumber\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERenumber sheets…\u003C/span\u003E numbers every sheet, or one subset&#039;s, in the order of the list: a prefix, a start and a step (\u003Ccode\u003EA-\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E101\u003C/code\u003E give A-101, A-102 …).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery change is saved in the set file at once.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"number-sheets-from-their-fields\"\u003ENumber sheets from their fields\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EInstead of numbering sheets by hand, let a rule build each number from the sheet&#039;s fields, so a sheet that moves to another level or discipline gets its new number by itself.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGive each sheet its fields: select it, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERename…\u003C/span\u003E and, in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFields of this sheet\u003C/span\u003E, write one per line, for example \u003Ccode\u003EDISCIPLINE = A\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003ELEVEL = 1\u003C/code\u003E. A field the whole set shares (say \u003Ccode\u003EDISCIPLINE = A\u003C/code\u003E) can go in the set&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties…\u003C/span\u003E instead.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the panel&#039;s menu and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENumbering rule…\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWrite the rule, for example \u003Ccode\u003E{DISCIPLINE}-{LEVEL}{SEQ:2}\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003E{SEQ}\u003C/code\u003E counts up among the sheets whose other fields read the same, so level 1 gets A-101, A-102 and level 2 starts again at A-201. \u003Ccode\u003E{SEQ:2}\u003C/code\u003E writes it with two digits; \u003Ccode\u003E{SUBSET}\u003C/code\u003E is the name of the subset a sheet is in.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose what happens when a field changes: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERenumber the sheets\u003C/span\u003E keeps the numbers following the rule after every change; the other choice numbers them once, now.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe rule is saved in the set file. While it renumbers by itself, change a sheet&#039;s fields rather than its number.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"fill-title-blocks-from-the-set\"\u003EFill title blocks from the set\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA title block gets its text from the set when its attributes are tagged with a field&#039;s name:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETag\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it shows\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESHEETNUMBER\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003ESHEET_NO\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDWG_NO\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe sheet&#039;s number\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESHEETTITLE\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003ETITLE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESHEET_TITLE\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe sheet&#039;s title\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESHEETDESC\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe sheet&#039;s description\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPROJECTNAME\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003EPROJECT\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe project name\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPROJECTNUMBER\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003EPROJECT_NO\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EJOB_NO\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe project number\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESHEETSETNAME\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe set&#039;s name\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESHEETINDEX\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESHEETCOUNT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;3 of 12&quot;, and 12\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYour own, such as \u003Ccode\u003ECLIENT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWhat you wrote for it in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESet fields…\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESet fields…\u003C/span\u003E holds the project name and number and your own fields, one per line as \u003Ccode\u003ENAME = value\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUpdate drawings\u003C/span\u003E writes the numbers, titles and fields into the title blocks of every drawing in the set, and renames each sheet&#039;s layout tab as the set says (by default number and title, such as \u003Ccode\u003EA-101 Floor plan\u003C/code\u003E; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESet fields…\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayout tab names\u003C/span\u003E changes that). Each drawing is saved in place, in its own format and version. After you renumber or rename sheets, run it again and the tabs and title blocks follow.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-sheet-list-on-the-cover-sheet\"\u003EA sheet list on the cover sheet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect the cover sheet in the list and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPut the sheet list on this sheet\u003C/span\u003E. A table of every sheet (number and title, under its subset) goes in the sheet&#039;s upper left corner. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUpdate drawings\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPublish to PDF\u003C/span\u003E keep it up to date. It is one block, \u003Ccode\u003ECC_SHEETLIST\u003C/code\u003E: move it like any block reference.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"publish-the-set\"\u003EPublish the set\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPublish to PDF\u003C/span\u003E makes one PDF with a page per sheet, in the set&#039;s order, at each sheet&#039;s own paper size, with a bookmark per sheet (&quot;A-101 — Floor plan&quot;). With a subset selected, CadCraft asks whether to publish just that subset. The title blocks in the PDF are filled from the set even before you update the drawings; the drawings themselves are not changed by publishing. The PDF downloads.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPublish to DWG…\u003C/span\u003E writes each sheet as a DWG of its own (DWG 2018): the model and that one sheet, with its title block filled from the set, named by its number and title (\u003Ccode\u003EA-101 Level 1 floor plan.dwg\u003C/code\u003E). Choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDWG\u003C/span\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDWG + PDF\u003C/span\u003E for the set&#039;s PDF beside them. A \u003Ccode\u003Epublish.json\u003C/code\u003E lists every file with its size and SHA-256. When the set&#039;s folder can be written (CadCraft Studio, or a folder you picked in the browser) the files go into a folder \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;set name&gt; - published\u003C/code\u003E; otherwise they download as one zip. Viewport \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/viewport-overrides\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/viewport-overrides\"\u003Eclass overrides\u003C/a\u003E come along in each DWG.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"archive-the-set\"\u003EArchive the set\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArchive as a zip\u003C/span\u003E packs the set file, every drawing it uses and what those drawings link to (linked drawings, and theirs in turn, and pictures), keeping their folders, plus a \u003Ccode\u003ETransmittal.txt\u003C/code\u003E that lists every file. A link that is not in the set&#039;s folder is listed there as missing. Links are found by their path from the drawing, then by their file name anywhere in the folder.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-set-file\"\u003EThe set file\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Ccode\u003E.ccsheets\u003C/code\u003E file is JSON you can read in any text editor:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;format&quot;: &quot;cadcraft-sheetset&quot;, &quot;version&quot;: 1,\n  &quot;name&quot;: &quot;Riverside Clinic&quot;,\n  &quot;properties&quot;: { &quot;PROJECTNAME&quot;: &quot;Riverside Clinic&quot;, &quot;PROJECTNUMBER&quot;: &quot;2026-014&quot; },\n  &quot;layoutNames&quot;: &quot;number-title&quot;,\n  &quot;sheets&quot;: [],\n  &quot;subsets&quot;: [ { &quot;id&quot;: &quot;plans&quot;, &quot;name&quot;: &quot;Plans&quot;, &quot;sheets&quot;: [\n    { &quot;id&quot;: &quot;p1&quot;, &quot;file&quot;: &quot;plans/Level 1.dwg&quot;, &quot;layout&quot;: &quot;A-101 Level 1 floor plan&quot;, &quot;number&quot;: &quot;A-101&quot;, &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Level 1 floor plan&quot; }\n  ] } ]\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPaths are relative to the set&#039;s folder, with \u003Ccode\u003E/\u003C/code\u003E between folders. CadCraft checks the file when it opens it: an absolute path, a sheet without a file or layout, or text that is not plain is left out, and the history says so.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"troubleshooting\"\u003ETroubleshooting\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat you see\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhy\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat to do\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;This folder is read only here&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe browser cannot write in a folder\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUse Chrome or Edge, or the desktop app; meanwhile \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload the set file\u003C/span\u003E keeps your changes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA sheet is left out of the PDF: &quot;has no layout named …&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe sheet&#039;s layout was renamed or removed in its drawing\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen the drawing and check its tabs; remove the sheet and add it again\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA title block stays empty\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIts attributes have other tags\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERename the attribute tags to the field names above (BEDIT)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;already has a layout named … so that tab was not renamed&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo sheets of one drawing would get the same tab name\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGive one of them another number or title\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to collect sheets (layouts) from several drawing files into a sheet set, number and title them, have their title blocks filled in for you, publish the whole set as one PDF, and pack everything into one zip to send. A sheet set lives in a small file of its own, \u003Cname\u003E.ccsheets, in the folder that holds the drawings. The set file lists the sheets with paths from that folder, so you can move or copy the whole folder. Opening a folder to read and write needs Chrome or Edge (they can let a page work in a folder you choose), or the desktop app. Other browsers open a set read only: you can look at it and publish it, and the set file downloads instead of saving. Start a set Find the Sheet set section in the left panel, and open its Set menu. Typing SHEETSET (or SSM) also brings you there. Choose New set…. Give the set a name, and if you like the project name and number. Pick the folder that holds the drawings. The set file is saved there. To open a set later, choose Open set… and pick the same folder. If the folder holds more than one set, CadCraft asks which. Add sheets Choose Add sheets from a drawing… and pick one of the drawings in the folder. CadCraft reads that drawing in the background (it does not open on screen) and lists its sheets. Tick the ones you want and click OK. Select a sheet and press F2 (or Rename and renumber…) to give it a number and a title, such as A-101 and Floor plan. New sheet from template… makes a new drawing from another drawing's sheet (its title block and viewports), named after the new sheet, and adds it. The set remembers the drawing you used as its template. Arrange the set Subsets group sheets, such as Architectural › Plans: select where it goes and choose New subset…. Order: drag a sheet to another place in the list, or onto a subset to move it there. With the keyboard, select it and press Alt+↑ or Alt+↓. Open a sheet: double-click it (or press Enter). Its drawing opens in its own tab, on that sheet. Remove: select it and press Delete. The drawing itself is not changed. Undo: Undo set change and Redo set change step through what you changed in the set. Renumber: Renumber sheets… numbers every sheet, or one subset's, in the order of the list: a prefix, a start and a step (A- and 101 give A-101, A-102 …). Every change is saved in the set file at once. Number sheets from their fields Instead of numbering sheets by hand, let a rule build each number from the sheet's fields, so a sheet that moves to another level or discipline gets its new number by itself. Give each sheet its fields: select it, click Rename… and, in Fields of this sheet, write one per line, for example DISCIPLINE = A and LEVEL = 1. A field the whole set shares (say DISCIPLINE = A) can go in the set's Properties… instead. Open the panel's menu and click Numbering rule…. Write the rule, for example {DISCIPLINE}-{LEVEL}{SEQ:2}. {SEQ} counts up among the sheets whose other fields read the same, so level 1 gets A-101, A-102 and level 2 starts again at A-201. {SEQ:2} writes it with two digits; {SUBSET} is the name of the subset a sheet is in. Choose what happens when a field changes: Renumber the sheets keeps the numbers following the rule after every change; the other choice numbers them once, now. The rule is saved in the set file. While it renumbers by itself, change a sheet's fields rather than its number. Fill title blocks from the set A title block gets its text from the set when its attributes are tagged with a field's name: Tag What it shows SHEETNUMBER (or SHEET_NO, DWG_NO) The sheet's number SHEETTITLE (or TITLE, SHEET_TITLE) The sheet's title SHEETDESC The sheet's description PROJECTNAME (or PROJECT) The project name PROJECTNUMBER (or PROJECT_NO, JOB_NO) The project number SHEETSETNAME The set's name SHEETINDEX, SHEETCOUNT \"3 of 12\", and 12 Your own, such as CLIENT What you wrote for it in Set fields… Set fields… holds the project name and number and your own fields, one per line as NAME = value. Update drawings writes the numbers, titles and fields into the title blocks of every drawing in the set, and renames each sheet's layout tab as the set says (by default number and title, such as A-101 Floor plan; Set fields… › Layout tab names changes that). Each drawing is saved in place, in its own format and version. After you renumber or rename sheets, run it again and the tabs and title blocks follow. A sheet list on the cover sheet Select the cover sheet in the list and choose Put the sheet list on this sheet. A table of every sheet (number and title, under its subset) goes in the sheet's upper left corner. Update drawings and Publish to PDF keep it up to date. It is one block, CC_SHEETLIST: move it like any block reference. Publish the set Publish to PDF makes one PDF with a page per sheet, in the set's order, at each sheet's own paper size, with a bookmark per sheet (\"A-101 — Floor plan\"). With a subset selected, CadCraft asks whether to publish just that subset. The title blocks in the PDF are filled from the set even before you update the drawings; the drawings themselves are not changed by publishing. The PDF downloads. Publish to DWG… writes each sheet as a DWG of its own (DWG 2018): the model and that one sheet, with its title block filled from the set, named by its number and title (A-101 Level 1 floor plan.dwg). Choose DWG, or DWG + PDF for the set's PDF beside them. A publish.json lists every file with its size and SHA-256. When the set's folder can be written (CadCraft Studio, or a folder you picked in the browser) the files go into a folder \u003Cset name\u003E - published; otherwise they download as one zip. Viewport class overrides come along in each DWG. Archive the set Archive as a zip packs the set file, every drawing it uses and what those drawings link to (linked drawings, and theirs in turn, and pictures), keeping their folders, plus a Transmittal.txt that lists every file. A link that is not in the set's folder is listed there as missing. Links are found by their path from the drawing, then by their file name anywhere in the folder. The set file The .ccsheets file is JSON you can read in any text editor: { \"format\": \"cadcraft-sheetset\", \"version\": 1, \"name\": \"Riverside Clinic\", \"properties\": { \"PROJECTNAME\": \"Riverside Clinic\", \"PROJECTNUMBER\": \"2026-014\" }, \"layoutNames\": \"number-title\", \"sheets\": [], \"subsets\": [ { \"id\": \"plans\", \"name\": \"Plans\", \"sheets\": [ { \"id\": \"p1\", \"file\": \"plans/Level 1.dwg\", \"layout\": \"A-101 Level 1 floor plan\", \"number\": \"A-101\", \"title\": \"Level 1 floor plan\" } ] } ] } Paths are relative to the set's folder, with / between folders. CadCraft checks the file when it opens it: an absolute path, a sheet without a file or layout, or text that is not plain is left out, and the history says so. Troubleshooting What you see Why What to do \"This folder is read only here\" The browser cannot write in a folder Use Chrome or Edge, or the desktop app; meanwhile Download the set file keeps your changes A sheet is left out of the PDF: \"has no layout named …\" The sheet's layout was renamed or removed in its drawing Open the drawing and check its tabs; remove the sheet and add it again A title block stays empty Its attributes have other tags Rename the attribute tags to the field names above (BEDIT) \"already has a layout named … so that tab was not renamed\" Two sheets of one drawing would get the same tab name Give one of them another number or title","lang":"en"},{"slug":"ucs-and-turned-view","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Turn the view and use your own coordinates (UCS)","summary":"Turn the view 90° or to any angle without moving anything in the drawing, and set a user coordinate system (UCS) so points, ortho, polar tracking, the grid and new text follow a building's own axes.","section":"tasks","order":48,"keywords":["ucs","user coordinate system","plan","turn view","rotate view","view twist","ucs z","ucs 3point","ucs object","ucs world","named ucs","ucsfollow","turnleft","turnright","turnworld","angled building","rotated grid"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"turn-the-view-a-quarter","text":"Turn the view a quarter","level":2},{"id":"line-up-with-an-angled-building","text":"Line up with an angled building","level":2},{"id":"other-ways-to-set-a-ucs","text":"Other ways to set a UCS","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to look at a drawing turned, so a building drawn at an angle reads square on screen, and how to type points along its own axes. Nothing in the drawing moves while you do this: objects keep their coordinates, a turn is not an edit, and the drawing is not marked as changed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"turn-the-view-a-quarter\"\u003ETurn the view a quarter\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETurn view 90° left\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETurn view 90° right\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe drawing turns on screen about the middle of the drawing area. Do it again to keep turning.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack to world\u003C/span\u003E to level it again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe commands behind them are \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ETURNLEFT\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ETURNRIGHT\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ETURNWORLD\u003C/code\u003E. To give one a key, add a line such as \u003Ccode\u003ETL=TURNLEFT\u003C/code\u003E to your aliases with \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EALIASEDIT\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"line-up-with-an-angled-building\"\u003ELine up with an angled building\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EUCS\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, then \u003Ccode\u003EOB\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a wall line near the end that should be the origin.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe UCS now starts at that end with its X axis along the wall, and the view turns so the wall is level on screen. The axis icon in the lower-left corner shows where the UCS axes point.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw as usual. With the UCS set:\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Ex,y\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E@dx,dy\u003C/code\u003E are measured along the UCS axes; \u003Ccode\u003Ed&lt;a\u003C/code\u003E measures the angle from the UCS X axis.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrtho\u003C/span\u003E, polar tracking and the grid follow the UCS axes, and so does the crosshair.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENew text and new paragraphs run along the UCS X axis; \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMLINEAR\u003C/code\u003E&#039;s horizontal and vertical follow it too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe coordinates in the status bar and from \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EID\u003C/code\u003E are UCS coordinates.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo type a world point while a UCS is set, put \u003Ccode\u003E*\u003C/code\u003E in front: \u003Ccode\u003E*0,0\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E@*100,0\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EUCS\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, then \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again to go back to the world.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"other-ways-to-set-a-ucs\"\u003EOther ways to set a UCS\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAt the \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EUCS\u003C/code\u003E prompt:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EYou type\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ea point\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA new origin; then click a point on the new X axis, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to keep the angle\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EZ\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETurn the UCS about its origin by an angle you type (degrees, counter-clockwise) or pick\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E3\u003C/code\u003E (3point)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAn origin, a point along the positive X axis, and a point on the positive-Y side\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EOB\u003C/code\u003E (OBject)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFrom a line, arc, circle, polyline segment, text, block reference, point or construction line\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E (Previous)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe UCS before this one (the last ten are kept while the drawing is open)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EW\u003C/code\u003E (World)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBack to the world\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ENA\u003C/code\u003E (NAmed)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E saves the current UCS under a name, \u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E restores one, \u003Ccode\u003ED\u003C/code\u003E deletes one, \u003Ccode\u003E?\u003C/code\u003E lists them\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery change turns the view so the new UCS X axis is level. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPLAN\u003C/code\u003E turns the view without changing the UCS: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for the current UCS, \u003Ccode\u003EU\u003C/code\u003E for a saved one, \u003Ccode\u003EW\u003C/code\u003E for the world.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach pane of a split screen keeps its own turn. The UCS belongs to the drawing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESheets work in paper coordinates; set a UCS on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab, or inside a viewport.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelection windows dragged in a turned view select what lies inside them on screen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELISP routines get points in the UCS from \u003Ccode\u003Egetpoint\u003C/code\u003E, can read \u003Ccode\u003EUCSORG\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EUCSXDIR\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EUCSYDIR\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EVIEWTWIST\u003C/code\u003E, and \u003Ccode\u003Etrans\u003C/code\u003E converts between the world, the UCS and the display.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA saved file keeps the UCS and the view&#039;s turn in a later build; until then they last while the drawing is open.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to look at a drawing turned, so a building drawn at an angle reads square on screen, and how to type points along its own axes. Nothing in the drawing moves while you do this: objects keep their coordinates, a turn is not an edit, and the drawing is not marked as changed. Turn the view a quarter Click View › More › Turn view 90° left or Turn view 90° right. The drawing turns on screen about the middle of the drawing area. Do it again to keep turning. Click View › More › Back to world to level it again. The commands behind them are TURNLEFT, TURNRIGHT and TURNWORLD. To give one a key, add a line such as TL=TURNLEFT to your aliases with ALIASEDIT. Line up with an angled building Type UCS and press Enter, then OB and Enter. Click a wall line near the end that should be the origin. The UCS now starts at that end with its X axis along the wall, and the view turns so the wall is level on screen. The axis icon in the lower-left corner shows where the UCS axes point. Draw as usual. With the UCS set: x,y and @dx,dy are measured along the UCS axes; d\u003Ca measures the angle from the UCS X axis. Ortho, polar tracking and the grid follow the UCS axes, and so does the crosshair. New text and new paragraphs run along the UCS X axis; DIMLINEAR's horizontal and vertical follow it too. The coordinates in the status bar and from ID are UCS coordinates. To type a world point while a UCS is set, put * in front: *0,0 or @*100,0. Type UCS, Enter, then Enter again to go back to the world. Other ways to set a UCS At the UCS prompt: You type What it does a point A new origin; then click a point on the new X axis, or press Enter to keep the angle Z Turn the UCS about its origin by an angle you type (degrees, counter-clockwise) or pick 3 (3point) An origin, a point along the positive X axis, and a point on the positive-Y side OB (OBject) From a line, arc, circle, polyline segment, text, block reference, point or construction line P (Previous) The UCS before this one (the last ten are kept while the drawing is open) W (World) Back to the world NA (NAmed) S saves the current UCS under a name, R restores one, D deletes one, ? lists them Every change turns the view so the new UCS X axis is level. PLAN turns the view without changing the UCS: Enter for the current UCS, U for a saved one, W for the world. Good to know Each pane of a split screen keeps its own turn. The UCS belongs to the drawing. Sheets work in paper coordinates; set a UCS on the Model tab, or inside a viewport. Selection windows dragged in a turned view select what lies inside them on screen. LISP routines get points in the UCS from getpoint, can read UCSORG, UCSXDIR, UCSYDIR and VIEWTWIST, and trans converts between the world, the UCS and the display. A saved file keeps the UCS and the view's turn in a later build; until then they last while the drawing is open.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"my-tools","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Buttons of your own (My tools)","summary":"Add toolbar buttons that run your own macros, give them shortcut keys, share them as a file, and bring buttons over from a CUIx customization file.","section":"tasks","order":49,"keywords":["my tools","custom buttons","toolbar buttons","macro","macros","cui","cuix","customize","customise","menu","shortcut keys","keyboard shortcuts","diesel","^c^c","import buttons","export buttons"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"add-a-button","text":"Add a button","level":2},{"id":"write-a-macro","text":"Write a macro","level":2},{"id":"shortcut-keys","text":"Shortcut keys","level":2},{"id":"share-your-buttons","text":"Share your buttons","level":2},{"id":"bring-buttons-over-from-a-cuix-file","text":"Bring buttons over from a CUIx file","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to put buttons of your own on the toolbar, each running a macro written the way AutoCAD writes them, how to give a button a key, and how to bring the buttons of a CUIx file over.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-a-button\"\u003EAdd a button\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) and stay on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E tab. Scroll to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMy tools\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a button…\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFill in the box:\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGroup\u003C/span\u003E: the toolbar group it goes in, for example \u003Ccode\u003EMy tools\u003C/code\u003E. A new name makes a new group.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELabel\u003C/span\u003E: the words on the button.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMacro\u003C/span\u003E: what it types, for example \u003Ccode\u003E^C^C_rectang;0,0;@100,50;\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShortcut key\u003C/span\u003E: click the box and press the keys, for example \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Option B\"\u003E⌃⌥B\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Alt+B\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIcon\u003C/span\u003E: a PNG or SVG picture of at most 64 KB, or none (a star shows).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe button appears at the end of the toolbar, in its group. Click it, or press its key, to run it. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E in the list change or remove it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"write-a-macro\"\u003EWrite a macro\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA macro is what you would type at the command line, with a few signs:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESign\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeans\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E^C\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECancel whatever runs (\u003Ccode\u003E^C^C\u003C/code\u003E is the usual start)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E;\u003C/code\u003E or a space\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E\\\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWait for you: a click or a typed answer, then the macro carries on\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E_\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E.\u003C/code\u003E before a command\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAccepted and ignored (international and built-in names)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E*\u003C/code\u003E at the very start\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERun again each time it ends, until you press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E^O\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E^G\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETurn Ortho, the grid on or off\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E$M=\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe rest is DIESEL. Only \u003Ccode\u003E$(if,…)\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E$(getvar,…)\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E$(=,…)\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E$(eq,…)\u003C/code\u003E are read; a button using anything else says so and does nothing\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EExamples:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E^C^C_line;0,0;@100,0;;\u003C/code\u003E draws a 100-long line from the origin.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E^C^C_circle;\\25;\u003C/code\u003E asks you for the centre, then draws a circle of radius 25 there.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E^C^C_turnright\u003C/code\u003E turns the view 90° right.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"shortcut-keys\"\u003EShortcut keys\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA key needs \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (Ctrl, or Cmd on a Mac) or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option\"\u003E⌥\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E with a letter or digit, or one of the free function keys \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F2\"\u003EF2\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF2\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F4\"\u003EF4\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF4\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F5\"\u003EF5\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF5\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F6\"\u003EF6\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF6\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F9\"\u003EF9\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF9\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Letters on their own go to the command line. Keys CadCraft, the app or the browser already use (such as \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control S\"\u003E⌃S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Z\"\u003E⌃Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) and keys another button has are refused, and the box says why.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"share-your-buttons\"\u003EShare your buttons\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport…\u003C/span\u003E saves every group and button (with icons and keys) as \u003Ccode\u003Emy-tools.json\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport…\u003C/span\u003E adds the groups of such a file after yours. A group name you already use gets &quot;(2)&quot;; a key that would clash is left off, and the panel says so.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"bring-buttons-over-from-a-cuix-file\"\u003EBring buttons over from a CUIx file\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport CUIx…\u003C/span\u003E and choose the \u003Ccode\u003E.cuix\u003C/code\u003E file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIts toolbars and ribbon panels become groups, each button with its macro and its picture (PNG, or BMP turned into PNG). Its shortcut keys come in a group of their own, each button with its key; the command aliases it may carry come in an \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAliases\u003C/span\u003E group. The panel lists what came over.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe file is only read. Commands CadCraft does not have are listed by name, not guessed: their buttons say so when clicked. DIESEL beyond the few functions above, menus, mouse buttons and workspaces are not read.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EButtons are kept in this browser (or this desktop app), not in drawings.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA button runs through the command line, so it is undone like anything you type: one \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E per command it ran.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to put buttons of your own on the toolbar, each running a macro written the way AutoCAD writes them, how to give a button a key, and how to bring the buttons of a CUIx file over. Add a button Open Settings (Ctrl+,) and stay on the CadCraft tab. Scroll to My tools. Click Add a button…. Fill in the box: Group: the toolbar group it goes in, for example My tools. A new name makes a new group. Label: the words on the button. Macro: what it types, for example ^C^C_rectang;0,0;@100,50;. Shortcut key: click the box and press the keys, for example Ctrl+Alt+B. Icon: a PNG or SVG picture of at most 64 KB, or none (a star shows). Click Save. The button appears at the end of the toolbar, in its group. Click it, or press its key, to run it. Edit and Delete in the list change or remove it. Write a macro A macro is what you would type at the command line, with a few signs: Sign Means ^C Cancel whatever runs (^C^C is the usual start) ; or a space Enter \\ Wait for you: a click or a typed answer, then the macro carries on _ or . before a command Accepted and ignored (international and built-in names) * at the very start Run again each time it ends, until you press Esc ^O, ^G Turn Ortho, the grid on or off $M= The rest is DIESEL. Only $(if,…), $(getvar,…), $(=,…) and $(eq,…) are read; a button using anything else says so and does nothing Examples: ^C^C_line;0,0;@100,0;; draws a 100-long line from the origin. ^C^C_circle;\\25; asks you for the centre, then draws a circle of radius 25 there. ^C^C_turnright turns the view 90° right. Shortcut keys A key needs Ctrl (Ctrl, or Cmd on a Mac) or Alt with a letter or digit, or one of the free function keys F2, F4, F5, F6, F9. Letters on their own go to the command line. Keys CadCraft, the app or the browser already use (such as Ctrl+S or Ctrl+Z) and keys another button has are refused, and the box says why. Share your buttons Export… saves every group and button (with icons and keys) as my-tools.json. Import… adds the groups of such a file after yours. A group name you already use gets \"(2)\"; a key that would clash is left off, and the panel says so. Bring buttons over from a CUIx file Click Import CUIx… and choose the .cuix file. Its toolbars and ribbon panels become groups, each button with its macro and its picture (PNG, or BMP turned into PNG). Its shortcut keys come in a group of their own, each button with its key; the command aliases it may carry come in an Aliases group. The panel lists what came over. The file is only read. Commands CadCraft does not have are listed by name, not guessed: their buttons say so when clicked. DIESEL beyond the few functions above, menus, mouse buttons and workspaces are not read. Good to know Buttons are kept in this browser (or this desktop app), not in drawings. A button runs through the command line, so it is undone like anything you type: one Ctrl+Z per command it ran.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"command-aliases","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Edit command aliases","summary":"Keep your own command aliases in one text file in the familiar format, import and export a .pgp file, and reload it with REINIT.","section":"tasks","order":49,"keywords":["alias","aliases","aliasedit","pgp","shortcut","short name","reinit","keyboard","customize","edit aliases"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"add-an-alias","text":"Add an alias","level":2},{"id":"lines-cadcraft-refuses","text":"Lines CadCraft refuses","level":2},{"id":"bring-your-aliases-from-another-program","text":"Bring your aliases from another program","level":2},{"id":"reload-the-file","text":"Reload the file","level":2},{"id":"keyboard-shortcuts-for-any-command","text":"Keyboard shortcuts for any command","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to add your own short names for commands, for example \u003Ccode\u003ELL\u003C/code\u003E for \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELINE\u003C/code\u003E, all in one text file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-an-alias\"\u003EAdd an alias\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit aliases…\u003C/span\u003E, or type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EALIASEDIT\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd a line with the alias, a comma and the command after a star:\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ELL,     *LINE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E. The command line says how many of your aliases are in use.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003ELL\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: LINE starts.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYour aliases sit on top of CadCraft&#039;s own. A line of yours wins over a built-in alias with the same name; remove your line and the built-in one is back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"lines-cadcraft-refuses\"\u003ELines CadCraft refuses\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe editor lists each problem with its line number while you type:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe command doesn&#039;t exist;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe alias is itself a command&#039;s name (\u003Ccode\u003ELINE, *CIRCLE\u003C/code\u003E would hide LINE);\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe line isn&#039;t \u003Ccode\u003EALIAS, *COMMAND\u003C/code\u003E;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe same alias is on two lines (the later one wins).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELines that start a program on your computer, like \u003Ccode\u003ENOTEPAD, START, 1, *File to edit: ,\u003C/code\u003E, are skipped with a note: an app in the browser can&#039;t start programs.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"bring-your-aliases-from-another-program\"\u003EBring your aliases from another program\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the alias editor, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport .pgp…\u003C/span\u003E and choose the file. You can also drop a \u003Ccode\u003E.pgp\u003C/code\u003E file on the drawing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECheck the problems listed (commands CadCraft doesn&#039;t have are refused, the rest work).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport .pgp\u003C/span\u003E saves CadCraft&#039;s aliases and yours as one file in the same format.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"reload-the-file\"\u003EReload the file\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EREINIT\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to apply your aliases file again. It is kept in this browser; the desktop app will keep it in its own folder, and the cloud service will sync it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"keyboard-shortcuts-for-any-command\"\u003EKeyboard shortcuts for any command\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn alias is typed; a shortcut is a key. To give a command a key of your own:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeyboard shortcuts…\u003C/span\u003E, or type \u003Ccode\u003EKEYS\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKey\u003C/span\u003E box and press the keys, for example \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option Shift C\"\u003E⌥⇧C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+Shift+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERuns\u003C/span\u003E, write the command, and if you like what to type after it: \u003Ccode\u003ECIRCLE\u003C/code\u003E, or \u003Ccode\u003EZOOM E\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA key needs \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option\"\u003E⌥\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E with a letter or digit, or a free function key (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F2\"\u003EF2\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF2\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F4\"\u003EF4\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF4\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F5\"\u003EF5\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF5\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F6\"\u003EF6\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF6\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F9\"\u003EF9\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF9\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E). Keys CadCraft, the app or the browser already use, and keys a My tools button has (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/my-tools\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/my-tools\"\u003EButtons of your own\u003C/a\u003E), are refused, and the box says why. Shortcuts are kept in this browser (or desktop app); \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove\u003C/span\u003E takes one away.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to add your own short names for commands, for example LL for LINE, all in one text file. Add an alias Open View › More › Edit aliases…, or type ALIASEDIT and press Enter. Add a line with the alias, a comma and the command after a star: LL, *LINE Click Save. The command line says how many of your aliases are in use. Type LL and press Enter: LINE starts. Your aliases sit on top of CadCraft's own. A line of yours wins over a built-in alias with the same name; remove your line and the built-in one is back. Lines CadCraft refuses The editor lists each problem with its line number while you type: the command doesn't exist; the alias is itself a command's name (LINE, *CIRCLE would hide LINE); the line isn't ALIAS, *COMMAND; the same alias is on two lines (the later one wins). Lines that start a program on your computer, like NOTEPAD, START, 1, *File to edit: ,, are skipped with a note: an app in the browser can't start programs. Bring your aliases from another program In the alias editor, click Import .pgp… and choose the file. You can also drop a .pgp file on the drawing. Check the problems listed (commands CadCraft doesn't have are refused, the rest work). Click Save. Export .pgp saves CadCraft's aliases and yours as one file in the same format. Reload the file Type REINIT and press Enter to apply your aliases file again. It is kept in this browser; the desktop app will keep it in its own folder, and the cloud service will sync it. Keyboard shortcuts for any command An alias is typed; a shortcut is a key. To give a command a key of your own: Click View › More › Keyboard shortcuts…, or type KEYS and press Enter. Click the Key box and press the keys, for example Alt+Shift+C. In Runs, write the command, and if you like what to type after it: CIRCLE, or ZOOM E. Click Add. A key needs Ctrl or Alt with a letter or digit, or a free function key (F2, F4, F5, F6, F9). Keys CadCraft, the app or the browser already use, and keys a My tools button has (see Buttons of your own), are refused, and the box says why. Shortcuts are kept in this browser (or desktop app); Remove takes one away.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"offset-trim-extend-fillet","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Offset, trim, extend, fillet and chamfer walls","summary":"Turn single lines into double walls with Offset, clean the corners with Fillet, and cut and lengthen lines with Trim and Extend.","section":"tasks","order":50,"keywords":["offset","trim","extend","fillet","chamfer","bevel","break","breakatpoint","lengthen","walls","corner","cut","lengthen","parallel","radius","cleanup"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-make","text":"What you will make","level":2},{"id":"draw-the-inside-face","text":"Draw the inside face","level":2},{"id":"offset-the-outside-face","text":"Offset the outside face","level":2},{"id":"clean-up-the-outer-corners-with-fillet","text":"Clean up the outer corners with Fillet","level":2},{"id":"bevel-a-corner-with-chamfer","text":"Bevel a corner with Chamfer","level":2},{"id":"cut-a-doorway-with-trim","text":"Cut a doorway with Trim","level":2},{"id":"extend-a-line-to-meet-another","text":"Extend a line to meet another","level":2},{"id":"break-and-lengthen","text":"Break and lengthen","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-make\"\u003EWhat you will make\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA small room with 200-thick walls: the inside face is drawn, the outside face is offset, the corners are cleaned up, and a doorway is cut through one wall.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-the-inside-face\"\u003EDraw the inside face\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E0,0\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E@5000,0\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E@0,4000\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E@-5000,0\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExtents\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou have four separate lines. (A closed polyline would offset in one go; separate lines show how Fillet cleans corners.)\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"offset-the-outside-face\"\u003EOffset the outside face\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EO\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The prompt reads \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify offset distance [Through] &lt;Through&gt;:\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E200\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003ESelect object to offset\u003C/code\u003E, click the bottom line.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify point on side to offset\u003C/code\u003E, click anywhere below it. A parallel line appears 200 below.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the right line, then click to its right.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the top line, then click above it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the left line, then click to its left.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to end the command.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou should see four outer lines, each the same length as its inner line, so the outer corners do not meet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E at the distance prompt to use the last distance again. Type \u003Ccode\u003ET\u003C/code\u003E for Through: then each copy goes through the point you click, at whatever distance that is.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EOFFSET\u003C/code\u003E works on lines, arcs, circles and polylines. Offsetting a closed polyline offsets the whole outline, corners included.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"clean-up-the-outer-corners-with-fillet\"\u003EClean up the outer corners with Fillet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EFILLET\u003C/code\u003E joins two lines at a corner: it trims or extends both so they meet. With a radius of 0 the corner is square.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EF\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The prompt reads \u003Ccode\u003ESelect first line [Radius]:\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf the last fillet you did used a radius, type \u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, type \u003Ccode\u003E0\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the outer bottom line near its right end.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003ESelect second line\u003C/code\u003E, click the outer right line near its bottom end.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe two lines now meet in a sharp corner, and the command ends.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to repeat \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EFILLET\u003C/code\u003E, and do the next corner.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERepeat until all four outer corners meet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick each line on the part you want to \u003Cstrong\u003Ekeep\u003C/strong\u003E. With a radius above 0, Fillet adds an arc between the lines instead.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EM\u003C/code\u003E at the first prompt to do all four corners in one go, or draw the outside face as one closed polyline and type \u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E: every corner is done at once. Hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E as you click the second line for a sharp corner without changing the radius.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"bevel-a-corner-with-chamfer\"\u003EBevel a corner with Chamfer\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECHAMFER\u003C/code\u003E cuts a corner off with a straight line.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003ECHA\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003ED\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Type \u003Ccode\u003E300\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for the first distance, then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to use 300 for the second too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the inner top line near its left end, then the inner left line near its top end.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe corner is cut back 300 along each line and a bevel line joins them. Type \u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E instead of \u003Ccode\u003ED\u003C/code\u003E to give a length on the first line and an angle.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"cut-a-doorway-with-trim\"\u003ECut a doorway with Trim\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFirst draw the two door jambs across the bottom wall, then trim the wall lines between them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E1000,200\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E@0,-600\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to repeat \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELINE\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E1900,200\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E@0,-600\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou now have two short lines crossing the bottom wall 900 apart.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003ETR\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The history says \u003Ccode\u003ECurrent settings: Quick mode, edges: No extend\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the inner bottom line between the two jambs. That piece disappears. (Rest the pointer on it first: the piece that will go turns grey and dashed.)\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the outer bottom line between the two jambs. That piece disappears too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the part of each jamb that sticks out above the wall, and the part below it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to end the command.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou should see a 900 opening in the bottom wall, with the jambs closing the wall ends.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn Quick mode (the default) Trim does not ask you to choose cutting edges first: every visible object is a cutting edge. Pick the piece you want gone, or drag a path through several pieces to trim them all at once. Type \u003Ccode\u003EU\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to put back the last pick or drag without leaving the command. Fence, crossing, chosen edges and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to extend are in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/trim-and-extend\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/trim-and-extend\"\u003ETrim and extend\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"extend-a-line-to-meet-another\"\u003EExtend a line to meet another\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXTEND\u003C/code\u003E lengthens a line, arc or polyline end until it meets the next object in that direction.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E1000,2000\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E@2000,0\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again. The line stops 2000 short of the right wall.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EEX\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the new line near its right end.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to end the command.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe line now runs to the inner face of the right wall. Every visible object is a boundary; pick near the end that should grow. Inside \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ETRIM\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click does the same.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"break-and-lengthen\"\u003EBreak and lengthen\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EBREAK\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EBR\u003C/code\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModify\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBreak\u003C/span\u003E) cuts a gap without a cutting edge: click the object where the gap starts (that click is the first point), then click where it ends. On a circle the part counter-clockwise from the first point to the second goes. \u003Ccode\u003EF\u003C/code\u003E at the second question lets you give the first point again, and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EBREAKATPOINT\u003C/code\u003E splits an object in two at one point with no gap.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELENGTHEN\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003ELEN\u003C/code\u003E) changes a length at one end:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003ELEN\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Click a line to see its current length; the prompt asks again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose how: \u003Ccode\u003EDE\u003C/code\u003E (Delta: an amount to add, negative to take off; \u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E for an arc&#039;s angle), \u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E (Percent: 50 halves it), \u003Ccode\u003ET\u003C/code\u003E (Total: the length it should be) or \u003Ccode\u003EDY\u003C/code\u003E (Dynamic: drag the end).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick each object near the end that should move. \u003Ccode\u003EU\u003C/code\u003E takes back the last one; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E ends.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELines, arcs and open polylines (their end segment) can be lengthened.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA whole line disappeared.\u003C/strong\u003E Nothing crossed it, so Quick mode erased it. Type \u003Ccode\u003EU\u003C/code\u003E to put it back; check that the cutting line really crosses it (zoom in) and is not on a layer that is off.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;No boundary in that direction.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E Extend found nothing to run into. Pick nearer the end that should grow.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;Radius is too large.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E The arc would run past the end of one of the lines. Use a smaller radius.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;Only the first or last segment of an open polyline can be cut back.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E Pick two segments of the polyline next to each other, or type \u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E to round every corner of it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe offset went to the wrong side.\u003C/strong\u003E The side is where you click after picking the object. Undo with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and click on the other side.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;Cannot offset that far.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E The offset would turn the arc, circle or polyline inside out. Use a smaller distance.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will make A small room with 200-thick walls: the inside face is drawn, the outside face is offset, the corners are cleaned up, and a doorway is cut through one wall. Draw the inside face Type L and press Enter. Type 0,0 and press Enter. Type @5000,0 and press Enter. Type @0,4000 and press Enter. Type @-5000,0 and press Enter. Type C and press Enter. Click Extents. You have four separate lines. (A closed polyline would offset in one go; separate lines show how Fillet cleans corners.) Offset the outside face Type O and press Enter. The prompt reads Specify offset distance [Through] \u003CThrough\u003E:. Type 200 and press Enter. At Select object to offset, click the bottom line. At Specify point on side to offset, click anywhere below it. A parallel line appears 200 below. Click the right line, then click to its right. Click the top line, then click above it. Click the left line, then click to its left. Press Enter to end the command. You should see four outer lines, each the same length as its inner line, so the outer corners do not meet. Press Enter at the distance prompt to use the last distance again. Type T for Through: then each copy goes through the point you click, at whatever distance that is. OFFSET works on lines, arcs, circles and polylines. Offsetting a closed polyline offsets the whole outline, corners included. Clean up the outer corners with Fillet FILLET joins two lines at a corner: it trims or extends both so they meet. With a radius of 0 the corner is square. Type F and press Enter. The prompt reads Select first line [Radius]:. If the last fillet you did used a radius, type R, press Enter, type 0 and press Enter. Click the outer bottom line near its right end. At Select second line, click the outer right line near its bottom end. The two lines now meet in a sharp corner, and the command ends. Press Enter to repeat FILLET, and do the next corner. Repeat until all four outer corners meet. Click each line on the part you want to keep. With a radius above 0, Fillet adds an arc between the lines instead. Type M at the first prompt to do all four corners in one go, or draw the outside face as one closed polyline and type P: every corner is done at once. Hold Shift as you click the second line for a sharp corner without changing the radius. Bevel a corner with Chamfer CHAMFER cuts a corner off with a straight line. Type CHA and press Enter. Type D and press Enter. Type 300 and press Enter for the first distance, then press Enter to use 300 for the second too. Click the inner top line near its left end, then the inner left line near its top end. The corner is cut back 300 along each line and a bevel line joins them. Type A instead of D to give a length on the first line and an angle. Cut a doorway with Trim First draw the two door jambs across the bottom wall, then trim the wall lines between them. Type L and press Enter. Type 1000,200 and press Enter. Type @0,-600 and press Enter, then press Enter again. Press Enter to repeat LINE. Type 1900,200 and press Enter. Type @0,-600 and press Enter, then press Enter again. You now have two short lines crossing the bottom wall 900 apart. Type TR and press Enter. The history says Current settings: Quick mode, edges: No extend. Click the inner bottom line between the two jambs. That piece disappears. (Rest the pointer on it first: the piece that will go turns grey and dashed.) Click the outer bottom line between the two jambs. That piece disappears too. Click the part of each jamb that sticks out above the wall, and the part below it. Press Enter to end the command. You should see a 900 opening in the bottom wall, with the jambs closing the wall ends. In Quick mode (the default) Trim does not ask you to choose cutting edges first: every visible object is a cutting edge. Pick the piece you want gone, or drag a path through several pieces to trim them all at once. Type U and press Enter to put back the last pick or drag without leaving the command. Fence, crossing, chosen edges and Shift to extend are in Trim and extend. Extend a line to meet another EXTEND lengthens a line, arc or polyline end until it meets the next object in that direction. Type L and press Enter. Type 1000,2000 and press Enter. Type @2000,0 and press Enter, then press Enter again. The line stops 2000 short of the right wall. Type EX and press Enter. Click the new line near its right end. Press Enter to end the command. The line now runs to the inner face of the right wall. Every visible object is a boundary; pick near the end that should grow. Inside TRIM, Shift+click does the same. Break and lengthen BREAK (BR, or Modify › More › Break) cuts a gap without a cutting edge: click the object where the gap starts (that click is the first point), then click where it ends. On a circle the part counter-clockwise from the first point to the second goes. F at the second question lets you give the first point again, and BREAKATPOINT splits an object in two at one point with no gap. LENGTHEN (LEN) changes a length at one end: Type LEN and press Enter. Click a line to see its current length; the prompt asks again. Choose how: DE (Delta: an amount to add, negative to take off; A for an arc's angle), P (Percent: 50 halves it), T (Total: the length it should be) or DY (Dynamic: drag the end). Click each object near the end that should move. U takes back the last one; Enter ends. Lines, arcs and open polylines (their end segment) can be lengthened. Common mistakes A whole line disappeared. Nothing crossed it, so Quick mode erased it. Type U to put it back; check that the cutting line really crosses it (zoom in) and is not on a layer that is off. \"No boundary in that direction.\" Extend found nothing to run into. Pick nearer the end that should grow. \"Radius is too large.\" The arc would run past the end of one of the lines. Use a smaller radius. \"Only the first or last segment of an open polyline can be cut back.\" Pick two segments of the polyline next to each other, or type P to round every corner of it. The offset went to the wrong side. The side is where you click after picking the object. Undo with Ctrl+Z and click on the other side. \"Cannot offset that far.\" The offset would turn the arc, circle or polyline inside out. Use a smaller distance.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"trim-and-extend","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Trim and extend","summary":"Cut lines back where others cross them and lengthen them to meet others, one piece at a time or many at once by dragging a path through them.","section":"tasks","order":52,"keywords":["trimextendmode","window drag","trim","extend","tr","ex","quick trim","drag","freehand","path","fence","crossing","shift","cutting edges","boundary edges","edgemode","mode","standard","quick","erase","preview","walls","cleanup"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"trim-one-piece","text":"Trim one piece","level":2},{"id":"trim-many-pieces-in-one-drag","text":"Trim many pieces in one drag","level":2},{"id":"extend-instead-with-shift","text":"Extend instead, with Shift","level":2},{"id":"trim-inside-a-window","text":"Trim inside a window","level":2},{"id":"choose-the-cutting-edges-standard-mode","text":"Choose the cutting edges (Standard mode)","level":2},{"id":"edges-that-would-reach","text":"Edges that would reach","level":2},{"id":"erase-without-leaving","text":"Erase without leaving","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ETRIM\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXTEND\u003C/code\u003E work, and the fast ways to use them: drag through many pieces at once, hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to switch between trimming and extending, and see what will go before you click.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"trim-one-piece\"\u003ETrim one piece\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw a long horizontal line and two short lines across it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003ETR\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The history says \u003Ccode\u003ECurrent settings: Quick mode, edges: No extend\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERest the pointer on the long line between the two short ones. That piece turns grey and dashed: it is what will go.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick. The piece disappears.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to end the command.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn Quick mode (the default) every visible object is a cutting edge, so you never choose edges first. Click the piece you want gone. An object that nothing crosses is erased whole, and the history says so.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"trim-many-pieces-in-one-drag\"\u003ETrim many pieces in one drag\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw a long horizontal line and ten short vertical lines across it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003ETR\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress the mouse button above the long line, to the left of the first vertical line, and drag across all ten above the long line.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile you drag, the path shows as a dashed line, and every piece it has crossed turns grey. Let go: all ten top pieces are trimmed in one step. Type \u003Ccode\u003EU\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to put them all back at once.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou can also click two empty spots: the line between them works like a dragged path. Type \u003Ccode\u003EF\u003C/code\u003E (Fence) to click a run of points instead, then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:touch\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ETouch\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDrag a finger the same way. A tap on a piece trims it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"extend-instead-with-shift\"\u003EExtend instead, with Shift\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw a vertical line, and five short horizontal lines to its left that stop before it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003ETR\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Rest the pointer on a short line: the part it would grow by shows grey and dashed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStill holding \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, drag down through the five short lines.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAll five now run to the vertical line. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E works the other way round in \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXTEND\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EEX\u003C/code\u003E): a click extends, a \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click trims. Extend lengthens the end nearest where you click or where the path crosses.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"trim-inside-a-window\"\u003ETrim inside a window\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003ETR\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick two corners round the pieces to remove.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery piece that lies at least partly inside the window is trimmed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo get a window just by dragging, without typing \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDragging to trim and extend\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EA window\u003C/span\u003E. Then pressing on an empty spot and dragging draws a box, and every piece it touches is trimmed (or extended, in \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXTEND\u003C/code\u003E, or with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E). \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EA path\u003C/span\u003E (the start) brings the freehand path back. The settings line at the start of TRIM says which one is on.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"choose-the-cutting-edges-standard-mode\"\u003EChoose the cutting edges (Standard mode)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESometimes only one line should cut, such as a wall face with many other lines crossing it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003ETR\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EO\u003C/code\u003E (mOde) and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, then type \u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E (Standard) and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003ESelect cutting edges\u003C/code\u003E, click the edge lines (or drag a window), then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Pressing \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E with nothing selected makes every object an edge.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick or drag through the pieces to remove.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOnly the chosen edges cut, and an object no edge crosses is left alone. The mode is kept until you close the page; type \u003Ccode\u003EO\u003C/code\u003E, then \u003Ccode\u003EQ\u003C/code\u003E to go back to Quick. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ETRIMEXTENDMODE\u003C/code\u003E sets it before you start, as the variable of that name does elsewhere: \u003Ccode\u003E0\u003C/code\u003E Standard, \u003Ccode\u003E1\u003C/code\u003E Quick. \u003Ccode\u003ET\u003C/code\u003E (cuTting edges) chooses new edges at any time; in \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXTEND\u003C/code\u003E it is \u003Ccode\u003EB\u003C/code\u003E (Boundary edges).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"edges-that-would-reach\"\u003EEdges that would reach\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003ETR\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EE\u003C/code\u003E (Edge) and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, then \u003Ccode\u003EE\u003C/code\u003E (Extend) and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENow a cutting line or arc that stops short counts as if it went on: a short line cuts where it would cross if it were longer. Type \u003Ccode\u003EE\u003C/code\u003E, then \u003Ccode\u003EN\u003C/code\u003E (No extend) to switch back. This is the same as EDGEMODE 1 and 0.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"erase-without-leaving\"\u003EErase without leaving\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E (eRase) and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, pick the objects to erase, and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. TRIM carries on.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA whole line disappeared.\u003C/strong\u003E In Quick mode an object nothing crosses is erased. Type \u003Ccode\u003EU\u003C/code\u003E to put it back, or switch to Standard mode (\u003Ccode\u003EO\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;Nothing to trim there: no chosen edge crosses that object.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E Standard mode only cuts at the edges you chose. Type \u003Ccode\u003ET\u003C/code\u003E to choose them again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;No boundary in that direction.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E Extend found nothing ahead. Click nearer the end that should grow, or turn on \u003Ccode\u003EE\u003C/code\u003E (Edge, Extend) for boundaries that stop short.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe drag drew a lasso instead.\u003C/strong\u003E A drag only trims while TRIM or EXTEND is asking for an object. With no command running, a drag selects (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/lasso-selection\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/lasso-selection\"\u003ELasso selection\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOnly lines, arcs, circles and polylines can be trimmed.\u003C/strong\u003E Explode a block first to trim its lines.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will learn How TRIM and EXTEND work, and the fast ways to use them: drag through many pieces at once, hold Shift to switch between trimming and extending, and see what will go before you click. Trim one piece Draw a long horizontal line and two short lines across it. Type TR and press Enter. The history says Current settings: Quick mode, edges: No extend. Rest the pointer on the long line between the two short ones. That piece turns grey and dashed: it is what will go. Click. The piece disappears. Press Enter to end the command. In Quick mode (the default) every visible object is a cutting edge, so you never choose edges first. Click the piece you want gone. An object that nothing crosses is erased whole, and the history says so. Trim many pieces in one drag Draw a long horizontal line and ten short vertical lines across it. Type TR and press Enter. Press the mouse button above the long line, to the left of the first vertical line, and drag across all ten above the long line. While you drag, the path shows as a dashed line, and every piece it has crossed turns grey. Let go: all ten top pieces are trimmed in one step. Type U and press Enter to put them all back at once. You can also click two empty spots: the line between them works like a dragged path. Type F (Fence) to click a run of points instead, then press Enter. Drag a finger the same way. A tap on a piece trims it. Extend instead, with Shift Draw a vertical line, and five short horizontal lines to its left that stop before it. Type TR and press Enter. Hold Shift. Rest the pointer on a short line: the part it would grow by shows grey and dashed. Still holding Shift, drag down through the five short lines. All five now run to the vertical line. Shift works the other way round in EXTEND (EX): a click extends, a Shift+click trims. Extend lengthens the end nearest where you click or where the path crosses. Trim inside a window Type TR and press Enter. Type C and press Enter. Click two corners round the pieces to remove. Every piece that lies at least partly inside the window is trimmed. To get a window just by dragging, without typing C: Settings › CadCraft › Dragging to trim and extend › A window. Then pressing on an empty spot and dragging draws a box, and every piece it touches is trimmed (or extended, in EXTEND, or with Shift). A path (the start) brings the freehand path back. The settings line at the start of TRIM says which one is on. Choose the cutting edges (Standard mode) Sometimes only one line should cut, such as a wall face with many other lines crossing it. Type TR and press Enter. Type O (mOde) and press Enter, then type S (Standard) and press Enter. At Select cutting edges, click the edge lines (or drag a window), then press Enter. Pressing Enter with nothing selected makes every object an edge. Click or drag through the pieces to remove. Only the chosen edges cut, and an object no edge crosses is left alone. The mode is kept until you close the page; type O, then Q to go back to Quick. TRIMEXTENDMODE sets it before you start, as the variable of that name does elsewhere: 0 Standard, 1 Quick. T (cuTting edges) chooses new edges at any time; in EXTEND it is B (Boundary edges). Edges that would reach Type TR and press Enter. Type E (Edge) and press Enter, then E (Extend) and press Enter. Now a cutting line or arc that stops short counts as if it went on: a short line cuts where it would cross if it were longer. Type E, then N (No extend) to switch back. This is the same as EDGEMODE 1 and 0. Erase without leaving Type R (eRase) and press Enter, pick the objects to erase, and press Enter. TRIM carries on. Common mistakes A whole line disappeared. In Quick mode an object nothing crosses is erased. Type U to put it back, or switch to Standard mode (O, S). \"Nothing to trim there: no chosen edge crosses that object.\" Standard mode only cuts at the edges you chose. Type T to choose them again. \"No boundary in that direction.\" Extend found nothing ahead. Click nearer the end that should grow, or turn on E (Edge, Extend) for boundaries that stop short. The drag drew a lasso instead. A drag only trims while TRIM or EXTEND is asking for an object. With no command running, a drag selects (see Lasso selection). Only lines, arcs, circles and polylines can be trimmed. Explode a block first to trim its lines.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"splines","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Splines and polylines: SPLINE, SPLINEDIT, PEDIT","summary":"Draw a smooth curve through points or from a control polygon, edit its fit and control points, and edit polylines (join, close, width, fit, spline, decurve) or turn a spline into a polyline.","section":"tasks","order":55,"keywords":["spline","splinedit","spl","spe","pedit","pe","fit points","control points","weights","nurbs","polyline edit","join","fit curve","decurve","convert to polyline","precision","tangent","curve","fit tolerance"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-make","text":"What you will make","level":2},{"id":"draw-a-spline-through-points","text":"Draw a spline through points","level":2},{"id":"draw-one-from-a-control-polygon","text":"Draw one from a control polygon","level":3},{"id":"turn-a-polyline-into-a-spline","text":"Turn a polyline into a spline","level":3},{"id":"edit-a-spline","text":"Edit a spline","level":2},{"id":"edit-a-polyline","text":"Edit a polyline","level":2},{"id":"in-files","text":"In files","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-make\"\u003EWhat you will make\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA curve through five points, then the same curve edited three ways, and a polyline built from three lines.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-a-spline-through-points\"\u003EDraw a spline through points\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003ESPL\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick or type the points the curve should pass through: \u003Ccode\u003E0,0\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E100,80\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E200,-30\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E350,50\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E500,0\u003C/code\u003E. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E after the last one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStart tangent\u003C/span\u003E: click a point the curve should leave towards, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to let it decide. Do the same for the end tangent.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe curve passes through every point. Type \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E instead of \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to close it (the curve comes back to the first point). \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"U\"\u003EU\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EU\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EUndo\u003C/code\u003E) takes the last point back while you are drawing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"draw-one-from-a-control-polygon\"\u003EDraw one from a control polygon\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EM\u003C/code\u003E at the first prompt, then \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E (Control vertices) and a degree from 1 to 5 (3 is the usual). Now the points you pick are the corners of a polygon that pulls the curve towards them; the curve starts on the first and ends on the last. A closed one wraps round, with no corner at the join.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"turn-a-polyline-into-a-spline\"\u003ETurn a polyline into a spline\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAt the first prompt type \u003Ccode\u003EO\u003C/code\u003E (Object) and select polylines or lines. They become splines through their vertices; the originals go.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"edit-a-spline\"\u003EEdit a spline\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect it and type \u003Ccode\u003ESPE\u003C/code\u003E (or type \u003Ccode\u003ESPE\u003C/code\u003E and click it). Grips show the fit points (or the control points of a spline that has none): drag one to reshape the curve. At the prompt:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFit data\u003C/strong\u003E (a spline drawn through points): \u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E adds a fit point (click where), \u003Ccode\u003ED\u003C/code\u003E deletes one (click near it), \u003Ccode\u003EM\u003C/code\u003E moves one (click near it, then click its new place), \u003Ccode\u003ET\u003C/code\u003E sets the end tangents, \u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E purges the fit points, so the spline is edited by its control points from then on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEdit vertex\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E adds a control point without changing the curve, \u003Ccode\u003EM\u003C/code\u003E moves one, \u003Ccode\u003EW\u003C/code\u003E gives one a \u003Cstrong\u003Eweight\u003C/strong\u003E (above 1 pulls the curve closer to it, below 1 lets it go).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOpen\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003EClose\u003C/strong\u003E, and \u003Cstrong\u003EReverse\u003C/strong\u003E (the curve runs the other way).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Econvert to Polyline\u003C/strong\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E): the spline becomes a polyline; the precision is how many segments each span gets (10 is the usual).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo\u003C/strong\u003E takes the last change back; \u003Cstrong\u003EeXit\u003C/strong\u003E ends.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E shows a spline&#039;s degree, how many fit and control points it has, its length, and lets you open or close it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"edit-a-polyline\"\u003EEdit a polyline\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect a polyline and type \u003Ccode\u003EPE\u003C/code\u003E. A line, an arc or a spline you pick is first offered as a polyline to turn into (a spline asks for the precision).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EClose\u003C/strong\u003E / \u003Cstrong\u003EOpen\u003C/strong\u003E joins or separates the ends.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EJoin\u003C/strong\u003E adds lines, arcs and polylines whose ends meet the polyline&#039;s end. \u003Ccode\u003EFuzz distance\u003C/code\u003E is how far apart ends may be and still join (0 means they must meet).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWidth\u003C/strong\u003E gives the whole polyline one width (0 removes it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFit\u003C/strong\u003E replaces the polyline by a smooth curve that passes through its vertices; \u003Cstrong\u003ESpline\u003C/strong\u003E by a cubic B-spline that follows them (it starts and ends at the first and last vertex and stays near the others). Both become a polyline of short segments, and the original corners are kept, so \u003Cstrong\u003EDecurve\u003C/strong\u003E brings them back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELtype gen\u003C/strong\u003E says whether a dashed linetype runs on along the whole polyline or starts again at each vertex.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EReverse\u003C/strong\u003E changes the order of its vertices. \u003Cstrong\u003EMultiple\u003C/strong\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EM\u003C/code\u003E at the first prompt) edits several polylines with the same options.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEditing the vertices of a polyline in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E or with grips stays as it was: PEDIT has no vertex editor.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"in-files\"\u003EIn files\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDWG and DXF 2000 keep a spline as a spline: degree, control points, knots, weights, fit points and tangents. A spline you open and don&#039;t change is written back exactly as it came. DXF R12 has no spline: it is saved as a polyline along the curve, and the history says how many.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOffset, trim, extend, fillet, break and join do not work on splines. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXPLODE\u003C/code\u003E a spline, or \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPEDIT\u003C/code\u003E it, to get a polyline those commands take.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe curve overshoots between close points.\u003C/strong\u003E A curve through points bends where the points do. Use fewer points, or a control polygon.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;That is not a spline.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E SPLINEDIT only takes splines; \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPEDIT\u003C/code\u003E edits polylines.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA closed fit spline has a slightly different shape in another program.\u003C/strong\u003E CadCraft closes it with one smooth join at the first point; another program may close it with a periodic curve. A closed control-point spline is the same in both.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will make A curve through five points, then the same curve edited three ways, and a polyline built from three lines. Draw a spline through points Type SPL and press Enter. Click or type the points the curve should pass through: 0,0, 100,80, 200,-30, 350,50, 500,0. Press Enter after the last one. Start tangent: click a point the curve should leave towards, or press Enter to let it decide. Do the same for the end tangent. The curve passes through every point. Type C instead of Enter to close it (the curve comes back to the first point). U (Undo) takes the last point back while you are drawing. Draw one from a control polygon Type M at the first prompt, then C (Control vertices) and a degree from 1 to 5 (3 is the usual). Now the points you pick are the corners of a polygon that pulls the curve towards them; the curve starts on the first and ends on the last. A closed one wraps round, with no corner at the join. Turn a polyline into a spline At the first prompt type O (Object) and select polylines or lines. They become splines through their vertices; the originals go. Edit a spline Select it and type SPE (or type SPE and click it). Grips show the fit points (or the control points of a spline that has none): drag one to reshape the curve. At the prompt: Fit data (a spline drawn through points): A adds a fit point (click where), D deletes one (click near it), M moves one (click near it, then click its new place), T sets the end tangents, P purges the fit points, so the spline is edited by its control points from then on. Edit vertex: A adds a control point without changing the curve, M moves one, W gives one a weight (above 1 pulls the curve closer to it, below 1 lets it go). Open or Close, and Reverse (the curve runs the other way). convert to Polyline (P): the spline becomes a polyline; the precision is how many segments each span gets (10 is the usual). Undo takes the last change back; eXit ends. Properties shows a spline's degree, how many fit and control points it has, its length, and lets you open or close it. Edit a polyline Select a polyline and type PE. A line, an arc or a spline you pick is first offered as a polyline to turn into (a spline asks for the precision). Close / Open joins or separates the ends. Join adds lines, arcs and polylines whose ends meet the polyline's end. Fuzz distance is how far apart ends may be and still join (0 means they must meet). Width gives the whole polyline one width (0 removes it). Fit replaces the polyline by a smooth curve that passes through its vertices; Spline by a cubic B-spline that follows them (it starts and ends at the first and last vertex and stays near the others). Both become a polyline of short segments, and the original corners are kept, so Decurve brings them back. Ltype gen says whether a dashed linetype runs on along the whole polyline or starts again at each vertex. Reverse changes the order of its vertices. Multiple (M at the first prompt) edits several polylines with the same options. Editing the vertices of a polyline in Properties or with grips stays as it was: PEDIT has no vertex editor. In files DWG and DXF 2000 keep a spline as a spline: degree, control points, knots, weights, fit points and tangents. A spline you open and don't change is written back exactly as it came. DXF R12 has no spline: it is saved as a polyline along the curve, and the history says how many. Offset, trim, extend, fillet, break and join do not work on splines. EXPLODE a spline, or PEDIT it, to get a polyline those commands take. Common mistakes The curve overshoots between close points. A curve through points bends where the points do. Use fewer points, or a control polygon. \"That is not a spline.\" SPLINEDIT only takes splines; PEDIT edits polylines. A closed fit spline has a slightly different shape in another program. CadCraft closes it with one smooth join at the first point; another program may close it with a periodic curve. A closed control-point spline is the same in both.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"grips-and-properties","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Edit with grips and Properties","summary":"Stretch, move and resize objects by their grips, and type exact lengths, radii, angles and coordinates in the Properties panel.","section":"tasks","order":60,"keywords":["grips","properties","stretch","length","radius","coordinates","edit","quick properties","properties palette","change","layer","colour","color"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"grips","text":"Grips","level":2},{"id":"move-a-grip","text":"Move a grip","level":3},{"id":"properties","text":"Properties","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETwo quick ways to change an object without a command: drag its \u003Cstrong\u003Egrips\u003C/strong\u003E, or type new values in the \u003Cstrong\u003EProperties\u003C/strong\u003E panel on the right.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"grips\"\u003EGrips\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen you select objects (up to 200 at a time) with no command running, small squares appear on them. These are grips:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EObject\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EGrips\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELine\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBoth ends, and the middle (moves the whole line)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECircle\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe center (moves it) and four points on the circle (change the radius)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArc\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe center, and both ends (change where it starts and stops)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPolyline\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEvery vertex\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EText, point, block reference\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe insertion point\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEllipse\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe center\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDimension\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIts defining points and the dimension line position\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EViewport\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe center (moves it) and the four corners (resize it)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"move-a-grip\"\u003EMove a grip\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw a line and click it. Three grips appear.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the grip at one end. The status bar says &quot;Click where this grip goes (Esc to cancel). Snaps and Ortho apply.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the crosshair. A preview follows.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the new position, or snap to a point on another object and click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe end moves and the rest of the line stays put. You can also press on a grip and drag it, then let go where it should go.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou cannot type a coordinate for a grip. For an exact position, snap to a point, or type the value in Properties.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or right-click to cancel a grip move.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"properties\"\u003EProperties\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe right panel shows the properties of what is selected.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a line. The panel heading reads &quot;Line&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength\u003C/span\u003E, type \u003Ccode\u003E2500\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe line is now exactly 2500 long, keeping its start point and angle. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or clicking elsewhere applies a value; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E puts the old value back. Every change is one undo step, also when many objects change at once.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFields take expressions (\u003Ccode\u003E=2*45\u003C/code\u003E) and lengths with units (\u003Ccode\u003E3&#039;6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E900mm\u003C/code\u003E), every point has X, Y and Z, and several objects can be changed together. All of it is in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/properties-panel\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/properties-panel\"\u003EThe Properties panel\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENo grips appear.\u003C/strong\u003E A command is running (press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), or more than 200 objects are selected, or the object is on a locked layer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe grip went to the wrong place.\u003C/strong\u003E A snap caught it. Hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while you click to ignore snaps.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETyping in Properties did nothing.\u003C/strong\u003E Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or click elsewhere to apply. A refused entry (zero or negative lengths and radii, text that is not a value) turns the field red and says why.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETyped letters went into a Properties field.\u003C/strong\u003E While a field has the cursor, keys type there. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, then type the command.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will learn Two quick ways to change an object without a command: drag its grips, or type new values in the Properties panel on the right. Grips When you select objects (up to 200 at a time) with no command running, small squares appear on them. These are grips: Object Grips Line Both ends, and the middle (moves the whole line) Circle The center (moves it) and four points on the circle (change the radius) Arc The center, and both ends (change where it starts and stops) Polyline Every vertex Text, point, block reference The insertion point Ellipse The center Dimension Its defining points and the dimension line position Viewport The center (moves it) and the four corners (resize it) Move a grip Draw a line and click it. Three grips appear. Click the grip at one end. The status bar says \"Click where this grip goes (Esc to cancel). Snaps and Ortho apply.\" Move the crosshair. A preview follows. Click the new position, or snap to a point on another object and click. The end moves and the rest of the line stays put. You can also press on a grip and drag it, then let go where it should go. You cannot type a coordinate for a grip. For an exact position, snap to a point, or type the value in Properties. Press Esc or right-click to cancel a grip move. Properties The right panel shows the properties of what is selected. Click a line. The panel heading reads \"Line\". Click in Length, type 2500 and press Enter. The line is now exactly 2500 long, keeping its start point and angle. Enter or clicking elsewhere applies a value; Esc puts the old value back. Every change is one undo step, also when many objects change at once. Fields take expressions (=2*45) and lengths with units (3'6\", 900mm), every point has X, Y and Z, and several objects can be changed together. All of it is in The Properties panel. Common mistakes No grips appear. A command is running (press Esc), or more than 200 objects are selected, or the object is on a locked layer. The grip went to the wrong place. A snap caught it. Hold Shift while you click to ignore snaps. Typing in Properties did nothing. Press Enter or click elsewhere to apply. A refused entry (zero or negative lengths and radii, text that is not a value) turns the field red and says why. Typed letters went into a Properties field. While a field has the cursor, keys type there. Press Enter or Esc, then type the command.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"use-with-ai-assistants","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Use CadCraft with an AI assistant","summary":"Let your own AI assistant or script work with CadCraft: what it may do, how to connect any MCP client, and how you stay in charge (permissions, undo, the activity log, Disconnect).","section":"tasks","order":60,"keywords":["ai","assistant","agent","mcp","model context protocol","script","automation","command line","cli","json","token","permissions","disconnect","activity","folders","paths","svg","batch"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"turn-it-on","text":"Turn it on","level":2},{"id":"let-it-use-files-in-folders-you-choose","text":"Let it use files in folders you choose","level":2},{"id":"connect-your-assistant","text":"Connect your assistant","level":2},{"id":"you-stay-in-charge","text":"You stay in charge","level":2},{"id":"what-an-assistant-can-ask-for","text":"What an assistant can ask for","level":2},{"id":"work-on-files-without-opening-cadcraft","text":"Work on files without opening CadCraft","level":2},{"id":"if-something-doesn-t-work","text":"If something doesn't work","level":2},{"id":"see-also","text":"See also","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to let an AI assistant (or a script you wrote) look at a drawing, run CadCraft&#039;s commands, plot and save, and how to decide what it is allowed to touch.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECadCraft has \u003Cstrong\u003Eno AI of its own\u003C/strong\u003E and sends nothing anywhere. You bring the assistant: any program that speaks the Model Context Protocol (MCP). CadCraft only gives it tools, and you choose which.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThere are two ways in:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe drawing you have open.\u003C/strong\u003E The assistant works in this window, next to you, through CadCraft Studio on your computer. This is what \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAssistants\u003C/span\u003E is for.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDrawings on your computer, without opening the app.\u003C/strong\u003E The CadCraft MCP server and the \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft\u003C/code\u003E command line read and write DWG and DXF files in folders you name. See &quot;Work on files without opening CadCraft&quot; below.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"turn-it-on\"\u003ETurn it on\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E (the gear in the app bar, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) and go to the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAssistants\u003C/span\u003E, tick \u003Cstrong\u003ELet AI assistants and scripts use CadCraft\u003C/strong\u003E. It is off until you do.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose what it may do. \u003Cstrong\u003ERead\u003C/strong\u003E is ticked to start with; tick the others only when you need them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EPermission\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat the assistant can do\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERead\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELook at the drawing: describe it, list and find objects, see the selection, describe the view and take a picture of it, check it against layer and drafting rules\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERun commands on the drawing and change the selection. Each action is one step you can undo\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESave\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESave the drawing (on the desktop, to its file; in a browser, as a download)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExport\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlot to PDF and make other files\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"let-it-use-files-in-folders-you-choose\"\u003ELet it use files in folders you choose\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn CadCraft Studio an assistant can open, save and plot files by name instead of using the file dialogs, but only inside the folders you add under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAssistants\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFolders it may use\u003C/span\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a folder…\u003C/span\u003E and pick the folder in Studio&#039;s own dialog.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe assistant can now list the files there (\u003Ccode\u003Efiles_list\u003C/code\u003E), open a drawing (\u003Ccode\u003Edrawing_open\u003C/code\u003E), save it there in a DWG version or as DXF, plot to a PDF or SVG file, load a LISP file, and give a command the file it asks for (\u003Ccode\u003EINSERT\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003EF\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EXATTACH\u003C/code\u003E, a point file) or say where a command&#039;s file goes (\u003Ccode\u003ECOUNT\u003C/code\u003E&#039;s CSV, \u003Ccode\u003EPUBLISH\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove\u003C/span\u003E takes a folder back at once. Paths anywhere else are refused, and a file that is already there is replaced only when the assistant asks for that or wrote the file itself.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpening still needs \u003Cstrong\u003ERead\u003C/strong\u003E; writing a drawing needs \u003Cstrong\u003ESave\u003C/strong\u003E; any other file needs \u003Cstrong\u003EExport\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETools outside the permissions you ticked are not offered to the assistant at all, and if it asks for one anyway, CadCraft refuses and writes it in the activity log. Turning the switch off ends every connection at once. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset CadCraft settings\u003C/span\u003E turns it off too.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"connect-your-assistant\"\u003EConnect your assistant\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAssistants connect through \u003Cstrong\u003ECadCraft Studio\u003C/strong\u003E on your computer: it runs a small server that only this computer can reach (address \u003Ccode\u003E127.0.0.1\u003C/code\u003E), protected by a token that Studio makes each time it starts. Open Studio, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConnect an assistant\u003C/span\u003E, and copy the address and the token.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn your assistant, add an MCP server of type HTTP with the address (it looks like \u003Ccode\u003Ehttp://127.0.0.1:47615/mcp\u003C/code\u003E) and the header \u003Ccode\u003EAuthorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;\u003C/code\u003E. Studio shows the settings to paste.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew token\u003C/span\u003E in Studio makes every connected assistant reconnect with the new token. Never share the token: anyone with it can use the apps you switched on, for as long as Studio is running.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA browser tab can&#039;t open a connection for an assistant: in the browser apps the switch has no effect until you use CadCraft Studio on your computer.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"you-stay-in-charge\"\u003EYou stay in charge\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEvery action is one undo step\u003C/strong\u003E, labelled with the assistant&#039;s name for it, for example &quot;Assistant: LINE&quot;. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to take back the assistant&#039;s last action as a whole.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EYou see what it does.\u003C/strong\u003E Its commands appear in the command history with \u003Cstrong\u003E[Assistant]\u003C/strong\u003E in front, and the drawing redraws as it works.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIt waits for you.\u003C/strong\u003E While you are in the middle of a command, the assistant can read but not change the drawing; it is told to try again when you are done.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EYou know when one is connected.\u003C/strong\u003E A button in the app bar names the assistant while it is connected. Click it to see what it may do, or to \u003Cstrong\u003EDisconnect\u003C/strong\u003E it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EActivity.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAssistants\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EActivity\u003C/span\u003E lists every call: when, which tool, done or refused, and the undo step it made. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave the log\u003C/span\u003E keeps a copy.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-an-assistant-can-ask-for\"\u003EWhat an assistant can ask for\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETool\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EPermission\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eapp_info\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Edrawing_describe\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eview_describe\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERead\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe drawing&#039;s name, units, layers, blocks, sheets; what is on screen\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Efiles_list\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Edrawing_open\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERead\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStudio: the files in your folders; open one in this window\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eobjects_list\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eobject_get\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eselection_get\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERead\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EObjects with their geometry, length and area (or only how many, by type and layer); the selection\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Edrawing_check\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERead\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERules such as allowed layers, nothing on layer 0, colours ByLayer, text heights\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eview_screenshot\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERead\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA picture of the drawing as shown\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Ecommand_run\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETypes at the command line: \u003Ccode\u003ELINE 0,0 @100,0 @0,50 \u003C/code\u003E reads as you would type it (a space ends an answer), or a list with one answer each. It can leave a command open and carry on in its next call\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Escript_run\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA script (\u003Ccode\u003E.scr\u003C/code\u003E) or a LISP file, as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAPPLOAD\u003C/span\u003E loads it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eblock_insert\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlaces a block with its attribute values in one step\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eselection_set\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eundo\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelects objects for the next command; undoes its own steps\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Edrawing_save\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESave\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECadCraft&#039;s own \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as\u003C/span\u003E into one of your folders\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Edrawing_plot\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExport\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA PDF or SVG of a sheet or of the model, at a scale or fitted (PDF underlays are not printed this way yet)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile an assistant runs a command, CadCraft asks its questions on the command line instead of in a dialog (for example whether to insert a whole drawing or one block), so the assistant can answer them. The library and Find still need you; the assistant is told to ask you.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"work-on-files-without-opening-cadcraft\"\u003EWork on files without opening CadCraft\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe CadCraft MCP server (\u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft-mcp\u003C/code\u003E) and command line (\u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft\u003C/code\u003E) run CadCraft&#039;s own drawing code in Node.js, with no window. They need Node.js 20 or newer.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe MCP server\u003C/strong\u003E works only inside the folders you name, and only with the permissions you give it: \u003Ccode\u003Enpx @getcadcraft/mcp --root ~/Drawings --allow read,edit\u003C/code\u003E. Without \u003Ccode\u003E--root\u003C/code\u003E it can&#039;t open any file; without \u003Ccode\u003E--allow\u003C/code\u003E it can only read. It can also read IFC models, takeoffs and the markups in PDF sets.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe command line\u003C/strong\u003E is for your own scripts: \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft info plan.dwg\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft run cleanup.scr plan.dwg -o plan-clean.dwg\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft convert plan.dwg plan.dxf\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft plot plan.dwg plan.pdf --all\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft plot plan.dwg plan.svg\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft check plan.dwg --rules rules.json\u003C/code\u003E. Several files at once: \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft convert *.dwg out --to dxf\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft plot *.dwg pdfs --to pdf\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft check *.dwg --rules rules.json\u003C/code\u003E. Add \u003Ccode\u003E--json\u003C/code\u003E for output a program can read.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf \u003Ccode\u003Enpx @getcadcraft/mcp\u003C/code\u003E says the package is not found, it has not been published yet. News on getcadcraft.com will say when it is.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"if-something-doesn-t-work\"\u003EIf something doesn&#039;t work\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe assistant sees no CadCraft tools:\u003C/strong\u003E check that the switch is on in this window, and that the window is open in Studio.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;needs the edit permission&quot;:\u003C/strong\u003E tick \u003Cstrong\u003EEdit\u003C/strong\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAssistants\u003C/span\u003E, then ask again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;outside the folders assistants may use&quot;:\u003C/strong\u003E add that folder under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAssistants\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFolders it may use\u003C/span\u003E, or ask the assistant to use a file in a folder you already added.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;401&quot; or &quot;token&quot; errors:\u003C/strong\u003E the token changed (Studio restarted, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew token\u003C/span\u003E). Copy the new one into the assistant&#039;s settings.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIts change was wrong:\u003C/strong\u003E press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. One press takes back one whole action of the assistant.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"see-also\"\u003ESee also\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/assistants-recipes\" data-topic=\"basics/assistants-recipes\"\u003ERecipes for AI assistants\u003C/a\u003E: worked examples, each with the tools it calls.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to let an AI assistant (or a script you wrote) look at a drawing, run CadCraft's commands, plot and save, and how to decide what it is allowed to touch. CadCraft has no AI of its own and sends nothing anywhere. You bring the assistant: any program that speaks the Model Context Protocol (MCP). CadCraft only gives it tools, and you choose which. There are two ways in: The drawing you have open. The assistant works in this window, next to you, through CadCraft Studio on your computer. This is what Settings › CadCraft › Assistants is for. Drawings on your computer, without opening the app. The CadCraft MCP server and the cadcraft command line read and write DWG and DXF files in folders you name. See \"Work on files without opening CadCraft\" below. Turn it on Open Settings (the gear in the app bar, or Ctrl+,) and go to the CadCraft tab. Under Assistants, tick Let AI assistants and scripts use CadCraft. It is off until you do. Choose what it may do. Read is ticked to start with; tick the others only when you need them. Permission What the assistant can do Read Look at the drawing: describe it, list and find objects, see the selection, describe the view and take a picture of it, check it against layer and drafting rules Edit Run commands on the drawing and change the selection. Each action is one step you can undo Save Save the drawing (on the desktop, to its file; in a browser, as a download) Export Plot to PDF and make other files Let it use files in folders you choose In CadCraft Studio an assistant can open, save and plot files by name instead of using the file dialogs, but only inside the folders you add under Assistants › Folders it may use: Choose Add a folder… and pick the folder in Studio's own dialog. The assistant can now list the files there (files_list), open a drawing (drawing_open), save it there in a DWG version or as DXF, plot to a PDF or SVG file, load a LISP file, and give a command the file it asks for (INSERT F, XATTACH, a point file) or say where a command's file goes (COUNT's CSV, PUBLISH). Remove takes a folder back at once. Paths anywhere else are refused, and a file that is already there is replaced only when the assistant asks for that or wrote the file itself. Opening still needs Read; writing a drawing needs Save; any other file needs Export. Tools outside the permissions you ticked are not offered to the assistant at all, and if it asks for one anyway, CadCraft refuses and writes it in the activity log. Turning the switch off ends every connection at once. Reset CadCraft settings turns it off too. Connect your assistant Assistants connect through CadCraft Studio on your computer: it runs a small server that only this computer can reach (address 127.0.0.1), protected by a token that Studio makes each time it starts. Open Studio, choose Connect an assistant, and copy the address and the token. In your assistant, add an MCP server of type HTTP with the address (it looks like http://127.0.0.1:47615/mcp) and the header Authorization: Bearer \u003Ctoken\u003E. Studio shows the settings to paste. New token in Studio makes every connected assistant reconnect with the new token. Never share the token: anyone with it can use the apps you switched on, for as long as Studio is running. A browser tab can't open a connection for an assistant: in the browser apps the switch has no effect until you use CadCraft Studio on your computer. You stay in charge Every action is one undo step, labelled with the assistant's name for it, for example \"Assistant: LINE\". Press Ctrl+Z to take back the assistant's last action as a whole. You see what it does. Its commands appear in the command history with [Assistant] in front, and the drawing redraws as it works. It waits for you. While you are in the middle of a command, the assistant can read but not change the drawing; it is told to try again when you are done. You know when one is connected. A button in the app bar names the assistant while it is connected. Click it to see what it may do, or to Disconnect it. Activity. Settings › CadCraft › Assistants › Activity lists every call: when, which tool, done or refused, and the undo step it made. Save the log keeps a copy. What an assistant can ask for Tool Permission What it does app_info, drawing_describe, view_describe Read The drawing's name, units, layers, blocks, sheets; what is on screen files_list, drawing_open Read Studio: the files in your folders; open one in this window objects_list, object_get, selection_get Read Objects with their geometry, length and area (or only how many, by type and layer); the selection drawing_check Read Rules such as allowed layers, nothing on layer 0, colours ByLayer, text heights view_screenshot Read A picture of the drawing as shown command_run Edit Types at the command line: LINE 0,0 @100,0 @0,50 reads as you would type it (a space ends an answer), or a list with one answer each. It can leave a command open and carry on in its next call script_run Edit A script (.scr) or a LISP file, as APPLOAD loads it block_insert Edit Places a block with its attribute values in one step selection_set, undo Edit Selects objects for the next command; undoes its own steps drawing_save Save CadCraft's own Save, or Save as into one of your folders drawing_plot Export A PDF or SVG of a sheet or of the model, at a scale or fitted (PDF underlays are not printed this way yet) While an assistant runs a command, CadCraft asks its questions on the command line instead of in a dialog (for example whether to insert a whole drawing or one block), so the assistant can answer them. The library and Find still need you; the assistant is told to ask you. Work on files without opening CadCraft The CadCraft MCP server (cadcraft-mcp) and command line (cadcraft) run CadCraft's own drawing code in Node.js, with no window. They need Node.js 20 or newer. The MCP server works only inside the folders you name, and only with the permissions you give it: npx @getcadcraft/mcp --root ~/Drawings --allow read,edit. Without --root it can't open any file; without --allow it can only read. It can also read IFC models, takeoffs and the markups in PDF sets. The command line is for your own scripts: cadcraft info plan.dwg, cadcraft run cleanup.scr plan.dwg -o plan-clean.dwg, cadcraft convert plan.dwg plan.dxf, cadcraft plot plan.dwg plan.pdf --all, cadcraft plot plan.dwg plan.svg, cadcraft check plan.dwg --rules rules.json. Several files at once: cadcraft convert *.dwg out --to dxf, cadcraft plot *.dwg pdfs --to pdf, cadcraft check *.dwg --rules rules.json. Add --json for output a program can read. If npx @getcadcraft/mcp says the package is not found, it has not been published yet. News on getcadcraft.com will say when it is. If something doesn't work The assistant sees no CadCraft tools: check that the switch is on in this window, and that the window is open in Studio. \"needs the edit permission\": tick Edit under Assistants, then ask again. \"outside the folders assistants may use\": add that folder under Assistants › Folders it may use, or ask the assistant to use a file in a folder you already added. \"401\" or \"token\" errors: the token changed (Studio restarted, or New token). Copy the new one into the assistant's settings. Its change was wrong: press Ctrl+Z. One press takes back one whole action of the assistant. See also Recipes for AI assistants: worked examples, each with the tools it calls.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"as-built-points","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Check as-built points","summary":"Read the points a survey crew measured and compare them with the drawing's points and blocks: within or outside a tolerance, not measured, extra — marked in the drawing and written as a CSV report.","section":"tasks","order":61,"keywords":["as built","as-built","asbuilt","survey","total station","setting out","stake out","point file","csv","txt","pnezd","penzd","xyz","tolerance","deviation","check","compare","exportpoints","layout points"],"headings":[],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EASBUILT\u003C/code\u003E (alias ASB) is the other half of \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXPORTPOINTS\u003C/code\u003E: you export the drawing&#039;s points for the field, the crew sets them out and measures what was built, and ASBUILT compares their file with the drawing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EASBUILT\u003C/code\u003E and select the same points and blocks you exported (Enter takes every POINT, as EXPORTPOINTS does).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPair the as-built points by\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cstrong\u003ENumber\u003C/strong\u003E (the crew kept your point numbers: give the first number you exported from) or \u003Cstrong\u003ENearest\u003C/strong\u003E (they numbered their own shots: each design point takes the closest one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGive the file&#039;s units and coordinates as you exported them (Drawing, Metres or Feet; World or UCS), and the \u003Cstrong\u003Etolerance\u003C/strong\u003E in drawing units.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick the crew&#039;s CSV or TXT file. CadCraft reads it the way CivilCraft does: commas, tabs, semicolons or spaces, a header row naming its columns, or PNEZD by column count.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe command line says how many points were paired, how many are within the tolerance, outside it, not measured and extra, and the largest distance. \u003Cstrong\u003EMark them in the drawing?\u003C/strong\u003E Yes puts them on the layer \u003Cstrong\u003EAS-BUILT\u003C/strong\u003E as one undo step: a point at each shot, its number and distance beside it, and a red line from the design point to every shot outside the tolerance. A CSV report downloads with every point: design and as-built coordinates, the differences east, north and in height, and the result.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee also \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/points-for-bim\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/points-for-bim\"\u003EPoints for BIM programs\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"ASBUILT (alias ASB) is the other half of EXPORTPOINTS: you export the drawing's points for the field, the crew sets them out and measures what was built, and ASBUILT compares their file with the drawing. Type ASBUILT and select the same points and blocks you exported (Enter takes every POINT, as EXPORTPOINTS does). Pair the as-built points by Number (the crew kept your point numbers: give the first number you exported from) or Nearest (they numbered their own shots: each design point takes the closest one). Give the file's units and coordinates as you exported them (Drawing, Metres or Feet; World or UCS), and the tolerance in drawing units. Pick the crew's CSV or TXT file. CadCraft reads it the way CivilCraft does: commas, tabs, semicolons or spaces, a header row naming its columns, or PNEZD by column count. The command line says how many points were paired, how many are within the tolerance, outside it, not measured and extra, and the largest distance. Mark them in the drawing? Yes puts them on the layer AS-BUILT as one undo step: a point at each shot, its number and distance beside it, and a red line from the design point to every shot outside the tolerance. A CSV report downloads with every point: design and as-built coordinates, the differences east, north and in height, and the result. See also Points for BIM programs.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"properties-panel","scope":"cadcraft","title":"The Properties panel","summary":"See and change everything about the selected objects, many at once: coordinates with Z, sizes, text, dimension settings, transparency and more. Fields take expressions and lengths with units.","section":"tasks","order":61,"keywords":["totals","total length","total area","sum","selection length","properties","properties palette","ctrl+1","pr","ch","mo","ddmodify","varies","quick select","pickadd","coordinates","z","elevation","thickness","transparency","material","hyperlink","expression","quickcalc","vertex","dimension style","tolerance","decimals"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"open-it","text":"Open it","level":2},{"id":"fold-it-away","text":"Fold it away","level":3},{"id":"sections","text":"Sections","level":2},{"id":"change-a-value","text":"Change a value","level":2},{"id":"expressions-and-units","text":"Expressions and units","level":3},{"id":"change-many-objects-at-once","text":"Change many objects at once","level":2},{"id":"totals-of-a-selection","text":"Totals of a selection","level":2},{"id":"coordinates","text":"Coordinates","level":2},{"id":"general","text":"General","level":2},{"id":"what-each-type-shows","text":"What each type shows","level":2},{"id":"polyline-vertices","text":"Polyline vertices","level":3},{"id":"dimensions","text":"Dimensions","level":2},{"id":"nothing-selected","text":"Nothing selected","level":2},{"id":"the-buttons-at-the-top","text":"The buttons at the top","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to open the Properties panel, change one object or a hundred in one step, type exact coordinates (with Z), use expressions and units in any number field, and what each kind of object shows.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-it\"\u003EOpen it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe panel sits on the right. To bring it to the front:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command 1\"\u003E⌘1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPROPERTIES\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003EPR\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ECH\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EMO\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDouble-click an object (text and multileaders open their text editor instead).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe panel is a movable panel like the others: drag its grip to dock it left, right or at the bottom, or drop it over the drawing to float it (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/arrange-panels\" data-topic=\"basics/arrange-panels\"\u003EArrange the panels and use a second monitor\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"fold-it-away\"\u003EFold it away\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe pin button at the top right of the panel turns on \u003Cstrong\u003Eauto-hide\u003C/strong\u003E: the panel folds to a strip at the right edge and the drawing gets the room. Point at the strip (or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command 1\"\u003E⌘1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) and it opens over the drawing; move away and it folds again. Click the pin again to keep it open. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPROPERTIESCLOSE\u003C/code\u003E folds it too.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"sections\"\u003ESections\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EProperties are grouped in sections you can fold: \u003Cstrong\u003EGeneral\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EGeometry\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EText\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ELines and arrows\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EFit\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EPrimary units\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EAlternate units\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ETolerances\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EPattern\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EMisc\u003C/strong\u003E, and for blocks \u003Cstrong\u003EAttributes\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003ELibrary item\u003C/strong\u003E. Click a section&#039;s name to fold or open it; CadCraft remembers which ones you folded.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDrag the thin line between the names and the values to make the names wider or narrower. Double-click it to put it back. With the keyboard, focus it and use the arrow keys.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-a-value\"\u003EChange a value\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a line. The heading reads &quot;Line&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength\u003C/span\u003E. While you edit, the field shows the full value, not the rounded one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E2500\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The line is now 2500 long, keeping its start point and angle.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or clicking elsewhere applies the value; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E puts the old value back. Every change is one undo step.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"expressions-and-units\"\u003EExpressions and units\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery number field takes more than a plain number:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EYou type\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EYou get\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E=2*45\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E90\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E=sqrt(2)*100\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E141.4214\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E1/4\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E6-1/2\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0.25, 6.5\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;-6 1/2&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E150, 150.5 (inches, in a drawing in feet and inches)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E900mm\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E3.5m\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E2ft\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EConverted into the drawing&#039;s units\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E45d30&#039;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E45°30&#039;15&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E45.5°, 45.5042° (angle fields)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E0.5r\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E100g\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERadians and grads, as degrees\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA length with a unit needs the drawing to have units: set \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnits\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew objects\u003C/span\u003E (nothing selected) first. A bad entry is refused with a note under the panel and nothing changes; it is never saved as 0. Functions: sin, cos, tan, asin, acos, atan, atan2, sqrt, pow, exp, ln, log, abs, round, ceil, floor, min, max, if; angles in degrees.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-many-objects-at-once\"\u003EChange many objects at once\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect several objects, for example 8 lines and 4 dimensions.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe heading says &quot;12 objects&quot;, and a list at the top offers \u003Cstrong\u003EAll (12)\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ELine (8)\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EDimension (4)\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAll\u003C/strong\u003E shows what every object has (layer, color, linetype…). Pick \u003Cstrong\u003EDimension (4)\u003C/strong\u003E to see dimension settings.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChange a value, for example \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDecimals\u003C/span\u003E to \u003Ccode\u003E1\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAll four dimensions change, in one undo step. A field that reads \u003Cstrong\u003EVaries\u003C/strong\u003E means the objects differ; typing a value sets it on all of them. Objects on locked layers are left as they are, and the note says how many.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"totals-of-a-selection\"\u003ETotals of a selection\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith several objects selected, the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETotals\u003C/span\u003E section at the top says:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EObjects\u003C/strong\u003E: how many of each kind, for example &quot;8 lines, 2 polylines, 1 circle&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETotal length\u003C/strong\u003E: the lines, arcs and polylines added up (splines count too), and how many objects that is.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETotal area\u003C/strong\u003E: the closed ones added up: circles, whole ellipses, closed polylines and hatches (a hatch&#039;s holes are taken off).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThey are in the drawing&#039;s length format (feet and inches when it uses them; areas then in square inches and square feet). The status bar shows the same length and area whenever objects are selected; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength and area of the selection\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHidden\u003C/span\u003E turns that off. For one object, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeometry\u003C/span\u003E shows its own length and area, as before.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"coordinates\"\u003ECoordinates\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery point shows \u003Cstrong\u003EX\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EY\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EZ\u003C/strong\u003E: start and end of a line, centers, insertion points, text positions, the current polyline vertex. Lines also show \u003Cstrong\u003EDelta X\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EDelta Y\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EDelta Z\u003C/strong\u003E (read-only). Polylines and hatches have an \u003Cstrong\u003EElevation\u003C/strong\u003E (one Z for the whole object).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe crosshair button beside a point&#039;s name lets you pick it in the drawing instead: click it, then click the new position (snaps work). Values are world coordinates.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"general\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EField\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it is\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayer\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EColor\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinetype\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinetype scale\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELineweight\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAs in the layers panel; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBy layer\u003C/span\u003E follows the layer\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETransparency\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBy layer, by block, or 0 to 90%\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThickness\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHow far lines, circles, arcs, polylines, points and text extend in Z\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMaterial\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBy layer, by block, Global or a name a drawing brought\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlot style\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERead-only: drawings plot by color, through the sheet&#039;s \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/plot-pdf#plot-style-tables\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/plot-pdf\" data-hash=\"plot-style-tables\"\u003Eplot style table\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHyperlink\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA web address kept with the object\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-each-type-shows\"\u003EWhat each type shows\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESelected\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EYou can change\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELine\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStart and end X, Y, Z; length; angle\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECircle\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECenter; radius; diameter; circumference; area (each sets the radius)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArc\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECenter; radius; start and end angle; total angle; chord (shows arc length and area)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEllipse\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECenter; major and minor radius; radius ratio; major axis angle; start and end angle\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPolyline\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe vertex browser (below); global width; elevation; closed; area when closed (scales it about its center)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EText\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EContents; style; justify; height; rotation; width factor; obliquing angle; defined width; line spacing; annotative; position\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBlock reference\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EInsertion point; rotation; scale X, Y, Z and uniform scale; attribute values\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHatch\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPattern; scale; angle; origin; associative; island detection; background color; elevation\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDimension\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESee below\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMultileader\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EText; height; width; text side; arrowhead size; landing distance; arrowhead point\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EViewport\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EScale; center and size on the sheet; view center in the model; on; display locked\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"polyline-vertices\"\u003EPolyline vertices\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECurrent vertex\u003C/span\u003E shows which vertex the fields below it belong to. Use the arrow buttons, or type a number, to move to another vertex; there is no limit on how many. Each vertex has X, Y, start and end segment width, and the bulge of the segment that starts there (0 = straight).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"dimensions\"\u003EDimensions\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA dimension shows its \u003Cstrong\u003EDimension style\u003C/strong\u003E, what it measures, its points, and every setting:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELines and arrows:\u003C/strong\u003E arrowhead for each end, arrow size, tick size, colors and lineweights of the dimension and extension lines, hiding either half or either extension line, extension line offset and extension.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EText:\u003C/strong\u003E override (leave empty for the measured value; \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;&gt;\u003C/code\u003E stands for it inside your own text), style, color, height, offset, vertical and horizontal position, always horizontal, rotation, background fill.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFit:\u003C/strong\u003E overall scale, baseline spacing, what moves when space is short, text movement.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPrimary units:\u003C/strong\u003E format, decimals, round off, prefix, suffix, measurement scale, decimal separator, suppress leading and trailing zeros; for angles the angle format and decimals.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAlternate units:\u003C/strong\u003E on or off, format, decimals, multiplier (25.4 turns inches into millimeters), round off, prefix and suffix.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETolerances:\u003C/strong\u003E none, symmetrical, deviation, limits or basic, with upper and lower values, decimals, height and position.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChanging any of them on an opened dimension drops the drawing the other program made of it, and CadCraft draws it again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe horizontal text position (beside the first or second extension line) and text below the line are drawn on linear and aligned dimensions; on other dimensions they are kept and saved but not drawn yet. The Fit choices are kept and saved but not drawn yet: CadCraft places text by its own rule (above the line, outside when it does not fit).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"nothing-selected\"\u003ENothing selected\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith nothing selected the panel shows:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENew objects:\u003C/strong\u003E the layer, color, linetype, lineweight and text height new objects get; the drawing&#039;s units and length format; the current layer&#039;s linetype and lineweight; sizes for new dimensions. On a sheet, the sheet&#039;s name, paper and orientation come first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EView:\u003C/strong\u003E the center, height and width of what is on screen. Type a value to move the view there.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMisc:\u003C/strong\u003E the UCS icon on or off, the UCS (always World: CadCraft has no user coordinate systems yet) and the annotation scale.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-buttons-at-the-top\"\u003EThe buttons at the top\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EButton\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDoes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFunnel\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EQSELECT\u003C/code\u003E: select by type and property\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPointer\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESELECT\u003C/code\u003E: select objects at the command line\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlus or 1\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWhether a click adds to the selection (plus) or replaces it (1); the same setting as Settings, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPin\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAuto-hide, above\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETyping in a field did nothing.\u003C/strong\u003E Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or click elsewhere to apply it. A red field and a note mean the entry was refused.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA length with a unit is refused.\u003C/strong\u003E The drawing has no units. Set \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnits\u003C/span\u003E with nothing selected.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOnly Layer and Color show.\u003C/strong\u003E Several kinds of objects are selected and \u003Cstrong\u003EAll\u003C/strong\u003E is chosen. Pick one kind in the list at the top.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESome objects did not change.\u003C/strong\u003E They are on a locked layer. Unlock the layer in the layers panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETyped letters went into a field.\u003C/strong\u003E While a field has the cursor, keys type there. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, then type the command.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to open the Properties panel, change one object or a hundred in one step, type exact coordinates (with Z), use expressions and units in any number field, and what each kind of object shows. Open it The panel sits on the right. To bring it to the front: Press Ctrl+1, or type PROPERTIES (or PR, CH, MO). Double-click an object (text and multileaders open their text editor instead). The panel is a movable panel like the others: drag its grip to dock it left, right or at the bottom, or drop it over the drawing to float it (see Arrange the panels and use a second monitor). Fold it away The pin button at the top right of the panel turns on auto-hide: the panel folds to a strip at the right edge and the drawing gets the room. Point at the strip (or press Ctrl+1) and it opens over the drawing; move away and it folds again. Click the pin again to keep it open. PROPERTIESCLOSE folds it too. Sections Properties are grouped in sections you can fold: General, Geometry, Text, Lines and arrows, Fit, Primary units, Alternate units, Tolerances, Pattern, Misc, and for blocks Attributes and Library item. Click a section's name to fold or open it; CadCraft remembers which ones you folded. Drag the thin line between the names and the values to make the names wider or narrower. Double-click it to put it back. With the keyboard, focus it and use the arrow keys. Change a value Click a line. The heading reads \"Line\". Click in Length. While you edit, the field shows the full value, not the rounded one. Type 2500 and press Enter. The line is now 2500 long, keeping its start point and angle. Enter or clicking elsewhere applies the value; Esc puts the old value back. Every change is one undo step. Expressions and units Every number field takes more than a plain number: You type You get =2*45 90 =sqrt(2)*100 141.4214 1/4, 6-1/2 0.25, 6.5 12'6\", 12'-6 1/2\" 150, 150.5 (inches, in a drawing in feet and inches) 900mm, 3.5m, 2ft Converted into the drawing's units 45d30', 45°30'15\" 45.5°, 45.5042° (angle fields) 0.5r, 100g Radians and grads, as degrees A length with a unit needs the drawing to have units: set Units under New objects (nothing selected) first. A bad entry is refused with a note under the panel and nothing changes; it is never saved as 0. Functions: sin, cos, tan, asin, acos, atan, atan2, sqrt, pow, exp, ln, log, abs, round, ceil, floor, min, max, if; angles in degrees. Change many objects at once Select several objects, for example 8 lines and 4 dimensions. The heading says \"12 objects\", and a list at the top offers All (12), Line (8) and Dimension (4). All shows what every object has (layer, color, linetype…). Pick Dimension (4) to see dimension settings. Change a value, for example Decimals to 1. All four dimensions change, in one undo step. A field that reads Varies means the objects differ; typing a value sets it on all of them. Objects on locked layers are left as they are, and the note says how many. Totals of a selection With several objects selected, the Totals section at the top says: Objects: how many of each kind, for example \"8 lines, 2 polylines, 1 circle\". Total length: the lines, arcs and polylines added up (splines count too), and how many objects that is. Total area: the closed ones added up: circles, whole ellipses, closed polylines and hatches (a hatch's holes are taken off). They are in the drawing's length format (feet and inches when it uses them; areas then in square inches and square feet). The status bar shows the same length and area whenever objects are selected; Settings › CadCraft › Length and area of the selection › Hidden turns that off. For one object, Geometry shows its own length and area, as before. Coordinates Every point shows X, Y and Z: start and end of a line, centers, insertion points, text positions, the current polyline vertex. Lines also show Delta X, Delta Y and Delta Z (read-only). Polylines and hatches have an Elevation (one Z for the whole object). The crosshair button beside a point's name lets you pick it in the drawing instead: click it, then click the new position (snaps work). Values are world coordinates. General Field What it is Layer, Color, Linetype, Linetype scale, Lineweight As in the layers panel; By layer follows the layer Transparency By layer, by block, or 0 to 90% Thickness How far lines, circles, arcs, polylines, points and text extend in Z Material By layer, by block, Global or a name a drawing brought Plot style Read-only: drawings plot by color, through the sheet's plot style table Hyperlink A web address kept with the object What each type shows Selected You can change Line Start and end X, Y, Z; length; angle Circle Center; radius; diameter; circumference; area (each sets the radius) Arc Center; radius; start and end angle; total angle; chord (shows arc length and area) Ellipse Center; major and minor radius; radius ratio; major axis angle; start and end angle Polyline The vertex browser (below); global width; elevation; closed; area when closed (scales it about its center) Text Contents; style; justify; height; rotation; width factor; obliquing angle; defined width; line spacing; annotative; position Block reference Insertion point; rotation; scale X, Y, Z and uniform scale; attribute values Hatch Pattern; scale; angle; origin; associative; island detection; background color; elevation Dimension See below Multileader Text; height; width; text side; arrowhead size; landing distance; arrowhead point Viewport Scale; center and size on the sheet; view center in the model; on; display locked Polyline vertices Current vertex shows which vertex the fields below it belong to. Use the arrow buttons, or type a number, to move to another vertex; there is no limit on how many. Each vertex has X, Y, start and end segment width, and the bulge of the segment that starts there (0 = straight). Dimensions A dimension shows its Dimension style, what it measures, its points, and every setting: Lines and arrows: arrowhead for each end, arrow size, tick size, colors and lineweights of the dimension and extension lines, hiding either half or either extension line, extension line offset and extension. Text: override (leave empty for the measured value; \u003C\u003E stands for it inside your own text), style, color, height, offset, vertical and horizontal position, always horizontal, rotation, background fill. Fit: overall scale, baseline spacing, what moves when space is short, text movement. Primary units: format, decimals, round off, prefix, suffix, measurement scale, decimal separator, suppress leading and trailing zeros; for angles the angle format and decimals. Alternate units: on or off, format, decimals, multiplier (25.4 turns inches into millimeters), round off, prefix and suffix. Tolerances: none, symmetrical, deviation, limits or basic, with upper and lower values, decimals, height and position. Changing any of them on an opened dimension drops the drawing the other program made of it, and CadCraft draws it again. The horizontal text position (beside the first or second extension line) and text below the line are drawn on linear and aligned dimensions; on other dimensions they are kept and saved but not drawn yet. The Fit choices are kept and saved but not drawn yet: CadCraft places text by its own rule (above the line, outside when it does not fit). Nothing selected With nothing selected the panel shows: New objects: the layer, color, linetype, lineweight and text height new objects get; the drawing's units and length format; the current layer's linetype and lineweight; sizes for new dimensions. On a sheet, the sheet's name, paper and orientation come first. View: the center, height and width of what is on screen. Type a value to move the view there. Misc: the UCS icon on or off, the UCS (always World: CadCraft has no user coordinate systems yet) and the annotation scale. The buttons at the top Button Does Funnel QSELECT: select by type and property Pointer SELECT: select objects at the command line Plus or 1 Whether a click adds to the selection (plus) or replaces it (1); the same setting as Settings, CadCraft Pin Auto-hide, above Common mistakes Typing in a field did nothing. Press Enter or click elsewhere to apply it. A red field and a note mean the entry was refused. A length with a unit is refused. The drawing has no units. Set Units with nothing selected. Only Layer and Color show. Several kinds of objects are selected and All is chosen. Pick one kind in the list at the top. Some objects did not change. They are on a locked layer. Unlock the layer in the layers panel. Typed letters went into a field. While a field has the cursor, keys type there. Press Enter or Esc, then type the command.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"bearings-and-angles","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Type and show bearings and angles in degrees, minutes, seconds","summary":"Type a direction as a surveyor's bearing such as N45°23'30\"E or as degrees-minutes-seconds anywhere an angle is asked for, and choose how the drawing shows angles with AUNITS.","section":"tasks","order":62,"keywords":["bearing","bearings","surveyor","quadrant bearing","N45E","degrees minutes seconds","DMS","angle","direction","lot","lots","lay out","traverse","AUNITS","ANGUNITS","angle format","polar","direct distance","DIST"],"headings":[{"id":"bearings","text":"Bearings","level":2},{"id":"degrees-minutes-seconds","text":"Degrees, minutes, seconds","level":2},{"id":"how-angles-are-shown","text":"How angles are shown","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EAnywhere CadCraft asks for an \u003Cstrong\u003Eangle or a direction\u003C/strong\u003E, you can type a \u003Cstrong\u003Equadrant bearing\u003C/strong\u003E or an angle in \u003Cstrong\u003Edegrees, minutes and seconds\u003C/strong\u003E, as well as a plain number of degrees.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"bearings\"\u003EBearings\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA bearing names north or south, an angle up to 90°, then east or west:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EYou type\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDirection\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EN45°23&#039;30&quot;E\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003EN45d23&#039;30&quot;E\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E45°23&#039;30&quot; from north toward east\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ES10-30-00W\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003ES 10 30 W\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E10°30&#039; from south toward west (dashes or spaces between the parts)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EN45.5E\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E45.5° from north toward east\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EN\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EW\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Edue north, east, south, west\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELetters can be any case. In Spanish, \u003Ccode\u003EO\u003C/code\u003E also means west (oeste). \u003Ccode\u003Ed\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E°\u003C/code\u003E mean the same; \u003Ccode\u003E&#039;\u003C/code\u003E is minutes and \u003Ccode\u003E&quot;\u003C/code\u003E is seconds.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo lay out a lot line 20 m long at N45°23&#039;E, start \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELINE\u003C/code\u003E and type the first point, then \u003Ccode\u003E@20&lt;N45d23&#039;E\u003C/code\u003E. The same works at \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EROTATE\u003C/code\u003E&#039;s angle prompt and every other prompt that asks for an angle, and in the angle box of \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDynamic input\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"degrees-minutes-seconds\"\u003EDegrees, minutes, seconds\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E45°23&#039;30&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E45d23&#039;30&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E45d30&#039;\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E45d\u003C/code\u003E is an angle from the X axis, counter-clockwise. The \u003Ccode\u003E°\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003Ed\u003C/code\u003E is needed: \u003Ccode\u003E45-23-30\u003C/code\u003E is read as arithmetic (45 − 23 − 30). Minutes and seconds must be under 60. A plain number, with arithmetic (\u003Ccode\u003E360/7\u003C/code\u003E), still works.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-angles-are-shown\"\u003EHow angles are shown\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EAUNITS\u003C/code\u003E (or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAngle format\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECurrent settings\u003C/span\u003E) and pick:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDecimal degrees\u003C/strong\u003E (the default): \u003Ccode\u003E44.6083°\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDegrees-minutes-seconds\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003E44°36&#039;30&quot;\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESurveyor&#039;s bearings\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003EN45°23&#039;30&quot;E\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe precision follows what you type for \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EAUNITS\u003C/code\u003E: 0 shows degrees, 2 minutes, 4 seconds, and 5 to 8 add decimals of a second. The format shows in \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIST\u003C/code\u003E, on the status bar, in the dynamic input and tracking tips, and in the angle boxes of \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E (a box you edit takes any of the forms above). It is one undo step and is saved in DWG and DXF files (\u003Ccode\u003E$AUNITS\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E$AUPREC\u003C/code\u003E); LISP reads and writes it as \u003Ccode\u003EAUNITS\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EAUPREC\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Anywhere CadCraft asks for an angle or a direction, you can type a quadrant bearing or an angle in degrees, minutes and seconds, as well as a plain number of degrees. Bearings A bearing names north or south, an angle up to 90°, then east or west: You type Direction N45°23'30\"E or N45d23'30\"E 45°23'30\" from north toward east S10-30-00W or S 10 30 W 10°30' from south toward west (dashes or spaces between the parts) N45.5E 45.5° from north toward east N, E, S, W due north, east, south, west Letters can be any case. In Spanish, O also means west (oeste). d and ° mean the same; ' is minutes and \" is seconds. To lay out a lot line 20 m long at N45°23'E, start LINE and type the first point, then @20\u003CN45d23'E. The same works at ROTATE's angle prompt and every other prompt that asks for an angle, and in the angle box of Dynamic input. Degrees, minutes, seconds 45°23'30\", 45d23'30\", 45d30' or 45d is an angle from the X axis, counter-clockwise. The ° or d is needed: 45-23-30 is read as arithmetic (45 − 23 − 30). Minutes and seconds must be under 60. A plain number, with arithmetic (360/7), still works. How angles are shown Type AUNITS (or choose Angle format in Properties › Current settings) and pick: Decimal degrees (the default): 44.6083°. Degrees-minutes-seconds: 44°36'30\". Surveyor's bearings: N45°23'30\"E. The precision follows what you type for AUNITS: 0 shows degrees, 2 minutes, 4 seconds, and 5 to 8 add decimals of a second. The format shows in DIST, on the status bar, in the dynamic input and tracking tips, and in the angle boxes of Properties (a box you edit takes any of the forms above). It is one undo step and is saved in DWG and DXF files ($AUNITS, $AUPREC); LISP reads and writes it as AUNITS and AUPREC.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"compare-settings","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Compare settings","summary":"Put two dimension styles, text styles, layers, sheets or unit settings side by side — in one drawing or across two open drawings — see what differs, and copy values across.","section":"tasks","order":63,"keywords":["compare","compare settings","compare styles","dimension style","text style","layer settings","page setup","units","differences","standards","copy settings","comparesettings"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"compare","text":"Compare","level":2},{"id":"copy-a-value-across","text":"Copy a value across","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to check whether two drawings (or two styles in one drawing) are set up the same, and bring one in line with the other one value at a time.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"compare\"\u003ECompare\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the drawings you want to compare, each in its own tab (or just one, to compare two of its own styles).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECompare settings…\u003C/span\u003E, or type \u003Ccode\u003ECMPSET\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECompare\u003C/span\u003E, choose what: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDimension styles\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EText styles\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayers\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESheets (page setup)\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnits\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn the left, choose the item in this drawing; under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAgainst\u003C/span\u003E, choose this drawing or another open one, and then its item.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery property shows on one row, this drawing&#039;s value on the left and the other&#039;s on the right. Rows that differ are tinted, and the line above the table says how many. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOnly what differs\u003C/span\u003E hides the rest. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings for new dimensions\u003C/span\u003E is what new dimensions in the drawing take.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"copy-a-value-across\"\u003ECopy a value across\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the arrow pointing right on a row (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy to the right\u003C/span\u003E) to copy the left value to the right, or the arrow pointing left for the other way.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe value lands in that drawing as one undo step: switch to its tab and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to take it back. Copying onto a layer or style the other drawing doesn&#039;t have yet makes it there.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to check whether two drawings (or two styles in one drawing) are set up the same, and bring one in line with the other one value at a time. Compare Open the drawings you want to compare, each in its own tab (or just one, to compare two of its own styles). Click View › More › Compare settings…, or type CMPSET and press Enter. In Compare, choose what: Dimension styles, Text styles, Layers, Sheets (page setup) or Units. On the left, choose the item in this drawing; under Against, choose this drawing or another open one, and then its item. Every property shows on one row, this drawing's value on the left and the other's on the right. Rows that differ are tinted, and the line above the table says how many. Only what differs hides the rest. Settings for new dimensions is what new dimensions in the drawing take. Copy a value across Click the arrow pointing right on a row (Copy to the right) to copy the left value to the right, or the arrow pointing left for the other way. The value lands in that drawing as one undo step: switch to its tab and press Ctrl+Z to take it back. Copying onto a layer or style the other drawing doesn't have yet makes it there.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"drawing-limits","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Set the drawing limits","summary":"LIMITS sets a rectangle for the drawing: the grid fills it, ZOOM All shows it, and with the limits on, points outside it are refused. They are saved in DWG and DXF files.","section":"tasks","order":63,"keywords":["limits","drawing limits","LIMITS","LIMMIN","LIMMAX","LIMCHECK","grid","outside limits","sheet size","drawing area","zoom all","boundary"],"headings":[{"id":"set-them","text":"Set them","level":2},{"id":"what-they-do","text":"What they do","level":2},{"id":"in-files-and-lisp","text":"In files and LISP","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDrawing limits\u003C/strong\u003E are a rectangle that says how big the drawing is meant to be: the area you plan to draw in, such as a sheet 420 × 297. A new drawing has none, so the grid and the drawing area are unbounded, as before.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"set-them\"\u003ESet them\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELIMITS\u003C/code\u003E and answer:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify lower left corner or [ON/OFF] &lt;0,0&gt;\u003C/code\u003E: a point, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to keep the current one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify upper right corner &lt;420,297&gt;\u003C/code\u003E: a point, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to keep the current one. It must be above and to the right of the first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOr type \u003Ccode\u003EON\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003EOFF\u003C/code\u003E at the first question to switch the check without changing the rectangle. Setting the corners is one undo step, and so is each ON and OFF.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-they-do\"\u003EWhat they do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe grid fills the limits\u003C/strong\u003E and stops at their edge. It is a picture of the area, so turn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGrid\u003C/span\u003E on to see it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EZOOM All\u003C/strong\u003E (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EZOOM\u003C/code\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAll\u003C/span\u003E) shows the limits and the drawing together; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExtents\u003C/span\u003E still shows only the drawing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWith the limits ON\u003C/strong\u003E, a point you type or click outside them is refused with \u003Ccode\u003E**Outside limits\u003C/code\u003E and the prompt stays. It covers the commands that make objects: \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELINE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPLINE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECIRCLE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EARC\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ERECTANG\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPOLYGON\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EELLIPSE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESPLINE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPOINT\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ETEXT\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMTEXT\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EINSERT\u003C/code\u003E, the dimension commands and a few more. Commands that only move, select or measure are not limited, and neither is pasting, a grip drag, or an object a LISP routine makes: the limits are a guide for drawing, not a wall. With the limits OFF nothing is refused.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EObjects that are already outside stay where they are.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"in-files-and-lisp\"\u003EIn files and LISP\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThey are saved in DWG and DXF as \u003Ccode\u003E$LIMMIN\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E$LIMMAX\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E$LIMCHECK\u003C/code\u003E. A file from another program that has only that program&#039;s default limits (0,0 to 12,9 or 420,297, check off) opens with none, so its grid is not squeezed into a small rectangle; a file with limits of its own, or with the check on, keeps them. In LISP, \u003Ccode\u003E(getvar &quot;LIMMIN&quot;)\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ELIMMAX\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003ELIMCHECK\u003C/code\u003E read them and \u003Ccode\u003Esetvar\u003C/code\u003E writes them, as one undo step.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe limits belong to the model. A sheet (a layout) has its own paper size.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Drawing limits are a rectangle that says how big the drawing is meant to be: the area you plan to draw in, such as a sheet 420 × 297. A new drawing has none, so the grid and the drawing area are unbounded, as before. Set them Type LIMITS and answer: Specify lower left corner or [ON/OFF] \u003C0,0\u003E: a point, or Enter to keep the current one. Specify upper right corner \u003C420,297\u003E: a point, or Enter to keep the current one. It must be above and to the right of the first. Or type ON or OFF at the first question to switch the check without changing the rectangle. Setting the corners is one undo step, and so is each ON and OFF. What they do The grid fills the limits and stops at their edge. It is a picture of the area, so turn Grid on to see it. ZOOM All (ZOOM, then All) shows the limits and the drawing together; Extents still shows only the drawing. With the limits ON, a point you type or click outside them is refused with **Outside limits and the prompt stays. It covers the commands that make objects: LINE, PLINE, CIRCLE, ARC, RECTANG, POLYGON, ELLIPSE, SPLINE, POINT, TEXT, MTEXT, INSERT, the dimension commands and a few more. Commands that only move, select or measure are not limited, and neither is pasting, a grip drag, or an object a LISP routine makes: the limits are a guide for drawing, not a wall. With the limits OFF nothing is refused. Objects that are already outside stay where they are. In files and LISP They are saved in DWG and DXF as $LIMMIN, $LIMMAX and $LIMCHECK. A file from another program that has only that program's default limits (0,0 to 12,9 or 420,297, check off) opens with none, so its grid is not squeezed into a small rectangle; a file with limits of its own, or with the check on, keeps them. In LISP, (getvar \"LIMMIN\"), LIMMAX and LIMCHECK read them and setvar writes them, as one undo step. The limits belong to the model. A sheet (a layout) has its own paper size.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"sketch-with-a-pen","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Sketch with a pen and get precise geometry","summary":"Draw freehand with a pen, mouse or finger and each stroke becomes a clean line, circle, arc, rectangle or polyline as you lift the pen, snapped to what is already drawn, with its size ready to type over.","section":"tasks","order":63,"keywords":["sketch","pen","stylus","freehand","finger","clean up","precise","snap","SKETCHLIVE","SKETCHFIX","size","type over"],"headings":[{"id":"type-over-the-size","text":"Type over the size","level":2},{"id":"cleaning-up-several-strokes-at-once","text":"Cleaning up several strokes at once","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EStart \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESKETCHLIVE\u003C/code\u003E (or choose \u003Cstrong\u003EDraw › More › Sketch with a pen\u003C/strong\u003E) and draw a stroke by pressing and dragging. When you lift the pen, CadCraft reads what you meant and puts it in the drawing:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EYour stroke\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat you get\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENearly straight\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA line. A nearly level or plumb line is made exactly level or plumb.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClosed and round\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA circle\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPart of a round shape\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAn arc\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClosed with four right-angled corners\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA rectangle\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAnything else\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA polyline with squared sides\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe ends of a stroke are pulled onto the nearest end of what is already drawn, so a sketched wall meets the wall beside it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"type-over-the-size\"\u003EType over the size\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAfter each stroke the command line shows its size (a line&#039;s \u003Cstrong\u003Elength\u003C/strong\u003E, a circle&#039;s or arc&#039;s \u003Cstrong\u003Eradius\u003C/strong\u003E, a rectangle&#039;s \u003Cstrong\u003Ewidth\u003C/strong\u003E) as the default. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to keep it, or type a number or a length such as \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E to replace it. A line keeps its first end, a circle its centre, a rectangle its first corner.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E on an empty prompt to finish, or type \u003Ccode\u003EU\u003C/code\u003E to take the last stroke back. The whole session is one undo step.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"cleaning-up-several-strokes-at-once\"\u003ECleaning up several strokes at once\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESKETCHFIX\u003C/code\u003E collects strokes and makes them all precise when you press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, which suits a rough layout drawn quickly.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEverything is worked out on your computer by rules, with no model and nothing sent anywhere.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"Start SKETCHLIVE (or choose Draw › More › Sketch with a pen) and draw a stroke by pressing and dragging. When you lift the pen, CadCraft reads what you meant and puts it in the drawing: Your stroke What you get Nearly straight A line. A nearly level or plumb line is made exactly level or plumb. Closed and round A circle Part of a round shape An arc Closed with four right-angled corners A rectangle Anything else A polyline with squared sides The ends of a stroke are pulled onto the nearest end of what is already drawn, so a sketched wall meets the wall beside it. Type over the size After each stroke the command line shows its size (a line's length, a circle's or arc's radius, a rectangle's width) as the default. Press Enter to keep it, or type a number or a length such as 12'6\" to replace it. A line keeps its first end, a circle its centre, a rectangle its first corner. Press Enter on an empty prompt to finish, or type U to take the last stroke back. The whole session is one undo step. Cleaning up several strokes at once SKETCHFIX collects strokes and makes them all precise when you press Enter, which suits a rough layout drawn quickly. Everything is worked out on your computer by rules, with no model and nothing sent anywhere.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"draft-without-sight","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Draft without sight","summary":"Turn on Access and CadCraft says every command and result through your screen reader, describes the drawing and what is near the cursor, lets you move the cursor with the keyboard, plays a sound for each snap and grip, and takes commands by voice where your browser can do that on your computer.","section":"tasks","order":64,"keywords":["accessibility","screen reader","blind","low vision","NVDA","VoiceOver","JAWS","Orca","keyboard cursor","voice commands","speech","sounds","snap sounds","describe drawing","ACCESS","DESCRIBE","WHEREAMI","NEARBY","SAYSEL","VOICE"],"headings":[{"id":"turn-it-on","text":"Turn it on","level":2},{"id":"keys-on-the-drawing","text":"Keys on the drawing","level":2},{"id":"commands","text":"Commands","level":2},{"id":"sounds-for-snaps-and-grips","text":"Sounds for snaps and grips","level":2},{"id":"commands-by-voice","text":"Commands by voice","level":2},{"id":"what-it-does-not-do-yet","text":"What it does not do yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAccess\u003C/strong\u003E lets you draw without seeing the screen. It is off until you turn it on, so nobody gets it by accident.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"turn-it-on\"\u003ETurn it on\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cstrong\u003ESettings\u003C/strong\u003E (the gear, or Ctrl+,), find \u003Cstrong\u003EDrafting without sight\u003C/strong\u003E and choose \u003Cstrong\u003EOn\u003C/strong\u003E. Or type \u003Ccode\u003EACCESS\u003C/code\u003E at the command line and answer \u003Ccode\u003EOn\u003C/code\u003E. It is remembered on this computer.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith Access on:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEverything the command line says is spoken\u003C/strong\u003E through your screen reader: what you typed, the prompt it asks, the answer, and every error. CadCraft uses its own polite and urgent live regions, so a screen reader that reads live regions needs nothing set up. Errors and cancels interrupt; everything else waits its turn.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe drawing area is reachable.\u003C/strong\u003E Press Tab until you hear &quot;Drawing&quot;, or press Shift+Tab from the command line. It is a region that passes keys through to CadCraft.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"keys-on-the-drawing\"\u003EKeys on the drawing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArrow keys\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMove the keyboard cursor. A step is about a hundredth of what the screen shows, rounded to 1, 2 or 5. Shift moves ten times as far, Ctrl a tenth.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEnter\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPick the point under the cursor, exactly as a click would.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESpace\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPress Enter at the command line: finish LINE, take the default.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETab, Shift+Tab\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EJump to the next or previous snap point near the cursor, nearest first.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EG\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStep through the grips of the selected objects.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ED\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDescribe the drawing: how many objects, of what kind, how big, what is selected.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EW\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESay where the cursor is and how far it is from the last point.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EN\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESay what is near the cursor, nearest first, with the distance and the side.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ES\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDescribe the selected objects.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ER\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESay the last thing again.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EV\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EVoice on or off.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E/\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGo to the command line.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E?\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EList these keys.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMoving with the keys says where you landed. When a command is waiting for a point, it also says how far and in which direction you are from the last point, for example &quot;2400 from the last point, to the right, at 0 degrees&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"commands\"\u003ECommands\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EACCESS\u003C/code\u003E turns Access, snap sounds and voice on or off and says the state.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDESCRIBE\u003C/code\u003E says what the drawing holds.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EWHEREAMI\u003C/code\u003E says where the cursor is.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ENEARBY\u003C/code\u003E says what lies close to the cursor.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESAYSEL\u003C/code\u003E says what is selected, one sentence per object.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EVOICE\u003C/code\u003E turns spoken commands on or off.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThese five read the drawing and print an answer; they change nothing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"sounds-for-snaps-and-grips\"\u003ESounds for snaps and grips\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETurn on \u003Cstrong\u003ESnap and grip sounds\u003C/strong\u003E in Settings. Each snap kind has its own pitch and sound, so you can tell an endpoint from a midpoint, a centre, an intersection and the rest, and a grip has a double tick. The sound pans left or right to follow the cursor across the screen. The sounds are made on your computer; nothing is downloaded.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"commands-by-voice\"\u003ECommands by voice\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETurn on \u003Cstrong\u003ECommands by voice\u003C/strong\u003E in Settings. Say a command name (&quot;line&quot;, &quot;circle&quot;, &quot;move&quot;), then say numbers as you would read them: &quot;point three hundred comma four hundred&quot;, &quot;distance twelve hundred&quot;, &quot;angle forty five&quot;. &quot;Enter&quot;, &quot;cancel&quot;, &quot;undo&quot; and &quot;redo&quot; do what they say. What was heard is spoken back, and a phrase that is not a command is reported, not guessed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWhere the speech is recognised.\u003C/strong\u003E CadCraft has no speech service of its own and sends no audio anywhere. It asks your browser to recognise speech on this computer. If your browser can, the choice is \u003Cstrong\u003EOn this computer\u003C/strong\u003E and no audio leaves it. If it cannot, voice stays off unless you choose \u003Cstrong\u003EBrowser online service\u003C/strong\u003E, which lets the browser send audio to its maker&#039;s speech service. Settings says which applies before you decide.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-does-not-do-yet\"\u003EWhat it does not do yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOnly CadCraft has Access. The other seven apps follow, with the same keys and sounds.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EText in a drawing (a note, a block name) is read out as it is written; it is never taken as an instruction.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIt has been tried with synthetic checks and the browser tests; hand checks with NVDA, VoiceOver and a blind or low-vision tester are still to come. If you try it, please tell us what is missing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Access lets you draw without seeing the screen. It is off until you turn it on, so nobody gets it by accident. Turn it on Open Settings (the gear, or Ctrl+,), find Drafting without sight and choose On. Or type ACCESS at the command line and answer On. It is remembered on this computer. With Access on: Everything the command line says is spoken through your screen reader: what you typed, the prompt it asks, the answer, and every error. CadCraft uses its own polite and urgent live regions, so a screen reader that reads live regions needs nothing set up. Errors and cancels interrupt; everything else waits its turn. The drawing area is reachable. Press Tab until you hear \"Drawing\", or press Shift+Tab from the command line. It is a region that passes keys through to CadCraft. Keys on the drawing Key What it does Arrow keys Move the keyboard cursor. A step is about a hundredth of what the screen shows, rounded to 1, 2 or 5. Shift moves ten times as far, Ctrl a tenth. Enter Pick the point under the cursor, exactly as a click would. Space Press Enter at the command line: finish LINE, take the default. Tab, Shift+Tab Jump to the next or previous snap point near the cursor, nearest first. G Step through the grips of the selected objects. D Describe the drawing: how many objects, of what kind, how big, what is selected. W Say where the cursor is and how far it is from the last point. N Say what is near the cursor, nearest first, with the distance and the side. S Describe the selected objects. R Say the last thing again. V Voice on or off. / Go to the command line. ? List these keys. Moving with the keys says where you landed. When a command is waiting for a point, it also says how far and in which direction you are from the last point, for example \"2400 from the last point, to the right, at 0 degrees\". Commands ACCESS turns Access, snap sounds and voice on or off and says the state. DESCRIBE says what the drawing holds. WHEREAMI says where the cursor is. NEARBY says what lies close to the cursor. SAYSEL says what is selected, one sentence per object. VOICE turns spoken commands on or off. These five read the drawing and print an answer; they change nothing. Sounds for snaps and grips Turn on Snap and grip sounds in Settings. Each snap kind has its own pitch and sound, so you can tell an endpoint from a midpoint, a centre, an intersection and the rest, and a grip has a double tick. The sound pans left or right to follow the cursor across the screen. The sounds are made on your computer; nothing is downloaded. Commands by voice Turn on Commands by voice in Settings. Say a command name (\"line\", \"circle\", \"move\"), then say numbers as you would read them: \"point three hundred comma four hundred\", \"distance twelve hundred\", \"angle forty five\". \"Enter\", \"cancel\", \"undo\" and \"redo\" do what they say. What was heard is spoken back, and a phrase that is not a command is reported, not guessed. Where the speech is recognised. CadCraft has no speech service of its own and sends no audio anywhere. It asks your browser to recognise speech on this computer. If your browser can, the choice is On this computer and no audio leaves it. If it cannot, voice stays off unless you choose Browser online service, which lets the browser send audio to its maker's speech service. Settings says which applies before you decide. What it does not do yet Only CadCraft has Access. The other seven apps follow, with the same keys and sounds. Text in a drawing (a note, a block name) is read out as it is written; it is never taken as an instruction. It has been tried with synthetic checks and the browser tests; hand checks with NVDA, VoiceOver and a blind or low-vision tester are still to come. If you try it, please tell us what is missing.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"shx-fonts","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Draw text in your own SHX fonts","summary":"Give CadCraft a folder of SHX fonts (or pick the files) and the text styles that name them are drawn in them, on the screen and in a plot or PDF, with the style's height, width factor and slant.","section":"tasks","order":64,"keywords":["shx","fonts","font","shape font","big font","bigfont","unifont","legacy","persian","farsi","arabic","text style","missing font","font substituted","SHXFONTS","folder","reverse","visual order","simplex","isocp"],"headings":[{"id":"load-your-fonts","text":"Load your fonts","level":2},{"id":"what-happens","text":"What happens","level":2},{"id":"fonts-already-in-visual-order","text":"Fonts already in visual order","level":2},{"id":"what-is-not-covered-yet","text":"What is not covered yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EDrawings from other programs often name \u003Cstrong\u003ESHX fonts\u003C/strong\u003E in their text styles, and a DWG does not carry them. Without the font, CadCraft draws that text in its own face, which is fine for ordinary Latin text but not for old fonts that map plain character codes to other letter shapes, such as legacy Persian annotation.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EGive CadCraft the fonts, and it draws the text in them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"load-your-fonts\"\u003ELoad your fonts\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESHXFONTS\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003ESHX\u003C/code\u003E) and choose:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFolder\u003C/span\u003E (CadCraft Studio): pick a folder. Every \u003Ccode\u003E.shx\u003C/code\u003E file in it is read now, and again each time CadCraft starts.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFiles\u003C/span\u003E (the browser): pick one or several \u003Ccode\u003E.shx\u003C/code\u003E files. They are kept in this browser and never uploaded.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EList\u003C/span\u003E: the fonts that are loaded, and the ones the open drawing names but you have not loaded.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClear\u003C/span\u003E: forget one font, or all of them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECadCraft cannot include third-party fonts, so it never ships any: they come from you. A drawing only names its fonts, and it keeps no copy of them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-happens\"\u003EWhat happens\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA text style&#039;s font name is matched to a loaded font by its name, ignoring case, the folder and \u003Ccode\u003E.shx\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003ESIMPLEX.SHX\u003C/code\u003E finds \u003Ccode\u003Esimplex\u003C/code\u003E). A text in such a style is drawn as lines, in the screen, in a plot and in a PDF, at the text&#039;s own place, height, rotation, width factor and oblique angle. Its width is the font&#039;s own, so \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECenter\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERight\u003C/span\u003E alignment, wrapping and picking match what you see. A style whose font is not loaded keeps the built-in face, and when you open a drawing that names fonts you have not loaded, the command line says so.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe Standard style counts too: a drawing whose Standard style names a font of its own keeps it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"fonts-already-in-visual-order\"\u003EFonts already in visual order\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMany old Persian and Arabic SHX fonts hold letters already shaped for the screen and drawn left to right, so a text typed in logical order comes out backwards. Type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESHXFONTS\u003C/code\u003E, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReverse\u003C/span\u003E, and name the font: its text is then laid out last letter first. Run it again to undo. The switch belongs to the font, is kept in this browser, and is not saved in the drawing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-not-covered-yet\"\u003EWhat is not covered yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EText with inline format codes (colours, stacks in an MTEXT) stays in the built-in face.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EBig fonts (the double-byte kind) fill in for codes the main font lacks, but a text in an East Asian code page that CadCraft has already turned into Unicode is not mapped back to the font&#039;s double-byte codes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe font reader follows the published description of the format and was tested on fonts made for CadCraft. If one of yours draws wrongly, send us the font and the drawing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Drawings from other programs often name SHX fonts in their text styles, and a DWG does not carry them. Without the font, CadCraft draws that text in its own face, which is fine for ordinary Latin text but not for old fonts that map plain character codes to other letter shapes, such as legacy Persian annotation. Give CadCraft the fonts, and it draws the text in them. Load your fonts Type SHXFONTS (SHX) and choose: Folder (CadCraft Studio): pick a folder. Every .shx file in it is read now, and again each time CadCraft starts. Files (the browser): pick one or several .shx files. They are kept in this browser and never uploaded. List: the fonts that are loaded, and the ones the open drawing names but you have not loaded. Remove and Clear: forget one font, or all of them. CadCraft cannot include third-party fonts, so it never ships any: they come from you. A drawing only names its fonts, and it keeps no copy of them. What happens A text style's font name is matched to a loaded font by its name, ignoring case, the folder and .shx (SIMPLEX.SHX finds simplex). A text in such a style is drawn as lines, in the screen, in a plot and in a PDF, at the text's own place, height, rotation, width factor and oblique angle. Its width is the font's own, so Center and Right alignment, wrapping and picking match what you see. A style whose font is not loaded keeps the built-in face, and when you open a drawing that names fonts you have not loaded, the command line says so. The Standard style counts too: a drawing whose Standard style names a font of its own keeps it. Fonts already in visual order Many old Persian and Arabic SHX fonts hold letters already shaped for the screen and drawn left to right, so a text typed in logical order comes out backwards. Type SHXFONTS, choose Reverse, and name the font: its text is then laid out last letter first. Run it again to undo. The switch belongs to the font, is kept in this browser, and is not saved in the drawing. What is not covered yet Text with inline format codes (colours, stacks in an MTEXT) stays in the built-in face. Big fonts (the double-byte kind) fill in for codes the main font lacks, but a text in an East Asian code page that CadCraft has already turned into Unicode is not mapped back to the font's double-byte codes. The font reader follows the published description of the format and was tested on fonts made for CadCraft. If one of yours draws wrongly, send us the font and the drawing.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"lay-out-sheets-for-me","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Lay out sheets for me","summary":"Put the pieces of a drawing on sheets automatically: CadCraft picks a paper size and a standard scale that fits, places each piece with a title and the scale under it, and leaves everything editable.","section":"tasks","order":64,"keywords":["sheets","layout","layouts","paper","scale","automatic","AUTOSHEET","title block","viewports","named views"],"headings":[{"id":"keynotes","text":"Keynotes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cstrong\u003EView › More › Lay out sheets for me\u003C/strong\u003E (or type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EAUTOSHEET\u003C/code\u003E). CadCraft finds the pieces of your drawing (your named views, or the separate clusters of the model), picks a paper size and a standard scale at which they fit, and puts them on new sheets with a title block, a caption and the scale under each.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBefore it runs you can change what it does: \u003Cstrong\u003EPieces\u003C/strong\u003E (named views, clusters, the whole drawing, or let it decide), \u003Cstrong\u003EPaper\u003C/strong\u003E (automatic or a size such as \u003Ccode\u003Eiso-a1\u003C/code\u003E), \u003Cstrong\u003EScale\u003C/strong\u003E (one scale for all, or each piece at its own) and \u003Cstrong\u003ESheets\u003C/strong\u003E (how many pieces share a sheet).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe sheets are ordinary sheets: move a viewport, change its scale or delete a sheet as usual. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Z\"\u003E⌃Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E once to take all the new sheets back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"keynotes\"\u003EKeynotes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EAUTOSHEET\u003C/code\u003E choose \u003Cstrong\u003EKeynotes\u003C/strong\u003E to add a numbered legend under the views of each sheet. It lists the layers drawn in those views, one numbered line each, as plain text you can edit like any other text. Choose \u003Cstrong\u003EFile\u003C/strong\u003E to read your own keynote list (a number and its words per line, separated by a tab, a comma or a semicolon): a layer whose name matches a number in your list takes its words. No keynote list comes with the app.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Choose View › More › Lay out sheets for me (or type AUTOSHEET). CadCraft finds the pieces of your drawing (your named views, or the separate clusters of the model), picks a paper size and a standard scale at which they fit, and puts them on new sheets with a title block, a caption and the scale under each. Before it runs you can change what it does: Pieces (named views, clusters, the whole drawing, or let it decide), Paper (automatic or a size such as iso-a1), Scale (one scale for all, or each piece at its own) and Sheets (how many pieces share a sheet). The sheets are ordinary sheets: move a viewport, change its scale or delete a sheet as usual. Press Ctrl+Z once to take all the new sheets back. Keynotes In AUTOSHEET choose Keynotes to add a numbered legend under the views of each sheet. It lists the layers drawn in those views, one numbered line each, as plain text you can edit like any other text. Choose File to read your own keynote list (a number and its words per line, separated by a tab, a comma or a semicolon): a layer whose name matches a number in your list takes its words. No keynote list comes with the app.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"text-to-diagram","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Draw a diagram from text","summary":"Paste a Mermaid flowchart, a PlantUML component diagram, D2 or a from-to list and CadCraft lays out blocks and connectors you can edit like any other objects.","section":"tasks","order":66,"keywords":["diagram","flowchart","mermaid","plantuml","d2","from to","from-to list","block diagram","connectors","text to diagram","DIAGRAM","TEXTDIAGRAM","system diagram","wiring diagram","piping","csv"],"headings":[{"id":"what-it-reads","text":"What it reads","level":2},{"id":"how-it-is-laid-out","text":"How it is laid out","level":2},{"id":"subgraphs-and-sequence-diagrams","text":"Subgraphs and sequence diagrams","level":2},{"id":"symbols-for-single-line-and-piping-diagrams","text":"Symbols for single-line and piping diagrams","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIAGRAM\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003ETEXTDIAGRAM\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ET2D\u003C/code\u003E). A box opens: paste the text, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraw\u003C/span\u003E, click the top left corner of the diagram and give the text height. CadCraft reads the text, lays the blocks out in layers with right-angle connectors, and draws them on a layer called \u003Ccode\u003EDiagram\u003C/code\u003E. The new objects are selected, so you can move them straight away. They are plain objects: change a block&#039;s text with \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ETEXTEDIT\u003C/code\u003E, stretch a connector with its grips, erase a block, add your own lines. One \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EUNDO\u003C/code\u003E takes the whole diagram away.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAt the command line, without the box, the text can be one line with \u003Ccode\u003E;\u003C/code\u003E between statements: \u003Ccode\u003Eflowchart LR; A[Pump] --&gt; B[Tank]\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-reads\"\u003EWhat it reads\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EText\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat is read\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMermaid\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode\u003Eflowchart\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003Egraph\u003C/code\u003E with \u003Ccode\u003ETD\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ETB\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EBT\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ELR\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003ERL\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EA[text]\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EA(text)\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EA([text])\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EA{text}\u003C/code\u003E (a decision, drawn as a diamond), quoted text, \u003Ccode\u003E--&gt;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E---\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E-.-&gt;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E==&gt;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E--x\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;--&gt;\u003C/code\u003E, a label as `--&gt;\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPlantUML\u003C/strong\u003E between \u003Ccode\u003E@startuml\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E@enduml\u003C/code\u003E (component and deployment)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E[Component]\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ecomponent\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Enode\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Edatabase\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ecloud\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eactor\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Erectangle\u003C/code\u003E and others with \u003Ccode\u003Eas\u003C/code\u003E names, \u003Ccode\u003EA --&gt; B : label\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E-&gt;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E..&gt;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E--\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eleft to right direction\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ED2\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Ea -&gt; b: label\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ea &lt;- b\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ea &lt;-&gt; b\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ea -- b\u003C/code\u003E, chains, \u003Ccode\u003Ea: Label\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Edirection: right\u003C/code\u003E, quoted names, \u003Ccode\u003E#\u003C/code\u003E comments\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA from-to list\u003C/strong\u003E (a table pasted as text or CSV)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ecolumns \u003Ccode\u003Efrom\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eto\u003C/code\u003E and optionally \u003Ccode\u003Elabel\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003Etype\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003Edashed\u003C/code\u003E draws a dashed connector), in any order, separated by commas, semicolons or tabs; without a header line the first three columns are from, to, label\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAnything else on a line (styles, notes, loop frames) is skipped, and the command line says how many lines were skipped. A diagram has at most 400 blocks and 1200 connectors.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-it-is-laid-out\"\u003EHow it is laid out\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBlocks go in layers following the connectors (a connector that loops back is drawn back up), ordered to keep crossings down, with right-angle connectors that leave the bottom of a block and enter the top of the next; \u003Ccode\u003ELR\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003ERL\u003C/code\u003E turn the layers into columns. The text height you give sets the size of everything.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"subgraphs-and-sequence-diagrams\"\u003ESubgraphs and sequence diagrams\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA Mermaid \u003Ccode\u003Esubgraph name [Title]\u003C/code\u003E … \u003Ccode\u003Eend\u003C/code\u003E draws a dashed box with its title round its blocks; subgraphs can nest. The layout does not keep other blocks out of a box, so move a stray block with its grips.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA Mermaid \u003Ccode\u003EsequenceDiagram\u003C/code\u003E, or PlantUML text with \u003Ccode\u003Eparticipant\u003C/code\u003E lines and \u003Ccode\u003E-&gt;\u003C/code\u003E messages, is drawn as blocks across the top, a dashed lifeline under each and one row per message. \u003Ccode\u003E--&gt;\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E--&gt;&gt;\u003C/code\u003E are dashed, \u003Ccode\u003E-&gt;&gt;\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E-)\u003C/code\u003E have an arrow head, \u003Ccode\u003Eautonumber\u003C/code\u003E numbers the messages, a message to oneself loops. Notes and \u003Ccode\u003Eloop\u003C/code\u003E / \u003Ccode\u003Ealt\u003C/code\u003E frames are skipped.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"symbols-for-single-line-and-piping-diagrams\"\u003ESymbols for single-line and piping diagrams\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA block can be a drawn symbol, with its name under it, instead of a box. Name the symbol in the text:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EText\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EHow\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMermaid\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EP1[Feed pump]:::pump-cf\u003C/code\u003E or a line \u003Ccode\u003Eclass P1 pump-cf\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ED2\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EP1.shape: pump-cf\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003EP1: Feed pump { shape: pump-cf }\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlantUML\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Ecomponent P1 &lt;&lt;pump-cf&gt;&gt;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA from-to list\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ecolumns \u003Ccode\u003Efrom_symbol\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003Eto_symbol\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe names are the P&amp;ID shapes (\u003Ccode\u003Epump-cf\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Etank\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Egate\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eball\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eexch-shell\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Einst-field\u003C/code\u003E and the rest of the P&amp;ID library) and the Electrical set (\u003Ccode\u003Econtact.no\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ebreaker\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Emotor.3ph\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Etransformer\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Elamp\u003C/code\u003E and so on). A name that two sets share goes to the P&amp;ID set; write \u003Ccode\u003Epid:\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003Eel:\u003C/code\u003E in front to choose. A name CadCraft does not know is skipped and counted. The symbols are drawn as plain lines, circles and arcs on the \u003Ccode\u003EDiagram\u003C/code\u003E layer, scaled to the text height; they are not the add-ons&#039; blocks with tags, so use the P&amp;ID or Electrical commands for a working drawing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAI assistants can do the same through the \u003Ccode\u003Ediagram_from_text\u003C/code\u003E tool in the window.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Type DIAGRAM (TEXTDIAGRAM, T2D). A box opens: paste the text, choose Draw, click the top left corner of the diagram and give the text height. CadCraft reads the text, lays the blocks out in layers with right-angle connectors, and draws them on a layer called Diagram. The new objects are selected, so you can move them straight away. They are plain objects: change a block's text with TEXTEDIT, stretch a connector with its grips, erase a block, add your own lines. One UNDO takes the whole diagram away. At the command line, without the box, the text can be one line with ; between statements: flowchart LR; A[Pump] --\u003E B[Tank]. What it reads Text What is read Mermaid flowchart or graph with TD, TB, BT, LR or RL A, A[text], A(text), A([text]), A{text} (a decision, drawn as a diamond), quoted text, --\u003E, ---, -.-\u003E, ==\u003E, --x, \u003C--\u003E, a label as `--\u003E PlantUML between @startuml and @enduml (component and deployment) [Component], component, node, database, cloud, actor, rectangle and others with as names, A --\u003E B : label, -\u003E, ..\u003E, --, left to right direction D2 a -\u003E b: label, a \u003C- b, a \u003C-\u003E b, a -- b, chains, a: Label, direction: right, quoted names, # comments A from-to list (a table pasted as text or CSV) columns from, to and optionally label and type (dashed draws a dashed connector), in any order, separated by commas, semicolons or tabs; without a header line the first three columns are from, to, label Anything else on a line (styles, notes, loop frames) is skipped, and the command line says how many lines were skipped. A diagram has at most 400 blocks and 1200 connectors. How it is laid out Blocks go in layers following the connectors (a connector that loops back is drawn back up), ordered to keep crossings down, with right-angle connectors that leave the bottom of a block and enter the top of the next; LR and RL turn the layers into columns. The text height you give sets the size of everything. Subgraphs and sequence diagrams A Mermaid subgraph name [Title] … end draws a dashed box with its title round its blocks; subgraphs can nest. The layout does not keep other blocks out of a box, so move a stray block with its grips. A Mermaid sequenceDiagram, or PlantUML text with participant lines and -\u003E messages, is drawn as blocks across the top, a dashed lifeline under each and one row per message. --\u003E and --\u003E\u003E are dashed, -\u003E\u003E and -) have an arrow head, autonumber numbers the messages, a message to oneself loops. Notes and loop / alt frames are skipped. Symbols for single-line and piping diagrams A block can be a drawn symbol, with its name under it, instead of a box. Name the symbol in the text: Text How Mermaid P1[Feed pump]:::pump-cf or a line class P1 pump-cf D2 P1.shape: pump-cf or P1: Feed pump { shape: pump-cf } PlantUML component P1 \u003C\u003Cpump-cf\u003E\u003E A from-to list columns from_symbol and to_symbol The names are the P&ID shapes (pump-cf, tank, gate, ball, exch-shell, inst-field and the rest of the P&ID library) and the Electrical set (contact.no, breaker, motor.3ph, transformer, lamp and so on). A name that two sets share goes to the P&ID set; write pid: or el: in front to choose. A name CadCraft does not know is skipped and counted. The symbols are drawn as plain lines, circles and arcs on the Diagram layer, scaled to the text height; they are not the add-ons' blocks with tags, so use the P&ID or Electrical commands for a working drawing. AI assistants can do the same through the diagram_from_text tool in the window.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"redline-to-revision","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Turn a markup into a revision","summary":"Read the redline package MarkupCraft writes, accept or reject each change, put a revision cloud and a revision log on the accepted ones, and save the answers file MarkupCraft reads back.","section":"tasks","order":66,"keywords":["redline","markup","revision","cloud","revision cloud","revision log","accept","reject","markupcraft","review","redline package","answers","REDLINE"],"headings":[{"id":"go-through-the-changes","text":"Go through the changes","level":2},{"id":"what-to-know","text":"What to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA reviewer marks up a plan set in MarkupCraft and sends you a \u003Cstrong\u003Eredline package\u003C/strong\u003E, a file ending in \u003Ccode\u003E.redline.json\u003C/code\u003E. Here you go through it change by change, and the accepted ones become revision clouds on the drawing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"go-through-the-changes\"\u003EGo through the changes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EREDLINE\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003ERDL\u003C/code\u003E) and pick the package. Choose:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EList\u003C/span\u003E: every change with its number, state, sheet, kind, who asked and what they wrote.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAccept\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReject\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDone\u003C/span\u003E: type the numbers (\u003Ccode\u003E1,3-5\u003C/code\u003E, or \u003Ccode\u003EAll\u003C/code\u003E) and an optional note for the answers file. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReopen\u003C/span\u003E puts changes back to open.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFrame\u003C/span\u003E: the part of the drawing the marked-up sheet shows. Click two corners; press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for the whole drawing. The changes are placed by where they were on the sheet, so this is what lines them up.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EApply\u003C/span\u003E: draws a revision cloud (layer \u003Ccode\u003EREV-CLOUD\u003C/code\u003E, with the revision letter) round each accepted or done change, and a revision log, a ruled table of what was accepted with your notes (layer \u003Ccode\u003EREV-LOG\u003C/code\u003E), to the right of the drawing. One undo step.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E: writes \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;project&gt;-&lt;revision&gt;.redline-answers.json\u003C/code\u003E. Open it in MarkupCraft and the markups close: Accepted, Rejected or Completed, with your notes as comments.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-to-know\"\u003EWhat to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat the reviewer wrote is shown as written. CadCraft never follows instructions found in a package; it only shows them to you.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERejected and open changes add nothing to the drawing. The package is read on your computer; nothing is uploaded.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf your assistant is connected, it can read a package, record decisions and apply them with the tools \u003Ccode\u003Eredline_read\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eredline_decide\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eredline_apply\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003Eredline_answers\u003C/code\u003E; the drawing changes in one undo step, &quot;Assistant: Redline to revision&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"A reviewer marks up a plan set in MarkupCraft and sends you a redline package, a file ending in .redline.json. Here you go through it change by change, and the accepted ones become revision clouds on the drawing. Go through the changes Type REDLINE (RDL) and pick the package. Choose: List: every change with its number, state, sheet, kind, who asked and what they wrote. Accept, Reject, Done: type the numbers (1,3-5, or All) and an optional note for the answers file. Reopen puts changes back to open. Frame: the part of the drawing the marked-up sheet shows. Click two corners; press Enter for the whole drawing. The changes are placed by where they were on the sheet, so this is what lines them up. Apply: draws a revision cloud (layer REV-CLOUD, with the revision letter) round each accepted or done change, and a revision log, a ruled table of what was accepted with your notes (layer REV-LOG), to the right of the drawing. One undo step. Save: writes \u003Cproject\u003E-\u003Crevision\u003E.redline-answers.json. Open it in MarkupCraft and the markups close: Accepted, Rejected or Completed, with your notes as comments. What to know What the reviewer wrote is shown as written. CadCraft never follows instructions found in a package; it only shows them to you. Rejected and open changes add nothing to the drawing. The package is read on your computer; nothing is uploaded. If your assistant is connected, it can read a package, record decisions and apply them with the tools redline_read, redline_decide, redline_apply and redline_answers; the drawing changes in one undo step, \"Assistant: Redline to revision\".","lang":"en"},{"slug":"tags","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Tags: attributes that feed schedules","summary":"Define a tag (its kind, the values it may take, the blocks it applies to), set it on block references, and list the references as a schedule you can save as CSV.","section":"tasks","order":67,"keywords":["tags","tag","attribute","schedule","data extraction","property","fire rating","pick list","block reference","TAGS","csv"],"headings":[{"id":"define-and-set","text":"Define and set","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003Etag\u003C/strong\u003E is a block attribute with a definition: what kind of value it holds, which values are allowed, and which blocks it applies to. The values are ordinary block attributes, so any other program reads them and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EATTEDIT\u003C/code\u003E shows them. Only the definitions are new, and they are kept in the drawing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"define-and-set\"\u003EDefine and set\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ETAGS\u003C/code\u003E and choose:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDefine\u003C/span\u003E: a name (letters, digits, space, \u003Ccode\u003E_ . -\u003C/code\u003E), a kind (\u003Ccode\u003Etext\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Enumber\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Einteger\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eyesno\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Elength\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eangle\u003C/code\u003E), the allowed values separated by \u003Ccode\u003E;\u003C/code\u003E (leave empty for any), and the block names it applies to (\u003Ccode\u003EDOOR*\u003C/code\u003E matches every block starting with DOOR; empty means every block).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESet\u003C/span\u003E: with block references selected, type \u003Ccode\u003EFireRating=60 min; Width=850\u003C/code\u003E. A value outside the allowed list, or not a number for a number tag, is refused and said. One undo step. A block that does not have the attribute gets it as a hidden attribute.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EsChedule\u003C/span\u003E: every reference to a block a tag applies to, optionally grouped by a tag or by block name, printed and saved as CSV.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove\u003C/span\u003E: forgets a definition; the values stay on the references.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERenaming a tag renames its values on every reference. Tag names are not case sensitive, as attribute names are not.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"A tag is a block attribute with a definition: what kind of value it holds, which values are allowed, and which blocks it applies to. The values are ordinary block attributes, so any other program reads them and ATTEDIT shows them. Only the definitions are new, and they are kept in the drawing. Define and set Type TAGS and choose: Define: a name (letters, digits, space, _ . -), a kind (text, number, integer, yesno, length, angle), the allowed values separated by ; (leave empty for any), and the block names it applies to (DOOR* matches every block starting with DOOR; empty means every block). Set: with block references selected, type FireRating=60 min; Width=850. A value outside the allowed list, or not a number for a number tag, is refused and said. One undo step. A block that does not have the attribute gets it as a hidden attribute. sChedule: every reference to a block a tag applies to, optionally grouped by a tag or by block name, printed and saved as CSV. Remove: forgets a definition; the values stay on the references. Renaming a tag renames its values on every reference. Tag names are not case sensitive, as attribute names are not.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"changes-in-words","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Say in words what changed between two versions","summary":"Compare two versions of a drawing in History and get a plain list: where on the sheet, how big, what was added, removed or changed, and the words that changed.","section":"tasks","order":68,"keywords":["changes","compare","revisions","history","what changed","list in words","version","difference","text changed"],"headings":[],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHistory\u003C/span\u003E, pick a version and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECompare\u003C/span\u003E. Beside the marks on the drawing there is a button, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EList in words\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt groups the differences by where they are (objects within about two metres of each other are one place) and says each place in a sentence: &quot;Near 5000, 1500 (2 m by 3 m): 3 lines added&quot;. Sizes are in the drawing&#039;s own units. Text that changed is quoted from and to; new and removed text is quoted too. Layers added or removed are listed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe list is printed on the command line and copied, so you can paste it into an email, or to your own assistant to put in its own words. CadCraft does the grouping by rules on your computer; nothing is sent anywhere.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Open History, pick a version and choose Compare. Beside the marks on the drawing there is a button, List in words. It groups the differences by where they are (objects within about two metres of each other are one place) and says each place in a sentence: \"Near 5000, 1500 (2 m by 3 m): 3 lines added\". Sizes are in the drawing's own units. Text that changed is quoted from and to; new and removed text is quoted too. Layers added or removed are listed. The list is printed on the command line and copied, so you can paste it into an email, or to your own assistant to put in its own words. CadCraft does the grouping by rules on your computer; nothing is sent anywhere.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"layers","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Organize a drawing with layers","summary":"Create layers for walls, doors and notes, set colors, make a layer current, and turn layers off, freeze, lock or keep them from plotting.","section":"tasks","order":70,"keywords":["layer","layers","la","current layer","freeze","thaw","lock","off","on","plot","no plot","colour","color","layer properties","layiso","layuniso","isolate","layoff","layfrz","laymcur","layon","laythw","rename","laymrg","merge","laydel","delete layer"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-make","text":"What you will make","level":2},{"id":"the-layers-panel","text":"The Layers panel","level":2},{"id":"create-layers","text":"Create layers","level":2},{"id":"give-each-layer-a-color","text":"Give each layer a color","level":2},{"id":"draw-on-a-layer","text":"Draw on a layer","level":2},{"id":"move-objects-to-another-layer","text":"Move objects to another layer","level":2},{"id":"off-freeze-lock-and-plot","text":"Off, freeze, lock and plot","level":2},{"id":"work-on-layers-by-picking-objects","text":"Work on layers by picking objects","level":2},{"id":"rename-merge-and-delete-layers","text":"Rename, merge and delete layers","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-make\"\u003EWhat you will make\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThree layers, Walls, Doors and Notes, each with its own color, with objects drawn on each and moved between them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-layers-panel\"\u003EThe Layers panel\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELayers live in the panel on the left, under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayers\u003C/span\u003E. Each row shows, from left to right:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ea dot: filled for the \u003Cstrong\u003Ecurrent\u003C/strong\u003E layer, where new objects go;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ea color swatch;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe layer name;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Efour buttons: on/off (eye), freeze (snowflake), lock (padlock) and plot (printer).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery drawing has a layer called \u003Ccode\u003E0\u003C/code\u003E. Typing \u003Ccode\u003ELA\u003C/code\u003E and pressing \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E jumps to the panel.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"create-layers\"\u003ECreate layers\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew layer\u003C/span\u003E above the list. A name box opens.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EWalls\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd\u003C/span\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe status bar says &quot;Added layer Walls and made it current.&quot; The new layer gets color 7 (black on the light theme, white on the dark one).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew layer\u003C/span\u003E again, type \u003Ccode\u003EDoors\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew layer\u003C/span\u003E again, type \u003Ccode\u003ENotes\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENotes is now current.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"give-each-layer-a-color\"\u003EGive each layer a color\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the color swatch on the Walls row. Your system&#039;s color picker opens.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose a color and confirm.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe swatch and everything on Walls that is colored \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBy layer\u003C/span\u003E change.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA picked color that matches one of the 255 standard CAD colors is stored as that number. Any other color is kept exactly in CadCraft, but is saved as the nearest standard color in a DWG or DXF file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-on-a-layer\"\u003EDraw on a layer\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the name \u003Cstrong\u003EWalls\u003C/strong\u003E in the list. Its dot fills and the status bar says &quot;Current layer: Walls&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw a rectangle (\u003Ccode\u003EREC\u003C/code\u003E). It goes on Walls.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cstrong\u003EDoors\u003C/strong\u003E, and draw an arc (\u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E). It goes on Doors.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou can also choose the current layer in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECurrent settings\u003C/span\u003E on the right (nothing selected), under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayer\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"move-objects-to-another-layer\"\u003EMove objects to another layer\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the objects.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the right panel, choose the new layer in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayer\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThey move to that layer and take its color if their color is \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBy layer\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"off-freeze-lock-and-plot\"\u003EOff, freeze, lock and plot\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EButton\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOn/off (eye)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHides the layer&#039;s objects. They cannot be seen, selected or snapped to.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFreeze (snowflake)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESame effect as off in CadCraft. Kept separate because other programs treat them differently, and the setting is saved.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELock (padlock)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EObjects stay visible and you can snap to them, but they cannot be selected or changed. Good for a background you trace over.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlot (printer)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWhen pressed, the layer is shown on screen but \u003Cstrong\u003Eleft out of plots\u003C/strong\u003E and PDFs. Use it for construction lines and notes to yourself.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHover a button to see what it will do (&quot;Turn off&quot;, &quot;Freeze&quot;, &quot;Lock&quot;, &quot;Do not plot this layer&quot;).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou cannot turn off the current layer: the status bar says &quot;That is the current layer. Make another layer current first.&quot; Clicking a layer&#039;s name to make it current also turns it on and thaws it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery layer change, in the panel or by a command, is one undo step: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E puts it back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"work-on-layers-by-picking-objects\"\u003EWork on layers by picking objects\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou do not need to know a layer&#039;s name: pick an object on it. Type the command, or open \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETools\u003C/span\u003E at the top of the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayers\u003C/span\u003E panel.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECommand\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYISO\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect objects, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: only their layers stay on, every other layer is turned off. If the current layer is turned off, one of the isolated layers becomes current.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYUNISO\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETurns back on the layers the last \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYISO\u003C/code\u003E turned off, and gives back the current layer it changed.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYOFF\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick objects one after another: each one&#039;s layer is turned off. Type \u003Ccode\u003EU\u003C/code\u003E to take back the last one, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to finish.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYFRZ\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same, freezing each layer.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYMCUR\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick an object: its layer becomes current (and is turned on and thawed). With one object already selected, that one is used.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYON\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETurns every layer on.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYTHW\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThaws every layer.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe current layer is never turned off or frozen this way: the history says so, and you can make another layer current with \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYMCUR\u003C/code\u003E first. Picking a block reference uses the reference&#039;s own layer.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"rename-merge-and-delete-layers\"\u003ERename, merge and delete layers\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ERENAME\u003C/code\u003E: type \u003Ccode\u003ELA\u003C/code\u003E, the old name and the new one. Every object on the layer follows. (\u003Ccode\u003EB\u003C/code\u003E renames a block the same way, with every reference to it.)\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYMRG\u003C/code\u003E: click an object on each layer to merge (or type \u003Ccode\u003EN\u003C/code\u003E and a name), press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, then pick the target layer and answer \u003Ccode\u003EY\u003C/code\u003E. The objects move to the target — in the model, on sheets and inside blocks — and the merged layers go.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYDEL\u003C/code\u003E: pick an object on the layer (or \u003Ccode\u003EN\u003C/code\u003E and its name) and answer \u003Ccode\u003EY\u003C/code\u003E. The layer goes \u003Cstrong\u003Ewith every object on it\u003C/strong\u003E, everywhere. Layer 0, DEFPOINTS and the current layer cannot be deleted.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach is one undo step. All three are in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETools\u003C/span\u003E at the top of the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayers\u003C/span\u003E panel.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick one wall line, then type \u003Ccode\u003ELAYISO\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Only Walls stays on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw or change what you need on Walls.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003ELAYUNISO\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Doors and Notes come back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENew lines went on the wrong layer.\u003C/strong\u003E Check which dot is filled before you draw. A new layer becomes current as soon as you add it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAn object will not select.\u003C/strong\u003E Its layer is locked, off or frozen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe layer still prints in another program.\u003C/strong\u003E DXF R12 cannot store &quot;do not plot&quot;. CadCraft keeps the setting for itself when the drawing has a sheet, but other programs will plot that layer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELayer names in capitals after saving.\u003C/strong\u003E DXF R12 stores names in capitals, so \u003Ccode\u003EWalls\u003C/code\u003E comes back as \u003Ccode\u003EWALLS\u003C/code\u003E, and spaces become \u003Ccode\u003E_\u003C/code\u003E. A saved DWG keeps names as you typed them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERenaming, merging or deleting a layer.\u003C/strong\u003E Use \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ERENAME\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYMRG\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYDEL\u003C/code\u003E (also in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETools\u003C/span\u003E): see below.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will make Three layers, Walls, Doors and Notes, each with its own color, with objects drawn on each and moved between them. The Layers panel Layers live in the panel on the left, under Layers. Each row shows, from left to right: a dot: filled for the current layer, where new objects go; a color swatch; the layer name; four buttons: on/off (eye), freeze (snowflake), lock (padlock) and plot (printer). Every drawing has a layer called 0. Typing LA and pressing Enter jumps to the panel. Create layers Click New layer above the list. A name box opens. Type Walls and press Enter (or click Add). The status bar says \"Added layer Walls and made it current.\" The new layer gets color 7 (black on the light theme, white on the dark one). Click New layer again, type Doors and press Enter. Click New layer again, type Notes and press Enter. Notes is now current. Give each layer a color Click the color swatch on the Walls row. Your system's color picker opens. Choose a color and confirm. The swatch and everything on Walls that is colored By layer change. A picked color that matches one of the 255 standard CAD colors is stored as that number. Any other color is kept exactly in CadCraft, but is saved as the nearest standard color in a DWG or DXF file. Draw on a layer Click the name Walls in the list. Its dot fills and the status bar says \"Current layer: Walls\". Draw a rectangle (REC). It goes on Walls. Click Doors, and draw an arc (A). It goes on Doors. You can also choose the current layer in Current settings on the right (nothing selected), under Layer. Move objects to another layer Select the objects. In the right panel, choose the new layer in Layer. They move to that layer and take its color if their color is By layer. Off, freeze, lock and plot Button What it does On/off (eye) Hides the layer's objects. They cannot be seen, selected or snapped to. Freeze (snowflake) Same effect as off in CadCraft. Kept separate because other programs treat them differently, and the setting is saved. Lock (padlock) Objects stay visible and you can snap to them, but they cannot be selected or changed. Good for a background you trace over. Plot (printer) When pressed, the layer is shown on screen but left out of plots and PDFs. Use it for construction lines and notes to yourself. Hover a button to see what it will do (\"Turn off\", \"Freeze\", \"Lock\", \"Do not plot this layer\"). You cannot turn off the current layer: the status bar says \"That is the current layer. Make another layer current first.\" Clicking a layer's name to make it current also turns it on and thaws it. Every layer change, in the panel or by a command, is one undo step: Ctrl+Z puts it back. Work on layers by picking objects You do not need to know a layer's name: pick an object on it. Type the command, or open Tools at the top of the Layers panel. Command What it does LAYISO Select objects, press Enter: only their layers stay on, every other layer is turned off. If the current layer is turned off, one of the isolated layers becomes current. LAYUNISO Turns back on the layers the last LAYISO turned off, and gives back the current layer it changed. LAYOFF Click objects one after another: each one's layer is turned off. Type U to take back the last one, Enter to finish. LAYFRZ The same, freezing each layer. LAYMCUR Click an object: its layer becomes current (and is turned on and thawed). With one object already selected, that one is used. LAYON Turns every layer on. LAYTHW Thaws every layer. The current layer is never turned off or frozen this way: the history says so, and you can make another layer current with LAYMCUR first. Picking a block reference uses the reference's own layer. Rename, merge and delete layers RENAME: type LA, the old name and the new one. Every object on the layer follows. (B renames a block the same way, with every reference to it.) LAYMRG: click an object on each layer to merge (or type N and a name), press Enter, then pick the target layer and answer Y. The objects move to the target — in the model, on sheets and inside blocks — and the merged layers go. LAYDEL: pick an object on the layer (or N and its name) and answer Y. The layer goes with every object on it, everywhere. Layer 0, DEFPOINTS and the current layer cannot be deleted. Each is one undo step. All three are in Tools at the top of the Layers panel. Click one wall line, then type LAYISO and press Enter. Only Walls stays on. Draw or change what you need on Walls. Type LAYUNISO and press Enter. Doors and Notes come back. Common mistakes New lines went on the wrong layer. Check which dot is filled before you draw. A new layer becomes current as soon as you add it. An object will not select. Its layer is locked, off or frozen. The layer still prints in another program. DXF R12 cannot store \"do not plot\". CadCraft keeps the setting for itself when the drawing has a sheet, but other programs will plot that layer. Layer names in capitals after saving. DXF R12 stores names in capitals, so Walls comes back as WALLS, and spaces become _. A saved DWG keeps names as you typed them. Renaming, merging or deleting a layer. Use RENAME, LAYMRG or LAYDEL (also in Tools): see below.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"classes","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Organize a drawing with classes","summary":"Classes sort objects a second way, across layers (Lighting-Fixtures-Spot): show and hide them, give them a colour, linetype, lineweight, fill and text style that objects can take \"by class\", and set the class new objects go in.","section":"tasks","order":71,"keywords":["class","classes","CLASSES","CLS","CLASS","by class","ByClass","active class","use at creation","None","design layer","layer scale","elevation","Vectorworks"],"headings":[{"id":"open-the-classes-panel","text":"Open the Classes panel","level":2},{"id":"put-objects-in-a-class","text":"Put objects in a class","level":2},{"id":"how-an-object-draws-object-class-layer","text":"How an object draws: object, class, layer","level":2},{"id":"rename-and-delete","text":"Rename and delete","level":2},{"id":"design-layers","text":"Design layers","level":2},{"id":"in-dwg-and-dxf-files","text":"In DWG and DXF files","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ELayers say \u003Cem\u003Ewhere\u003C/em\u003E an object belongs in the drawing; classes say \u003Cem\u003Ewhat\u003C/em\u003E it is. A wall on layer \u003Ccode\u003ELevel 1\u003C/code\u003E can be in class \u003Ccode\u003EWalls-Exterior\u003C/code\u003E, a spot light on layer \u003Ccode\u003ECeiling\u003C/code\u003E in class \u003Ccode\u003ELighting-Fixtures-Spot\u003C/code\u003E. You can hide every light on every layer at once, or plot every exterior wall heavier, without moving anything between layers.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery object starts in class \u003Cstrong\u003ENone\u003C/strong\u003E. A drawing with no classes of its own works exactly as before.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-the-classes-panel\"\u003EOpen the Classes panel\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003ECLASSES\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003ECLS\u003C/code\u003E) and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClasses…\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E menu of the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView\u003C/span\u003E group. The panel opens over the drawing and stays open while you draw.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAdd a class:\u003C/strong\u003E type its name in the box at the top and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. A \u003Ccode\u003E-\u003C/code\u003E makes a level: \u003Ccode\u003ELighting-Fixtures-Spot\u003C/code\u003E sits under \u003Ccode\u003ELighting-Fixtures\u003C/code\u003E, which sits under \u003Ccode\u003ELighting\u003C/code\u003E; the missing levels are added for you.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EShow or hide:\u003C/strong\u003E the eye beside a class. Hiding a class hides every class under it, on screen and on paper.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EActive class:\u003C/strong\u003E the round button. New objects you draw go in the active class.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPick a class:\u003C/strong\u003E click its name. The buttons and settings below the list then work on it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe number at the end of each row is how many objects are in that class (in the model, on the sheets and inside block definitions).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"put-objects-in-a-class\"\u003EPut objects in a class\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect the objects, pick a class in the panel and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESet class\u003C/span\u003E. Tick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBy class\u003C/span\u003E first to make them take their colour, linetype, lineweight, fill and text style from the class. You can also:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Etype \u003Ccode\u003ECLASS\u003C/code\u003E with objects selected and enter a class name (a new name makes the class);\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Etype \u003Ccode\u003ECLASS\u003C/code\u003E with nothing selected to set the active class; \u003Ccode\u003E?\u003C/code\u003E lists the classes with their counts;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Euse the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClass\u003C/span\u003E section of the Properties panel: the class, and a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E… by class\u003C/span\u003E switch for colour, linetype and lineweight (fill for hatches, text style for text);\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eselect by class with \u003Ccode\u003EQSELECT\u003C/code\u003E: choose the property \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClass\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect its objects\u003C/span\u003E selects every object of the picked class on the open sheet (or in the model).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-an-object-draws-object-class-layer\"\u003EHow an object draws: object, class, layer\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn object&#039;s colour, linetype, lineweight, fill and text style can be \u003Cstrong\u003Eby class\u003C/strong\u003E, the way they can be by layer. CadCraft looks in this order:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe object&#039;s own value, unless that value is by class.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe class&#039;s value, when the object takes it by class and the class has one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe layer&#039;s value.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA class whose colour (or linetype …) is \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENot set\u003C/span\u003E passes the object on to its layer. Pick a class and set its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EColor\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinetype\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELineweight\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFill (hatches)\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EText style\u003C/span\u003E below the buttons. Tick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUse at creation\u003C/span\u003E to make new objects drawn in that class take all of them by class.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach change in the panel is one undo step.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"rename-and-delete\"\u003ERename and delete\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERename\u003C/span\u003E edits the name in place: press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to keep it, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to leave it. The classes under it follow (\u003Ccode\u003ELighting-Spot\u003C/code\u003E becomes \u003Ccode\u003ELight-Spot\u003C/code\u003E when \u003Ccode\u003ELighting\u003C/code\u003E becomes \u003Ccode\u003ELight\u003C/code\u003E), and so do their objects and any viewport overrides.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E removes the class and every class under it. Their objects move to \u003Cstrong\u003ENone\u003C/strong\u003E. One \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E puts it all back. \u003Cstrong\u003ENone\u003C/strong\u003E cannot be renamed or deleted.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"design-layers\"\u003EDesign layers\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDesign layers\u003C/span\u003E each layer can carry a scale (\u003Ccode\u003E1:50\u003C/code\u003E) and an elevation. A design layer is an ordinary layer: model space and sheets work as before, and the scale and elevation are kept with the drawing for the tools that will use them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"in-dwg-and-dxf-files\"\u003EIn DWG and DXF files\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClasses are kept in every DWG version CadCraft saves and in DXF (R12 and 2000). Each object&#039;s class is its own extended data (application \u003Ccode\u003ECADCRAFT_CLASS\u003C/code\u003E), so AutoCAD and other programs keep it with the object. The class list, colours and visibility are kept in one unused block named \u003Ccode\u003ECADCRAFT_CLASSES\u003C/code\u003E; if another program purges that block, CadCraft still finds every object&#039;s class and the classes come back with default settings.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOther programs do not know about classes: they draw each object with its own or its layer&#039;s values.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee also \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/viewport-overrides\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/viewport-overrides\"\u003EViewport class overrides\u003C/a\u003E and \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/layers\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/layers\"\u003EOrganize a drawing with layers\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Layers say where an object belongs in the drawing; classes say what it is. A wall on layer Level 1 can be in class Walls-Exterior, a spot light on layer Ceiling in class Lighting-Fixtures-Spot. You can hide every light on every layer at once, or plot every exterior wall heavier, without moving anything between layers. Every object starts in class None. A drawing with no classes of its own works exactly as before. Open the Classes panel Type CLASSES (or CLS) and press Enter, or choose Classes… in the More menu of the View group. The panel opens over the drawing and stays open while you draw. Add a class: type its name in the box at the top and press Enter. A - makes a level: Lighting-Fixtures-Spot sits under Lighting-Fixtures, which sits under Lighting; the missing levels are added for you. Show or hide: the eye beside a class. Hiding a class hides every class under it, on screen and on paper. Active class: the round button. New objects you draw go in the active class. Pick a class: click its name. The buttons and settings below the list then work on it. The number at the end of each row is how many objects are in that class (in the model, on the sheets and inside block definitions). Put objects in a class Select the objects, pick a class in the panel and choose Set class. Tick By class first to make them take their colour, linetype, lineweight, fill and text style from the class. You can also: type CLASS with objects selected and enter a class name (a new name makes the class); type CLASS with nothing selected to set the active class; ? lists the classes with their counts; use the Class section of the Properties panel: the class, and a … by class switch for colour, linetype and lineweight (fill for hatches, text style for text); select by class with QSELECT: choose the property Class. Select its objects selects every object of the picked class on the open sheet (or in the model). How an object draws: object, class, layer An object's colour, linetype, lineweight, fill and text style can be by class, the way they can be by layer. CadCraft looks in this order: The object's own value, unless that value is by class. The class's value, when the object takes it by class and the class has one. The layer's value. A class whose colour (or linetype …) is Not set passes the object on to its layer. Pick a class and set its Color, Linetype, Lineweight, Fill (hatches) and Text style below the buttons. Tick Use at creation to make new objects drawn in that class take all of them by class. Each change in the panel is one undo step. Rename and delete Rename edits the name in place: press Enter to keep it, Esc to leave it. The classes under it follow (Lighting-Spot becomes Light-Spot when Lighting becomes Light), and so do their objects and any viewport overrides. Delete removes the class and every class under it. Their objects move to None. One Undo puts it all back. None cannot be renamed or deleted. Design layers Under Design layers each layer can carry a scale (1:50) and an elevation. A design layer is an ordinary layer: model space and sheets work as before, and the scale and elevation are kept with the drawing for the tools that will use them. In DWG and DXF files Classes are kept in every DWG version CadCraft saves and in DXF (R12 and 2000). Each object's class is its own extended data (application CADCRAFT_CLASS), so AutoCAD and other programs keep it with the object. The class list, colours and visibility are kept in one unused block named CADCRAFT_CLASSES; if another program purges that block, CadCraft still finds every object's class and the classes come back with default settings. Other programs do not know about classes: they draw each object with its own or its layer's values. See also Viewport class overrides and Organize a drawing with layers.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"merge-drawings","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Merge two people's edits of one drawing","summary":"Let git, Fossil or Mercurial merge two copies of a DWG or DXF object by object, using the key CadCraft keeps on every object.","section":"tasks","order":74,"keywords":["merge","git","fossil","mercurial","version control","diff","conflict","three-way","cadcraft merge","key","gitattributes","DWG","DXF"],"headings":[{"id":"what-makes-it-work","text":"What makes it work","level":2},{"id":"set-it-up","text":"Set it up","level":2},{"id":"the-rules","text":"The rules","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ETwo people edit the same drawing. If they changed different objects, the merge can be automatic: nothing to redo by hand.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-makes-it-work\"\u003EWhat makes it work\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECadCraft gives every object you draw a short \u003Cstrong\u003Ekey\u003C/strong\u003E that never changes and is never given to two objects. It is saved with the object in the DWG or DXF (as extra data other programs keep). The merge looks objects up by that key, not by their position in the file, because every program renumbers positions when it saves. Turn this off in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › CadCraft › Merge-friendly files if you never merge; the files get about 10 percent smaller.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA drawing saved before keys existed still merges: its objects get a key from their place in the file, the same for everyone who opens it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"set-it-up\"\u003ESet it up\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EGit needs two lines once per repository:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E# .gitattributes\n*.dwg merge=cadcraft\n*.dxf merge=cadcraft\n\n# one time, on each computer\ngit config merge.cadcraft.driver &quot;node /path/to/tools/cli/merge.mjs %O %A %B&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWithout git, run it by hand: \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft merge &lt;base&gt; &lt;ours&gt; &lt;theirs&gt; -o &lt;result&gt;\u003C/code\u003E. Fossil and Mercurial take the same three files in their own merge-tool setting.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-rules\"\u003EThe rules\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAn object changed on one side only is taken. The same change on both sides is taken once.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe same property changed two ways is a \u003Cstrong\u003Econflict\u003C/strong\u003E: your side stays and the report lists both values. Open the result, look at that object, fix it by hand.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAn object added on either side is kept. An object removed on one side and untouched on the other is removed. Removed on one side and changed on the other: removed, and reported.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELayers, blocks, text and dimension styles, sheets and named views merge the same way. Constraints follow the objects they name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe result is written in the same file format and version as yours.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENot merged: the order objects are drawn in is yours, then the objects the other side added. Changes to the same block definition on both sides are a conflict, never mixed. Nothing is uploaded; it all runs on your computer.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Two people edit the same drawing. If they changed different objects, the merge can be automatic: nothing to redo by hand. What makes it work CadCraft gives every object you draw a short key that never changes and is never given to two objects. It is saved with the object in the DWG or DXF (as extra data other programs keep). The merge looks objects up by that key, not by their position in the file, because every program renumbers positions when it saves. Turn this off in Settings › CadCraft › Merge-friendly files if you never merge; the files get about 10 percent smaller. A drawing saved before keys existed still merges: its objects get a key from their place in the file, the same for everyone who opens it. Set it up Git needs two lines once per repository: # .gitattributes *.dwg merge=cadcraft *.dxf merge=cadcraft # one time, on each computer git config merge.cadcraft.driver \"node /path/to/tools/cli/merge.mjs %O %A %B\" Without git, run it by hand: cadcraft merge \u003Cbase\u003E \u003Cours\u003E \u003Ctheirs\u003E -o \u003Cresult\u003E. Fossil and Mercurial take the same three files in their own merge-tool setting. The rules An object changed on one side only is taken. The same change on both sides is taken once. The same property changed two ways is a conflict: your side stays and the report lists both values. Open the result, look at that object, fix it by hand. An object added on either side is kept. An object removed on one side and untouched on the other is removed. Removed on one side and changed on the other: removed, and reported. Layers, blocks, text and dimension styles, sheets and named views merge the same way. Constraints follow the objects they name. The result is written in the same file format and version as yours. Not merged: the order objects are drawn in is yours, then the objects the other side added. Changes to the same block definition on both sides are a conflict, never mixed. Nothing is uploaded; it all runs on your computer.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"text","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Add text and labels","summary":"Place room labels with single-line text, write wrapped notes with paragraph text typed in place, and edit text afterwards.","section":"tasks","order":80,"keywords":["text","dtext","mtext","mleader","multileader","leader","revcloud","revision cloud","cloud","find","replace","find and replace","paragraph","wrap","line spacing","label","note","annotation","height","justify","textmask","background mask","mask","wipeout","rotation","edit text","ddedit","textedit","font"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-make","text":"What you will make","level":2},{"id":"text-height-and-drawing-scale","text":"Text height and drawing scale","level":2},{"id":"place-a-centered-label","text":"Place a centered label","level":2},{"id":"write-a-note-that-wraps","text":"Write a note that wraps","level":2},{"id":"several-single-lines-instead","text":"Several single lines instead","level":3},{"id":"justification-options","text":"Justification options","level":2},{"id":"edit-text","text":"Edit text","level":2},{"id":"formatting-inside-paragraph-text-from-other-programs","text":"Formatting inside paragraph text from other programs","level":2},{"id":"point-at-something-multileaders","text":"Point at something: multileaders","level":2},{"id":"mark-changes-with-a-revision-cloud","text":"Mark changes with a revision cloud","level":2},{"id":"find-and-replace-words","text":"Find and replace words","level":2},{"id":"hide-the-lines-behind-a-label","text":"Hide the lines behind a label","level":2},{"id":"font","text":"Font","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-make\"\u003EWhat you will make\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETwo room labels, centered in their rooms, a note that wraps to its width, then a correction to one of the labels.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"text-height-and-drawing-scale\"\u003EText height and drawing scale\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EText height is in drawing units. In a drawing in millimeters that will be plotted at 1:50, text 2.5 mm tall on paper must be 2.5 × 50 = 125 units tall in the model. On a sheet tab you draw in paper units, so 2.5 there is 2.5 mm on paper.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"place-a-centered-label\"\u003EPlace a centered label\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EDT\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EText\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar). The prompt reads \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify start point of text [Justify]:\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EJ\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003EEnter an option [Left/Center/Right/Middle/TL/MC] &lt;L&gt;:\u003C/code\u003E, type \u003Ccode\u003EMC\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify insertion point of text\u003C/code\u003E, click the middle of a room.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify height\u003C/code\u003E, type \u003Ccode\u003E250\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify rotation angle of text &lt;0&gt;\u003C/code\u003E, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003EEnter text\u003C/code\u003E, type \u003Ccode\u003EKITCHEN\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe label appears, centered on the point you clicked.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E on the empty \u003Ccode\u003EEnter text\u003C/code\u003E prompt to end the command.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"write-a-note-that-wraps\"\u003EWrite a note that wraps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EParagraph text (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMTEXT\u003C/code\u003E) wraps to a width and is typed straight onto the drawing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003ET\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EParagraph\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify first corner\u003C/code\u003E, click where the note&#039;s top-left corner goes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo change the height first, type \u003Ccode\u003EH\u003C/code\u003E, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, type \u003Ccode\u003E250\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify opposite corner [Height/Justify/Line spacing/Rotation/Width]\u003C/code\u003E, click to the right of the first corner. The distance across is the width the text wraps at; how far down you click does not limit it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA box opens over the drawing with the cursor in it. Type \u003Ccode\u003EALL DIMENSIONS ARE IN MILLIMETERS UNLESS NOTED\u003C/code\u003E. The words wrap at the width as you type.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to start a new paragraph and type \u003Ccode\u003ECHECK ON SITE\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Return\"\u003E⌘ Return\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Enter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOK\u003C/span\u003E, or click anywhere outside the box. The note is one object.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe bar above the box changes \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHeight\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELine spacing\u003C/span\u003E (1 is normal, 1.5 is half as much again), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWidth\u003C/span\u003E (0 = no wrapping) and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJustify\u003C/span\u003E while you type. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECancel\u003C/span\u003E closes it without adding anything.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe options at the second corner: \u003Ccode\u003EH\u003C/code\u003E height, \u003Ccode\u003EJ\u003C/code\u003E justification (\u003Ccode\u003ETL\u003C/code\u003E top left … \u003Ccode\u003EBR\u003C/code\u003E bottom right: the corner or middle of the box that stays put), \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E line spacing, \u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E rotation, \u003Ccode\u003EW\u003C/code\u003E a typed width instead of the second corner.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile the box is open, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E types a space and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E starts a new paragraph. A saved DWG keeps paragraph text as MTEXT; a DXF R12 has no paragraph text, so each line is saved as its own text.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"several-single-lines-instead\"\u003ESeveral single lines instead\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ETEXT\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EDT\u003C/code\u003E) asks for one line at a time: each \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E starts the next line, 1.667 times the text height lower, and each line is its own object. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E on an empty line to finish.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"justification-options\"\u003EJustification options\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EOption\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EThe point you click is\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELeft (default)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe left end of the baseline\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECenter\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe middle of the baseline\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERight\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe right end of the baseline\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMiddle, MC\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe middle of the text, both across and up and down\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETL\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe top-left corner\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"edit-text\"\u003EEdit text\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDouble-click a text object. It opens in the same box as new paragraph text, with the cursor at the end.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChange the words.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Return\"\u003E⌘ Return\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Enter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or click outside the box. The text on the drawing changes; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E leaves it as it was.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ETEXTEDIT\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EED\u003C/code\u003E) does the same: pick one text after another, and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E when done.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect a text to change it in the right panel instead: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EText\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHeight\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWrap width\u003C/span\u003E (0 = no wrapping), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELine spacing\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERotation\u003C/span\u003E, the X and Y position and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJustify\u003C/span\u003E. The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJustify\u003C/span\u003E list also offers Bottom left, which text from other programs sometimes uses.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"formatting-inside-paragraph-text-from-other-programs\"\u003EFormatting inside paragraph text from other programs\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EParagraph text in a DWG or DXF can carry codes inside it: colors per word, bold and italic, underline, overline and strikethrough, stacked fractions such as 1/2, and a different height for a word. CadCraft keeps those codes, draws them (on screen and in a plotted PDF) and saves them back unchanged.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen you edit such a text (double-click, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ETEXTEDIT\u003C/code\u003E, the Text field of the right panel, or \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EFIND\u003C/code\u003E with Replace), you edit its plain words. A word you type goes into the run next to it and takes its color and style; the other codes stay where they are. If you retype a stacked fraction&#039;s numbers, that fraction becomes plain text. Setting the whole text from the Text field keeps the codes only where it can tell which words stayed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFont names (\u003Ccode\u003E\\F\u003C/code\u003E) are kept and saved, but everything is drawn in the one Helvetica-style font. There is no toolbar for typing codes yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"point-at-something-multileaders\"\u003EPoint at something: multileaders\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELeader\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAnnotate\u003C/span\u003E group (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMLEADER\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EMLD\u003C/code\u003E) draws an arrow, a leader line, a short landing and a note, as one object.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELeader\u003C/span\u003E. At \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify leader arrowhead location\u003C/code\u003E, click what the note is about.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify leader landing location\u003C/code\u003E, click where the note goes. The landing turns toward the side you clicked.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the note in the box that opens, and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Return\"\u003E⌘ Return\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Enter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E at the first question set the arrowhead size and the landing length; the text height comes from the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EText height\u003C/span\u003E for new dimensions, times \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOverall scale\u003C/span\u003E. Double-click a multileader to change its words. Grips move the arrowhead and the landing; the note follows the landing. In \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EText\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHeight\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWrap width\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArrowhead size\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELanding distance\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMultileaders save as real multileaders in DWG. DXF has no multileader in R12 or 2000, so there they are saved as the lines, arrowhead and text they draw.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mark-changes-with-a-revision-cloud\"\u003EMark changes with a revision cloud\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAnnotate\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERevision cloud\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EREVCLOUD\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ERC\u003C/code\u003E) draws a cloud of arcs round what changed. The history shows the arc length and the type it will use.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERectangular\u003C/strong\u003E (the first time): click two corners.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E \u003Cstrong\u003EPolygonal\u003C/strong\u003E: click the corners, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E closes the cloud.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EF\u003C/code\u003E \u003Cstrong\u003EFreehand\u003C/strong\u003E: click where it starts and move the pointer round the area without clicking; an arc is added every arc length. Click or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to close it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EO\u003C/code\u003E \u003Cstrong\u003EObject\u003C/strong\u003E: pick a closed polyline, circle or ellipse (or a line, arc or open polyline); it becomes the cloud. \u003Ccode\u003EReverse direction?\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003EY\u003C/code\u003E turns the arcs inward.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E \u003Cstrong\u003EArc length\u003C/strong\u003E: the length of each arc. Choose one that suits the plot scale.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe cloud is a closed polyline of arcs, so it saves in every format.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"find-and-replace-words\"\u003EFind and replace words\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFind and replace\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EFIND\u003C/code\u003E) looks through text, the values of block attributes and dimension text on the open tab (or only in the selected objects).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the words in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFind\u003C/span\u003E. The list shows each place they are, and the count.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMatch case\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWhole words only\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUse wildcards (* and ?)\u003C/span\u003E if you need them, and untick what not to look in.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect found\u003C/span\u003E to select those objects, or type the new words in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReplace with\u003C/span\u003E (the list shows what each becomes) and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReplace all\u003C/span\u003E. One \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes every replacement.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA dimension&#039;s measured value is not text, so it is never replaced: in \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;&gt; TYP.\u003C/code\u003E, only \u003Ccode\u003ETYP.\u003C/code\u003E can be.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"hide-the-lines-behind-a-label\"\u003EHide the lines behind a label\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the texts, type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ETEXTMASK\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EB\u003C/code\u003E paints the drawing&#039;s background behind them; \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E asks for a colour number. \u003Ccode\u003EO\u003C/code\u003E takes the mask away.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EBorder offset factor\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E keeps 1.5, half a text height round the text.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe mask covers lines under the text, on screen and when you plot. DWG 2004 or later and DXF 2000 keep it; the 2000 DWG format and DXF R12 cannot, and the save says so.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"font\"\u003EFont\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAll text is drawn in a Helvetica-style font, on screen and in PDFs. Text from opened drawings keeps its words, height and position, but not its original font (the name of a font inside paragraph text is kept in the file, see above).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn plotted PDFs, text in any European script, Cyrillic or Greek prints as itself (the PDF carries the letters it needs, and you can still select and search the text). Chinese, Japanese and Korean text prints as itself too (the plot fetches a larger font the first time it is needed). Characters no font has a letter for, such as Arabic, print as &quot;?&quot;, and the history says how many there were.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe text is too small to see.\u003C/strong\u003E The height is in drawing units. In a large drawing, use a large height (see above), or zoom in.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe command ended after one line.\u003C/strong\u003E You pressed \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E on an empty line in \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ETEXT\u003C/code\u003E. Start it again, or use paragraph text (\u003Ccode\u003ET\u003C/code\u003E), where \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E starts a new paragraph.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ET\u003C/code\u003E opened a box instead of asking for a line.\u003C/strong\u003E As in AutoCAD, \u003Ccode\u003ET\u003C/code\u003E is paragraph text (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMTEXT\u003C/code\u003E); single-line text is \u003Ccode\u003EDT\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe text wraps in a different place in AutoCAD.\u003C/strong\u003E CadCraft wraps with Helvetica widths; another font can fit a word more or less on a line.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EText in a mirrored copy.\u003C/strong\u003E It is placed mirrored but stays readable left to right.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will make Two room labels, centered in their rooms, a note that wraps to its width, then a correction to one of the labels. Text height and drawing scale Text height is in drawing units. In a drawing in millimeters that will be plotted at 1:50, text 2.5 mm tall on paper must be 2.5 × 50 = 125 units tall in the model. On a sheet tab you draw in paper units, so 2.5 there is 2.5 mm on paper. Place a centered label Type DT and press Enter (or click Text in the toolbar). The prompt reads Specify start point of text [Justify]:. Type J and press Enter. At Enter an option [Left/Center/Right/Middle/TL/MC] \u003CL\u003E:, type MC and press Enter. At Specify insertion point of text, click the middle of a room. At Specify height, type 250 and press Enter. At Specify rotation angle of text \u003C0\u003E, press Enter. At Enter text, type KITCHEN and press Enter. The label appears, centered on the point you clicked. Press Enter on the empty Enter text prompt to end the command. Write a note that wraps Paragraph text (MTEXT) wraps to a width and is typed straight onto the drawing. Type T and press Enter, or click Paragraph in the toolbar. At Specify first corner, click where the note's top-left corner goes. To change the height first, type H, press Enter, type 250 and press Enter. At Specify opposite corner [Height/Justify/Line spacing/Rotation/Width], click to the right of the first corner. The distance across is the width the text wraps at; how far down you click does not limit it. A box opens over the drawing with the cursor in it. Type ALL DIMENSIONS ARE IN MILLIMETERS UNLESS NOTED. The words wrap at the width as you type. Press Enter to start a new paragraph and type CHECK ON SITE. Press Ctrl+Enter, click OK, or click anywhere outside the box. The note is one object. The bar above the box changes Height, Line spacing (1 is normal, 1.5 is half as much again), Width (0 = no wrapping) and Justify while you type. Esc or Cancel closes it without adding anything. The options at the second corner: H height, J justification (TL top left … BR bottom right: the corner or middle of the box that stays put), L line spacing, R rotation, W a typed width instead of the second corner. While the box is open, Space types a space and Enter starts a new paragraph. A saved DWG keeps paragraph text as MTEXT; a DXF R12 has no paragraph text, so each line is saved as its own text. Several single lines instead TEXT (DT) asks for one line at a time: each Enter starts the next line, 1.667 times the text height lower, and each line is its own object. Press Enter on an empty line to finish. Justification options Option The point you click is Left (default) The left end of the baseline Center The middle of the baseline Right The right end of the baseline Middle, MC The middle of the text, both across and up and down TL The top-left corner Edit text Double-click a text object. It opens in the same box as new paragraph text, with the cursor at the end. Change the words. Press Ctrl+Enter or click outside the box. The text on the drawing changes; Esc leaves it as it was. TEXTEDIT (ED) does the same: pick one text after another, and press Enter when done. Select a text to change it in the right panel instead: Text, Height, Wrap width (0 = no wrapping), Line spacing, Rotation, the X and Y position and Justify. The Justify list also offers Bottom left, which text from other programs sometimes uses. Formatting inside paragraph text from other programs Paragraph text in a DWG or DXF can carry codes inside it: colors per word, bold and italic, underline, overline and strikethrough, stacked fractions such as 1/2, and a different height for a word. CadCraft keeps those codes, draws them (on screen and in a plotted PDF) and saves them back unchanged. When you edit such a text (double-click, TEXTEDIT, the Text field of the right panel, or FIND with Replace), you edit its plain words. A word you type goes into the run next to it and takes its color and style; the other codes stay where they are. If you retype a stacked fraction's numbers, that fraction becomes plain text. Setting the whole text from the Text field keeps the codes only where it can tell which words stayed. Font names (\\F) are kept and saved, but everything is drawn in the one Helvetica-style font. There is no toolbar for typing codes yet. Point at something: multileaders Leader in the Annotate group (MLEADER, MLD) draws an arrow, a leader line, a short landing and a note, as one object. Click Leader. At Specify leader arrowhead location, click what the note is about. At Specify leader landing location, click where the note goes. The landing turns toward the side you clicked. Type the note in the box that opens, and press Ctrl+Enter. A and L at the first question set the arrowhead size and the landing length; the text height comes from the Text height for new dimensions, times Overall scale. Double-click a multileader to change its words. Grips move the arrowhead and the landing; the note follows the landing. In Properties: Text, Height, Wrap width, Arrowhead size and Landing distance. Multileaders save as real multileaders in DWG. DXF has no multileader in R12 or 2000, so there they are saved as the lines, arrowhead and text they draw. Mark changes with a revision cloud Annotate › More › Revision cloud (REVCLOUD, RC) draws a cloud of arcs round what changed. The history shows the arc length and the type it will use. Rectangular (the first time): click two corners. P Polygonal: click the corners, Enter closes the cloud. F Freehand: click where it starts and move the pointer round the area without clicking; an arc is added every arc length. Click or press Enter to close it. O Object: pick a closed polyline, circle or ellipse (or a line, arc or open polyline); it becomes the cloud. Reverse direction? Y turns the arcs inward. A Arc length: the length of each arc. Choose one that suits the plot scale. The cloud is a closed polyline of arcs, so it saves in every format. Find and replace words Edit › More › Find and replace (FIND) looks through text, the values of block attributes and dimension text on the open tab (or only in the selected objects). Type the words in Find. The list shows each place they are, and the count. Choose Match case, Whole words only or Use wildcards (* and ?) if you need them, and untick what not to look in. Click Select found to select those objects, or type the new words in Replace with (the list shows what each becomes) and click Replace all. One Ctrl+Z undoes every replacement. A dimension's measured value is not text, so it is never replaced: in \u003C\u003E TYP., only TYP. can be. Hide the lines behind a label Select the texts, type TEXTMASK and press Enter. B paints the drawing's background behind them; C asks for a colour number. O takes the mask away. Border offset factor: Enter keeps 1.5, half a text height round the text. The mask covers lines under the text, on screen and when you plot. DWG 2004 or later and DXF 2000 keep it; the 2000 DWG format and DXF R12 cannot, and the save says so. Font All text is drawn in a Helvetica-style font, on screen and in PDFs. Text from opened drawings keeps its words, height and position, but not its original font (the name of a font inside paragraph text is kept in the file, see above). In plotted PDFs, text in any European script, Cyrillic or Greek prints as itself (the PDF carries the letters it needs, and you can still select and search the text). Chinese, Japanese and Korean text prints as itself too (the plot fetches a larger font the first time it is needed). Characters no font has a letter for, such as Arabic, print as \"?\", and the history says how many there were. Common mistakes The text is too small to see. The height is in drawing units. In a large drawing, use a large height (see above), or zoom in. The command ended after one line. You pressed Enter on an empty line in TEXT. Start it again, or use paragraph text (T), where Enter starts a new paragraph. T opened a box instead of asking for a line. As in AutoCAD, T is paragraph text (MTEXT); single-line text is DT. The text wraps in a different place in AutoCAD. CadCraft wraps with Helvetica widths; another font can fit a word more or less on a line. Text in a mirrored copy. It is placed mirrored but stays readable left to right.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"hatch","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Hatch and fill areas","summary":"Fill an area with a solid color or a standard pattern by clicking inside it or by picking closed shapes, set the pattern's scale and angle, and change a hatch afterwards.","section":"tasks","order":85,"keywords":["hatch","bhatch","fill","solid fill","pattern","ansi31","ansi37","brick","earth","net","section lines","poché","island","boundary","scale","angle"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-make","text":"What you will make","level":2},{"id":"hatch-by-clicking-inside-an-area","text":"Hatch by clicking inside an area","level":2},{"id":"a-solid-fill","text":"A solid fill","level":2},{"id":"hatch-closed-shapes-you-pick","text":"Hatch closed shapes you pick","level":2},{"id":"patterns","text":"Patterns","level":2},{"id":"change-a-hatch","text":"Change a hatch","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-make\"\u003EWhat you will make\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA wall section filled with the ANSI31 pattern, a column in it left empty, and a solid fill in the column.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"hatch-by-clicking-inside-an-area\"\u003EHatch by clicking inside an area\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw a rectangle (\u003Ccode\u003EREC\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E0,0\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E@2000,1200\u003C/code\u003E) and a circle inside it (\u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1000,600\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E300\u003C/code\u003E). Click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExtents\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHatch\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraw\u003C/span\u003E group, or type \u003Ccode\u003EH\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The history says which pattern is current, for example \u003Ccode\u003ECurrent hatch: ANSI31, scale 1, angle 0°\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, type \u003Ccode\u003EANSI31\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003ESC\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, type \u003Ccode\u003E20\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Pattern sizes are on paper, so a drawing plotted at 1:20 wants scale 20.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003EPick internal point\u003C/code\u003E, click inside the rectangle, outside the circle. The hatch shows as a preview.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The rectangle is hatched; the circle inside stays empty, because it is an island.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick inside several areas before pressing \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to make them one hatch. \u003Ccode\u003EU\u003C/code\u003E takes back the last area.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-solid-fill\"\u003EA solid fill\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EH\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, type \u003Ccode\u003ESOLID\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick inside the circle, then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"hatch-closed-shapes-you-pick\"\u003EHatch closed shapes you pick\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EInstead of clicking inside, type \u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E (Select objects) at \u003Ccode\u003EPick internal point\u003C/code\u003E and pick closed polylines, circles or closed ellipses, then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Shapes inside others are left empty in turn (the outer one hatched, the next one in empty, and so on).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"patterns\"\u003EPatterns\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E?\u003C/code\u003E at the pattern question for the list. The standard ones are ANSI31 to ANSI38, LINE, NET, NET3, DASH, SQUARE, BRICK, DOTS and EARTH, and SOLID. In a drawing in inches (or with feet and inches as its length format) the inch sizes of the patterns are used, as in AutoCAD; otherwise the millimeter ones.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAngle\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E) turns the whole pattern: ANSI31 at 90° slopes the other way.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-a-hatch\"\u003EChange a hatch\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect it (click one of its lines). In \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPattern\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPattern scale\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPattern angle\u003C/span\u003E. Hatches from opened drawings show their pattern when it is one of the standard ones; others show as a light tone of their color.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;No closed boundary round that point.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E There is a gap in the outline, or part of it is off screen: CadCraft looks only at what is on screen. Close the gap (zoom in on the corners), or zoom out.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;The pattern is too dense to draw at this scale.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E The lines would be closer than the drawing can show. Use a larger scale: try the plot scale (20 for 1:20).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe pattern looks huge or tiny.\u003C/strong\u003E The scale is in drawing units per pattern unit; see step 4 above.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMoving the outline does not move the hatch.\u003C/strong\u003E A hatch is not tied to its boundary. Move both, or hatch again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will make A wall section filled with the ANSI31 pattern, a column in it left empty, and a solid fill in the column. Hatch by clicking inside an area Draw a rectangle (REC, 0,0, @2000,1200) and a circle inside it (C, 1000,600, 300). Click Extents. Click Hatch in the Draw group, or type H and press Enter. The history says which pattern is current, for example Current hatch: ANSI31, scale 1, angle 0°. Type P and press Enter, type ANSI31 and press Enter. Type SC and press Enter, type 20 and press Enter. Pattern sizes are on paper, so a drawing plotted at 1:20 wants scale 20. At Pick internal point, click inside the rectangle, outside the circle. The hatch shows as a preview. Press Enter. The rectangle is hatched; the circle inside stays empty, because it is an island. Click inside several areas before pressing Enter to make them one hatch. U takes back the last area. A solid fill Type H and press Enter. Type P, press Enter, type SOLID and press Enter. Click inside the circle, then press Enter. Hatch closed shapes you pick Instead of clicking inside, type S (Select objects) at Pick internal point and pick closed polylines, circles or closed ellipses, then press Enter. Shapes inside others are left empty in turn (the outer one hatched, the next one in empty, and so on). Patterns Type ? at the pattern question for the list. The standard ones are ANSI31 to ANSI38, LINE, NET, NET3, DASH, SQUARE, BRICK, DOTS and EARTH, and SOLID. In a drawing in inches (or with feet and inches as its length format) the inch sizes of the patterns are used, as in AutoCAD; otherwise the millimeter ones. Angle (A) turns the whole pattern: ANSI31 at 90° slopes the other way. Change a hatch Select it (click one of its lines). In Properties: Pattern, Pattern scale and Pattern angle. Hatches from opened drawings show their pattern when it is one of the standard ones; others show as a light tone of their color. Common mistakes \"No closed boundary round that point.\" There is a gap in the outline, or part of it is off screen: CadCraft looks only at what is on screen. Close the gap (zoom in on the corners), or zoom out. \"The pattern is too dense to draw at this scale.\" The lines would be closer than the drawing can show. Use a larger scale: try the plot scale (20 for 1:20). The pattern looks huge or tiny. The scale is in drawing units per pattern unit; see step 4 above. Moving the outline does not move the hatch. A hatch is not tied to its boundary. Move both, or hatch again.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"pictures","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Pictures in a drawing","summary":"Place a PNG or JPEG picture, clip it to a rectangle or a polygon, show the pictures a DWG uses, and plot them sharp.","section":"tasks","order":87,"keywords":["pastearea","parea","copy area","copied area","markupcraft","true size","real size","overlay","ole","ole2frame","embedded object","paste picture","paste screenshot","ctrl+v","clipboard image","screenshot","picture","image","imageattach","iat","image","imageclip","clip","png","jpeg","jpg","logo","photo","raster","imagedef","xref","plot","pdf","resolution"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-make","text":"What you will make","level":2},{"id":"place-a-picture","text":"Place a picture","level":2},{"id":"paste-a-screenshot-or-a-copied-picture","text":"Paste a screenshot or a copied picture","level":2},{"id":"paste-an-area-copied-in-markupcraft","text":"Paste an area copied in MarkupCraft","level":2},{"id":"show-the-pictures-a-dwg-uses","text":"Show the pictures a DWG uses","level":2},{"id":"clip-a-picture","text":"Clip a picture","level":2},{"id":"plot-pictures","text":"Plot pictures","level":2},{"id":"what-is-saved-where","text":"What is saved where","level":2},{"id":"objects-from-other-programs-ole","text":"Objects from other programs (OLE)","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-make\"\u003EWhat you will make\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA company logo placed in a title block, clipped to the part you want, and a PDF in which it prints at its own resolution.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"place-a-picture\"\u003EPlace a picture\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EIMAGEATTACH\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003EIAT\u003C/code\u003E) and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Pick a PNG or JPEG file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify the lower-left corner\u003C/code\u003E: click, or type a point.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify its width\u003C/code\u003E: the width in drawing units. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E keeps one unit per pixel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify its rotation angle\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for none.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe picture is one object: select it by its frame, and move, copy, rotate, scale or erase it like any block.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe picture file stays with the drawing while it is open. A saved DWG names the file, as other programs do; it does not hold the pixels. Keep the picture next to the drawing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"paste-a-screenshot-or-a-copied-picture\"\u003EPaste a screenshot or a copied picture\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECopy the picture (a screenshot with \u003Cspan class=\"hp\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:windows\" title=\"Windows\"\u003EWindows key+Shift+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:mac\" title=\"Mac\"\u003ECmd+Ctrl+Shift+4\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:linux\" title=\"Linux\"\u003Eyour screenshot tool\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/span\u003E, or Copy image in another program or a browser).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPoint at where it should go on the drawing and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe picture&#039;s lower-left corner lands at the pointer (or the middle of the view), 40 % as wide as what you see; move, scale and clip it like any picture. Its name is the drawing&#039;s with \u003Ccode\u003E-picture-1.png\u003C/code\u003E (then \u003Ccode\u003E-2\u003C/code\u003E, …), and the history says so. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste a picture\u003C/span\u003E does the same from the menu (the browser may ask first).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA DWG keeps only the name of a picture&#039;s file, so saving the drawing also saves the picture as its own file. Keep the two in the same folder: the drawing shows the picture from there. In the browser that is a second download, and the history says so. With nothing but CadCraft objects on the clipboard, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E pastes those, as before.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"paste-an-area-copied-in-markupcraft\"\u003EPaste an area copied in MarkupCraft\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy area\u003C/span\u003E in MarkupCraft copies part of a sheet as a picture that knows how big it is. CadCraft pastes it at that size.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn MarkupCraft, copy the area (a rectangle, a circle or a freeform shape; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy as overlay\u003C/span\u003E if you want to see your drawing through it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn CadCraft, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPASTEAREA\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003EPAREA\u003C/code\u003E), or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste a copied area\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify the lower-left corner of the copied area\u003C/code\u003E: click, or type a point. A frame of the area&#039;s size follows the pointer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow big it lands:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe sheet had a drawing scale:\u003C/strong\u003E at its real size, in the drawing&#039;s units (a 1:50 detail of a 2 m stair is 2 m long in a metre drawing, 78.74 in an inch drawing). Nothing more is asked.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe sheet had no scale:\u003C/strong\u003E CadCraft says its size on paper and asks for a scale: type \u003Ccode\u003E100\u003C/code\u003E for a 1:100 sheet, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for the paper size, or type \u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E, click two points on the pasted picture and type how far apart they really are (as \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESCANSCALE\u003C/code\u003E does). The lower-left corner stays where you put it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA drawing with no units takes millimeters, as a PDF underlay does. An overlay keeps its paper see-through and starts as faded as it was copied; outside a circle or a freeform shape is see-through too. The pasted area is a picture like any other: move, rotate, scale, clip or erase it, and the whole paste is one undo step. Saved as a DWG it is an IMAGE with its fade and transparency.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E pastes the area when the picture on the clipboard is the area&#039;s, or when the area was copied after anything you copied in CadCraft (and in the last 6 hours). Otherwise it pastes as before. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPASTEAREA\u003C/code\u003E pastes the last copied area at any time.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"show-the-pictures-a-dwg-uses\"\u003EShow the pictures a DWG uses\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA DWG stores only where its pictures were, so CadCraft shows each one as a frame with its file name.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EIMAGE\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003EIM\u003C/code\u003E). The command line lists each picture file and whether it shows.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick the files (several at once). A file whose name contains the one the drawing names also counts, because exports often add a project prefix.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"clip-a-picture\"\u003EClip a picture\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EIMAGECLIP\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003EICL\u003C/code\u003E) and select the picture.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E and click two corners, or \u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E and click the points of a polygon, then \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003ED\u003C/code\u003E removes the clip.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"plot-pictures\"\u003EPlot pictures\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPictures go into the PDF as pictures, at their own resolution and under the lines. In the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlot\u003C/span\u003E dialog, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPictures\u003C/span\u003E can cap them at 600, 300 or 150 dpi to make the file smaller. A JPEG goes in as it is.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-saved-where\"\u003EWhat is saved where\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFormat\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EPictures\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDWG (every version)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAs pictures (IMAGE), with their clip\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDXF\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAs their frames with the file name; the save says so\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"objects-from-other-programs-ole\"\u003EObjects from other programs (OLE)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA drawing can hold objects another program made, such as a picture from a paint program or a spreadsheet range (OLE objects). CadCraft doesn&#039;t run or edit them, and can&#039;t insert new ones. What it does:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIn a DXF\u003C/strong\u003E, an OLE object opens as a frame. If the object saved a picture of itself and that picture is a bitmap, the picture is drawn inside the frame; otherwise the frame carries a label with the object&#039;s type, such as &quot;Microsoft Excel Worksheet (OLE)&quot;. You can move, copy and erase the frame like a block. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESAVEAS\u003C/code\u003E to DXF 2000 writes it as an OLE object again, with its data unchanged.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIn a DWG\u003C/strong\u003E, the objects are kept and written back when you save as a DWG, byte for byte, but they are \u003Cstrong\u003Enot shown\u003C/strong\u003E: a DWG keeps an object&#039;s place inside its own data, and CadCraft doesn&#039;t read it yet. The history says how many were kept. Type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EOLELIST\u003C/code\u003E to see what they are (type, size, whether each saved a picture) and to put a copy of a picture where you choose: \u003Cstrong\u003EPlace\u003C/strong\u003E asks for the number, the lower left corner and the width, and draws a frame with the picture in it. That copy is a frame you can move or erase; the object itself stays in the drawing and is still written back when you save.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAn object that came from a DXF is saved in a DWG as its frame and label only; the history says so. Save as DXF 2000 to keep the object itself.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPictures that are metafiles (what many programs save) are not drawn: the frame and label show instead.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EYou see\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhy\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFix\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA frame with a file name\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECadCraft does not have the file\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EIMAGE\u003C/code\u003E, then pick the file\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;That is not a picture&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe object picked is not a picture\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPick the picture&#039;s frame\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E","text":"What you will make A company logo placed in a title block, clipped to the part you want, and a PDF in which it prints at its own resolution. Place a picture Type IMAGEATTACH (or IAT) and press Enter. Pick a PNG or JPEG file. Specify the lower-left corner: click, or type a point. Specify its width: the width in drawing units. Enter keeps one unit per pixel. Specify its rotation angle: Enter for none. The picture is one object: select it by its frame, and move, copy, rotate, scale or erase it like any block. The picture file stays with the drawing while it is open. A saved DWG names the file, as other programs do; it does not hold the pixels. Keep the picture next to the drawing. Paste a screenshot or a copied picture Copy the picture (a screenshot with Windows key+Shift+S / Cmd+Ctrl+Shift+4 / your screenshot tool, or Copy image in another program or a browser). Point at where it should go on the drawing and press Ctrl+V. The picture's lower-left corner lands at the pointer (or the middle of the view), 40 % as wide as what you see; move, scale and clip it like any picture. Its name is the drawing's with -picture-1.png (then -2, …), and the history says so. Edit › More › Paste a picture does the same from the menu (the browser may ask first). A DWG keeps only the name of a picture's file, so saving the drawing also saves the picture as its own file. Keep the two in the same folder: the drawing shows the picture from there. In the browser that is a second download, and the history says so. With nothing but CadCraft objects on the clipboard, Ctrl+V pastes those, as before. Paste an area copied in MarkupCraft Copy area in MarkupCraft copies part of a sheet as a picture that knows how big it is. CadCraft pastes it at that size. In MarkupCraft, copy the area (a rectangle, a circle or a freeform shape; Copy as overlay if you want to see your drawing through it). In CadCraft, press Ctrl+V, or type PASTEAREA (or PAREA), or choose Edit › More › Paste a copied area. Specify the lower-left corner of the copied area: click, or type a point. A frame of the area's size follows the pointer. How big it lands: The sheet had a drawing scale: at its real size, in the drawing's units (a 1:50 detail of a 2 m stair is 2 m long in a metre drawing, 78.74 in an inch drawing). Nothing more is asked. The sheet had no scale: CadCraft says its size on paper and asks for a scale: type 100 for a 1:100 sheet, press Enter for the paper size, or type P, click two points on the pasted picture and type how far apart they really are (as SCANSCALE does). The lower-left corner stays where you put it. A drawing with no units takes millimeters, as a PDF underlay does. An overlay keeps its paper see-through and starts as faded as it was copied; outside a circle or a freeform shape is see-through too. The pasted area is a picture like any other: move, rotate, scale, clip or erase it, and the whole paste is one undo step. Saved as a DWG it is an IMAGE with its fade and transparency. Ctrl+V pastes the area when the picture on the clipboard is the area's, or when the area was copied after anything you copied in CadCraft (and in the last 6 hours). Otherwise it pastes as before. PASTEAREA pastes the last copied area at any time. Show the pictures a DWG uses A DWG stores only where its pictures were, so CadCraft shows each one as a frame with its file name. Type IMAGE (or IM). The command line lists each picture file and whether it shows. Pick the files (several at once). A file whose name contains the one the drawing names also counts, because exports often add a project prefix. Clip a picture Type IMAGECLIP (or ICL) and select the picture. Choose R and click two corners, or P and click the points of a polygon, then Enter. D removes the clip. Plot pictures Pictures go into the PDF as pictures, at their own resolution and under the lines. In the Plot dialog, Pictures can cap them at 600, 300 or 150 dpi to make the file smaller. A JPEG goes in as it is. What is saved where Format Pictures DWG (every version) As pictures (IMAGE), with their clip DXF As their frames with the file name; the save says so Objects from other programs (OLE) A drawing can hold objects another program made, such as a picture from a paint program or a spreadsheet range (OLE objects). CadCraft doesn't run or edit them, and can't insert new ones. What it does: In a DXF, an OLE object opens as a frame. If the object saved a picture of itself and that picture is a bitmap, the picture is drawn inside the frame; otherwise the frame carries a label with the object's type, such as \"Microsoft Excel Worksheet (OLE)\". You can move, copy and erase the frame like a block. SAVEAS to DXF 2000 writes it as an OLE object again, with its data unchanged. In a DWG, the objects are kept and written back when you save as a DWG, byte for byte, but they are not shown: a DWG keeps an object's place inside its own data, and CadCraft doesn't read it yet. The history says how many were kept. Type OLELIST to see what they are (type, size, whether each saved a picture) and to put a copy of a picture where you choose: Place asks for the number, the lower left corner and the width, and draws a frame with the picture in it. That copy is a frame you can move or erase; the object itself stays in the drawing and is still written back when you save. An object that came from a DXF is saved in a DWG as its frame and label only; the history says so. Save as DXF 2000 to keep the object itself. Pictures that are metafiles (what many programs save) are not drawn: the frame and label show instead. Common mistakes You see Why Fix A frame with a file name CadCraft does not have the file IMAGE, then pick the file \"That is not a picture\" The object picked is not a picture Pick the picture's frame","lang":"en"},{"slug":"point-clouds","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Trace as-built lines over a point cloud","summary":"Attach a laser scan (LAS, LAZ, E57, PLY, PCD, PTX, PTS or XYZ) under the drawing, cut a plan slice through it, and draw walls, doors and columns with object snap on its points.","section":"tasks","order":88,"keywords":["point cloud","pointcloudattach","pcattach","pointcloud","pc","laser scan","scan","lidar","las","laz","e57","ply","pcd","ptx","xyz","pts","as-built","as built","survey","slice","plan cut","node","snap","trace","existing conditions"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-make","text":"What you will make","level":2},{"id":"attach-the-cloud","text":"Attach the cloud","level":2},{"id":"cut-a-plan-slice-and-trace","text":"Cut a plan slice and trace","level":2},{"id":"more-options","text":"More options","level":2},{"id":"several-scans","text":"Several scans","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-make\"\u003EWhat you will make\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn as-built floor plan: the walls of a scanned building drawn as lines and polylines exactly on the scan&#039;s points.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"attach-the-cloud\"\u003EAttach the cloud\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet the drawing&#039;s units first if it has none (the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnits\u003C/span\u003E box in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E, or \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EUNITS\u003C/code\u003E): CadCraft converts the cloud from its own units to the drawing&#039;s.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPOINTCLOUDATTACH\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003EPCATTACH\u003C/code\u003E) and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Pick a \u003Ccode\u003E.las\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.laz\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.e57\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.ply\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.pcd\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.ptx\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.pts\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E.xyz\u003C/code\u003E file (the topic on point cloud formats says what each one brings, and what to export from a scanner&#039;s own software). You can also open the file with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen\u003C/span\u003E, drop it on the page, or send it from the BIM viewer&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETrace in CadCraft\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ELower-left corner of the cloud\u003C/code\u003E: press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to keep the file&#039;s own coordinates (a scan registered to the project lines up with the drawing), or click where its lower-left corner goes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe command line says how many points show. The view zooms to the cloud; it lies under the linework, every point seen from above.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"cut-a-plan-slice-and-trace\"\u003ECut a plan slice and trace\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPOINTCLOUD\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003EPC\u003C/code\u003E), then \u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E for \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESlice\u003C/span\u003E. Only a thin band 1.2 m above the cloud&#039;s lowest point shows: walls, door openings and columns read like a floor plan.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EH\u003C/code\u003E sets the height of the cut, \u003Ccode\u003ET\u003C/code\u003E its thickness (in drawing units). \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E ends the command.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETurn on object snap (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F3\"\u003EF3\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF3\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENode\u003C/span\u003E among the snaps. Draw with \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELINE\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPLINE\u003C/code\u003E: the cursor snaps to the slice&#039;s points, labelled &quot;Point cloud&quot;. The drawing&#039;s own endpoints, midpoints and centers win over the cloud, so closing a polyline lands on its first point.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChange the cut height to trace another level, then turn the slice off (\u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E again) to see the whole cloud.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"more-options\"\u003EMore options\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPOINTCLOUD\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E colors the points by the file&#039;s colors, height, intensity, LiDAR class or one color; \u003Ccode\u003ESI\u003C/code\u003E sets the point size; \u003Ccode\u003EV\u003C/code\u003E hides or shows the cloud; \u003Ccode\u003EZ\u003C/code\u003E zooms to it; \u003Ccode\u003ED\u003C/code\u003E detaches it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe 3D tab shows the cloud too, its lowest point on the ground, with the same slice.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe cloud is not part of the drawing: it is not saved in the DWG or DXF, undo does not change it, and it is not plotted. Attach it again when you open the drawing later. Files are read on this computer; nothing is uploaded. Very large scans are thinned evenly to 2 million points. E57 files open up to 6 million points and 512 MB for now: export larger scans as LAS or LAZ.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"several-scans\"\u003ESeveral scans\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EScans taken from different positions can be registered into one frame, refined with ICP and exported as one LAS or E57: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/register-scans\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/register-scans\"\u003ERegister several scans into one\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you will make An as-built floor plan: the walls of a scanned building drawn as lines and polylines exactly on the scan's points. Attach the cloud Set the drawing's units first if it has none (the Units box in Properties, or UNITS): CadCraft converts the cloud from its own units to the drawing's. Type POINTCLOUDATTACH (or PCATTACH) and press Enter. Pick a .las, .laz, .e57, .ply, .pcd, .ptx, .pts or .xyz file (the topic on point cloud formats says what each one brings, and what to export from a scanner's own software). You can also open the file with Open, drop it on the page, or send it from the BIM viewer's Trace in CadCraft. Lower-left corner of the cloud: press Enter to keep the file's own coordinates (a scan registered to the project lines up with the drawing), or click where its lower-left corner goes. The command line says how many points show. The view zooms to the cloud; it lies under the linework, every point seen from above. Cut a plan slice and trace Type POINTCLOUD (or PC), then S for Slice. Only a thin band 1.2 m above the cloud's lowest point shows: walls, door openings and columns read like a floor plan. H sets the height of the cut, T its thickness (in drawing units). Enter ends the command. Turn on object snap (F3) with Node among the snaps. Draw with LINE or PLINE: the cursor snaps to the slice's points, labelled \"Point cloud\". The drawing's own endpoints, midpoints and centers win over the cloud, so closing a polyline lands on its first point. Change the cut height to trace another level, then turn the slice off (S again) to see the whole cloud. More options In POINTCLOUD: C colors the points by the file's colors, height, intensity, LiDAR class or one color; SI sets the point size; V hides or shows the cloud; Z zooms to it; D detaches it. The 3D tab shows the cloud too, its lowest point on the ground, with the same slice. The cloud is not part of the drawing: it is not saved in the DWG or DXF, undo does not change it, and it is not plotted. Attach it again when you open the drawing later. Files are read on this computer; nothing is uploaded. Very large scans are thinned evenly to 2 million points. E57 files open up to 6 million points and 512 MB for now: export larger scans as LAS or LAZ. Several scans Scans taken from different positions can be registered into one frame, refined with ICP and exported as one LAS or E57: see Register several scans into one.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"map-layers","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Map layers under the drawing","summary":"Show street maps, aerial pictures or a picture with a world file under the model space, placed by the drawing's coordinate system.","section":"tasks","order":89,"keywords":["map","map layers","maplayers","ml","geomap","basemap","aerial","satellite","tiles","xyz","wms","wmts","map service","world file","coordinate system","epsg","state plane","utm","georeference","site plan","geolocation","geographic location","geodata","saved","dwg","dxf"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-make","text":"What you will make","level":2},{"id":"tell-cadcraft-where-the-drawing-is","text":"Tell CadCraft where the drawing is","level":2},{"id":"add-a-layer","text":"Add a layer","level":2},{"id":"saved-with-the-drawing","text":"Saved with the drawing","level":2},{"id":"drawings-from-other-programs","text":"Drawings from other programs","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-make\"\u003EWhat you will make\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA site plan drawn over an aerial picture or a street map, each in the right place.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"tell-cadcraft-where-the-drawing-is\"\u003ETell CadCraft where the drawing is\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet the drawing&#039;s units in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnits\u003C/span\u003E (for example feet or meters).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMAPLAYERS\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003EML\u003C/code\u003E) and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWhere the drawing is\u003C/span\u003E, type a name or an EPSG code in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFind a system\u003C/span\u003E (for example \u003Ccode\u003E2277\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003ETexas Central\u003C/code\u003E) and choose it in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESystem\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf the drawing is drawn in that system&#039;s own coordinates, leave the placement at 0. If not, give one drawing point (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrawing X\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrawing Y\u003C/span\u003E), where that point is on the map grid (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEasting\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENorthing\u003C/span\u003E), and how far the drawing is turned from east (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETurned (°)\u003C/span\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUse this system\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-a-layer\"\u003EAdd a layer\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETurn on \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMap layers from the internet\u003C/span\u003E at the top of the dialog. Until then nothing is asked of any server; when it is on, the servers you add see the area on screen and your internet address, never your drawing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a layer\u003C/span\u003E, choose what it comes from:\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETiles (XYZ)\u003C/strong\u003E: a tile address with \u003Ccode\u003E{z}\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E{x}\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E{y}\u003C/code\u003E, a name and the credit its provider asks for.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWMS\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EWMTS\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003EArcGIS\u003C/strong\u003E: the service&#039;s address, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGet layers\u003C/span\u003E and choose one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA picture\u003C/strong\u003E: a PNG or JPEG with its world file (and its \u003Ccode\u003E.prj\u003C/code\u003E when there is one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe layer shows under the drawing. Its credit shows at the bottom right of the drawing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach layer in the list can be hidden, made more or less see-through, moved up or down, or removed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach change to the system or the layers is one step of \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EUNDO\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"saved-with-the-drawing\"\u003ESaved with the drawing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe coordinate system and the list of layers are saved in the drawing when you save it as DWG or DXF, and come back when you open it again, on any computer. Pictures from files are not saved: add them again after opening. The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMap layers from the internet\u003C/span\u003E switch is not saved in the drawing: it belongs to this browser, so a drawing someone sends you asks no server for anything until you turn it on yourself.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat other programs see:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESaved as\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EThe drawing&#039;s location\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EThe map layers\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDWG 2010, 2013 or 2018, DXF 2000\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWritten the standard way programs read a drawing&#039;s geographic location: the system&#039;s EPSG code, one drawing point and where it is on the map grid, the units and north.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECadCraft only\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDWG 2000, 2004 or 2007, DXF R12\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECadCraft only (CadCraft says so when you save)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECadCraft only\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EChecked:\u003C/strong\u003E the ODA File Converter reads the location with no errors, and a DXF library (ezdxf) reads the EPSG code and the placement exactly.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENot checked yet:\u003C/strong\u003E whether other drafting programs recognise the system from its EPSG code. A program that keeps its own list of systems under other names may show the drawing with no location.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOther programs show a block named \u003Ccode\u003ECADCRAFT_GEO\u003C/code\u003E in the block list. It is CadCraft&#039;s own copy of the system and the layers; it is never placed in the drawing. Leave it there.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"drawings-from-other-programs\"\u003EDrawings from other programs\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen a drawing from another program has a geographic location, CadCraft reads it:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf it names an EPSG code, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWhere the drawing is\u003C/span\u003E shows that system and the map layers are placed by it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf CadCraft does not know the system (another program&#039;s own name for it, a local grid, or a scale factor), the location is not used, but it is kept and saved back as it was, so the other program still finds it. Choose a system in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWhere the drawing is\u003C/span\u003E to replace it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMap layers are drawn on screen only: they do not plot. Use only map services whose terms allow it, and keep their credit visible.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"What you will make A site plan drawn over an aerial picture or a street map, each in the right place. Tell CadCraft where the drawing is Set the drawing's units in Properties › Units (for example feet or meters). Type MAPLAYERS (or ML) and press Enter. Under Where the drawing is, type a name or an EPSG code in Find a system (for example 2277 or Texas Central) and choose it in System. If the drawing is drawn in that system's own coordinates, leave the placement at 0. If not, give one drawing point (Drawing X, Drawing Y), where that point is on the map grid (Easting, Northing), and how far the drawing is turned from east (Turned (°)). Click Use this system. Add a layer Turn on Map layers from the internet at the top of the dialog. Until then nothing is asked of any server; when it is on, the servers you add see the area on screen and your internet address, never your drawing. In Add a layer, choose what it comes from: Tiles (XYZ): a tile address with {z}, {x} and {y}, a name and the credit its provider asks for. WMS, WMTS or ArcGIS: the service's address, then Get layers and choose one. A picture: a PNG or JPEG with its world file (and its .prj when there is one). The layer shows under the drawing. Its credit shows at the bottom right of the drawing. Each layer in the list can be hidden, made more or less see-through, moved up or down, or removed. Each change to the system or the layers is one step of UNDO. Saved with the drawing The coordinate system and the list of layers are saved in the drawing when you save it as DWG or DXF, and come back when you open it again, on any computer. Pictures from files are not saved: add them again after opening. The Map layers from the internet switch is not saved in the drawing: it belongs to this browser, so a drawing someone sends you asks no server for anything until you turn it on yourself. What other programs see: Saved as The drawing's location The map layers DWG 2010, 2013 or 2018, DXF 2000 Written the standard way programs read a drawing's geographic location: the system's EPSG code, one drawing point and where it is on the map grid, the units and north. CadCraft only DWG 2000, 2004 or 2007, DXF R12 CadCraft only (CadCraft says so when you save) CadCraft only Checked: the ODA File Converter reads the location with no errors, and a DXF library (ezdxf) reads the EPSG code and the placement exactly. Not checked yet: whether other drafting programs recognise the system from its EPSG code. A program that keeps its own list of systems under other names may show the drawing with no location. Other programs show a block named CADCRAFT_GEO in the block list. It is CadCraft's own copy of the system and the layers; it is never placed in the drawing. Leave it there. Drawings from other programs When a drawing from another program has a geographic location, CadCraft reads it: If it names an EPSG code, Where the drawing is shows that system and the map layers are placed by it. If CadCraft does not know the system (another program's own name for it, a local grid, or a scale factor), the location is not used, but it is kept and saved back as it was, so the other program still finds it. Choose a system in Where the drawing is to replace it. Map layers are drawn on screen only: they do not plot. Use only map services whose terms allow it, and keep their credit visible.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"dimensions","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Dimension a plan","summary":"Add linear, aligned, angular, radius and diameter dimensions, chain them with Continue and Baseline, and override the text.","section":"tasks","order":90,"keywords":["dimension","dim","dimlinear","dli","dimaligned","dimangular","dimradius","dimdiameter","dimcontinue","dimbaseline","chain","string","annotate","dimension tool","where","find","toolbar","measure","mm","cm","m","km","units"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-make","text":"What you will make","level":2},{"id":"where-the-dimension-tools-are","text":"Where the dimension tools are","level":2},{"id":"draw-something-to-dimension","text":"Draw something to dimension","level":2},{"id":"linear-horizontal-or-vertical","text":"Linear: horizontal or vertical","level":2},{"id":"continue-a-string-of-dimensions","text":"Continue: a string of dimensions","level":2},{"id":"baseline-dimensions-stacked-from-one-line","text":"Baseline: dimensions stacked from one line","level":2},{"id":"aligned-true-length-of-a-sloping-line","text":"Aligned: true length of a sloping line","level":2},{"id":"radius-and-diameter","text":"Radius and diameter","level":2},{"id":"angular","text":"Angular","level":2},{"id":"change-the-text","text":"Change the text","level":2},{"id":"dimensions-follow-the-geometry","text":"Dimensions follow the geometry","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-make\"\u003EWhat you will make\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA dimensioned room: overall width and height, a string of dimensions along one wall, an angled wall, a curved corner and an angle.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBefore you start, read \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/dimension-settings\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/dimension-settings\"\u003ESet dimension sizes\u003C/a\u003E if your drawing is large: dimension text is 2.5 drawing units tall by default, which is tiny in a drawing measured in millimeters. For the steps below, type \u003Ccode\u003EDIMSCALE\u003C/code\u003E, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, type \u003Ccode\u003E50\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"where-the-dimension-tools-are\"\u003EWhere the dimension tools are\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith the CadCraft menu style (the one CadCraft starts with), the dimension tools are in the toolbar above the drawing, in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAnnotate\u003C/span\u003E group: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinear\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAligned\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAngle\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERadius\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDiameter\u003C/span\u003E. The group&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E menu has \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EContinue dimension\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBaseline dimension\u003C/span\u003E. Hold the pointer over a button to see what it does.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrafting classic\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERibbon\u003C/span\u003E menu style, open the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAnnotate\u003C/span\u003E tab: the tools are in its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDimensions\u003C/span\u003E panel. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrafting classic\u003C/span\u003E also has \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinear\u003C/span\u003E on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHome\u003C/span\u003E tab, under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAnnotation\u003C/span\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/menus-keys-and-mouse\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/menus-keys-and-mouse\"\u003EMenus, keys and mouse\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOr type the command at the command line under the drawing and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003EDLI\u003C/code\u003E (linear), \u003Ccode\u003EDAL\u003C/code\u003E (aligned), \u003Ccode\u003EDAN\u003C/code\u003E (angular), \u003Ccode\u003EDRA\u003C/code\u003E (radius), \u003Ccode\u003EDDI\u003C/code\u003E (diameter), \u003Ccode\u003EDCO\u003C/code\u003E (continue), \u003Ccode\u003EDBA\u003C/code\u003E (baseline).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn a narrow screen the toolbar is one row: swipe it sideways to reach \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAnnotate\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo show the lengths in millimetres, centimetres, metres or kilometres, see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/dimension-settings#dimensions-in-mm-cm-m-or-km\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/dimension-settings\" data-hash=\"dimensions-in-mm-cm-m-or-km\"\u003EDimensions in mm, cm, m or km\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-something-to-dimension\"\u003EDraw something to dimension\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EPL\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E0,0\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E@6000,0\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E@1500&lt;60\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E@0,2700\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E@-6750,0\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The outline closes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to start a circle.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick inside the room, away from the walls, for the center.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E600\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. This is a column.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExtents\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"linear-horizontal-or-vertical\"\u003ELinear: horizontal or vertical\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EDLI\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify first extension line origin\u003C/code\u003E, snap to the lower-left corner (Endpoint) and click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify second extension line origin\u003C/code\u003E, snap to the lower-right corner and click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the crosshair below the room. The dimension follows, horizontal.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick to place the dimension line.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe history says \u003Ccode\u003EDimension text = 6000\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhere you place the line decides the direction: below or above gives a horizontal dimension, left or right gives a vertical one. To force it, type \u003Ccode\u003EH\u003C/code\u003E (horizontal), \u003Ccode\u003EV\u003C/code\u003E (vertical) or \u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E (rotated, then an angle) before you place it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E at the first prompt instead of picking a point, then click a line, arc, circle or polyline segment. CadCraft takes both ends for you.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"continue-a-string-of-dimensions\"\u003EContinue: a string of dimensions\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMCONTINUE\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAnnotate\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EContinue dimension\u003C/span\u003E, or \u003Ccode\u003EDCO\u003C/code\u003E) carries on from the last linear or aligned dimension you made, each new one starting where the last one ended.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EDLI\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E0,0\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for the first origin.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E2000,0\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for the second origin.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick below the room to place it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EDCO\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E4500,0\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. A second dimension follows on from the first, on the same line.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESnap to the lower-right corner and click. A third one completes the string.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to end the command.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHere the points are typed as absolute coordinates on purpose. An \u003Ccode\u003E@\u003C/code\u003E point counts from the last point you gave, and the last point is where you placed the previous dimension line, not the end of the dimension. Absolute points, snaps or a bare distance with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrtho\u003C/span\u003E on are safer in a dimension string.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EU\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to take back the last dimension in the string without leaving the command.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"baseline-dimensions-stacked-from-one-line\"\u003EBaseline: dimensions stacked from one line\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMBASELINE\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAnnotate\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBaseline dimension\u003C/span\u003E, or \u003Ccode\u003EDBA\u003C/code\u003E) measures every new dimension from the first origin of the last one, and stacks each one a step further out.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EDLI\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESnap to the lower-left corner and click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESnap to the upper-left corner and click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the crosshair to the left of the room and click. A vertical dimension appears.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EDBA\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESnap to the upper end of the 60-degree wall (Endpoint) and click. A second vertical dimension appears, one step further out than the first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to end.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"aligned-true-length-of-a-sloping-line\"\u003EAligned: true length of a sloping line\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EDAL\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to select an object.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the 60-degree wall.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove outward and click to place.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe text reads 1500: the true length, measured along the wall.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"radius-and-diameter\"\u003ERadius and diameter\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EDRA\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the column circle.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the crosshair. The leader points from the center toward the crosshair.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick to place the text.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe text reads \u003Ccode\u003ER600\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMDIAMETER\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EDDI\u003C/code\u003E) does the same with a ⌀ sign and the diameter.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"angular\"\u003EAngular\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe walls here are one polyline, so measure the corner by its vertex. (Between two separate lines, you can click the lines instead.)\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EDAN\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003ESelect arc, circle or line, or Enter for a vertex\u003C/code\u003E, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify angle vertex\u003C/code\u003E, snap to the lower-right corner and click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify first angle endpoint\u003C/code\u003E, snap to the lower-left corner and click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify second angle endpoint\u003C/code\u003E, snap to the upper end of the 60-degree wall and click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the crosshair inside the room, near the corner, and click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe text reads \u003Ccode\u003E120°\u003C/code\u003E, the inside angle of the corner. Placed on the other side of the corner, the dimension measures the angle on that side instead. Click an arc at the first prompt to dimension the angle it spans, or click two lines to dimension the angle between them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-the-text\"\u003EChange the text\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo write your own text, type \u003Ccode\u003ET\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E before you place the dimension, then type the text. In your text, \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;&gt;\u003C/code\u003E stands for the measured value: \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;&gt; TYP.\u003C/code\u003E gives \u003Ccode\u003E6000 TYP.\u003C/code\u003E. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E on an empty line to keep the measured value.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo change an existing dimension:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the right panel, type in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EText\u003C/span\u003E. Leave it empty for the measured value.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"dimensions-follow-the-geometry\"\u003EDimensions follow the geometry\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA dimension remembers its points. Drag one of its grips, or move, rotate or scale it with the objects it measures, and it measures again. If you stretch the geometry without moving the dimension, the dimension does not follow: it keeps its own points.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe text and arrows are microscopic or huge.\u003C/strong\u003E Set the overall scale: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/dimension-settings\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/dimension-settings\"\u003ESet dimension sizes\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;Pick a point inside the angle you want to measure.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E The point you clicked for an angular dimension sits on one of the legs. Move it into the angle.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EContinue started from the wrong dimension.\u003C/strong\u003E It always continues from the last linear or aligned dimension made in this session. Make (or remake) the one to continue from first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe dimension came out vertical.\u003C/strong\u003E You placed it to the side. Type \u003Ccode\u003EH\u003C/code\u003E before placing it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFeet and inches.\u003C/strong\u003E Set the drawing&#039;s length format to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArchitectural\u003C/span\u003E first, or a dimension&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFormat\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/dimension-settings#feet-and-inches-in-dimensions\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/dimension-settings\" data-hash=\"feet-and-inches-in-dimensions\"\u003EFeet and inches in dimensions\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe numbers are not in the unit you want.\u003C/strong\u003E Choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnits in the text\u003C/span\u003E: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/dimension-settings#dimensions-in-mm-cm-m-or-km\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/dimension-settings\" data-hash=\"dimensions-in-mm-cm-m-or-km\"\u003EDimensions in mm, cm, m or km\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will make A dimensioned room: overall width and height, a string of dimensions along one wall, an angled wall, a curved corner and an angle. Before you start, read Set dimension sizes if your drawing is large: dimension text is 2.5 drawing units tall by default, which is tiny in a drawing measured in millimeters. For the steps below, type DIMSCALE, press Enter, type 50 and press Enter first. Where the dimension tools are With the CadCraft menu style (the one CadCraft starts with), the dimension tools are in the toolbar above the drawing, in the Annotate group: Linear, Aligned, Angle, Radius and Diameter. The group's More menu has Continue dimension and Baseline dimension. Hold the pointer over a button to see what it does. With the Drafting classic or Ribbon menu style, open the Annotate tab: the tools are in its Dimensions panel. Drafting classic also has Linear on the Home tab, under Annotation. See Menus, keys and mouse. Or type the command at the command line under the drawing and press Enter: DLI (linear), DAL (aligned), DAN (angular), DRA (radius), DDI (diameter), DCO (continue), DBA (baseline). On a narrow screen the toolbar is one row: swipe it sideways to reach Annotate. To show the lengths in millimetres, centimetres, metres or kilometres, see Dimensions in mm, cm, m or km. Draw something to dimension Type PL and press Enter. Type 0,0 and press Enter. Type @6000,0 and press Enter. Type @1500\u003C60 and press Enter. Type @0,2700 and press Enter. Type @-6750,0 and press Enter. Type C and press Enter. The outline closes. Type C and press Enter to start a circle. Click inside the room, away from the walls, for the center. Type 600 and press Enter. This is a column. Click Extents. Linear: horizontal or vertical Type DLI and press Enter. At Specify first extension line origin, snap to the lower-left corner (Endpoint) and click. At Specify second extension line origin, snap to the lower-right corner and click. Move the crosshair below the room. The dimension follows, horizontal. Click to place the dimension line. The history says Dimension text = 6000. Where you place the line decides the direction: below or above gives a horizontal dimension, left or right gives a vertical one. To force it, type H (horizontal), V (vertical) or R (rotated, then an angle) before you place it. Press Enter at the first prompt instead of picking a point, then click a line, arc, circle or polyline segment. CadCraft takes both ends for you. Continue: a string of dimensions DIMCONTINUE (Annotate › More › Continue dimension, or DCO) carries on from the last linear or aligned dimension you made, each new one starting where the last one ended. Type DLI and press Enter. Type 0,0 and press Enter for the first origin. Type 2000,0 and press Enter for the second origin. Click below the room to place it. Type DCO and press Enter. Type 4500,0 and press Enter. A second dimension follows on from the first, on the same line. Snap to the lower-right corner and click. A third one completes the string. Press Enter to end the command. Here the points are typed as absolute coordinates on purpose. An @ point counts from the last point you gave, and the last point is where you placed the previous dimension line, not the end of the dimension. Absolute points, snaps or a bare distance with Ortho on are safer in a dimension string. Type U and press Enter to take back the last dimension in the string without leaving the command. Baseline: dimensions stacked from one line DIMBASELINE (Annotate › More › Baseline dimension, or DBA) measures every new dimension from the first origin of the last one, and stacks each one a step further out. Type DLI and press Enter. Snap to the lower-left corner and click. Snap to the upper-left corner and click. Move the crosshair to the left of the room and click. A vertical dimension appears. Type DBA and press Enter. Snap to the upper end of the 60-degree wall (Endpoint) and click. A second vertical dimension appears, one step further out than the first. Press Enter to end. Aligned: true length of a sloping line Type DAL and press Enter. Press Enter to select an object. Click the 60-degree wall. Move outward and click to place. The text reads 1500: the true length, measured along the wall. Radius and diameter Type DRA and press Enter. Click the column circle. Move the crosshair. The leader points from the center toward the crosshair. Click to place the text. The text reads R600. DIMDIAMETER (DDI) does the same with a ⌀ sign and the diameter. Angular The walls here are one polyline, so measure the corner by its vertex. (Between two separate lines, you can click the lines instead.) Type DAN and press Enter. At Select arc, circle or line, or Enter for a vertex, press Enter. At Specify angle vertex, snap to the lower-right corner and click. At Specify first angle endpoint, snap to the lower-left corner and click. At Specify second angle endpoint, snap to the upper end of the 60-degree wall and click. Move the crosshair inside the room, near the corner, and click. The text reads 120°, the inside angle of the corner. Placed on the other side of the corner, the dimension measures the angle on that side instead. Click an arc at the first prompt to dimension the angle it spans, or click two lines to dimension the angle between them. Change the text To write your own text, type T and press Enter before you place the dimension, then type the text. In your text, \u003C\u003E stands for the measured value: \u003C\u003E TYP. gives 6000 TYP.. Press Enter on an empty line to keep the measured value. To change an existing dimension: Click it. In the right panel, type in Text. Leave it empty for the measured value. Press Enter. Dimensions follow the geometry A dimension remembers its points. Drag one of its grips, or move, rotate or scale it with the objects it measures, and it measures again. If you stretch the geometry without moving the dimension, the dimension does not follow: it keeps its own points. Common mistakes The text and arrows are microscopic or huge. Set the overall scale: see Set dimension sizes. \"Pick a point inside the angle you want to measure.\" The point you clicked for an angular dimension sits on one of the legs. Move it into the angle. Continue started from the wrong dimension. It always continues from the last linear or aligned dimension made in this session. Make (or remake) the one to continue from first. The dimension came out vertical. You placed it to the side. Type H before placing it. Feet and inches. Set the drawing's length format to Architectural first, or a dimension's Format in Properties: see Feet and inches in dimensions. The numbers are not in the unit you want. Choose Units in the text: see Dimensions in mm, cm, m or km.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"work-live","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Work on one drawing together with Live TeamWork","summary":"What changes in CadCraft while you are in a Live TeamWork session from CadCraft Studio: other people's cursors and selections, objects someone is editing, the host's locks, your own undo, and joining from a browser on the same network.","section":"tasks","order":92,"keywords":["live","live teamwork","studio live","together","collaborate","co-edit","session","share live","join","cursor","held","editing","lock","livelock","liveunlock","livehold","hold layer","hold area","padlock","undo","host","assistant","same file","many people","slow","limit","who changed","recent edits","edit log"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"everyone-draws-at-once","text":"Everyone draws at once","level":2},{"id":"other-people-on-your-drawing","text":"Other people on your drawing","level":2},{"id":"hold-a-layer-or-an-area-while-you-work-there","text":"Hold a layer or an area while you work there","level":2},{"id":"when-an-edit-is-taken-back","text":"When an edit is taken back","level":2},{"id":"undo-undoes-your-own-work","text":"Undo undoes your own work","level":2},{"id":"locks-the-host","text":"Locks (the host)","level":2},{"id":"join-with-the-same-file","text":"Join with the same file","level":2},{"id":"assistants-in-a-live-session","text":"Assistants in a live session","level":2},{"id":"join-from-a-browser","text":"Join from a browser","level":2},{"id":"many-people-on-one-drawing","text":"Many people on one drawing","level":2},{"id":"when-the-session-ends","text":"When the session ends","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat you see and what happens to your edits while several people work on one drawing at the same time. CadCraft Studio starts and joins the Live TeamWork session (Share live, Join); this page is about the drawing itself.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"everyone-draws-at-once\"\u003EEveryone draws at once\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDraw and edit as usual. Your own edits show at once, even before the others have them, and theirs appear on your screen a moment after they make them. Every command works: drawing, modifying, layers, blocks, sheets, dimensions, constraints.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETwo people can change the same object. When you change different things, such as the colour and an end point of one line, both changes are kept. When you change the same thing, the later change wins.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat stays yours alone: the current layer, colour and text height for new objects, your dimension settings, your view, split screen and UCS. The drawing is shared; how you look at it is not.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"other-people-on-your-drawing\"\u003EOther people on your drawing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECursors:\u003C/strong\u003E each person&#039;s pointer shows with their name in their own colour, and the command they are running. You see the cursors of people working near what is on your screen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESelections:\u003C/strong\u003E what someone has selected is outlined in their colour.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBeing edited:\u003C/strong\u003E an object someone has selected carries a label such as &quot;Ana is editing&quot;. You can still change its colour or move it, but you can&#039;t delete, explode or retype it until they let go. Selecting it tells you who has it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWho changed it last:\u003C/strong\u003E select one object that someone else changed in this session and the command line says who, and what the change was (&quot;Ben changed that last: CIRCLE.&quot;). Studio&#039;s Live panel lists every recent edit under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERecent edits\u003C/span\u003E. Nothing of this is saved in the drawing: it lasts as long as the session.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"hold-a-layer-or-an-area-while-you-work-there\"\u003EHold a layer or an area while you work there\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a big drawing with several people, say which part is yours for now:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003ELIVEHOLD\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E and type a layer name (Enter keeps the current layer), or choose \u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E and pick two corners of an area.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOther people&#039;s edits on that layer, or to objects in that area, are taken back and they are told you are working there. You see your area as a dashed frame; others see it in your colour with your name, and the layers you hold are listed at the top left of their drawing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ELIVEHOLD\u003C/code\u003E, then \u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E, releases everything you hold. Leaving the session releases it too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAnyone in the session can hold, the host included; the host&#039;s locks still apply on top. A hold is never saved in the file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-an-edit-is-taken-back\"\u003EWhen an edit is taken back\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe host&#039;s copy of the drawing checks every edit. A few can&#039;t go in, and CadCraft takes them back on your screen and says why on the command line, for example:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Esomeone deleted the object, or the layer or block it needs, just before you;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Esomeone is editing that object, or holds its layer or the area it is in;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe host locked it;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe block you place was purged, or a layer you delete still has objects on it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"undo-undoes-your-own-work\"\u003EUndo undoes your own work\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E undo your own last command, all of it, and leave everyone else&#039;s work alone. When someone changed the same thing since, nothing is undone and the command line says who changed it. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E redoes.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"locks-the-host\"\u003ELocks (the host)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe host can protect objects or a whole layer so that nobody changes them, the host included, until they are unlocked:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the objects, then type \u003Ccode\u003ELIVELOCK\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Or type \u003Ccode\u003ELIVELOCK\u003C/code\u003E with nothing selected and choose \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E to lock a layer by name: objects added to that layer later are locked too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELocked objects are drawn dashed with a &quot;Locked by the host&quot; label on everyone&#039;s screen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ELIVEUNLOCK\u003C/code\u003E unlocks them the same way.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELocks belong to the session: they end when it ends and are never saved in the file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"join-with-the-same-file\"\u003EJoin with the same file\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf you opened the very file the host opened (from a shared folder or the same copy), join from your own CadCraft window in Studio: open the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELive\u003C/span\u003E panel and use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJoin here\u003C/span\u003E with the host&#039;s code. Only the changes made since the host opened it travel, so a large drawing is ready at once. If your file is a different one, the host&#039;s drawing replaces it on your screen (your file stays as it is). This works only while your drawing is unchanged since you opened it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"assistants-in-a-live-session\"\u003EAssistants in a live session\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn assistant connected to your CadCraft window (see the help on working with AI assistants) joins the session as its own participant, shown to everyone as &quot;Assistant (via &lt;your name&gt;)&quot;. The host lets it in, as for a person; the host&#039;s own assistant joins at once. Its changes have their own undo: your \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes your work, and the assistant&#039;s undo undoes only its own. It leaves the session when you disconnect it or leave, and it can only work in the session while you can edit.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"join-from-a-browser\"\u003EJoin from a browser\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA teammate without Studio can scan the picture under the host&#039;s code with a phone, tablet or computer on the same network. CadCraft opens in the browser, asks for your name on the command line, and fills with the host&#039;s drawing once the host lets you in. Invite links, for joining from another network, are not open yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"many-people-on-one-drawing\"\u003EMany people on one drawing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery edit goes to everyone in the session, so the more people there are, the longer each change takes to reach each of them, and slower laptops and connections fall behind first. A session&#039;s usual size is 20 people; the host can allow more for one session (see the help on working together live). If the drawing feels slow with many people, fewer people, fewer of them editing at the same moment, or a better connection helps.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-the-session-ends\"\u003EWhen the session ends\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOnly the host saves the drawing. When the host ends the session, everyone else keeps an unsaved copy: save it if you want to keep it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESomething taken back is drawn on top of the objects around it on your own screen until you reopen the drawing. Hatches and filled objects that overlap may look different from the host&#039;s copy; the drawing itself is the same.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"What you will learn What you see and what happens to your edits while several people work on one drawing at the same time. CadCraft Studio starts and joins the Live TeamWork session (Share live, Join); this page is about the drawing itself. Everyone draws at once Draw and edit as usual. Your own edits show at once, even before the others have them, and theirs appear on your screen a moment after they make them. Every command works: drawing, modifying, layers, blocks, sheets, dimensions, constraints. Two people can change the same object. When you change different things, such as the colour and an end point of one line, both changes are kept. When you change the same thing, the later change wins. What stays yours alone: the current layer, colour and text height for new objects, your dimension settings, your view, split screen and UCS. The drawing is shared; how you look at it is not. Other people on your drawing Cursors: each person's pointer shows with their name in their own colour, and the command they are running. You see the cursors of people working near what is on your screen. Selections: what someone has selected is outlined in their colour. Being edited: an object someone has selected carries a label such as \"Ana is editing\". You can still change its colour or move it, but you can't delete, explode or retype it until they let go. Selecting it tells you who has it. Who changed it last: select one object that someone else changed in this session and the command line says who, and what the change was (\"Ben changed that last: CIRCLE.\"). Studio's Live panel lists every recent edit under Recent edits. Nothing of this is saved in the drawing: it lasts as long as the session. Hold a layer or an area while you work there On a big drawing with several people, say which part is yours for now: Type LIVEHOLD and press Enter. Choose L and type a layer name (Enter keeps the current layer), or choose A and pick two corners of an area. Other people's edits on that layer, or to objects in that area, are taken back and they are told you are working there. You see your area as a dashed frame; others see it in your colour with your name, and the layers you hold are listed at the top left of their drawing. LIVEHOLD, then R, releases everything you hold. Leaving the session releases it too. Anyone in the session can hold, the host included; the host's locks still apply on top. A hold is never saved in the file. When an edit is taken back The host's copy of the drawing checks every edit. A few can't go in, and CadCraft takes them back on your screen and says why on the command line, for example: someone deleted the object, or the layer or block it needs, just before you; someone is editing that object, or holds its layer or the area it is in; the host locked it; the block you place was purged, or a layer you delete still has objects on it. Undo undoes your own work Ctrl+Z and Undo undo your own last command, all of it, and leave everyone else's work alone. When someone changed the same thing since, nothing is undone and the command line says who changed it. Ctrl+Y redoes. Locks (the host) The host can protect objects or a whole layer so that nobody changes them, the host included, until they are unlocked: Select the objects, then type LIVELOCK and press Enter. Or type LIVELOCK with nothing selected and choose L to lock a layer by name: objects added to that layer later are locked too. Locked objects are drawn dashed with a \"Locked by the host\" label on everyone's screen. LIVEUNLOCK unlocks them the same way. Locks belong to the session: they end when it ends and are never saved in the file. Join with the same file If you opened the very file the host opened (from a shared folder or the same copy), join from your own CadCraft window in Studio: open the Live panel and use Join here with the host's code. Only the changes made since the host opened it travel, so a large drawing is ready at once. If your file is a different one, the host's drawing replaces it on your screen (your file stays as it is). This works only while your drawing is unchanged since you opened it. Assistants in a live session An assistant connected to your CadCraft window (see the help on working with AI assistants) joins the session as its own participant, shown to everyone as \"Assistant (via \u003Cyour name\u003E)\". The host lets it in, as for a person; the host's own assistant joins at once. Its changes have their own undo: your Ctrl+Z undoes your work, and the assistant's undo undoes only its own. It leaves the session when you disconnect it or leave, and it can only work in the session while you can edit. Join from a browser A teammate without Studio can scan the picture under the host's code with a phone, tablet or computer on the same network. CadCraft opens in the browser, asks for your name on the command line, and fills with the host's drawing once the host lets you in. Invite links, for joining from another network, are not open yet. Many people on one drawing Every edit goes to everyone in the session, so the more people there are, the longer each change takes to reach each of them, and slower laptops and connections fall behind first. A session's usual size is 20 people; the host can allow more for one session (see the help on working together live). If the drawing feels slow with many people, fewer people, fewer of them editing at the same moment, or a better connection helps. When the session ends Only the host saves the drawing. When the host ends the session, everyone else keeps an unsaved copy: save it if you want to keep it. Something taken back is drawn on top of the objects around it on your own screen until you reopen the drawing. Hatches and filled objects that overlap may look different from the host's copy; the drawing itself is the same.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"dimension-settings","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Set dimension sizes, decimals and scale","summary":"Choose text height, arrow size, decimals and overall scale for new dimensions, change existing ones, and understand how dimension styles work in CadCraft.","section":"tasks","order":100,"keywords":["dimension style","dimstyle","dimscale","dimtxt","dimasz","dimdec","dimgap","dimexe","dimexo","overall scale","decimals","arrow size","annotation scale","dimtextunit","units in the text","mm","cm","m","km","millimetres","metres","dimlfac","dimpost"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"how-dimension-sizes-work","text":"How dimension sizes work","level":2},{"id":"set-the-sizes-for-new-dimensions","text":"Set the sizes for new dimensions","level":2},{"id":"in-the-current-settings-panel","text":"In the Current settings panel","level":3},{"id":"at-the-command-line","text":"At the command line","level":3},{"id":"feet-and-inches-in-dimensions","text":"Feet and inches in dimensions","level":3},{"id":"dimensions-in-mm-cm-m-or-km","text":"Dimensions in mm, cm, m or km","level":2},{"id":"change-dimensions-you-already-made","text":"Change dimensions you already made","level":2},{"id":"dimensions-from-opened-drawings","text":"Dimensions from opened drawings","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2},{"id":"named-dimension-styles","text":"Named dimension styles","level":2},{"id":"change-dimension-text-and-extension-lines","text":"Change dimension text and extension lines","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow big dimension text and arrows are, how to set them for a drawing plotted at 1:50 or 1:100, and how to change dimensions you already made.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-dimension-sizes-work\"\u003EHow dimension sizes work\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery dimension carries its own set of sizes: text height, arrow size, decimals, the gap round the text, how far extension lines run past the dimension line and how far they start from the object. The defaults are close to the common ISO style:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESetting\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDefault\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECommand\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EText height\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2.5\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMTXT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArrow size\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2.5\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMASZ\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDecimals\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2 (trailing zeros dropped)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMDEC\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGap round the text\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0.625\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMGAP\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExtension past the dimension line\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E1.25\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMEXE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOffset from the object\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0.625\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMEXO\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOverall scale\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E1\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMSCALE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery size is multiplied by the \u003Cstrong\u003Eoverall scale\u003C/strong\u003E. Set the sizes as you want them on paper (2.5 mm text), and set the overall scale to the plot scale (50 for 1:50). The text is then 2.5 × 50 = 125 drawing units tall, which plots at 2.5 mm.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENamed dimension styles are managed with \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMSTYLE\u003C/code\u003E: see the section below. Each dimension also keeps the settings it was made with, so you can still change one dimension alone in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"set-the-sizes-for-new-dimensions\"\u003ESet the sizes for new dimensions\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEither way below changes only dimensions you make afterwards.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"in-the-current-settings-panel\"\u003EIn the Current settings panel\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E so nothing is selected. On the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab the right panel reads \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECurrent settings\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EScroll down the panel to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew dimensions\u003C/span\u003E, past the layer, colour, units and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayer 0\u003C/span\u003E rows (on a small screen it starts below the bottom edge). Click in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOverall scale\u003C/span\u003E, type \u003Ccode\u003E50\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EText height\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArrow size\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDecimals\u003C/span\u003E the same way if you want other values.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe status bar confirms each one, for example &quot;Overall scale for new dimensions: 50&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"at-the-command-line\"\u003EAt the command line\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EDIMSCALE\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003EEnter new value for DIMSCALE &lt;1&gt;\u003C/code\u003E, type \u003Ccode\u003E50\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe history confirms &quot;DIMSCALE = 50. New dimensions use it; select existing ones to change theirs.&quot; \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMTXT\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMASZ\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMDEC\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMGAP\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMEXE\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMEXO\u003C/code\u003E work the same way. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E at the prompt to keep the current value.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"feet-and-inches-in-dimensions\"\u003EFeet and inches in dimensions\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENew dimensions use the drawing&#039;s length format: set it with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength format\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECurrent settings\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EUNITS\u003C/code\u003E (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/units-papers-scales#feet-and-inches-and-fractions\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/units-papers-scales\" data-hash=\"feet-and-inches-and-fractions\"\u003EFeet and inches\u003C/a\u003E), and the next dimension reads \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;-6 1/2&quot;\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMLUNIT\u003C/code\u003E sets only the dimensions&#039; format (2 decimal, 3 engineering, 4 architectural, 5 fractional). For a dimension you already made, select it and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFormat\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E; with a fractional format, its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFraction\u003C/span\u003E box takes the power of two (4 = 1/16).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"dimensions-in-mm-cm-m-or-km\"\u003EDimensions in mm, cm, m or km\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA dimension measures in drawing units. It can write that length in millimetres, centimetres, metres or kilometres instead, with the unit after the number: a 1250 mm wall reads \u003Ccode\u003E1250 mm\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E125 cm\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1.25 m\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E0.00125 km\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet the drawing&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnits\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECurrent settings\u003C/span\u003E first (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/units-papers-scales#drawing-units\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/units-papers-scales\" data-hash=\"drawing-units\"\u003EUnits, paper sizes and scales\u003C/a\u003E), so CadCraft knows what one drawing unit is. A unitless drawing is read as millimetres, and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMTEXTUNIT\u003C/code\u003E says so.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFor new dimensions: press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E so nothing is selected, and under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew dimensions\u003C/span\u003E choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnits in the text\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrawing units\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Emm\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ecm\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Em\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003Ekm\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFor dimensions you already made: select them, and in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPrimary units\u003C/span\u003E choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnits in the text\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOr type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMTEXTUNIT\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EDTU\u003C/code\u003E) and answer \u003Ccode\u003ED\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EMM\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ECM\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EM\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003EKM\u003C/code\u003E: with dimensions selected it changes them (one undo step), with nothing selected it sets new dimensions.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDecimals\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMDEC\u003C/code\u003E) still sets how many decimal places show: with metres and 3 decimals, 1234 mm reads \u003Ccode\u003E1.234 m\u003C/code\u003E. The text is written as a decimal number. On a sheet, dimensions measure in the sheet&#039;s paper unit (millimetres or inches).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA saved DWG or DXF keeps the choice as the dimension&#039;s measurement scale (DIMLFAC) and a suffix (DIMPOST, \u003Ccode\u003E m\u003C/code\u003E), so other programs show the same text. If you change the drawing&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnits\u003C/span\u003E later, dimensions written in mm, cm, m or km are refitted, so they keep saying the right length. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESet by hand (scale or suffix)\u003C/span\u003E means the dimension&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMeasurement scale\u003C/span\u003E or suffix was set some other way; choose a unit to replace them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-dimensions-you-already-made\"\u003EChange dimensions you already made\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick one dimension. The right panel&#039;s heading reads \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDimension\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe settings are in sections; scroll down the panel to reach the later ones:\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDimension style\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeometry\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKind\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMeasurement\u003C/span\u003E (read only)\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EText\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EText override\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EText height\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELines and arrows\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArrow size\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOverall scale\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPrimary units\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDecimals\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType a new value and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe dimension redraws at once. For angular dimensions, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDecimals (°)\u003C/span\u003E sets the decimals of the angle.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith several dimensions selected, the panel shows the rows they share, and a value that differs between them reads \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EVaries\u003C/span\u003E. A value you type changes every selected dimension in one step, so one \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E puts them all back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"dimensions-from-opened-drawings\"\u003EDimensions from opened drawings\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA dimension from an opened DWG or DXF is shown exactly as the other program drew it. Its panel says &quot;Shown as the program that made it drew it. Change it and CadCraft draws it again in its own style.&quot; As soon as you change any value, move a grip, or move the dimension, CadCraft redraws it with its own arrowheads and font, using the sizes it read from the file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESome dimensions can only be read as drawn, not as editable dimensions; the panel then shows &quot;Drawn from&quot; and a block name, and the &quot;About this file&quot; box counts them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EChanging DIMSCALE did not change anything on screen.\u003C/strong\u003E The setting is for new dimensions. Select existing ones and change \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOverall scale\u003C/span\u003E in the panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDecimals show fewer digits than set.\u003C/strong\u003E Trailing zeros are dropped: 2 decimals show \u003Ccode\u003E1250\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E1250.5\u003C/code\u003E, not \u003Ccode\u003E1250.00\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EText is the right height on paper in a viewport but wrong in a model plot.\u003C/strong\u003E The overall scale must match the scale you plot at. Plotting at Fit to paper gives an odd scale; choose a standard scale in the plot dialog.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUnits such as mm or m in the text.\u003C/strong\u003E Choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnits in the text\u003C/span\u003E (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/dimension-settings#dimensions-in-mm-cm-m-or-km\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/dimension-settings\" data-hash=\"dimensions-in-mm-cm-m-or-km\"\u003EDimensions in mm, cm, m or km\u003C/a\u003E). For any other word after the number, type it in an override: \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;&gt; TYP.\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"named-dimension-styles\"\u003ENamed dimension styles\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EDIMSTYLE\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003ED\u003C/code\u003E) to open the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDimension style manager\u003C/span\u003E. It lists the drawing&#039;s styles, how many dimensions use each, and which one is current. \u003Cstrong\u003EStandard\u003C/strong\u003E is always there.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESet current\u003C/span\u003E: new dimensions use that style&#039;s settings and carry its name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew…\u003C/span\u003E: a new style that starts as a copy of one you choose.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModify…\u003C/span\u003E: the same fields as the dimension section of \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E (lines and arrows, text, fit, units, alternate units, tolerances). Press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOK\u003C/span\u003E and the style changes \u003Cstrong\u003Eand every dimension that uses it changes with it\u003C/strong\u003E, setting by setting. A setting you changed on one dimension alone stays as it is unless the style changed that setting.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERename…\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E: Standard can&#039;t be renamed or deleted, nor can the current style or one that dimensions use.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECompare…\u003C/span\u003E: what differs between two styles.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EApply to selected\u003C/span\u003E: select dimensions first; they take the style&#039;s settings and its name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E-DIMSTYLE\u003C/code\u003E does the same on the command line: \u003Ccode\u003ERestore\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESave\u003C/code\u003E (the current settings, including what you typed with \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMTXT\u003C/code\u003E and the others, under a name), \u003Ccode\u003EDelete\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EApply\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ECompare\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EreName\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EList\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe styles are saved in a DWG or DXF (its dimension style table) and read from the files you open. The \u003Cem\u003Ecurrent\u003C/em\u003E style is not saved: a drawing opens with Standard current. Standard keeps its own settings while another style is current.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-dimension-text-and-extension-lines\"\u003EChange dimension text and extension lines\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMEDIT\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EDED\u003C/code\u003E) works on the dimensions you select: \u003Cstrong\u003EHome\u003C/strong\u003E puts the text back where the style puts it, \u003Cstrong\u003ENew\u003C/strong\u003E types other text (\u003Ccode\u003E&lt;&gt;\u003C/code\u003E stands for the measurement), \u003Cstrong\u003ERotate\u003C/strong\u003E turns the text, \u003Cstrong\u003EOblique\u003C/strong\u003E slants the extension lines of linear and aligned dimensions to an angle you type (press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for none). \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMTEDIT\u003C/code\u003E moves the text to the \u003Cstrong\u003ELeft\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ERight\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003ECenter\u003C/strong\u003E of the dimension line, or turns it (\u003Cstrong\u003EAngle\u003C/strong\u003E), or sends it \u003Cstrong\u003EHome\u003C/strong\u003E. The text always sits on the dimension line: it can&#039;t be dragged to a free place.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you will learn How big dimension text and arrows are, how to set them for a drawing plotted at 1:50 or 1:100, and how to change dimensions you already made. How dimension sizes work Every dimension carries its own set of sizes: text height, arrow size, decimals, the gap round the text, how far extension lines run past the dimension line and how far they start from the object. The defaults are close to the common ISO style: Setting Default Command Text height 2.5 DIMTXT Arrow size 2.5 DIMASZ Decimals 2 (trailing zeros dropped) DIMDEC Gap round the text 0.625 DIMGAP Extension past the dimension line 1.25 DIMEXE Offset from the object 0.625 DIMEXO Overall scale 1 DIMSCALE Every size is multiplied by the overall scale. Set the sizes as you want them on paper (2.5 mm text), and set the overall scale to the plot scale (50 for 1:50). The text is then 2.5 × 50 = 125 drawing units tall, which plots at 2.5 mm. Named dimension styles are managed with DIMSTYLE: see the section below. Each dimension also keeps the settings it was made with, so you can still change one dimension alone in Properties. Set the sizes for new dimensions Either way below changes only dimensions you make afterwards. In the Current settings panel Press Esc so nothing is selected. On the Model tab the right panel reads Current settings. Scroll down the panel to New dimensions, past the layer, colour, units and Layer 0 rows (on a small screen it starts below the bottom edge). Click in Overall scale, type 50 and press Enter. Set Text height, Arrow size and Decimals the same way if you want other values. The status bar confirms each one, for example \"Overall scale for new dimensions: 50\". At the command line Type DIMSCALE and press Enter. At Enter new value for DIMSCALE \u003C1\u003E, type 50 and press Enter. The history confirms \"DIMSCALE = 50. New dimensions use it; select existing ones to change theirs.\" DIMTXT, DIMASZ, DIMDEC, DIMGAP, DIMEXE and DIMEXO work the same way. Press Enter at the prompt to keep the current value. Feet and inches in dimensions New dimensions use the drawing's length format: set it with Length format in Current settings or UNITS (see Feet and inches), and the next dimension reads 12'-6 1/2\". DIMLUNIT sets only the dimensions' format (2 decimal, 3 engineering, 4 architectural, 5 fractional). For a dimension you already made, select it and choose Format in Properties; with a fractional format, its Fraction box takes the power of two (4 = 1/16). Dimensions in mm, cm, m or km A dimension measures in drawing units. It can write that length in millimetres, centimetres, metres or kilometres instead, with the unit after the number: a 1250 mm wall reads 1250 mm, 125 cm, 1.25 m or 0.00125 km. Set the drawing's Units in Current settings first (see Units, paper sizes and scales), so CadCraft knows what one drawing unit is. A unitless drawing is read as millimetres, and DIMTEXTUNIT says so. For new dimensions: press Esc so nothing is selected, and under New dimensions choose Units in the text: Drawing units, mm, cm, m or km. For dimensions you already made: select them, and in Properties › Primary units choose Units in the text. Or type DIMTEXTUNIT (DTU) and answer D, MM, CM, M or KM: with dimensions selected it changes them (one undo step), with nothing selected it sets new dimensions. Decimals (DIMDEC) still sets how many decimal places show: with metres and 3 decimals, 1234 mm reads 1.234 m. The text is written as a decimal number. On a sheet, dimensions measure in the sheet's paper unit (millimetres or inches). A saved DWG or DXF keeps the choice as the dimension's measurement scale (DIMLFAC) and a suffix (DIMPOST, m), so other programs show the same text. If you change the drawing's Units later, dimensions written in mm, cm, m or km are refitted, so they keep saying the right length. Set by hand (scale or suffix) means the dimension's Measurement scale or suffix was set some other way; choose a unit to replace them. Change dimensions you already made Click one dimension. The right panel's heading reads Dimension. The settings are in sections; scroll down the panel to reach the later ones: General: Dimension style Geometry: Kind and Measurement (read only) Text: Text override and Text height Lines and arrows: Arrow size Fit: Overall scale Primary units: Decimals Type a new value and press Enter. The dimension redraws at once. For angular dimensions, Decimals (°) sets the decimals of the angle. With several dimensions selected, the panel shows the rows they share, and a value that differs between them reads Varies. A value you type changes every selected dimension in one step, so one Ctrl+Z puts them all back. Dimensions from opened drawings A dimension from an opened DWG or DXF is shown exactly as the other program drew it. Its panel says \"Shown as the program that made it drew it. Change it and CadCraft draws it again in its own style.\" As soon as you change any value, move a grip, or move the dimension, CadCraft redraws it with its own arrowheads and font, using the sizes it read from the file. Some dimensions can only be read as drawn, not as editable dimensions; the panel then shows \"Drawn from\" and a block name, and the \"About this file\" box counts them. Common mistakes Changing DIMSCALE did not change anything on screen. The setting is for new dimensions. Select existing ones and change Overall scale in the panel. Decimals show fewer digits than set. Trailing zeros are dropped: 2 decimals show 1250 and 1250.5, not 1250.00. Text is the right height on paper in a viewport but wrong in a model plot. The overall scale must match the scale you plot at. Plotting at Fit to paper gives an odd scale; choose a standard scale in the plot dialog. Units such as mm or m in the text. Choose Units in the text (see Dimensions in mm, cm, m or km). For any other word after the number, type it in an override: \u003C\u003E TYP.. Named dimension styles Type DIMSTYLE (D) to open the Dimension style manager. It lists the drawing's styles, how many dimensions use each, and which one is current. Standard is always there. Set current: new dimensions use that style's settings and carry its name. New…: a new style that starts as a copy of one you choose. Modify…: the same fields as the dimension section of Properties (lines and arrows, text, fit, units, alternate units, tolerances). Press OK and the style changes and every dimension that uses it changes with it, setting by setting. A setting you changed on one dimension alone stays as it is unless the style changed that setting. Rename… and Delete: Standard can't be renamed or deleted, nor can the current style or one that dimensions use. Compare…: what differs between two styles. Apply to selected: select dimensions first; they take the style's settings and its name. -DIMSTYLE does the same on the command line: Restore, Save (the current settings, including what you typed with DIMTXT and the others, under a name), Delete, Apply, Compare, reName, List. The styles are saved in a DWG or DXF (its dimension style table) and read from the files you open. The current style is not saved: a drawing opens with Standard current. Standard keeps its own settings while another style is current. Change dimension text and extension lines DIMEDIT (DED) works on the dimensions you select: Home puts the text back where the style puts it, New types other text (\u003C\u003E stands for the measurement), Rotate turns the text, Oblique slants the extension lines of linear and aligned dimensions to an angle you type (press Enter for none). DIMTEDIT moves the text to the Left, Right or Center of the dimension line, or turns it (Angle), or sends it Home. The text always sits on the dimension line: it can't be dragged to a free place.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"change-dimension-text-size","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Change dimension text size","summary":"Make dimension text bigger or smaller, for new dimensions and for the ones already in the drawing.","section":"tasks","order":101,"keywords":["dimension text size","text height","dimtxt","dimscale","dimension text too small","dimension text too big","bigger dimension text","annotation scale"],"headings":[{"id":"for-the-dimensions-you-make-next","text":"For the dimensions you make next","level":2},{"id":"for-dimensions-already-in-the-drawing","text":"For dimensions already in the drawing","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"for-the-dimensions-you-make-next\"\u003EFor the dimensions you make next\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E so nothing is selected: the right panel reads \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECurrent settings\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EScroll down the panel to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew dimensions\u003C/span\u003E. Type the height you want on paper in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EText height\u003C/span\u003E (for example \u003Ccode\u003E2.5\u003C/code\u003E for 2.5 mm, or \u003Ccode\u003E3/32&quot;\u003C/code\u003E) and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOverall scale\u003C/span\u003E to the plot scale (\u003Ccode\u003E50\u003C/code\u003E for 1:50, \u003Ccode\u003E48\u003C/code\u003E for 1/4&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;): every size is multiplied by it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn the command line, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMTXT\u003C/code\u003E sets the height and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMSCALE\u003C/code\u003E the overall scale.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"for-dimensions-already-in-the-drawing\"\u003EFor dimensions already in the drawing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the dimensions (one, or several of them: pick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDimension\u003C/span\u003E in the list at the top of Properties).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the new \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EText height\u003C/span\u003E (in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EText\u003C/span\u003E section), or a new \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOverall scale\u003C/span\u003E (in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit\u003C/span\u003E section further down), and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThey redraw at once, in one undo step. A dimension from another program&#039;s file is redrawn in CadCraft&#039;s style as soon as you change it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EText that is right on screen but tiny on the plotted sheet usually means the overall scale is 1: set it to the sheet&#039;s scale.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/dimension-settings\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/dimension-settings\"\u003ESet dimension sizes, decimals and scale\u003C/a\u003E for every setting.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"For the dimensions you make next Press Esc so nothing is selected: the right panel reads Current settings. Scroll down the panel to New dimensions. Type the height you want on paper in Text height (for example 2.5 for 2.5 mm, or 3/32\") and press Enter. Set Overall scale to the plot scale (50 for 1:50, 48 for 1/4\" = 1'-0\"): every size is multiplied by it. On the command line, DIMTXT sets the height and DIMSCALE the overall scale. For dimensions already in the drawing Select the dimensions (one, or several of them: pick Dimension in the list at the top of Properties). Type the new Text height (in the Text section), or a new Overall scale (in the Fit section further down), and press Enter. They redraw at once, in one undo step. A dimension from another program's file is redrawn in CadCraft's style as soon as you change it. Text that is right on screen but tiny on the plotted sheet usually means the overall scale is 1: set it to the sheet's scale. See Set dimension sizes, decimals and scale for every setting.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"blocks","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Make and insert blocks","summary":"Turn a drawn door into a block, place it again at other positions, scales and angles, and tidy up unused blocks.","section":"tasks","order":110,"keywords":["block","bmake","insert","ddinsert","symbol","purge","explode","redefine","base point","reference","count","bcount","schedule","csv","attributes","data extraction"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-make","text":"What you will make","level":2},{"id":"draw-the-door","text":"Draw the door","level":2},{"id":"make-the-block","text":"Make the block","level":2},{"id":"insert-the-block","text":"Insert the block","level":2},{"id":"insert-turned-or-scaled","text":"Insert turned or scaled","level":2},{"id":"change-a-placed-block","text":"Change a placed block","level":2},{"id":"redefine-a-block","text":"Redefine a block","level":2},{"id":"count-blocks-and-export-a-schedule","text":"Count blocks and export a schedule","level":2},{"id":"explode-and-purge","text":"Explode and purge","level":2},{"id":"block-names","text":"Block names","level":2},{"id":"write-a-block-or-objects-to-a-file-of-their-own","text":"Write a block or objects to a file of their own","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-make\"\u003EWhat you will make\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA door symbol made into a block called \u003Ccode\u003EDOOR-900\u003C/code\u003E, then placed three times: as drawn, turned 90 degrees, and mirrored.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA block is a named group of objects. You draw it once; every place you use it is a \u003Cstrong\u003Eblock reference\u003C/strong\u003E that points back to the one definition. The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBlocks\u003C/span\u003E section in the left panel lists the blocks in the drawing and how often each is used.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-the-door\"\u003EDraw the door\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E0,0\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E@0,900\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again. This is the door leaf.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to give the center first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E0,0\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for the center.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E900,0\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for the start point.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E0,900\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for the end point. This is the swing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExtents\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EArcs go counterclockwise from the start point to the end point.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"make-the-block\"\u003EMake the block\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EB\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake\u003C/span\u003E in the Blocks group.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003EEnter block name\u003C/code\u003E, type \u003Ccode\u003EDOOR-900\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify insertion base point\u003C/code\u003E, type \u003Ccode\u003E0,0\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. This is the hinge: the point that lands where you place the block.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects\u003C/code\u003E, drag a window round the leaf and the arc.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe history says \u003Ccode\u003EBlock &quot;DOOR-900&quot; made from 2 objects, and they were replaced by one reference to it.\u003C/code\u003E The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBlocks\u003C/span\u003E panel lists DOOR-900 with \u003Ccode\u003E1×\u003C/code\u003E beside it: one reference. Click the door: it now selects as one object, a &quot;Block reference&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou can select the objects first and then type \u003Ccode\u003EB\u003C/code\u003E. CadCraft still asks for the name and base point, then uses your selection.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"insert-the-block\"\u003EInsert the block\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EI\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInsert\u003C/span\u003E next to DOOR-900 in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBlocks\u003C/span\u003E panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003EEnter block name &lt;DOOR-900&gt;\u003C/code\u003E, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. (The panel&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInsert\u003C/span\u003E button fills in the name for you.)\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe door follows the crosshair. Click where the hinge should go.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA second door appears.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"insert-turned-or-scaled\"\u003EInsert turned or scaled\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EI\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to accept the name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify insertion point [Scale/Rotate]\u003C/code\u003E, type \u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E90\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The preview turns.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick to place it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E instead to set a scale factor; it applies to both directions. The scale and rotation are remembered for the next insert. To mirror a door, insert it and then use \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMIRROR\u003C/code\u003E on the reference.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-a-placed-block\"\u003EChange a placed block\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect a block reference. In the right panel you can change its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EY\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale X\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale Y\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERotation\u003C/span\u003E. A negative \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale X\u003C/span\u003E flips it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"redefine-a-block\"\u003ERedefine a block\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo change every door at once, make a block with the same name:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw the new version of the door.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EB\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, and type \u003Ccode\u003EDOOR-900\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003EBlock &quot;DOOR-900&quot; already exists. Redefine it?\u003C/code\u003E, type \u003Ccode\u003EY\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGive the base point and select the new objects, as before.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery reference to DOOR-900 now shows the new version.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOr edit the block itself: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBlocks\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit block\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EBEDIT\u003C/code\u003E) opens it on its own, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave block\u003C/span\u003E puts it back. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/attributes#change-a-block-the-block-editor\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/attributes\" data-hash=\"change-a-block-the-block-editor\"\u003EBlock attributes\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"count-blocks-and-export-a-schedule\"\u003ECount blocks and export a schedule\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECOUNT\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBlocks\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECount blocks\u003C/span\u003E), then:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eclick one door: every door in this space is selected, and the history says how many there are here and in the whole drawing;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eor press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: the history lists every block with its count, most first;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eor type \u003Ccode\u003EE\u003C/code\u003E, then \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E for a CSV of the counts (in the model, on sheets, in total) or \u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E for one row per reference with its layer, position, rotation and every attribute value — a schedule you can open in a spreadsheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnnamed blocks (dimensions, tables) and references inside other blocks are not counted. Cells that start like a formula (\u003Ccode\u003E=\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E+\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E-\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E@\u003C/code\u003E) get a leading \u003Ccode\u003E&#039;\u003C/code\u003E so a spreadsheet shows them as text.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"explode-and-purge\"\u003EExplode and purge\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXPLODE\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EX\u003C/code\u003E) turns a selected block reference back into separate objects. The definition stays.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPURGE\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EPU\u003C/code\u003E) removes block definitions that nothing uses any more. The history says how many it removed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"block-names\"\u003EBlock names\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA name can be up to 255 characters and can contain spaces, but it cannot start with an asterisk, and cannot contain \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E&gt;\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E/\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E\\\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E&quot;\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E:\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E;\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E?\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E*\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E|\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E=\u003C/code\u003E or a backtick. When you save DXF R12, names are written in capitals, other characters than letters, digits, \u003Ccode\u003E$\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E_\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E-\u003C/code\u003E become \u003Ccode\u003E_\u003C/code\u003E, and names are cut to 31 characters; the history says how many changed. Short names in capitals, digits and hyphens, like \u003Ccode\u003EDOOR-900\u003C/code\u003E, survive unchanged. A saved DWG keeps names as you typed them, upper and lower case; only characters DWG does not allow change (a \u003Ccode\u003E|\u003C/code\u003E becomes \u003Ccode\u003E$0$\u003C/code\u003E, others become \u003Ccode\u003E_\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"write-a-block-or-objects-to-a-file-of-their-own\"\u003EWrite a block or objects to a file of their own\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EW\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EWBLOCK\u003C/code\u003E) and choose \u003Cstrong\u003EObjects\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EBlock\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003EEntire drawing\u003C/strong\u003E. The result is a new DWG or DXF holding only the layers, styles and blocks it uses, with your base point as its origin. For objects you can keep them, turn them into a block, or delete them from this drawing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe block landed far from the crosshair.\u003C/strong\u003E The base point was not on the objects. Make the block again with the base point on a corner or a hinge.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;No objects selected; nothing made.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E You pressed \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E before selecting anything.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;This drawing has no blocks yet: F inserts one from a file, L from the library (or make one with BLOCK).&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E Type \u003Ccode\u003EF\u003C/code\u003E to bring a block in from another drawing, or \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E for the library. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/insert-from-file-and-library\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/insert-from-file-and-library\"\u003EInsert from a file or the library\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;A viewport cannot go in a block.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E Leave viewport frames out of the selection.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will make A door symbol made into a block called DOOR-900, then placed three times: as drawn, turned 90 degrees, and mirrored. A block is a named group of objects. You draw it once; every place you use it is a block reference that points back to the one definition. The Blocks section in the left panel lists the blocks in the drawing and how often each is used. Draw the door Type L and press Enter. Type 0,0 and press Enter. Type @0,900 and press Enter, then press Enter again. This is the door leaf. Type A and press Enter. Type C and press Enter to give the center first. Type 0,0 and press Enter for the center. Type 900,0 and press Enter for the start point. Type 0,900 and press Enter for the end point. This is the swing. Click Extents. Arcs go counterclockwise from the start point to the end point. Make the block Type B and press Enter, or click Make in the Blocks group. At Enter block name, type DOOR-900 and press Enter. At Specify insertion base point, type 0,0 and press Enter. This is the hinge: the point that lands where you place the block. At Select objects, drag a window round the leaf and the arc. Press Enter. The history says Block \"DOOR-900\" made from 2 objects, and they were replaced by one reference to it. The Blocks panel lists DOOR-900 with 1× beside it: one reference. Click the door: it now selects as one object, a \"Block reference\". You can select the objects first and then type B. CadCraft still asks for the name and base point, then uses your selection. Insert the block Type I and press Enter, or click Insert next to DOOR-900 in the Blocks panel. At Enter block name \u003CDOOR-900\u003E, press Enter. (The panel's Insert button fills in the name for you.) The door follows the crosshair. Click where the hinge should go. A second door appears. Insert turned or scaled Type I and press Enter, then press Enter to accept the name. At Specify insertion point [Scale/Rotate], type R and press Enter. Type 90 and press Enter. The preview turns. Click to place it. Type S instead to set a scale factor; it applies to both directions. The scale and rotation are remembered for the next insert. To mirror a door, insert it and then use MIRROR on the reference. Change a placed block Select a block reference. In the right panel you can change its X, Y, Scale X, Scale Y and Rotation. A negative Scale X flips it. Redefine a block To change every door at once, make a block with the same name: Draw the new version of the door. Type B and press Enter, and type DOOR-900. At Block \"DOOR-900\" already exists. Redefine it?, type Y and press Enter. Give the base point and select the new objects, as before. Every reference to DOOR-900 now shows the new version. Or edit the block itself: Blocks › More › Edit block (BEDIT) opens it on its own, and Save block puts it back. See Block attributes. Count blocks and export a schedule Type COUNT (or Blocks › More › Count blocks), then: click one door: every door in this space is selected, and the history says how many there are here and in the whole drawing; or press Enter: the history lists every block with its count, most first; or type E, then C for a CSV of the counts (in the model, on sheets, in total) or A for one row per reference with its layer, position, rotation and every attribute value — a schedule you can open in a spreadsheet. Unnamed blocks (dimensions, tables) and references inside other blocks are not counted. Cells that start like a formula (=, +, -, @) get a leading ' so a spreadsheet shows them as text. Explode and purge EXPLODE (X) turns a selected block reference back into separate objects. The definition stays. PURGE (PU) removes block definitions that nothing uses any more. The history says how many it removed. Block names A name can be up to 255 characters and can contain spaces, but it cannot start with an asterisk, and cannot contain \u003C \u003E / \\ \" : ; ? * | = or a backtick. When you save DXF R12, names are written in capitals, other characters than letters, digits, $, _ and - become _, and names are cut to 31 characters; the history says how many changed. Short names in capitals, digits and hyphens, like DOOR-900, survive unchanged. A saved DWG keeps names as you typed them, upper and lower case; only characters DWG does not allow change (a | becomes $0$, others become _). Write a block or objects to a file of their own Type W (WBLOCK) and choose Objects, Block or Entire drawing. The result is a new DWG or DXF holding only the layers, styles and blocks it uses, with your base point as its origin. For objects you can keep them, turn them into a block, or delete them from this drawing. Common mistakes The block landed far from the crosshair. The base point was not on the objects. Make the block again with the base point on a corner or a hinge. \"No objects selected; nothing made.\" You pressed Enter before selecting anything. \"This drawing has no blocks yet: F inserts one from a file, L from the library (or make one with BLOCK).\" Type F to bring a block in from another drawing, or L for the library. See Insert from a file or the library. \"A viewport cannot go in a block.\" Leave viewport frames out of the selection.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"attributes","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Block attributes","summary":"See and change the values a block reference carries, such as an item number or a maker, and keep them when you save a copy.","section":"tasks","order":115,"keywords":["eattedit","attedit","ddatte","battman","attsync","refedit","refclose","title block","edit in place","attribute editor","attdef","define attribute","bedit","block editor","bclose","attribute","attributes","attdef","attrib","tag","item number","value","invisible","constant","block","properties","list","equipment tag"],"headings":[{"id":"what-attributes-are","text":"What attributes are","level":2},{"id":"where-attributes-come-from","text":"Where attributes come from","level":2},{"id":"define-attributes-for-your-own-blocks","text":"Define attributes for your own blocks","level":2},{"id":"change-a-block-the-block-editor","text":"Change a block: the block editor","level":2},{"id":"edit-a-title-block-where-it-is-refedit","text":"Edit a title block where it is (REFEDIT)","level":2},{"id":"fill-them-in-when-you-place-a-block","text":"Fill them in when you place a block","level":2},{"id":"see-and-change-the-values","text":"See and change the values","level":2},{"id":"change-the-attribute-definitions-battman-attsync","text":"Change the attribute definitions (BATTMAN, ATTSYNC)","level":2},{"id":"list-them","text":"List them","level":2},{"id":"moving-turning-and-exploding","text":"Moving, turning and exploding","level":2},{"id":"saving","text":"Saving","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-attributes-are\"\u003EWhat attributes are\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn attribute is a named value that travels with a block reference: an equipment tag&#039;s item number, a room tag&#039;s name, a door&#039;s fire rating. The block&#039;s definition says which attributes it has (each has a \u003Cstrong\u003Etag\u003C/strong\u003E, a \u003Cstrong\u003Eprompt\u003C/strong\u003E, a \u003Cstrong\u003Edefault\u003C/strong\u003E and a place, size and angle for its text). Every reference to the block carries its own values.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESome attributes are \u003Cstrong\u003Einvisible\u003C/strong\u003E: they hold data, such as a maker or a model number, but never draw. Some are \u003Cstrong\u003Econstant\u003C/strong\u003E: the same for every reference, fixed by the block.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"where-attributes-come-from\"\u003EWhere attributes come from\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EBlocks in DWG and DXF files you open keep their attributes, with each reference&#039;s values.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EBlocks you bring in with \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EINSERT\u003C/code\u003E and the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E option keep theirs. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/insert-from-file-and-library\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/insert-from-file-and-library\"\u003EInsert from a file or the library\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELibrary items come in as blocks with attributes: a visible item number and the item&#039;s data, invisible.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EBlocks you make yourself: define attributes with \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EATTDEF\u003C/code\u003E first (below), then select them with the other objects when you make the block.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"define-attributes-for-your-own-blocks\"\u003EDefine attributes for your own blocks\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw the block&#039;s lines, for example a room tag.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBlocks\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDefine attribute\u003C/span\u003E, or type \u003Ccode\u003EATT\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe history shows the modes (\u003Ccode\u003EInvisible=No Constant=No Preset=No\u003C/code\u003E). Type \u003Ccode\u003EI\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E to switch one, and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E when they are right. Invisible values are kept but not drawn; a constant one has the same value in every reference; a preset one is not asked for when you insert the block.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the tag (for example \u003Ccode\u003EROOM_NO\u003C/code\u003E: upper case, no spaces), the prompt (\u003Ccode\u003ERoom number\u003C/code\u003E) and a default value (\u003Ccode\u003E101\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick where the value goes, and give the height and the rotation. The tag shows on the drawing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMake the block with \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EBLOCK\u003C/code\u003E, selecting the lines and the attribute definitions together. The reference that replaces them gets the default values; each new \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EINSERT\u003C/code\u003E asks for them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA selected attribute definition shows \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETag\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPrompt\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDefault\u003C/span\u003E and the modes in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-a-block-the-block-editor\"\u003EChange a block: the block editor\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBlocks\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit block\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EBEDIT\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EBE\u003C/code\u003E) opens a block on its own: type its name (or select a reference first). A bar at the top says which block you are editing. Draw, erase and change its objects with the usual commands, and add or change attribute definitions. Then click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave block\u003C/span\u003E (or type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EBCLOSE\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E): every reference shows the change, and one \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes it all. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDiscard changes\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EBCLOSE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ED\u003C/code\u003E) leaves the block as it was.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen you added, removed or changed attribute definitions, saving also brings every reference in line: values you typed are kept, a new attribute gets its default, a removed one goes, and each attribute goes back to where the definition puts it. The history says how many references were updated.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"edit-a-title-block-where-it-is-refedit\"\u003EEdit a title block where it is (REFEDIT)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA title block on a sheet often sits inside a block. To change its lines or texts without leaving the sheet:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the title block, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBlocks\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit in place\u003C/span\u003E (or type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EREFEDIT\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIts contents become ordinary objects where they are; everything else is faded and cannot be changed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChange, erase or draw: new objects become part of the block.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave the block\u003C/span\u003E in the bar at the top (or type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EREFCLOSE\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E). Every reference shows the change, and one \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes the whole edit. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDiscard\u003C/span\u003E leaves the block as it was.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA linked drawing, a dynamic block, or a reference scaled differently across and up cannot be edited in place: use \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EBEDIT\u003C/code\u003E (or open the linked drawing).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESave or discard in the block editor before you save the drawing: a file saved while the editor is open has the block as it was before.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"fill-them-in-when-you-place-a-block\"\u003EFill them in when you place a block\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EI\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, type the block name and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick where it goes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECadCraft asks for each value, for example \u003Ccode\u003EEnter Item number (ITEM) &lt;&gt;\u003C/code\u003E. Type the value and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to keep the default shown in angle brackets.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EValues the block marks as preset, and constant ones, are not asked for. They take their default.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"see-and-change-the-values\"\u003ESee and change the values\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a block reference.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the right panel, under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAttributes\u003C/span\u003E, each tag has its value. Invisible ones say \u003Ccode\u003Ehidden\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a value, type the new one and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe drawing shows the new value at once. Each change is one step: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe attribute editor.\u003C/strong\u003E Double-click a reference with attributes (or \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEATTEDIT\u003C/code\u003E): the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit attributes\u003C/span\u003E dialog lists every tag with its prompt and value. Type new values; click a row to change that attribute&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHeight\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERotation\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWidth factor\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJustify\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayer\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EColor\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInvisible\u003C/span\u003E for this reference only. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EApply\u003C/span\u003E keeps the dialog open; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOK\u003C/span\u003E applies and closes. Each \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EApply\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOK\u003C/span\u003E is one undo step.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOn the command line\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EATTEDIT\u003C/code\u003E asks each value in turn: type a new one, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to keep it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-the-attribute-definitions-battman-attsync\"\u003EChange the attribute definitions (BATTMAN, ATTSYNC)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBlocks\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAttribute manager\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EBATTMAN\u003C/code\u003E) lists a block&#039;s attributes: change a tag, prompt or default, tick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInvisible\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConstant\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPreset\u003C/span\u003E, and move them up or down (the order values are asked in). With \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUpdate the references (values kept)\u003C/span\u003E ticked, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EApply\u003C/span\u003E also brings every reference in line; a renamed tag keeps its values. Each \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EApply\u003C/span\u003E is one undo step.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EATTSYNC\u003C/code\u003E brings references in line with their block&#039;s definitions by hand: \u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E picks a reference, \u003Ccode\u003EN\u003C/code\u003E names a block, \u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E does every block. Values are kept.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe values of a library item&#039;s own fields (its id, version, license and attribution) are shown but not editable, so that checking for updates keeps working. Its item number and quantity are yours to change.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"list-them\"\u003EList them\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect references and type \u003Ccode\u003ELI\u003C/code\u003E (for \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELIST\u003C/code\u003E). The history shows each attribute as \u003Ccode\u003ETAG = value\u003C/code\u003E, with \u003Ccode\u003E(invisible)\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E(constant)\u003C/code\u003E where that applies.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"moving-turning-and-exploding\"\u003EMoving, turning and exploding\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMOVE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EROTATE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESCALE\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMIRROR\u003C/code\u003E keep the values; the text moves with the block and stays readable.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXPLODE\u003C/code\u003E turns the visible attributes into plain text with their values. The invisible ones are dropped.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"saving\"\u003ESaving\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA saved DWG and a saved DXF both keep every attribute: each block&#039;s definitions (invisible, constant and preset ones too) and each reference&#039;s values. Other CAD programs read them as ordinary block attributes.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn a DXF R12, a value longer than 255 characters is cut to 255, and the history says so.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA value does not show on the drawing.\u003C/strong\u003E It is invisible, or it is empty. Invisible values still show in the right panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe value I typed went to the command line.\u003C/strong\u003E Click into the value box in the right panel first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What attributes are An attribute is a named value that travels with a block reference: an equipment tag's item number, a room tag's name, a door's fire rating. The block's definition says which attributes it has (each has a tag, a prompt, a default and a place, size and angle for its text). Every reference to the block carries its own values. Some attributes are invisible: they hold data, such as a maker or a model number, but never draw. Some are constant: the same for every reference, fixed by the block. Where attributes come from Blocks in DWG and DXF files you open keep their attributes, with each reference's values. Blocks you bring in with INSERT and the File option keep theirs. See Insert from a file or the library. Library items come in as blocks with attributes: a visible item number and the item's data, invisible. Blocks you make yourself: define attributes with ATTDEF first (below), then select them with the other objects when you make the block. Define attributes for your own blocks Draw the block's lines, for example a room tag. Choose Blocks › More › Define attribute, or type ATT and press Enter. The history shows the modes (Invisible=No Constant=No Preset=No). Type I, C or P to switch one, and press Enter when they are right. Invisible values are kept but not drawn; a constant one has the same value in every reference; a preset one is not asked for when you insert the block. Type the tag (for example ROOM_NO: upper case, no spaces), the prompt (Room number) and a default value (101). Click where the value goes, and give the height and the rotation. The tag shows on the drawing. Make the block with BLOCK, selecting the lines and the attribute definitions together. The reference that replaces them gets the default values; each new INSERT asks for them. A selected attribute definition shows Tag, Prompt, Default and the modes in Properties. Change a block: the block editor Blocks › More › Edit block (BEDIT, BE) opens a block on its own: type its name (or select a reference first). A bar at the top says which block you are editing. Draw, erase and change its objects with the usual commands, and add or change attribute definitions. Then click Save block (or type BCLOSE and S): every reference shows the change, and one Ctrl+Z undoes it all. Discard changes (BCLOSE, D) leaves the block as it was. When you added, removed or changed attribute definitions, saving also brings every reference in line: values you typed are kept, a new attribute gets its default, a removed one goes, and each attribute goes back to where the definition puts it. The history says how many references were updated. Edit a title block where it is (REFEDIT) A title block on a sheet often sits inside a block. To change its lines or texts without leaving the sheet: Select the title block, then Blocks › More › Edit in place (or type REFEDIT). Its contents become ordinary objects where they are; everything else is faded and cannot be changed. Change, erase or draw: new objects become part of the block. Click Save the block in the bar at the top (or type REFCLOSE and S). Every reference shows the change, and one Ctrl+Z undoes the whole edit. Discard leaves the block as it was. A linked drawing, a dynamic block, or a reference scaled differently across and up cannot be edited in place: use BEDIT (or open the linked drawing). Save or discard in the block editor before you save the drawing: a file saved while the editor is open has the block as it was before. Fill them in when you place a block Type I and press Enter, type the block name and press Enter. Click where it goes. CadCraft asks for each value, for example Enter Item number (ITEM) \u003C\u003E. Type the value and press Enter, or press Enter to keep the default shown in angle brackets. Values the block marks as preset, and constant ones, are not asked for. They take their default. See and change the values Click a block reference. In the right panel, under Attributes, each tag has its value. Invisible ones say hidden. Click a value, type the new one and press Enter. The drawing shows the new value at once. Each change is one step: Ctrl+Z undoes it. The attribute editor. Double-click a reference with attributes (or EATTEDIT): the Edit attributes dialog lists every tag with its prompt and value. Type new values; click a row to change that attribute's Height, Rotation, Width factor, Justify, Layer, Color and Invisible for this reference only. Apply keeps the dialog open; OK applies and closes. Each Apply or OK is one undo step. On the command line, ATTEDIT asks each value in turn: type a new one, or press Enter to keep it. Change the attribute definitions (BATTMAN, ATTSYNC) Blocks › More › Attribute manager (BATTMAN) lists a block's attributes: change a tag, prompt or default, tick Invisible, Constant or Preset, and move them up or down (the order values are asked in). With Update the references (values kept) ticked, Apply also brings every reference in line; a renamed tag keeps its values. Each Apply is one undo step. ATTSYNC brings references in line with their block's definitions by hand: S picks a reference, N names a block, A does every block. Values are kept. The values of a library item's own fields (its id, version, license and attribution) are shown but not editable, so that checking for updates keeps working. Its item number and quantity are yours to change. List them Select references and type LI (for LIST). The history shows each attribute as TAG = value, with (invisible) or (constant) where that applies. Moving, turning and exploding MOVE, ROTATE, SCALE and MIRROR keep the values; the text moves with the block and stays readable. EXPLODE turns the visible attributes into plain text with their values. The invisible ones are dropped. Saving A saved DWG and a saved DXF both keep every attribute: each block's definitions (invisible, constant and preset ones too) and each reference's values. Other CAD programs read them as ordinary block attributes. In a DXF R12, a value longer than 255 characters is cut to 255, and the history says so. Common mistakes A value does not show on the drawing. It is invisible, or it is empty. Invisible values still show in the right panel. The value I typed went to the command line. Click into the value box in the right panel first.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"insert-from-file-and-library","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Insert from a file or the library","summary":"Bring a block, or a whole drawing, in from another DWG or DXF, and place items from the Library, a file or another library, with their credits and updates.","section":"tasks","order":120,"keywords":["insert","file","library","librarycraft","item","equipment","block from another drawing","wblock","design center","credits","attribution","license","update","feed","item.json"],"headings":[{"id":"insert-a-block-from-another-drawing","text":"Insert a block from another drawing","level":2},{"id":"when-a-name-is-already-used","text":"When a name is already used","level":3},{"id":"open-the-library","text":"Open the library","level":2},{"id":"place-an-item","text":"Place an item","level":2},{"id":"items-in-your-language","text":"Items in your language","level":2},{"id":"check-for-updates","text":"Check for updates","level":2},{"id":"credit-what-you-used","text":"Credit what you used","level":2},{"id":"sign-in-to-the-library","text":"Sign in to the library","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"insert-a-block-from-another-drawing\"\u003EInsert a block from another drawing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EI\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInsert\u003C/span\u003E in the Blocks group.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003EEnter block name or [File/Library]\u003C/code\u003E, type \u003Ccode\u003EF\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the DWG or DXF on this computer. It is read here; it is not opened, and nothing is uploaded.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInsert from\u003C/span\u003E dialog, pick a block from the list (type in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFilter blocks\u003C/span\u003E to find it), or keep \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThe whole drawing\u003C/span\u003E. Click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInsert\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick where it goes, and fill in any attributes it asks for.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe whole drawing comes in as one block named after the file. Blocks inside the block you picked come too. The layers they use are added to this drawing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf the other drawing is in different units (inches and millimeters, say), CadCraft scales the reference to match and says so in the history.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"when-a-name-is-already-used\"\u003EWhen a name is already used\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf this drawing already has a block with the same name, CadCraft asks:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeep existing\u003C/span\u003E uses this drawing&#039;s block of that name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERename\u003C/span\u003E brings the new one in as \u003Ccode\u003EName (2)\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe whole insert is one step: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes the reference and the copied definitions away again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-the-library\"\u003EOpen the library\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELibrary…\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBlocks\u003C/span\u003E panel, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELibrary\u003C/span\u003E in the Blocks group, or type \u003Ccode\u003ELIB\u003C/code\u003E. At the \u003Ccode\u003EINSERT\u003C/code\u003E prompt, \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E opens it too.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe library has four sources, as tabs:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELibrary\u003C/span\u003E: the CadCraft library. Search by name, maker or model, and filter by category.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFiles…\u003C/span\u003E: an item file (\u003Ccode\u003Eitem.json\u003C/code\u003E) from any library, or a drawing, spreadsheet or 3D model to make items from: a DWG or DXF (each block becomes an item), a CSV with name and size columns, or a glTF or OBJ model. Nothing is uploaded.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStandard\u003C/span\u003E: steel sections (as sections, elevations or 3D members) and standard parts such as bolts, nuts and bearings, made here from published tables. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/steel-and-standard-parts\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/steel-and-standard-parts\"\u003EPlace steel sections and standard parts\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOther feeds\u003C/span\u003E: other libraries that answer the same way Library does. Add their address once; this browser remembers it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach card shows a preview, the name, the maker and model, the license and the version. A withdrawn item is marked \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWithdrawn\u003C/span\u003E with the reason; CadCraft asks before you place one.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"place-an-item\"\u003EPlace an item\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlace\u003C/span\u003E on a card.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick where the item goes. Type \u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E first to turn it, or \u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E to scale it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003EEnter Item number (CC-ITEM)\u003C/code\u003E, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for the next free number, or type your own.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe item becomes a block named \u003Ccode\u003ELIB_&lt;name&gt;_v&lt;version&gt;\u003C/code\u003E, drawn in this drawing&#039;s units. If the drawing has no units, the item goes in at millimeters and the history says so; set \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnits\u003C/span\u003E in the right panel first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIts data rides along as invisible attributes: the item&#039;s id and version, license and attribution, maker, model, category and sizes. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/attributes\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/attributes\"\u003EBlock attributes\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"items-in-your-language\"\u003EItems in your language\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn item can carry its name and description in other languages. When it has them in the language CadCraft is set to (Settings › General › Language), the card and the placed block&#039;s item name use them. The block itself keeps a name made from the item&#039;s own name, so a drawing has one block per item whichever language placed it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"check-for-updates\"\u003ECheck for updates\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the library.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck for updates\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach library item in the drawing is listed with its item numbers and whether it is up to date, has a newer version (and what changed), or was withdrawn. Click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUpdate\u003C/span\u003E on a row, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUpdate all\u003C/span\u003E. Every reference moves to the new version&#039;s block and keeps its item number, quantity, place, angle and layer. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes an update.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"credit-what-you-used\"\u003ECredit what you used\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELibrary credits\u003C/span\u003E. Each item is listed with its attribution line and license.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy attribution\u003C/span\u003E copies one item&#039;s line; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy all\u003C/span\u003E copies every line.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload CSV\u003C/span\u003E saves the list with the item numbers, makers, models, licenses and attributions. It opens in any spreadsheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELicenses such as CC BY ask you to credit the author where you use the work. Put the lines on the drawing, in the specification or in the project notes.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"sign-in-to-the-library\"\u003ESign in to the library\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESign in to the library\u003C/span\u003E. A small window opens on the site; once you are signed in there, CadCraft can show your team&#039;s items and register what you place, so you hear when an item you used changes. The sign-in lasts for this browser tab. Revoke it any time from your account&#039;s security page.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;Making items from IFC types is in BimCraft.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E IFC and family files are read in the BimCraft app. Make the item there or on the Library, then use its \u003Ccode\u003Eitem.json\u003C/code\u003E here.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;The library could not be reached.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E You are offline, or the library is down. Items already in the drawing are not affected.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAn item is far too big or too small.\u003C/strong\u003E The drawing&#039;s units are wrong or not set. Undo, set \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnits\u003C/span\u003E, and place it again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Insert a block from another drawing Type I and press Enter, or click Insert in the Blocks group. At Enter block name or [File/Library], type F and press Enter. Choose the DWG or DXF on this computer. It is read here; it is not opened, and nothing is uploaded. In the Insert from dialog, pick a block from the list (type in Filter blocks to find it), or keep The whole drawing. Click Insert. Click where it goes, and fill in any attributes it asks for. The whole drawing comes in as one block named after the file. Blocks inside the block you picked come too. The layers they use are added to this drawing. If the other drawing is in different units (inches and millimeters, say), CadCraft scales the reference to match and says so in the history. When a name is already used If this drawing already has a block with the same name, CadCraft asks: Keep existing uses this drawing's block of that name. Rename brings the new one in as Name (2). The whole insert is one step: Ctrl+Z takes the reference and the copied definitions away again. Open the library Click Library… in the Blocks panel, or Library in the Blocks group, or type LIB. At the INSERT prompt, L opens it too. The library has four sources, as tabs: Library: the CadCraft library. Search by name, maker or model, and filter by category. Files…: an item file (item.json) from any library, or a drawing, spreadsheet or 3D model to make items from: a DWG or DXF (each block becomes an item), a CSV with name and size columns, or a glTF or OBJ model. Nothing is uploaded. Standard: steel sections (as sections, elevations or 3D members) and standard parts such as bolts, nuts and bearings, made here from published tables. See Place steel sections and standard parts. Other feeds: other libraries that answer the same way Library does. Add their address once; this browser remembers it. Each card shows a preview, the name, the maker and model, the license and the version. A withdrawn item is marked Withdrawn with the reason; CadCraft asks before you place one. Place an item Click Place on a card. Click where the item goes. Type R first to turn it, or S to scale it. At Enter Item number (CC-ITEM), press Enter for the next free number, or type your own. The item becomes a block named LIB_\u003Cname\u003E_v\u003Cversion\u003E, drawn in this drawing's units. If the drawing has no units, the item goes in at millimeters and the history says so; set Units in the right panel first. Its data rides along as invisible attributes: the item's id and version, license and attribution, maker, model, category and sizes. See Block attributes. Items in your language An item can carry its name and description in other languages. When it has them in the language CadCraft is set to (Settings › General › Language), the card and the placed block's item name use them. The block itself keeps a name made from the item's own name, so a drawing has one block per item whichever language placed it. Check for updates Open the library. Click Check for updates. Each library item in the drawing is listed with its item numbers and whether it is up to date, has a newer version (and what changed), or was withdrawn. Click Update on a row, or Update all. Every reference moves to the new version's block and keeps its item number, quantity, place, angle and layer. Ctrl+Z undoes an update. Credit what you used Click Library credits. Each item is listed with its attribution line and license. Copy attribution copies one item's line; Copy all copies every line. Download CSV saves the list with the item numbers, makers, models, licenses and attributions. It opens in any spreadsheet. Licenses such as CC BY ask you to credit the author where you use the work. Put the lines on the drawing, in the specification or in the project notes. Sign in to the library Click Sign in to the library. A small window opens on the site; once you are signed in there, CadCraft can show your team's items and register what you place, so you hear when an item you used changes. The sign-in lasts for this browser tab. Revoke it any time from your account's security page. Common mistakes \"Making items from IFC types is in BimCraft.\" IFC and family files are read in the BimCraft app. Make the item there or on the Library, then use its item.json here. \"The library could not be reached.\" You are offline, or the library is down. Items already in the drawing are not affected. An item is far too big or too small. The drawing's units are wrong or not set. Undo, set Units, and place it again.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"sheets-and-viewports","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Set up a sheet with a title block and viewports","summary":"Make a sheet (layout) with a border and title block, set the viewport scale, add a second viewport for a detail, and fill in the title block.","section":"tasks","order":120,"keywords":["layout","sheet","paper space","viewport","mview","vpview","title block","scale","paper size","border","vports","plot scale","mspace","pspace","model space","enter viewport","double-click viewport"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-make","text":"What you will make","level":2},{"id":"model-and-sheets","text":"Model and sheets","level":2},{"id":"before-you-start","text":"Before you start","level":2},{"id":"make-the-sheet","text":"Make the sheet","level":2},{"id":"choose-the-paper","text":"Choose the paper","level":2},{"id":"set-the-viewport-scale","text":"Set the viewport scale","level":2},{"id":"show-part-of-the-model-in-a-viewport","text":"Show part of the model in a viewport","level":2},{"id":"add-a-second-viewport-for-a-detail","text":"Add a second viewport for a detail","level":2},{"id":"fill-in-the-title-block","text":"Fill in the title block","level":2},{"id":"draw-on-the-sheet","text":"Draw on the sheet","level":2},{"id":"work-on-the-model-through-a-viewport","text":"Work on the model through a viewport","level":2},{"id":"label-a-view-with-its-name","text":"Label a view with its name","level":2},{"id":"freeze-a-layer-in-one-viewport","text":"Freeze a layer in one viewport","level":2},{"id":"more-sheets","text":"More sheets","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-make\"\u003EWhat you will make\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn A3 sheet with a title block, the whole plan in one viewport at 1:100, and a second viewport showing one corner at 1:20.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"model-and-sheets\"\u003EModel and sheets\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou draw the building on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab, at full size: a 6 m wall is 6000 units long in millimeters. A \u003Cstrong\u003Esheet\u003C/strong\u003E (a layout) is a piece of paper, measured in millimeters or inches. A \u003Cstrong\u003Eviewport\u003C/strong\u003E is a window cut in the sheet that shows the model at a scale.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"before-you-start\"\u003EBefore you start\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen or draw a plan on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E so nothing is selected.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECurrent settings\u003C/span\u003E, set \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnits\u003C/span\u003E to the unit you drew in, for example \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMillimetres\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnits matter: a viewport scale such as 1:100 means one millimeter on the paper shows 100 real millimeters, so CadCraft needs to know what one drawing unit is. A drawing left as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnitless\u003C/span\u003E is taken to be in the paper&#039;s unit (millimeters on ISO paper, inches on US paper).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"make-the-sheet\"\u003EMake the sheet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E+\u003C/span\u003E button to the right of the tabs under the drawing, or type \u003Ccode\u003ELAYOUT\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew sheet\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA new tab, \u003Ccode\u003ELayout1\u003C/code\u003E, opens. You should see a sheet on a darker desk: a border, a title block in the lower-right corner and one viewport above it showing the whole model at the largest standard scale that fits. The history names the paper: ISO A3 for metric and unitless drawings, Tabloid (ANSI B) for drawings in inches or feet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExtents\u003C/span\u003E to fit the sheet on screen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"choose-the-paper\"\u003EChoose the paper\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E so nothing is selected. The right panel heading reads &quot;Sheet: Layout1&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EName\u003C/span\u003E, type \u003Ccode\u003EA-101 Plan\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The tab is renamed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaper\u003C/span\u003E, choose a size.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrientation\u003C/span\u003E, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELandscape\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPortrait\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-warning\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003EWarning\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChanging the paper does not move or resize what is already on the sheet. After changing it, check that the border, the title block and the viewport still fit, and move or resize them with grips. Switching between millimeter and inch paper keeps the numbers, so everything changes size: the status bar warns you.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"set-the-viewport-scale\"\u003ESet the viewport scale\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the viewport&#039;s frame (its edge). The panel heading reads &quot;Viewport&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale\u003C/span\u003E, choose \u003Ccode\u003E1:100\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe model inside the viewport redraws at 1:100, centered where it was. The list offers metric scales from 10:1 to 1:10000; drawings in inches or feet also get architectural scales (1/4&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot; and the like) and engineering scales (1&quot; = 20&#039; and the like). \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit the whole model\u003C/span\u003E sets an exact, non-standard scale that fits everything.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf the plan is no longer centered, change \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView centre X\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView centre Y\u003C/span\u003E: they are the model point shown in the middle of the viewport.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"show-part-of-the-model-in-a-viewport\"\u003EShow part of the model in a viewport\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith the viewport selected, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPick a window on the model…\u003C/span\u003E in the right panel (or type \u003Ccode\u003EVPVIEW\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECadCraft switches to the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab. Click one corner of the area to show.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the opposite corner.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou are back on the sheet, and the viewport shows that area at the largest standard scale that fits; the history says which. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow the whole model\u003C/span\u003E goes back to the whole plan at a standard scale.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-a-second-viewport-for-a-detail\"\u003EAdd a second viewport for a detail\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMake room on the sheet: select the first viewport and drag a corner grip to make it smaller.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EMV\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EViewport\u003C/span\u003E in the Sheets group.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick one corner of the new viewport on the sheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the opposite corner.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe new viewport shows the whole model. The history says its scale.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the new viewport&#039;s frame.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPick a window on the model…\u003C/span\u003E and pick a small area round one corner of the plan.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale\u003C/span\u003E, choose \u003Ccode\u003E1:20\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EViewport frames are on the layer \u003Ccode\u003EViewports\u003C/code\u003E, which is set not to plot, so the frames show on screen but not in the PDF.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"fill-in-the-title-block\"\u003EFill in the title block\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe title block is made of ordinary lines and text on the layer \u003Ccode\u003ETitle block\u003C/code\u003E. It starts with the drawing name, the scale of the first viewport, today&#039;s date and the sheet number.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDouble-click the large title text. The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EText\u003C/span\u003E box in the right panel gets the cursor.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the title of the sheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDo the same for the other fields. Use \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ETEXT\u003C/code\u003E to write in the empty &quot;DRAWN BY&quot; cell. The SCALE text does not update itself when you change a viewport&#039;s scale: edit it by hand.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-on-the-sheet\"\u003EDraw on the sheet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAnything you draw on a sheet tab is on the paper, in paper units: text 3.5 high is 3.5 mm tall on an ISO sheet. Notes, north arrows and sheet borders belong here.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo change the plan itself, go to the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab, or work through a viewport (next section); every viewport shows the change.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"work-on-the-model-through-a-viewport\"\u003EWork on the model through a viewport\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDouble-click inside a viewport, away from its frame. Or type \u003Ccode\u003EMS\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: with one viewport on the sheet it goes straight in; with several, click inside the one you want.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe frame is drawn heavier, the history says you are inside, and the sheet&#039;s tab stays selected. Everything you draw or change now is on the model, in model units, as on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab: a line drawn inside a viewport at 1:100 is in real millimeters. Layers frozen in this viewport cannot be picked here.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETurn the wheel or drag with the middle button to zoom and pan what the viewport shows. The sheet stays where it is.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDouble-click anywhere outside the frame, or type \u003Ccode\u003EPS\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou are back on the sheet. The viewport keeps the view you left it with; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E puts the old view back in one step. Clicking any tab under the drawing also leaves the viewport.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA viewport whose display is locked (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDisplay locked\u003C/span\u003E in Properties) keeps its view: inside it, pan and zoom move the whole sheet instead. A viewport that is turned off cannot be entered.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"label-a-view-with-its-name\"\u003ELabel a view with its name\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA view title under a viewport can show the viewport&#039;s view name and keep up with it:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGive the view a name: select the viewport&#039;s frame and fill in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView name\u003C/span\u003E in Properties — or save the view on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab with \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ENAMEDVIEW\u003C/code\u003E and, with the viewport selected, type \u003Ccode\u003EVPVIEW\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EV\u003C/code\u003E and the view&#039;s name: the viewport shows that view and takes its name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EVIEWLABEL\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EVL\u003C/code\u003E) and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, then click where the label goes (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E puts it under the viewport&#039;s lower-left corner).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen the view name changes, the label changes with it, in the same undo step.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"freeze-a-layer-in-one-viewport\"\u003EFreeze a layer in one viewport\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo hide a layer in one viewport but not the others (furniture in a detail, say):\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the viewport&#039;s frame, or work inside it (double-click).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayers\u003C/span\u003E panel gets one more button on each row, and a line above the list says so.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick that button on the layer&#039;s row.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe layer disappears from that viewport only. Click again to thaw it; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes either. For several layers or viewports at once, type \u003Ccode\u003EVPLAYER\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003EF\u003C/code\u003E (Freeze) or \u003Ccode\u003ET\u003C/code\u003E (Thaw), the layer names (\u003Ccode\u003EA-*\u003C/code\u003E takes every layer starting with A-), then \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E (Current), \u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E (All) or \u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E (Select). \u003Ccode\u003EVPLAYER\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003EV\u003C/code\u003E (Vpvisdflt) picks layers that new viewports start frozen in.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"more-sheets\"\u003EMore sheets\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E+\u003C/span\u003E adds another sheet (\u003Ccode\u003ELayout2\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ELayout3\u003C/code\u003E …) with the next sheet number. To delete the open sheet, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete this sheet\u003C/span\u003E in the right panel. It goes without asking; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E brings it back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPlot a sheet with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlot this sheet\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command P\"\u003E⌘P\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+P\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/plot-pdf\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/plot-pdf\"\u003EPlot to PDF\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe viewport shows nothing.\u003C/strong\u003E The model is empty, or the view is aimed somewhere else. Select the frame and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow the whole model\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe scale list shows only ratios and no feet-and-inch scales.\u003C/strong\u003E Set \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnits\u003C/span\u003E to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInches\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFeet\u003C/span\u003E on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E1:100 looks ten times too big or small.\u003C/strong\u003E The drawing units are wrong. Set \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnits\u003C/span\u003E to what one unit really is.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EClicking the plan inside the viewport selects nothing.\u003C/strong\u003E On a sheet you select the sheet&#039;s own objects. Double-click inside the viewport first to work on the plan through it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EClicks outside the viewport do nothing.\u003C/strong\u003E You are inside a viewport: double-click outside it, or type \u003Ccode\u003EPS\u003C/code\u003E, to work on the sheet again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;Viewports go on a layout.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMVIEW\u003C/code\u003E only works on a sheet tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will make An A3 sheet with a title block, the whole plan in one viewport at 1:100, and a second viewport showing one corner at 1:20. Model and sheets You draw the building on the Model tab, at full size: a 6 m wall is 6000 units long in millimeters. A sheet (a layout) is a piece of paper, measured in millimeters or inches. A viewport is a window cut in the sheet that shows the model at a scale. Before you start Open or draw a plan on the Model tab. Press Esc so nothing is selected. In Current settings, set Units to the unit you drew in, for example Millimetres. Units matter: a viewport scale such as 1:100 means one millimeter on the paper shows 100 real millimeters, so CadCraft needs to know what one drawing unit is. A drawing left as Unitless is taken to be in the paper's unit (millimeters on ISO paper, inches on US paper). Make the sheet Click the + button to the right of the tabs under the drawing, or type LAYOUT and press Enter, or click New sheet. A new tab, Layout1, opens. You should see a sheet on a darker desk: a border, a title block in the lower-right corner and one viewport above it showing the whole model at the largest standard scale that fits. The history names the paper: ISO A3 for metric and unitless drawings, Tabloid (ANSI B) for drawings in inches or feet. Click Extents to fit the sheet on screen. Choose the paper Press Esc so nothing is selected. The right panel heading reads \"Sheet: Layout1\". In Name, type A-101 Plan and press Enter. The tab is renamed. In Paper, choose a size. In Orientation, choose Landscape or Portrait. Changing the paper does not move or resize what is already on the sheet. After changing it, check that the border, the title block and the viewport still fit, and move or resize them with grips. Switching between millimeter and inch paper keeps the numbers, so everything changes size: the status bar warns you. Set the viewport scale Click the viewport's frame (its edge). The panel heading reads \"Viewport\". In Scale, choose 1:100. The model inside the viewport redraws at 1:100, centered where it was. The list offers metric scales from 10:1 to 1:10000; drawings in inches or feet also get architectural scales (1/4\" = 1'-0\" and the like) and engineering scales (1\" = 20' and the like). Fit the whole model sets an exact, non-standard scale that fits everything. If the plan is no longer centered, change View centre X and View centre Y: they are the model point shown in the middle of the viewport. Show part of the model in a viewport With the viewport selected, click Pick a window on the model… in the right panel (or type VPVIEW and press Enter). CadCraft switches to the Model tab. Click one corner of the area to show. Click the opposite corner. You are back on the sheet, and the viewport shows that area at the largest standard scale that fits; the history says which. Show the whole model goes back to the whole plan at a standard scale. Add a second viewport for a detail Make room on the sheet: select the first viewport and drag a corner grip to make it smaller. Type MV and press Enter, or click Viewport in the Sheets group. Click one corner of the new viewport on the sheet. Click the opposite corner. The new viewport shows the whole model. The history says its scale. Click the new viewport's frame. Click Pick a window on the model… and pick a small area round one corner of the plan. In Scale, choose 1:20. Viewport frames are on the layer Viewports, which is set not to plot, so the frames show on screen but not in the PDF. Fill in the title block The title block is made of ordinary lines and text on the layer Title block. It starts with the drawing name, the scale of the first viewport, today's date and the sheet number. Double-click the large title text. The Text box in the right panel gets the cursor. Type the title of the sheet. Press Tab. Do the same for the other fields. Use TEXT to write in the empty \"DRAWN BY\" cell. The SCALE text does not update itself when you change a viewport's scale: edit it by hand. Draw on the sheet Anything you draw on a sheet tab is on the paper, in paper units: text 3.5 high is 3.5 mm tall on an ISO sheet. Notes, north arrows and sheet borders belong here. To change the plan itself, go to the Model tab, or work through a viewport (next section); every viewport shows the change. Work on the model through a viewport Double-click inside a viewport, away from its frame. Or type MS and press Enter: with one viewport on the sheet it goes straight in; with several, click inside the one you want. The frame is drawn heavier, the history says you are inside, and the sheet's tab stays selected. Everything you draw or change now is on the model, in model units, as on the Model tab: a line drawn inside a viewport at 1:100 is in real millimeters. Layers frozen in this viewport cannot be picked here. Turn the wheel or drag with the middle button to zoom and pan what the viewport shows. The sheet stays where it is. Double-click anywhere outside the frame, or type PS and press Enter. You are back on the sheet. The viewport keeps the view you left it with; Ctrl+Z puts the old view back in one step. Clicking any tab under the drawing also leaves the viewport. A viewport whose display is locked (Display locked in Properties) keeps its view: inside it, pan and zoom move the whole sheet instead. A viewport that is turned off cannot be entered. Label a view with its name A view title under a viewport can show the viewport's view name and keep up with it: Give the view a name: select the viewport's frame and fill in View name in Properties — or save the view on the Model tab with NAMEDVIEW and, with the viewport selected, type VPVIEW, V and the view's name: the viewport shows that view and takes its name. Type VIEWLABEL (VL) and press Enter, then click where the label goes (Enter puts it under the viewport's lower-left corner). When the view name changes, the label changes with it, in the same undo step. Freeze a layer in one viewport To hide a layer in one viewport but not the others (furniture in a detail, say): Select the viewport's frame, or work inside it (double-click). The Layers panel gets one more button on each row, and a line above the list says so. Click that button on the layer's row. The layer disappears from that viewport only. Click again to thaw it; Ctrl+Z undoes either. For several layers or viewports at once, type VPLAYER: F (Freeze) or T (Thaw), the layer names (A-* takes every layer starting with A-), then C (Current), A (All) or S (Select). VPLAYER V (Vpvisdflt) picks layers that new viewports start frozen in. More sheets Each + adds another sheet (Layout2, Layout3 …) with the next sheet number. To delete the open sheet, press Esc and click Delete this sheet in the right panel. It goes without asking; Ctrl+Z brings it back. Plot a sheet with Plot this sheet or Ctrl+P; see Plot to PDF. Common mistakes The viewport shows nothing. The model is empty, or the view is aimed somewhere else. Select the frame and click Show the whole model. The scale list shows only ratios and no feet-and-inch scales. Set Units to Inches or Feet on the Model tab. 1:100 looks ten times too big or small. The drawing units are wrong. Set Units to what one unit really is. Clicking the plan inside the viewport selects nothing. On a sheet you select the sheet's own objects. Double-click inside the viewport first to work on the plan through it. Clicks outside the viewport do nothing. You are inside a viewport: double-click outside it, or type PS, to work on the sheet again. \"Viewports go on a layout.\" MVIEW only works on a sheet tab.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"steel-and-standard-parts","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Place steel sections and standard parts","summary":"Draw steel sections, elevations and 3D members from the published section tables, place bolts, nuts, washers, pins, bearings, keys and gears from their standards, and list them in a parts list.","section":"tasks","order":121,"keywords":["steel","section","profile","shape","w shape","wide flange","i-beam","channel","angle","hss","tube","pipe","ipe","hea","heb","upn","beam","column","member","elevation","aisc","en 10365","bolt","screw","nut","washer","pin","dowel","bearing","key","keyway","gear","iso 4017","iso 4014","iso 4032","iso 7089","iso 2338","iso 15","din 6885","asme","standard parts","parts list","bill of materials","bom","steelmember","partslist"],"headings":[{"id":"place-a-steel-section","text":"Place a steel section","level":2},{"id":"make-a-3d-member","text":"Make a 3D member","level":2},{"id":"place-a-standard-part","text":"Place a standard part","level":2},{"id":"make-a-parts-list","text":"Make a parts list","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe library&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStandard\u003C/span\u003E tab has two kinds of things, made right here from published tables (nothing is downloaded):\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESteel sections\u003C/strong\u003E: 1,335 sizes. W, S, C, L, HSS and Pipe from the AISC Shapes Database v16.0; IPE, HEA, HEB and UPN from EN 10365:2017. DetailCraft uses the same table.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EStandard parts\u003C/strong\u003E: hex head screws (ISO 4017), hex bolts (ISO 4014), hex nuts (ISO 4032), plain washers (ISO 7089), inch hex bolts and nuts (ASME B18.2.1 and B18.2.2), parallel pins (ISO 2338), deep groove ball bearings (ISO 15), parallel keys (DIN 6885-1) and spur gears (modules from ISO 54).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEverything here is shared under CC0: use it for anything, no credit needed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"place-a-steel-section\"\u003EPlace a steel section\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the library (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELibrary\u003C/span\u003E in the Blocks group, or type \u003Ccode\u003ELIB\u003C/code\u003E) and click the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStandard\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick the family in the first list, for example \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EW shapes\u003C/span\u003E. Type in the search box to narrow the sizes: \u003Ccode\u003EW12\u003C/code\u003E shows every W12.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlace as\u003C/span\u003E, choose:\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESection\u003C/span\u003E: the cross-section, with its centre at the insertion point.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EElevation\u003C/span\u003E: the member seen from the side, as long as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength (mm)\u003C/span\u003E says, from the middle of its left end.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D member\u003C/span\u003E: a solid on the 3D tab (below).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlace\u003C/span\u003E on the size&#039;s card, then click where it goes, as for any library item.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA section or elevation becomes a block. Its attributes carry the size, the table it comes from, the length (for an elevation) and a material you can fill in.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ES shapes, channels and UPN are drawn with parallel flanges of the average thickness, and angles with square corners: the real flanges are sloped and the corners rounded. W, IPE, HEA and HEB have their root fillets, and HSS their corner radii.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"make-a-3d-member\"\u003EMake a 3D member\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D member\u003C/span\u003E and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlace\u003C/span\u003E, or type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESTEELMEMBER\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003ESMEMBER\u003C/code\u003E) at the command line:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003ESection size\u003C/code\u003E, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for the size shown, or type one, for example \u003Ccode\u003EHSS6x6x1/4\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003EIPE300\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the start point, then the end point. The member runs between them, sitting on the ground.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFor a column, type \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E, click its base point and type its height.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe member is a closed solid on the 3D tab. Move or copy it there like any other solid. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes it away again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"place-a-standard-part\"\u003EPlace a standard part\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStandard\u003C/span\u003E tab, pick the family, for example \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHex head screws\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFor a screw, bolt, pin or key, type its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength (mm)\u003C/span\u003E (inches for the inch families). For a gear, type the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETeeth\u003C/span\u003E, the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFace width (mm)\u003C/span\u003E and the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBore (mm)\u003C/span\u003E. Leave a box empty for the usual value shown in it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlace\u003C/span\u003E on the size and click where it goes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EScrews, bolts and pins are drawn from the side with the bearing face of the head at the insertion point; nuts, washers, bearings and gears face on; keys from above. Threads are drawn the usual way: the root as a thin line, the end of the thread as a thick one. Gears are drawn as their tip circle, pitch circle and root circle, not tooth by tooth.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach part&#039;s block carries its type, standard, size, length and material as attributes, and its weight from the nominal shape in steel. Change the material in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E panel.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe same parts are in the Library as items too, and PartCraft opens them as parametric parts (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStandard part\u003C/span\u003E in its File group).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"make-a-parts-list\"\u003EMake a parts list\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the library, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EParts list\u003C/span\u003E: every standard part and steel section in the drawing, grouped by type, standard, size, length and material, with the quantity and the item numbers. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload CSV\u003C/span\u003E saves it for a spreadsheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo draw it, type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPARTSLIST\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EPLIST\u003C/code\u003E), click the top left corner of the table, and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for the usual text height or type one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe list is read from the blocks&#039; attributes, so it is still right after the drawing is saved as DWG or DXF and opened again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;No steel section is called …&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E: type the size the way the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStandard\u003C/span\u003E tab lists it: \u003Ccode\u003EW12x26\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EL4x4x1/2\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EHSS6.625x0.280\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EPipe4STD\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EIPE300\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe member is on the 3D tab, not in the plan.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESTEELMEMBER\u003C/code\u003E makes a solid; open the 3D tab to see it. To show the steel in the plan, place a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESection\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EElevation\u003C/span\u003E too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA length out of range.\u003C/strong\u003E A bolt must be longer than its head, and a key longer than it is wide. The line under the cards says which value was refused.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"The library's Standard tab has two kinds of things, made right here from published tables (nothing is downloaded): Steel sections: 1,335 sizes. W, S, C, L, HSS and Pipe from the AISC Shapes Database v16.0; IPE, HEA, HEB and UPN from EN 10365:2017. DetailCraft uses the same table. Standard parts: hex head screws (ISO 4017), hex bolts (ISO 4014), hex nuts (ISO 4032), plain washers (ISO 7089), inch hex bolts and nuts (ASME B18.2.1 and B18.2.2), parallel pins (ISO 2338), deep groove ball bearings (ISO 15), parallel keys (DIN 6885-1) and spur gears (modules from ISO 54). Everything here is shared under CC0: use it for anything, no credit needed. Place a steel section Open the library (Library in the Blocks group, or type LIB) and click the Standard tab. Pick the family in the first list, for example W shapes. Type in the search box to narrow the sizes: W12 shows every W12. In Place as, choose: Section: the cross-section, with its centre at the insertion point. Elevation: the member seen from the side, as long as Length (mm) says, from the middle of its left end. 3D member: a solid on the 3D tab (below). Click Place on the size's card, then click where it goes, as for any library item. A section or elevation becomes a block. Its attributes carry the size, the table it comes from, the length (for an elevation) and a material you can fill in. S shapes, channels and UPN are drawn with parallel flanges of the average thickness, and angles with square corners: the real flanges are sloped and the corners rounded. W, IPE, HEA and HEB have their root fillets, and HSS their corner radii. Make a 3D member Choose 3D member and click Place, or type STEELMEMBER (SMEMBER) at the command line: At Section size, press Enter for the size shown, or type one, for example HSS6x6x1/4 or IPE300. Click the start point, then the end point. The member runs between them, sitting on the ground. For a column, type C, click its base point and type its height. The member is a closed solid on the 3D tab. Move or copy it there like any other solid. Ctrl+Z takes it away again. Place a standard part On the Standard tab, pick the family, for example Hex head screws. For a screw, bolt, pin or key, type its Length (mm) (inches for the inch families). For a gear, type the Teeth, the Face width (mm) and the Bore (mm). Leave a box empty for the usual value shown in it. Click Place on the size and click where it goes. Screws, bolts and pins are drawn from the side with the bearing face of the head at the insertion point; nuts, washers, bearings and gears face on; keys from above. Threads are drawn the usual way: the root as a thin line, the end of the thread as a thick one. Gears are drawn as their tip circle, pitch circle and root circle, not tooth by tooth. Each part's block carries its type, standard, size, length and material as attributes, and its weight from the nominal shape in steel. Change the material in the Properties panel. The same parts are in the Library as items too, and PartCraft opens them as parametric parts (Standard part in its File group). Make a parts list In the library, click Parts list: every standard part and steel section in the drawing, grouped by type, standard, size, length and material, with the quantity and the item numbers. Download CSV saves it for a spreadsheet. To draw it, type PARTSLIST (PLIST), click the top left corner of the table, and press Enter for the usual text height or type one. The list is read from the blocks' attributes, so it is still right after the drawing is saved as DWG or DXF and opened again. Common mistakes \"No steel section is called …\": type the size the way the Standard tab lists it: W12x26, L4x4x1/2, HSS6.625x0.280, Pipe4STD, IPE300. The member is on the 3D tab, not in the plan. STEELMEMBER makes a solid; open the 3D tab to see it. To show the steel in the plan, place a Section or Elevation too. A length out of range. A bolt must be longer than its head, and a key longer than it is wide. The line under the cards says which value was refused.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"viewport-overrides","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Viewport class overrides","summary":"On a sheet, hide a class or give it another colour, lineweight or fill in one viewport only; the model and the other viewports stay as they are.","section":"tasks","order":121,"keywords":["viewport","class override","viewport override","hide class in viewport","sheet","layout","VPLAYER","plot","Vectorworks"],"headings":[{"id":"what-an-override-changes","text":"What an override changes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA sheet often shows the same model more than once: a plan with the furniture and a plan without it, or a lighting plan where only the lights are in colour. A viewport class override changes how one \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/classes\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/classes\"\u003Eclass\u003C/a\u003E shows \u003Cstrong\u003Ein that viewport only\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the sheet and select one viewport.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the Classes panel (\u003Ccode\u003ECLASSES\u003C/code\u003E) and pick the class.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThis viewport\u003C/span\u003E, tick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHide in this viewport\u003C/span\u003E, or set \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EColor here\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELineweight here\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFill here\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe list below shows every class this viewport changes. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClear this viewport&#039;s overrides\u003C/span\u003E takes them all off. Each change is one undo step.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-an-override-changes\"\u003EWhat an override changes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EHide\u003C/strong\u003E hides the class and every class under it in this viewport.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EColor, lineweight and fill\u003C/strong\u003E change the objects of the class whose value is not set on the object itself, that is, objects that take it by class or by layer. An object with its own colour keeps it, as with a viewport layer override.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOverrides show on screen and in plots and PDFs, including a sheet set published to PDF. They work together with the viewport&#039;s frozen layers and layer overrides.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOverrides are saved with the drawing in DWG and DXF (with the class list, in the block \u003Ccode\u003ECADCRAFT_CLASSES\u003C/code\u003E) and come back on the same viewport when the file is opened in CadCraft.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"A sheet often shows the same model more than once: a plan with the furniture and a plan without it, or a lighting plan where only the lights are in colour. A viewport class override changes how one class shows in that viewport only. Open the sheet and select one viewport. Open the Classes panel (CLASSES) and pick the class. Under This viewport, tick Hide in this viewport, or set Color here, Lineweight here or Fill here. The list below shows every class this viewport changes. Clear this viewport's overrides takes them all off. Each change is one undo step. What an override changes Hide hides the class and every class under it in this viewport. Color, lineweight and fill change the objects of the class whose value is not set on the object itself, that is, objects that take it by class or by layer. An object with its own colour keeps it, as with a viewport layer override. Overrides show on screen and in plots and PDFs, including a sheet set published to PDF. They work together with the viewport's frozen layers and layer overrides. Overrides are saved with the drawing in DWG and DXF (with the class list, in the block CADCRAFT_CLASSES) and come back on the same viewport when the file is opened in CadCraft.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"constraints","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Constrain a drawing","summary":"Keep lines horizontal, ends together and sizes fixed with geometric and dimensional constraints, and let the drawing follow when you change a size.","section":"tasks","order":125,"keywords":["constraint","constraints","parametric","geomconstraint","gcon","dimconstraint","dcon","gchorizontal","gcvertical","gcparallel","gcperpendicular","gccoincident","gctangent","gcequal","gcfix","gcsymmetric","gccollinear","gcconcentric","dclinear","dchorizontal","dcvertical","dcaligned","dcradius","dcdiameter","dcangular","dcconvert","autoconstrain","delconstraint","constraintbar","cbar","dcdisplay","degrees of freedom","fully constrained"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-make","text":"What you will make","level":2},{"id":"draw-and-constrain-the-outline","text":"Draw and constrain the outline","level":2},{"id":"add-the-sizes","text":"Add the sizes","level":2},{"id":"moving-constrained-objects","text":"Moving constrained objects","level":2},{"id":"mirror-and-trim-constrained-objects","text":"Mirror and trim constrained objects","level":2},{"id":"the-constraints","text":"The constraints","level":2},{"id":"see-select-and-remove","text":"See, select and remove","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-make\"\u003EWhat you will make\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA plate whose width you change in one place: its sides stay square, one corner stays put, its height stays half its width.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EConstraints work on lines, polylines, circles, arcs and points on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab. Each one you add is one undo step.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-and-constrain-the-outline\"\u003EDraw and constrain the outline\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EREC\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Type \u003Ccode\u003E0,0\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E100,50\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EAUTOCONSTRAIN\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Type \u003Ccode\u003EALL\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, then \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe history says &quot;4 constraints applied&quot;. Small boxes with \u003Cstrong\u003EH\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EV\u003C/strong\u003E appear beside the sides: these are the constraint bars.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EGCFIX\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Click near the lower-left corner. A box with ⊕ appears: that corner stays where it is.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EAUTOCONSTRAIN\u003C/code\u003E adds coincident constraints where ends nearly meet and horizontal or vertical ones where lines are nearly level or plumb. Type \u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E at its prompt to change how near (0.05 drawing units and 1° to start with).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-the-sizes\"\u003EAdd the sizes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EDCHORIZONTAL\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick near the lower-left corner, then near the lower-right corner.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick below the rectangle for the dimension line.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe prompt shows \u003Ccode\u003EDimension text &lt;d1=100&gt;\u003C/code\u003E. Type \u003Ccode\u003Ewidth=120\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe rectangle is now 120 wide, and the label reads \u003Ccode\u003Ewidth=120\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EDCVERTICAL\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, type \u003Ccode\u003EO\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, then click the right side.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick to the right of it, type \u003Ccode\u003Ewidth/2\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt is 60 high. The label reads \u003Ccode\u003Efx: d1=width/2\u003C/code\u003E: an expression drives it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe history now says the outline is fully constrained. To change its size, change \u003Ccode\u003Ewidth\u003C/code\u003E (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/parameters\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/parameters\"\u003EUse parameters and equations\u003C/a\u003E), or double-click the \u003Ccode\u003Ewidth=120\u003C/code\u003E label, type a new value and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EApply\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"moving-constrained-objects\"\u003EMoving constrained objects\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMove, rotate, scale, grips and the Properties panel all keep the constraints: what you moved is held, and the rest follows. When the constraints cannot follow, the edit is not made and the history says why, for example &quot;Not done: the constraints hold that object where it is&quot;. Erasing an object removes its constraints with it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mirror-and-trim-constrained-objects\"\u003EMirror and trim constrained objects\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMIRROR\u003C/code\u003E copies the constraints among the objects you mirror onto the copies. Sides (which way a tangent touches, which side a distance is measured to) are worked out again for the mirrored copy, and copied dimensions get names of their own, as a paste does. With \u003Ccode\u003EErase source objects\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003EY\u003C/code\u003E, the constraints stay on the mirrored objects. The history says how many came along.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ETRIM\u003C/code\u003E keeps a line&#039;s or an arc&#039;s constraints on what is left: a constraint on an end goes to the piece that still has that end, a rule about direction (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHorizontal\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EVertical\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EParallel\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPerpendicular\u003C/span\u003E) holds on every piece, and a dimension between the two outer ends keeps measuring them. Constraints that held the part that was cut away, or a length the cut changed (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEqual\u003C/span\u003E, an arc&#039;s sweep), go. A trimmed circle keeps its center and radius constraints. Trimmed polylines lose theirs.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-constraints\"\u003EThe constraints\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECommand\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKeeps\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EGCHORIZONTAL\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EGCVERTICAL\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ea line or segment level or plumb (\u003Ccode\u003E2P\u003C/code\u003E: two points level or plumb)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EGCPARALLEL\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EGCPERPENDICULAR\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EGCCOLLINEAR\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Etwo lines parallel, square or on one line\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EGCCOINCIDENT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Etwo points together; \u003Ccode\u003EO\u003C/code\u003E: a point on a line, circle or arc\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EGCTANGENT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ea line and a circle or arc, or two of them, touching\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EGCCONCENTRIC\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EGCEQUAL\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Etwo circles or arcs on one centre; two lengths or two radii equal\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EGCSYMMETRIC\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Etwo points, lines or circles mirrored across a line\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EGCFIX\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ea point (click near it) or a whole object (\u003Ccode\u003EO\u003C/code\u003E) where it is\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDCLINEAR\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDCHORIZONTAL\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDCVERTICAL\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDCALIGNED\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ea distance\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDCRADIUS\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDCDIAMETER\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDCANGULAR\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ea radius, a diameter, an angle between two lines or an arc&#039;s angle\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EGEOMCONSTRAINT\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMCONSTRAINT\u003C/code\u003E ask which one; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConstrain\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDimension\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EParametric\u003C/span\u003E group run them, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E there lists each. Where you click picks the part: a click near an end, the middle or a center takes that point; a click on a polyline takes the segment under it. The first object stays and the second moves to meet it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA constraint that conflicts with others, or adds nothing because the others already imply it, is refused with a message and nothing moves.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"see-select-and-remove\"\u003ESee, select and remove\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a box in a constraint bar (or a coincident square, or a dimension label) to select that constraint; the history names it. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to remove it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDELCONSTRAINT\u003C/code\u003E removes every constraint from the objects you select.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECONSTRAINTBAR\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E shows every bar, \u003Ccode\u003EH\u003C/code\u003E hides them; select objects first to show or hide theirs. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E has \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow constraint bars\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHide constraint bars\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDCDISPLAY\u003C/code\u003E shows or hides the dimensional constraints. They never plot.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDCCONVERT\u003C/code\u003E turns dimensions drawn on lines, circles and arcs into dimensional constraints.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf you have used AutoCAD&#039;s parametric drawing, the command names and prompts are the same. Constraints and parameters are saved in DWG and DXF files in a form other programs ignore; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/files#constraints-in-files\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/files\" data-hash=\"constraints-in-files\"\u003EFiles\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"What you will make A plate whose width you change in one place: its sides stay square, one corner stays put, its height stays half its width. Constraints work on lines, polylines, circles, arcs and points on the Model tab. Each one you add is one undo step. Draw and constrain the outline Type REC and press Enter. Type 0,0, Enter, 100,50, Enter. Type AUTOCONSTRAIN and press Enter. Type ALL, Enter, then Enter again. The history says \"4 constraints applied\". Small boxes with H and V appear beside the sides: these are the constraint bars. Type GCFIX and press Enter. Click near the lower-left corner. A box with ⊕ appears: that corner stays where it is. AUTOCONSTRAIN adds coincident constraints where ends nearly meet and horizontal or vertical ones where lines are nearly level or plumb. Type S at its prompt to change how near (0.05 drawing units and 1° to start with). Add the sizes Type DCHORIZONTAL and press Enter. Click near the lower-left corner, then near the lower-right corner. Click below the rectangle for the dimension line. The prompt shows Dimension text \u003Cd1=100\u003E. Type width=120 and press Enter. The rectangle is now 120 wide, and the label reads width=120. Type DCVERTICAL and press Enter, type O and press Enter, then click the right side. Click to the right of it, type width/2 and press Enter. It is 60 high. The label reads fx: d1=width/2: an expression drives it. The history now says the outline is fully constrained. To change its size, change width (see Use parameters and equations), or double-click the width=120 label, type a new value and choose Apply. Moving constrained objects Move, rotate, scale, grips and the Properties panel all keep the constraints: what you moved is held, and the rest follows. When the constraints cannot follow, the edit is not made and the history says why, for example \"Not done: the constraints hold that object where it is\". Erasing an object removes its constraints with it. Mirror and trim constrained objects MIRROR copies the constraints among the objects you mirror onto the copies. Sides (which way a tangent touches, which side a distance is measured to) are worked out again for the mirrored copy, and copied dimensions get names of their own, as a paste does. With Erase source objects Y, the constraints stay on the mirrored objects. The history says how many came along. TRIM keeps a line's or an arc's constraints on what is left: a constraint on an end goes to the piece that still has that end, a rule about direction (Horizontal, Vertical, Parallel, Perpendicular) holds on every piece, and a dimension between the two outer ends keeps measuring them. Constraints that held the part that was cut away, or a length the cut changed (Equal, an arc's sweep), go. A trimmed circle keeps its center and radius constraints. Trimmed polylines lose theirs. The constraints Command Keeps GCHORIZONTAL, GCVERTICAL a line or segment level or plumb (2P: two points level or plumb) GCPARALLEL, GCPERPENDICULAR, GCCOLLINEAR two lines parallel, square or on one line GCCOINCIDENT two points together; O: a point on a line, circle or arc GCTANGENT a line and a circle or arc, or two of them, touching GCCONCENTRIC, GCEQUAL two circles or arcs on one centre; two lengths or two radii equal GCSYMMETRIC two points, lines or circles mirrored across a line GCFIX a point (click near it) or a whole object (O) where it is DCLINEAR, DCHORIZONTAL, DCVERTICAL, DCALIGNED a distance DCRADIUS, DCDIAMETER, DCANGULAR a radius, a diameter, an angle between two lines or an arc's angle GEOMCONSTRAINT and DIMCONSTRAINT ask which one; Constrain and Dimension in the Parametric group run them, and More there lists each. Where you click picks the part: a click near an end, the middle or a center takes that point; a click on a polyline takes the segment under it. The first object stays and the second moves to meet it. A constraint that conflicts with others, or adds nothing because the others already imply it, is refused with a message and nothing moves. See, select and remove Click a box in a constraint bar (or a coincident square, or a dimension label) to select that constraint; the history names it. Press Delete to remove it. DELCONSTRAINT removes every constraint from the objects you select. CONSTRAINTBAR: S shows every bar, H hides them; select objects first to show or hide theirs. More has Show constraint bars and Hide constraint bars. DCDISPLAY shows or hides the dimensional constraints. They never plot. DCCONVERT turns dimensions drawn on lines, circles and arcs into dimensional constraints. If you have used AutoCAD's parametric drawing, the command names and prompts are the same. Constraints and parameters are saved in DWG and DXF files in a form other programs ignore; see Files.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"parameters","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Use parameters and equations","summary":"Name sizes, drive them with expressions like width / 2, and type a number on a driven size to change what drives it.","section":"tasks","order":126,"keywords":["parameters","par","parameters manager","equations","expressions","variables","two-way","design intent","user parameter","fx"],"headings":[{"id":"one-table-for-every-size","text":"One table for every size","level":2},{"id":"add-your-own","text":"Add your own","level":2},{"id":"expressions","text":"Expressions","level":2},{"id":"at-the-command-line","text":"At the command line","level":2},{"id":"what-is-refused","text":"What is refused","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"one-table-for-every-size\"\u003EOne table for every size\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EParameters\u003C/span\u003E panel (the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EParameters\u003C/span\u003E button in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EParametric\u003C/span\u003E group, or type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPARAMETERS\u003C/code\u003E) lists every dimensional constraint (\u003Ccode\u003Ed1\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Erad1\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Edia1\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eang1\u003C/code\u003E, or the name you gave it) and your own parameters together. Any name can be used in any expression.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach row has:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EName\u003C/strong\u003E: type a new one to rename it; every expression that uses it follows.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EExpression\u003C/strong\u003E: a number (\u003Ccode\u003E40\u003C/code\u003E) or an expression (\u003Ccode\u003Ewidth / 2 + gap\u003C/code\u003E). Type in it and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: the drawing follows.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EValue\u003C/strong\u003E: what it works out to. On a row an expression drives, you can type a number here too: CadCraft works out the parameter behind it. With \u003Ccode\u003Ed1 = width / 2\u003C/code\u003E, typing \u003Ccode\u003E30\u003C/code\u003E in d1&#039;s value sets \u003Ccode\u003Ewidth\u003C/code\u003E to 60.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen two parameters drive a value (\u003Ccode\u003Ewidth / 2 + gap\u003C/code\u003E), a dialog asks which one to change. Every change is one undo step (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-your-own\"\u003EAdd your own\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EParameters\u003C/span\u003E panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType a name (\u003Ccode\u003Egap\u003C/code\u003E) and a value (\u003Ccode\u003E10\u003C/code\u003E), then choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUse it: in d1&#039;s expression, type \u003Ccode\u003Ewidth / 2 + gap\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENames start with a letter or \u003Ccode\u003E_\u003C/code\u003E and hold letters, digits and \u003Ccode\u003E_\u003C/code\u003E. Function names, \u003Ccode\u003Epi\u003C/code\u003E and unit names cannot be names.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"expressions\"\u003EExpressions\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWrite\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeans\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E+ - * / ^ %\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eadd, subtract, multiply, divide, power, remainder\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E( )\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Egrouping\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Esin cos tan asin acos atan atan2\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Etrigonometry in degrees\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Esqrt pow exp ln log abs sign\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eroots, powers, logarithms\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eceil floor int round min max\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Erounding and limits\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eif(a &gt; b, x, y)\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ea choice\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Epi\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E3.14159…\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENumbers may carry a unit: \u003Ccode\u003E25mm\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1in\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E2&#039;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E90deg\u003C/code\u003E. Lengths with a unit are turned into millimetres, so use units only in drawings measured in millimetres; elsewhere type plain numbers.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"at-the-command-line\"\u003EAt the command line\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E-PARAMETERS\u003C/code\u003E does the same without the panel: \u003Ccode\u003EN\u003C/code\u003E new, \u003Ccode\u003EE\u003C/code\u003E edit (a dimensional constraint too), \u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E rename, \u003Ccode\u003ED\u003C/code\u003E delete, \u003Ccode\u003E?\u003C/code\u003E list.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-refused\"\u003EWhat is refused\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAn expression that uses itself, directly or through others: &quot;width, d1 use each other.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA value a size cannot take: a negative length, an angle over 180°.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA value the drawing cannot take with its other constraints: nothing changes, and the message names the constraints in the way.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDeleting a parameter something uses: the panel offers to write its value into them first. A dimensional constraint&#039;s name is deleted by deleting the constraint.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"One table for every size The Parameters panel (the Parameters button in the Parametric group, or type PARAMETERS) lists every dimensional constraint (d1, rad1, dia1, ang1, or the name you gave it) and your own parameters together. Any name can be used in any expression. Each row has: Name: type a new one to rename it; every expression that uses it follows. Expression: a number (40) or an expression (width / 2 + gap). Type in it and press Enter: the drawing follows. Value: what it works out to. On a row an expression drives, you can type a number here too: CadCraft works out the parameter behind it. With d1 = width / 2, typing 30 in d1's value sets width to 60. When two parameters drive a value (width / 2 + gap), a dialog asks which one to change. Every change is one undo step (Ctrl+Z). Add your own Open the Parameters panel. Type a name (gap) and a value (10), then choose Add. Use it: in d1's expression, type width / 2 + gap and press Enter. Names start with a letter or _ and hold letters, digits and _. Function names, pi and unit names cannot be names. Expressions Write Means + - * / ^ % add, subtract, multiply, divide, power, remainder ( ) grouping sin cos tan asin acos atan atan2 trigonometry in degrees sqrt pow exp ln log abs sign roots, powers, logarithms ceil floor int round min max rounding and limits if(a \u003E b, x, y) a choice pi 3.14159… Numbers may carry a unit: 25mm, 1in, 2', 90deg. Lengths with a unit are turned into millimetres, so use units only in drawings measured in millimetres; elsewhere type plain numbers. At the command line -PARAMETERS does the same without the panel: N new, E edit (a dimensional constraint too), R rename, D delete, ? list. What is refused An expression that uses itself, directly or through others: \"width, d1 use each other.\" A value a size cannot take: a negative length, an angle over 180°. A value the drawing cannot take with its other constraints: nothing changes, and the message names the constraints in the way. Deleting a parameter something uses: the panel offers to write its value into them first. A dimensional constraint's name is deleted by deleting the constraint.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"solidworks-sketches","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Move sketches to and from other part modelers","summary":"Write a macro that rebuilds your drawing as one constrained sketch in a part modeler, and open a sketch exported from one.","section":"tasks","order":127,"keywords":["sketch","macro","swb","vba","export","part modeler","global variables","equations","relations","cadcraft-sw-export","swbout"],"headings":[{"id":"send-a-drawing-as-a-sketch","text":"Send a drawing as a sketch","level":2},{"id":"open-a-sketch-from-a-part","text":"Open a sketch from a part","level":2}],"html":"\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-warning\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003EWarning\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESolidWorks is not available where CadCraft is built, so neither direction has been tested in SolidWorks yet. What CadCraft writes is checked for structure only. Check the result before you rely on it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"send-a-drawing-as-a-sketch\"\u003ESend a drawing as a sketch\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EConstrain and dimension the drawing (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/constraints\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/constraints\"\u003EConstrain a drawing\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EParametric\u003C/span\u003E group, open \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMacro for SolidWorks (.swb)\u003C/span\u003E, or type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESWBOUT\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA file named after the drawing, ending \u003Ccode\u003E.swb\u003C/code\u003E, downloads.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn SolidWorks, choose \u003Cstrong\u003ETools &gt; Macro &gt; Run\u003C/strong\u003E and pick that file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe macro makes a new part from your default part template, opens a sketch on the Front Plane and draws the model&#039;s lines, circles, arcs, points and polylines in it, with:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eyour constraints as sketch relations,\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eyour dimensional constraints as dimensions with the same names (\u003Ccode\u003Ed1@Sketch1\u003C/code\u003E),\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eyour own parameters as global variables,\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eevery expression as an equation.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe part uses the drawing&#039;s length unit. Text, blocks, hatches, dimensions and sheets are not part of the sketch; the history lists what was left out. Expressions with no equation form there (\u003Ccode\u003Eif\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Emin\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Emax\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eround\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E%\u003C/code\u003E and a few others) are written as their current value, and the history says which. Anything the macro cannot make is written to a log file next to the macro, and a message sums up at the end.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-a-sketch-from-a-part\"\u003EOpen a sketch from a part\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn SolidWorks, run the CadCraft export macro (download it in the PartCraft app: File &gt; Export &gt; Export macro to bring parts back) on the part. It writes a \u003Ccode\u003E.json\u003C/code\u003E file in the \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft-sw-export\u003C/code\u003E format next to the part.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn CadCraft, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen\u003C/span\u003E and pick that \u003Ccode\u003E.json\u003C/code\u003E file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhen the part has several sketches, choose one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe sketch becomes the drawing, in millimetres: its lines, arcs, circles and points, its relations as constraints, its dimensions as dimensional constraints named as they were (\u003Ccode\u003ED1\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ED2\u003C/code\u003E …), its global variables as parameters, and the equations on its dimensions as their expressions. The history lists what was not carried over, such as relations CadCraft does not have. Where the sketch sits in the part is not kept: it is drawn flat, as seen on its plane.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"SolidWorks is not available where CadCraft is built, so neither direction has been tested in SolidWorks yet. What CadCraft writes is checked for structure only. Check the result before you rely on it. Send a drawing as a sketch Constrain and dimension the drawing (see Constrain a drawing). In the Parametric group, open More and choose Macro for SolidWorks (.swb), or type SWBOUT. A file named after the drawing, ending .swb, downloads. In SolidWorks, choose Tools \u003E Macro \u003E Run and pick that file. The macro makes a new part from your default part template, opens a sketch on the Front Plane and draws the model's lines, circles, arcs, points and polylines in it, with: your constraints as sketch relations, your dimensional constraints as dimensions with the same names (d1@Sketch1), your own parameters as global variables, every expression as an equation. The part uses the drawing's length unit. Text, blocks, hatches, dimensions and sheets are not part of the sketch; the history lists what was left out. Expressions with no equation form there (if, min, max, round, % and a few others) are written as their current value, and the history says which. Anything the macro cannot make is written to a log file next to the macro, and a message sums up at the end. Open a sketch from a part In SolidWorks, run the CadCraft export macro (download it in the PartCraft app: File \u003E Export \u003E Export macro to bring parts back) on the part. It writes a .json file in the cadcraft-sw-export format next to the part. In CadCraft, choose Open and pick that .json file. When the part has several sketches, choose one. The sketch becomes the drawing, in millimetres: its lines, arcs, circles and points, its relations as constraints, its dimensions as dimensional constraints named as they were (D1, D2 …), its global variables as parameters, and the equations on its dimensions as their expressions. The history lists what was not carried over, such as relations CadCraft does not have. Where the sketch sits in the part is not kept: it is drawn flat, as seen on its plane.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"3d-solids","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Model in 3D","summary":"See the drawing in 3D on the 3D tab and make simple solids from it: extrude (with a taper or along a path), revolve, push and pull faces, boxes, cylinders and wedges, join, subtract and intersect them, then move, copy, rotate and erase them, and place flat 2D views of them on a sheet.","section":"tasks","order":128,"keywords":["3d","three d","solid","solids","3dsolid","region","body","acis","sat","sab","solids from other programs","open 3d drawing","extrude","ext","revolve","rev","sweep","taper","path","presspull","press pull","push pull","box","cylinder","cyl","wedge","3dorbit","orbit","view","vscurrent","visual style","shaded","hidden line","wireframe","x-ray","xray","iso","perspective","parallel","glb","gltf","obj","stl","export3d","stlout","objout","glbout","3dface","view cube","union","uni","subtract","su","intersect","in","boolean","combine","join solids","viewbase","flatshot","flat view","2d view","drawing view","front view","top view","side view","hidden lines"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-make","text":"What you will make","level":2},{"id":"extrude-a-shape","text":"Extrude a shape","level":2},{"id":"revolve-and-sweep","text":"Revolve and sweep","level":2},{"id":"push-and-pull-a-face","text":"Push and pull a face","level":2},{"id":"boxes-cylinders-and-wedges","text":"Boxes, cylinders and wedges","level":2},{"id":"move-copy-rotate-and-erase-solids","text":"Move, copy, rotate and erase solids","level":2},{"id":"join-subtract-and-intersect-solids","text":"Join, subtract and intersect solids","level":2},{"id":"flat-views-on-a-sheet","text":"Flat views on a sheet","level":2},{"id":"look-around","text":"Look around","level":2},{"id":"solids-from-other-programs","text":"Solids from other programs","level":2},{"id":"save-and-export","text":"Save and export","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-make\"\u003EWhat you will make\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA 1000 × 600 room footprint extruded into a block 400 high, a face pulled up, and a cylinder beside it — then the model saved with the drawing and exported for a 3D viewer or a printer.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E tab sits beside \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E under the drawing. It shows the model-space drawing lying on the ground in its layer colors (layers that are off or frozen stay hidden) and the solids you make. The first time it opens, the 3D view loads; after that it switches at once.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"extrude-a-shape\"\u003EExtrude a shape\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab, type \u003Ccode\u003EREC\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Type \u003Ccode\u003E0,0\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E@1000,600\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E tab. The toolbar shows the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E group in place of the drawing groups.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExtrude\u003C/span\u003E (or type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXTRUDE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EEXT\u003C/code\u003E). Click inside the rectangle, then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E400\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The history says &quot;Extruded 1 solid, 400 high&quot; with its volume.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClosed polylines (arcs included), rectangles, polygons, circles and ellipses extrude; lines and open polylines do not, and the history says why. You can also select the shapes on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab first and type \u003Ccode\u003EEXTRUDE\u003C/code\u003E there. Instead of typing the height you can click it: the outline follows the cursor up and down. A negative height goes down. Type \u003Ccode\u003ED\u003C/code\u003E at the height prompt to delete the 2D shapes after extruding (\u003Ccode\u003ED\u003C/code\u003E again keeps them).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAt the height prompt, \u003Ccode\u003ET\u003C/code\u003E (Taper angle) asks for an angle in degrees: the sides lean in by it, so the top is smaller (a negative angle makes it larger); an angle too steep for the shape is refused. \u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E (Path) sweeps the shapes along a path instead (see below).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"revolve-and-sweep\"\u003ERevolve and sweep\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EREVOLVE\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EREV\u003C/code\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERevolve about an axis\u003C/span\u003E): select closed shapes, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, then click the two ends of the axis (or type \u003Ccode\u003EX\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003EY\u003C/code\u003E for the drawing&#039;s axis through 0,0), then type an angle or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for all the way round. The shape turns about the axis and stands up out of the ground: draw half a vase&#039;s outline beside a line, revolve it about the line. The shape may touch the axis but not cross it. A full turn has 48 steps.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESWEEP\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESweep along a path\u003C/span\u003E, or \u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E at EXTRUDE&#039;s height prompt): select closed shapes (the profile), press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, then click the path: a line, polyline, arc, circle or ellipse. The profile stands up across the path, its middle on it, and runs along it; corners are mitered, and a closed path makes a ring. A small rectangle swept along a room&#039;s outline makes a skirting; a circle along a polyline makes a pipe.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"push-and-pull-a-face\"\u003EPush and pull a face\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPress/pull\u003C/span\u003E (or type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPRESSPULL\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the top face of the block.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E100\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or move the cursor and click. The block is 500 high.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"boxes-cylinders-and-wedges\"\u003EBoxes, cylinders and wedges\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EBOX\u003C/code\u003E: click or type two corners, then a height.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECYLINDER\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003ECYL\u003C/code\u003E): the center, the radius, then a height. Cylinders and extruded circles have 48 sides, shaded smooth.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EWEDGE\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EWE\u003C/code\u003E): two corners and a height. It is full height at the first corner and slopes to nothing at the other.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn the 3D tab a click lands on the face or edge under the cursor, else on the ground, so a box can stand on another solid.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"move-copy-rotate-and-erase-solids\"\u003EMove, copy, rotate and erase solids\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn the 3D tab, a click selects a solid; drag left to right for a window, right to left for a crossing. Then:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMOVE\u003C/code\u003E: click a base point and where it goes, or type \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;100,0,0&gt;\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003E@100,0\u003C/code\u003E) for a distance along X, Y and Z.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECOPY\u003C/code\u003E: the same, leaving the original.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EROTATE\u003C/code\u003E: type an angle (it turns about the middle of the selection, around Z), or click the center, a start point and the angle.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EERASE\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"join-subtract-and-intersect-solids\"\u003EJoin, subtract and intersect solids\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E in the 3D toolbar, or type the command:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EUNION\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EUNI\u003C/code\u003E): select the solids, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: they become one solid.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESUBTRACT\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003ESU\u003C/code\u003E): select the solids to keep and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, then the ones to take away and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. A hole through a block: draw a cylinder through it, then subtract the cylinder.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EINTERSECT\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EIN\u003C/code\u003E): select the solids, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: only what they all have in common is left.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe history says the volume of the result. Each is one undo step. The first one you run loads the 3D boolean engine (about 0.5 MB; included in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E). Only closed solids can be combined: a solid with open edges (from another program&#039;s loose 3D faces) is refused with that reason. The result is one solid; curved faces stay made of flat facets, as the cylinder was.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDouble-click a solid to edit its faces and edges on their own; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E closes it again. Every 3D change is one undo step in the same history as the drawing: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes the last thing you did on either tab.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"flat-views-on-a-sheet\"\u003EFlat views on a sheet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen a sheet tab (or stay on \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E), then type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EVIEWBASE\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003EFLATSHOT\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the view: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for the front, or \u003Ccode\u003ET\u003C/code\u003E (top), \u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E (right), \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E (left), \u003Ccode\u003EBA\u003C/code\u003E (back), \u003Ccode\u003EBO\u003C/code\u003E (bottom), \u003Ccode\u003EI\u003C/code\u003E (isometric).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E shows hidden lines, dashed; \u003Ccode\u003EH\u003C/code\u003E leaves them out.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes the scale CadCraft suggests: on a sheet, the largest standard scale (such as 1:20) at which the view fits in under half the paper. Or type one: \u003Ccode\u003E1:50\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E2:1\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick, or type, where the view&#039;s center goes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe view is one block reference, \u003Ccode\u003EVIEW-FRONT-1\u003C/code\u003E and so on: visible edges on layer VIEW-VISIBLE (0.5 mm), hidden ones on VIEW-HIDDEN (dashed, 0.25 mm). Move it, dimension it and plot it like any other drawing. It is a picture of the solids when you made it: after changing them, erase the view and run \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EVIEWBASE\u003C/code\u003E again. For more views, run it once per view.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"look-around\"\u003ELook around\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDrag with the wheel to orbit, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E + wheel drag or the right button to pan, and turn the wheel to zoom at the cursor. On a trackpad, two fingers orbit and a pinch zooms. A right click without dragging is \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, as on the other tabs. \u003Ccode\u003E3DORBIT\u003C/code\u003E lets the left button orbit until you press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EViews\u003C/span\u003E (or \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EVIEW\u003C/code\u003E): Iso, Top, Bottom, Front, Back, Left, Right, Perspective or Parallel, and Zoom to everything.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStyle\u003C/span\u003E (or \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EVSCURRENT\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EVS\u003C/code\u003E): Shaded, Shaded with edges, Hidden line, Wireframe or X-ray.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPoints you click snap to the solids (corners, edges, faces) and to the 2D drawing on the ground: the ends and middles of its lines and arcs, and points along them. The 2D lines are never selected on the 3D tab. When a command asks for 2D objects (EXTRUDE, REVOLVE, SWEEP), a click picks one, a drag left to right takes what is wholly inside the window, and a drag right to left takes what it crosses, as on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA view cube sits at the top right of the 3D tab: click a face, edge or corner to look from there, drag it to orbit, and the house button goes back to the iso view. That is \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAutomatic\u003C/span\u003E, the start of \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECorner indicator\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/settings\"\u003ECadCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E. Set to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView cube\u003C/span\u003E, the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab shows a cube too, looking from the top; clicking any other side opens the 3D tab looking from there. Set to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAxis icon\u003C/span\u003E, both tabs show the axis marker instead.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"solids-from-other-programs\"\u003ESolids from other programs\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA drawing from another program can hold 3D solids, regions and bodies (3DSOLID, REGION, BODY: the other program keeps their shape as ACIS data). CadCraft shows the ones in model space on the 3D tab, in their layer&#039;s colour and with their own edges, the first time you open the tab; the command line then says how many were drawn.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThey show as they are: you cannot select, move or change them. Layers that are off or frozen hide them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESaving as DWG writes each one back unchanged, in any DWG version, so the other program gets them again. DXF files do not keep them: CadCraft writes them into DWG only, and says so when you save as DXF.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrawn: flat faces, and faces on cylinders, cones, spheres, tori and simple spline surfaces, with straight, round and spline edges. A face on any other kind of surface, or one CadCraft cannot lay out, is not guessed at: it is left out and listed, for example &quot;Not drawn yet (3D solids): 2 faces on spline-surface (sweepsur), a surface not drawn yet.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESolids inside blocks are not shown yet; &quot;About this file&quot; counts them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECurved faces are drawn as small flat pieces, about 7.5° apart, so a curved solid looks a little faceted up close.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"save-and-export\"\u003ESave and export\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESaving keeps the 3D model, in DWG and in DXF. Every face goes in as a 3D face on the layer \u003Ccode\u003ECC-3D\u003C/code\u003E, so other programs show the solids, and CadCraft keeps its own exact copy in the file, which it reads back when you open the drawing again, with the 3D view as it was (camera, projection and visual style). 3D faces made by other programs come in on the 3D tab as loose faces, kept together as one group. A 3D face inside a block comes in once for each reference to that block in the model, placed and turned as the reference is; in the block itself, and on a sheet, a 3D face is drawn flat, as its outline.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E in the 3D group downloads the solids alone: GLB for viewers and the web (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EGLBOUT\u003C/code\u003E), OBJ (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EOBJOUT\u003C/code\u003E) or STL for 3D printing, in millimeters (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESTLOUT\u003C/code\u003E). \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXPORT3D\u003C/code\u003E asks which.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe 3D model is real size. A drawing without units counts as millimeters, so \u003Ccode\u003E1000\u003C/code\u003E is one meter. If you change the drawing&#039;s units later (Properties, with nothing selected), the solids are scaled with them so they stay on the drawing, and the history says so. One \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes the change of units and the scaling together.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"What you will make A 1000 × 600 room footprint extruded into a block 400 high, a face pulled up, and a cylinder beside it — then the model saved with the drawing and exported for a 3D viewer or a printer. The 3D tab sits beside Model under the drawing. It shows the model-space drawing lying on the ground in its layer colors (layers that are off or frozen stay hidden) and the solids you make. The first time it opens, the 3D view loads; after that it switches at once. Extrude a shape On the Model tab, type REC and press Enter. Type 0,0, Enter, @1000,600, Enter. Click the 3D tab. The toolbar shows the 3D group in place of the drawing groups. Click Extrude (or type EXTRUDE, EXT). Click inside the rectangle, then press Enter. Type 400 and press Enter. The history says \"Extruded 1 solid, 400 high\" with its volume. Closed polylines (arcs included), rectangles, polygons, circles and ellipses extrude; lines and open polylines do not, and the history says why. You can also select the shapes on the Model tab first and type EXTRUDE there. Instead of typing the height you can click it: the outline follows the cursor up and down. A negative height goes down. Type D at the height prompt to delete the 2D shapes after extruding (D again keeps them). At the height prompt, T (Taper angle) asks for an angle in degrees: the sides lean in by it, so the top is smaller (a negative angle makes it larger); an angle too steep for the shape is refused. P (Path) sweeps the shapes along a path instead (see below). Revolve and sweep REVOLVE (REV, or More › Revolve about an axis): select closed shapes, press Enter, then click the two ends of the axis (or type X or Y for the drawing's axis through 0,0), then type an angle or press Enter for all the way round. The shape turns about the axis and stands up out of the ground: draw half a vase's outline beside a line, revolve it about the line. The shape may touch the axis but not cross it. A full turn has 48 steps. SWEEP (or More › Sweep along a path, or P at EXTRUDE's height prompt): select closed shapes (the profile), press Enter, then click the path: a line, polyline, arc, circle or ellipse. The profile stands up across the path, its middle on it, and runs along it; corners are mitered, and a closed path makes a ring. A small rectangle swept along a room's outline makes a skirting; a circle along a polyline makes a pipe. Push and pull a face Click Press/pull (or type PRESSPULL). Click the top face of the block. Type 100 and press Enter, or move the cursor and click. The block is 500 high. Boxes, cylinders and wedges BOX: click or type two corners, then a height. CYLINDER (CYL): the center, the radius, then a height. Cylinders and extruded circles have 48 sides, shaded smooth. WEDGE (WE): two corners and a height. It is full height at the first corner and slopes to nothing at the other. On the 3D tab a click lands on the face or edge under the cursor, else on the ground, so a box can stand on another solid. Move, copy, rotate and erase solids On the 3D tab, a click selects a solid; drag left to right for a window, right to left for a crossing. Then: MOVE: click a base point and where it goes, or type \u003C100,0,0\u003E (or @100,0) for a distance along X, Y and Z. COPY: the same, leaving the original. ROTATE: type an angle (it turns about the middle of the selection, around Z), or click the center, a start point and the angle. ERASE or Delete. Join, subtract and intersect solids Open More in the 3D toolbar, or type the command: UNION (UNI): select the solids, press Enter: they become one solid. SUBTRACT (SU): select the solids to keep and press Enter, then the ones to take away and press Enter. A hole through a block: draw a cylinder through it, then subtract the cylinder. INTERSECT (IN): select the solids, press Enter: only what they all have in common is left. The history says the volume of the result. Each is one undo step. The first one you run loads the 3D boolean engine (about 0.5 MB; included in Save for offline). Only closed solids can be combined: a solid with open edges (from another program's loose 3D faces) is refused with that reason. The result is one solid; curved faces stay made of flat facets, as the cylinder was. Double-click a solid to edit its faces and edges on their own; Esc closes it again. Every 3D change is one undo step in the same history as the drawing: Ctrl+Z undoes the last thing you did on either tab. Flat views on a sheet Open a sheet tab (or stay on Model), then type VIEWBASE (or FLATSHOT). Choose the view: Enter for the front, or T (top), R (right), L (left), BA (back), BO (bottom), I (isometric). Enter shows hidden lines, dashed; H leaves them out. Enter takes the scale CadCraft suggests: on a sheet, the largest standard scale (such as 1:20) at which the view fits in under half the paper. Or type one: 1:50, 2:1. Click, or type, where the view's center goes. The view is one block reference, VIEW-FRONT-1 and so on: visible edges on layer VIEW-VISIBLE (0.5 mm), hidden ones on VIEW-HIDDEN (dashed, 0.25 mm). Move it, dimension it and plot it like any other drawing. It is a picture of the solids when you made it: after changing them, erase the view and run VIEWBASE again. For more views, run it once per view. Look around Drag with the wheel to orbit, Shift + wheel drag or the right button to pan, and turn the wheel to zoom at the cursor. On a trackpad, two fingers orbit and a pinch zooms. A right click without dragging is Enter, as on the other tabs. 3DORBIT lets the left button orbit until you press Enter or Esc. Views (or VIEW): Iso, Top, Bottom, Front, Back, Left, Right, Perspective or Parallel, and Zoom to everything. Style (or VSCURRENT, VS): Shaded, Shaded with edges, Hidden line, Wireframe or X-ray. Points you click snap to the solids (corners, edges, faces) and to the 2D drawing on the ground: the ends and middles of its lines and arcs, and points along them. The 2D lines are never selected on the 3D tab. When a command asks for 2D objects (EXTRUDE, REVOLVE, SWEEP), a click picks one, a drag left to right takes what is wholly inside the window, and a drag right to left takes what it crosses, as on the Model tab. A view cube sits at the top right of the 3D tab: click a face, edge or corner to look from there, drag it to orbit, and the house button goes back to the iso view. That is Automatic, the start of Corner indicator in CadCraft settings. Set to View cube, the Model tab shows a cube too, looking from the top; clicking any other side opens the 3D tab looking from there. Set to Axis icon, both tabs show the axis marker instead. Solids from other programs A drawing from another program can hold 3D solids, regions and bodies (3DSOLID, REGION, BODY: the other program keeps their shape as ACIS data). CadCraft shows the ones in model space on the 3D tab, in their layer's colour and with their own edges, the first time you open the tab; the command line then says how many were drawn. They show as they are: you cannot select, move or change them. Layers that are off or frozen hide them. Saving as DWG writes each one back unchanged, in any DWG version, so the other program gets them again. DXF files do not keep them: CadCraft writes them into DWG only, and says so when you save as DXF. Drawn: flat faces, and faces on cylinders, cones, spheres, tori and simple spline surfaces, with straight, round and spline edges. A face on any other kind of surface, or one CadCraft cannot lay out, is not guessed at: it is left out and listed, for example \"Not drawn yet (3D solids): 2 faces on spline-surface (sweepsur), a surface not drawn yet.\" Solids inside blocks are not shown yet; \"About this file\" counts them. Curved faces are drawn as small flat pieces, about 7.5° apart, so a curved solid looks a little faceted up close. Save and export Saving keeps the 3D model, in DWG and in DXF. Every face goes in as a 3D face on the layer CC-3D, so other programs show the solids, and CadCraft keeps its own exact copy in the file, which it reads back when you open the drawing again, with the 3D view as it was (camera, projection and visual style). 3D faces made by other programs come in on the 3D tab as loose faces, kept together as one group. A 3D face inside a block comes in once for each reference to that block in the model, placed and turned as the reference is; in the block itself, and on a sheet, a 3D face is drawn flat, as its outline. Export in the 3D group downloads the solids alone: GLB for viewers and the web (GLBOUT), OBJ (OBJOUT) or STL for 3D printing, in millimeters (STLOUT). EXPORT3D asks which. The 3D model is real size. A drawing without units counts as millimeters, so 1000 is one meter. If you change the drawing's units later (Properties, with nothing selected), the solids are scaled with them so they stay on the drawing, and the history says so. One Ctrl+Z undoes the change of units and the scaling together.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"plot-pdf","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Plot to PDF","summary":"Make a PDF of a sheet at full size, or of the model at a scale or fitted to the paper, print it from the browser or from Studio, or save it as a PNG or SVG.","section":"tasks","order":130,"keywords":["plot","print","printer","copies","png","svg","picture","pdf","publish","exportpdf","paper","scale","monochrome","grayscale","black and white","line weight","pen","page setup","plot window","plot style","plot style table","ctb","color dependent","screening","plotstyles","stylesmanager"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-make","text":"What you will make","level":2},{"id":"plot-a-sheet","text":"Plot a sheet","level":2},{"id":"every-sheet-in-one-pdf","text":"Every sheet in one PDF","level":2},{"id":"plot-the-model","text":"Plot the model","level":2},{"id":"plot-style-tables","text":"Plot style tables","level":2},{"id":"print","text":"Print","level":2},{"id":"picture-and-svg-files","text":"Picture and SVG files","level":2},{"id":"pictures","text":"Pictures","level":2},{"id":"settings-that-are-remembered","text":"Settings that are remembered","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-make\"\u003EWhat you will make\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA PDF of a sheet at full size, then a quick PDF of the model at 1:100, and a printout.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe PDF is made inside your browser; nothing is uploaded. It is a vector PDF: lines stay sharp at any zoom, and text can be searched.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"plot-a-sheet\"\u003EPlot a sheet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the sheet&#039;s tab under the drawing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command P\"\u003E⌘P\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+P\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlot\u003C/span\u003E, or type \u003Ccode\u003EPLOT\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlot to PDF\u003C/span\u003E dialog opens. It says which sheet it plots and on what paper, &quot;at full size … as you see it&quot;. The preview on the right shows the sheet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlot style\u003C/span\u003E, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMonochrome (black)\u003C/span\u003E for black lines, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EColour (as drawn)\u003C/span\u003E to keep the colors, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGrayscale\u003C/span\u003E, or a table of your own (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/plot-pdf#plot-style-tables\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/plot-pdf\" data-hash=\"plot-style-tables\"\u003EPlot style tables\u003C/a\u003E). The sheet keeps this choice: it is saved with the drawing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELine weight\u003C/span\u003E, choose a pen width from 0.13 mm to 0.70 mm. Lines with the default lineweight print at this width; lines with their own lineweight (layer or object, or their color&#039;s pen in the table) print at that.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload PDF\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe browser downloads a PDF named after the drawing and the sheet, for example \u003Ccode\u003EDrawing1-A-101 Plan.pdf\u003C/code\u003E. The history reports its size in bytes and its paper size in millimeters.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA sheet always plots at 1:1 on its own paper. To change the paper, change it on the sheet itself (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/sheets-and-viewports\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/sheets-and-viewports\"\u003ESet up a sheet\u003C/a\u003E). Layers set not to plot are left out, and so are viewport frames.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"every-sheet-in-one-pdf\"\u003EEvery sheet in one PDF\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith two or more sheets, the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlot to PDF\u003C/span\u003E dialog has \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAll sheets in one PDF\u003C/span\u003E; or type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPUBLISH\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes every sheet; \u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E (Some) asks for their names, separated by commas. One PDF downloads with a page per sheet, in tab order, each at its own paper size, and a bookmark per sheet with its name. Each sheet plots with its own plot style table; a sheet with none chosen uses the one you last chose in the dialog, and the pen width you last chose.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"plot-the-model\"\u003EPlot the model\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command P\"\u003E⌘P\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+P\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe dialog says it plots the model onto a sheet of paper, and shows more settings:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArea\u003C/span\u003E, choose what to plot:\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEverything (extents)\u003C/span\u003E: the whole drawing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWhat is on screen\u003C/span\u003E: the area you see in the drawing now.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EA window…\u003C/span\u003E: then click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPick\u003C/span\u003E, click two corners on the drawing, and the dialog comes back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaper\u003C/span\u003E, choose a size: ISO A4 to A0, Letter, Legal, Tabloid and ANSI C and D, or ARCH C, D and E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrientation\u003C/span\u003E, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELandscape\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPortrait\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale\u003C/span\u003E, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit to paper\u003C/span\u003E or a scale such as \u003Ccode\u003E1:100\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnder the settings, a line reports the scale and how big the area comes out on the paper. If it is bigger than the paper, it says so: the edges are cut off. Choose a smaller scale, a bigger paper, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit to paper\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload PDF\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA model plot leaves a 5 mm margin round the edge, because most printers cannot print to the edge. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit to paper\u003C/span\u003E gives an odd scale (the history and the info line say &quot;(fitted)&quot;); choose a standard scale when the drawing must measure true.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EScales read in real units. For 1:100 to be right, set \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnits\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECurrent settings\u003C/span\u003E to what one drawing unit is. A unitless drawing is taken to be in millimeters.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"plot-style-tables\"\u003EPlot style tables\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA plot style table says what each of the 255 standard drawing colors prints as, the way a color-dependent plot style table (CTB) does in other CAD programs. Three are built in:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETable\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EPrints\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EColour (as drawn)\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEvery object in its own color.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMonochrome (black)\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEvery color black: the usual choice for drawings.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGrayscale\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEvery color as its gray, so colors stay apart on a black-and-white printer.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo make your own, for example red lines thick and black, everything else thin:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlot to PDF\u003C/span\u003E dialog, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit tables…\u003C/span\u003E (or type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPLOTSTYLES\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew\u003C/span\u003E, and type a name such as \u003Ccode\u003Eoffice\u003C/code\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EName\u003C/span\u003E. To start from a built-in table instead, choose it in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETable\u003C/span\u003E and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a color in the grid. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click selects a range of colors, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click adds one more, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect every colour\u003C/span\u003E takes them all.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet what the selected colors print as: The table itself has two more settings: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELineweights\u003C/span\u003E, the widths it knows (a printed line goes to the nearest of them), and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinetype scale\u003C/span\u003E, a factor on the dashes of every linetype that is not an ISO one.\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPrints in\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIts own colour\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBlack\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAnother colour…\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAs a gray\u003C/span\u003E: the color as its gray.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInk (%)\u003C/span\u003E: 100 is full ink, 50 is half way to white (screening), for background lines.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELineweight\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIts own lineweight\u003C/span\u003E keeps the object&#039;s or layer&#039;s lineweight; a value prints every line of that color at that width.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinetype\u003C/span\u003E: print every line of that color with another linetype (for example everything red dashed); \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUse the object&#039;s linetype\u003C/span\u003E leaves them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELine end\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELine join\u003C/span\u003E: butt, square or round ends, and miter, bevel or round corners (round, as before, when you set nothing).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFill style\u003C/span\u003E: a solid fill of that color prints as hatch lines (horizontal or vertical bars, slants, crosshatch, diamonds, square dots) instead of ink, for printers and plans where flat ink is too heavy.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDone\u003C/span\u003E. The table is kept in the drawing, and the sheet (or the model) now plots with it. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECancel\u003C/span\u003E drops the changes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA colour with its own pen shows a dot in its corner, and a dot at the bottom when it sets a lineweight. An object with a true color (not one of the 255) uses the pen of the nearest standard color.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe drawing keeps its tables and each sheet&#039;s choice when you save as DWG (2000 to 2018) or DXF (R12 or 2000), and opens with them again. Other programs see the name in each sheet&#039;s page setup (\u003Ccode\u003Eoffice.ctb\u003C/code\u003E), not the table itself. A drawing from another program whose page setup names a table such as \u003Ccode\u003Emonochrome.ctb\u003C/code\u003E plots with the built-in table of that name; for any other name the dialog says the drawing does not have it, and plots the drawing colors until you choose a table or make one with that name.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo use a table in another drawing, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlot style tables\u003C/span\u003E (it saves \u003Ccode\u003Eoffice.plotstyle.json\u003C/code\u003E), then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport…\u003C/span\u003E in the other drawing. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport…\u003C/span\u003E also takes a \u003Ccode\u003E.ctb\u003C/code\u003E file from another program: it is read from the published description of the format and \u003Cstrong\u003Ehas not yet been checked against a real file\u003C/strong\u003E. The colour, screening and lineweight of each color are read; grayscale, pen numbers, linetypes, line ends and joins and fill styles are not, and a named table (\u003Ccode\u003E.stb\u003C/code\u003E) is refused. Compare the first plot with what you expect, and send us the file if it is wrong.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"print\"\u003EPrint\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIn the browser\u003C/strong\u003E, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPrint…\u003C/span\u003E. CadCraft makes the PDF and opens the browser&#039;s own print dialog for it. Choose &quot;Actual size&quot; or 100% (not &quot;Fit&quot;) there to keep the scale. If the browser will not print from the page, the PDF opens in a new tab instead: print it from there with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command P\"\u003E⌘P\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+P\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou can also click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen to print\u003C/span\u003E: the PDF opens in a new browser tab, and you print it from there.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ESafari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESafari may show the PDF and wait for you to use \u003Cstrong\u003EFile\u003C/strong\u003E, then \u003Cstrong\u003EPrint\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf your browser blocks the new tab, CadCraft downloads the PDF instead and says so in the history. Allow pop-ups for the site to open it in a tab next time.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIn CadCraft Studio\u003C/strong\u003E, the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlot to PDF\u003C/span\u003E dialog lists the printers of the computer. Choose the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPrinter\u003C/span\u003E, the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaper in the printer\u003C/span\u003E and the number of \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopies\u003C/span\u003E, then click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPrint…\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESame as the plot\u003C/span\u003E picks the printer&#039;s paper that is the size of the sheet (A3 for an A3 sheet); when the printer has none, it uses its own paper and fits the sheet to it. On Windows the sheet is sent as a 300 dpi picture, because Windows cannot print a PDF without a PDF program; on Linux and macOS the PDF itself goes to the system&#039;s print service. Nothing leaves the computer.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"picture-and-svg-files\"\u003EPicture and SVG files\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload PNG\u003C/span\u003E saves the plotted sheet as a picture at 300 dpi (lowered only when a very large sheet would be bigger than the browser can make; the history says so). \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload SVG\u003C/span\u003E saves it as a scalable drawing in millimeters, with the lines and text of the plot. Both come from the same plot as the PDF, with the same plot style, line weight and area. Pictures placed in the drawing are in the PDF only; the history says how many were left out.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"pictures\"\u003EPictures\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPictures print at their own resolution, under the lines. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPictures\u003C/span\u003E in the dialog caps them at 600, 300 or 150 dpi for a smaller file. Lines, text and hatches always stay sharp (vector), and transparency stays transparency. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/pictures\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/pictures\"\u003EPictures in a drawing\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"settings-that-are-remembered\"\u003ESettings that are remembered\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe dialog remembers your last paper, orientation, scale and line weight in this browser. The plot style table is kept with each sheet in the drawing; a sheet with none chosen starts on the one you chose last. A picked window is kept until you reload the page; after that the dialog starts at \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEverything (extents)\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe PDF is blank or the buttons are grey.\u003C/strong\u003E There is nothing to plot (an empty model), or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EA window…\u003C/span\u003E is chosen and no window is picked yet (&quot;Pick the window to plot.&quot;).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EText shows &quot;?&quot; in the PDF.\u003C/strong\u003E Text in Polish, Czech, Cyrillic, Greek and the other scripts of Europe prints as itself: the PDF then carries the letters it needs (from the Liberation Sans font, which has Helvetica&#039;s widths), and the text can still be selected and searched. Chinese, Japanese and Korean text prints as itself too: when a drawing has it, the plot fetches a larger font (about 10 MB, once) and the PDF carries only the characters used, so it needs a connection the first time (not in an offline copy that was saved before). Rare ideographs outside that font, and scripts such as Arabic and Hebrew, print as &quot;?&quot;, and the history counts them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAll lines are the same thickness.\u003C/strong\u003E Their lineweight is the default: give layers or objects a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELineweight\u003C/span\u003E in the right panel (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/units-papers-scales#line-weights-and-linetypes\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/units-papers-scales\" data-hash=\"line-weights-and-linetypes\"\u003ELine weights and linetypes\u003C/a\u003E), or set lineweights per color in a \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/plot-pdf#plot-style-tables\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/plot-pdf\" data-hash=\"plot-style-tables\"\u003Eplot style table\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;(not in this drawing)&quot; next to the plot style.\u003C/strong\u003E The drawing came from another program and names a table it does not include. Choose one of the tables, or make one with that name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EConstruction lines printed.\u003C/strong\u003E Set their layer not to plot: click its printer button in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayers\u003C/span\u003E panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe printout is the wrong size.\u003C/strong\u003E The printer scaled it. Print at actual size.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command P\"\u003E⌘P\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+P\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E opened the browser&#039;s print dialog.\u003C/strong\u003E The cursor was in a box in the right panel, or another dialog was open. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to leave the box, then try again, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlot\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command P\"\u003E⌘P\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+P\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E did nothing.\u003C/strong\u003E A command is running. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will make A PDF of a sheet at full size, then a quick PDF of the model at 1:100, and a printout. The PDF is made inside your browser; nothing is uploaded. It is a vector PDF: lines stay sharp at any zoom, and text can be searched. Plot a sheet Click the sheet's tab under the drawing. Press Ctrl+P, click Plot, or type PLOT and press Enter. The Plot to PDF dialog opens. It says which sheet it plots and on what paper, \"at full size … as you see it\". The preview on the right shows the sheet. In Plot style, choose Monochrome (black) for black lines, Colour (as drawn) to keep the colors, Grayscale, or a table of your own (see Plot style tables). The sheet keeps this choice: it is saved with the drawing. In Line weight, choose a pen width from 0.13 mm to 0.70 mm. Lines with the default lineweight print at this width; lines with their own lineweight (layer or object, or their color's pen in the table) print at that. Click Download PDF. The browser downloads a PDF named after the drawing and the sheet, for example Drawing1-A-101 Plan.pdf. The history reports its size in bytes and its paper size in millimeters. A sheet always plots at 1:1 on its own paper. To change the paper, change it on the sheet itself (see Set up a sheet). Layers set not to plot are left out, and so are viewport frames. Every sheet in one PDF With two or more sheets, the Plot to PDF dialog has All sheets in one PDF; or type PUBLISH. Enter takes every sheet; S (Some) asks for their names, separated by commas. One PDF downloads with a page per sheet, in tab order, each at its own paper size, and a bookmark per sheet with its name. Each sheet plots with its own plot style table; a sheet with none chosen uses the one you last chose in the dialog, and the pen width you last chose. Plot the model Click the Model tab. Press Ctrl+P. The dialog says it plots the model onto a sheet of paper, and shows more settings: In Area, choose what to plot: Everything (extents): the whole drawing. What is on screen: the area you see in the drawing now. A window…: then click Pick, click two corners on the drawing, and the dialog comes back. In Paper, choose a size: ISO A4 to A0, Letter, Legal, Tabloid and ANSI C and D, or ARCH C, D and E. In Orientation, choose Landscape or Portrait. In Scale, choose Fit to paper or a scale such as 1:100. Under the settings, a line reports the scale and how big the area comes out on the paper. If it is bigger than the paper, it says so: the edges are cut off. Choose a smaller scale, a bigger paper, or Fit to paper. Click Download PDF. A model plot leaves a 5 mm margin round the edge, because most printers cannot print to the edge. Fit to paper gives an odd scale (the history and the info line say \"(fitted)\"); choose a standard scale when the drawing must measure true. Scales read in real units. For 1:100 to be right, set Units in Current settings to what one drawing unit is. A unitless drawing is taken to be in millimeters. Plot style tables A plot style table says what each of the 255 standard drawing colors prints as, the way a color-dependent plot style table (CTB) does in other CAD programs. Three are built in: Table Prints Colour (as drawn) Every object in its own color. Monochrome (black) Every color black: the usual choice for drawings. Grayscale Every color as its gray, so colors stay apart on a black-and-white printer. To make your own, for example red lines thick and black, everything else thin: In the Plot to PDF dialog, click Edit tables… (or type PLOTSTYLES). Click New, and type a name such as office in Name. To start from a built-in table instead, choose it in Table and click Copy. Click a color in the grid. Shift+click selects a range of colors, Ctrl+click adds one more, and Select every colour takes them all. Set what the selected colors print as: The table itself has two more settings: Lineweights, the widths it knows (a printed line goes to the nearest of them), and Linetype scale, a factor on the dashes of every linetype that is not an ISO one. Prints in: Its own colour, Black or Another colour…. As a gray: the color as its gray. Ink (%): 100 is full ink, 50 is half way to white (screening), for background lines. Lineweight: Its own lineweight keeps the object's or layer's lineweight; a value prints every line of that color at that width. Linetype: print every line of that color with another linetype (for example everything red dashed); Use the object's linetype leaves them. Line end and Line join: butt, square or round ends, and miter, bevel or round corners (round, as before, when you set nothing). Fill style: a solid fill of that color prints as hatch lines (horizontal or vertical bars, slants, crosshatch, diamonds, square dots) instead of ink, for printers and plans where flat ink is too heavy. Click Done. The table is kept in the drawing, and the sheet (or the model) now plots with it. Cancel drops the changes. A colour with its own pen shows a dot in its corner, and a dot at the bottom when it sets a lineweight. An object with a true color (not one of the 255) uses the pen of the nearest standard color. The drawing keeps its tables and each sheet's choice when you save as DWG (2000 to 2018) or DXF (R12 or 2000), and opens with them again. Other programs see the name in each sheet's page setup (office.ctb), not the table itself. A drawing from another program whose page setup names a table such as monochrome.ctb plots with the built-in table of that name; for any other name the dialog says the drawing does not have it, and plots the drawing colors until you choose a table or make one with that name. To use a table in another drawing, click Export in Plot style tables (it saves office.plotstyle.json), then Import… in the other drawing. Import… also takes a .ctb file from another program: it is read from the published description of the format and has not yet been checked against a real file. The colour, screening and lineweight of each color are read; grayscale, pen numbers, linetypes, line ends and joins and fill styles are not, and a named table (.stb) is refused. Compare the first plot with what you expect, and send us the file if it is wrong. Print In the browser, click Print…. CadCraft makes the PDF and opens the browser's own print dialog for it. Choose \"Actual size\" or 100% (not \"Fit\") there to keep the scale. If the browser will not print from the page, the PDF opens in a new tab instead: print it from there with Ctrl+P. You can also click Open to print: the PDF opens in a new browser tab, and you print it from there. Safari may show the PDF and wait for you to use File, then Print. If your browser blocks the new tab, CadCraft downloads the PDF instead and says so in the history. Allow pop-ups for the site to open it in a tab next time. In CadCraft Studio, the Plot to PDF dialog lists the printers of the computer. Choose the Printer, the Paper in the printer and the number of Copies, then click Print…. Same as the plot picks the printer's paper that is the size of the sheet (A3 for an A3 sheet); when the printer has none, it uses its own paper and fits the sheet to it. On Windows the sheet is sent as a 300 dpi picture, because Windows cannot print a PDF without a PDF program; on Linux and macOS the PDF itself goes to the system's print service. Nothing leaves the computer. Picture and SVG files Download PNG saves the plotted sheet as a picture at 300 dpi (lowered only when a very large sheet would be bigger than the browser can make; the history says so). Download SVG saves it as a scalable drawing in millimeters, with the lines and text of the plot. Both come from the same plot as the PDF, with the same plot style, line weight and area. Pictures placed in the drawing are in the PDF only; the history says how many were left out. Pictures Pictures print at their own resolution, under the lines. Pictures in the dialog caps them at 600, 300 or 150 dpi for a smaller file. Lines, text and hatches always stay sharp (vector), and transparency stays transparency. See Pictures in a drawing. Settings that are remembered The dialog remembers your last paper, orientation, scale and line weight in this browser. The plot style table is kept with each sheet in the drawing; a sheet with none chosen starts on the one you chose last. A picked window is kept until you reload the page; after that the dialog starts at Everything (extents). Common mistakes The PDF is blank or the buttons are grey. There is nothing to plot (an empty model), or A window… is chosen and no window is picked yet (\"Pick the window to plot.\"). Text shows \"?\" in the PDF. Text in Polish, Czech, Cyrillic, Greek and the other scripts of Europe prints as itself: the PDF then carries the letters it needs (from the Liberation Sans font, which has Helvetica's widths), and the text can still be selected and searched. Chinese, Japanese and Korean text prints as itself too: when a drawing has it, the plot fetches a larger font (about 10 MB, once) and the PDF carries only the characters used, so it needs a connection the first time (not in an offline copy that was saved before). Rare ideographs outside that font, and scripts such as Arabic and Hebrew, print as \"?\", and the history counts them. All lines are the same thickness. Their lineweight is the default: give layers or objects a Lineweight in the right panel (see Line weights and linetypes), or set lineweights per color in a plot style table. \"(not in this drawing)\" next to the plot style. The drawing came from another program and names a table it does not include. Choose one of the tables, or make one with that name. Construction lines printed. Set their layer not to plot: click its printer button in the Layers panel. The printout is the wrong size. The printer scaled it. Print at actual size. Ctrl+P opened the browser's print dialog. The cursor was in a box in the right panel, or another dialog was open. Press Enter or Esc to leave the box, then try again, or click Plot. Ctrl+P did nothing. A command is running. Press Esc first.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"history","scope":"cadcraft","title":"History: versions and what changed","summary":"See the versions of a drawing (copies Studio keeps when you save, and the cloud's), open or restore one, and compare two with what changed shown in colour.","section":"tasks","order":135,"keywords":["history","versions","version history","restore","previous version","undo a save","compare","compare drawings","dwgcompare","changes","added","removed","changed","snapshot","backup","cloud versions"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-make","text":"What you will make","level":2},{"id":"open-history","text":"Open History","level":2},{"id":"where-the-versions-come-from","text":"Where the versions come from","level":2},{"id":"open-or-restore-a-version","text":"Open or restore a version","level":2},{"id":"compare","text":"Compare","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-make\"\u003EWhat you will make\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA look at an earlier version of a drawing, and a list of what is different from now: what was added, what was removed and what changed, marked in colour on the drawing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-history\"\u003EOpen History\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EHISTORY\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003EHIST\u003C/code\u003E) and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHistory…\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E menu of the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView\u003C/span\u003E group. The panel opens beside the drawing and stays open while you work, so you can pan and zoom the drawing with the colours on it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHistory works in CadCraft Studio. In a browser, the panel only says so.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"where-the-versions-come-from\"\u003EWhere the versions come from\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOn this computer.\u003C/strong\u003E Each time you open a drawing from a file in Studio, and each time you save it, Studio keeps a copy of the file in its own storage on this computer. The first copy is \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAs opened\u003C/span\u003E: the file as it was before your first save over it. Studio keeps the newest 25 copies of each drawing, and the oldest go first when all the copies together pass 400 MB. A save that changes nothing keeps nothing. Nothing is uploaded.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIn the cloud.\u003C/strong\u003E When you open a drawing from your cloud files in Studio, the panel also lists \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIn the cloud\u003C/span\u003E: every version the cloud holds, with its number, who saved it and the note. The panel only reads them. CadCraft does not send its own saves to the cloud yet, so these are the versions saved from other devices and the web. Sign in again in Studio if it says the sign-in ran out.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA drawing you have not saved yet has no copies: save it first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-or-restore-a-version\"\u003EOpen or restore a version\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen\u003C/span\u003E puts that version in a tab of its own. Your drawing is not touched.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERestore\u003C/span\u003E replaces this drawing with that version. It asks first. Studio keeps a copy of the drawing as it is now in History (marked \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBefore a restore\u003C/span\u003E) before it does, and nothing is written to the file until you save. A restore cannot be undone with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E; use that copy.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"compare\"\u003ECompare\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECompare\u003C/span\u003E on a version. It is compared with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThe drawing as it is now\u003C/span\u003E; to compare it with another version instead, choose that one in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECompare with\u003C/span\u003E first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERead the result under the list: how many objects were added, removed and changed, and a list of them. Click an item in the list to move the view to it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn the drawing:\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Egreen\u003C/strong\u003E objects were added,\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Ered\u003C/strong\u003E objects were removed (they are in the older version only),\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eorange\u003C/strong\u003E objects changed: the new shape is solid and the old one dashed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow on the drawing\u003C/span\u003E turns the colours off and on; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClear the colours\u003C/span\u003E takes the result away. Closing the panel clears them too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe list says what changed in a changed object: its text, that it moved, its shape, its layer, colour, linetype or lineweight. Each sheet is compared on its own; the colours show on the sheet that is open.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA drawing has no lasting number for each object, so CadCraft matches objects by what they are: the same object on both sides is unchanged; an object of the same type and layer that lies within its own size of an old one is the same object, changed; whatever is left is added or removed. An object moved far from where it was shows as removed in one place and added in the other. A block reference counts the content of its block.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;No copies yet.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E Studio keeps a copy at each open and save of a drawing that has a file on this computer. A drawing opened from a browser tab, or never saved, has none.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe cloud list is missing.\u003C/strong\u003E It appears for a drawing opened from the cloud in Studio; a file opened from the disk is not a cloud drawing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEverything looks changed.\u003C/strong\u003E Two versions saved by different programs write the same drawing in different ways (names of anonymous blocks, rounding). Compare versions saved by CadCraft.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will make A look at an earlier version of a drawing, and a list of what is different from now: what was added, what was removed and what changed, marked in colour on the drawing. Open History Type HISTORY (or HIST) and press Enter, or choose History… in the More menu of the View group. The panel opens beside the drawing and stays open while you work, so you can pan and zoom the drawing with the colours on it. History works in CadCraft Studio. In a browser, the panel only says so. Where the versions come from On this computer. Each time you open a drawing from a file in Studio, and each time you save it, Studio keeps a copy of the file in its own storage on this computer. The first copy is As opened: the file as it was before your first save over it. Studio keeps the newest 25 copies of each drawing, and the oldest go first when all the copies together pass 400 MB. A save that changes nothing keeps nothing. Nothing is uploaded. In the cloud. When you open a drawing from your cloud files in Studio, the panel also lists In the cloud: every version the cloud holds, with its number, who saved it and the note. The panel only reads them. CadCraft does not send its own saves to the cloud yet, so these are the versions saved from other devices and the web. Sign in again in Studio if it says the sign-in ran out. A drawing you have not saved yet has no copies: save it first. Open or restore a version Open puts that version in a tab of its own. Your drawing is not touched. Restore replaces this drawing with that version. It asks first. Studio keeps a copy of the drawing as it is now in History (marked Before a restore) before it does, and nothing is written to the file until you save. A restore cannot be undone with Ctrl+Z; use that copy. Compare Click Compare on a version. It is compared with The drawing as it is now; to compare it with another version instead, choose that one in Compare with first. Read the result under the list: how many objects were added, removed and changed, and a list of them. Click an item in the list to move the view to it. On the drawing: green objects were added, red objects were removed (they are in the older version only), orange objects changed: the new shape is solid and the old one dashed. Show on the drawing turns the colours off and on; Clear the colours takes the result away. Closing the panel clears them too. The list says what changed in a changed object: its text, that it moved, its shape, its layer, colour, linetype or lineweight. Each sheet is compared on its own; the colours show on the sheet that is open. A drawing has no lasting number for each object, so CadCraft matches objects by what they are: the same object on both sides is unchanged; an object of the same type and layer that lies within its own size of an old one is the same object, changed; whatever is left is added or removed. An object moved far from where it was shows as removed in one place and added in the other. A block reference counts the content of its block. Common mistakes \"No copies yet.\" Studio keeps a copy at each open and save of a drawing that has a file on this computer. A drawing opened from a browser tab, or never saved, has none. The cloud list is missing. It appears for a drawing opened from the cloud in Studio; a file opened from the disk is not a cloud drawing. Everything looks changed. Two versions saved by different programs write the same drawing in different ways (names of anonymous blocks, rounding). Compare versions saved by CadCraft.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"pdf-underlay","scope":"cadcraft","title":"PDF underlays and PDF import","summary":"Put a PDF page under your drawing to trace and plot it, or turn the page's lines and text into drawing objects.","section":"tasks","order":135,"keywords":["pdf","pdfattach","pdfimport","pdfclip","pdfadjust","pdflayers","underlay","trace","import pdf","scan","floor plan","fade","clip","layers","snap","open pdf"],"headings":[{"id":"two-ways-to-bring-in-a-pdf-page","text":"Two ways to bring in a PDF page","level":2},{"id":"attach-a-page","text":"Attach a page","level":2},{"id":"trace-it","text":"Trace it","level":2},{"id":"adjust-it","text":"Adjust it","level":2},{"id":"plot-and-save","text":"Plot and save","level":2},{"id":"import-a-page","text":"Import a page","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"two-ways-to-bring-in-a-pdf-page\"\u003ETwo ways to bring in a PDF page\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAttach\u003C/strong\u003E (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPDFATTACH\u003C/code\u003E) keeps the PDF as a picture under your drawing. You trace it, snap to its lines, fade it, clip it, and it prints with the sheet. The drawing keeps only the file&#039;s name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EImport\u003C/strong\u003E (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPDFIMPORT\u003C/code\u003E) turns the page&#039;s lines, curves, fills and text into ordinary drawing objects, on layers named after the PDF. They are yours to edit, and the PDF is not needed afterwards.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpening a PDF with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen\u003C/span\u003E asks which of the two you want.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"attach-a-page\"\u003EAttach a page\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EPDFATTACH\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003EPDFATT\u003C/code\u003E) and choose the PDF. If it has several pages, type the page number.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the insertion point (the page&#039;s lower-left corner).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for the page&#039;s true size, or type the scale: drawing units per PDF point (a point is 1/72 inch, so 0.3528 in a millimetre drawing).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for no turn, or type an angle.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe page is an object like a block reference: move, rotate, scale, copy, erase and put it on a layer. Switch its layer off and it disappears. Zoom in and the page is drawn again sharply.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"trace-it\"\u003ETrace it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESnap\u003C/span\u003E on, the cursor snaps to the PDF&#039;s own lines: end points, midpoints and crossings (and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENearest\u003C/span\u003E when that snap is on). Text drawn as letters on the page is not snapped to.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"adjust-it\"\u003EAdjust it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPDFADJUST\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Cstrong\u003EFade\u003C/strong\u003E (0 to 80 percent), \u003Cstrong\u003EContrast\u003C/strong\u003E (0 to 100), \u003Cstrong\u003EMonochrome\u003C/strong\u003E, and \u003Cstrong\u003EOnoff\u003C/strong\u003E to hide it without erasing it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPDFCLIP\u003C/code\u003E: show only a rectangle or polygon of the page; \u003Cstrong\u003EDelete\u003C/strong\u003E removes the clip.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPDFLAYERS\u003C/code\u003E: a PDF can have its own layers. The command lists them; type a number to switch that layer on or off for this underlay. The layers the file has off start off.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"plot-and-save\"\u003EPlot and save\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPLOT prints the page as its lines, fills and text (so it stays sharp and searchable). Pictures inside the PDF are shown on screen but are not printed yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA DWG or DXF 2000 keeps the underlay as a link: the file&#039;s name, the page, where it lies, its scale, turn, clip, fade and contrast. The PDF itself is not in the drawing. Reopen the drawing, type \u003Ccode\u003EPDF\u003C/code\u003E and pick the file again; the pages are shown as frames until you do, and take their true size when you do. Older DXF (R12) has no underlays: it keeps the frame and the name. How other programs read the saved clip and scale has not been checked in them yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"import-a-page\"\u003EImport a page\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EPDFIMPORT\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003EPDFIM\u003C/code\u003E) and choose the PDF and page.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick where the page&#039;s lower-left corner goes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for true size, type a scale, or choose \u003Cstrong\u003ETwo points\u003C/strong\u003E: the page is drawn at true size, then you click two points of a distance you know and type the real distance; everything is scaled to match.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELines become lines and polylines, circles and arcs are recognised, filled shapes become solid hatches, and text becomes text on a layer ending in \u003Ccode\u003E-TEXT\u003C/code\u003E. It is one undo step. Pictures in the PDF are not imported: attach the page instead. A scan (a page that is only a picture) has no lines to import.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPDFs are read as untrusted files: no scripts run, and a very large page is cut short and said so.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Two ways to bring in a PDF page Attach (PDFATTACH) keeps the PDF as a picture under your drawing. You trace it, snap to its lines, fade it, clip it, and it prints with the sheet. The drawing keeps only the file's name. Import (PDFIMPORT) turns the page's lines, curves, fills and text into ordinary drawing objects, on layers named after the PDF. They are yours to edit, and the PDF is not needed afterwards. Opening a PDF with Open asks which of the two you want. Attach a page Type PDFATTACH (or PDFATT) and choose the PDF. If it has several pages, type the page number. Click the insertion point (the page's lower-left corner). Press Enter for the page's true size, or type the scale: drawing units per PDF point (a point is 1/72 inch, so 0.3528 in a millimetre drawing). Press Enter for no turn, or type an angle. The page is an object like a block reference: move, rotate, scale, copy, erase and put it on a layer. Switch its layer off and it disappears. Zoom in and the page is drawn again sharply. Trace it With Snap on, the cursor snaps to the PDF's own lines: end points, midpoints and crossings (and Nearest when that snap is on). Text drawn as letters on the page is not snapped to. Adjust it PDFADJUST: Fade (0 to 80 percent), Contrast (0 to 100), Monochrome, and Onoff to hide it without erasing it. PDFCLIP: show only a rectangle or polygon of the page; Delete removes the clip. PDFLAYERS: a PDF can have its own layers. The command lists them; type a number to switch that layer on or off for this underlay. The layers the file has off start off. Plot and save PLOT prints the page as its lines, fills and text (so it stays sharp and searchable). Pictures inside the PDF are shown on screen but are not printed yet. A DWG or DXF 2000 keeps the underlay as a link: the file's name, the page, where it lies, its scale, turn, clip, fade and contrast. The PDF itself is not in the drawing. Reopen the drawing, type PDF and pick the file again; the pages are shown as frames until you do, and take their true size when you do. Older DXF (R12) has no underlays: it keeps the frame and the name. How other programs read the saved clip and scale has not been checked in them yet. Import a page Type PDFIMPORT (or PDFIM) and choose the PDF and page. Click where the page's lower-left corner goes. Press Enter for true size, type a scale, or choose Two points: the page is drawn at true size, then you click two points of a distance you know and type the real distance; everything is scaled to match. Lines become lines and polylines, circles and arcs are recognised, filled shapes become solid hatches, and text becomes text on a layer ending in -TEXT. It is one undo step. Pictures in the PDF are not imported: attach the page instead. A scan (a page that is only a picture) has no lines to import. PDFs are read as untrusted files: no scripts run, and a very large page is cut short and said so.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"documents","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Documents: check out, revisions and release","summary":"Keep a project folder's drawings under control: check a file out so nobody else changes it, check it in as a new revision, send it for review and release it with an approver's name; history, what uses what, a bill of materials, and title blocks that show the revision.","section":"tasks","order":136,"keywords":["project settings","approvers","admins","revision letters","ASME","read-only","compare revisions","open copy","partcraft","bimcraft","documents","DOCUMENTS","DOCS","vault","document management","pdm","check out","check in","checkout","checkin","lock","revision","rev","revision letter","release","released","approve","approval","review","obsolete","where used","bom","bill of materials","title block","revision block","project folder"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-make","text":"What you will make","level":2},{"id":"open-documents","text":"Open Documents","level":2},{"id":"start-a-vault","text":"Start a vault","level":2},{"id":"change-a-drawing","text":"Change a drawing","level":2},{"id":"review-and-release","text":"Review and release","level":2},{"id":"project-settings","text":"Project settings","level":2},{"id":"someone-left-a-file-checked-out","text":"Someone left a file checked out","level":2},{"id":"history-revisions-uses-used-by","text":"History, revisions, uses, used by","level":2},{"id":"a-revision-block-on-the-sheet","text":"A revision block on the sheet","level":2},{"id":"revision-in-the-title-block","text":"Revision in the title block","level":2},{"id":"partcraft-and-bimcraft","text":"PartCraft and BimCraft","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-make\"\u003EWhat you will make\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA project folder where every drawing has a revision (A.1, A.2 … then A, B …) and a state (Work in progress, For review, Released, Obsolete), where only one person at a time can change a file, and where every check-out, check-in, approval and broken check-out is written down with who did it and when.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEverything is kept in the folder itself, in a hidden \u003Ccode\u003E.ccvault\u003C/code\u003E folder beside your drawings. A folder on a shared drive or a synced folder works for a whole team. There is no server and nothing is uploaded.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-documents\"\u003EOpen Documents\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EDOCUMENTS\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003EDOCS\u003C/code\u003E) and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The panel opens beside the drawing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChoose the project folder\u003C/span\u003E and pick the folder that holds the project&#039;s drawings. Project folders work in CadCraft Studio and in browsers that can open a folder (Chrome, Edge). In other browsers the panel says so.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe first time, type your name (it goes on everything you do) and the name of this computer. If you are signed in, your name is already there.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"start-a-vault\"\u003EStart a vault\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA folder that is not a vault yet offers \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStart a vault in this folder\u003C/span\u003E. Type the project&#039;s name. Then open \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFiles not in the vault\u003C/span\u003E and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd\u003C/span\u003E beside each file to bring under control. A file you add starts at revision \u003Cstrong\u003EA.1\u003C/strong\u003E, Work in progress.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-a-drawing\"\u003EChange a drawing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen\u003C/span\u003E beside a drawing. It opens in CadCraft. If it is not checked out to you, look but do not change it: Save refuses to write over it and says why (use Save As to keep a copy of your own).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck out\u003C/span\u003E. Nobody else can check it out now; the panel shows them who has it, on which computer, since when.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck in\u003C/span\u003E and say what changed. The drawing you have open is saved into the project folder in its own format and becomes the next revision (A.1, then A.2). Checking in with no change keeps the revision.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo check-out\u003C/span\u003E gives the file back without a new revision.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"review-and-release\"\u003EReview and release\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESend for review\u003C/span\u003E: the file is read-only while it is reviewed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EApprove\u003C/span\u003E: type the approver&#039;s name. A.3 becomes \u003Cstrong\u003EA\u003C/strong\u003E, Released. Released files are read-only.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESend back\u003C/span\u003E: back to Work in progress, with the reason.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew revision\u003C/span\u003E on a released file checks it out for the next letter: its first check-in is \u003Cstrong\u003EB.1\u003C/strong\u003E. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo check-out\u003C/span\u003E before that leaves it at A.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake obsolete\u003C/span\u003E retires a released file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA project can keep a list of who may approve, and can require that the approver is not the person who checked the file in (see Project settings below).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"project-settings\"\u003EProject settings\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProject settings\u003C/span\u003E in the panel:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EApprovers\u003C/strong\u003E: one name per line. When there are any, only they can approve a release.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe approver must be someone other than the author\u003C/strong\u003E: the &quot;four eyes&quot; rule.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAdmins\u003C/strong\u003E: one name per line. When there are any, only they can break someone else&#039;s check-out or change these settings.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERevision letters to leave out\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUse the ASME Y14.35 sequence\u003C/span\u003E skips I, O, Q, S, X and Z, or type your own letters. Letters already given stay; the choice applies to the next revisions.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA check-out counts as stale after\u003C/strong\u003E: the number of days (14 by default).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave settings\u003C/span\u003E. The change is written in the log with who made it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"someone-left-a-file-checked-out\"\u003ESomeone left a file checked out\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBreak check-out\u003C/span\u003E frees a file someone else has checked out (they are away, or their computer died). You must type a reason: it is written in the log with whose check-out it was. When the project has admins, only they can do this. A check-out older than 14 days is marked as a long time.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"history-revisions-uses-used-by\"\u003EHistory, revisions, uses, used by\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose a file&#039;s name to see:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHistory\u003C/span\u003E: every event, newest first, with who, which computer and when.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERevisions\u003C/span\u003E: each revision kept. A copy of every revision is in the vault (the same content is kept once). \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen copy\u003C/span\u003E opens that revision as a plain copy that cannot overwrite the vault&#039;s file. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECompare with\u003C/span\u003E lists what was added, removed and changed between that revision and the one before it (or any other you choose), and colours it on the drawing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUses\u003C/span\u003E: the drawings it links to (xrefs), the documents an IFC model points to, the parts of a PartCraft assembly.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUsed by\u003C/span\u003E: every file in the vault that uses this one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBill of materials (CSV)\u003C/span\u003E saves the list of everything a file uses, level by level, with quantities, revisions and states, for a spreadsheet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-revision-block-on-the-sheet\"\u003EA revision block on the sheet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith a drawing open that has a layout, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERevision block\u003C/span\u003E. A table of the released revisions (revision, date, by, approved, description) is drawn at the top right of the sheet you are viewing. When you approve a release, the table is brought up to date.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"revision-in-the-title-block\"\u003ERevision in the title block\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EGive your title block&#039;s attributes one of these tags and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFill title blocks\u003C/span\u003E on the open drawing:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETag\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFilled with\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EVAULT.REV\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EREV\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EREVISION\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EREV_NO\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ethe revision (B, or B.2 while it is worked on)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EVAULT.STATE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ethe state (Released …)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EVAULT.DATE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EREV_DATE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EREVDATE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ethe date of the latest revision\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EVAULT.APPROVER\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EAPPROVED_BY\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ewho approved the latest release\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"partcraft-and-bimcraft\"\u003EPartCraft and BimCraft\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPartCraft and BimCraft have the same Documents panel for their own files: parts and assemblies in PartCraft, IFC models in BimCraft. Checking in saves the model that is open into the project folder, and an assembly shows which parts it uses and a bill of materials.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETwo people on the same shared folder cannot both check out a file: the check-out is a file the system refuses to make twice. In a browser, and in a Studio whose shell is older than this feature, this is as strict as the folder allows: two check-outs in the same split second could both succeed. The panel says which applies.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDo not edit or delete the \u003Ccode\u003E.ccvault\u003C/code\u003E folder by hand. The log in it is the record; the vault rebuilds its list from it if needed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChanging a file in the folder without checking it out is shown as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChanged in the folder without a check-out\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will make A project folder where every drawing has a revision (A.1, A.2 … then A, B …) and a state (Work in progress, For review, Released, Obsolete), where only one person at a time can change a file, and where every check-out, check-in, approval and broken check-out is written down with who did it and when. Everything is kept in the folder itself, in a hidden .ccvault folder beside your drawings. A folder on a shared drive or a synced folder works for a whole team. There is no server and nothing is uploaded. Open Documents Type DOCUMENTS (or DOCS) and press Enter. The panel opens beside the drawing. Choose Choose the project folder and pick the folder that holds the project's drawings. Project folders work in CadCraft Studio and in browsers that can open a folder (Chrome, Edge). In other browsers the panel says so. The first time, type your name (it goes on everything you do) and the name of this computer. If you are signed in, your name is already there. Start a vault A folder that is not a vault yet offers Start a vault in this folder. Type the project's name. Then open Files not in the vault and choose Add beside each file to bring under control. A file you add starts at revision A.1, Work in progress. Change a drawing Choose Open beside a drawing. It opens in CadCraft. If it is not checked out to you, look but do not change it: Save refuses to write over it and says why (use Save As to keep a copy of your own). Choose Check out. Nobody else can check it out now; the panel shows them who has it, on which computer, since when. Draw. Choose Check in and say what changed. The drawing you have open is saved into the project folder in its own format and becomes the next revision (A.1, then A.2). Checking in with no change keeps the revision. Undo check-out gives the file back without a new revision. Review and release Send for review: the file is read-only while it is reviewed. Approve: type the approver's name. A.3 becomes A, Released. Released files are read-only. Send back: back to Work in progress, with the reason. New revision on a released file checks it out for the next letter: its first check-in is B.1. Undo check-out before that leaves it at A. Make obsolete retires a released file. A project can keep a list of who may approve, and can require that the approver is not the person who checked the file in (see Project settings below). Project settings Open Project settings in the panel: Approvers: one name per line. When there are any, only they can approve a release. The approver must be someone other than the author: the \"four eyes\" rule. Admins: one name per line. When there are any, only they can break someone else's check-out or change these settings. Revision letters to leave out: Use the ASME Y14.35 sequence skips I, O, Q, S, X and Z, or type your own letters. Letters already given stay; the choice applies to the next revisions. A check-out counts as stale after: the number of days (14 by default). Choose Save settings. The change is written in the log with who made it. Someone left a file checked out Break check-out frees a file someone else has checked out (they are away, or their computer died). You must type a reason: it is written in the log with whose check-out it was. When the project has admins, only they can do this. A check-out older than 14 days is marked as a long time. History, revisions, uses, used by Choose a file's name to see: History: every event, newest first, with who, which computer and when. Revisions: each revision kept. A copy of every revision is in the vault (the same content is kept once). Open copy opens that revision as a plain copy that cannot overwrite the vault's file. Compare with lists what was added, removed and changed between that revision and the one before it (or any other you choose), and colours it on the drawing. Uses: the drawings it links to (xrefs), the documents an IFC model points to, the parts of a PartCraft assembly. Used by: every file in the vault that uses this one. Bill of materials (CSV) saves the list of everything a file uses, level by level, with quantities, revisions and states, for a spreadsheet. A revision block on the sheet With a drawing open that has a layout, choose Revision block. A table of the released revisions (revision, date, by, approved, description) is drawn at the top right of the sheet you are viewing. When you approve a release, the table is brought up to date. Revision in the title block Give your title block's attributes one of these tags and choose Fill title blocks on the open drawing: Tag Filled with VAULT.REV, REV, REVISION, REV_NO the revision (B, or B.2 while it is worked on) VAULT.STATE the state (Released …) VAULT.DATE, REV_DATE, REVDATE the date of the latest revision VAULT.APPROVER, APPROVED_BY who approved the latest release PartCraft and BimCraft PartCraft and BimCraft have the same Documents panel for their own files: parts and assemblies in PartCraft, IFC models in BimCraft. Checking in saves the model that is open into the project folder, and an assembly shows which parts it uses and a bill of materials. Good to know Two people on the same shared folder cannot both check out a file: the check-out is a file the system refuses to make twice. In a browser, and in a Studio whose shell is older than this feature, this is as strict as the folder allows: two check-outs in the same split second could both succeed. The panel says which applies. Do not edit or delete the .ccvault folder by hand. The log in it is the record; the vault rebuilds its list from it if needed. Changing a file in the folder without checking it out is shown as Changed in the folder without a check-out.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"scanned-drawings","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Scanned drawings and the trace","summary":"Bring in a scanned sheet, scale and square it, clean it up, erase parts of it, snap to its lines and trace it into lines and arcs.","section":"tasks","order":136,"keywords":["scan","scanned drawing","raster","tiff","jpeg","png","scale","align","deskew","despeckle","threshold","black and white","crop","erase","trace","vectorize","snap","arcs","hatching","paper drawings","old drawings","scanattach","scantrace"],"headings":[{"id":"from-a-paper-sheet-to-drawing-objects","text":"From a paper sheet to drawing objects","level":2},{"id":"snap-to-the-scan-s-lines","text":"Snap to the scan's lines","level":2},{"id":"the-trace","text":"The trace","level":2},{"id":"what-it-does-not-read","text":"What it does not read","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"from-a-paper-sheet-to-drawing-objects\"\u003EFrom a paper sheet to drawing objects\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA scanned drawing is a picture. CadCraft keeps the picture you gave it and never changes it: each clean-up step makes a new picture, one undo step each, and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESCANINFO\u003C/code\u003E lists the original file and the steps taken since. Nothing is sent anywhere and nothing is guessed by a trained model: the clean-up and the trace are ordinary image processing, and they run on your computer.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EInsert it.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESCANATTACH\u003C/code\u003E reads a PNG, a JPEG, a TIFF (one page, or all the pages of a multi-page file) or a PDF page that is a picture. It asks for the page, the lower-left corner and the width. For a PDF page it also asks how many pixels per inch to draw the page at.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EScale it.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESCANSCALE\u003C/code\u003E: pick two points on the scan, then type the true distance between them. The sheet is scaled about the first point.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAlign it.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESCANALIGN\u003C/code\u003E: pick two points on the scan and the two places they belong; the sheet moves and turns to fit, and scales too unless you answer No.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESquare it.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESCANDESKEW\u003C/code\u003E measures the angle the sheet&#039;s long lines lie at and shows it, with how sure it is. Accept it, or type your own, and the sheet is turned. A sheet with few long lines gets a warning: check the angle before you accept it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EClean it.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESCANCLEAN\u003C/code\u003E makes the sheet black and white (Auto evens out the paper first; Adaptive follows the paper&#039;s changes more closely; Level is one number for the whole sheet) and removes specks up to the size you set.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECrop and erase.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESCANCROP\u003C/code\u003E keeps a window. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESCANERASE\u003C/code\u003E erases a rectangle, a polygon or a lasso (press and drag): stamps, notes, a coffee ring.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe cleaned picture is what a saved DWG holds (an IMAGE and its IMAGEDEF, with the picture&#039;s file beside the drawing, as for any picture).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"snap-to-the-scan-s-lines\"\u003ESnap to the scan&#039;s lines\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ERASTERSNAP\u003C/code\u003E on (the default), object snaps also find the lines of a black-and-white scan: the ends of lines, their middles, corners and crossings, shown with the usual snap marks. The part of the sheet under the pointer is traced as you move; zoom in enough that a pixel is a few screen pixels. A line longer than the part being traced is followed to its ends.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-trace\"\u003EThe trace\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESCANTRACE\u003C/code\u003E turns the lines of the sheet into lines and arcs on a new layer (SCAN-TRACE), as one undo step, with the scan still under them. You set:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETolerance:\u003C/strong\u003E how far a traced line or arc may lie from the lines on the scan, in drawing units (the report also gives pixels). A tight tolerance follows every wobble; a loose one makes fewer, straighter pieces.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWindow:\u003C/strong\u003E a corner of the sheet, or the whole sheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EArcs:\u003C/strong\u003E off makes lines only.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELeftovers:\u003C/strong\u003E what the trace left alone is drawn as boxes on a layer you can switch off, or not drawn.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe trace finds the centre of each stroke, follows it, fits straight lines and circular arcs to it, joins runs that were cut where another line crossed, makes nearly level and nearly plumb lines exactly level and plumb, and closes corners. \u003Cstrong\u003EText and hatching stay as raster:\u003C/strong\u003E pieces the size of lettering are set aside, and bundles of short parallel lines are taken for hatching and set aside.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen it finishes it says how many lines and arcs it made, the tolerance, and how many pieces it left as raster. \u003Cstrong\u003EThat is what the pixels gave, to that tolerance, and it is not a claim that the result is right.\u003C/strong\u003E Compare it with the sheet: a trace can miss a faint line, join two lines that lie very close, or draw a line where there is only a stain. Clean the scan first (squared, black and white, specks gone) and the result is much better.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-does-not-read\"\u003EWhat it does not read\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETIFF files stored in tiles, BigTIFF, TIFF with JPEG or deflate compression, Group 3 fax coding and colours kept in separate planes are refused with a message; save them again as strips with LZW, PackBits, Group 4 or no compression. Interlaced PNG files are refused too. Files over 128 MB or 100 million pixels are refused, and a trace window can hold at most 64 million pixels.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"From a paper sheet to drawing objects A scanned drawing is a picture. CadCraft keeps the picture you gave it and never changes it: each clean-up step makes a new picture, one undo step each, and SCANINFO lists the original file and the steps taken since. Nothing is sent anywhere and nothing is guessed by a trained model: the clean-up and the trace are ordinary image processing, and they run on your computer. Insert it. SCANATTACH reads a PNG, a JPEG, a TIFF (one page, or all the pages of a multi-page file) or a PDF page that is a picture. It asks for the page, the lower-left corner and the width. For a PDF page it also asks how many pixels per inch to draw the page at. Scale it. SCANSCALE: pick two points on the scan, then type the true distance between them. The sheet is scaled about the first point. Align it. SCANALIGN: pick two points on the scan and the two places they belong; the sheet moves and turns to fit, and scales too unless you answer No. Square it. SCANDESKEW measures the angle the sheet's long lines lie at and shows it, with how sure it is. Accept it, or type your own, and the sheet is turned. A sheet with few long lines gets a warning: check the angle before you accept it. Clean it. SCANCLEAN makes the sheet black and white (Auto evens out the paper first; Adaptive follows the paper's changes more closely; Level is one number for the whole sheet) and removes specks up to the size you set. Crop and erase. SCANCROP keeps a window. SCANERASE erases a rectangle, a polygon or a lasso (press and drag): stamps, notes, a coffee ring. The cleaned picture is what a saved DWG holds (an IMAGE and its IMAGEDEF, with the picture's file beside the drawing, as for any picture). Snap to the scan's lines With RASTERSNAP on (the default), object snaps also find the lines of a black-and-white scan: the ends of lines, their middles, corners and crossings, shown with the usual snap marks. The part of the sheet under the pointer is traced as you move; zoom in enough that a pixel is a few screen pixels. A line longer than the part being traced is followed to its ends. The trace SCANTRACE turns the lines of the sheet into lines and arcs on a new layer (SCAN-TRACE), as one undo step, with the scan still under them. You set: Tolerance: how far a traced line or arc may lie from the lines on the scan, in drawing units (the report also gives pixels). A tight tolerance follows every wobble; a loose one makes fewer, straighter pieces. Window: a corner of the sheet, or the whole sheet. Arcs: off makes lines only. Leftovers: what the trace left alone is drawn as boxes on a layer you can switch off, or not drawn. The trace finds the centre of each stroke, follows it, fits straight lines and circular arcs to it, joins runs that were cut where another line crossed, makes nearly level and nearly plumb lines exactly level and plumb, and closes corners. Text and hatching stay as raster: pieces the size of lettering are set aside, and bundles of short parallel lines are taken for hatching and set aside. When it finishes it says how many lines and arcs it made, the tolerance, and how many pieces it left as raster. That is what the pixels gave, to that tolerance, and it is not a claim that the result is right. Compare it with the sheet: a trace can miss a faint line, join two lines that lie very close, or draw a line where there is only a stain. Clean the scan first (squared, black and white, specks gone) and the result is much better. What it does not read TIFF files stored in tiles, BigTIFF, TIFF with JPEG or deflate compression, Group 3 fax coding and colours kept in separate planes are refused with a message; save them again as strips with LZW, PackBits, Group 4 or no compression. Interlaced PNG files are refused too. Files over 128 MB or 100 million pixels are refused, and a trace window can hold at most 64 million pixels.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"laser-cutting","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Send a drawing to a laser","summary":"Open the Laser panel, choose what each layer does (cut, score, engrave, fill, picture), preview the head's moves, save GRBL laser-mode G-code, or send it from the browser after framing the job.","section":"tasks","order":137,"keywords":["laser","laser cutter","laser engraver","GRBL","G-code","gcode","engrave","fill","dither","picture engraving","score","cut","Web Serial","frame the job","power","speed","passes","LASER","LASERCUT","CNC"],"headings":[{"id":"what-is-sent","text":"What is sent","level":2},{"id":"choose-what-each-layer-does","text":"Choose what each layer does","level":2},{"id":"preview-then-save","text":"Preview, then save","level":2},{"id":"send-from-the-browser","text":"Send from the browser","level":2},{"id":"not-built-yet","text":"Not built yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELaser\u003C/span\u003E panel turns the layers of a drawing into a program for a laser that runs GRBL (most hobby and small-shop diode and CO2 controllers). Type \u003Ccode\u003ELASER\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003ELASERCUT\u003C/code\u003E) and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELaser…\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E menu of the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView\u003C/span\u003E group. In the ribbon styles it is in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E menu and beside \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlot\u003C/span\u003E. The panel opens over the drawing and stays open while you draw: the preview follows every change.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-warning\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003EWarning\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETest every setting on scrap first, wear eye protection that fits your laser, keep the lid closed where there is one, and never leave a running laser alone. The numbers a layer starts with are placeholders, not recommendations for any material.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-sent\"\u003EWhat is sent\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe panel reads the model (not the sheets), on layers that are on and thawed:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELines, arcs, circles, polylines (arc segments stay arcs), ellipses and splines. A shape that ends where it starts counts as closed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EBlocks are taken apart: an object on layer 0 inside a block goes with the layer of the block reference.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA hatch sends its boundary, so a hatched logo on a \u003Cstrong\u003EFill\u003C/strong\u003E layer is filled.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA picture (\u003Ccode\u003EIMAGEATTACH\u003C/code\u003E) is engraved on a layer set to \u003Cstrong\u003EEngrave picture\u003C/strong\u003E, when it lies square (not turned or slanted) and its file is open in CadCraft.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EText, dimensions, leaders, points and construction lines are not sent; the panel says how many it left out. To burn text, draw the letters as shapes or hatch them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESizes go out in millimetres from the drawing&#039;s units (\u003Ccode\u003EUNITS\u003C/code\u003E): a drawing in inches is multiplied by 25.4. A drawing with no units set is sent one unit to one millimetre. Tick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOnly the selected objects\u003C/span\u003E to send part of the drawing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"choose-what-each-layer-does\"\u003EChoose what each layer does\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery layer with something on it is a row. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDoes\u003C/span\u003E sets it to \u003Cstrong\u003ECut\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EScore\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EEngrave lines\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EFill shapes\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EEngrave picture\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003ENot sent\u003C/strong\u003E, with a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPower %\u003C/span\u003E, a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpeed mm/min\u003C/span\u003E and a number of \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPasses\u003C/span\u003E. Each change is one undo step and is saved in the drawing: DWG (every version CadCraft saves) and DXF keep the settings, so the drawing opens with them next time. Other programs open the file as before; they see one unused block named CADCRAFT_LASER (purging it there removes the settings).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFill\u003C/strong\u003E burns the inside of closed shapes in lines, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELine spacing\u003C/span\u003E apart, at an \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAngle\u003C/span\u003E, both directions or one, with an overscan so the head is at speed when the laser comes on; cross-hatch adds a second pass at 90°. A shape with a hole stays open at the hole.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEngrave picture\u003C/strong\u003E turns a picture into dots (Floyd–Steinberg, Atkinson, ordered, or plain black and white at a threshold) or, with \u003Cem\u003EGrey levels\u003C/em\u003E, into variable power.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOrder: pictures and fills first, then line engraving, then scores, then cuts, so a cut-out part does not move while it is engraved. Within one layer the paths run \u003Cstrong\u003Einside-first\u003C/strong\u003E (a hole before the plate it is in), nearest next.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"preview-then-save\"\u003EPreview, then save\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe preview draws what the head does: burning lines in colour, fast moves in grey, and a green box round everything that burns. Below it are the size, the length of burn and a time estimate (without acceleration; a real machine takes longer). \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave G-code…\u003C/span\u003E writes GRBL laser-mode G-code: \u003Ccode\u003E$32=1\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EM4\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003EM3\u003C/code\u003E) with S values, \u003Ccode\u003EG0\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EG1\u003C/code\u003E moves, \u003Ccode\u003EM5\u003C/code\u003E at the end. X0 Y0 is the job&#039;s lower left corner unless you choose the drawing&#039;s origin: set your machine&#039;s work zero there.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"send-from-the-browser\"\u003ESend from the browser\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn Chrome and Edge, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConnect…\u003C/span\u003E asks you to choose the laser&#039;s USB port. Nothing connects or sends until you press a button. Press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFrame the job\u003C/span\u003E first: the head traces the box of the job with the laser \u003Cstrong\u003Eoff\u003C/strong\u003E (or at the low power you set for a pointer) so you can see where it will burn. Only then does \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESend job\u003C/span\u003E come on, and it goes off again if you change anything. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPause\u003C/span\u003E holds the head, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStop\u003C/span\u003E holds it, resets the controller (the laser goes off) and sends \u003Ccode\u003EM5\u003C/code\u003E. Where Web Serial is missing (Firefox, Safari, and CadCraft Studio, which has no Web Serial yet) the panel offers saving only: save the G-code and send it with your laser&#039;s own program.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENot built yet: other controllers&#039; formats (Ruida, Marlin lasers, vendor files), tabs and lead-ins, kerf offset, material libraries, rotary and Z axes, camera alignment, text as outlines.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The Laser panel turns the layers of a drawing into a program for a laser that runs GRBL (most hobby and small-shop diode and CO2 controllers). Type LASER (or LASERCUT) and press Enter, or choose Laser… in the More menu of the View group. In the ribbon styles it is in the File menu and beside Plot. The panel opens over the drawing and stays open while you draw: the preview follows every change. Test every setting on scrap first, wear eye protection that fits your laser, keep the lid closed where there is one, and never leave a running laser alone. The numbers a layer starts with are placeholders, not recommendations for any material. What is sent The panel reads the model (not the sheets), on layers that are on and thawed: Lines, arcs, circles, polylines (arc segments stay arcs), ellipses and splines. A shape that ends where it starts counts as closed. Blocks are taken apart: an object on layer 0 inside a block goes with the layer of the block reference. A hatch sends its boundary, so a hatched logo on a Fill layer is filled. A picture (IMAGEATTACH) is engraved on a layer set to Engrave picture, when it lies square (not turned or slanted) and its file is open in CadCraft. Text, dimensions, leaders, points and construction lines are not sent; the panel says how many it left out. To burn text, draw the letters as shapes or hatch them. Sizes go out in millimetres from the drawing's units (UNITS): a drawing in inches is multiplied by 25.4. A drawing with no units set is sent one unit to one millimetre. Tick Only the selected objects to send part of the drawing. Choose what each layer does Every layer with something on it is a row. Does sets it to Cut, Score, Engrave lines, Fill shapes, Engrave picture or Not sent, with a Power %, a Speed mm/min and a number of Passes. Each change is one undo step and is saved in the drawing: DWG (every version CadCraft saves) and DXF keep the settings, so the drawing opens with them next time. Other programs open the file as before; they see one unused block named CADCRAFT_LASER (purging it there removes the settings). Fill burns the inside of closed shapes in lines, Line spacing apart, at an Angle, both directions or one, with an overscan so the head is at speed when the laser comes on; cross-hatch adds a second pass at 90°. A shape with a hole stays open at the hole. Engrave picture turns a picture into dots (Floyd–Steinberg, Atkinson, ordered, or plain black and white at a threshold) or, with Grey levels, into variable power. Order: pictures and fills first, then line engraving, then scores, then cuts, so a cut-out part does not move while it is engraved. Within one layer the paths run inside-first (a hole before the plate it is in), nearest next. Preview, then save The preview draws what the head does: burning lines in colour, fast moves in grey, and a green box round everything that burns. Below it are the size, the length of burn and a time estimate (without acceleration; a real machine takes longer). Save G-code… writes GRBL laser-mode G-code: $32=1, M4 (or M3) with S values, G0 and G1 moves, M5 at the end. X0 Y0 is the job's lower left corner unless you choose the drawing's origin: set your machine's work zero there. Send from the browser In Chrome and Edge, Connect… asks you to choose the laser's USB port. Nothing connects or sends until you press a button. Press Frame the job first: the head traces the box of the job with the laser off (or at the low power you set for a pointer) so you can see where it will burn. Only then does Send job come on, and it goes off again if you change anything. Pause holds the head, Stop holds it, resets the controller (the laser goes off) and sends M5. Where Web Serial is missing (Firefox, Safari, and CadCraft Studio, which has no Web Serial yet) the panel offers saving only: save the G-code and send it with your laser's own program. Not built yet Not built yet: other controllers' formats (Ruida, Marlin lasers, vendor files), tabs and lead-ins, kerf offset, material libraries, rotary and Z axes, camera alignment, text as outlines.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"usd","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Export the 3D model as USD","summary":"Write the 3D tab's solids as USD for rendering programs and other 3D software.","section":"tasks","order":140,"keywords":["usd","usda","usdc","usdz","openusd","export","3d","solids","usdout","export3d"],"headings":[],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe 3D tab&#039;s solids can be written as USD.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EUSDOUT\u003C/code\u003E and choose \u003Ccode\u003E.usda\u003C/code\u003E (text), \u003Ccode\u003E.usdc\u003C/code\u003E (binary) or \u003Ccode\u003E.usdz\u003C/code\u003E (one package file), or\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Etype \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXPORT3D\u003C/code\u003E and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUSD\u003C/span\u003E for a \u003Ccode\u003E.usdz\u003C/code\u003E package.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach solid becomes one object with its faces as a mesh and its colour as a material, in metres with Z up. A solid used several times is written once and placed where each copy is.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The 3D tab's solids can be written as USD. Type USDOUT and choose .usda (text), .usdc (binary) or .usdz (one package file), or type EXPORT3D and choose USD for a .usdz package. Each solid becomes one object with its faces as a mesh and its colour as a material, in metres with Z up. A solid used several times is written once and placed where each copy is.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"open-dwg-save-dxf","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Open a DWG, edit it and save a copy","summary":"Open a drawing someone sent you, check what came in, make a change, and send back a copy as DWG or DXF.","section":"tasks","order":140,"keywords":["dwg","dxf","open","import","export","save","r12","round trip","consultant","xref","check drawing","saveas","save as","dwg save","format"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-do","text":"What you will do","level":2},{"id":"open-the-file","text":"Open the file","level":2},{"id":"check-what-came-in","text":"Check what came in","level":2},{"id":"make-your-change","text":"Make your change","level":2},{"id":"plot-a-sheet-for-review","text":"Plot a sheet for review","level":2},{"id":"save-a-copy","text":"Save a copy","level":2},{"id":"save-as-dwg-or-dxf","text":"Save as DWG or DXF","level":2},{"id":"what-the-other-office-will-see","text":"What the other office will see","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-do\"\u003EWhat you will do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen a DWG from a consultant, check what CadCraft could and could not show, add a note, plot a sheet to PDF and save a copy to send back, as DWG or DXF.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-the-file\"\u003EOpen the file\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the \u003Ccode\u003E.dwg\u003C/code\u003E file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe status line shows &quot;Reading …&quot; while the drawing is read. When it is done, the drawing fills the screen, and the history says, for example, &quot;Opened plan.dwg: 18,240 objects on 64 layers&quot;, with how long it took.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"net:offline\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EOffline\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou are offline. If the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E button reads &quot;Offline ready&quot;, DWG files open normally. If not, and no DWG has been opened in this tab yet, opening a DWG can fail because CadCraft&#039;s DWG reader is fetched with the first DWG: open a DXF instead, or save CadCraft for offline use next time you are online. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/offline\" data-topic=\"basics/offline\"\u003EWorking offline\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"check-what-came-in\"\u003ECheck what came in\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELook at the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrawing\u003C/span\u003E section of the left panel: format and version, units, number of objects and layers, and sheets.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERead the &quot;About this file&quot; box under it, if there is one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe box lists:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENot shown yet:\u003C/strong\u003E objects CadCraft cannot draw yet, by kind and count. They are left out, not guessed at.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELayouts (sheets) came in:\u003C/strong\u003E open them with the tabs under the drawing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDimensions that could only be read as drawn.\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick through the tabs under the drawing to see each sheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECheck \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnits\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECurrent settings\u003C/span\u003E (nothing selected, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab). If it says \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnitless\u003C/span\u003E but you know the drawing is in millimeters, set it, so that sheet and plot scales read right.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf you expected a part of the drawing and cannot see it, check the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayers\u003C/span\u003E panel: the layer may be off or frozen in the file. Click its name to make it current; that turns it on.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"make-your-change\"\u003EMake your change\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMake a layer for your notes: click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew layer\u003C/span\u003E, type \u003Ccode\u003ENOTES\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EDT\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick where the note goes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType a text height that suits the drawing (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/text\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/text\"\u003EAdd text and labels\u003C/a\u003E) and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to keep the rotation at 0.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType your note and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again to finish.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEdits to imported dimensions are fine: as soon as you change one, CadCraft redraws it in its own style.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"plot-a-sheet-for-review\"\u003EPlot a sheet for review\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the sheet&#039;s tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command P\"\u003E⌘P\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+P\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload PDF\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/plot-pdf\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/plot-pdf\"\u003EPlot to PDF\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"save-a-copy\"\u003ESave a copy\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou opened a DWG, so the browser downloads \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;name&gt;.dwg\u003C/code\u003E, in the version the DWG came in. The history says, for example, &quot;Saved a copy as Plan.dwg (DWG (AutoCAD 2018)).&quot; and lists, on the lines under it, anything that changed on the way.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERead those lines before you send the file. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/files#what-a-dwg-keeps-and-changes\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/files\" data-hash=\"what-a-dwg-keeps-and-changes\"\u003EWhat a DWG keeps and changes\u003C/a\u003E explains each one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYour original DWG is never changed. Every save is a new download.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"save-as-dwg-or-dxf\"\u003ESave as DWG or DXF\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo choose the format yourself:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command S\"\u003E⇧⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave a copy as\u003C/span\u003E dialog opens with three choices, each with the file name it will give: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDWG\u003C/span\u003E, with its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EVersion\u003C/span\u003E list, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDXF R12\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDXF 2000\u003C/span\u003E. The drawing&#039;s current format and version are already picked.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the format you want. For DWG, pick the version the other office uses, or an older one: AutoCAD 2018, 2013, 2010, 2007, 2004 or 2000.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe copy downloads. From now on, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E saves this drawing in that format, and new drawings start with it too, in this browser. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECancel\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E saves nothing, and the history says &quot;Not saved.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhich one to pick:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDWG\u003C/strong\u003E keeps the most: sheets with their viewports, real dimensions, hatch patterns, layer and block names as you typed them, and multi-line text as multi-line text. The AutoCAD 2000 version opens in the most programs; 2004 and later also keep true colors.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDXF 2000\u003C/strong\u003E is a text file that keeps almost what a DWG keeps: every sheet as a layout with its viewports, real dimensions, hatch patterns, true colours, lineweights, linetypes, and names as you typed them. It opens in AutoCAD 2000 and later and most CAD programs. Multileaders become the lines, arrowheads and text they draw. A DXF opened in version 2000 or later saves as DXF 2000.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDXF R12\u003C/strong\u003E opens in almost any CAD program. It keeps the first sheet with its viewports, and CadCraft&#039;s other sheets. It simplifies newer things, such as multi-line text and hatches.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-the-other-office-will-see\"\u003EWhat the other office will see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn a DWG:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELines, arcs, circles, ellipses, polylines, text, multi-line text, blocks with their attributes, solid fills, hatch patterns and layers as you see them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EReal dimensions: the other program re-measures them when they are stretched.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEvery sheet, with its paper and its viewports.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMultileaders as multileaders. In the AutoCAD 2000 version, true colors become the nearest standard color.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn a DXF R12:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELines, arcs, circles, polylines, text, block references and dimensions as you see them. Dimensions stay real dimensions.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELayer names in capitals, with spaces turned into \u003Ccode\u003E_\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe first sheet as the drawing&#039;s paper space, with its viewports. Further sheets arrive as blocks named \u003Ccode\u003ECCLAYOUT_1\u003C/code\u003E and so on; if they need every sheet as a layout, plot PDFs of each sheet for them as well.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMulti-line text as separate lines, ellipses as polylines, hatches as outlines.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEither way, anything listed as &quot;Not shown yet&quot; when you opened the DWG is \u003Cstrong\u003Enot\u003C/strong\u003E in the copy, unless the history says it was kept: a DWG&#039;s OLE objects and its 3D solids in model space are written back when you save as DWG. Tell them, and send a PDF as well.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;CadCraft opens DWG and DXF drawings.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E The file has another extension. Check it is a \u003Ccode\u003E.dwg\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E.dxf\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;This drawing could not be read.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E The DWG is damaged or in a version the reader does not handle. Ask for a DXF, or for the DWG saved in an older version.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;This is a binary DXF.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E Ask for, or save, a text (ASCII) DXF.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe saved file is called \u003Ccode\u003Ename (1).dwg\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003Ename (1).dxf\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/strong\u003E The browser does that when a file with the same name is already in the folder.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThey have an older program that cannot open the DWG.\u003C/strong\u003E Save as DWG with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EVersion\u003C/span\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAutoCAD 2000\u003C/span\u003E, or as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDXF R12\u003C/span\u003E: almost any CAD program opens it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EParts of the drawing are missing.\u003C/strong\u003E Linked drawings (xrefs) are not loaded until you pick their files (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EXREF\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E), and pictures show as frames with their file names.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will do Open a DWG from a consultant, check what CadCraft could and could not show, add a note, plot a sheet to PDF and save a copy to send back, as DWG or DXF. Open the file Press Ctrl+O, or click Open. Choose the .dwg file. The status line shows \"Reading …\" while the drawing is read. When it is done, the drawing fills the screen, and the history says, for example, \"Opened plan.dwg: 18,240 objects on 64 layers\", with how long it took. You are offline. If the Save for offline button reads \"Offline ready\", DWG files open normally. If not, and no DWG has been opened in this tab yet, opening a DWG can fail because CadCraft's DWG reader is fetched with the first DWG: open a DXF instead, or save CadCraft for offline use next time you are online. See Working offline. Check what came in Look at the Drawing section of the left panel: format and version, units, number of objects and layers, and sheets. Read the \"About this file\" box under it, if there is one. The box lists: Not shown yet: objects CadCraft cannot draw yet, by kind and count. They are left out, not guessed at. Layouts (sheets) came in: open them with the tabs under the drawing. Dimensions that could only be read as drawn. Click through the tabs under the drawing to see each sheet. Check Units in Current settings (nothing selected, Model tab). If it says Unitless but you know the drawing is in millimeters, set it, so that sheet and plot scales read right. If you expected a part of the drawing and cannot see it, check the Layers panel: the layer may be off or frozen in the file. Click its name to make it current; that turns it on. Make your change Make a layer for your notes: click New layer, type NOTES and press Enter. Type DT and press Enter. Click where the note goes. Type a text height that suits the drawing (see Add text and labels) and press Enter. Press Enter to keep the rotation at 0. Type your note and press Enter. Press Enter again to finish. Edits to imported dimensions are fine: as soon as you change one, CadCraft redraws it in its own style. Plot a sheet for review Click the sheet's tab. Press Ctrl+P. Click Download PDF. See Plot to PDF. Save a copy Press Ctrl+S, or click Save. You opened a DWG, so the browser downloads \u003Cname\u003E.dwg, in the version the DWG came in. The history says, for example, \"Saved a copy as Plan.dwg (DWG (AutoCAD 2018)).\" and lists, on the lines under it, anything that changed on the way. Read those lines before you send the file. What a DWG keeps and changes explains each one. Your original DWG is never changed. Every save is a new download. Save as DWG or DXF To choose the format yourself: Press Ctrl+Shift+S, or click Save as. The Save a copy as dialog opens with three choices, each with the file name it will give: DWG, with its Version list, DXF R12 and DXF 2000. The drawing's current format and version are already picked. Click the format you want. For DWG, pick the version the other office uses, or an older one: AutoCAD 2018, 2013, 2010, 2007, 2004 or 2000. Click Save. The copy downloads. From now on, Ctrl+S saves this drawing in that format, and new drawings start with it too, in this browser. Cancel or Esc saves nothing, and the history says \"Not saved.\" Which one to pick: DWG keeps the most: sheets with their viewports, real dimensions, hatch patterns, layer and block names as you typed them, and multi-line text as multi-line text. The AutoCAD 2000 version opens in the most programs; 2004 and later also keep true colors. DXF 2000 is a text file that keeps almost what a DWG keeps: every sheet as a layout with its viewports, real dimensions, hatch patterns, true colours, lineweights, linetypes, and names as you typed them. It opens in AutoCAD 2000 and later and most CAD programs. Multileaders become the lines, arrowheads and text they draw. A DXF opened in version 2000 or later saves as DXF 2000. DXF R12 opens in almost any CAD program. It keeps the first sheet with its viewports, and CadCraft's other sheets. It simplifies newer things, such as multi-line text and hatches. What the other office will see In a DWG: Lines, arcs, circles, ellipses, polylines, text, multi-line text, blocks with their attributes, solid fills, hatch patterns and layers as you see them. Real dimensions: the other program re-measures them when they are stretched. Every sheet, with its paper and its viewports. Multileaders as multileaders. In the AutoCAD 2000 version, true colors become the nearest standard color. In a DXF R12: Lines, arcs, circles, polylines, text, block references and dimensions as you see them. Dimensions stay real dimensions. Layer names in capitals, with spaces turned into _. The first sheet as the drawing's paper space, with its viewports. Further sheets arrive as blocks named CCLAYOUT_1 and so on; if they need every sheet as a layout, plot PDFs of each sheet for them as well. Multi-line text as separate lines, ellipses as polylines, hatches as outlines. Either way, anything listed as \"Not shown yet\" when you opened the DWG is not in the copy, unless the history says it was kept: a DWG's OLE objects and its 3D solids in model space are written back when you save as DWG. Tell them, and send a PDF as well. Common mistakes \"CadCraft opens DWG and DXF drawings.\" The file has another extension. Check it is a .dwg or .dxf. \"This drawing could not be read.\" The DWG is damaged or in a version the reader does not handle. Ask for a DXF, or for the DWG saved in an older version. \"This is a binary DXF.\" Ask for, or save, a text (ASCII) DXF. The saved file is called name (1).dwg or name (1).dxf. The browser does that when a file with the same name is already in the folder. They have an older program that cannot open the DWG. Save as DWG with Version AutoCAD 2000, or as DXF R12: almost any CAD program opens it. Parts of the drawing are missing. Linked drawings (xrefs) are not loaded until you pick their files (XREF, R), and pictures show as frames with their file names.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"points-for-bim","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Points for BIM programs","summary":"Export points, and the insertion points of blocks with a description, to a CSV or TXT file that BIM and survey programs import.","section":"tasks","order":175,"keywords":["exportpoints","export points","points","csv","txt","pnezd","penzd","xyz","survey points","coordinates","bim","setting out","point file","northing","easting","elevation"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-make","text":"What you will make","level":2},{"id":"export-the-points","text":"Export the points","level":2},{"id":"what-is-in-the-file","text":"What is in the file","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-make\"\u003EWhat you will make\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA point file: one line per point, with its number, northing, easting, elevation and description, which BIM programs (Revit and Archicad among them) and survey software can import to place survey points, site markers or equipment.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"export-the-points\"\u003EExport the points\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the points and the block references whose insertion points you want, or select nothing to take every point.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXPORTPOINTS\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003EEXPPTS\u003C/code\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBlocks\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport points\u003C/span\u003E) and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again at \u003Ccode\u003ESelect points and blocks\u003C/code\u003E when nothing was selected).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhen you picked blocks with attributes, CadCraft lists their tags: type the one whose value is the description (for example \u003Ccode\u003EITEM\u003C/code\u003E), or anything else to use each block&#039;s name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the columns: \u003Ccode\u003EPNEZD\u003C/code\u003E (point, northing, easting, elevation, description: the usual one), \u003Ccode\u003EPENZD\u003C/code\u003E (easting before northing) or \u003Ccode\u003EXYZ\u003C/code\u003E (only the coordinates).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the units: \u003Ccode\u003ED\u003C/code\u003E as drawn, or \u003Ccode\u003EM\u003C/code\u003E metres or \u003Ccode\u003EF\u003C/code\u003E feet (only when the drawing&#039;s units are set: Properties › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnits\u003C/span\u003E with nothing selected).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith a UCS set, choose \u003Ccode\u003EW\u003C/code\u003E world or \u003Ccode\u003EU\u003C/code\u003E UCS coordinates (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/ucs-and-turned-view\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/ucs-and-turned-view\"\u003EUCS and a turned view\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E for a comma-separated \u003Ccode\u003E.csv\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003ET\u003C/code\u003E for a tab-separated \u003Ccode\u003E.txt\u003C/code\u003E, and the first point number.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe file downloads as \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;drawing&gt;-points.csv\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003E.txt\u003C/code\u003E), and the history says how many points it holds.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-in-the-file\"\u003EWhat is in the file\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOne point per line, ending with a line break (CR LF), three decimals.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA point&#039;s elevation is its Z (0 when it has none). A block&#039;s point is its insertion point.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA description that holds a comma (or a tab in a TXT) or a quote is put in double quotes, with its quotes doubled.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe format is the one CivilCraft reads and writes, so the file also opens there as points.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOnly \u003Ccode\u003ED\u003C/code\u003E is offered for the units: the drawing has no units set. Set them, or convert in the other program.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe points are far from where you expect in the other program: export world coordinates (\u003Ccode\u003EW\u003C/code\u003E), or the UCS the other program&#039;s survey point uses (\u003Ccode\u003EU\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will make A point file: one line per point, with its number, northing, easting, elevation and description, which BIM programs (Revit and Archicad among them) and survey software can import to place survey points, site markers or equipment. Export the points Select the points and the block references whose insertion points you want, or select nothing to take every point. Type EXPORTPOINTS (or EXPPTS, or Blocks › More › Export points) and press Enter (press Enter again at Select points and blocks when nothing was selected). When you picked blocks with attributes, CadCraft lists their tags: type the one whose value is the description (for example ITEM), or anything else to use each block's name. Choose the columns: PNEZD (point, northing, easting, elevation, description: the usual one), PENZD (easting before northing) or XYZ (only the coordinates). Choose the units: D as drawn, or M metres or F feet (only when the drawing's units are set: Properties › Units with nothing selected). With a UCS set, choose W world or U UCS coordinates (see UCS and a turned view). Choose C for a comma-separated .csv or T for a tab-separated .txt, and the first point number. The file downloads as \u003Cdrawing\u003E-points.csv (or .txt), and the history says how many points it holds. What is in the file One point per line, ending with a line break (CR LF), three decimals. A point's elevation is its Z (0 when it has none). A block's point is its insertion point. A description that holds a comma (or a tab in a TXT) or a quote is put in double quotes, with its quotes doubled. The format is the one CivilCraft reads and writes, so the file also opens there as points. Common mistakes Only D is offered for the units: the drawing has no units set. Set them, or convert in the other program. The points are far from where you expect in the other program: export world coordinates (W), or the UCS the other program's survey point uses (U).","lang":"en"},{"slug":"coming-from","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Coming from other drafting software","summary":"How familiar commands, keys and habits map to CadCraft, and the differences that trip up experienced drafters.","section":"switching","order":10,"keywords":["switching","differences","migrate","habits","aliases","dynamic input","pickfirst","pickadd","qsave","saveas","save as","mtext","ctb","polar tracking","xref","keyboard shortcuts","familiar","copyclip","pasteclip","pasteorig","copybase","clipboard"],"headings":[{"id":"what-maps-directly","text":"What maps directly","level":2},{"id":"differences-that-trip-people-up","text":"Differences that trip people up","level":2},{"id":"typed-points-are-absolute","text":"Typed points are absolute","level":3},{"id":"shift-skips-snaps-it-does-not-give-ortho","text":"Shift skips snaps; it does not give Ortho","level":3},{"id":"right-click-is-always-enter","text":"Right-click is always Enter","level":3},{"id":"no-space-bar-panning","text":"No Space-bar panning","level":3},{"id":"trim-and-extend","text":"Trim and Extend","level":3},{"id":"fillet-and-chamfer","text":"Fillet and chamfer","level":3},{"id":"paragraph-text-and-single-line-text","text":"Paragraph text and single-line text","level":3},{"id":"save-means-download-a-copy","text":"Save means \"download a copy\"","level":3},{"id":"layers-simpler","text":"Layers: simpler","level":3},{"id":"dimension-styles","text":"Dimension styles","level":3},{"id":"plotting-is-simpler","text":"Plotting is simpler","level":3},{"id":"working-inside-a-viewport","text":"Working inside a viewport","level":3},{"id":"tracking-and-dynamic-input-yes-grid-snap-not-yet","text":"Tracking and dynamic input yes, grid snap not yet","level":3},{"id":"feet-and-inches","text":"Feet and inches","level":3},{"id":"copy-and-paste-stays-inside-cadcraft","text":"Copy and paste stays inside CadCraft","level":3},{"id":"browser-keys-you-cannot-use","text":"Browser keys you cannot use","level":3},{"id":"commands-you-may-reach-for-that-are-not-here-yet","text":"Commands you may reach for that are not here yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EIf you have used AutoCAD, or a program built on the same model, most of what you know works here: the command line, aliases such as \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ETR\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EO\u003C/code\u003E, Enter or Space to repeat, window and crossing selection, object snaps on F3, Ortho on F8, model space with layouts and viewports. This page lists what maps directly, and then the differences that catch people out. Read the second half even if you skim the first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-maps-directly\"\u003EWhat maps directly\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EHabit\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIn CadCraft\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EPL\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EREC\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EPOL\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EEL\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EPO\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ET\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESame commands, same aliases\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EM\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ECO\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ERO\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESC\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EMI\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EO\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ETR\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EEX\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EF\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EX\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESame\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDLI\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDAL\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDAN\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDRA\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDDI\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDCO\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDBA\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESame dimension commands\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDIMSCALE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDIMTXT\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDIMASZ\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDIMDEC\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDIMGAP\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDIMEXE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDIMEXO\u003C/code\u003E typed at the command line\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESame, for new dimensions\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EB\u003C/code\u003E to make a block, \u003Ccode\u003EI\u003C/code\u003E to insert, \u003Ccode\u003EPU\u003C/code\u003E to purge\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESame (purge removes unused blocks only)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ELAYOUT\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EMV\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EPLOT\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDI\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EID\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ELI\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EZ\u003C/code\u003E then \u003Ccode\u003EE\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003EW\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESame\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETyping \u003Ccode\u003E_LINE\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E-LAYER\u003C/code\u003E from a script or a habit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe leading \u003Ccode\u003E_\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E-\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E&#039;\u003C/code\u003E is ignored\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEnter or Space on an empty prompt repeats the last command\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESame\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEsc cancels; Esc again clears the selection\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESame\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E@dx,dy\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E@d&lt;a\u003C/code\u003E, typed angles counterclockwise from east\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESame\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWindow left to right, crossing right to left, click-click; press and drag for a lasso, Space cycles window / crossing / fence (LASSOMODE)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESame\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect first, then the command (noun-verb)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESame; a finished command clears the selection\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EALL\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E at Select objects\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESame\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShift+click removes from the selection\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESame. Settings can make a click replace the selection instead, as PICKADD 0 does: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/settings\"\u003ECadCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECtrl+C, Ctrl+X, Ctrl+V, Ctrl+Shift+V (COPYCLIP, CUTCLIP, PASTECLIP, PASTEORIG), COPYBASE\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESame commands and keys; pasting places the copy by its lower-left corner, as PASTECLIP does\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EF3 object snap, F7 grid, F8 Ortho\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESame keys\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDouble-click the wheel to zoom extents\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESame\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMirrored text stays readable\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESame (as with MIRRTEXT 0)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECtrl+Z, Ctrl+Y, Ctrl+S, Ctrl+O, Ctrl+P, Ctrl+A\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESame; on a Mac, Command works too\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/commands\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/commands\"\u003ECommand reference\u003C/a\u003E lists every command and alias.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf you like the ribbon of AutoCAD, pick the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrafting classic\u003C/span\u003E menu style in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMenus, keys and mouse\u003C/span\u003E: Home, Insert, Annotate, Parametric, View, Manage and Output tabs over the same command line, and the middle button pans while a right click is Enter. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/menus-keys-and-mouse\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/menus-keys-and-mouse\"\u003EMenus, keys and mouse\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/command-map\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/command-map\"\u003Ecommand map\u003C/a\u003E lists every command CadCraft has with its short names, and the \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/cheat-sheet\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/cheat-sheet\"\u003Echeat sheet\u003C/a\u003E the 40 you will use most (also as a one-page PDF).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"differences-that-trip-people-up\"\u003EDifferences that trip people up\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"typed-points-are-absolute\"\u003ETyped points are absolute\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith dynamic input on, the other program treats a typed \u003Ccode\u003E100,0\u003C/code\u003E for a second point as relative. \u003Cstrong\u003ECadCraft&#039;s command line does not:\u003C/strong\u003E there, \u003Ccode\u003Ex,y\u003C/code\u003E is always measured from the origin. With \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDyn\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F12\"\u003EF12\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF12\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) on, use its fields instead: distance, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, angle. Use \u003Ccode\u003E@100,0\u003C/code\u003E for &quot;100 to the right of the last point&quot;, or move the crosshair the right way and type a bare \u003Ccode\u003E100\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"shift-skips-snaps-it-does-not-give-ortho\"\u003EShift skips snaps; it does not give Ortho\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHolding Shift while you click ignores object snaps for that click. It does not temporarily switch Ortho. Use F8 or the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrtho\u003C/span\u003E button.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"right-click-is-always-enter\"\u003ERight-click is always Enter\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThere is no right-click menu. A right-click in the drawing is Enter: it ends a command, finishes a selection, or repeats the last command. During a grip move or a selection box, it cancels instead. Drag with the right button to pan.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"no-space-bar-panning\"\u003ENo Space-bar panning\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESpace is Enter, so holding Space and dragging does not pan. Pan with the middle button or the right button, or zoom out and back in at another spot with the wheel.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"trim-and-extend\"\u003ETrim and Extend\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETrim and Extend start in Quick mode: every visible object is a cutting edge or a boundary, the piece under the pointer shows what will go, a dragged path trims every piece it crosses, and an object nothing crosses is erased. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E switches between trim and extend. \u003Ccode\u003EO\u003C/code\u003E switches to Standard mode (choose the edges first), \u003Ccode\u003EF\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E are fence and crossing, \u003Ccode\u003EE\u003C/code\u003E is EDGEMODE, \u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E erases. There is no \u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E (Project) option: CadCraft trims in the plane of the drawing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"fillet-and-chamfer\"\u003EFillet and chamfer\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EFILLET\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECHAMFER\u003C/code\u003E take the options you know: Undo, Polyline, Radius (or Distance, Angle and mEthod), Trim and Multiple, and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click for a sharp corner. A fillet between a polyline and a line adds the arc as its own object instead of joining them into the polyline, and polyline corners next to an arc segment are left as they are.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"paragraph-text-and-single-line-text\"\u003EParagraph text and single-line text\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ET\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EMT\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EMTEXT\u003C/code\u003E are paragraph text typed in place, with a width, line spacing and justification; \u003Ccode\u003EDT\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EDTEXT\u003C/code\u003E are single-line text, as you know them. There are no text styles or columns, and you cannot type in-text formatting (bold, stacked fractions) yet; formatting that comes in a file is kept, drawn and saved (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/text#formatting-inside-paragraph-text-from-other-programs\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/text\" data-hash=\"formatting-inside-paragraph-text-from-other-programs\"\u003EAdd text and labels\u003C/a\u003E). Everything draws in a Helvetica-style font.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"save-means-download-a-copy\"\u003ESave means &quot;download a copy&quot;\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESAVE\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EQSAVE\u003C/code\u003E download a copy in the drawing&#039;s format: a DWG you opened saves as DWG, a DXF as DXF. In the browser nothing is overwritten in place, and opened files are never changed. In CadCraft Studio, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E writes back to the file you opened.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESAVEAS\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command S\"\u003E⇧⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) is a small dialog, not a file browser: pick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDWG\u003C/span\u003E (with its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EVersion\u003C/span\u003E, AutoCAD 2000 to 2018), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDXF R12\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDXF 2000\u003C/span\u003E and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E. The browser decides where the download goes. \u003Ccode\u003EDXFOUT\u003C/code\u003E always downloads DXF R12.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA DWG you opened saves in the version it came in; a new drawing saves in the AutoCAD 2000 format unless you pick another. Sheets, dimensions and hatch patterns are kept. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/files#saving\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/files\" data-hash=\"saving\"\u003EFiles: what opens, what saves\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"layers-simpler\"\u003ELayers: simpler\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEvery layer change (on, freeze, lock, color, plot, linetype, lineweight, current layer) is one undo step.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOff and freeze have the same effect in CadCraft.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELayers a drawing freezes in one viewport stay frozen there: on screen, in plots and when you save it as DWG or DXF.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA viewport that shows a layer in its own colour, linetype, lineweight or transparency keeps doing so on screen, in plots and in a saved DWG (DXF keeps the layer&#039;s own settings; the save says so). Changing those per viewport is not built yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThere is no layer properties manager and no layer states; \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ERENAME\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYMRG\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYDEL\u003C/code\u003E rename, merge and delete layers. Linetypes and lineweights are set in the right panel (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/units-papers-scales#line-weights-and-linetypes\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/units-papers-scales\" data-hash=\"line-weights-and-linetypes\"\u003ELine weights and linetypes\u003C/a\u003E); \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYISO\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYOFF\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYFRZ\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYMCUR\u003C/code\u003E work by picking.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESaving to DXF R12 writes layer names in capitals. A DWG keeps them as you typed them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"dimension-styles\"\u003EDimension styles\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDIMSTYLE\u003C/code\u003E opens a manager for named styles (new, modify, set current, compare, apply). \u003Ccode\u003EDIMSCALE\u003C/code\u003E and the other settings still change what new dimensions use, and each dimension keeps its own sizes, which you can also change in the Properties panel. The current style is not saved in the file. Dimensions follow the drawing&#039;s length format (decimal, or feet and inches with \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EUNITS\u003C/code\u003E), or their own \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFormat\u003C/span\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/dimension-settings\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/dimension-settings\"\u003ESet dimension sizes\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"plotting-is-simpler\"\u003EPlotting is simpler\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPlot style tables work by color, as CTB files do: Monochrome, Colour and Grayscale are built in, you can make your own, and each sheet keeps its table. Reading CTB files is built from the published description and has only been tried on files we made; named plot style tables (STB) and page setups are not built yet. Lines print at their lineweight, the others at one pen width. Sheets plot at 1:1 on their own paper; the model plots at a chosen scale or fitted. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/plot-pdf\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/plot-pdf\"\u003EPlot to PDF\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"working-inside-a-viewport\"\u003EWorking inside a viewport\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAs you know it: double-click inside a viewport (or type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMSPACE\u003C/code\u003E) to work on the model through it, and double-click outside it (or \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPSPACE\u003C/code\u003E) to go back to the sheet. Inside, pan and zoom change what the viewport shows, unless its display is locked; then they move the sheet. Clicking a tab under the drawing also leaves the viewport. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/sheets-and-viewports#work-on-the-model-through-a-viewport\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/sheets-and-viewports\" data-hash=\"work-on-the-model-through-a-viewport\"\u003EWork on the model through a viewport\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"tracking-and-dynamic-input-yes-grid-snap-not-yet\"\u003ETracking and dynamic input yes, grid snap not yet\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe grid is for looking at only; points do not snap to it (there is no F9 snap mode). Polar tracking (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F10\"\u003EF10\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF10\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, step set with \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPOLARANG\u003C/code\u003E) and object snap tracking (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F11\"\u003EF11\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF11\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: rest on a snap point to acquire it) work as you know them; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/object-snaps#polar-tracking\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/object-snaps\" data-hash=\"polar-tracking\"\u003ESnap to objects\u003C/a\u003E. Dynamic input (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F12\"\u003EF12\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF12\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) shows distance and angle (or X and Y) by the crosshair; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/coordinate-entry#dynamic-input\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/coordinate-entry\" data-hash=\"dynamic-input\"\u003ECoordinate and distance entry\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"feet-and-inches\"\u003EFeet and inches\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength format\u003C/span\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArchitectural\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEngineering\u003C/span\u003E (or type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EUNITS\u003C/code\u003E), and type lengths as you know them: \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;6-1/2&quot;\u003C/code\u003E. Use a hyphen, not a space, before a fraction: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E is \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Set \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnits\u003C/span\u003E to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInches\u003C/span\u003E too, so that sheets and plots offer architectural scales. The format is kept in saved DWG and DXF files. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/units-papers-scales#feet-and-inches-and-fractions\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/units-papers-scales\" data-hash=\"feet-and-inches-and-fractions\"\u003EFeet and inches\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"copy-and-paste-stays-inside-cadcraft\"\u003ECopy and paste stays inside CadCraft\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command X\"\u003E⌘X\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+X\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E copy, cut and paste objects within a drawing, between drawings, and between CadCraft tabs in the same browser. The clipboard is CadCraft&#039;s own: you cannot paste CadCraft objects into another program, or objects from another program into CadCraft. Pasting from a drawing in other units does not rescale: use \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESCALE\u003C/code\u003E afterwards. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E is a duplicate that moves with the pointer, a CadCraft addition. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/copy-paste\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/copy-paste\"\u003ECopy, paste and duplicate\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"browser-keys-you-cannot-use\"\u003EBrowser keys you cannot use\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA web page cannot take \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command N\"\u003E⌘N\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+N\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command W\"\u003E⌘W\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+W\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command T\"\u003E⌘T\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+T\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E from the browser: they open and close windows and tabs. Use the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew\u003C/span\u003E button or type \u003Ccode\u003ENEW\u003C/code\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/keys\" data-topic=\"basics/keys\"\u003EKeys, mice and trackpads\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"commands-you-may-reach-for-that-are-not-here-yet\"\u003ECommands you may reach for that are not here yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ETABLE\u003C/code\u003E is not built. Typing it says &quot;Unknown command&quot;. (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPEDIT\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESPLINE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESPLINEDIT\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EXREF\u003C/code\u003E are built.) \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/a\u003E lists what to do instead today.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"If you have used AutoCAD, or a program built on the same model, most of what you know works here: the command line, aliases such as L, C, TR and O, Enter or Space to repeat, window and crossing selection, object snaps on F3, Ortho on F8, model space with layouts and viewports. This page lists what maps directly, and then the differences that catch people out. Read the second half even if you skim the first. What maps directly Habit In CadCraft L, PL, C, A, REC, POL, EL, PO, T, DT Same commands, same aliases M, CO, RO, SC, MI, O, TR, EX, F, X, E Same DLI, DAL, DAN, DRA, DDI, DCO, DBA Same dimension commands DIMSCALE, DIMTXT, DIMASZ, DIMDEC, DIMGAP, DIMEXE, DIMEXO typed at the command line Same, for new dimensions B to make a block, I to insert, PU to purge Same (purge removes unused blocks only) LAYOUT, MV, PLOT, DI, ID, LI, Z then E or W Same Typing _LINE or -LAYER from a script or a habit The leading _, -, . or ' is ignored Enter or Space on an empty prompt repeats the last command Same Esc cancels; Esc again clears the selection Same @dx,dy, @d\u003Ca, typed angles counterclockwise from east Same Window left to right, crossing right to left, click-click; press and drag for a lasso, Space cycles window / crossing / fence (LASSOMODE) Same Select first, then the command (noun-verb) Same; a finished command clears the selection ALL and L at Select objects Same Shift+click removes from the selection Same. Settings can make a click replace the selection instead, as PICKADD 0 does: see CadCraft settings Ctrl+C, Ctrl+X, Ctrl+V, Ctrl+Shift+V (COPYCLIP, CUTCLIP, PASTECLIP, PASTEORIG), COPYBASE Same commands and keys; pasting places the copy by its lower-left corner, as PASTECLIP does F3 object snap, F7 grid, F8 Ortho Same keys Double-click the wheel to zoom extents Same Mirrored text stays readable Same (as with MIRRTEXT 0) Ctrl+Z, Ctrl+Y, Ctrl+S, Ctrl+O, Ctrl+P, Ctrl+A Same; on a Mac, Command works too The Command reference lists every command and alias. If you like the ribbon of AutoCAD, pick the Drafting classic menu style in Settings › CadCraft › Menus, keys and mouse: Home, Insert, Annotate, Parametric, View, Manage and Output tabs over the same command line, and the middle button pans while a right click is Enter. See Menus, keys and mouse. The command map lists every command CadCraft has with its short names, and the cheat sheet the 40 you will use most (also as a one-page PDF). Differences that trip people up Typed points are absolute With dynamic input on, the other program treats a typed 100,0 for a second point as relative. CadCraft's command line does not: there, x,y is always measured from the origin. With Dyn (F12) on, use its fields instead: distance, Tab, angle. Use @100,0 for \"100 to the right of the last point\", or move the crosshair the right way and type a bare 100. Shift skips snaps; it does not give Ortho Holding Shift while you click ignores object snaps for that click. It does not temporarily switch Ortho. Use F8 or the Ortho button. Right-click is always Enter There is no right-click menu. A right-click in the drawing is Enter: it ends a command, finishes a selection, or repeats the last command. During a grip move or a selection box, it cancels instead. Drag with the right button to pan. No Space-bar panning Space is Enter, so holding Space and dragging does not pan. Pan with the middle button or the right button, or zoom out and back in at another spot with the wheel. Trim and Extend Trim and Extend start in Quick mode: every visible object is a cutting edge or a boundary, the piece under the pointer shows what will go, a dragged path trims every piece it crosses, and an object nothing crosses is erased. Shift switches between trim and extend. O switches to Standard mode (choose the edges first), F and C are fence and crossing, E is EDGEMODE, R erases. There is no P (Project) option: CadCraft trims in the plane of the drawing. Fillet and chamfer FILLET and CHAMFER take the options you know: Undo, Polyline, Radius (or Distance, Angle and mEthod), Trim and Multiple, and Shift+click for a sharp corner. A fillet between a polyline and a line adds the arc as its own object instead of joining them into the polyline, and polyline corners next to an arc segment are left as they are. Paragraph text and single-line text T, MT and MTEXT are paragraph text typed in place, with a width, line spacing and justification; DT and DTEXT are single-line text, as you know them. There are no text styles or columns, and you cannot type in-text formatting (bold, stacked fractions) yet; formatting that comes in a file is kept, drawn and saved (see Add text and labels). Everything draws in a Helvetica-style font. Save means \"download a copy\" Ctrl+S, SAVE and QSAVE download a copy in the drawing's format: a DWG you opened saves as DWG, a DXF as DXF. In the browser nothing is overwritten in place, and opened files are never changed. In CadCraft Studio, Ctrl+S writes back to the file you opened. SAVEAS (Ctrl+Shift+S) is a small dialog, not a file browser: pick DWG (with its Version, AutoCAD 2000 to 2018), DXF R12 or DXF 2000 and click Save. The browser decides where the download goes. DXFOUT always downloads DXF R12. A DWG you opened saves in the version it came in; a new drawing saves in the AutoCAD 2000 format unless you pick another. Sheets, dimensions and hatch patterns are kept. See Files: what opens, what saves. Layers: simpler Every layer change (on, freeze, lock, color, plot, linetype, lineweight, current layer) is one undo step. Off and freeze have the same effect in CadCraft. Layers a drawing freezes in one viewport stay frozen there: on screen, in plots and when you save it as DWG or DXF. A viewport that shows a layer in its own colour, linetype, lineweight or transparency keeps doing so on screen, in plots and in a saved DWG (DXF keeps the layer's own settings; the save says so). Changing those per viewport is not built yet. There is no layer properties manager and no layer states; RENAME, LAYMRG and LAYDEL rename, merge and delete layers. Linetypes and lineweights are set in the right panel (see Line weights and linetypes); LAYISO, LAYOFF, LAYFRZ and LAYMCUR work by picking. Saving to DXF R12 writes layer names in capitals. A DWG keeps them as you typed them. Dimension styles DIMSTYLE opens a manager for named styles (new, modify, set current, compare, apply). DIMSCALE and the other settings still change what new dimensions use, and each dimension keeps its own sizes, which you can also change in the Properties panel. The current style is not saved in the file. Dimensions follow the drawing's length format (decimal, or feet and inches with UNITS), or their own Format. See Set dimension sizes. Plotting is simpler Plot style tables work by color, as CTB files do: Monochrome, Colour and Grayscale are built in, you can make your own, and each sheet keeps its table. Reading CTB files is built from the published description and has only been tried on files we made; named plot style tables (STB) and page setups are not built yet. Lines print at their lineweight, the others at one pen width. Sheets plot at 1:1 on their own paper; the model plots at a chosen scale or fitted. See Plot to PDF. Working inside a viewport As you know it: double-click inside a viewport (or type MSPACE) to work on the model through it, and double-click outside it (or PSPACE) to go back to the sheet. Inside, pan and zoom change what the viewport shows, unless its display is locked; then they move the sheet. Clicking a tab under the drawing also leaves the viewport. See Work on the model through a viewport. Tracking and dynamic input yes, grid snap not yet The grid is for looking at only; points do not snap to it (there is no F9 snap mode). Polar tracking (F10, step set with POLARANG) and object snap tracking (F11: rest on a snap point to acquire it) work as you know them; see Snap to objects. Dynamic input (F12) shows distance and angle (or X and Y) by the crosshair; see Coordinate and distance entry. Feet and inches Choose Length format Architectural or Engineering (or type UNITS), and type lengths as you know them: 12'6\", 12'6-1/2\". Use a hyphen, not a space, before a fraction: Space is Enter. Set Units to Inches too, so that sheets and plots offer architectural scales. The format is kept in saved DWG and DXF files. See Feet and inches. Copy and paste stays inside CadCraft Ctrl+C, Ctrl+X and Ctrl+V copy, cut and paste objects within a drawing, between drawings, and between CadCraft tabs in the same browser. The clipboard is CadCraft's own: you cannot paste CadCraft objects into another program, or objects from another program into CadCraft. Pasting from a drawing in other units does not rescale: use SCALE afterwards. Ctrl+D is a duplicate that moves with the pointer, a CadCraft addition. See Copy, paste and duplicate. Browser keys you cannot use A web page cannot take Ctrl+N, Ctrl+W or Ctrl+T from the browser: they open and close windows and tabs. Use the New button or type NEW. See Keys, mice and trackpads. Commands you may reach for that are not here yet TABLE is not built. Typing it says \"Unknown command\". (PEDIT, SPLINE, SPLINEDIT and XREF are built.) Not built yet lists what to do instead today.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"command-map","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Command map: what to type","summary":"Every command CadCraft has with its short names, A to Z, made from the program itself, and the habits from other drafting programs that differ.","section":"switching","order":215,"keywords":["command map","commands","aliases","short names","coming from","command list","what to type","erase","trim","equivalent","differences"],"headings":[{"id":"all-commands-275","text":"All commands (275)","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ECadCraft takes the command names and short names people know from other drafting programs: type \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E for a line, \u003Ccode\u003ETR\u003C/code\u003E to trim, \u003Ccode\u003EE\u003C/code\u003E to erase. So there is no translation table to learn for most of them: the name you know is the name here. The list below is every command CadCraft has, made from the program itself. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E repeats the last one; \u003Ccode\u003EHELP\u003C/code\u003E in the command line prints the same list.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe few that are not the same, or not built:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESave\u003C/strong\u003E downloads a copy in the browser (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESAVE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESAVEAS\u003C/code\u003E); in CadCraft Studio it writes the file. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/coming-from\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/coming-from\"\u003EComing from other drafting software\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPLOT\u003C/strong\u003E makes a PDF (also PNG and SVG), with a plot style table; there is no page setup manager.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDIMSTYLE\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EPEDIT\u003C/strong\u003E are built; \u003Cstrong\u003EPEDIT\u003C/strong\u003E has no Edit-vertex option (grips do that).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETABLE\u003C/strong\u003E is not built; \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EXREF\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESPLINE\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EWBLOCK\u003C/code\u003E are.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA routine&#039;s own commands (AutoLISP \u003Ccode\u003EC:NAME\u003C/code\u003E) show up after you load it with \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EAPPLOAD\u003C/code\u003E; they are not listed here.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"all-commands-275\"\u003EAll commands (275)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECommand\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EShort names\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E3DORBIT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E3DO\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EORBIT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOrbit the 3D view with the left button\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EACCESS\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EAX\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETurn drafting without sight on or off: results spoken through your screen reader, a keyboard cursor on the drawing, snap sounds and spoken commands.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EALIASEDIT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit command aliases in one text file (the familiar L, *LINE format)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EALIGN\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EAL\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMove and turn objects so that source points land on destination points (one, two or three pairs), and scale them if you want\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EALIGNPANEL\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EALP\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen the Align and distribute panel\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EALIGNTO\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELine up the selected objects&#039; left, centre, right, top, middle or bottom edges, on the selection, the first or last picked object, or a point\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EAPPLOAD\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EAP\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELoad applications: LISP routines (.lsp) and their dialog files (.dcl); the Startup Suite (AP)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EARC\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThree points, or center, start and end\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EAREA\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EAA\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArea and perimeter by points or of an object; Add and Subtract keep a running total (AA)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EARRAY\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EAR\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECopies in rows and columns, or round a center (AR)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EARRAYPOLAR\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECopies round a center point\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EARRAYRECT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECopies in rows and columns\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EASBUILT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EASB\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EASBUILT reads the as-built points a crew measured (CSV / TXT, as EXPORTPOINTS writes) and compares them with the drawing’s points: within or outside the tolerance, not measured, extra. Marks them on the layer AS-BUILT and downloads a CSV report.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EATTDEF\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EATT\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDDATTDEF\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E-ATTDEF\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDefine an attribute (a tag a block asks a value for) to put in a block (ATT)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EATTEDIT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EATE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E-ATTEDIT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit a block reference&#039;s attribute values on the command line, one after another (Enter keeps a value)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EATTSYNC\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBring a block&#039;s references in line with its attribute definitions: new ones added with their defaults, removed ones dropped, positions and text reset; values kept\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EAUNITS\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EANGUNITS\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHow angles are shown and typed: decimal degrees, degrees-minutes-seconds or bearings like N45°23&#039;30&quot;E (ANGUNITS)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EAUTOCOMPLETE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EAUTOC\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrawing autocomplete on or off, or list what the drawing&#039;s patterns suggest now.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EAUTOCONSTRAIN\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAdd coincident, horizontal and vertical constraints where the geometry is already close\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EAUTODIM\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EADIM\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDimension by rules: overall size, chains along the sides, openings, hole diameters and grid spacing, without repeating what is already dimensioned.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EAUTOSHEET\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EASH\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELays out sheets by itself: picks paper and standard scales and places every view with a caption and a title block.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EBATTMAN\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBlock attribute manager: order, tags, prompts, defaults and modes of a block&#039;s attributes, then its references updated\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EBCLOSE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClose the block editor: save the block or discard the changes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EBEDIT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EBE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBlock editor: edit a block&#039;s objects and attributes; BCLOSE saves (BE)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EBLOCK\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EB\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EBMAKE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMake a block from objects (they become one reference)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EBOX\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA box: two corners and a height (3D)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EBPARAMETER\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EBPARAM\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot built yet: adding a parameter (such as Visibility) to a block.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EBREAK\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EBR\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBreak a line, arc, circle or polyline between two points (BR)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EBREAKATPOINT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESplit a line, arc or polyline in two at a point\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EBVHIDE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EBVH\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIn the block editor: hide objects in the current visibility state.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EBVSHOW\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EBVSH\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIn the block editor: show objects the current visibility state hides (they show faded to pick).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EBVSTATE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EBVS\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIn the block editor, on a block with visibility states: switch to a state, or add, rename or delete one.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ECAL\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWork out a length or number: + − * / and brackets, as you type them anywhere\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ECHAMFER\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ECHA\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBevel the corner between two lines (D two distances, A length and angle, P every corner of a polyline)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ECIRCLE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECenter and radius, diameter, 2 or 3 points\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ECLASS\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPut the selected objects in a class, or with nothing selected set the active class (? lists them)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ECLASSES\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ECLS\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe Classes panel: classes sort objects across layers; show, hide and style them, and override them per viewport (CLS)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ECOMPARESETTINGS\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ECMPSET\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECompare dimension styles, text styles, layers, sheets or units: two in this drawing, or this drawing against another open one, and copy a value across.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ECONSTRAINTBAR\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ECBAR\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShow or hide the constraint bars (Showall / Hideall, or per object)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ECOPY\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ECO\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ECP\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECopy objects, as many times as you click\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ECOPYBASE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECopy objects to the clipboard with a base point you pick\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ECOPYCLIP\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECopy objects to the clipboard (Ctrl+C); PASTECLIP places them\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ECOUNT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EBCOUNT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECount block references: pick one to select and count every reference of its block\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ECURSORSIZE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe crosshair&#039;s size: 100 runs it edge to edge across the drawing (Settings › Cursor › Full screen)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ECUTCLIP\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECut objects to the clipboard (Ctrl+X): copy, then erase\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ECYLINDER\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ECYL\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA cylinder: centre, radius and height (3D)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDCALIGNED\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDCALI\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDimensional constraint: true distance between points, a point and a line, or two lines\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDCANGULAR\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDCANG\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDimensional constraint: angle between two lines, or of an arc\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDCCONVERT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETurn dimensions drawn on constrainable geometry into dimensional constraints\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDCDIAMETER\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDCDIA\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDimensional constraint: diameter of a circle or arc\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDCDISPLAY\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShow or hide dimensional constraints on the drawing\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDCHORIZONTAL\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDCHOR\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDimensional constraint: horizontal distance\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDCLINEAR\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDCLIN\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDimensional constraint: horizontal or vertical distance, from where you place it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDCRADIUS\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDCRAD\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDimensional constraint: radius of a circle or arc\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDCVERTICAL\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDCVER\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDimensional constraint: vertical distance\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDELBLOCKS\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDelete blocks: every reference (nested ones too, on every sheet) and the definitions\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDELCONSTRAINT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDELCON\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERemove every constraint (geometric and dimensional) from the objects you select\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDESCRIBE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDESC\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESay what the drawing holds: how many objects, of what kind, how big, what is selected.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDESELECT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EAI_DESELECT\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESELECTNONE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClear the selection (Esc)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDETAILS\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDET\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYour firm&#039;s own details, found for what you are drawing: find, add a block, list, remove, turn the offers on or off.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDIAGRAM\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ETEXTDIAGRAM\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ET2D\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDraw a diagram from text: a Mermaid flowchart, a PlantUML component diagram, D2 or a from-to list, as blocks and connectors you can edit\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDIMALIGNED\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDAL\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDIMALI\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETrue distance between two points\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDIMANGULAR\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDAN\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDIMANG\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAngle between two lines, of an arc, or by three points\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDIMASZ\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDimension setting: Arrow size\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDIMBASELINE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDBA\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDIMBASE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDimensions stacked from one base line\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDIMCONSTRAINT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDCON\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDimensional constraints: linear, aligned, angular, radius, diameter, convert\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDIMCONTINUE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDCO\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDIMCONT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA chain of dimensions, each from the end of the last\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDIMDEC\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDimension setting: Decimal places\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDIMDIAMETER\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDDI\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDIMDIA\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDiameter of an arc or circle\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDIMEDIT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDED\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDIMED\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChange dimension text (Home, New text, Rotate) or slant the extension lines (Oblique)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDIMEXE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDimension setting: Extension beyond the dimension line\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDIMEXO\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDimension setting: Offset from the origin\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDIMGAP\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDimension setting: Gap round the text\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDIMLINEAR\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDLI\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDIMLIN\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHorizontal or vertical distance (R for any angle)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDIMLUNIT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDimension setting: length format (1 scientific, 2 decimal, 3 engineering, 4 architectural, 5 fractional)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDIMRADIUS\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDRA\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDIMRAD\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERadius of an arc or circle\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDIMSCALE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDimension setting: Overall scale (sizes × this)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDIMSTYLE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDST\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDDIM\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ED\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMake, change, set current, delete, apply and compare named dimension styles (the dialog; -DIMSTYLE on the command line)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDIMTEDIT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDIMTED\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMove dimension text to the left, right or center of the dimension line, or turn it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDIMTEXTUNIT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDTU\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDimension text in mm, cm, m or km, or in the drawing&#039;s own units: the selected dimensions, else new ones (DTU)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDIMTXT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDimension setting: Text height\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDIST\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDI\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDistance and angle between two points\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDISTRIBUTE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESpace the selected objects evenly, side to side or up and down: equal steps between centres, or equal gaps; the outer two stay\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDOCUMENTS\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDOCS\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen the Documents panel: check drawings out and in, revisions, release states and approval for a project folder\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDUPLICATE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDUP\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA copy that moves with the pointer: click to put it down (Ctrl+D)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDXFOUT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDownload the drawing as DXF R12 (Save keeps its own format)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EEATTEDIT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDDATTE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit a block reference&#039;s attributes: values, text and properties, one attribute at a time\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EELLIPSE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EEL\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAxis endpoints and the other half-axis\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EERASE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDELETE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERemove objects\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EEXPLODE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EX\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBreak polylines, blocks and dimensions into their parts\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EEXPORT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EEXP\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESave a copy of the drawing in a file type an add-on writes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EEXPORT3D\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E3DEXPORT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExport the 3D model: GLB, OBJ or STL\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EEXPORTPOINTS\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EEXPPTS\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWrite points, and blocks&#039; insertion points with one attribute as the description, to a CSV or TXT file (PNEZD, PENZD or XYZ) for BIM and survey programs\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EEXTEND\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EEX\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELengthen objects to meet others: click near an end, drag through several, or Shift+click to trim\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EEXTRUDE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EEXT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExtrude closed 2D objects into solids (3D)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EFILLET\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EF\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERound or square the corner between two objects (R radius, P every corner of a polyline, M several)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EFIND\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFind and replace words in text, attribute values and dimension text\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EGCCOINCIDENT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EConstraint: two points meet, or a point stays on an object\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EGCCOLLINEAR\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EConstraint: two lines stay on one line\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EGCCONCENTRIC\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EConstraint: two circles or arcs share a centre\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EGCEQUAL\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EConstraint: two lines stay the same length, or two circles/arcs the same radius\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EGCFIX\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EConstraint: a point or a whole object stays where it is\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EGCHORIZONTAL\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EConstraint: a line (or two points) stays horizontal\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EGCPARALLEL\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EConstraint: two lines stay parallel\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EGCPERPENDICULAR\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EConstraint: two lines stay at right angles\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EGCSYMMETRIC\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EConstraint: two points, lines or circles mirror each other across a line\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EGCTANGENT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EConstraint: a line and a circle or arc (or two of them) stay tangent\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EGCVERTICAL\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EConstraint: a line (or two points) stays vertical\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EGDSIN\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EGDS\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EView an IC layout (a GDSII stream file): every layer and datatype as a drawing layer, flat, in user units; for viewing and measuring only (lab spike)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EGEOMCONSTRAINT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EGCON\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGeometric constraints: horizontal, vertical, parallel, tangent, coincident…\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EGLBOUT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExport the 3D model as GLB (glTF)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EGRIDMOVE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EGM\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMove a grid line and carry what hangs on it: walls stretch, columns, bubbles and tags move, dimensions follow.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EHATCH\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EH\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EBH\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EBHATCH\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E-HATCH\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFill an area with a solid fill or a pattern: click inside it, or select closed objects (H)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EHELP\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E?\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThis list\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EHISTORY\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EHIST\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHISTORY (HIST): the versions of this drawing — copies kept on this computer when Studio saves it, and the cloud&#039;s versions — to open, restore, and compare with what changed shown in colour.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EID\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECoordinates of a point\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EIMAGE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EIM\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGive CadCraft the picture files a drawing uses (a DWG stores only where they were).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EIMAGEATTACH\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EIAT\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EIMAGEATT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlace a picture (PNG or JPEG) from a file.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EIMAGECLIP\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EICL\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClip a picture to a rectangle or a polygon, or remove its clip.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EIMPORT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EIMP\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EImport a file of a kind an add-on reads, into this drawing\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EINSERT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EI\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDDINSERT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlace a block (S scale, R rotate); F from a DWG/DXF file, L from the library\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EINTERSECT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EIN\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EKeep only what solids have in common (3D)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EJOIN\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EJ\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EJoin lines, arcs and polylines that meet end to end into one polyline; lines on one line into one line (J)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ELASER\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ELASERCUT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen the Laser panel: send layers to a laser cutter or engraver (GRBL G-code)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ELASSOMODE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPress and drag draws a lasso (1) or a rectangle (0)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ELAYDEL\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDelete a layer and every object on it (in the model, on sheets and in blocks)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ELAYER\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ELA\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShow the layers panel\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ELAYFRZ\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFreeze the layer of each object you pick\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ELAYISO\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIsolate the layers of the selected objects (every other layer off)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ELAYMCUR\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMake the layer of the object you pick current\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ELAYMRG\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMerge layers: their objects move to a target layer, and the merged layers go\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ELAYOFF\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETurn off the layer of each object you pick\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ELAYON\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETurn every layer on\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ELAYOUT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ELO\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ENEWLAYOUT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA new sheet with a title block and a viewport\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ELAYTHW\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThaw every layer\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ELAYUNISO\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETurn back on the layers LAYISO turned off\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ELENGTHEN\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ELEN\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELengthen or shorten lines, arcs and polylines: by an amount, a percentage, to a total, or by dragging (LEN)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ELIBRARY\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ELIB\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe library panel: place items, check for updates, credits\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ELIMITS\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESet the drawing limits: ON, OFF, or the lower-left and upper-right corners. With the limits ON, points outside them are refused\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ELINE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStraight segments, each its own object\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ELINETYPE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ELT\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ECELTYPE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe linetype new objects get (by layer, by block, or a named one)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ELINKVIEWS\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESYNCVIEWS\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan and zoom every pane together (split screen), to compare two drawings or revisions\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ELIST\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ELI\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ELS\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDescribe objects in the history\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ELTSCALE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ELTS\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe drawing&#039;s linetype scale (every dash and gap is multiplied by it)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ELWDISPLAY\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ELWT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShow or hide lineweights on screen (the LWT button in the status bar)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ELWEIGHT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ELW\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ELINEWEIGHT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe lineweight new objects get (by layer, by block, default, or mm)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EMAPLAYERS\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EML\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EGEOMAP\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Emap layers under the model space: the drawing’s coordinate system, tiles, WMS, WMTS, ArcGIS, pictures\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EMATCHPROP\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EMA\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EPAINTER\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMatch properties: copy one object&#039;s layer, color and more onto others (MA)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EMEASUREGEOM\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EMEA\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMeasure a distance, a radius, an angle or an area (MEA)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EMIRROR\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EMI\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMirror objects across a line\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EMLEADER\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EMLD\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EMLEAD\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMultileader: an arrow, a leader and a note, as one object (MLD)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EMOVE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EM\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMove objects by a base point and a second point\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EMSPACE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EMS\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWork on the model through a viewport of the open sheet (or double-click inside the viewport).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EMTEXT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EMT\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ET\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EParagraph text: drag its width, then type in place; Enter starts a new paragraph (T)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EMVIEW\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EMV\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA viewport onto the model, on a layout\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ENAMEDVIEW\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ENV\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EVIEWS\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENamed views: save the view on screen (or a window) under a name and go back to it (NV)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ENEARBY\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESay what lies close to the cursor, nearest first, with the distance and the side.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ENEW\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStart a new drawing\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EOBJOUT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExport the 3D model as OBJ\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EOFFSET\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EO\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EParallel copies at a distance\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EOLELIST\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EOLES\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EList the OLE objects a drawing carries and put a copy of one&#039;s picture where you choose\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EOPEN\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen a DWG or DXF from this computer\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EOPENIN3D\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen this drawing in the 3D modeling app, in a new tab (nothing is uploaded)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPARAMETERS\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPAR\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EPARAMETERSMANAGER\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EPARAM\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe Parameters panel: user parameters and dimensional constraints in one table\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPARTSLIST\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPLIST\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA parts list table of every standard part and steel section placed from the Library\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPASTEAREA\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPAREA\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPaste an area copied in MarkupCraft (Copy area) at its true size, as a picture.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPASTECLIP\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPaste the clipboard: it follows the cursor, click where it goes (Ctrl+V)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPASTEORIG\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPaste the clipboard at the coordinates it was copied from (Ctrl+Shift+V)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPDF\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGive CadCraft the PDF files the drawing&#039;s underlays use\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPDFADJUST\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFade, contrast, monochrome or switch off a PDF underlay\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPDFATTACH\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPDFATT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAttach a PDF page as an underlay (PDFATTACH)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPDFCLIP\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClip a PDF underlay to a rectangle or polygon\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPDFIMPORT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPDFIM\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETurn a PDF page&#039;s lines, fills and text into drawing objects (PDFIMPORT)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPDFLAYERS\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESwitch the layers of a PDF underlay on or off\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPEDIT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit a polyline: close, open, join, width, fit curve, spline curve, decurve, linetype generation, reverse; lines, arcs and splines can be turned into polylines\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPHOTOSKETCH\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPSK\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETurns a photo of a hand sketch on paper into lines and arcs at a scale, straightening the perspective and giving each pen colour its own layer.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPLAN\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETurn the view so the UCS X axis (Current ucs, a saved Ucs, or the World) is level on screen. Nothing in the drawing moves.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPLINE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPL\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA polyline of line and arc segments\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPLOT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPRINT\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EPUBLISH\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EEXPORTPDF\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlot to PDF: a layout, or the model at a scale\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPLOTSTYLES\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESTYLESMANAGER\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EPSTABLES\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlot style tables: set what each colour prints as (colour, black, gray, ink, lineweight); kept with the drawing (STYLESMANAGER)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPOINT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPO\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPoint objects\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPOINTCLOUD\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPC\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ethe attached point cloud: slice, height, thickness, color, size, visible, zoom, detach\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPOINTCLOUDATTACH\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPCATTACH\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EPOINTCLOUDATT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eattach a point cloud (LAS, LAZ, XYZ, PTS) under the drawing\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPOLARANG\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe polar tracking step in degrees (90, 45, 30, 22.5, 15 …)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPOLYGON\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPOL\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA regular polygon\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPRESSPULL\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPush or pull a face of a solid (3D)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPROPERTIES\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPR\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EPROPS\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ECH\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EMO\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDDMODIFY\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen the Properties panel (also Ctrl+1, or double-click an object)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPROPERTIESCLOSE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPRCLOSE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFold the Properties panel to the edge of the window\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPSPACE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPS\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELeave the viewport you are working in and go back to the sheet (or double-click outside it).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPUBLISH\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E-PUBLISH\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlot several or all sheets into one PDF (a page and a bookmark per sheet)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPURGE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPU\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERemove block definitions nothing uses\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EQPMODE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EQP\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EQuick Properties by the cursor on or off\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EQSELECT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EQS\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EQuick select: every object of a type whose layer, color, size or name matches (QS)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ERASTERSNAP\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETurn snapping to the lines of scanned sheets on or off.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ERAY\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERay: a construction line from a point, endless one way.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ERECTANG\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EREC\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ERECTANGLE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA closed polyline from two corners\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EREDLINE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ERDL\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERedline to revision: read a MarkupCraft redline package, accept or reject each change, cloud the accepted ones with a revision log, and save the answers file (RDL)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EREDO\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERedo what was undone\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EREFCLOSE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFinish editing a block in place: save it, or discard the changes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EREFEDIT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit a block in place, where a reference of it sits, with the rest of the drawing faded; REFCLOSE saves it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EREGEN\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ERE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERebuild what is drawn in this tab from the drawing\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EREGENALL\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EREA\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERebuild what is drawn in every tab, pane and open drawing\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EREGISTERSCANS\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EREGSCAN\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESCANREG\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERegister several scans: place them in one frame by picked point pairs or targets, refine with ICP, export one LAS or E57\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EREINIT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EReload your aliases file\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ERENAME\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E-RENAME\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EREN\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERename a layer or a block (every object or reference follows)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EREVCLOUD\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ERC\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERevision cloud: round a rectangle, a polygon, a freehand path or an object (RC)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EREVOLVE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EREV\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETurn closed 2D objects about an axis into solids (3D)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EROTATE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ERO\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERotate objects about a base point\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESAMPLES\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESAMPLE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen one of CadCraft&#039;s sample drawings (an architectural set, an as-built) to learn from\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESAVE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EQSAVE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDownload the drawing in its format (DWG or DXF)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESAVEAS\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDownload the drawing as DWG or DXF: choose which\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESAYSEL\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESay what is selected, one sentence each.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESCALE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESC\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EScale objects about a base point\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESCALELISTEDIT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESCALELIST\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAdd, edit and delete the drawing&#039;s own scales; they appear in the viewport and plot scale lists and are saved in the file (SCALELIST)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESCANALIGN\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESCL\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAlign a scanned sheet to two points: move, turn and, if you choose, scale it.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESCANATTACH\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESCA\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESCANIMAGE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EInsert a scanned drawing: a PNG, JPEG or TIFF (one page or all pages of a multi-page file) or a PDF page that is a picture.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESCANCLEAN\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESCC\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMake a scanned sheet black and white and remove specks.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESCANCROP\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESCR\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECrop a scanned sheet to a window.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESCANDESKEW\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESCD\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFind the angle a scanned sheet&#039;s long lines lie at and turn the sheet square once you accept the angle.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESCANERASE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESCE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EErase an area of a scanned sheet: a rectangle, a polygon or a lasso.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESCANINFO\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESCI\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShow a scanned sheet&#039;s original file and the steps taken since.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESCANSCALE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESCS\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EScale a scanned sheet: pick two points on it and type the true distance between them.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESCANTRACE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESCT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETrace a scanned sheet into lines and arcs on a new layer, to a tolerance you set. Text and hatching stay as raster.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESELECT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect objects now (for P, Previous, in the next command)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESELECTALL\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EAI_SELALL\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect every object you can edit on this tab (Ctrl+A)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESELECTSIMILAR\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESIM\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect every object like the ones you pick: same type, layer and color (SIM)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESHEETSET\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESSM\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESHEETSETMANAGER\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShows the Sheet Set panel: sheets from many drawings, numbered, published as one PDF.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESHORTCUTS\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EKEYS\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGive any command a key of your own (Ctrl or Alt with a letter, or a free function key), kept in this browser or desktop app.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESHXFONTS\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESHX\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGive CadCraft SHX fonts from a folder or files, so text styles that name them draw in them (list, remove, reverse)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESKETCHFIX\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESKF\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDraw freehand strokes; each becomes a clean line, circle, arc, rectangle or polyline, squared and joined to the others.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESKETCHLIVE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESKL\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESketch with a pen: each stroke becomes a precise line, circle, arc or rectangle as you lift the pen, snapped to the drawing, with its size to type over.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESPLINE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESPL\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDraw a spline through points (or with a control polygon), or turn a polyline into one\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESPLINEDIT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESPE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit a spline: fit points, control points and weights, open or close it, reverse it, or convert it to a polyline\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESPLIT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESplit the drawing area into panes (as VPORTS on the model; from a sheet too)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESTEELMEMBER\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESMEMBER\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA steel member in 3D from a standard section: two points (a beam) or a base point and a height (Column)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESTLOUT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExport the 3D model as STL (millimeters)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESTRETCH\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStretch: drag a crossing window over the ends to move; what is inside moves, the rest stays (S)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESUBTRACT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESU\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETake solids away from others (3D)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESWBOUT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESWMACRO\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDownload a macro for SolidWorks (.swb) that rebuilds the model as one constrained sketch\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESWEEP\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESweep closed 2D objects along a path (line, polyline, arc, circle) into solids (3D)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ETAGS\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETags on block references: define a tag (kind, allowed values, blocks it applies to), set it on the selected references, and list them as a schedule that you can save as CSV\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ETEXT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDT\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDTEXT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESingle-line text; Enter starts the next line\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ETEXTEDIT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EED\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDDEDIT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit text in place: pick a text (or double-click one) (ED)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ETEXTMASK\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ETMASK\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPut a background mask behind texts (the drawing&#039;s background or a colour), or take it away.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ETOOLBAR\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChoose what the toolbar buttons show: icons and text, just icons, or just text (Ctrl+F1)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ETOOLBARDOCK\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ETBDOCK\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPut the toolbar on the top, bottom, left or right of the window\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ETOOLBARROWS\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChoose how many rows the toolbar uses: wrap to fit, 2, or 1 (Shift+Ctrl+F1)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ETOUR\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ETUTORIAL\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETake the six-step first-drawing tour again\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ETRIM\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ETR\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECut objects back to where others cross them: click a piece, drag through several, or Shift+click to extend\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ETRIMEXTENDMODE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHow TRIM and EXTEND start: 1 Quick (every object is a cutting edge or boundary), 0 Standard (choose the edges first)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ETTR\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA circle tangent to two objects, with a radius you give (the same as CIRCLE T).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ETURNLEFT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETurn the view 90° left (counter-clockwise): UCS Z -90, and the view follows. Nothing in the drawing moves.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ETURNRIGHT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETurn the view 90° right (clockwise): UCS Z 90, and the view follows. Nothing in the drawing moves.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ETURNWORLD\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBack to the world: UCS World, and the view is level again.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EUCS\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYour own coordinate system: an origin and an X axis (origin, Z angle, 3point, OBject, Previous, World, NAmed). Points you type, ortho, polar tracking, the grid and new text follow it; the view turns so its X axis is level. Nothing in the drawing moves.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EUNDO\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EU\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUndo the last command\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EUNION\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EUNI\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EJoin solids into one (3D)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EUNITS\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EUN\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDDUNITS\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHow lengths are shown and typed: decimal, feet and inches, fractions (UN)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EUSDOUT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExport the 3D model as USD (.usda text, .usdc binary or a .usdz package)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EVIEW\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E-VIEW\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EV\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStandard 3D views, perspective or parallel (3D)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EVIEWBASE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EFLATSHOT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlace a flat 2D view of the 3D solids (front, top, side or isometric, hidden lines dashed) on a sheet or in the model\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EVIEWLABEL\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EVL\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA view title on a sheet that shows its viewport&#039;s view name, and changes with it.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EVOICE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETurn spoken commands on or off. They are recognised on this computer.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EVPLAYER\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFreeze or thaw layers in some viewports only (Freeze, Thaw, Reset), and choose which layers new viewports start frozen (Vpvisdflt).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EVPORTS\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EVIEWPORTS\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESplit the drawing area into panes, each with its own view (on a sheet: a viewport, as MVIEW)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EVPVIEW\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESet what a viewport shows: pick a window on the model\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EVSCURRENT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EVS\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESHADEMODE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EVisual style of the 3D view: shaded, edges, hidden line, wireframe, X-ray\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EWBLOCK\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EW\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E-WBLOCK\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWrite a block, some objects or the whole drawing to a new DWG or DXF file (W)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EWEDGE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EWE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA wedge: two corners and a height; it slopes away from the first corner (3D)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EWHEREAMI\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EWHERE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESay where the cursor is.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EXATTACH\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EXA\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELink another DWG or DXF into this drawing, shown read-only (XREF lists and reloads them)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EXCLIP\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EXC\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClip block references and linked drawings to a rectangle or a polygon, or remove the clip.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EXLINE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EXL\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EConstruction line, endless both ways: through two points, horizontal, vertical, at an angle, bisecting an angle, or offset from a line.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EXREF\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EXR\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E-XREF\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinked drawings (external references): list, attach, reload, bind, detach\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EZOOM\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EZ\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExtents, or a window\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E","text":"CadCraft takes the command names and short names people know from other drafting programs: type L for a line, TR to trim, E to erase. So there is no translation table to learn for most of them: the name you know is the name here. The list below is every command CadCraft has, made from the program itself. Enter repeats the last one; HELP in the command line prints the same list. The few that are not the same, or not built: Save downloads a copy in the browser (SAVE, SAVEAS); in CadCraft Studio it writes the file. See Coming from other drafting software. PLOT makes a PDF (also PNG and SVG), with a plot style table; there is no page setup manager. DIMSTYLE and PEDIT are built; PEDIT has no Edit-vertex option (grips do that). TABLE is not built; XREF, SPLINE and WBLOCK are. A routine's own commands (AutoLISP C:NAME) show up after you load it with APPLOAD; they are not listed here. All commands (275) Command Short names What it does 3DORBIT 3DO, ORBIT Orbit the 3D view with the left button ACCESS AX Turn drafting without sight on or off: results spoken through your screen reader, a keyboard cursor on the drawing, snap sounds and spoken commands. ALIASEDIT Edit command aliases in one text file (the familiar L, *LINE format) ALIGN AL Move and turn objects so that source points land on destination points (one, two or three pairs), and scale them if you want ALIGNPANEL ALP Open the Align and distribute panel ALIGNTO Line up the selected objects' left, centre, right, top, middle or bottom edges, on the selection, the first or last picked object, or a point APPLOAD AP Load applications: LISP routines (.lsp) and their dialog files (.dcl); the Startup Suite (AP) ARC A Three points, or center, start and end AREA AA Area and perimeter by points or of an object; Add and Subtract keep a running total (AA) ARRAY AR Copies in rows and columns, or round a center (AR) ARRAYPOLAR Copies round a center point ARRAYRECT Copies in rows and columns ASBUILT ASB ASBUILT reads the as-built points a crew measured (CSV / TXT, as EXPORTPOINTS writes) and compares them with the drawing’s points: within or outside the tolerance, not measured, extra. Marks them on the layer AS-BUILT and downloads a CSV report. ATTDEF ATT, DDATTDEF, -ATTDEF Define an attribute (a tag a block asks a value for) to put in a block (ATT) ATTEDIT ATE, -ATTEDIT Edit a block reference's attribute values on the command line, one after another (Enter keeps a value) ATTSYNC Bring a block's references in line with its attribute definitions: new ones added with their defaults, removed ones dropped, positions and text reset; values kept AUNITS ANGUNITS How angles are shown and typed: decimal degrees, degrees-minutes-seconds or bearings like N45°23'30\"E (ANGUNITS) AUTOCOMPLETE AUTOC Drawing autocomplete on or off, or list what the drawing's patterns suggest now. AUTOCONSTRAIN Add coincident, horizontal and vertical constraints where the geometry is already close AUTODIM ADIM Dimension by rules: overall size, chains along the sides, openings, hole diameters and grid spacing, without repeating what is already dimensioned. AUTOSHEET ASH Lays out sheets by itself: picks paper and standard scales and places every view with a caption and a title block. BATTMAN Block attribute manager: order, tags, prompts, defaults and modes of a block's attributes, then its references updated BCLOSE Close the block editor: save the block or discard the changes BEDIT BE Block editor: edit a block's objects and attributes; BCLOSE saves (BE) BLOCK B, BMAKE Make a block from objects (they become one reference) BOX A box: two corners and a height (3D) BPARAMETER BPARAM Not built yet: adding a parameter (such as Visibility) to a block. BREAK BR Break a line, arc, circle or polyline between two points (BR) BREAKATPOINT Split a line, arc or polyline in two at a point BVHIDE BVH In the block editor: hide objects in the current visibility state. BVSHOW BVSH In the block editor: show objects the current visibility state hides (they show faded to pick). BVSTATE BVS In the block editor, on a block with visibility states: switch to a state, or add, rename or delete one. CAL Work out a length or number: + − * / and brackets, as you type them anywhere CHAMFER CHA Bevel the corner between two lines (D two distances, A length and angle, P every corner of a polyline) CIRCLE C Center and radius, diameter, 2 or 3 points CLASS Put the selected objects in a class, or with nothing selected set the active class (? lists them) CLASSES CLS The Classes panel: classes sort objects across layers; show, hide and style them, and override them per viewport (CLS) COMPARESETTINGS CMPSET Compare dimension styles, text styles, layers, sheets or units: two in this drawing, or this drawing against another open one, and copy a value across. CONSTRAINTBAR CBAR Show or hide the constraint bars (Showall / Hideall, or per object) COPY CO, CP Copy objects, as many times as you click COPYBASE Copy objects to the clipboard with a base point you pick COPYCLIP Copy objects to the clipboard (Ctrl+C); PASTECLIP places them COUNT BCOUNT Count block references: pick one to select and count every reference of its block CURSORSIZE The crosshair's size: 100 runs it edge to edge across the drawing (Settings › Cursor › Full screen) CUTCLIP Cut objects to the clipboard (Ctrl+X): copy, then erase CYLINDER CYL A cylinder: centre, radius and height (3D) DCALIGNED DCALI Dimensional constraint: true distance between points, a point and a line, or two lines DCANGULAR DCANG Dimensional constraint: angle between two lines, or of an arc DCCONVERT Turn dimensions drawn on constrainable geometry into dimensional constraints DCDIAMETER DCDIA Dimensional constraint: diameter of a circle or arc DCDISPLAY Show or hide dimensional constraints on the drawing DCHORIZONTAL DCHOR Dimensional constraint: horizontal distance DCLINEAR DCLIN Dimensional constraint: horizontal or vertical distance, from where you place it DCRADIUS DCRAD Dimensional constraint: radius of a circle or arc DCVERTICAL DCVER Dimensional constraint: vertical distance DELBLOCKS Delete blocks: every reference (nested ones too, on every sheet) and the definitions DELCONSTRAINT DELCON Remove every constraint (geometric and dimensional) from the objects you select DESCRIBE DESC Say what the drawing holds: how many objects, of what kind, how big, what is selected. DESELECT AI_DESELECT, SELECTNONE Clear the selection (Esc) DETAILS DET Your firm's own details, found for what you are drawing: find, add a block, list, remove, turn the offers on or off. DIAGRAM TEXTDIAGRAM, T2D Draw a diagram from text: a Mermaid flowchart, a PlantUML component diagram, D2 or a from-to list, as blocks and connectors you can edit DIMALIGNED DAL, DIMALI True distance between two points DIMANGULAR DAN, DIMANG Angle between two lines, of an arc, or by three points DIMASZ Dimension setting: Arrow size DIMBASELINE DBA, DIMBASE Dimensions stacked from one base line DIMCONSTRAINT DCON Dimensional constraints: linear, aligned, angular, radius, diameter, convert DIMCONTINUE DCO, DIMCONT A chain of dimensions, each from the end of the last DIMDEC Dimension setting: Decimal places DIMDIAMETER DDI, DIMDIA Diameter of an arc or circle DIMEDIT DED, DIMED Change dimension text (Home, New text, Rotate) or slant the extension lines (Oblique) DIMEXE Dimension setting: Extension beyond the dimension line DIMEXO Dimension setting: Offset from the origin DIMGAP Dimension setting: Gap round the text DIMLINEAR DLI, DIMLIN Horizontal or vertical distance (R for any angle) DIMLUNIT Dimension setting: length format (1 scientific, 2 decimal, 3 engineering, 4 architectural, 5 fractional) DIMRADIUS DRA, DIMRAD Radius of an arc or circle DIMSCALE Dimension setting: Overall scale (sizes × this) DIMSTYLE DST, DDIM, D Make, change, set current, delete, apply and compare named dimension styles (the dialog; -DIMSTYLE on the command line) DIMTEDIT DIMTED Move dimension text to the left, right or center of the dimension line, or turn it DIMTEXTUNIT DTU Dimension text in mm, cm, m or km, or in the drawing's own units: the selected dimensions, else new ones (DTU) DIMTXT Dimension setting: Text height DIST DI Distance and angle between two points DISTRIBUTE Space the selected objects evenly, side to side or up and down: equal steps between centres, or equal gaps; the outer two stay DOCUMENTS DOCS Open the Documents panel: check drawings out and in, revisions, release states and approval for a project folder DUPLICATE DUP A copy that moves with the pointer: click to put it down (Ctrl+D) DXFOUT Download the drawing as DXF R12 (Save keeps its own format) EATTEDIT DDATTE Edit a block reference's attributes: values, text and properties, one attribute at a time ELLIPSE EL Axis endpoints and the other half-axis ERASE E, DELETE Remove objects EXPLODE X Break polylines, blocks and dimensions into their parts EXPORT EXP Save a copy of the drawing in a file type an add-on writes EXPORT3D 3DEXPORT Export the 3D model: GLB, OBJ or STL EXPORTPOINTS EXPPTS Write points, and blocks' insertion points with one attribute as the description, to a CSV or TXT file (PNEZD, PENZD or XYZ) for BIM and survey programs EXTEND EX Lengthen objects to meet others: click near an end, drag through several, or Shift+click to trim EXTRUDE EXT Extrude closed 2D objects into solids (3D) FILLET F Round or square the corner between two objects (R radius, P every corner of a polyline, M several) FIND Find and replace words in text, attribute values and dimension text GCCOINCIDENT Constraint: two points meet, or a point stays on an object GCCOLLINEAR Constraint: two lines stay on one line GCCONCENTRIC Constraint: two circles or arcs share a centre GCEQUAL Constraint: two lines stay the same length, or two circles/arcs the same radius GCFIX Constraint: a point or a whole object stays where it is GCHORIZONTAL Constraint: a line (or two points) stays horizontal GCPARALLEL Constraint: two lines stay parallel GCPERPENDICULAR Constraint: two lines stay at right angles GCSYMMETRIC Constraint: two points, lines or circles mirror each other across a line GCTANGENT Constraint: a line and a circle or arc (or two of them) stay tangent GCVERTICAL Constraint: a line (or two points) stays vertical GDSIN GDS View an IC layout (a GDSII stream file): every layer and datatype as a drawing layer, flat, in user units; for viewing and measuring only (lab spike) GEOMCONSTRAINT GCON Geometric constraints: horizontal, vertical, parallel, tangent, coincident… GLBOUT Export the 3D model as GLB (glTF) GRIDMOVE GM Move a grid line and carry what hangs on it: walls stretch, columns, bubbles and tags move, dimensions follow. HATCH H, BH, BHATCH, -HATCH Fill an area with a solid fill or a pattern: click inside it, or select closed objects (H) HELP ? This list HISTORY HIST HISTORY (HIST): the versions of this drawing — copies kept on this computer when Studio saves it, and the cloud's versions — to open, restore, and compare with what changed shown in colour. ID Coordinates of a point IMAGE IM Give CadCraft the picture files a drawing uses (a DWG stores only where they were). IMAGEATTACH IAT, IMAGEATT Place a picture (PNG or JPEG) from a file. IMAGECLIP ICL Clip a picture to a rectangle or a polygon, or remove its clip. IMPORT IMP Import a file of a kind an add-on reads, into this drawing INSERT I, DDINSERT Place a block (S scale, R rotate); F from a DWG/DXF file, L from the library INTERSECT IN Keep only what solids have in common (3D) JOIN J Join lines, arcs and polylines that meet end to end into one polyline; lines on one line into one line (J) LASER LASERCUT Open the Laser panel: send layers to a laser cutter or engraver (GRBL G-code) LASSOMODE Press and drag draws a lasso (1) or a rectangle (0) LAYDEL Delete a layer and every object on it (in the model, on sheets and in blocks) LAYER LA Show the layers panel LAYFRZ Freeze the layer of each object you pick LAYISO Isolate the layers of the selected objects (every other layer off) LAYMCUR Make the layer of the object you pick current LAYMRG Merge layers: their objects move to a target layer, and the merged layers go LAYOFF Turn off the layer of each object you pick LAYON Turn every layer on LAYOUT LO, NEWLAYOUT A new sheet with a title block and a viewport LAYTHW Thaw every layer LAYUNISO Turn back on the layers LAYISO turned off LENGTHEN LEN Lengthen or shorten lines, arcs and polylines: by an amount, a percentage, to a total, or by dragging (LEN) LIBRARY LIB The library panel: place items, check for updates, credits LIMITS Set the drawing limits: ON, OFF, or the lower-left and upper-right corners. With the limits ON, points outside them are refused LINE L Straight segments, each its own object LINETYPE LT, CELTYPE The linetype new objects get (by layer, by block, or a named one) LINKVIEWS SYNCVIEWS Pan and zoom every pane together (split screen), to compare two drawings or revisions LIST LI, LS Describe objects in the history LTSCALE LTS The drawing's linetype scale (every dash and gap is multiplied by it) LWDISPLAY LWT Show or hide lineweights on screen (the LWT button in the status bar) LWEIGHT LW, LINEWEIGHT The lineweight new objects get (by layer, by block, default, or mm) MAPLAYERS ML, GEOMAP map layers under the model space: the drawing’s coordinate system, tiles, WMS, WMTS, ArcGIS, pictures MATCHPROP MA, PAINTER Match properties: copy one object's layer, color and more onto others (MA) MEASUREGEOM MEA Measure a distance, a radius, an angle or an area (MEA) MIRROR MI Mirror objects across a line MLEADER MLD, MLEAD Multileader: an arrow, a leader and a note, as one object (MLD) MOVE M Move objects by a base point and a second point MSPACE MS Work on the model through a viewport of the open sheet (or double-click inside the viewport). MTEXT MT, T Paragraph text: drag its width, then type in place; Enter starts a new paragraph (T) MVIEW MV A viewport onto the model, on a layout NAMEDVIEW NV, VIEWS Named views: save the view on screen (or a window) under a name and go back to it (NV) NEARBY Say what lies close to the cursor, nearest first, with the distance and the side. NEW Start a new drawing OBJOUT Export the 3D model as OBJ OFFSET O Parallel copies at a distance OLELIST OLES List the OLE objects a drawing carries and put a copy of one's picture where you choose OPEN Open a DWG or DXF from this computer OPENIN3D Open this drawing in the 3D modeling app, in a new tab (nothing is uploaded) PARAMETERS PAR, PARAMETERSMANAGER, PARAM The Parameters panel: user parameters and dimensional constraints in one table PARTSLIST PLIST A parts list table of every standard part and steel section placed from the Library PASTEAREA PAREA Paste an area copied in MarkupCraft (Copy area) at its true size, as a picture. PASTECLIP Paste the clipboard: it follows the cursor, click where it goes (Ctrl+V) PASTEORIG Paste the clipboard at the coordinates it was copied from (Ctrl+Shift+V) PDF Give CadCraft the PDF files the drawing's underlays use PDFADJUST Fade, contrast, monochrome or switch off a PDF underlay PDFATTACH PDFATT Attach a PDF page as an underlay (PDFATTACH) PDFCLIP Clip a PDF underlay to a rectangle or polygon PDFIMPORT PDFIM Turn a PDF page's lines, fills and text into drawing objects (PDFIMPORT) PDFLAYERS Switch the layers of a PDF underlay on or off PEDIT PE Edit a polyline: close, open, join, width, fit curve, spline curve, decurve, linetype generation, reverse; lines, arcs and splines can be turned into polylines PHOTOSKETCH PSK Turns a photo of a hand sketch on paper into lines and arcs at a scale, straightening the perspective and giving each pen colour its own layer. PLAN Turn the view so the UCS X axis (Current ucs, a saved Ucs, or the World) is level on screen. Nothing in the drawing moves. PLINE PL A polyline of line and arc segments PLOT PRINT, PUBLISH, EXPORTPDF Plot to PDF: a layout, or the model at a scale PLOTSTYLES STYLESMANAGER, PSTABLES Plot style tables: set what each colour prints as (colour, black, gray, ink, lineweight); kept with the drawing (STYLESMANAGER) POINT PO Point objects POINTCLOUD PC the attached point cloud: slice, height, thickness, color, size, visible, zoom, detach POINTCLOUDATTACH PCATTACH, POINTCLOUDATT attach a point cloud (LAS, LAZ, XYZ, PTS) under the drawing POLARANG The polar tracking step in degrees (90, 45, 30, 22.5, 15 …) POLYGON POL A regular polygon PRESSPULL Push or pull a face of a solid (3D) PROPERTIES PR, PROPS, CH, MO, DDMODIFY Open the Properties panel (also Ctrl+1, or double-click an object) PROPERTIESCLOSE PRCLOSE Fold the Properties panel to the edge of the window PSPACE PS Leave the viewport you are working in and go back to the sheet (or double-click outside it). PUBLISH -PUBLISH Plot several or all sheets into one PDF (a page and a bookmark per sheet) PURGE PU Remove block definitions nothing uses QPMODE QP Quick Properties by the cursor on or off QSELECT QS Quick select: every object of a type whose layer, color, size or name matches (QS) RASTERSNAP Turn snapping to the lines of scanned sheets on or off. RAY Ray: a construction line from a point, endless one way. RECTANG REC, RECTANGLE A closed polyline from two corners REDLINE RDL Redline to revision: read a MarkupCraft redline package, accept or reject each change, cloud the accepted ones with a revision log, and save the answers file (RDL) REDO Redo what was undone REFCLOSE Finish editing a block in place: save it, or discard the changes REFEDIT Edit a block in place, where a reference of it sits, with the rest of the drawing faded; REFCLOSE saves it REGEN RE Rebuild what is drawn in this tab from the drawing REGENALL REA Rebuild what is drawn in every tab, pane and open drawing REGISTERSCANS REGSCAN, SCANREG Register several scans: place them in one frame by picked point pairs or targets, refine with ICP, export one LAS or E57 REINIT Reload your aliases file RENAME -RENAME, REN Rename a layer or a block (every object or reference follows) REVCLOUD RC Revision cloud: round a rectangle, a polygon, a freehand path or an object (RC) REVOLVE REV Turn closed 2D objects about an axis into solids (3D) ROTATE RO Rotate objects about a base point SAMPLES SAMPLE Open one of CadCraft's sample drawings (an architectural set, an as-built) to learn from SAVE QSAVE Download the drawing in its format (DWG or DXF) SAVEAS Download the drawing as DWG or DXF: choose which SAYSEL Say what is selected, one sentence each. SCALE SC Scale objects about a base point SCALELISTEDIT SCALELIST Add, edit and delete the drawing's own scales; they appear in the viewport and plot scale lists and are saved in the file (SCALELIST) SCANALIGN SCL Align a scanned sheet to two points: move, turn and, if you choose, scale it. SCANATTACH SCA, SCANIMAGE Insert a scanned drawing: a PNG, JPEG or TIFF (one page or all pages of a multi-page file) or a PDF page that is a picture. SCANCLEAN SCC Make a scanned sheet black and white and remove specks. SCANCROP SCR Crop a scanned sheet to a window. SCANDESKEW SCD Find the angle a scanned sheet's long lines lie at and turn the sheet square once you accept the angle. SCANERASE SCE Erase an area of a scanned sheet: a rectangle, a polygon or a lasso. SCANINFO SCI Show a scanned sheet's original file and the steps taken since. SCANSCALE SCS Scale a scanned sheet: pick two points on it and type the true distance between them. SCANTRACE SCT Trace a scanned sheet into lines and arcs on a new layer, to a tolerance you set. Text and hatching stay as raster. SELECT Select objects now (for P, Previous, in the next command) SELECTALL AI_SELALL Select every object you can edit on this tab (Ctrl+A) SELECTSIMILAR SIM Select every object like the ones you pick: same type, layer and color (SIM) SHEETSET SSM, SHEETSETMANAGER Shows the Sheet Set panel: sheets from many drawings, numbered, published as one PDF. SHORTCUTS KEYS Give any command a key of your own (Ctrl or Alt with a letter, or a free function key), kept in this browser or desktop app. SHXFONTS SHX Give CadCraft SHX fonts from a folder or files, so text styles that name them draw in them (list, remove, reverse) SKETCHFIX SKF Draw freehand strokes; each becomes a clean line, circle, arc, rectangle or polyline, squared and joined to the others. SKETCHLIVE SKL Sketch with a pen: each stroke becomes a precise line, circle, arc or rectangle as you lift the pen, snapped to the drawing, with its size to type over. SPLINE SPL Draw a spline through points (or with a control polygon), or turn a polyline into one SPLINEDIT SPE Edit a spline: fit points, control points and weights, open or close it, reverse it, or convert it to a polyline SPLIT Split the drawing area into panes (as VPORTS on the model; from a sheet too) STEELMEMBER SMEMBER A steel member in 3D from a standard section: two points (a beam) or a base point and a height (Column) STLOUT Export the 3D model as STL (millimeters) STRETCH S Stretch: drag a crossing window over the ends to move; what is inside moves, the rest stays (S) SUBTRACT SU Take solids away from others (3D) SWBOUT SWMACRO Download a macro for SolidWorks (.swb) that rebuilds the model as one constrained sketch SWEEP Sweep closed 2D objects along a path (line, polyline, arc, circle) into solids (3D) TAGS Tags on block references: define a tag (kind, allowed values, blocks it applies to), set it on the selected references, and list them as a schedule that you can save as CSV TEXT DT, DTEXT Single-line text; Enter starts the next line TEXTEDIT ED, DDEDIT Edit text in place: pick a text (or double-click one) (ED) TEXTMASK TMASK Put a background mask behind texts (the drawing's background or a colour), or take it away. TOOLBAR Choose what the toolbar buttons show: icons and text, just icons, or just text (Ctrl+F1) TOOLBARDOCK TBDOCK Put the toolbar on the top, bottom, left or right of the window TOOLBARROWS Choose how many rows the toolbar uses: wrap to fit, 2, or 1 (Shift+Ctrl+F1) TOUR TUTORIAL Take the six-step first-drawing tour again TRIM TR Cut objects back to where others cross them: click a piece, drag through several, or Shift+click to extend TRIMEXTENDMODE How TRIM and EXTEND start: 1 Quick (every object is a cutting edge or boundary), 0 Standard (choose the edges first) TTR A circle tangent to two objects, with a radius you give (the same as CIRCLE T). TURNLEFT Turn the view 90° left (counter-clockwise): UCS Z -90, and the view follows. Nothing in the drawing moves. TURNRIGHT Turn the view 90° right (clockwise): UCS Z 90, and the view follows. Nothing in the drawing moves. TURNWORLD Back to the world: UCS World, and the view is level again. UCS Your own coordinate system: an origin and an X axis (origin, Z angle, 3point, OBject, Previous, World, NAmed). Points you type, ortho, polar tracking, the grid and new text follow it; the view turns so its X axis is level. Nothing in the drawing moves. UNDO U Undo the last command UNION UNI Join solids into one (3D) UNITS UN, DDUNITS How lengths are shown and typed: decimal, feet and inches, fractions (UN) USDOUT Export the 3D model as USD (.usda text, .usdc binary or a .usdz package) VIEW -VIEW, V Standard 3D views, perspective or parallel (3D) VIEWBASE FLATSHOT Place a flat 2D view of the 3D solids (front, top, side or isometric, hidden lines dashed) on a sheet or in the model VIEWLABEL VL A view title on a sheet that shows its viewport's view name, and changes with it. VOICE Turn spoken commands on or off. They are recognised on this computer. VPLAYER Freeze or thaw layers in some viewports only (Freeze, Thaw, Reset), and choose which layers new viewports start frozen (Vpvisdflt). VPORTS VIEWPORTS Split the drawing area into panes, each with its own view (on a sheet: a viewport, as MVIEW) VPVIEW Set what a viewport shows: pick a window on the model VSCURRENT VS, SHADEMODE Visual style of the 3D view: shaded, edges, hidden line, wireframe, X-ray WBLOCK W, -WBLOCK Write a block, some objects or the whole drawing to a new DWG or DXF file (W) WEDGE WE A wedge: two corners and a height; it slopes away from the first corner (3D) WHEREAMI WHERE Say where the cursor is. XATTACH XA Link another DWG or DXF into this drawing, shown read-only (XREF lists and reloads them) XCLIP XC Clip block references and linked drawings to a rectangle or a polygon, or remove the clip. XLINE XL Construction line, endless both ways: through two points, horizontal, vertical, at an angle, bisecting an angle, or offset from a line. XREF XR, -XREF Linked drawings (external references): list, attach, reload, bind, detach ZOOM Z Extents, or a window","lang":"en"},{"slug":"settings","scope":"cadcraft","title":"CadCraft settings","summary":"The settings that belong to the drafting app: cursor size, where a pasted copy hangs from the cursor, what a click does to the selection, how many rows the toolbar uses, and the corner indicator.","section":"reference","order":5,"keywords":["cursorsize","full screen crosshair","full-screen cursor","edge to edge","toolbar rows","toolbar display","ctrl f1","settings","preferences","options","corner indicator","view cube","axis icon","ucs icon","cursor","crosshair","pick box","paste point","base point","clicking objects","selection","pickadd","shift","reset"],"headings":[{"id":"cursor","text":"Cursor","level":2},{"id":"paste-point","text":"Paste point","level":2},{"id":"clicking-objects","text":"Clicking objects","level":2},{"id":"clicking-an-empty-spot","text":"Clicking an empty spot","level":2},{"id":"dragging-to-select","text":"Dragging to select","level":2},{"id":"dragging-to-trim-and-extend","text":"Dragging to trim and extend","level":2},{"id":"toolbar-position","text":"Toolbar position","level":2},{"id":"toolbar-rows","text":"Toolbar rows","level":2},{"id":"corner-indicator","text":"Corner indicator","level":2},{"id":"length-and-area-of-the-selection","text":"Length and area of the selection","level":2},{"id":"grid-ortho-and-snaps","text":"Grid, Ortho and snaps","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E with the gear in the app bar or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The first tab, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E, holds the settings that belong to this app; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E holds the ones every app in the family shares (theme, panel text size and widths, the toolbar&#039;s look and order). CadCraft&#039;s settings exist only in the app: you change them here, and this browser remembers them. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset CadCraft settings\u003C/span\u003E puts every one back to the start.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"cursor\"\u003ECursor\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESmall\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EMedium\u003C/strong\u003E (the start), \u003Cstrong\u003ELarge\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003EFull screen\u003C/strong\u003E: the size of the crosshair and of the pick box in the drawing. A bigger pick box also picks objects from a little farther away, which helps on a touch screen or a very fine drawing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFull screen\u003C/strong\u003E runs the crosshair&#039;s two lines edge to edge across the drawing, also when no command is running, so you can check by eye that two things line up across the whole view. With the view split, the lines stay in the pane you are working in. The same as AutoCAD&#039;s \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECURSORSIZE\u003C/code\u003E 100: type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECURSORSIZE\u003C/code\u003E and 100 for full screen; a smaller number picks the nearest size (under 5 Small, 5 to 9 Medium, 10 to 99 Large).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"paste-point\"\u003EPaste point\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhere a pasted copy hangs from the cursor after \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPASTECLIP\u003C/code\u003E), until you click:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELower-left corner\u003C/strong\u003E (the start): the lower-left corner of the copied objects&#039; extents, as in AutoCAD.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECenter\u003C/strong\u003E: the middle of their extents. Handy for placing a symbol by eye.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA copy made with \u003Ccode\u003ECOPYBASE\u003C/code\u003E always hangs from the base point you picked. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste in place\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command V\"\u003E⇧⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) ignores this setting: it keeps the original coordinates. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/copy-paste\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/copy-paste\"\u003ECopy, paste and duplicate\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"clicking-objects\"\u003EClicking objects\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat a click in the drawing does to the selection when no command is running:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAdds to the selection\u003C/strong\u003E (the start): each click or window adds objects; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click, or a \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+window, takes them out.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EReplaces the selection\u003C/strong\u003E: a click or window selects only what it picks. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click adds an object, or takes out one that is already selected; a \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+window adds.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAt a \u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects:\u003C/code\u003E prompt inside a command, clicks always add (and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E removes), whatever this setting says. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/select-objects\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/select-objects\"\u003ESelect objects\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"clicking-an-empty-spot\"\u003EClicking an empty spot\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat a click where there is no object does when no command is running. Either way it starts a selection window (a second click finishes it).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EClears the selection\u003C/strong\u003E (the start): what was selected is let go. Hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E as you click to keep it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EKeeps the selection\u003C/strong\u003E: the selection stays, as in other drafting programs; the window adds to it. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to clear the selection.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"dragging-to-select\"\u003EDragging to select\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat pressing and dragging on an empty spot draws:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELasso\u003C/strong\u003E (the start): a freehand outline. Moving right first selects what lies wholly inside it, left first what it touches; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while dragging switches between window, crossing and fence.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERectangle\u003C/strong\u003E: the box, as a click and a second click draw it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELASSOMODE\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E1\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E0\u003C/code\u003E sets the same. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/lasso-selection\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/lasso-selection\"\u003ELasso selection\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"dragging-to-trim-and-extend\"\u003EDragging to trim and extend\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat pressing on an empty spot and dragging does in \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ETRIM\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXTEND\u003C/code\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA path\u003C/strong\u003E (the start): a freehand path; every piece it crosses is trimmed (or extended).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA window\u003C/strong\u003E: a box; every piece it touches is trimmed, as the \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E option does.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/trim-and-extend\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/trim-and-extend\"\u003ETrim and extend\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"toolbar-position\"\u003EToolbar position\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EToolbar position\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cstrong\u003ETop\u003C/strong\u003E (the start, how it has always been), \u003Cstrong\u003EBottom\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ELeft\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003ERight\u003C/strong\u003E. On the left or the right the tool groups stack in a column that scrolls when it is taller than the window, and the buttons wrap two or three to a line. The choice is saved with your settings, and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ETOOLBARDOCK\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003ETBDOCK\u003C/code\u003E) sets it from the command line. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset the layout\u003C/span\u003E puts the toolbar back on top.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe menu styles&#039; bars sit inside the toolbar and move with it, but the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERibbon\u003C/span\u003E style is made to run across the top or the bottom: on a side it gets a narrow column that scrolls. On a phone the toolbar stays where the phone layout puts it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"toolbar-rows\"\u003EToolbar rows\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow many lines the tools use above the drawing, in the CadCraft toolbar and in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrafting classic\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERibbon\u003C/span\u003E styles:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWrap to fit\u003C/strong\u003E (the start): the tools wrap to the width of the window.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E2 rows\u003C/strong\u003E: at most two lines. When the tools wrap to two lines or fewer at this width they just wrap; otherwise they sit in two rows that scroll sideways.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E1 row\u003C/strong\u003E: one line that scrolls sideways, which leaves the drawing the most room.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F1\"\u003E⌃⇧ F1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+Ctrl+F1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E steps through the three, and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ETOOLBARROWS\u003C/code\u003E sets one. What the buttons show (icons and text, just icons, or just text) is a setting of \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EToolbar\u003C/span\u003E; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control F1\"\u003E⌃ F1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+F1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E steps through it and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ETOOLBAR\u003C/code\u003E sets it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"corner-indicator\"\u003ECorner indicator\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat sits in the corner of the drawing:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAutomatic\u003C/strong\u003E (the start): the axis icon on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab and the view cube on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAxis icon\u003C/strong\u003E: the X and Y marker at the bottom left of the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab, and a small X, Y and Z marker on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EView cube\u003C/strong\u003E: a cube at the top right of both tabs. On the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab it shows the drawing from the top, and clicking another face, edge or corner opens the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E tab looking from there. On the 3D tab it turns with the view: click to look from a side, drag to orbit, and the house goes back to the iso view. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/3d-solids\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/3d-solids\"\u003EModel in 3D\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENone\u003C/strong\u003E: nothing in the corner.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESheets show neither.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"length-and-area-of-the-selection\"\u003ELength and area of the selection\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIn the status bar\u003C/strong\u003E (the start): with objects selected, the status bar shows their total length and the total area of the closed ones. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/properties-panel#totals-of-a-selection\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/properties-panel\" data-hash=\"totals-of-a-selection\"\u003ETotals of a selection\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EHidden\u003C/strong\u003E: the status bar leaves them out. Properties still shows them for several objects.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"grid-ortho-and-snaps\"\u003EGrid, Ortho and snaps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThese are not in the dialog: the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGrid\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrtho\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESnap\u003C/span\u003E buttons in the status bar (or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F7\"\u003EF7\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF7\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F8\"\u003EF8\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF8\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F3\"\u003EF3\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF3\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) and the list of object snaps beside them are remembered as you leave them. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/object-snaps\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/object-snaps\"\u003ESnap to points on objects\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Open Settings with the gear in the app bar or Ctrl+,. The first tab, CadCraft, holds the settings that belong to this app; General holds the ones every app in the family shares (theme, panel text size and widths, the toolbar's look and order). CadCraft's settings exist only in the app: you change them here, and this browser remembers them. Reset CadCraft settings puts every one back to the start. Cursor Small, Medium (the start), Large or Full screen: the size of the crosshair and of the pick box in the drawing. A bigger pick box also picks objects from a little farther away, which helps on a touch screen or a very fine drawing. Full screen runs the crosshair's two lines edge to edge across the drawing, also when no command is running, so you can check by eye that two things line up across the whole view. With the view split, the lines stay in the pane you are working in. The same as AutoCAD's CURSORSIZE 100: type CURSORSIZE and 100 for full screen; a smaller number picks the nearest size (under 5 Small, 5 to 9 Medium, 10 to 99 Large). Paste point Where a pasted copy hangs from the cursor after Ctrl+V (PASTECLIP), until you click: Lower-left corner (the start): the lower-left corner of the copied objects' extents, as in AutoCAD. Center: the middle of their extents. Handy for placing a symbol by eye. A copy made with COPYBASE always hangs from the base point you picked. Paste in place (Ctrl+Shift+V) ignores this setting: it keeps the original coordinates. See Copy, paste and duplicate. Clicking objects What a click in the drawing does to the selection when no command is running: Adds to the selection (the start): each click or window adds objects; Shift+click, or a Shift+window, takes them out. Replaces the selection: a click or window selects only what it picks. Shift+click adds an object, or takes out one that is already selected; a Shift+window adds. At a Select objects: prompt inside a command, clicks always add (and Shift removes), whatever this setting says. See Select objects. Clicking an empty spot What a click where there is no object does when no command is running. Either way it starts a selection window (a second click finishes it). Clears the selection (the start): what was selected is let go. Hold Shift as you click to keep it. Keeps the selection: the selection stays, as in other drafting programs; the window adds to it. Press Esc to clear the selection. Dragging to select What pressing and dragging on an empty spot draws: Lasso (the start): a freehand outline. Moving right first selects what lies wholly inside it, left first what it touches; Space while dragging switches between window, crossing and fence. Rectangle: the box, as a click and a second click draw it. LASSOMODE 1 or 0 sets the same. See Lasso selection. Dragging to trim and extend What pressing on an empty spot and dragging does in TRIM and EXTEND: A path (the start): a freehand path; every piece it crosses is trimmed (or extended). A window: a box; every piece it touches is trimmed, as the C option does. See Trim and extend. Toolbar position CadCraft › Toolbar position: Top (the start, how it has always been), Bottom, Left or Right. On the left or the right the tool groups stack in a column that scrolls when it is taller than the window, and the buttons wrap two or three to a line. The choice is saved with your settings, and TOOLBARDOCK (TBDOCK) sets it from the command line. General › Reset the layout puts the toolbar back on top. The menu styles' bars sit inside the toolbar and move with it, but the Ribbon style is made to run across the top or the bottom: on a side it gets a narrow column that scrolls. On a phone the toolbar stays where the phone layout puts it. Toolbar rows How many lines the tools use above the drawing, in the CadCraft toolbar and in the Drafting classic and Ribbon styles: Wrap to fit (the start): the tools wrap to the width of the window. 2 rows: at most two lines. When the tools wrap to two lines or fewer at this width they just wrap; otherwise they sit in two rows that scroll sideways. 1 row: one line that scrolls sideways, which leaves the drawing the most room. Shift+Ctrl+F1 steps through the three, and TOOLBARROWS sets one. What the buttons show (icons and text, just icons, or just text) is a setting of General › Toolbar; Ctrl+F1 steps through it and TOOLBAR sets it. Corner indicator What sits in the corner of the drawing: Automatic (the start): the axis icon on the Model tab and the view cube on the 3D tab. Axis icon: the X and Y marker at the bottom left of the Model tab, and a small X, Y and Z marker on the 3D tab. View cube: a cube at the top right of both tabs. On the Model tab it shows the drawing from the top, and clicking another face, edge or corner opens the 3D tab looking from there. On the 3D tab it turns with the view: click to look from a side, drag to orbit, and the house goes back to the iso view. See Model in 3D. None: nothing in the corner. Sheets show neither. Length and area of the selection In the status bar (the start): with objects selected, the status bar shows their total length and the total area of the closed ones. See Totals of a selection. Hidden: the status bar leaves them out. Properties still shows them for several objects. Grid, Ortho and snaps These are not in the dialog: the Grid, Ortho and Snap buttons in the status bar (or F7, F8, F3) and the list of object snaps beside them are remembered as you leave them. See Snap to points on objects.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"menus-keys-and-mouse","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Menus, keys and mouse","summary":"Pick a menu style that matches the way you already work, give any command your own key or a key that types a command, move commands between ribbon tabs, and set what the wheel and the mouse buttons do.","section":"reference","order":6,"keywords":["menu style","menus","ribbon","toolbar","command line","keyboard shortcuts","hotkeys","keys","customize","remap","rebind","shortcuts","text keys","mouse","wheel","invert","zoom direction","middle button","right button","pan","export","import","switching","familiar","drafting classic"],"headings":[{"id":"the-three-styles","text":"The three styles","level":2},{"id":"make-it-your-own","text":"Make it your own","level":2},{"id":"keys-that-type-a-command","text":"Keys that type a command","level":2},{"id":"mouse","text":"Mouse","level":2},{"id":"take-it-with-you","text":"Take it with you","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ECadCraft&#039;s commands are the same whichever way you reach them, and the command line is always there with its aliases (\u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ETR\u003C/code\u003E …). A \u003Cstrong\u003Emenu style\u003C/strong\u003E only decides where the buttons sit on screen, what they are called there and which keys run them. Choose one in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMenus, keys and mouse\u003C/span\u003E; the change is immediate, and this browser remembers it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-three-styles\"\u003EThe three styles\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EStyle\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat you get\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECadCraft&#039;s own toolbar: File, Draw, Modify, Annotate, Parametric, Blocks, Sheets, Edit and View, each with a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E menu. The start.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrafting classic\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA ribbon laid out the way classic 2D drafting programs lay theirs: a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E menu and quick buttons, then the tabs \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHome\u003C/span\u003E (Draw, Modify, Annotation, Layers, Block, Properties, Utilities, Clipboard), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInsert\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAnnotate\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EParametric\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EManage\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOutput\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E. Type commands as always. The middle button pans; a right click is \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, and dragging with the right button does nothing.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERibbon\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA ribbon of plain task tabs: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraw\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModify\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAnnotate\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EParametric\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBlocks\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESheets\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETools\u003C/span\u003E, with a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E menu and quick buttons on the right. The middle and right buttons pan, as in CadCraft&#039;s own style.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery style keeps CadCraft&#039;s keys: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command S\"\u003E⇧⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command P\"\u003E⌘P\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+P\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command X\"\u003E⌘X\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+X\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F3\"\u003EF3\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF3\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F7\"\u003EF7\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF7\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F8\"\u003EF8\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF8\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F10\"\u003EF10\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF10\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F11\"\u003EF11\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF11\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F12\"\u003EF12\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF12\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for the drafting aids. Letters are never keys: CadCraft types them into the command line.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe ribbons also hold the Layers panel&#039;s tools and the drafting aids of the status bar. The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E tab fills once the 3D tab under the drawing has been opened; a 3D tool picked on the Model tab opens the 3D tab first, and a 2D tool picked on the 3D tab goes back to the Model tab first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA key that belongs to CadCraft&#039;s own layout but that your style gave another command does nothing, and the status bar says where its command went (&quot;In My keys, Ortho is Ctrl+Alt+O&quot;). \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and the arrows always keep their meaning. While a command runs, or while letters are typed at the command line, keys belong to the command line.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"make-it-your-own\"\u003EMake it your own\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick the style closest to what you want, then click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECustomize…\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeys and menus\u003C/span\u003E, find a command (the search box helps). \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a key\u003C/span\u003E and press the key. Click a key to take it off.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith a ribbon, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWhere it sits\u003C/span\u003E moves the command to another tab and panel, to the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E menu, or to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeys only\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMouse\u003C/span\u003E, set the wheel and the buttons that pan the drawing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGive it a name and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESaving a built-in style makes your own copy (&quot;My Drafting classic&quot;); the built-in one never changes. A key given to two commands is shown in red and has to be fixed before you can save.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"keys-that-type-a-command\"\u003EKeys that type a command\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESHORTCUTS\u003C/code\u003E (View › More › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeyboard shortcuts…\u003C/span\u003E) gives a key a whole command line, such as \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Option L\"\u003E⌃⌥L\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Alt+L\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for \u003Ccode\u003ELINE\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F9\"\u003EF9\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF9\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for \u003Ccode\u003EZOOM E\u003C/code\u003E. These keys belong to the style in use: the first time this version opens, the keys you made in an older version are carried into your own style &quot;My CadCraft keys&quot;, once, and work as before. Exporting the style takes them along.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mouse\"\u003EMouse\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMouse wheel\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScroll up zooms in\u003C/span\u003E (the start) or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScroll up zooms out\u003C/span\u003E, and how fast. A trackpad pinch always zooms the way your fingers move.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPan the drawing with\u003C/strong\u003E: the middle button, the right button, or both. A right click without dragging is always \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The left button always picks and draws in CadCraft, so ticking it changes nothing here.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDrag in the 3D views\u003C/strong\u003E: the 3D tab keeps its own buttons in every style (left orbits, middle and right pan).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"take-it-with-you\"\u003ETake it with you\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E saves the style in use as a small \u003Ccode\u003E.ccinput.json\u003C/code\u003E file; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport…\u003C/span\u003E adds one, from another computer or a colleague. An imported file can only point at CadCraft&#039;s own commands: anything else in it is left out and counted in the status bar. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete this style\u003C/span\u003E removes one of your own.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThese settings stay in this browser. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUse this style in every app\u003C/span\u003E under the style cards makes every app open in its matching style (its own toolbar, a ribbon or Drafting classic), with the changes you made in each app. CadCraft has no menu-bar style: when another app&#039;s menu bar is chosen for every app, CadCraft keeps its own toolbar.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERelated: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/keyboard\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/keyboard\"\u003EKeyboard and mouse\u003C/a\u003E · \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/settings\"\u003ECadCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E · \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/coming-from\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/coming-from\"\u003EComing from other drafting software\u003C/a\u003E · \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/command-aliases\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/command-aliases\"\u003ECommand aliases\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/p\u003E","text":"CadCraft's commands are the same whichever way you reach them, and the command line is always there with its aliases (L, C, TR …). A menu style only decides where the buttons sit on screen, what they are called there and which keys run them. Choose one in Settings (Ctrl+,) › CadCraft › Menus, keys and mouse; the change is immediate, and this browser remembers it. The three styles Style What you get CadCraft CadCraft's own toolbar: File, Draw, Modify, Annotate, Parametric, Blocks, Sheets, Edit and View, each with a More menu. The start. Drafting classic A ribbon laid out the way classic 2D drafting programs lay theirs: a File menu and quick buttons, then the tabs Home (Draw, Modify, Annotation, Layers, Block, Properties, Utilities, Clipboard), Insert, Annotate, Parametric, View, Manage, Output and 3D. Type commands as always. The middle button pans; a right click is Enter, and dragging with the right button does nothing. Ribbon A ribbon of plain task tabs: Draw, Modify, Annotate, Parametric, Blocks, Sheets, View, 3D and Tools, with a File menu and quick buttons on the right. The middle and right buttons pan, as in CadCraft's own style. Every style keeps CadCraft's keys: Ctrl+Z, Ctrl+Y, Ctrl+S, Ctrl+Shift+S, Ctrl+O, Ctrl+P, Ctrl+C, Ctrl+X, Ctrl+V, Ctrl+D, Ctrl+A, and F3, F7, F8, F10, F11, F12 for the drafting aids. Letters are never keys: CadCraft types them into the command line. The ribbons also hold the Layers panel's tools and the drafting aids of the status bar. The 3D tab fills once the 3D tab under the drawing has been opened; a 3D tool picked on the Model tab opens the 3D tab first, and a 2D tool picked on the 3D tab goes back to the Model tab first. A key that belongs to CadCraft's own layout but that your style gave another command does nothing, and the status bar says where its command went (\"In My keys, Ortho is Ctrl+Alt+O\"). Delete, Esc, Enter, Tab, Space and the arrows always keep their meaning. While a command runs, or while letters are typed at the command line, keys belong to the command line. Make it your own Pick the style closest to what you want, then click Customize…. On Keys and menus, find a command (the search box helps). Add a key and press the key. Click a key to take it off. With a ribbon, Where it sits moves the command to another tab and panel, to the File menu, or to Keys only. On Mouse, set the wheel and the buttons that pan the drawing. Give it a name and click Save. Saving a built-in style makes your own copy (\"My Drafting classic\"); the built-in one never changes. A key given to two commands is shown in red and has to be fixed before you can save. Keys that type a command SHORTCUTS (View › More › Keyboard shortcuts…) gives a key a whole command line, such as Ctrl+Alt+L for LINE or F9 for ZOOM E. These keys belong to the style in use: the first time this version opens, the keys you made in an older version are carried into your own style \"My CadCraft keys\", once, and work as before. Exporting the style takes them along. Mouse Mouse wheel: Scroll up zooms in (the start) or Scroll up zooms out, and how fast. A trackpad pinch always zooms the way your fingers move. Pan the drawing with: the middle button, the right button, or both. A right click without dragging is always Enter. The left button always picks and draws in CadCraft, so ticking it changes nothing here. Drag in the 3D views: the 3D tab keeps its own buttons in every style (left orbits, middle and right pan). Take it with you Export saves the style in use as a small .ccinput.json file; Import… adds one, from another computer or a colleague. An imported file can only point at CadCraft's own commands: anything else in it is left out and counted in the status bar. Delete this style removes one of your own. These settings stay in this browser. Use this style in every app under the style cards makes every app open in its matching style (its own toolbar, a ribbon or Drafting classic), with the changes you made in each app. CadCraft has no menu-bar style: when another app's menu bar is chosen for every app, CadCraft keeps its own toolbar. Related: Keyboard and mouse · CadCraft settings · Coming from other drafting software · Command aliases","lang":"en"},{"slug":"keyboard","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Keyboard and mouse","summary":"Every key, mouse action and command-line key in the CadCraft drafting app, as the code handles them.","section":"reference","order":10,"keywords":["keyboard","shortcuts","keys","mouse","wheel","pan","zoom","right click","middle button","f3","f7","f8","ctrl","command key","hotkeys","copy","cut","paste","duplicate","clipboard","select all"],"headings":[{"id":"typing-commands","text":"Typing commands","level":2},{"id":"drawing-and-editing","text":"Drawing and editing","level":2},{"id":"copy-cut-and-paste","text":"Copy, cut and paste","level":2},{"id":"toggles","text":"Toggles","level":2},{"id":"help","text":"Help","level":2},{"id":"screen","text":"Screen","level":2},{"id":"dialogs","text":"Dialogs","level":2},{"id":"panel-fields","text":"Panel fields","level":2},{"id":"mouse","text":"Mouse","level":2},{"id":"keys-the-browser-keeps","text":"Keys the browser keeps","level":2},{"id":"where-move-lives","text":"Where Move lives","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EShortcuts are shown for your computer. On a Mac, every \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E shortcut below uses the Command key, and the Control key works too. How keys are written, and how to use F-keys on a Mac, is in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/keys\" data-topic=\"basics/keys\"\u003EKeys, mice and trackpads\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"typing-commands\"\u003ETyping commands\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAny letter, digit or symbol\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGoes to the command line, wherever the pointer is, unless a box in a panel has the cursor\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESends what you typed. On an empty prompt: repeats the last command, ends a command, finishes a selection, or accepts the default in \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;…&gt;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESame as \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, except while CadCraft asks for text (text, block names, dimension text), where it types a space\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECancels the command. With no command running: cancels a grip move or a selection box, then clears the selection\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAccepts the suggested command shown to the right of the command line. With nothing typed and the pointer over overlapping objects: highlights the next one there (click to pick it)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↑\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↓\"\u003E↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESteps through the lines you typed before (the last 100)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"drawing-and-editing\"\u003EDrawing and editing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUndo the last command\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command Z\"\u003E⇧⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERedo\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect every object you can edit on the open tab (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESELECTALL\u003C/code\u003E). If you have typed something at the command line, it selects that text instead\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EErase the selected objects (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EERASE\u003C/code\u003E), unless you have typed something at the command line\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EErase the selected objects when the command line does not have the cursor; at the command line it deletes what you typed\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWith no command running: clear the selection (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDESELECT\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESave a copy in the drawing&#039;s format (DWG or DXF)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command S\"\u003E⇧⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESave as: choose DWG or DXF, then save a copy\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen a DWG or DXF\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command P\"\u003E⌘P\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+P\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlot to PDF\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUndo, redo, select all and plot do nothing while a command is running: press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"copy-cut-and-paste\"\u003ECopy, cut and paste\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECopy the selection to the clipboard (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECOPYCLIP\u003C/code\u003E). Nothing selected: CadCraft asks for objects\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command X\"\u003E⌘X\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+X\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECut: copy the selection, then erase it (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECUTCLIP\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPaste: the copy follows the cursor; click, or type a point, to put it down (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPASTECLIP\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command V\"\u003E⇧⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPaste in place: at the coordinates it was copied from (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPASTEORIG\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDuplicate: a copy of the selection moves with the pointer; click to put it down, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E leaves nothing (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDUPLICATE\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe same actions are in the toolbar&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E group (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E) and its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E menu. While another command is running, finish it or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E first; pressing \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again while a copy is following the cursor starts over. When you have typed something at the command line, or a command is asking for text, these keys copy, cut and paste that text instead. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/copy-paste\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/copy-paste\"\u003ECopy, paste and duplicate\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"toggles\"\u003EToggles\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EToggle\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EStatus bar button\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F3\"\u003EF3\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF3\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EObject snaps on or off\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESnap\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F7\"\u003EF7\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF7\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGrid on or off\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGrid\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F8\"\u003EF8\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF8\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOrtho on or off\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrtho\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F10\"\u003EF10\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF10\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPolar tracking on or off\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPolar\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F11\"\u003EF11\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF11\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EObject snap tracking on or off\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETrack\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F12\"\u003EF12\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF12\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDynamic input on or off\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDyn\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a Mac, F3, F7 and F8 are usually Mission Control and media keys. Hold \u003Cstrong\u003Efn\u003C/strong\u003E with them, or turn on standard function keys in System Settings, as described in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/keys\" data-topic=\"basics/keys\"\u003EKeys, mice and trackpads\u003C/a\u003E. The status bar buttons always work.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:firefox\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EFirefox\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EF7 in Firefox can also ask to turn on caret browsing. Choose \u003Cstrong\u003ENo\u003C/strong\u003E, and use the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGrid\u003C/span\u003E button if it keeps asking.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"help\"\u003EHelp\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpens or closes this help, beside the drawing. It works even while a command is running, and never reaches the command line.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWith the cursor in the help panel: clears the search, or closes the panel\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHelp\u003C/span\u003E button in the bar at the top does the same as \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. While you type in the help&#039;s search box, nothing reaches the command line. Click on the drawing to give the keys back to CadCraft; the panel stays open beside it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a Mac keyboard, hold \u003Cstrong\u003Efn\u003C/strong\u003E and press F1, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHelp\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"screen\"\u003EScreen\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECycle normal, full screen, and full screen with the bars hidden. This uses the Control key on every computer, Mac included.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"←\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"→\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWith a tab under the drawing focused: the previous or next tab\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn full screen, the browser may use the first \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to leave full screen. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again to cancel a command.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"dialogs\"\u003EDialogs\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECloses the dialog (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlot to PDF\u003C/span\u003E, or a question such as &quot;Unsaved changes&quot;, where it means \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECancel\u003C/span\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMoves between the dialog&#039;s settings and buttons\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile a dialog is open, the drawing&#039;s keys are off.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"panel-fields\"\u003EPanel fields\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn a box in the right or left panel:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EApplies the value and leaves the box\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPuts the old value back and leaves the box\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EApplies and moves to the next box\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile a box has the cursor, letters type into it, not into the command line.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mouse\"\u003EMouse\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EAction\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPicks a point when a command asks for one; picks an object when it asks for objects; otherwise selects an object, or a grip\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShift+click\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERemoves a selected object from the selection (with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClicking objects\u003C/span\u003E set to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReplaces the selection\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/settings\"\u003ESettings\u003C/a\u003E: adds it). While picking a point: ignores object snaps for that click\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick on empty space, then click again\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA selection box: left to right is a window, right to left a crossing\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag on empty space\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA lasso: moving right first is a window, left first a crossing; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while dragging cycles window, crossing and fence; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E drops it (LASSOMODE 0, or Settings, makes it the rectangle again)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag through pieces in TRIM or EXTEND\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETrims (or extends) every piece the path crosses; hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for the other one\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag a grip\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMoves the grip (or click the grip, then click where it goes)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDouble-click text\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelects it and puts the cursor in its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EText\u003C/span\u003E box in the right panel\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERight-click\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEnter. During a grip move or a selection box: cancels it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag with the right button\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPans\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWheel\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZooms in and out about the crosshair\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag with the wheel (middle button)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPans\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDouble-click the wheel\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZooms to the whole drawing (extents)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrop a DWG or DXF on the drawing\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpens it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a trackpad or a touch screen, see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/mouse-trackpad-touch\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/mouse-trackpad-touch\"\u003EMouse, trackpad and touch\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"keys-the-browser-keeps\"\u003EKeys the browser keeps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command N\"\u003E⌘N\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+N\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command W\"\u003E⌘W\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+W\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command T\"\u003E⌘T\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+T\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E always belong to the browser. CadCraft does not use them: use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew\u003C/span\u003E or type \u003Ccode\u003ENEW\u003C/code\u003E for a new drawing. CadCraft&#039;s clipboard is its own: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E copy objects between CadCraft drawings and tabs, not to or from other programs.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E opens your browser&#039;s history instead of redoing, use \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command Z\"\u003E⇧⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedo\u003C/span\u003E button.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"where-move-lives\"\u003EWhere Move lives\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMove is the command \u003Ccode\u003EMOVE\u003C/code\u003E (key \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"M\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E): pick the objects, then a base point and a second point or a typed distance. \u003Ccode\u003EALIGN\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EDISTRIBUTE\u003C/code\u003E are there too. BimCraft uses the same names for the same jobs on building elements, but its \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"M\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E is Measure: its Move is \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift M\"\u003E⇧M\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+M\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Shortcuts are shown for your computer. On a Mac, every Ctrl shortcut below uses the Command key, and the Control key works too. How keys are written, and how to use F-keys on a Mac, is in Keys, mice and trackpads. Typing commands Key What it does Any letter, digit or symbol Goes to the command line, wherever the pointer is, unless a box in a panel has the cursor Enter Sends what you typed. On an empty prompt: repeats the last command, ends a command, finishes a selection, or accepts the default in \u003C…\u003E Space Same as Enter, except while CadCraft asks for text (text, block names, dimension text), where it types a space Esc Cancels the command. With no command running: cancels a grip move or a selection box, then clears the selection Tab Accepts the suggested command shown to the right of the command line. With nothing typed and the pointer over overlapping objects: highlights the next one there (click to pick it) ↑ ↓ Steps through the lines you typed before (the last 100) Drawing and editing Key What it does Ctrl+Z Undo the last command Ctrl+Y or Ctrl+Shift+Z Redo Ctrl+A Select every object you can edit on the open tab (SELECTALL). If you have typed something at the command line, it selects that text instead Delete Erase the selected objects (ERASE), unless you have typed something at the command line Backspace Erase the selected objects when the command line does not have the cursor; at the command line it deletes what you typed Esc With no command running: clear the selection (DESELECT) Ctrl+S Save a copy in the drawing's format (DWG or DXF) Ctrl+Shift+S Save as: choose DWG or DXF, then save a copy Ctrl+O Open a DWG or DXF Ctrl+P Plot to PDF Undo, redo, select all and plot do nothing while a command is running: press Esc first. Copy, cut and paste Key What it does Ctrl+C Copy the selection to the clipboard (COPYCLIP). Nothing selected: CadCraft asks for objects Ctrl+X Cut: copy the selection, then erase it (CUTCLIP) Ctrl+V Paste: the copy follows the cursor; click, or type a point, to put it down (PASTECLIP) Ctrl+Shift+V Paste in place: at the coordinates it was copied from (PASTEORIG) Ctrl+D Duplicate: a copy of the selection moves with the pointer; click to put it down, Esc leaves nothing (DUPLICATE) The same actions are in the toolbar's Edit group (Copy, Paste) and its More menu. While another command is running, finish it or press Esc first; pressing Ctrl+V or Ctrl+D again while a copy is following the cursor starts over. When you have typed something at the command line, or a command is asking for text, these keys copy, cut and paste that text instead. See Copy, paste and duplicate. Toggles Key Toggle Status bar button F3 Object snaps on or off Snap F7 Grid on or off Grid F8 Ortho on or off Ortho F10 Polar tracking on or off Polar F11 Object snap tracking on or off Track F12 Dynamic input on or off Dyn On a Mac, F3, F7 and F8 are usually Mission Control and media keys. Hold fn with them, or turn on standard function keys in System Settings, as described in Keys, mice and trackpads. The status bar buttons always work. F7 in Firefox can also ask to turn on caret browsing. Choose No, and use the Grid button if it keeps asking. Help Key What it does F1 Opens or closes this help, beside the drawing. It works even while a command is running, and never reaches the command line. Esc With the cursor in the help panel: clears the search, or closes the panel The Help button in the bar at the top does the same as F1. While you type in the help's search box, nothing reaches the command line. Click on the drawing to give the keys back to CadCraft; the panel stays open beside it. On a Mac keyboard, hold fn and press F1, or click Help. Screen Key What it does Ctrl+Shift+F Cycle normal, full screen, and full screen with the bars hidden. This uses the Control key on every computer, Mac included. ← → With a tab under the drawing focused: the previous or next tab In full screen, the browser may use the first Esc to leave full screen. Press Esc again to cancel a command. Dialogs Key What it does Esc Closes the dialog (Plot to PDF, or a question such as \"Unsaved changes\", where it means Cancel) Tab Moves between the dialog's settings and buttons While a dialog is open, the drawing's keys are off. Panel fields In a box in the right or left panel: Key What it does Enter Applies the value and leaves the box Esc Puts the old value back and leaves the box Tab Applies and moves to the next box While a box has the cursor, letters type into it, not into the command line. Mouse Action What it does Click Picks a point when a command asks for one; picks an object when it asks for objects; otherwise selects an object, or a grip Shift+click Removes a selected object from the selection (with Clicking objects set to Replaces the selection in Settings: adds it). While picking a point: ignores object snaps for that click Click on empty space, then click again A selection box: left to right is a window, right to left a crossing Drag on empty space A lasso: moving right first is a window, left first a crossing; Space while dragging cycles window, crossing and fence; Esc drops it (LASSOMODE 0, or Settings, makes it the rectangle again) Drag through pieces in TRIM or EXTEND Trims (or extends) every piece the path crosses; hold Shift for the other one Drag a grip Moves the grip (or click the grip, then click where it goes) Double-click text Selects it and puts the cursor in its Text box in the right panel Right-click Enter. During a grip move or a selection box: cancels it Drag with the right button Pans Wheel Zooms in and out about the crosshair Drag with the wheel (middle button) Pans Double-click the wheel Zooms to the whole drawing (extents) Drop a DWG or DXF on the drawing Opens it On a trackpad or a touch screen, see Mouse, trackpad and touch. Keys the browser keeps Ctrl+N, Ctrl+W and Ctrl+T always belong to the browser. CadCraft does not use them: use New or type NEW for a new drawing. CadCraft's clipboard is its own: Ctrl+C and Ctrl+V copy objects between CadCraft drawings and tabs, not to or from other programs. If Ctrl+Y opens your browser's history instead of redoing, use Ctrl+Shift+Z or the Redo button. Where Move lives Move is the command MOVE (key M): pick the objects, then a base point and a second point or a typed distance. ALIGN and DISTRIBUTE are there too. BimCraft uses the same names for the same jobs on building elements, but its M is Measure: its Move is Shift+M.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"commands","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Command reference","summary":"Every command in the CadCraft drafting app, with its aliases, the questions it asks, its options and defaults.","section":"reference","order":20,"keywords":["commands","aliases","command list","area","perimeter","measuregeom","line","pline","circle","arc","trim","offset","dimlinear","block","insert","layout","plot","zoom","help","copyclip","cutclip","pasteclip","pasteorig","copybase","duplicate","selectall"],"headings":[{"id":"draw","text":"Draw","level":2},{"id":"line","text":"LINE","level":3},{"id":"pline","text":"PLINE","level":3},{"id":"circle","text":"CIRCLE","level":3},{"id":"arc","text":"ARC","level":3},{"id":"rectang","text":"RECTANG","level":3},{"id":"polygon","text":"POLYGON","level":3},{"id":"ellipse","text":"ELLIPSE","level":3},{"id":"hatch","text":"HATCH","level":3},{"id":"point","text":"POINT","level":3},{"id":"xline","text":"XLINE","level":3},{"id":"ray","text":"RAY","level":3},{"id":"text","text":"TEXT","level":3},{"id":"modify","text":"Modify","level":2},{"id":"textedit","text":"TEXTEDIT","level":3},{"id":"erase","text":"ERASE","level":3},{"id":"move","text":"MOVE","level":3},{"id":"copy","text":"COPY","level":3},{"id":"rotate","text":"ROTATE","level":3},{"id":"scale","text":"SCALE","level":3},{"id":"mirror","text":"MIRROR","level":3},{"id":"align","text":"ALIGN","level":3},{"id":"alignto","text":"ALIGNTO","level":3},{"id":"distribute","text":"DISTRIBUTE","level":3},{"id":"alignpanel","text":"ALIGNPANEL","level":3},{"id":"laser","text":"LASER","level":3},{"id":"stretch","text":"STRETCH","level":3},{"id":"array-arrayrect-arraypolar","text":"ARRAY, ARRAYRECT, ARRAYPOLAR","level":3},{"id":"offset","text":"OFFSET","level":3},{"id":"trimextendmode","text":"TRIMEXTENDMODE","level":3},{"id":"trim","text":"TRIM","level":3},{"id":"extend","text":"EXTEND","level":3},{"id":"linkviews","text":"LINKVIEWS","level":3},{"id":"openin3d","text":"OPENIN3D","level":3},{"id":"lassomode","text":"LASSOMODE","level":3},{"id":"fillet","text":"FILLET","level":3},{"id":"chamfer","text":"CHAMFER","level":3},{"id":"break-and-breakatpoint","text":"BREAK and BREAKATPOINT","level":3},{"id":"lengthen","text":"LENGTHEN","level":3},{"id":"join","text":"JOIN","level":3},{"id":"matchprop","text":"MATCHPROP","level":3},{"id":"explode","text":"EXPLODE","level":3},{"id":"clipboard","text":"Clipboard","level":2},{"id":"copyclip","text":"COPYCLIP","level":3},{"id":"cutclip","text":"CUTCLIP","level":3},{"id":"copybase","text":"COPYBASE","level":3},{"id":"pasteclip","text":"PASTECLIP","level":3},{"id":"pastearea","text":"PASTEAREA","level":3},{"id":"pasteorig","text":"PASTEORIG","level":3},{"id":"duplicate","text":"DUPLICATE","level":3},{"id":"select","text":"SELECT","level":3},{"id":"qselect","text":"QSELECT","level":3},{"id":"selectsimilar","text":"SELECTSIMILAR","level":3},{"id":"find","text":"FIND","level":3},{"id":"selectall-and-deselect","text":"SELECTALL and DESELECT","level":3},{"id":"dimensions","text":"Dimensions","level":2},{"id":"dimlinear","text":"DIMLINEAR","level":3},{"id":"dimaligned","text":"DIMALIGNED","level":3},{"id":"dimangular","text":"DIMANGULAR","level":3},{"id":"dimradius-and-dimdiameter","text":"DIMRADIUS and DIMDIAMETER","level":3},{"id":"dimcontinue","text":"DIMCONTINUE","level":3},{"id":"dimbaseline","text":"DIMBASELINE","level":3},{"id":"dimscale-dimtxt-dimasz-dimdec-dimgap-dimexe-dimexo","text":"DIMSCALE, DIMTXT, DIMASZ, DIMDEC, DIMGAP, DIMEXE, DIMEXO","level":3},{"id":"dimlunit","text":"DIMLUNIT","level":3},{"id":"dimstyle-and-dimstyle","text":"DIMSTYLE and -DIMSTYLE","level":3},{"id":"dimedit-and-dimtedit","text":"DIMEDIT and DIMTEDIT","level":3},{"id":"dimtextunit","text":"DIMTEXTUNIT","level":3},{"id":"revcloud","text":"REVCLOUD","level":3},{"id":"mleader","text":"MLEADER","level":3},{"id":"parametric","text":"Parametric","level":2},{"id":"geomconstraint-and-the-gc-commands","text":"GEOMCONSTRAINT and the GC commands","level":3},{"id":"dimconstraint-and-the-dc-commands","text":"DIMCONSTRAINT and the DC commands","level":3},{"id":"autoconstrain-delconstraint-constraintbar-dcdisplay","text":"AUTOCONSTRAIN, DELCONSTRAINT, CONSTRAINTBAR, DCDISPLAY","level":3},{"id":"parameters-and-parameters","text":"PARAMETERS and -PARAMETERS","level":3},{"id":"swbout","text":"SWBOUT","level":3},{"id":"blocks","text":"Blocks","level":2},{"id":"scalelistedit","text":"SCALELISTEDIT","level":3},{"id":"spline-splinedit-and-pedit","text":"SPLINE, SPLINEDIT and PEDIT","level":3},{"id":"block","text":"BLOCK","level":3},{"id":"wblock","text":"WBLOCK","level":3},{"id":"attdef","text":"ATTDEF","level":3},{"id":"eattedit-and-attedit","text":"EATTEDIT and ATTEDIT","level":3},{"id":"attsync","text":"ATTSYNC","level":3},{"id":"battman","text":"BATTMAN","level":3},{"id":"refedit-and-refclose","text":"REFEDIT and REFCLOSE","level":3},{"id":"bedit-and-bclose","text":"BEDIT and BCLOSE","level":3},{"id":"viewbase","text":"VIEWBASE","level":3},{"id":"publish","text":"PUBLISH","level":3},{"id":"sheetset","text":"SHEETSET","level":3},{"id":"xattach-xref","text":"XATTACH, XREF","level":3},{"id":"count","text":"COUNT","level":3},{"id":"insert","text":"INSERT","level":3},{"id":"library","text":"LIBRARY","level":3},{"id":"steelmember","text":"STEELMEMBER","level":3},{"id":"partslist","text":"PARTSLIST","level":3},{"id":"purge","text":"PURGE","level":3},{"id":"delblocks","text":"DELBLOCKS","level":3},{"id":"sheets-and-plotting","text":"Sheets and plotting","level":2},{"id":"layout","text":"LAYOUT","level":3},{"id":"mview","text":"MVIEW","level":3},{"id":"vports","text":"VPORTS","level":3},{"id":"vpview","text":"VPVIEW","level":3},{"id":"ucs","text":"UCS","level":3},{"id":"plan","text":"PLAN","level":3},{"id":"turnleft-turnright-turnworld","text":"TURNLEFT, TURNRIGHT, TURNWORLD","level":3},{"id":"vplayer","text":"VPLAYER","level":3},{"id":"mspace","text":"MSPACE","level":3},{"id":"pspace","text":"PSPACE","level":3},{"id":"plot","text":"PLOT","level":3},{"id":"plotstyles","text":"PLOTSTYLES","level":3},{"id":"inquiry","text":"Inquiry","level":2},{"id":"cal","text":"CAL","level":3},{"id":"dist","text":"DIST","level":3},{"id":"area","text":"AREA","level":3},{"id":"measuregeom","text":"MEASUREGEOM","level":3},{"id":"id","text":"ID","level":3},{"id":"list","text":"LIST","level":3},{"id":"view-file-and-other","text":"View, file and other","level":2},{"id":"zoom","text":"ZOOM","level":3},{"id":"pdfattach-pdfimport-and-the-pdf-commands","text":"PDFATTACH, PDFIMPORT and the PDF commands","level":3},{"id":"autocomplete","text":"AUTOCOMPLETE","level":3},{"id":"autosheet","text":"AUTOSHEET","level":3},{"id":"photosketch","text":"PHOTOSKETCH","level":3},{"id":"autodim","text":"AUTODIM","level":3},{"id":"gridmove","text":"GRIDMOVE","level":3},{"id":"details","text":"DETAILS","level":3},{"id":"sketchfix","text":"SKETCHFIX","level":3},{"id":"scanattach-and-the-scanned-drawing-commands","text":"SCANATTACH and the scanned-drawing commands","level":3},{"id":"regen-and-regenall","text":"REGEN and REGENALL","level":3},{"id":"namedview","text":"NAMEDVIEW","level":3},{"id":"undo-and-redo","text":"UNDO and REDO","level":3},{"id":"layer","text":"LAYER","level":3},{"id":"linetype-lweight-ltscale","text":"LINETYPE, LWEIGHT, LTSCALE","level":3},{"id":"rename-laymrg-laydel","text":"RENAME, LAYMRG, LAYDEL","level":3},{"id":"layiso-layuniso-layoff-layfrz-laymcur-layon-laythw","text":"LAYISO, LAYUNISO, LAYOFF, LAYFRZ, LAYMCUR, LAYON, LAYTHW","level":3},{"id":"new-open-save-saveas-dxfout","text":"NEW, OPEN, SAVE, SAVEAS, DXFOUT","level":3},{"id":"tour","text":"TOUR","level":3},{"id":"toolbardock","text":"TOOLBARDOCK","level":3},{"id":"polarang","text":"POLARANG","level":3},{"id":"units","text":"UNITS","level":3},{"id":"gdsin","text":"GDSIN","level":3},{"id":"diagram","text":"DIAGRAM","level":3},{"id":"olelist","text":"OLELIST","level":3},{"id":"shxfonts","text":"SHXFONTS","level":3},{"id":"limits","text":"LIMITS","level":3},{"id":"aunits","text":"AUNITS","level":3},{"id":"appload","text":"APPLOAD","level":3},{"id":"aliasedit-reinit","text":"ALIASEDIT, REINIT","level":3},{"id":"help","text":"HELP","level":3},{"id":"select-objects-prompt","text":"Select objects prompt","level":2},{"id":"access","text":"ACCESS","level":3},{"id":"describe","text":"DESCRIBE","level":3},{"id":"whereami","text":"WHEREAMI","level":3},{"id":"nearby","text":"NEARBY","level":3},{"id":"saysel","text":"SAYSEL","level":3},{"id":"voice","text":"VOICE","level":3}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EType a command name or an alias and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Case does not matter, and a leading \u003Ccode\u003E_\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E-\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E&#039;\u003C/code\u003E is ignored. Type \u003Ccode\u003EHELP\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003E?\u003C/code\u003E) to print a short list of every command in the command line history; press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for this help.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to read the prompts: options are in square brackets, and you choose one by typing its capital letters (\u003Ccode\u003ED\u003C/code\u003E for Diameter). A default is in angle brackets; press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to accept it. Where a point is asked for, you can click or type any of the formats in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/coordinate-entry\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/coordinate-entry\"\u003ECoordinate and distance entry\u003C/a\u003E. Where a distance is asked for, you can type a number or click two points.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPressing \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E where a point or an object is required, and there is no default, ends the command. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E cancels it. Whatever a command did is one undo step.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw\"\u003EDraw\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"line\"\u003ELINE\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E. Straight segments, each its own object.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify first point\u003C/code\u003E (Enter: start from the last point you gave)\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify next point [Undo]\u003C/code\u003E, repeated. After two segments: \u003Ccode\u003E[Undo/Close]\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EU\u003C/code\u003E takes back the last segment, \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E closes to the first point and ends. Enter ends.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"pline\"\u003EPLINE\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EPL\u003C/code\u003E. One polyline of line and arc segments.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify start point\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify next point [Arc/Close/Undo]\u003C/code\u003E (Close once there are two segments)\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E switches to arcs: \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify endpoint of arc [Line/CLose/Undo]\u003C/code\u003E. Each arc continues tangent to the segment before. \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E goes back to lines, \u003Ccode\u003ECL\u003C/code\u003E closes with an arc.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEnter ends; the polyline stays open.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"circle\"\u003ECIRCLE\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify center point for circle [3P/2P/Ttr (tan tan radius)]\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify radius of circle [Diameter] &lt;last radius&gt;\u003C/code\u003E; \u003Ccode\u003ED\u003C/code\u003E asks \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify diameter of circle\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E2P\u003C/code\u003E: two ends of a diameter. \u003Ccode\u003E3P\u003C/code\u003E: three points on the circle.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ET\u003C/code\u003E (also the command \u003Ccode\u003ETTR\u003C/code\u003E, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraw\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECircle: Tan, Tan, Radius\u003C/span\u003E): \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify point on object for first tangent of circle\u003C/code\u003E, the second, then the radius (Enter: the last one). The circle touches both objects (lines, arcs, circles, polyline segments, also inside blocks); of the circles that do, the one touching nearest your picks. &quot;Circle does not exist&quot; when none fits.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ETTT\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraw\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECircle: Tan, Tan, Tan\u003C/span\u003E): three objects; the circle touching all three, nearest your picks (three lines give the inner circle or an outer one, depending on where you pick).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"arc\"\u003EARC\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify start point of arc [Center]\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify second point of arc\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify end point of arc\u003C/code\u003E: an arc through three points.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify center point of arc\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify start point of arc\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify end point of arc\u003C/code\u003E. The arc runs counterclockwise from start to end.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"rectang\"\u003ERECTANG\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EREC\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ERECTANGLE\u003C/code\u003E. A closed polyline.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify first corner point\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify other corner point\u003C/code\u003E. Type \u003Ccode\u003E@width,height\u003C/code\u003E for the second corner.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"polygon\"\u003EPOLYGON\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EPOL\u003C/code\u003E. A regular polygon as a closed polyline.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EEnter number of sides &lt;4&gt;\u003C/code\u003E (3 to 1024)\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify center of polygon\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EEnter an option [Inscribed in circle/Circumscribed about circle] &lt;I&gt;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify radius of circle\u003C/code\u003E: click or type a point; its distance sets the size and its direction turns the polygon.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"ellipse\"\u003EELLIPSE\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EEL\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify axis endpoint of ellipse [Center]\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify other endpoint of axis\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify distance to other axis\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify center of ellipse\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify endpoint of axis\u003C/code\u003E, then the distance to the other axis.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"hatch\"\u003EHATCH\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EH\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EBH\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EBHATCH\u003C/code\u003E; or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHatch\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar. Prints the current pattern, scale and angle first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPick internal point [Select objects/Pattern/SCale/Angle]\u003C/code\u003E, repeated (\u003Ccode\u003EUndo\u003C/code\u003E once there is an area): each click adds the closed area round it, with the closed shapes inside it as islands. Enter makes one hatch of all of them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ESelect closed objects\u003C/code\u003E (closed polylines, circles, closed ellipses).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003EEnter a pattern name, SOLID, or ? for the list &lt;current&gt;\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003ESC\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify pattern scale\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify pattern angle\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/hatch\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/hatch\"\u003EHatch and fill areas\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"point\"\u003EPOINT\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EPO\u003C/code\u003E; or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraw\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPoint\u003C/span\u003E. Point objects, one per click, until Enter.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify a point\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"xline\"\u003EXLINE\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EXL\u003C/code\u003E; or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraw\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConstruction line\u003C/span\u003E. Construction lines, endless both ways. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/construction-lines\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/construction-lines\"\u003EConstruction lines and rays\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify a point [Hor/Ver/Ang/Bisect/Offset]\u003C/code\u003E, then \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify through point\u003C/code\u003E for each line, until Enter.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003EEnter angle of construction line [Reference] &lt;0&gt;\u003C/code\u003E; \u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E asks \u003Ccode\u003ESelect a line object\u003C/code\u003E first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EB\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify angle vertex point\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify angle start point\u003C/code\u003E, then \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify angle end point\u003C/code\u003E for each line.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EO\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify offset distance [Through] &lt;Through&gt;\u003C/code\u003E, then \u003Ccode\u003ESelect a line object\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify side to offset\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify through point\u003C/code\u003E) for each line.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"ray\"\u003ERAY\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENo aliases; or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraw\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERay\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify start point\u003C/code\u003E, then \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify through point\u003C/code\u003E for each ray, until Enter.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"text\"\u003ETEXT\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EDT\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDTEXT\u003C/code\u003E; or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EText\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar. Single-line text; each line is its own object.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify start point of text [Justify]\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EJ\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003EEnter an option [Left/Center/Right/Middle/TL/MC] &lt;L&gt;\u003C/code\u003E, then \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify insertion point of text\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify height &lt;current text height&gt;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify rotation angle of text &lt;0&gt;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EEnter text\u003C/code\u003E, repeated: each Enter starts the next line below. Enter on an empty line ends. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E types a space here.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"modify\"\u003EModify\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EMT\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ET\u003C/code\u003E; or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EParagraph\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar. Paragraph text that wraps to a width, typed in place.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify first corner\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify opposite corner [Height/Justify/Line spacing/Rotation/Width]\u003C/code\u003E: the width across the two corners is where the text wraps. \u003Ccode\u003EH\u003C/code\u003E asks \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify height\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EJ\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003EEnter justification [TL/TC/TR/ML/MC/MR/BL/BC/BR] &lt;TL&gt;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003EEnter line spacing factor (0.25 to 4)\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify rotation angle\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EW\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify width (0 for no wrapping)\u003C/code\u003E and skips the second corner.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThen a box opens on the drawing: type, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for a new paragraph, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Return\"\u003E⌘ Return\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Enter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or a click outside to finish, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to cancel. Its bar sets height, line spacing, width and justification. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/text#write-a-note-that-wraps\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/text\" data-hash=\"write-a-note-that-wraps\"\u003EAdd text and labels\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"textedit\"\u003ETEXTEDIT\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EED\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDDEDIT\u003C/code\u003E; or double-click a text. \u003Ccode\u003ESelect a text to edit\u003C/code\u003E, repeated until \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: each opens in the same box. Started on a selected or double-clicked text, it edits that one and ends.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"erase\"\u003EERASE\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDELETE\u003C/code\u003E; or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E with objects selected.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects\u003C/code\u003E (skipped when objects are already selected)\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"move\"\u003EMOVE\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EM\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify base point [Displacement]\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify second point\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ED\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify displacement\u003C/code\u003E, typed as \u003Ccode\u003Edx,dy\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"copy\"\u003ECOPY\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003ECO\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ECP\u003C/code\u003E. Copies as many times as you give points.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify base point [Displacement]\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify second point [Undo]\u003C/code\u003E, repeated until Enter. \u003Ccode\u003EU\u003C/code\u003E takes back the last copy.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"rotate\"\u003EROTATE\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003ERO\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify base point\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify rotation angle &lt;last angle&gt;\u003C/code\u003E: degrees, counterclockwise positive; or click a point to rotate toward it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"scale\"\u003ESCALE\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003ESC\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify base point\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify scale factor\u003C/code\u003E: type a factor such as \u003Ccode\u003E0.5\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E2\u003C/code\u003E. A clicked point gives its distance from the base point as the factor.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"mirror\"\u003EMIRROR\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EMI\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify first point of mirror line\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify second point of mirror line\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EErase source objects? [Yes/No] &lt;N&gt;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"align\"\u003EALIGN\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EAL\u003C/code\u003E; or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModify\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAlign\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify first source point\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify first destination point\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify second source point (Enter to continue)\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify second destination point\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify third source point (Enter to continue)\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify third destination point\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith two pairs: \u003Ccode\u003EScale objects based on alignment points? [Yes/No] &lt;N&gt;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/modify-objects#align-objects\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/modify-objects\" data-hash=\"align-objects\"\u003EAlign objects\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"alignto\"\u003EALIGNTO\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects\u003C/code\u003E (or the selection), \u003Ccode\u003EAlign [Left/Center/Right/Top/Middle/Bottom] &lt;Left&gt;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ETo [Selection/First/Last/Point] &lt;Selection&gt;\u003C/code\u003E; \u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E asks for the point. The selection stays.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"distribute\"\u003EDISTRIBUTE\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects\u003C/code\u003E (three at least), \u003Ccode\u003EDistribute [Horizontally/Vertically] &lt;Horizontally&gt;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESpace evenly [Centers/Gaps] &lt;Gaps&gt;\u003C/code\u003E. The outer two stay; the selection stays.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"alignpanel\"\u003EALIGNPANEL\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EALP\u003C/code\u003E; or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModify\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAlign and distribute\u003C/span\u003E. Opens the panel that runs ALIGNTO and DISTRIBUTE by clicks.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"laser\"\u003ELASER\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003ELASERCUT\u003C/code\u003E; or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELaser…\u003C/span\u003E. Opens the Laser panel over the drawing: what each layer does (cut, score, engrave, fill, picture), the preview, GRBL G-code to save, and framing and sending over Web Serial. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/laser-cutting\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/laser-cutting\"\u003ESend a drawing to a laser\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"stretch\"\u003ESTRETCH\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E; or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStretch\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects to stretch\u003C/code\u003E: drag crossing windows (or type \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E) over the ends to move. \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify base point [Displacement]\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify second point\u003C/code\u003E. Points inside the windows move: line ends, polyline vertices (arc segments keep their height), dimension points; circles, ellipses, text, points and blocks move whole when their center or insertion point is inside. A selection made before the command keeps the crossing windows (and lassos) that made it, so they stretch the same way. Without a window, the objects move whole.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"array-arrayrect-arraypolar\"\u003EARRAY, ARRAYRECT, ARRAYPOLAR\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EAR\u003C/code\u003E; or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArray\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects\u003C/code\u003E, then \u003Ccode\u003EEnter array type [Rectangular/POlar] &lt;Rectangular&gt;\u003C/code\u003E (ARRAY only).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERectangular starts at 4 columns × 3 rows, spaced 1.5 times the objects&#039; size, shown as a preview. \u003Ccode\u003EEnter an option [COUnt/Spacing/COLumns/Rows/eXit] &lt;X&gt;\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ECOU\u003C/code\u003E asks columns and rows, \u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E the distances between columns and between rows (negative goes left or down), \u003Ccode\u003ECOL\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E one direction each.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPolar asks \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify center point of array\u003C/code\u003E, starts at 6 items all the way round, rotated. \u003Ccode\u003EEnter an option [Items/Fill angle/ROTate items/eXit] &lt;X&gt;\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003EI\u003C/code\u003E items, \u003Ccode\u003EF\u003C/code\u003E the angle to fill (+ counterclockwise, − clockwise), \u003Ccode\u003EROT\u003C/code\u003E Yes or No.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (eXit) makes the copies, as plain objects. One undo step.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"offset\"\u003EOFFSET\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EO\u003C/code\u003E. Lines, arcs, circles and polylines.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify offset distance [Through] &lt;last distance, or Through&gt;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESelect object to offset\u003C/code\u003E, then \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify point on side to offset\u003C/code\u003E (or, with Through, \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify through point\u003C/code\u003E), repeated until Enter.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"trimextendmode\"\u003ETRIMEXTENDMODE\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EEnter new value for TRIMEXTENDMODE (1 Quick, 0 Standard)\u003C/code\u003E: how \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ETRIM\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXTEND\u003C/code\u003E start. \u003Ccode\u003E1\u003C/code\u003E Quick: every object is a cutting edge or boundary. \u003Ccode\u003E0\u003C/code\u003E Standard: they ask for the edges first. Also \u003Ccode\u003E(setvar &quot;TRIMEXTENDMODE&quot; 0)\u003C/code\u003E in LISP.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"trim\"\u003ETRIM\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003ETR\u003C/code\u003E. Prints \u003Ccode\u003ECurrent settings: Quick mode (TRIMEXTENDMODE 1), edges: No extend, dragging draws a path\u003C/code\u003E first. In Quick mode every visible object is a cutting edge, and an object nothing crosses is erased; in Standard mode it asks \u003Ccode\u003ESelect cutting edges (Enter = every object)\u003C/code\u003E first and cuts only at those.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESelect object to trim or shift-select to extend [cuTting edges/Fence/Crossing/mOde/Edge/eRase/Undo]\u003C/code\u003E, repeated: click the piece to remove (it shows grey and dashed while the pointer rests on it), drag a path through several pieces (all go in one step), or click two empty spots for a fence between them. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E extends instead. \u003Ccode\u003ET\u003C/code\u003E chooses cutting edges (Standard mode), \u003Ccode\u003EF\u003C/code\u003E asks fence points, \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E two window corners (pieces partly inside go), \u003Ccode\u003EO\u003C/code\u003E asks \u003Ccode\u003EEnter a trim mode option [Quick/Standard]\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EE\u003C/code\u003E asks \u003Ccode\u003EEnter an implied edge extension mode [Extend/No extend]\u003C/code\u003E (EDGEMODE), \u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E erases objects you select, \u003Ccode\u003EU\u003C/code\u003E undoes the last pick or drag. Enter ends. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/trim-and-extend\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/trim-and-extend\"\u003ETrim and extend\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"extend\"\u003EEXTEND\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EEX\u003C/code\u003E. The same as TRIM the other way round: \u003Ccode\u003ESelect object to extend or shift-select to trim [Boundary edges/Fence/Crossing/mOde/Edge/Undo]\u003C/code\u003E. A click lengthens the end nearest it to the next boundary; a dragged path or fence lengthens every object it crosses; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E trims. \u003Ccode\u003EB\u003C/code\u003E chooses boundary edges (Standard mode). Mode and Edge are shared with TRIM.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"linkviews\"\u003ELINKVIEWS\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003ESYNCVIEWS\u003C/code\u003E; or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELink the panes&#039; views\u003C/span\u003E, or the link icon on the drawing tabs. \u003Ccode\u003ELink the panes&#039; views [ON/OFF]\u003C/code\u003E (Enter takes the other state): while on, a pan or zoom in the active pane moves every pane showing the same kind of space. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/split-screen\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/split-screen\"\u003ESplit the screen\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"openin3d\"\u003EOPENIN3D\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENo aliases; or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen in ModelCraft\u003C/span\u003E where the 3D modeling app takes a handed-over drawing. Opens it there in a new tab, as a DWG (nothing is uploaded). If the browser blocks the tab, it says so.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"lassomode\"\u003ELASSOMODE\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EEnter new value for LASSOMODE (1 = lasso, 0 = rectangle)\u003C/code\u003E: what pressing and dragging on an empty spot draws. The same as Settings › CadCraft › Dragging to select. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/lasso-selection\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/lasso-selection\"\u003ELasso selection\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"fillet\"\u003EFILLET\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EF\u003C/code\u003E. Lines, arcs, circles and polylines. Prints \u003Ccode\u003ECurrent settings: Mode = TRIM, Radius = 0\u003C/code\u003E first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESelect first object [Undo/Polyline/Radius/Trim/Multiple]\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E asks \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify fillet radius &lt;last&gt;\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003ET\u003C/code\u003E asks \u003Ccode\u003EEnter Trim mode option [Trim/No trim]\u003C/code\u003E: with No trim the objects stay as they are and only the arc is added.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EM\u003C/code\u003E keeps the command going for more corners until \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E; \u003Ccode\u003EU\u003C/code\u003E takes back the last corner without leaving the command.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E asks \u003Ccode\u003ESelect 2D polyline [Radius]\u003C/code\u003E and rounds every corner where two straight segments meet. Fillets it already has are replaced by the new radius; radius 0 takes them out. Corners whose sides are too short for the radius are left and counted.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESelect second object or shift-select to apply corner [Radius]\u003C/code\u003E. Hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E as you click for a sharp corner, whatever the radius.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick each object on the part you want to keep. Two lines that do not meet are extended. Two parallel lines get a half circle across the gap, at the end of the first line nearest your click. A circle is never cut. Two segments of one polyline next to each other get the arc inside the polyline; a polyline and another object work when the cut lands on the polyline&#039;s first or last segment.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"chamfer\"\u003ECHAMFER\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003ECHA\u003C/code\u003E. Lines and the straight segments of polylines. Prints the current distances (or length and angle) first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESelect first line [Undo/Polyline/Distance/Angle/Trim/mEthod/Multiple]\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ED\u003C/code\u003E asks \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify first chamfer distance &lt;last&gt;\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify second chamfer distance &lt;the first&gt;\u003C/code\u003E: the first is cut from the first line you pick, the second from the other.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E asks \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify chamfer length on the first line\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify chamfer angle from the first line\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EE\u003C/code\u003E (mEthod) chooses which of the two the next chamfer uses. \u003Ccode\u003ET\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EM\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EU\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E work as in \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EFILLET\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESelect second line or shift-select to apply corner [Distance/Angle/mEthod]\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click makes a sharp corner.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA distance longer than the line is refused (\u003Ccode\u003EDistance is too large.\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"break-and-breakatpoint\"\u003EBREAK and BREAKATPOINT\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EBR\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003ESelect object\u003C/code\u003E (where you click is the first point), \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify second break point [First point]\u003C/code\u003E; \u003Ccode\u003EF\u003C/code\u003E asks \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify first break point\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify second break point\u003C/code\u003E. The part between goes; on a circle, counter-clockwise from the first point. BREAKATPOINT asks \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify break point\u003C/code\u003E and splits the object in two there (not a circle).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"lengthen\"\u003ELENGTHEN\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003ELEN\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003ESelect an object to measure [DElta/Percent/Total/DYnamic]\u003C/code\u003E: a pick prints \u003Ccode\u003ECurrent length\u003C/code\u003E (and an arc&#039;s included angle) and asks again. \u003Ccode\u003EDE\u003C/code\u003E asks \u003Ccode\u003EEnter delta length (negative shortens) [Angle]\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003EEnter percentage length\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ET\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify total length [Angle]\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDY\u003C/code\u003E drags. Then \u003Ccode\u003ESelect an object to change [Undo]\u003C/code\u003E, repeated: the end nearest the click moves. Lines, arcs and open polylines.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"join\"\u003EJOIN\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EJ\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects to join\u003C/code\u003E. Lines, arcs and open polylines that meet end to end become polylines (closed when the chain closes); lines on one line become one line; arcs of one circle become one arc or the circle. The history says how many objects joined and how many did not.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"matchprop\"\u003EMATCHPROP\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EMA\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EPAINTER\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003ESelect source object\u003C/code\u003E, then \u003Ccode\u003ESelect destination objects [Settings]\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Copies layer and color, and between objects of the same kind the text height and width, the dimension sizes, or the hatch pattern. \u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E asks \u003Ccode\u003ETurn a property on or off [Color/LAyer/Text/Dimension/Hatch]\u003C/code\u003E, repeated until \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E; the choice is remembered for the session.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"explode\"\u003EEXPLODE\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EX\u003C/code\u003E. Polylines become lines and arcs, block references become their objects, dimensions become lines, arrowheads and text.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"clipboard\"\u003EClipboard\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECadCraft&#039;s own clipboard: it works within a drawing, between drawings and between CadCraft tabs in this browser. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/copy-paste\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/copy-paste\"\u003ECopy, paste and duplicate\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"copyclip\"\u003ECOPYCLIP\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E group. Copies objects to the clipboard, with the blocks and layers they use. The objects stay selected.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects to copy\u003C/code\u003E (skipped when objects are already selected)\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"cutclip\"\u003ECUTCLIP\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command X\"\u003E⌘X\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+X\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E menu. Copies objects to the clipboard, then erases them (one undo step).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects to cut\u003C/code\u003E (skipped when objects are already selected)\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"copybase\"\u003ECOPYBASE\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECopies objects to the clipboard with a base point you pick; pasting places that point.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify base point\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects to copy\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"pasteclip\"\u003EPASTECLIP\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E group. The copy follows the cursor, hanging from the lower-left corner of its extents (or its center, or the \u003Ccode\u003ECOPYBASE\u003C/code\u003E point; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/settings\"\u003ECadCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify insertion point\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe pasted objects are selected. A block the drawing already has under the same name keeps this drawing&#039;s definition.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen an area copied in MarkupCraft is newer than what CadCraft copied (and less than 6 hours old), \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EPASTECLIP\u003C/code\u003E paste that area instead: see \u003Ccode\u003EPASTEAREA\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"pastearea\"\u003EPASTEAREA\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EPAREA\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste a copied area\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E menu. Pastes an area copied with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy area\u003C/span\u003E in MarkupCraft as a picture at its true size. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/pictures\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/pictures\"\u003EPictures in a drawing\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify the lower-left corner of the copied area\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EScale its paper size by (100 for a 1:100 sheet; Points: two points on it and their true distance) [Points] &lt;1&gt;\u003C/code\u003E (only when the sheet had no drawing scale): a number, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for the paper size, or \u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E, then \u003Ccode\u003EFirst point on the pasted area\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESecond point on the pasted area\u003C/code\u003E and their true distance.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"pasteorig\"\u003EPASTEORIG\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command V\"\u003E⇧⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste in place\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E menu. Pastes at the coordinates the objects were copied from. Asks nothing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"duplicate\"\u003EDUPLICATE\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EDUP\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDuplicate and move\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E menu. A copy of the objects moves with the pointer from where it is; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E leaves nothing. Does not use the clipboard.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects to duplicate\u003C/code\u003E (skipped when objects are already selected)\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify where the copy goes (Esc leaves nothing)\u003C/code\u003E: click, type a point, \u003Ccode\u003E@dx,dy\u003C/code\u003E (from where the pointer was), or a distance toward the cursor.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"select\"\u003ESELECT\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAsks \u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects\u003C/code\u003E (with every option below) and leaves what you chose selected, for the next command or for \u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E later.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"qselect\"\u003EQSELECT\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EQS\u003C/code\u003E. In the browser, the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuick select\u003C/span\u003E dialog (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/select-objects#select-by-a-rule-quick-select\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/select-objects\" data-hash=\"select-by-a-rule-quick-select\"\u003ESelect objects\u003C/a\u003E). Without it, the command line asks \u003Ccode\u003EEnter object type\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EEnter property\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EEnter operator [=/&lt;&gt;/&gt;/&lt;/*/Select all] &lt;=&gt;\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EEnter value\u003C/code\u003E. The matches become the selection.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"selectsimilar\"\u003ESELECTSIMILAR\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003ESIM\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects [SEttings]\u003C/code\u003E (or the current selection): selects every object of the same type with the same layer and color (and block name, hatch pattern). \u003Ccode\u003ESE\u003C/code\u003E asks \u003Ccode\u003ETurn a property on or off [LAyer/COlor/NAme/PAttern/HEight]\u003C/code\u003E, repeated until \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"find\"\u003EFIND\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENo aliases; or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFind and replace\u003C/span\u003E. In the browser, the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFind and replace\u003C/span\u003E dialog (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/text#find-and-replace-words\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/text\" data-hash=\"find-and-replace-words\"\u003EAdd text and labels\u003C/a\u003E). Without it: \u003Ccode\u003EEnter text to find\u003C/code\u003E, then \u003Ccode\u003EEnter replacement text (Enter: select what is found)\u003C/code\u003E. Looks in text, attribute values and dimension text, on the open tab or in the selection.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"selectall-and-deselect\"\u003ESELECTALL and DESELECT\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESELECTALL\u003C/code\u003E (alias \u003Ccode\u003EAI_SELALL\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) selects every object you can edit on the open tab. \u003Ccode\u003EDESELECT\u003C/code\u003E (aliases \u003Ccode\u003EAI_DESELECT\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESELECTNONE\u003C/code\u003E; or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) clears the selection.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"dimensions\"\u003EDimensions\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"dimlinear\"\u003EDIMLINEAR\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EDLI\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDIMLIN\u003C/code\u003E. Horizontal or vertical distance.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify first extension line origin [Select object]\u003C/code\u003E (Enter or \u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ESelect object to dimension\u003C/code\u003E, then click a line, arc, circle or polyline segment)\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify second extension line origin\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify dimension line location [Text/Horizontal/Vertical/Rotated]\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003ET\u003C/code\u003E asks for text (\u003Ccode\u003E&lt;&gt;\u003C/code\u003E is the measured value), \u003Ccode\u003EH\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EV\u003C/code\u003E fix the direction, \u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E asks \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify angle of dimension line &lt;0&gt;\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"dimaligned\"\u003EDIMALIGNED\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EDAL\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDIMALI\u003C/code\u003E. The true distance between two points.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOrigins as in \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMLINEAR\u003C/code\u003E, then \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify dimension line location [Text]\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"dimangular\"\u003EDIMANGULAR\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EDAN\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDIMANG\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESelect arc, circle or line, or Enter for a vertex\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EArc: its angle. Circle: \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify second angle endpoint\u003C/code\u003E. Line: \u003Ccode\u003ESelect second line\u003C/code\u003E. Enter: \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify angle vertex\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify first angle endpoint\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify second angle endpoint\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify dimension line location [Text]\u003C/code\u003E: click inside the angle to measure.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"dimradius-and-dimdiameter\"\u003EDIMRADIUS and DIMDIAMETER\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EDRA\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDIMRAD\u003C/code\u003E; \u003Ccode\u003EDDI\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDIMDIA\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESelect arc or circle\u003C/code\u003E, then \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify dimension line location [Text]\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"dimcontinue\"\u003EDIMCONTINUE\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EDCO\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDIMCONT\u003C/code\u003E. Continues from the last linear or aligned dimension made (or asks \u003Ccode\u003ESelect linear or aligned dimension\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify second extension line origin [Undo]\u003C/code\u003E, repeated until Enter.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"dimbaseline\"\u003EDIMBASELINE\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EDBA\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDIMBASE\u003C/code\u003E. Like \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMCONTINUE\u003C/code\u003E, but every dimension starts from the first origin and is stacked further out.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"dimscale-dimtxt-dimasz-dimdec-dimgap-dimexe-dimexo\"\u003EDIMSCALE, DIMTXT, DIMASZ, DIMDEC, DIMGAP, DIMEXE, DIMEXO\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENo aliases. Each asks \u003Ccode\u003EEnter new value for … &lt;current&gt;\u003C/code\u003E and sets that size for \u003Cstrong\u003Enew\u003C/strong\u003E dimensions: overall scale, text height, arrow size, decimal places (up to 8), gap round the text, extension beyond the dimension line, offset from the origin. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/dimension-settings\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/dimension-settings\"\u003ESet dimension sizes\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"dimlunit\"\u003EDIMLUNIT\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDimension setting: the format of new dimensions only (1 scientific, 2 decimal, 3 engineering, 4 architectural, 5 fractional).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"dimstyle-and-dimstyle\"\u003EDIMSTYLE and -DIMSTYLE\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EDST\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDDIM\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ED\u003C/code\u003E. The Dimension style manager: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESet current\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew…\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModify…\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERename…\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECompare…\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EApply to selected\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003E-DIMSTYLE\u003C/code\u003E on the command line: \u003Ccode\u003EDimension style [Restore/Save/Delete/Apply/Compare/reName/List]\u003C/code\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/dimension-settings#named-dimension-styles\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/dimension-settings\" data-hash=\"named-dimension-styles\"\u003ESet dimension sizes\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"dimedit-and-dimtedit\"\u003EDIMEDIT and DIMTEDIT\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDIMEDIT\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EDED\u003C/code\u003E): \u003Ccode\u003EDimension edit [Home/New/Rotate/Oblique] &lt;Home&gt;\u003C/code\u003E, then the dimensions (or those already selected). New asks for the text (\u003Ccode\u003E&lt;&gt;\u003C/code\u003E = the measurement); Rotate for an angle (0 puts the text back); Oblique for the extension lines&#039; angle from the X axis (Enter = none; linear and aligned dimensions only). \u003Ccode\u003EDIMTEDIT\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EDIMTED\u003C/code\u003E): \u003Ccode\u003EDimension text [Left/Right/Center/Home/Angle] &lt;Center&gt;\u003C/code\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/dimension-settings#change-dimension-text-and-extension-lines\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/dimension-settings\" data-hash=\"change-dimension-text-and-extension-lines\"\u003ESet dimension sizes\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"dimtextunit\"\u003EDIMTEXTUNIT\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EDTU\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003EUnits in the text of new dimensions [Drawing units/mm/cm/m/km]\u003C/code\u003E, or with dimensions selected \u003Ccode\u003EUnits in the text of the selected dimensions\u003C/code\u003E. Writes the measured length in that unit with the unit after it (DIMLFAC and a DIMPOST suffix); \u003Ccode\u003ED\u003C/code\u003E goes back to drawing units. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/dimension-settings#dimensions-in-mm-cm-m-or-km\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/dimension-settings\" data-hash=\"dimensions-in-mm-cm-m-or-km\"\u003EDimensions in mm, cm, m or km\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"revcloud\"\u003EREVCLOUD\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003ERC\u003C/code\u003E. Prints \u003Ccode\u003EArc length: … Type: …\u003C/code\u003E, then \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify first point [Arc length/Object/Rectangular/Polygonal/Freehand]\u003C/code\u003E. Rectangular asks \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify opposite corner\u003C/code\u003E; Polygonal \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify next point (Enter closes the cloud) [Undo]\u003C/code\u003E; Freehand follows the pointer until a click or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E; \u003Ccode\u003EO\u003C/code\u003E asks \u003Ccode\u003ESelect object\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EReverse direction? [Yes/No] &lt;N&gt;\u003C/code\u003E and turns the object into the cloud. \u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E asks \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify the length of each arc\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EF\u003C/code\u003E choose the type for the next cloud. The result is a closed polyline of arcs.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"mleader\"\u003EMLEADER\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EMLD\u003C/code\u003E; or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELeader\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify leader arrowhead location [Arrowhead size/Landing distance]\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify leader landing location\u003C/code\u003E, then the text in the in-place box (or, without it, \u003Ccode\u003EEnter text (an empty line ends)\u003C/code\u003E). Saved as a real multileader in DWG; DXF keeps the lines, arrowhead and text it draws.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"parametric\"\u003EParametric\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/constraints\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/constraints\"\u003EConstrain a drawing\u003C/a\u003E and \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/parameters\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/parameters\"\u003EUse parameters and equations\u003C/a\u003E. Constraints work on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"geomconstraint-and-the-gc-commands\"\u003EGEOMCONSTRAINT and the GC commands\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAlias \u003Ccode\u003EGCON\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003EEnter constraint type [Horizontal/Vertical/Perpendicular/PArallel/Tangent/Coincident/CONcentric/COLlinear/Symmetric/Equal/Fix] &lt;Horizontal&gt;\u003C/code\u003E, then that command&#039;s prompts.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EGCHORIZONTAL\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EGCVERTICAL\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ESelect an object [2Points] &lt;2Points&gt;\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EGCPARALLEL\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EGCPERPENDICULAR\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EGCCOLLINEAR\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EGCCONCENTRIC\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EGCEQUAL\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EGCTANGENT\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ESelect first object\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESelect second object\u003C/code\u003E (the second one moves).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EGCCOINCIDENT\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ESelect first point [Object/Autoconstrain] &lt;Object&gt;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESelect second point [Object] &lt;Object&gt;\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EGCFIX\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ESelect point [Object] &lt;Object&gt;\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EGCSYMMETRIC\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ESelect first object [2Points] &lt;2Points&gt;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESelect second object\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESelect symmetry line\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"dimconstraint-and-the-dc-commands\"\u003EDIMCONSTRAINT and the DC commands\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAlias \u003Ccode\u003EDCON\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ESelect associative dimension to convert [LInear/Horizontal/Vertical/ALigned/ANgular/Radial/Diameter/Convert] &lt;ALigned&gt;\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDCLINEAR\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDCHORIZONTAL\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDCVERTICAL\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify first constraint point [Object] &lt;Object&gt;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify second constraint point\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify dimension line location\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDimension text &lt;d1=40&gt;\u003C/code\u003E (a number, an expression, or \u003Ccode\u003Ename=expression\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDCALIGNED\u003C/code\u003E: as above, with \u003Ccode\u003E[Object/Point &amp; line/2Lines]\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDCRADIUS\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDCDIAMETER\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ESelect arc or circle\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDCANGULAR\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ESelect first line or arc\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESelect second line\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDCCONVERT\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ESelect dimensions to convert\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"autoconstrain-delconstraint-constraintbar-dcdisplay\"\u003EAUTOCONSTRAIN, DELCONSTRAINT, CONSTRAINTBAR, DCDISPLAY\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EAUTOCONSTRAIN\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects [Settings]\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E asks the distance and angle tolerances.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDELCONSTRAINT\u003C/code\u003E (alias \u003Ccode\u003EDELCON\u003C/code\u003E): \u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects\u003C/code\u003E; their constraints go.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECONSTRAINTBAR\u003C/code\u003E (alias \u003Ccode\u003ECBAR\u003C/code\u003E): \u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects [Showall/Hideall]\u003C/code\u003E, then \u003Ccode\u003E[Show/Hide]\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDCDISPLAY\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003EEnter an option [Show/Hide]\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"parameters-and-parameters\"\u003EPARAMETERS and -PARAMETERS\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPARAMETERS\u003C/code\u003E (aliases \u003Ccode\u003EPAR\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EPARAM\u003C/code\u003E) opens the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EParameters\u003C/span\u003E panel. \u003Ccode\u003E-PARAMETERS\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003EEnter an option [New/Edit/Rename/Delete/?] &lt;?&gt;\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"swbout\"\u003ESWBOUT\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAlias \u003Ccode\u003ESWMACRO\u003C/code\u003E. Downloads a macro (.swb) that rebuilds the model as one constrained sketch in SolidWorks. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/solidworks-sketches\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/solidworks-sketches\"\u003EMove sketches to and from other part modelers\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"blocks\"\u003EBlocks\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"scalelistedit\"\u003ESCALELISTEDIT\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003ESCALELIST\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003EScale list [Add/Edit/Delete/List/Reset]\u003C/code\u003E until \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Add: \u003Ccode\u003EScale name\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EPaper units\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDrawing units\u003C/code\u003E. Edit and Delete ask for the scale&#039;s name. The drawing&#039;s own scales join the viewport and plot scale lists and are saved with the drawing. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/units-papers-scales#scales\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/units-papers-scales\" data-hash=\"scales\"\u003EUnits, papers and scales\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"spline-splinedit-and-pedit\"\u003ESPLINE, SPLINEDIT and PEDIT\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESPLINE\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003ESPL\u003C/code\u003E): \u003Ccode\u003EFirst point of the spline [Method/Object] (Fit points)\u003C/code\u003E. Fit: points the curve passes through; \u003Ccode\u003EClose\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EUndo\u003C/code\u003E, then \u003Ccode\u003EStart tangent\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EEnd tangent\u003C/code\u003E (a point, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E). Method › Control vertices asks a degree (1 to 5) and draws from a control polygon. Object turns polylines into splines through their vertices. \u003Ccode\u003ESPLINEDIT\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003ESPE\u003C/code\u003E): \u003Ccode\u003E[Fit data/Edit vertex/convert to Polyline/Open or Close/Reverse/Undo/eXit]\u003C/code\u003E; Fit data \u003Ccode\u003E[Add/Delete/Move/Purge/Tangents/eXit]\u003C/code\u003E; Edit vertex \u003Ccode\u003E[Add control point/Move/Weight/eXit]\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003EPEDIT\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EPE\u003C/code\u003E): \u003Ccode\u003E[Close/Open/Join/Width/Fit/Spline/Decurve/Ltype gen/Reverse/Undo/eXit]\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EM\u003C/code\u003E for several objects; lines, arcs and splines are offered as polylines first. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/splines\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/splines\"\u003ESplines and polylines\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"block\"\u003EBLOCK\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EB\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EBMAKE\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EEnter block name\u003C/code\u003E. If it exists: \u003Ccode\u003EBlock &quot;…&quot; already exists. Redefine it? [Yes/No] &lt;N&gt;\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify insertion base point\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects\u003C/code\u003E. They are replaced by one reference to the new block.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"wblock\"\u003EWBLOCK\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EW\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E-WBLOCK\u003C/code\u003E. Writes a block, some objects or the whole drawing as a new DWG or DXF file. \u003Ccode\u003EWBLOCK source [Block/Entire drawing/Objects] &lt;Objects&gt;\u003C/code\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EObjects\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify insertion base point\u003C/code\u003E (it becomes the new drawing&#039;s origin), \u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects\u003C/code\u003E, then \u003Ccode\u003EFile format [DWG/DXF/R12 DXF]\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EFile name for the new drawing\u003C/code\u003E, then \u003Ccode\u003EWhat to do with the objects [Retain/Convert to block/Delete]\u003C/code\u003E. Convert replaces them with one reference to a block of the file&#039;s name; Delete removes them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBlock\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003EBlock to write out\u003C/code\u003E. The block&#039;s own objects, with its base point as the origin, nested blocks included.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEntire drawing\u003C/strong\u003E: every object in model space. Sheets are not written; use \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESAVEAS\u003C/code\u003E for a copy that keeps them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe new file holds only the layers, linetypes, text and dimension styles and blocks that its objects use, and the drawing&#039;s units. The version of a DWG is the one \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESAVEAS\u003C/code\u003E last used. In the browser the file downloads; in Studio the save dialog opens.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"attdef\"\u003EATTDEF\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EATT\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDDATTDEF\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E-ATTDEF\u003C/code\u003E; or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBlocks\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDefine attribute\u003C/span\u003E. Prints the modes, then \u003Ccode\u003EEnter an option to change (Enter when done) [Invisible/Constant/Preset]\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EEnter attribute tag name\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EEnter attribute prompt\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EEnter default attribute value\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EEnter attribute value\u003C/code\u003E when constant), \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify start point of text\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify text height\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify rotation angle of text\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EBLOCK\u003C/code\u003E takes attribute definitions into the block.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"eattedit-and-attedit\"\u003EEATTEDIT and ATTEDIT\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EEATTEDIT\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EDDATTE\u003C/code\u003E, or double-click a reference with attributes; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBlocks\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit attributes\u003C/span\u003E): \u003Ccode\u003ESelect a block reference with attributes\u003C/code\u003E, then the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit attributes\u003C/span\u003E dialog. \u003Ccode\u003EATTEDIT\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EATE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E-ATTEDIT\u003C/code\u003E): the same reference, then \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;prompt&gt; (&lt;TAG&gt;) &lt;value&gt;\u003C/code\u003E for each attribute; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E keeps a value. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/attributes#see-and-change-the-values\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/attributes\" data-hash=\"see-and-change-the-values\"\u003EAttributes\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"attsync\"\u003EATTSYNC\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EUpdate the attributes of which block&#039;s references [Name/Select/All] &lt;Select&gt;\u003C/code\u003E. Values kept; new attributes get their default, removed ones go, placements reset.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"battman\"\u003EBATTMAN\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBlocks\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAttribute manager\u003C/span\u003E. The dialog: block, attributes (tag, prompt, default, modes, order), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUpdate the references (values kept)\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EApply\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"refedit-and-refclose\"\u003EREFEDIT and REFCLOSE\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBlocks\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit in place\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003EREFEDIT\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ESelect the block reference to edit in place\u003C/code\u003E (or the selection). \u003Ccode\u003EREFCLOSE\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ESave the changes to &quot;…&quot; [Save/Discard] &lt;Save&gt;\u003C/code\u003E; the bar&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave the block\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDiscard\u003C/span\u003E do the same. The whole edit is one undo step. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/attributes#edit-a-title-block-where-it-is-refedit\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/attributes\" data-hash=\"edit-a-title-block-where-it-is-refedit\"\u003EAttributes\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"bedit-and-bclose\"\u003EBEDIT and BCLOSE\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EBE\u003C/code\u003E; or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBlocks\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit block\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003EEnter block name to edit &lt;the selected reference&#039;s block&gt;\u003C/code\u003E opens the block editor. \u003Ccode\u003EBCLOSE\u003C/code\u003E asks \u003Ccode\u003EClose the block editor on &quot;…&quot; [Save/Discard] &lt;Save&gt;\u003C/code\u003E; the banner&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave block\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDiscard changes\u003C/span\u003E do the same. Everything done in the editor is one undo step.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"viewbase\"\u003EVIEWBASE\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAlias: \u003Ccode\u003EFLATSHOT\u003C/code\u003E. A flat 2D view of the solids on the 3D tab: \u003Ccode\u003EView [Front/Top/Right/Left/BAck/BOttom/Iso]\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EHidden lines [Show/Hide]\u003C/code\u003E, a scale (\u003Ccode\u003E1:20\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E2:1\u003C/code\u003E; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes the one that fits the sheet), then the view&#039;s center. One block reference, visible edges on VIEW-VISIBLE, hidden ones dashed on VIEW-HIDDEN. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/3d-solids#flat-views-on-a-sheet\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/3d-solids\" data-hash=\"flat-views-on-a-sheet\"\u003EFlat views on a sheet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"publish\"\u003EPUBLISH\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003E-PUBLISH\u003C/code\u003E; or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAll sheets in one PDF\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlot to PDF\u003C/span\u003E dialog. \u003Ccode\u003ESheets to plot [All/Some]\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E plots every sheet, \u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E asks for names separated by commas. One PDF, a page and a bookmark per sheet. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/plot-pdf#every-sheet-in-one-pdf\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/plot-pdf\" data-hash=\"every-sheet-in-one-pdf\"\u003EEvery sheet in one PDF\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"sheetset\"\u003ESHEETSET\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003ESSM\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESHEETSETMANAGER\u003C/code\u003E. Brings you to the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESheet set\u003C/span\u003E section of the left panel: sheets from many drawings, numbered and titled, their title blocks filled from the set, published as one PDF. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/sheet-sets\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/sheet-sets\"\u003ESheet sets\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"xattach-xref\"\u003EXATTACH, XREF\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EXATTACH\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EXA\u003C/code\u003E): pick a DWG or DXF, then its insertion point. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EXREF\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EXR\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E-XREF\u003C/code\u003E): \u003Ccode\u003ELinked drawings [?/Attach/Reload/Bind/Detach] (Enter: ?)\u003C/code\u003E; with more than one link, pick one of its references or type \u003Ccode\u003EN\u003C/code\u003E and its name. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/files#linked-drawings-external-references\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/files\" data-hash=\"linked-drawings-external-references\"\u003ELinked drawings\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"count\"\u003ECOUNT\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EBCOUNT\u003C/code\u003E; or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBlocks\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECount blocks\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003ESelect a block reference to count, or [All/Export] (Enter: All)\u003C/code\u003E: a pick selects and counts every reference of that block in this space; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E lists them all; \u003Ccode\u003EE\u003C/code\u003E then \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E (counts) or \u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E (attributes) downloads a CSV. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/blocks#count-blocks-and-export-a-schedule\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/blocks\" data-hash=\"count-blocks-and-export-a-schedule\"\u003ECount blocks and export a schedule\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"insert\"\u003EINSERT\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EI\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDDINSERT\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EEnter block name or [File/Library] &lt;last block&gt;\u003C/code\u003E (case does not matter). \u003Ccode\u003EF\u003C/code\u003E asks for a DWG or DXF, then the whole drawing or one of its blocks; a name already used asks \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeep existing\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERename\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E opens the library panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify insertion point [Scale/Rotate]\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E asks \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify scale factor\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E asks \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify rotation angle\u003C/code\u003E; both are remembered.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThen \u003Ccode\u003EEnter &lt;prompt&gt; (&lt;TAG&gt;) &lt;default&gt;\u003C/code\u003E for each attribute the block asks for. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/attributes\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/attributes\"\u003EBlock attributes\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"library\"\u003ELIBRARY\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003ELIB\u003C/code\u003E. Opens the library panel: place items, check for updates, library credits. No questions. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/insert-from-file-and-library\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/insert-from-file-and-library\"\u003EInsert from a file or the library\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"steelmember\"\u003ESTEELMEMBER\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003ESMEMBER\u003C/code\u003E; or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D member\u003C/span\u003E on the library&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStandard\u003C/span\u003E tab. \u003Ccode\u003ESection size &lt;W12x26&gt;\u003C/code\u003E (as the tab lists it), then \u003Ccode\u003EStart point [Column]\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EEnd point\u003C/code\u003E: a solid member on the 3D tab between the two points, sitting on the ground. \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E asks for the column&#039;s base point and its height. One undo step. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/steel-and-standard-parts\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/steel-and-standard-parts\"\u003EPlace steel sections and standard parts\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"partslist\"\u003EPARTSLIST\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EPLIST\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003ETop left corner of the parts list\u003C/code\u003E, then \u003Ccode\u003EText height &lt;2.5&gt;\u003C/code\u003E: draws a table of every standard part and steel section placed from the library (item numbers, quantity, part, standard, size, length, material, mass). One undo step. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/steel-and-standard-parts#make-a-parts-list\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/steel-and-standard-parts\" data-hash=\"make-a-parts-list\"\u003EPlace steel sections and standard parts\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"purge\"\u003EPURGE\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EPU\u003C/code\u003E. Removes block definitions that nothing uses. No questions.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"delblocks\"\u003EDELBLOCKS\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOr \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBlocks\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete blocks\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003ESelect block to delete [Name/eXit]\u003C/code\u003E: pick a block reference, or \u003Ccode\u003EN\u003C/code\u003E for the list of blocks with a filter (choose several), or \u003Ccode\u003EX\u003C/code\u003E / \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to end. Every reference goes — in the model, on every sheet and inside other blocks — and then the definition, and the command line says what was deleted and what couldn&#039;t be. Unnamed blocks and linked drawings are not offered. The prompt comes back for the next block; the whole command is one undo step.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"sheets-and-plotting\"\u003ESheets and plotting\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"layout\"\u003ELAYOUT\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003ELO\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ENEWLAYOUT\u003C/code\u003E; or the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E+\u003C/span\u003E button under the drawing. A new sheet with a border, a title block and one viewport, on ISO A3 (or Tabloid for drawings in inches or feet). No questions.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"mview\"\u003EMVIEW\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EMV\u003C/code\u003E. On a sheet only (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EVPORTS\u003C/code\u003E on a sheet does the same).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify corner of viewport\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify opposite corner\u003C/code\u003E. The viewport shows the whole model at a standard scale.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"vports\"\u003EVPORTS\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EVIEWPORTS\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESPLIT\u003C/code\u003E is the same from a sheet too, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESplit side by side\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESplit stacked\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFour panes\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOne pane\u003C/span\u003E run it. On the model: \u003Ccode\u003EEnter number of panes [SIngle/2/3/4] &lt;3&gt;\u003C/code\u003E; \u003Ccode\u003E2\u003C/code\u003E asks \u003Ccode\u003E[Horizontal/Vertical] &lt;Vertical&gt;\u003C/code\u003E (Vertical = side by side), \u003Ccode\u003E3\u003C/code\u003E asks \u003Ccode\u003E[Left/Right] &lt;Right&gt;\u003C/code\u003E (where the big pane goes). Each pane keeps its own view and space; a click makes a pane active. On a sheet it is \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMVIEW\u003C/code\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/split-screen\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/split-screen\"\u003ESplit the screen\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"vpview\"\u003EVPVIEW\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENo aliases. On a sheet only.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESelect viewport\u003C/code\u003E (skipped if one is selected), then on the model: \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify first corner of the window to show\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify opposite corner\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"ucs\"\u003EUCS\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENo aliases. A coordinate system of your own; not an edit. \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify origin of UCS [NAmed/OBject/Previous/World/Z/3point] &lt;World&gt;\u003C/code\u003E: a point is a new origin, then \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify point on X-axis (Enter keeps the angle)\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003EZ\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify rotation angle about Z axis &lt;90&gt;\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003E3\u003C/code\u003E: origin, a point on the positive X axis, a point on the positive-Y side. \u003Ccode\u003EOB\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ESelect object to align UCS\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003ENA\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003E[Restore/Save/Delete/?]\u003C/code\u003E and a name. The view turns so the new X axis is level. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/ucs-and-turned-view\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/ucs-and-turned-view\"\u003ETurn the view and use your own coordinates\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"plan\"\u003EPLAN\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENo aliases. \u003Ccode\u003EEnter an option [Current ucs/Ucs/World] &lt;Current ucs&gt;\u003C/code\u003E: turns the active pane&#039;s view so that UCS&#039;s X axis (or the world&#039;s) is level. Nothing in the drawing moves.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"turnleft-turnright-turnworld\"\u003ETURNLEFT, TURNRIGHT, TURNWORLD\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENo aliases; or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETurn view 90° left\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETurn view 90° right\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack to world\u003C/span\u003E. The same as \u003Ccode\u003EUCS Z -90\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EUCS Z 90\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EUCS W\u003C/code\u003E, with the view following.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"vplayer\"\u003EVPLAYER\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENo aliases. On a sheet, or inside a viewport. \u003Ccode\u003EEnter an option [?/Freeze/Thaw/Reset/Vpvisdflt]\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003EF\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ET\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E ask for layer names (commas, \u003Ccode\u003E*\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E?\u003C/code\u003E wildcards), then \u003Ccode\u003E[All/Select/Current] &lt;Current&gt;\u003C/code\u003E viewports; Reset puts each layer back to its &quot;frozen in new viewports&quot; default; \u003Ccode\u003EV\u003C/code\u003E sets that default (\u003Ccode\u003E[Frozen/Thawed]\u003C/code\u003E), which \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMVIEW\u003C/code\u003E uses. \u003Ccode\u003E?\u003C/code\u003E lists the layers frozen in the current viewport. The Layers panel&#039;s viewport column does the same for one viewport. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/sheets-and-viewports#freeze-a-layer-in-one-viewport\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/sheets-and-viewports\" data-hash=\"freeze-a-layer-in-one-viewport\"\u003EFreeze a layer in one viewport\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"mspace\"\u003EMSPACE\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EMS\u003C/code\u003E; or double-click inside a viewport. On a sheet only. Work on the model through a viewport: the one selected, the only one on the sheet, or \u003Ccode\u003EClick inside the viewport to work in\u003C/code\u003E. Pan and zoom inside change the viewport&#039;s view (a display-locked one keeps it and the sheet moves instead); the new view is written as one undo step. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/sheets-and-viewports#work-on-the-model-through-a-viewport\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/sheets-and-viewports\" data-hash=\"work-on-the-model-through-a-viewport\"\u003EWork on the model through a viewport\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"pspace\"\u003EPSPACE\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EPS\u003C/code\u003E; or double-click outside the viewport, or click a tab under the drawing. Back to the sheet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"plot\"\u003EPLOT\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EPRINT\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EPUBLISH\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EEXPORTPDF\u003C/code\u003E; or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command P\"\u003E⌘P\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+P\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Opens the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlot to PDF\u003C/span\u003E dialog. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/plot-pdf\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/plot-pdf\"\u003EPlot to PDF\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"plotstyles\"\u003EPLOTSTYLES\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003ESTYLESMANAGER\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EPSTABLES\u003C/code\u003E; or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit tables…\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlot to PDF\u003C/span\u003E dialog. Opens \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlot style tables\u003C/span\u003E: what each color prints as (color, gray, ink, lineweight), kept with the drawing. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/plot-pdf#plot-style-tables\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/plot-pdf\" data-hash=\"plot-style-tables\"\u003EPlot style tables\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"inquiry\"\u003EInquiry\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"cal\"\u003ECAL\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPrints the value of a calculation, as it would be read anywhere a length is typed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EExpression\u003C/code\u003E: type it, for example \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;6&quot;+3/4&quot;\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E(24&#039;-0&quot;)/3\u003C/code\u003E, and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. CadCraft prints \u003Ccode\u003E= 12&#039;-6 3/4&quot;   (150.75 drawing units)\u003C/code\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/coordinate-entry#arithmetic-in-a-typed-value\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/coordinate-entry\" data-hash=\"arithmetic-in-a-typed-value\"\u003EArithmetic in a typed value\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"dist\"\u003EDIST\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EDI\u003C/code\u003E; or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMeasure distance\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify first point\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify second point\u003C/code\u003E. Prints distance, angle, Delta X and Delta Y.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"area\"\u003EAREA\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EAA\u003C/code\u003E; or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArea\u003C/span\u003E. Prints the area and perimeter.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify first corner point [Object/Add area/Subtract area] &lt;Object&gt;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick or type corners; at \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify next point (Enter closes) [Undo]\u003C/code\u003E press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to close the outline. \u003Ccode\u003EU\u003C/code\u003E takes back the last corner.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003EO\u003C/code\u003E, then \u003Ccode\u003ESelect object\u003C/code\u003E: a circle (prints its circumference), an ellipse, a polyline (an open one is closed by a straight line for the area, and its length is printed) or a hatch (holes are taken off).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E (Add area) and \u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E (Subtract area) keep a running total: the prompt starts with \u003Ccode\u003E(ADD mode)\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E(SUBTRACT mode)\u003C/code\u003E, each area is added or taken off, and \u003Ccode\u003ETotal area = …\u003C/code\u003E is printed after each. In these modes \u003Ccode\u003EO\u003C/code\u003E picks objects until \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E at the first prompt ends the command.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"measuregeom\"\u003EMEASUREGEOM\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EMEA\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003EEnter an option [Distance/Radius/Angle/ARea] &lt;Distance&gt;\u003C/code\u003E; after each measurement it asks again, with \u003Ccode\u003EeXit\u003C/code\u003E the default.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ED\u003C/code\u003E: two points, as \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIST\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ESelect arc or circle\u003C/code\u003E; prints the radius and diameter.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E: an arc (its included angle), a line and then a second line (the angle between them, toward their far ends), or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and three points (vertex, then the two ends).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EAR\u003C/code\u003E: as \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EAREA\u003C/code\u003E, one area.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"id\"\u003EID\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENo aliases. \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify point\u003C/code\u003E; prints its X and Y.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"list\"\u003ELIST\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003ELI\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ELS\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects\u003C/code\u003E; prints each object&#039;s type, layer and main measurements.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"view-file-and-other\"\u003EView, file and other\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"zoom\"\u003EZOOM\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EZ\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify corner of window, enter a scale factor (nX) [Extents/All/Window/Previous/Scale/Center]\u003C/code\u003E. Click two corners for a window. \u003Ccode\u003EE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E or Enter: the whole drawing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E (Previous, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPrevious\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar) goes back to the view before, up to 10 views per tab; a run of wheel steps or one pan counts as one view.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA number such as \u003Ccode\u003E2\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E2x\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E0.5x\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E and then the number) zooms in or out by that factor round the middle of the screen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E asks \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify center point\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EEnter height or magnification (nX)\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"pdfattach-pdfimport-and-the-pdf-commands\"\u003EPDFATTACH, PDFIMPORT and the PDF commands\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EPDFATT\u003C/code\u003E for \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPDFATTACH\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EPDFIM\u003C/code\u003E for \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPDFIMPORT\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPDFATTACH\u003C/code\u003E puts a PDF page under the drawing (file, page, insertion point, scale, rotation); \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPDFIMPORT\u003C/code\u003E turns a page&#039;s lines, curves, fills and text into drawing objects on layers named after the PDF, at true size, a typed scale, or scaled from two points and a real distance. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPDFCLIP\u003C/code\u003E clips an underlay, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPDFADJUST\u003C/code\u003E sets fade, contrast, monochrome or switches it off, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPDFLAYERS\u003C/code\u003E switches the PDF&#039;s own layers, and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPDF\u003C/code\u003E gives CadCraft the files a drawing&#039;s underlays use again. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/pdf-underlay\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/pdf-underlay\"\u003EPDF underlays\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"autocomplete\"\u003EAUTOCOMPLETE\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAlias: \u003Ccode\u003EAUTOC\u003C/code\u003E. Drawing autocomplete: with no command running, a grey ghost shows what your drawing&#039;s own patterns say comes next. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EAUTOCOMPLETE\u003C/code\u003E turns it \u003Ccode\u003EOn\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003EOff\u003C/code\u003E (also Settings › CadCraft) or \u003Ccode\u003EShow\u003C/code\u003Es what it would suggest now. \u003Cstrong\u003ETab\u003C/strong\u003E draws the ghost as one undo step, \u003Cstrong\u003EEsc\u003C/strong\u003E dismisses it until the drawing changes. It finds three things in the newest objects: the next copy of something drawn twice or more at an even step (studs, columns, a stair&#039;s riser and tread), the next steps of a polyline stair, and the missing side of a room or wall chain. In the middle of \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELINE\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPLINE\u003C/code\u003E the ghost is the \u003Cstrong\u003Enext point\u003C/strong\u003E: the next step of a stair (riser, tread, riser…), the fourth corner of a rectangle, the point that closes it, or one more equal step. Bring the cursor near it and press \u003Cstrong\u003ETab\u003C/strong\u003E, as if you had clicked there; carry on or press Enter. Worked out on this computer by rules, with no model.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"autosheet\"\u003EAUTOSHEET\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAlias: \u003Ccode\u003EASH\u003C/code\u003E. Sheets that lay themselves out. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EAUTOSHEET\u003C/code\u003E takes the pieces of the drawing (your named views, or the separate pieces of the model, or the whole model), chooses a paper size (or the one you give), gives every piece a standard scale (one scale for all, or the largest each can take) and places the views in rows on new sheets with a title block and a caption (name and scale) under each. What does not fit on one sheet gets a sheet of its own. One undo step. Worked out on this computer by rules, with no model.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"photosketch\"\u003EPHOTOSKETCH\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAlias: \u003Ccode\u003EPSK\u003C/code\u003E. Sketch photo to drawing. Choose a photo (JPEG, PNG or TIFF) of a hand sketch on a sheet of paper. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPHOTOSKETCH\u003C/code\u003E finds the sheet&#039;s four corners, straightens the perspective, takes the paper size you name (\u003Ccode\u003EPaper\u003C/code\u003E, or the nearest standard one) and the \u003Ccode\u003EScale\u003C/code\u003E of the sketch (1 : n), then traces the pen lines into lines, arcs and circles, a layer for each pen colour (black, red, blue, green). If it cannot find the paper&#039;s edge it takes the whole photo as the sheet and says so. Lettering is left out. One undo step. Done on this computer with classic image processing, no model, nothing sent anywhere. See also the scanned-drawing commands.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"autodim\"\u003EAUTODIM\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAlias: \u003Ccode\u003EADIM\u003C/code\u003E. Auto-dimension by rules. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EAUTODIM\u003C/code\u003E dimensions the selected objects (or the whole drawing) from their level and plumb edges: a chain of dimensions along the sides you choose (\u003Ccode\u003ESides\u003C/code\u003E), one overall dimension outside it, the width of each opening (a gap between collinear wall lines), a diameter for each size of hole, and with \u003Ccode\u003EGrid\u003C/code\u003E the spacing between grid lines. A dimension that is already there is not made again. One undo step. Worked out on this computer by rules, with no model.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"gridmove\"\u003EGRIDMOVE\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAlias: \u003Ccode\u003EGM\u003C/code\u003E. Moving a grid line carries the set. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EGRIDMOVE\u003C/code\u003E moves a grid line (a line on a layer whose name says grid or axis) and what hangs on it: walls with an end on the line stretch to follow it, walls along it move whole, columns (block references and circles on the line), its bubble and tag move, and dimensions whose points sit on it measure the new spacing. Give two points (only the part across the line counts) or \u003Ccode\u003EDistance\u003C/code\u003E. One undo step takes it all back. Done by rules on this computer.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"details\"\u003EDETAILS\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAlias: \u003Ccode\u003EDET\u003C/code\u003E. Details that find you. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDETAILS\u003C/code\u003E keeps your firm&#039;s own details: \u003Ccode\u003EAdd\u003C/code\u003E puts a block of this drawing in the list, \u003Ccode\u003EList\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003ERemove\u003C/code\u003E manage it, and \u003Ccode\u003EFind\u003C/code\u003E ranks them for what is selected (or for what you drew last) by the words on its layers, block names, hatch patterns and notes against each detail&#039;s name and notes, so a window head finds your sill flashing. Pick a number and a point to place it. With \u003Ccode\u003EHint\u003C/code\u003E on (also Settings › CadCraft), selecting something a detail matches puts one line in the status bar. The list is kept in this browser, on this computer, and that list is the one searched. \u003Ccode\u003EShare\u003C/code\u003E puts it in your Documents folder too (\u003Ccode\u003E.cadcraft/firm-details.json\u003C/code\u003E, with each block&#039;s drawing): \u003Ccode\u003EPull\u003C/code\u003E adds what your team has and you lack, \u003Ccode\u003EPush\u003C/code\u003E adds what you have and they lack, \u003Ccode\u003EBoth\u003C/code\u003E does both, and nothing is ever removed from either side. Open the Documents folder first (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDOCUMENTS\u003C/code\u003E); when that folder is a Team Cloud Plan folder, the whole team shares one list. A detail item in the Library panel has an \u003Cstrong\u003EAdd to my firm&#039;s details\u003C/strong\u003E button that puts it in the same list. Worked out on this computer by words, with no model.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"sketchfix\"\u003ESKETCHFIX\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAlias: \u003Ccode\u003ESKF\u003C/code\u003E. Pen sketches become precise geometry. Press and drag one freehand stroke at a time (pen, mouse or finger), then Enter. Each stroke is read as a line, circle, arc, rectangle or polyline: nearly level and plumb lines are made exactly level and plumb, a closed round stroke becomes a circle, a closed stroke with four right-angled corners a rectangle, and the ends of the strokes are pulled together and onto the ends already in the drawing. One undo step. Worked out on this computer by rules, with no model.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"scanattach-and-the-scanned-drawing-commands\"\u003ESCANATTACH and the scanned-drawing commands\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003ESCA\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESCANATTACH\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESCS\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESCANSCALE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESCL\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESCANALIGN\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESCD\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESCANDESKEW\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESCC\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESCANCLEAN\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESCR\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESCANCROP\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESCE\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESCANERASE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESCT\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESCANTRACE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESCI\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESCANINFO\u003C/code\u003E; \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ERASTERSNAP\u003C/code\u003E has none. They work on a picture, one undo step each. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESCANATTACH\u003C/code\u003E inserts a PNG, JPEG, TIFF (one page or all) or a PDF page that is a picture. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESCANSCALE\u003C/code\u003E scales the sheet from two picked points and the true distance. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESCANALIGN\u003C/code\u003E moves and turns it onto two points (\u003Ccode\u003EScale the sheet to fit\u003C/code\u003E Yes/No). \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESCANDESKEW\u003C/code\u003E shows the angle of the sheet&#039;s long lines and turns the sheet once you \u003Ccode\u003EAccept\u003C/code\u003E or give a \u003Ccode\u003EValue\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESCANCLEAN\u003C/code\u003E makes it black and white (\u003Ccode\u003EThreshold\u003C/code\u003E Auto/Adaptive/Level, \u003Ccode\u003ESpecks\u003C/code\u003E) and \u003Ccode\u003ERun\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESCANCROP\u003C/code\u003E keeps a window. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESCANERASE\u003C/code\u003E erases a \u003Ccode\u003ERectangle\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EPolygon\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003ELasso\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESCANTRACE\u003C/code\u003E traces lines and arcs onto a new layer (\u003Ccode\u003ETolerance\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EWindow\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EArcs\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ELeftovers\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ERun\u003C/code\u003E) and reports what it found and what it left as raster. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESCANINFO\u003C/code\u003E lists the original file and the steps. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ERASTERSNAP\u003C/code\u003E turns snapping to the lines of scanned sheets On or Off. See the topic Scanned drawings and the trace.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"regen-and-regenall\"\u003EREGEN and REGENALL\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003ERE\u003C/code\u003E for \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EREGEN\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EREA\u003C/code\u003E for \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EREGENALL\u003C/code\u003E. Rebuild what is drawn on screen from the drawing: \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EREGEN\u003C/code\u003E the tab you are looking at (on a sheet, the model its viewports show too), \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EREGENALL\u003C/code\u003E every tab, every split-screen pane and every open drawing. Use them when the picture looks stale after a scripted or very large change, or an assistant edited the drawing; the drawing itself is not touched, so there is nothing to undo.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"namedview\"\u003ENAMEDVIEW\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003ENV\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EVIEWS\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003EEnter an option [?/Restore/Save/Window/Delete] &lt;Restore&gt;\u003C/code\u003E, then the view&#039;s name. \u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E saves the view on screen, \u003Ccode\u003EW\u003C/code\u003E a window you pick, \u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E shows a saved view, \u003Ccode\u003ED\u003C/code\u003E deletes one, \u003Ccode\u003E?\u003C/code\u003E lists the views of this tab. Saving under an existing name replaces it; each change is one undo step. Views belong to the tab they were saved on. DWG and DXF files keep them (the VIEW table); a sheet&#039;s views come back on the first sheet, since the file does not say which.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"undo-and-redo\"\u003EUNDO and REDO\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EU\u003C/code\u003E for \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EUNDO\u003C/code\u003E; \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EREDO\u003C/code\u003E has none. Also \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"layer\"\u003ELAYER\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003ELA\u003C/code\u003E. Jumps to the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayers\u003C/span\u003E panel.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"linetype-lweight-ltscale\"\u003ELINETYPE, LWEIGHT, LTSCALE\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELINETYPE\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003ELT\u003C/code\u003E): \u003Ccode\u003EEnter linetype for new objects\u003C/code\u003E — \u003Ccode\u003EByLayer\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EByBlock\u003C/code\u003E or a name (\u003Ccode\u003E?\u003C/code\u003E lists them: Continuous, the drawing&#039;s own, and DASHED, HIDDEN, CENTER, PHANTOM, DOT, DASHDOT, BORDER, DIVIDE with their \u003Ccode\u003E2\u003C/code\u003E (half) and \u003Ccode\u003EX2\u003C/code\u003E (double) sizes). \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELWEIGHT\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003ELW\u003C/code\u003E): the lineweight for new objects in mm, or \u003Ccode\u003EByLayer\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EByBlock\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDefault\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELTSCALE\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003ELTS\u003C/code\u003E): the drawing&#039;s linetype scale (undoable). \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELWDISPLAY\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003ELWT\u003C/code\u003E): \u003Ccode\u003EShow lineweights [ON/OFF]\u003C/code\u003E — the same as the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELWT\u003C/span\u003E button in the status bar. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/units-papers-scales#line-weights-and-linetypes\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/units-papers-scales\" data-hash=\"line-weights-and-linetypes\"\u003ELine weights and linetypes\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"rename-laymrg-laydel\"\u003ERENAME, LAYMRG, LAYDEL\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ERENAME\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003E-RENAME\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EREN\u003C/code\u003E): \u003Ccode\u003EEnter object type to rename [LAyer/Block]\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EEnter old name\u003C/code\u003E, then the new name; objects or references follow. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYMRG\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ESelect object on layer to merge or [Name]\u003C/code\u003E until \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, then \u003Ccode\u003ESelect object on target layer or [Name]\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EY\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYDEL\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ESelect object on layer to delete or [Name]\u003C/code\u003E, then \u003Ccode\u003EY\u003C/code\u003E: the layer and every object on it go (not layer 0, DEFPOINTS or the current layer). One undo step each. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/layers#rename-merge-and-delete-layers\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/layers\" data-hash=\"rename-merge-and-delete-layers\"\u003EOrganize a drawing with layers\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"layiso-layuniso-layoff-layfrz-laymcur-layon-laythw\"\u003ELAYISO, LAYUNISO, LAYOFF, LAYFRZ, LAYMCUR, LAYON, LAYTHW\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELayers by picking objects; also in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayers\u003C/span\u003E panel&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETools\u003C/span\u003E menu. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYISO\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects on the layer(s) to be isolated\u003C/code\u003E, then \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: every other layer goes off. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYUNISO\u003C/code\u003E turns them back on. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYOFF\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYFRZ\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ESelect an object on the layer to be turned off\u003C/code\u003E (or frozen), over and over; \u003Ccode\u003EU\u003C/code\u003E takes back the last one, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E ends. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYMCUR\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ESelect object whose layer will become current\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYON\u003C/code\u003E turns every layer on, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYTHW\u003C/code\u003E thaws every layer. Each command is one undo step. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/layers#work-on-layers-by-picking-objects\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/layers\" data-hash=\"work-on-layers-by-picking-objects\"\u003EOrganize a drawing with layers\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"new-open-save-saveas-dxfout\"\u003ENEW, OPEN, SAVE, SAVEAS, DXFOUT\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ENEW\u003C/code\u003E starts an empty drawing and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EOPEN\u003C/code\u003E opens a DWG or DXF, each in its own tab over the drawing (an untouched new drawing is used instead of a new tab). Closing a tab with unsaved changes asks first. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/split-screen\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/split-screen\"\u003ESplit the screen\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESAVE\u003C/code\u003E (alias \u003Ccode\u003EQSAVE\u003C/code\u003E, also \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) downloads a copy in the drawing&#039;s format: DWG if it was opened from a DWG, DXF if from a DXF. A new drawing uses the format last chosen in Save as in this browser, else DWG.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESAVEAS\u003C/code\u003E (also \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command S\"\u003E⇧⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) opens the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave a copy as\u003C/span\u003E dialog: choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDWG\u003C/span\u003E (and its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EVersion\u003C/span\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDXF R12\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDXF 2000\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E. The choice sticks for this drawing and for new drawings in this browser.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDXFOUT\u003C/code\u003E always downloads a DXF R12 copy, without changing the format \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESAVE\u003C/code\u003E uses.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/files#saving\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/files\" data-hash=\"saving\"\u003EFiles: what opens, what saves\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"tour\"\u003ETOUR\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003ETUTORIAL\u003C/code\u003E. Starts the six-step first-drawing tour again. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/getting-started#prefer-to-be-guided\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/getting-started\" data-hash=\"prefer-to-be-guided\"\u003EGetting started\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"toolbardock\"\u003ETOOLBARDOCK\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003ETBDOCK\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003EToolbar position [Top/Bottom/Left/Right]\u003C/code\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/settings#toolbar-position\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/settings\" data-hash=\"toolbar-position\"\u003ESettings\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"polarang\"\u003EPOLARANG\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EEnter polar angle step in degrees &lt;90&gt;\u003C/code\u003E: the step \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPolar\u003C/span\u003E tracking lines up at (more than 0, up to 180). Kept in this browser.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"units\"\u003EUNITS\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EUN\u003C/code\u003E. How lengths are written and typed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EEnter length format [Scientific/Decimal/Engineering/Architectural/Fractional] &lt;current&gt;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EArchitectural and Fractional: \u003Ccode\u003EEnter denominator of smallest fraction to display (1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 128 or 256)\u003C/code\u003E. The others: \u003Ccode\u003EEnter number of decimal places (0 to 8)\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENew dimensions follow it. One undo step. Same as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength format\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPrecision\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECurrent settings\u003C/span\u003E; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/units-papers-scales#feet-and-inches-and-fractions\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/units-papers-scales\" data-hash=\"feet-and-inches-and-fractions\"\u003EFeet and inches\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"gdsin\"\u003EGDSIN\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EGDS\u003C/code\u003E. View an IC layout (a GDSII stream file): lab spike, for viewing and measuring only.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA file picker takes a \u003Ccode\u003E.gds\u003C/code\u003E file; with several top cells, \u003Ccode\u003EEnter the top cell to draw\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEvery layer and datatype becomes a drawing layer (\u003Ccode\u003EGDS 1\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EGDS 2/5\u003C/code\u003E), references and arrays are placed flat, in the file&#039;s user unit (usually micrometres). One undo step. Nothing is simulated or checked and the file is not written back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"diagram\"\u003EDIAGRAM\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003ETEXTDIAGRAM\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ET2D\u003C/code\u003E. A diagram from text.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA box takes the text (or one line with \u003Ccode\u003E;\u003C/code\u003E between statements at the command line): a Mermaid flowchart, a PlantUML component diagram, D2 or a from-to list.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify the top left corner of the diagram\u003C/code\u003E, then \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify the text height\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBlocks and connectors are ordinary objects on layer \u003Ccode\u003EDiagram\u003C/code\u003E, selected when it ends. One undo step. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/text-to-diagram\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/text-to-diagram\"\u003EDraw a diagram from text\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"olelist\"\u003EOLELIST\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EOLES\u003C/code\u003E. The OLE objects a DWG carries that CadCraft cannot place by itself.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELists each one: number, type, size, whether it saved a picture.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EEnter an option [Place/eXit] &lt;eXit&gt;\u003C/code\u003E: Place asks for the number, the lower left corner and the width, and draws a frame with the picture. One undo step; the kept object is not changed. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/pictures#objects-from-other-programs-ole\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/pictures\" data-hash=\"objects-from-other-programs-ole\"\u003EPictures\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"shxfonts\"\u003ESHXFONTS\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003ESHX\u003C/code\u003E. Your own SHX fonts.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EEnter an SHX font option [Folder/Files/List/Remove/Reverse/Clear] &lt;List&gt;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFolder\u003C/span\u003E reads every \u003Ccode\u003E.shx\u003C/code\u003E in a folder (CadCraft Studio; again at each start), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFiles\u003C/span\u003E takes picked files (the browser), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReverse\u003C/span\u003E lays out one font&#039;s letters last first. Text styles that name a loaded font draw in it. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/shx-fonts\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/shx-fonts\"\u003EDraw text in your own SHX fonts\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"limits\"\u003ELIMITS\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow big the drawing is meant to be.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify lower left corner or [ON/OFF] &lt;current&gt;\u003C/code\u003E: a point, \u003Ccode\u003EON\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003EOFF\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify upper right corner &lt;current&gt;\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe grid fills the limits, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EZOOM\u003C/code\u003E All shows them, and with them ON a point outside is refused (\u003Ccode\u003E**Outside limits\u003C/code\u003E). One undo step; saved in DWG and DXF. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/drawing-limits\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/drawing-limits\"\u003ESet the drawing limits\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"aunits\"\u003EAUNITS\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EANGUNITS\u003C/code\u003E. How angles are shown and typed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EEnter angle format [Decimal degrees/Degrees-minutes-seconds/Surveyor&#039;s bearings] &lt;current&gt;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EEnter number of decimal places (0 to 8)\u003C/code\u003E, or for the other two \u003Ccode\u003EEnter precision\u003C/code\u003E (0 degrees, 2 minutes, 4 seconds, 5 to 8 decimals of a second).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOne undo step; saved in DWG and DXF. Same as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAngle format\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECurrent settings\u003C/span\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/bearings-and-angles\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/bearings-and-angles\"\u003EType and show bearings and angles\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"appload\"\u003EAPPLOAD\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EAP\u003C/code\u003E; or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELoad application…\u003C/span\u003E. The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELoad applications\u003C/span\u003E dialog: load \u003Ccode\u003E.lsp\u003C/code\u003E routines and \u003Ccode\u003E.dcl\u003C/code\u003E dialog files, unload them, load them again from the history, and the Startup Suite. A routine&#039;s \u003Ccode\u003E(defun c:NAME …)\u003C/code\u003E commands then work like any other. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/load-applications\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/load-applications\"\u003ELoad applications\u003C/a\u003E and \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/run-lisp\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/run-lisp\"\u003ERun LISP routines\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"aliasedit-reinit\"\u003EALIASEDIT, REINIT\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EALIASEDIT\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit aliases…\u003C/span\u003E) opens your aliases file (\u003Ccode\u003EALIAS, *COMMAND\u003C/code\u003E lines); \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E applies it. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EREINIT\u003C/code\u003E applies it again. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/command-aliases\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/command-aliases\"\u003EEdit command aliases\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"help\"\u003EHELP\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003E?\u003C/code\u003E. Prints every command, its aliases and a one-line description in the command line history. It does not open this help: press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHelp\u003C/span\u003E for that.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"select-objects-prompt\"\u003ESelect objects prompt\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAt any \u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects\u003C/code\u003E prompt: click objects, draw windows (left to right) and crossings (right to left) with two clicks, or press and drag a lasso (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while dragging: window, crossing, fence), \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click to remove, and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to finish. You can also type \u003Ccode\u003EALL\u003C/code\u003E (everything), \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E (the last object), \u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E (Previous: what the last command used), \u003Ccode\u003EF\u003C/code\u003E (Fence: objects a line of points crosses; \u003Ccode\u003EU\u003C/code\u003E takes back a point), \u003Ccode\u003EW\u003C/code\u003E (Window), \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E (Crossing), \u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E (Remove mode: picks take objects out; the prompt reads \u003Ccode\u003ERemove objects\u003C/code\u003E) and \u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E (Add again). \u003Ccode\u003E?\u003C/code\u003E lists them. They are not shown in the prompt. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E over overlapping objects highlights the next one; click to pick it. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/select-objects\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/select-objects\"\u003ESelect objects\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"access\"\u003EACCESS\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAlias: \u003Ccode\u003EAX\u003C/code\u003E. Draft without sight. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EACCESS\u003C/code\u003E turns Access on or off (\u003Ccode\u003EOn\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EOff\u003C/code\u003E) and switches snap sounds (\u003Ccode\u003ESound\u003C/code\u003E) and voice commands (\u003Ccode\u003EVoice\u003C/code\u003E); it says the state first. With Access on, every command and result is spoken through your screen reader and the drawing area has a keyboard cursor. The help topic &quot;Draft without sight&quot; has the keys.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"describe\"\u003EDESCRIBE\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAlias: \u003Ccode\u003EDESC\u003C/code\u003E. Say what the drawing holds: how many objects, of what kind, how big, how many layers, what is selected. Reads only; changes nothing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"whereami\"\u003EWHEREAMI\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAlias: \u003Ccode\u003EWHERE\u003C/code\u003E. Say where the cursor is, in the drawing&#039;s units.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"nearby\"\u003ENEARBY\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESay what lies close to the cursor, nearest first, each with its distance and the side it is on.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"saysel\"\u003ESAYSEL\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESay what is selected, one sentence per object (line length, ends and angle; circle radius and centre; text as written).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"voice\"\u003EVOICE\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETurn spoken commands on or off. They are recognised on this computer; where the browser cannot do that, voice stays off unless you choose the browser&#039;s online service in Settings.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Type a command name or an alias and press Enter or Space. Case does not matter, and a leading _, -, . or ' is ignored. Type HELP (or ?) to print a short list of every command in the command line history; press F1 for this help. How to read the prompts: options are in square brackets, and you choose one by typing its capital letters (D for Diameter). A default is in angle brackets; press Enter to accept it. Where a point is asked for, you can click or type any of the formats in Coordinate and distance entry. Where a distance is asked for, you can type a number or click two points. Pressing Enter where a point or an object is required, and there is no default, ends the command. Esc cancels it. Whatever a command did is one undo step. Draw LINE Aliases: L. Straight segments, each its own object. Specify first point (Enter: start from the last point you gave) Specify next point [Undo], repeated. After two segments: [Undo/Close]. U takes back the last segment, C closes to the first point and ends. Enter ends. PLINE Aliases: PL. One polyline of line and arc segments. Specify start point Specify next point [Arc/Close/Undo] (Close once there are two segments) A switches to arcs: Specify endpoint of arc [Line/CLose/Undo]. Each arc continues tangent to the segment before. L goes back to lines, CL closes with an arc. Enter ends; the polyline stays open. CIRCLE Aliases: C. Specify center point for circle [3P/2P/Ttr (tan tan radius)] Specify radius of circle [Diameter] \u003Clast radius\u003E; D asks Specify diameter of circle. 2P: two ends of a diameter. 3P: three points on the circle. T (also the command TTR, and Draw › More › Circle: Tan, Tan, Radius): Specify point on object for first tangent of circle, the second, then the radius (Enter: the last one). The circle touches both objects (lines, arcs, circles, polyline segments, also inside blocks); of the circles that do, the one touching nearest your picks. \"Circle does not exist\" when none fits. TTT (or Draw › More › Circle: Tan, Tan, Tan): three objects; the circle touching all three, nearest your picks (three lines give the inner circle or an outer one, depending on where you pick). ARC Aliases: A. Specify start point of arc [Center], Specify second point of arc, Specify end point of arc: an arc through three points. C: Specify center point of arc, Specify start point of arc, Specify end point of arc. The arc runs counterclockwise from start to end. RECTANG Aliases: REC, RECTANGLE. A closed polyline. Specify first corner point, Specify other corner point. Type @width,height for the second corner. POLYGON Aliases: POL. A regular polygon as a closed polyline. Enter number of sides \u003C4\u003E (3 to 1024) Specify center of polygon Enter an option [Inscribed in circle/Circumscribed about circle] \u003CI\u003E Specify radius of circle: click or type a point; its distance sets the size and its direction turns the polygon. ELLIPSE Aliases: EL. Specify axis endpoint of ellipse [Center], Specify other endpoint of axis, Specify distance to other axis C: Specify center of ellipse, Specify endpoint of axis, then the distance to the other axis. HATCH Aliases: H, BH, BHATCH; or Hatch in the toolbar. Prints the current pattern, scale and angle first. Pick internal point [Select objects/Pattern/SCale/Angle], repeated (Undo once there is an area): each click adds the closed area round it, with the closed shapes inside it as islands. Enter makes one hatch of all of them. S: Select closed objects (closed polylines, circles, closed ellipses). P: Enter a pattern name, SOLID, or ? for the list \u003Ccurrent\u003E. SC: Specify pattern scale. A: Specify pattern angle. See Hatch and fill areas. POINT Aliases: PO; or Draw › More › Point. Point objects, one per click, until Enter. Specify a point XLINE Aliases: XL; or Draw › More › Construction line. Construction lines, endless both ways. See Construction lines and rays. Specify a point [Hor/Ver/Ang/Bisect/Offset], then Specify through point for each line, until Enter. A: Enter angle of construction line [Reference] \u003C0\u003E; R asks Select a line object first. B: Specify angle vertex point, Specify angle start point, then Specify angle end point for each line. O: Specify offset distance [Through] \u003CThrough\u003E, then Select a line object and Specify side to offset (or Specify through point) for each line. RAY No aliases; or Draw › More › Ray. Specify start point, then Specify through point for each ray, until Enter. TEXT Aliases: DT, DTEXT; or Text in the toolbar. Single-line text; each line is its own object. Specify start point of text [Justify] J: Enter an option [Left/Center/Right/Middle/TL/MC] \u003CL\u003E, then Specify insertion point of text Specify height \u003Ccurrent text height\u003E Specify rotation angle of text \u003C0\u003E Enter text, repeated: each Enter starts the next line below. Enter on an empty line ends. Space types a space here. Modify Aliases: MT, T; or Paragraph in the toolbar. Paragraph text that wraps to a width, typed in place. Specify first corner Specify opposite corner [Height/Justify/Line spacing/Rotation/Width]: the width across the two corners is where the text wraps. H asks Specify height, J Enter justification [TL/TC/TR/ML/MC/MR/BL/BC/BR] \u003CTL\u003E, L Enter line spacing factor (0.25 to 4), R Specify rotation angle, W Specify width (0 for no wrapping) and skips the second corner. Then a box opens on the drawing: type, Enter for a new paragraph, Ctrl+Enter or a click outside to finish, Esc to cancel. Its bar sets height, line spacing, width and justification. See Add text and labels. TEXTEDIT Aliases: ED, DDEDIT; or double-click a text. Select a text to edit, repeated until Enter: each opens in the same box. Started on a selected or double-clicked text, it edits that one and ends. ERASE Aliases: E, DELETE; or press Delete with objects selected. Select objects (skipped when objects are already selected) MOVE Aliases: M. Select objects Specify base point [Displacement], Specify second point D: Specify displacement, typed as dx,dy. COPY Aliases: CO, CP. Copies as many times as you give points. Select objects Specify base point [Displacement] Specify second point [Undo], repeated until Enter. U takes back the last copy. ROTATE Aliases: RO. Select objects, Specify base point Specify rotation angle \u003Clast angle\u003E: degrees, counterclockwise positive; or click a point to rotate toward it. SCALE Aliases: SC. Select objects, Specify base point Specify scale factor: type a factor such as 0.5 or 2. A clicked point gives its distance from the base point as the factor. MIRROR Aliases: MI. Select objects Specify first point of mirror line, Specify second point of mirror line Erase source objects? [Yes/No] \u003CN\u003E ALIGN Aliases: AL; or Modify › More › Align. Select objects Specify first source point, Specify first destination point Specify second source point (Enter to continue), Specify second destination point Specify third source point (Enter to continue), Specify third destination point With two pairs: Scale objects based on alignment points? [Yes/No] \u003CN\u003E See Align objects. ALIGNTO Select objects (or the selection), Align [Left/Center/Right/Top/Middle/Bottom] \u003CLeft\u003E, To [Selection/First/Last/Point] \u003CSelection\u003E; P asks for the point. The selection stays. DISTRIBUTE Select objects (three at least), Distribute [Horizontally/Vertically] \u003CHorizontally\u003E, Space evenly [Centers/Gaps] \u003CGaps\u003E. The outer two stay; the selection stays. ALIGNPANEL Aliases: ALP; or Modify › More › Align and distribute. Opens the panel that runs ALIGNTO and DISTRIBUTE by clicks. LASER Aliases: LASERCUT; or View › More › Laser…. Opens the Laser panel over the drawing: what each layer does (cut, score, engrave, fill, picture), the preview, GRBL G-code to save, and framing and sending over Web Serial. See Send a drawing to a laser. STRETCH Aliases: S; or Stretch. Select objects to stretch: drag crossing windows (or type C) over the ends to move. Specify base point [Displacement], Specify second point. Points inside the windows move: line ends, polyline vertices (arc segments keep their height), dimension points; circles, ellipses, text, points and blocks move whole when their center or insertion point is inside. A selection made before the command keeps the crossing windows (and lassos) that made it, so they stretch the same way. Without a window, the objects move whole. ARRAY, ARRAYRECT, ARRAYPOLAR Aliases: AR; or Array. Select objects, then Enter array type [Rectangular/POlar] \u003CRectangular\u003E (ARRAY only). Rectangular starts at 4 columns × 3 rows, spaced 1.5 times the objects' size, shown as a preview. Enter an option [COUnt/Spacing/COLumns/Rows/eXit] \u003CX\u003E: COU asks columns and rows, S the distances between columns and between rows (negative goes left or down), COL and R one direction each. Polar asks Specify center point of array, starts at 6 items all the way round, rotated. Enter an option [Items/Fill angle/ROTate items/eXit] \u003CX\u003E: I items, F the angle to fill (+ counterclockwise, − clockwise), ROT Yes or No. Enter (eXit) makes the copies, as plain objects. One undo step. OFFSET Aliases: O. Lines, arcs, circles and polylines. Specify offset distance [Through] \u003Clast distance, or Through\u003E Select object to offset, then Specify point on side to offset (or, with Through, Specify through point), repeated until Enter. TRIMEXTENDMODE Enter new value for TRIMEXTENDMODE (1 Quick, 0 Standard): how TRIM and EXTEND start. 1 Quick: every object is a cutting edge or boundary. 0 Standard: they ask for the edges first. Also (setvar \"TRIMEXTENDMODE\" 0) in LISP. TRIM Aliases: TR. Prints Current settings: Quick mode (TRIMEXTENDMODE 1), edges: No extend, dragging draws a path first. In Quick mode every visible object is a cutting edge, and an object nothing crosses is erased; in Standard mode it asks Select cutting edges (Enter = every object) first and cuts only at those. Select object to trim or shift-select to extend [cuTting edges/Fence/Crossing/mOde/Edge/eRase/Undo], repeated: click the piece to remove (it shows grey and dashed while the pointer rests on it), drag a path through several pieces (all go in one step), or click two empty spots for a fence between them. Shift extends instead. T chooses cutting edges (Standard mode), F asks fence points, C two window corners (pieces partly inside go), O asks Enter a trim mode option [Quick/Standard], E asks Enter an implied edge extension mode [Extend/No extend] (EDGEMODE), R erases objects you select, U undoes the last pick or drag. Enter ends. See Trim and extend. EXTEND Aliases: EX. The same as TRIM the other way round: Select object to extend or shift-select to trim [Boundary edges/Fence/Crossing/mOde/Edge/Undo]. A click lengthens the end nearest it to the next boundary; a dragged path or fence lengthens every object it crosses; Shift trims. B chooses boundary edges (Standard mode). Mode and Edge are shared with TRIM. LINKVIEWS Aliases: SYNCVIEWS; or View › More › Link the panes' views, or the link icon on the drawing tabs. Link the panes' views [ON/OFF] (Enter takes the other state): while on, a pan or zoom in the active pane moves every pane showing the same kind of space. See Split the screen. OPENIN3D No aliases; or View › More › Open in ModelCraft where the 3D modeling app takes a handed-over drawing. Opens it there in a new tab, as a DWG (nothing is uploaded). If the browser blocks the tab, it says so. LASSOMODE Enter new value for LASSOMODE (1 = lasso, 0 = rectangle): what pressing and dragging on an empty spot draws. The same as Settings › CadCraft › Dragging to select. See Lasso selection. FILLET Aliases: F. Lines, arcs, circles and polylines. Prints Current settings: Mode = TRIM, Radius = 0 first. Select first object [Undo/Polyline/Radius/Trim/Multiple] R asks Specify fillet radius \u003Clast\u003E. T asks Enter Trim mode option [Trim/No trim]: with No trim the objects stay as they are and only the arc is added. M keeps the command going for more corners until Enter; U takes back the last corner without leaving the command. P asks Select 2D polyline [Radius] and rounds every corner where two straight segments meet. Fillets it already has are replaced by the new radius; radius 0 takes them out. Corners whose sides are too short for the radius are left and counted. Select second object or shift-select to apply corner [Radius]. Hold Shift as you click for a sharp corner, whatever the radius. Click each object on the part you want to keep. Two lines that do not meet are extended. Two parallel lines get a half circle across the gap, at the end of the first line nearest your click. A circle is never cut. Two segments of one polyline next to each other get the arc inside the polyline; a polyline and another object work when the cut lands on the polyline's first or last segment. CHAMFER Aliases: CHA. Lines and the straight segments of polylines. Prints the current distances (or length and angle) first. Select first line [Undo/Polyline/Distance/Angle/Trim/mEthod/Multiple] D asks Specify first chamfer distance \u003Clast\u003E and Specify second chamfer distance \u003Cthe first\u003E: the first is cut from the first line you pick, the second from the other. A asks Specify chamfer length on the first line and Specify chamfer angle from the first line. E (mEthod) chooses which of the two the next chamfer uses. T, M, U and P work as in FILLET. Select second line or shift-select to apply corner [Distance/Angle/mEthod]. Shift+click makes a sharp corner. A distance longer than the line is refused (Distance is too large.). BREAK and BREAKATPOINT Aliases: BR. Select object (where you click is the first point), Specify second break point [First point]; F asks Specify first break point and Specify second break point. The part between goes; on a circle, counter-clockwise from the first point. BREAKATPOINT asks Specify break point and splits the object in two there (not a circle). LENGTHEN Aliases: LEN. Select an object to measure [DElta/Percent/Total/DYnamic]: a pick prints Current length (and an arc's included angle) and asks again. DE asks Enter delta length (negative shortens) [Angle], P Enter percentage length, T Specify total length [Angle], DY drags. Then Select an object to change [Undo], repeated: the end nearest the click moves. Lines, arcs and open polylines. JOIN Aliases: J. Select objects to join. Lines, arcs and open polylines that meet end to end become polylines (closed when the chain closes); lines on one line become one line; arcs of one circle become one arc or the circle. The history says how many objects joined and how many did not. MATCHPROP Aliases: MA, PAINTER. Select source object, then Select destination objects [Settings] and Enter. Copies layer and color, and between objects of the same kind the text height and width, the dimension sizes, or the hatch pattern. S asks Turn a property on or off [Color/LAyer/Text/Dimension/Hatch], repeated until Enter; the choice is remembered for the session. EXPLODE Aliases: X. Polylines become lines and arcs, block references become their objects, dimensions become lines, arrowheads and text. Select objects Clipboard CadCraft's own clipboard: it works within a drawing, between drawings and between CadCraft tabs in this browser. See Copy, paste and duplicate. COPYCLIP Ctrl+C, or Copy in the Edit group. Copies objects to the clipboard, with the blocks and layers they use. The objects stay selected. Select objects to copy (skipped when objects are already selected) CUTCLIP Ctrl+X, or Cut in the More menu. Copies objects to the clipboard, then erases them (one undo step). Select objects to cut (skipped when objects are already selected) COPYBASE Copies objects to the clipboard with a base point you pick; pasting places that point. Specify base point Select objects to copy PASTECLIP Ctrl+V, or Paste in the Edit group. The copy follows the cursor, hanging from the lower-left corner of its extents (or its center, or the COPYBASE point; see CadCraft settings). Specify insertion point The pasted objects are selected. A block the drawing already has under the same name keeps this drawing's definition. When an area copied in MarkupCraft is newer than what CadCraft copied (and less than 6 hours old), Ctrl+V and PASTECLIP paste that area instead: see PASTEAREA. PASTEAREA Aliases: PAREA. Paste a copied area in the More menu. Pastes an area copied with Copy area in MarkupCraft as a picture at its true size. See Pictures in a drawing. Specify the lower-left corner of the copied area Scale its paper size by (100 for a 1:100 sheet; Points: two points on it and their true distance) [Points] \u003C1\u003E (only when the sheet had no drawing scale): a number, Enter for the paper size, or P, then First point on the pasted area, Second point on the pasted area and their true distance. PASTEORIG Ctrl+Shift+V, or Paste in place in the More menu. Pastes at the coordinates the objects were copied from. Asks nothing. DUPLICATE Aliases: DUP. Ctrl+D, or Duplicate and move in the More menu. A copy of the objects moves with the pointer from where it is; Esc leaves nothing. Does not use the clipboard. Select objects to duplicate (skipped when objects are already selected) Specify where the copy goes (Esc leaves nothing): click, type a point, @dx,dy (from where the pointer was), or a distance toward the cursor. SELECT Asks Select objects (with every option below) and leaves what you chose selected, for the next command or for P later. QSELECT Aliases: QS. In the browser, the Quick select dialog (see Select objects). Without it, the command line asks Enter object type, Enter property, Enter operator [=/\u003C\u003E/\u003E/\u003C/*/Select all] \u003C=\u003E and Enter value. The matches become the selection. SELECTSIMILAR Aliases: SIM. Select objects [SEttings] (or the current selection): selects every object of the same type with the same layer and color (and block name, hatch pattern). SE asks Turn a property on or off [LAyer/COlor/NAme/PAttern/HEight], repeated until Enter. FIND No aliases; or Edit › More › Find and replace. In the browser, the Find and replace dialog (see Add text and labels). Without it: Enter text to find, then Enter replacement text (Enter: select what is found). Looks in text, attribute values and dimension text, on the open tab or in the selection. SELECTALL and DESELECT SELECTALL (alias AI_SELALL, Ctrl+A) selects every object you can edit on the open tab. DESELECT (aliases AI_DESELECT, SELECTNONE; or Esc) clears the selection. Dimensions DIMLINEAR Aliases: DLI, DIMLIN. Horizontal or vertical distance. Specify first extension line origin [Select object] (Enter or S: Select object to dimension, then click a line, arc, circle or polyline segment) Specify second extension line origin Specify dimension line location [Text/Horizontal/Vertical/Rotated]. T asks for text (\u003C\u003E is the measured value), H and V fix the direction, R asks Specify angle of dimension line \u003C0\u003E. DIMALIGNED Aliases: DAL, DIMALI. The true distance between two points. Origins as in DIMLINEAR, then Specify dimension line location [Text]. DIMANGULAR Aliases: DAN, DIMANG. Select arc, circle or line, or Enter for a vertex Arc: its angle. Circle: Specify second angle endpoint. Line: Select second line. Enter: Specify angle vertex, Specify first angle endpoint, Specify second angle endpoint. Specify dimension line location [Text]: click inside the angle to measure. DIMRADIUS and DIMDIAMETER Aliases: DRA, DIMRAD; DDI, DIMDIA. Select arc or circle, then Specify dimension line location [Text]. DIMCONTINUE Aliases: DCO, DIMCONT. Continues from the last linear or aligned dimension made (or asks Select linear or aligned dimension). Specify second extension line origin [Undo], repeated until Enter. DIMBASELINE Aliases: DBA, DIMBASE. Like DIMCONTINUE, but every dimension starts from the first origin and is stacked further out. DIMSCALE, DIMTXT, DIMASZ, DIMDEC, DIMGAP, DIMEXE, DIMEXO No aliases. Each asks Enter new value for … \u003Ccurrent\u003E and sets that size for new dimensions: overall scale, text height, arrow size, decimal places (up to 8), gap round the text, extension beyond the dimension line, offset from the origin. See Set dimension sizes. DIMLUNIT Dimension setting: the format of new dimensions only (1 scientific, 2 decimal, 3 engineering, 4 architectural, 5 fractional). DIMSTYLE and -DIMSTYLE Aliases: DST, DDIM, D. The Dimension style manager: Set current, New…, Modify…, Rename…, Delete, Compare…, Apply to selected. -DIMSTYLE on the command line: Dimension style [Restore/Save/Delete/Apply/Compare/reName/List]. See Set dimension sizes. DIMEDIT and DIMTEDIT DIMEDIT (DED): Dimension edit [Home/New/Rotate/Oblique] \u003CHome\u003E, then the dimensions (or those already selected). New asks for the text (\u003C\u003E = the measurement); Rotate for an angle (0 puts the text back); Oblique for the extension lines' angle from the X axis (Enter = none; linear and aligned dimensions only). DIMTEDIT (DIMTED): Dimension text [Left/Right/Center/Home/Angle] \u003CCenter\u003E. See Set dimension sizes. DIMTEXTUNIT Aliases: DTU. Units in the text of new dimensions [Drawing units/mm/cm/m/km], or with dimensions selected Units in the text of the selected dimensions. Writes the measured length in that unit with the unit after it (DIMLFAC and a DIMPOST suffix); D goes back to drawing units. See Dimensions in mm, cm, m or km. REVCLOUD Aliases: RC. Prints Arc length: … Type: …, then Specify first point [Arc length/Object/Rectangular/Polygonal/Freehand]. Rectangular asks Specify opposite corner; Polygonal Specify next point (Enter closes the cloud) [Undo]; Freehand follows the pointer until a click or Enter; O asks Select object and Reverse direction? [Yes/No] \u003CN\u003E and turns the object into the cloud. A asks Specify the length of each arc. R, P and F choose the type for the next cloud. The result is a closed polyline of arcs. MLEADER Aliases: MLD; or Leader. Specify leader arrowhead location [Arrowhead size/Landing distance], Specify leader landing location, then the text in the in-place box (or, without it, Enter text (an empty line ends)). Saved as a real multileader in DWG; DXF keeps the lines, arrowhead and text it draws. Parametric See Constrain a drawing and Use parameters and equations. Constraints work on the Model tab. GEOMCONSTRAINT and the GC commands Alias GCON: Enter constraint type [Horizontal/Vertical/Perpendicular/PArallel/Tangent/Coincident/CONcentric/COLlinear/Symmetric/Equal/Fix] \u003CHorizontal\u003E, then that command's prompts. GCHORIZONTAL, GCVERTICAL: Select an object [2Points] \u003C2Points\u003E. GCPARALLEL, GCPERPENDICULAR, GCCOLLINEAR, GCCONCENTRIC, GCEQUAL, GCTANGENT: Select first object, Select second object (the second one moves). GCCOINCIDENT: Select first point [Object/Autoconstrain] \u003CObject\u003E, Select second point [Object] \u003CObject\u003E. GCFIX: Select point [Object] \u003CObject\u003E. GCSYMMETRIC: Select first object [2Points] \u003C2Points\u003E, Select second object, Select symmetry line. DIMCONSTRAINT and the DC commands Alias DCON: Select associative dimension to convert [LInear/Horizontal/Vertical/ALigned/ANgular/Radial/Diameter/Convert] \u003CALigned\u003E. DCLINEAR, DCHORIZONTAL, DCVERTICAL: Specify first constraint point [Object] \u003CObject\u003E, Specify second constraint point, Specify dimension line location, Dimension text \u003Cd1=40\u003E (a number, an expression, or name=expression). DCALIGNED: as above, with [Object/Point & line/2Lines]. DCRADIUS, DCDIAMETER: Select arc or circle. DCANGULAR: Select first line or arc, Select second line. DCCONVERT: Select dimensions to convert. AUTOCONSTRAIN, DELCONSTRAINT, CONSTRAINTBAR, DCDISPLAY AUTOCONSTRAIN: Select objects [Settings]. S asks the distance and angle tolerances. DELCONSTRAINT (alias DELCON): Select objects; their constraints go. CONSTRAINTBAR (alias CBAR): Select objects [Showall/Hideall], then [Show/Hide]. DCDISPLAY: Enter an option [Show/Hide]. PARAMETERS and -PARAMETERS PARAMETERS (aliases PAR, PARAM) opens the Parameters panel. -PARAMETERS: Enter an option [New/Edit/Rename/Delete/?] \u003C?\u003E. SWBOUT Alias SWMACRO. Downloads a macro (.swb) that rebuilds the model as one constrained sketch in SolidWorks. See Move sketches to and from other part modelers. Blocks SCALELISTEDIT Aliases: SCALELIST. Scale list [Add/Edit/Delete/List/Reset] until Enter. Add: Scale name, Paper units, Drawing units. Edit and Delete ask for the scale's name. The drawing's own scales join the viewport and plot scale lists and are saved with the drawing. See Units, papers and scales. SPLINE, SPLINEDIT and PEDIT SPLINE (SPL): First point of the spline [Method/Object] (Fit points). Fit: points the curve passes through; Close, Undo, then Start tangent and End tangent (a point, or Enter). Method › Control vertices asks a degree (1 to 5) and draws from a control polygon. Object turns polylines into splines through their vertices. SPLINEDIT (SPE): [Fit data/Edit vertex/convert to Polyline/Open or Close/Reverse/Undo/eXit]; Fit data [Add/Delete/Move/Purge/Tangents/eXit]; Edit vertex [Add control point/Move/Weight/eXit]. PEDIT (PE): [Close/Open/Join/Width/Fit/Spline/Decurve/Ltype gen/Reverse/Undo/eXit], M for several objects; lines, arcs and splines are offered as polylines first. See Splines and polylines. BLOCK Aliases: B, BMAKE. Enter block name. If it exists: Block \"…\" already exists. Redefine it? [Yes/No] \u003CN\u003E. Specify insertion base point Select objects. They are replaced by one reference to the new block. WBLOCK Aliases: W, -WBLOCK. Writes a block, some objects or the whole drawing as a new DWG or DXF file. WBLOCK source [Block/Entire drawing/Objects] \u003CObjects\u003E: Objects: Specify insertion base point (it becomes the new drawing's origin), Select objects, then File format [DWG/DXF/R12 DXF] and File name for the new drawing, then What to do with the objects [Retain/Convert to block/Delete]. Convert replaces them with one reference to a block of the file's name; Delete removes them. Block: Block to write out. The block's own objects, with its base point as the origin, nested blocks included. Entire drawing: every object in model space. Sheets are not written; use SAVEAS for a copy that keeps them. The new file holds only the layers, linetypes, text and dimension styles and blocks that its objects use, and the drawing's units. The version of a DWG is the one SAVEAS last used. In the browser the file downloads; in Studio the save dialog opens. ATTDEF Aliases: ATT, DDATTDEF, -ATTDEF; or Blocks › More › Define attribute. Prints the modes, then Enter an option to change (Enter when done) [Invisible/Constant/Preset], Enter attribute tag name, Enter attribute prompt, Enter default attribute value (Enter attribute value when constant), Specify start point of text, Specify text height, Specify rotation angle of text. BLOCK takes attribute definitions into the block. EATTEDIT and ATTEDIT EATTEDIT (DDATTE, or double-click a reference with attributes; Blocks › More › Edit attributes): Select a block reference with attributes, then the Edit attributes dialog. ATTEDIT (ATE, -ATTEDIT): the same reference, then \u003Cprompt\u003E (\u003CTAG\u003E) \u003Cvalue\u003E for each attribute; Enter keeps a value. See Attributes. ATTSYNC Update the attributes of which block's references [Name/Select/All] \u003CSelect\u003E. Values kept; new attributes get their default, removed ones go, placements reset. BATTMAN Blocks › More › Attribute manager. The dialog: block, attributes (tag, prompt, default, modes, order), Update the references (values kept), Apply. REFEDIT and REFCLOSE Blocks › More › Edit in place. REFEDIT: Select the block reference to edit in place (or the selection). REFCLOSE: Save the changes to \"…\" [Save/Discard] \u003CSave\u003E; the bar's Save the block and Discard do the same. The whole edit is one undo step. See Attributes. BEDIT and BCLOSE BE; or Blocks › More › Edit block. Enter block name to edit \u003Cthe selected reference's block\u003E opens the block editor. BCLOSE asks Close the block editor on \"…\" [Save/Discard] \u003CSave\u003E; the banner's Save block and Discard changes do the same. Everything done in the editor is one undo step. VIEWBASE Alias: FLATSHOT. A flat 2D view of the solids on the 3D tab: View [Front/Top/Right/Left/BAck/BOttom/Iso], Hidden lines [Show/Hide], a scale (1:20, 2:1; Enter takes the one that fits the sheet), then the view's center. One block reference, visible edges on VIEW-VISIBLE, hidden ones dashed on VIEW-HIDDEN. See Flat views on a sheet. PUBLISH Aliases: -PUBLISH; or All sheets in one PDF in the Plot to PDF dialog. Sheets to plot [All/Some]: Enter plots every sheet, S asks for names separated by commas. One PDF, a page and a bookmark per sheet. See Every sheet in one PDF. SHEETSET Aliases: SSM, SHEETSETMANAGER. Brings you to the Sheet set section of the left panel: sheets from many drawings, numbered and titled, their title blocks filled from the set, published as one PDF. See Sheet sets. XATTACH, XREF XATTACH (XA): pick a DWG or DXF, then its insertion point. XREF (XR, -XREF): Linked drawings [?/Attach/Reload/Bind/Detach] (Enter: ?); with more than one link, pick one of its references or type N and its name. See Linked drawings. COUNT Aliases: BCOUNT; or Blocks › More › Count blocks. Select a block reference to count, or [All/Export] (Enter: All): a pick selects and counts every reference of that block in this space; Enter or A lists them all; E then C (counts) or A (attributes) downloads a CSV. See Count blocks and export a schedule. INSERT Aliases: I, DDINSERT. Enter block name or [File/Library] \u003Clast block\u003E (case does not matter). F asks for a DWG or DXF, then the whole drawing or one of its blocks; a name already used asks Keep existing or Rename. L opens the library panel. Specify insertion point [Scale/Rotate]. S asks Specify scale factor, R asks Specify rotation angle; both are remembered. Then Enter \u003Cprompt\u003E (\u003CTAG\u003E) \u003Cdefault\u003E for each attribute the block asks for. See Block attributes. LIBRARY Aliases: LIB. Opens the library panel: place items, check for updates, library credits. No questions. See Insert from a file or the library. STEELMEMBER Aliases: SMEMBER; or 3D member on the library's Standard tab. Section size \u003CW12x26\u003E (as the tab lists it), then Start point [Column] and End point: a solid member on the 3D tab between the two points, sitting on the ground. C asks for the column's base point and its height. One undo step. See Place steel sections and standard parts. PARTSLIST Aliases: PLIST. Top left corner of the parts list, then Text height \u003C2.5\u003E: draws a table of every standard part and steel section placed from the library (item numbers, quantity, part, standard, size, length, material, mass). One undo step. See Place steel sections and standard parts. PURGE Aliases: PU. Removes block definitions that nothing uses. No questions. DELBLOCKS Or Blocks › More › Delete blocks. Select block to delete [Name/eXit]: pick a block reference, or N for the list of blocks with a filter (choose several), or X / Enter to end. Every reference goes — in the model, on every sheet and inside other blocks — and then the definition, and the command line says what was deleted and what couldn't be. Unnamed blocks and linked drawings are not offered. The prompt comes back for the next block; the whole command is one undo step. Sheets and plotting LAYOUT Aliases: LO, NEWLAYOUT; or the + button under the drawing. A new sheet with a border, a title block and one viewport, on ISO A3 (or Tabloid for drawings in inches or feet). No questions. MVIEW Aliases: MV. On a sheet only (VPORTS on a sheet does the same). Specify corner of viewport, Specify opposite corner. The viewport shows the whole model at a standard scale. VPORTS Aliases: VIEWPORTS. SPLIT is the same from a sheet too, and View › More › Split side by side, Split stacked, Four panes, One pane run it. On the model: Enter number of panes [SIngle/2/3/4] \u003C3\u003E; 2 asks [Horizontal/Vertical] \u003CVertical\u003E (Vertical = side by side), 3 asks [Left/Right] \u003CRight\u003E (where the big pane goes). Each pane keeps its own view and space; a click makes a pane active. On a sheet it is MVIEW. See Split the screen. VPVIEW No aliases. On a sheet only. Select viewport (skipped if one is selected), then on the model: Specify first corner of the window to show, Specify opposite corner. UCS No aliases. A coordinate system of your own; not an edit. Specify origin of UCS [NAmed/OBject/Previous/World/Z/3point] \u003CWorld\u003E: a point is a new origin, then Specify point on X-axis (Enter keeps the angle). Z: Specify rotation angle about Z axis \u003C90\u003E. 3: origin, a point on the positive X axis, a point on the positive-Y side. OB: Select object to align UCS. NA: [Restore/Save/Delete/?] and a name. The view turns so the new X axis is level. See Turn the view and use your own coordinates. PLAN No aliases. Enter an option [Current ucs/Ucs/World] \u003CCurrent ucs\u003E: turns the active pane's view so that UCS's X axis (or the world's) is level. Nothing in the drawing moves. TURNLEFT, TURNRIGHT, TURNWORLD No aliases; or View › More › Turn view 90° left, Turn view 90° right, Back to world. The same as UCS Z -90, UCS Z 90 and UCS W, with the view following. VPLAYER No aliases. On a sheet, or inside a viewport. Enter an option [?/Freeze/Thaw/Reset/Vpvisdflt]: F, T, R ask for layer names (commas, * and ? wildcards), then [All/Select/Current] \u003CCurrent\u003E viewports; Reset puts each layer back to its \"frozen in new viewports\" default; V sets that default ([Frozen/Thawed]), which MVIEW uses. ? lists the layers frozen in the current viewport. The Layers panel's viewport column does the same for one viewport. See Freeze a layer in one viewport. MSPACE Aliases: MS; or double-click inside a viewport. On a sheet only. Work on the model through a viewport: the one selected, the only one on the sheet, or Click inside the viewport to work in. Pan and zoom inside change the viewport's view (a display-locked one keeps it and the sheet moves instead); the new view is written as one undo step. See Work on the model through a viewport. PSPACE Aliases: PS; or double-click outside the viewport, or click a tab under the drawing. Back to the sheet. PLOT Aliases: PRINT, PUBLISH, EXPORTPDF; or Ctrl+P. Opens the Plot to PDF dialog. See Plot to PDF. PLOTSTYLES Aliases: STYLESMANAGER, PSTABLES; or Edit tables… in the Plot to PDF dialog. Opens Plot style tables: what each color prints as (color, gray, ink, lineweight), kept with the drawing. See Plot style tables. Inquiry CAL Prints the value of a calculation, as it would be read anywhere a length is typed. Expression: type it, for example 12'6\"+3/4\" or (24'-0\")/3, and press Enter. CadCraft prints = 12'-6 3/4\" (150.75 drawing units). See Arithmetic in a typed value. DIST Aliases: DI; or View › More › Measure distance. Specify first point, Specify second point. Prints distance, angle, Delta X and Delta Y. AREA Aliases: AA; or View › More › Area. Prints the area and perimeter. Specify first corner point [Object/Add area/Subtract area] \u003CObject\u003E Click or type corners; at Specify next point (Enter closes) [Undo] press Enter to close the outline. U takes back the last corner. Enter or O, then Select object: a circle (prints its circumference), an ellipse, a polyline (an open one is closed by a straight line for the area, and its length is printed) or a hatch (holes are taken off). A (Add area) and S (Subtract area) keep a running total: the prompt starts with (ADD mode) or (SUBTRACT mode), each area is added or taken off, and Total area = … is printed after each. In these modes O picks objects until Enter, and Enter at the first prompt ends the command. MEASUREGEOM Aliases: MEA. Enter an option [Distance/Radius/Angle/ARea] \u003CDistance\u003E; after each measurement it asks again, with eXit the default. D: two points, as DIST. R: Select arc or circle; prints the radius and diameter. A: an arc (its included angle), a line and then a second line (the angle between them, toward their far ends), or Enter and three points (vertex, then the two ends). AR: as AREA, one area. ID No aliases. Specify point; prints its X and Y. LIST Aliases: LI, LS. Select objects; prints each object's type, layer and main measurements. View, file and other ZOOM Aliases: Z. Specify corner of window, enter a scale factor (nX) [Extents/All/Window/Previous/Scale/Center]. Click two corners for a window. E, A or Enter: the whole drawing. P (Previous, or Previous in the toolbar) goes back to the view before, up to 10 views per tab; a run of wheel steps or one pan counts as one view. A number such as 2, 2x or 0.5x (or S and then the number) zooms in or out by that factor round the middle of the screen. C asks Specify center point and Enter height or magnification (nX). PDFATTACH, PDFIMPORT and the PDF commands Aliases: PDFATT for PDFATTACH, PDFIM for PDFIMPORT. PDFATTACH puts a PDF page under the drawing (file, page, insertion point, scale, rotation); PDFIMPORT turns a page's lines, curves, fills and text into drawing objects on layers named after the PDF, at true size, a typed scale, or scaled from two points and a real distance. PDFCLIP clips an underlay, PDFADJUST sets fade, contrast, monochrome or switches it off, PDFLAYERS switches the PDF's own layers, and PDF gives CadCraft the files a drawing's underlays use again. See PDF underlays. AUTOCOMPLETE Alias: AUTOC. Drawing autocomplete: with no command running, a grey ghost shows what your drawing's own patterns say comes next. AUTOCOMPLETE turns it On or Off (also Settings › CadCraft) or Shows what it would suggest now. Tab draws the ghost as one undo step, Esc dismisses it until the drawing changes. It finds three things in the newest objects: the next copy of something drawn twice or more at an even step (studs, columns, a stair's riser and tread), the next steps of a polyline stair, and the missing side of a room or wall chain. In the middle of LINE or PLINE the ghost is the next point: the next step of a stair (riser, tread, riser…), the fourth corner of a rectangle, the point that closes it, or one more equal step. Bring the cursor near it and press Tab, as if you had clicked there; carry on or press Enter. Worked out on this computer by rules, with no model. AUTOSHEET Alias: ASH. Sheets that lay themselves out. AUTOSHEET takes the pieces of the drawing (your named views, or the separate pieces of the model, or the whole model), chooses a paper size (or the one you give), gives every piece a standard scale (one scale for all, or the largest each can take) and places the views in rows on new sheets with a title block and a caption (name and scale) under each. What does not fit on one sheet gets a sheet of its own. One undo step. Worked out on this computer by rules, with no model. PHOTOSKETCH Alias: PSK. Sketch photo to drawing. Choose a photo (JPEG, PNG or TIFF) of a hand sketch on a sheet of paper. PHOTOSKETCH finds the sheet's four corners, straightens the perspective, takes the paper size you name (Paper, or the nearest standard one) and the Scale of the sketch (1 : n), then traces the pen lines into lines, arcs and circles, a layer for each pen colour (black, red, blue, green). If it cannot find the paper's edge it takes the whole photo as the sheet and says so. Lettering is left out. One undo step. Done on this computer with classic image processing, no model, nothing sent anywhere. See also the scanned-drawing commands. AUTODIM Alias: ADIM. Auto-dimension by rules. AUTODIM dimensions the selected objects (or the whole drawing) from their level and plumb edges: a chain of dimensions along the sides you choose (Sides), one overall dimension outside it, the width of each opening (a gap between collinear wall lines), a diameter for each size of hole, and with Grid the spacing between grid lines. A dimension that is already there is not made again. One undo step. Worked out on this computer by rules, with no model. GRIDMOVE Alias: GM. Moving a grid line carries the set. GRIDMOVE moves a grid line (a line on a layer whose name says grid or axis) and what hangs on it: walls with an end on the line stretch to follow it, walls along it move whole, columns (block references and circles on the line), its bubble and tag move, and dimensions whose points sit on it measure the new spacing. Give two points (only the part across the line counts) or Distance. One undo step takes it all back. Done by rules on this computer. DETAILS Alias: DET. Details that find you. DETAILS keeps your firm's own details: Add puts a block of this drawing in the list, List and Remove manage it, and Find ranks them for what is selected (or for what you drew last) by the words on its layers, block names, hatch patterns and notes against each detail's name and notes, so a window head finds your sill flashing. Pick a number and a point to place it. With Hint on (also Settings › CadCraft), selecting something a detail matches puts one line in the status bar. The list is kept in this browser, on this computer, and that list is the one searched. Share puts it in your Documents folder too (.cadcraft/firm-details.json, with each block's drawing): Pull adds what your team has and you lack, Push adds what you have and they lack, Both does both, and nothing is ever removed from either side. Open the Documents folder first (DOCUMENTS); when that folder is a Team Cloud Plan folder, the whole team shares one list. A detail item in the Library panel has an Add to my firm's details button that puts it in the same list. Worked out on this computer by words, with no model. SKETCHFIX Alias: SKF. Pen sketches become precise geometry. Press and drag one freehand stroke at a time (pen, mouse or finger), then Enter. Each stroke is read as a line, circle, arc, rectangle or polyline: nearly level and plumb lines are made exactly level and plumb, a closed round stroke becomes a circle, a closed stroke with four right-angled corners a rectangle, and the ends of the strokes are pulled together and onto the ends already in the drawing. One undo step. Worked out on this computer by rules, with no model. SCANATTACH and the scanned-drawing commands Aliases: SCA SCANATTACH, SCS SCANSCALE, SCL SCANALIGN, SCD SCANDESKEW, SCC SCANCLEAN, SCR SCANCROP, SCE SCANERASE, SCT SCANTRACE, SCI SCANINFO; RASTERSNAP has none. They work on a picture, one undo step each. SCANATTACH inserts a PNG, JPEG, TIFF (one page or all) or a PDF page that is a picture. SCANSCALE scales the sheet from two picked points and the true distance. SCANALIGN moves and turns it onto two points (Scale the sheet to fit Yes/No). SCANDESKEW shows the angle of the sheet's long lines and turns the sheet once you Accept or give a Value. SCANCLEAN makes it black and white (Threshold Auto/Adaptive/Level, Specks) and Run. SCANCROP keeps a window. SCANERASE erases a Rectangle, Polygon or Lasso. SCANTRACE traces lines and arcs onto a new layer (Tolerance, Window, Arcs, Leftovers, Run) and reports what it found and what it left as raster. SCANINFO lists the original file and the steps. RASTERSNAP turns snapping to the lines of scanned sheets On or Off. See the topic Scanned drawings and the trace. REGEN and REGENALL Aliases: RE for REGEN, REA for REGENALL. Rebuild what is drawn on screen from the drawing: REGEN the tab you are looking at (on a sheet, the model its viewports show too), REGENALL every tab, every split-screen pane and every open drawing. Use them when the picture looks stale after a scripted or very large change, or an assistant edited the drawing; the drawing itself is not touched, so there is nothing to undo. NAMEDVIEW Aliases: NV, VIEWS. Enter an option [?/Restore/Save/Window/Delete] \u003CRestore\u003E, then the view's name. S saves the view on screen, W a window you pick, R shows a saved view, D deletes one, ? lists the views of this tab. Saving under an existing name replaces it; each change is one undo step. Views belong to the tab they were saved on. DWG and DXF files keep them (the VIEW table); a sheet's views come back on the first sheet, since the file does not say which. UNDO and REDO Aliases: U for UNDO; REDO has none. Also Ctrl+Z and Ctrl+Y. LAYER Aliases: LA. Jumps to the Layers panel. LINETYPE, LWEIGHT, LTSCALE LINETYPE (LT): Enter linetype for new objects — ByLayer, ByBlock or a name (? lists them: Continuous, the drawing's own, and DASHED, HIDDEN, CENTER, PHANTOM, DOT, DASHDOT, BORDER, DIVIDE with their 2 (half) and X2 (double) sizes). LWEIGHT (LW): the lineweight for new objects in mm, or ByLayer, ByBlock, Default. LTSCALE (LTS): the drawing's linetype scale (undoable). LWDISPLAY (LWT): Show lineweights [ON/OFF] — the same as the LWT button in the status bar. See Line weights and linetypes. RENAME, LAYMRG, LAYDEL RENAME (-RENAME, REN): Enter object type to rename [LAyer/Block], Enter old name, then the new name; objects or references follow. LAYMRG: Select object on layer to merge or [Name] until Enter, then Select object on target layer or [Name] and Y. LAYDEL: Select object on layer to delete or [Name], then Y: the layer and every object on it go (not layer 0, DEFPOINTS or the current layer). One undo step each. See Organize a drawing with layers. LAYISO, LAYUNISO, LAYOFF, LAYFRZ, LAYMCUR, LAYON, LAYTHW Layers by picking objects; also in the Layers panel's Tools menu. LAYISO: Select objects on the layer(s) to be isolated, then Enter: every other layer goes off. LAYUNISO turns them back on. LAYOFF and LAYFRZ: Select an object on the layer to be turned off (or frozen), over and over; U takes back the last one, Enter ends. LAYMCUR: Select object whose layer will become current. LAYON turns every layer on, LAYTHW thaws every layer. Each command is one undo step. See Organize a drawing with layers. NEW, OPEN, SAVE, SAVEAS, DXFOUT NEW starts an empty drawing and OPEN opens a DWG or DXF, each in its own tab over the drawing (an untouched new drawing is used instead of a new tab). Closing a tab with unsaved changes asks first. See Split the screen. SAVE (alias QSAVE, also Ctrl+S) downloads a copy in the drawing's format: DWG if it was opened from a DWG, DXF if from a DXF. A new drawing uses the format last chosen in Save as in this browser, else DWG. SAVEAS (also Ctrl+Shift+S) opens the Save a copy as dialog: choose DWG (and its Version), DXF R12 or DXF 2000, then Save. The choice sticks for this drawing and for new drawings in this browser. DXFOUT always downloads a DXF R12 copy, without changing the format SAVE uses. See Files: what opens, what saves. TOUR Aliases: TUTORIAL. Starts the six-step first-drawing tour again. See Getting started. TOOLBARDOCK Aliases: TBDOCK. Toolbar position [Top/Bottom/Left/Right]. See Settings. POLARANG Enter polar angle step in degrees \u003C90\u003E: the step Polar tracking lines up at (more than 0, up to 180). Kept in this browser. UNITS Aliases: UN. How lengths are written and typed. Enter length format [Scientific/Decimal/Engineering/Architectural/Fractional] \u003Ccurrent\u003E Architectural and Fractional: Enter denominator of smallest fraction to display (1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 128 or 256). The others: Enter number of decimal places (0 to 8). New dimensions follow it. One undo step. Same as Length format and Precision in Current settings; see Feet and inches. GDSIN Aliases: GDS. View an IC layout (a GDSII stream file): lab spike, for viewing and measuring only. A file picker takes a .gds file; with several top cells, Enter the top cell to draw. Every layer and datatype becomes a drawing layer (GDS 1, GDS 2/5), references and arrays are placed flat, in the file's user unit (usually micrometres). One undo step. Nothing is simulated or checked and the file is not written back. DIAGRAM Aliases: TEXTDIAGRAM, T2D. A diagram from text. A box takes the text (or one line with ; between statements at the command line): a Mermaid flowchart, a PlantUML component diagram, D2 or a from-to list. Specify the top left corner of the diagram, then Specify the text height. Blocks and connectors are ordinary objects on layer Diagram, selected when it ends. One undo step. See Draw a diagram from text. OLELIST Aliases: OLES. The OLE objects a DWG carries that CadCraft cannot place by itself. Lists each one: number, type, size, whether it saved a picture. Enter an option [Place/eXit] \u003CeXit\u003E: Place asks for the number, the lower left corner and the width, and draws a frame with the picture. One undo step; the kept object is not changed. See Pictures. SHXFONTS Aliases: SHX. Your own SHX fonts. Enter an SHX font option [Folder/Files/List/Remove/Reverse/Clear] \u003CList\u003E Folder reads every .shx in a folder (CadCraft Studio; again at each start), Files takes picked files (the browser), Reverse lays out one font's letters last first. Text styles that name a loaded font draw in it. See Draw text in your own SHX fonts. LIMITS How big the drawing is meant to be. Specify lower left corner or [ON/OFF] \u003Ccurrent\u003E: a point, ON or OFF. Specify upper right corner \u003Ccurrent\u003E. The grid fills the limits, ZOOM All shows them, and with them ON a point outside is refused (**Outside limits). One undo step; saved in DWG and DXF. See Set the drawing limits. AUNITS Aliases: ANGUNITS. How angles are shown and typed. Enter angle format [Decimal degrees/Degrees-minutes-seconds/Surveyor's bearings] \u003Ccurrent\u003E Enter number of decimal places (0 to 8), or for the other two Enter precision (0 degrees, 2 minutes, 4 seconds, 5 to 8 decimals of a second). One undo step; saved in DWG and DXF. Same as Angle format in Current settings. See Type and show bearings and angles. APPLOAD Aliases: AP; or View › More › Load application…. The Load applications dialog: load .lsp routines and .dcl dialog files, unload them, load them again from the history, and the Startup Suite. A routine's (defun c:NAME …) commands then work like any other. See Load applications and Run LISP routines. ALIASEDIT, REINIT ALIASEDIT (or View › More › Edit aliases…) opens your aliases file (ALIAS, *COMMAND lines); Save applies it. REINIT applies it again. See Edit command aliases. HELP Aliases: ?. Prints every command, its aliases and a one-line description in the command line history. It does not open this help: press F1 or click Help for that. Select objects prompt At any Select objects prompt: click objects, draw windows (left to right) and crossings (right to left) with two clicks, or press and drag a lasso (Space while dragging: window, crossing, fence), Shift+click to remove, and press Enter to finish. You can also type ALL (everything), L (the last object), P (Previous: what the last command used), F (Fence: objects a line of points crosses; U takes back a point), W (Window), C (Crossing), R (Remove mode: picks take objects out; the prompt reads Remove objects) and A (Add again). ? lists them. They are not shown in the prompt. Tab over overlapping objects highlights the next one; click to pick it. See Select objects. ACCESS Alias: AX. Draft without sight. ACCESS turns Access on or off (On, Off) and switches snap sounds (Sound) and voice commands (Voice); it says the state first. With Access on, every command and result is spoken through your screen reader and the drawing area has a keyboard cursor. The help topic \"Draft without sight\" has the keys. DESCRIBE Alias: DESC. Say what the drawing holds: how many objects, of what kind, how big, how many layers, what is selected. Reads only; changes nothing. WHEREAMI Alias: WHERE. Say where the cursor is, in the drawing's units. NEARBY Say what lies close to the cursor, nearest first, each with its distance and the side it is on. SAYSEL Say what is selected, one sentence per object (line length, ends and angle; circle radius and centre; text as written). VOICE Turn spoken commands on or off. They are recognised on this computer; where the browser cannot do that, voice stays off unless you choose the browser's online service in Settings.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"coordinate-entry","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Coordinate and distance entry","summary":"Every way to type a point, a distance, an angle or a number at the CadCraft command line, with examples.","section":"reference","order":30,"keywords":["dynamic input","dyn","f12","tab","coordinates","absolute","relative","polar","@","direct distance","angle","degrees","last point","lastpoint","typing points","input","arithmetic","calculation","calculator","cal","plus","fraction","expression"],"headings":[{"id":"points","text":"Points","level":2},{"id":"the-last-point","text":"The last point","level":2},{"id":"direct-distance-entry","text":"Direct distance entry","level":2},{"id":"angles","text":"Angles","level":2},{"id":"distances","text":"Distances","level":2},{"id":"arithmetic-in-a-typed-value","text":"Arithmetic in a typed value","level":2},{"id":"dynamic-input","text":"Dynamic input","level":2},{"id":"options-and-defaults","text":"Options and defaults","level":2},{"id":"the-coordinate-display","text":"The coordinate display","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"points\"\u003EPoints\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen the prompt asks for a point, type one of these and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EYou type\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeaning\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EExample\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Ex,y\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAbsolute: measured from the origin 0,0\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E2500,1200\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E#x,y\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAlso absolute (accepted for habit)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E#2500,1200\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E@dx,dy\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERelative: across and up from the last point\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E@600,0\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E@d&lt;a\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERelative polar: distance and angle from the last point\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E@1500&lt;45\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Ed&lt;a\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAbsolute polar: distance and angle from the origin\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E3000&lt;90\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ea single number\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDirect distance: that far from the base point, toward the crosshair\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E1200\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E*x,y\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E@*dx,dy\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWorld coordinates while a UCS is set\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E*0,0\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith a UCS set (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EUCS\u003C/code\u003E), \u003Ccode\u003Ex,y\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E@dx,dy\u003C/code\u003E and the angle in \u003Ccode\u003Ed&lt;a\u003C/code\u003E are measured along the UCS axes. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/ucs-and-turned-view\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/ucs-and-turned-view\"\u003ETurn the view and use your own coordinates\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOr click in the drawing; object snaps and Ortho apply to clicks.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERules:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA comma separates X from Y. Decimals use a period: \u003Ccode\u003E1250.75,300\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENegative values go left and down: \u003Ccode\u003E@-600,0\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDo not type spaces: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E sends the line, like \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENumbers may use exponents: \u003Ccode\u003E1e3\u003C/code\u003E is 1000.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENo units are typed, and no feet-and-inch notation. Type plain numbers in drawing units.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-last-point\"\u003EThe last point\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E@\u003C/code\u003E counts from the \u003Cstrong\u003Elast point you gave at any point prompt\u003C/strong\u003E, in any command: a click, a snap or a typed point. It is remembered between commands, so you can start a new line \u003Ccode\u003E@0,500\u003C/code\u003E away from where the last one ended. Before you have given any point, \u003Ccode\u003E@\u003C/code\u003E counts from 0,0.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESome places where this matters:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECOPY\u003C/code\u003E, after the first copy the last point is that copy&#039;s point. \u003Ccode\u003E@600,0\u003C/code\u003E twice places copies 600 and 1200 from the original.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAfter you place a dimension, the last point is where you placed the dimension line, not the dimension&#039;s end.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen in doubt, use an absolute point, a snap, or a direct distance.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"direct-distance-entry\"\u003EDirect distance entry\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA bare number is a distance from the command&#039;s \u003Cstrong\u003Ebase point\u003C/strong\u003E (the point the rubber band starts from) toward the crosshair:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the crosshair in the direction you want.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the distance and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrtho\u003C/span\u003E on, the direction snaps to straight across or straight up and down, which makes this the fastest way to draw square walls. If there is no base point, or the crosshair sits exactly on it, the number is refused with &quot;Point or option keyword required.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"angles\"\u003EAngles\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn degrees, as a plain number: \u003Ccode\u003E30\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E-45\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E112.5\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMeasured counterclockwise from the positive X direction (pointing right): 0 is right, 90 up, 180 left, 270 or -90 down.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhere an angle is asked for (rotation, text angle), you can also click: the angle is measured from the base point to the click. Before a base point exists, the first click sets it and the prompt asks for a second point.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"distances\"\u003EDistances\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhere a distance is asked for (radius, offset, text height, scale factor):\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Etype a positive number (in a feet-and-inch drawing also \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E6-1/2\u003C/code\u003E: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/units-papers-scales#feet-and-inches-and-fractions\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/units-papers-scales\" data-hash=\"feet-and-inches-and-fractions\"\u003EFeet and inches\u003C/a\u003E), or\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eclick a point: the distance from the command&#039;s base point to it, or\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eclick two points when there is no base point: the prompt changes to \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify second point\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E&quot;Requires a positive number or two points.&quot; means the value was not a number, or was negative.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"arithmetic-in-a-typed-value\"\u003EArithmetic in a typed value\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAnywhere a length, a coordinate, an angle or a number is typed (the command line, dynamic input, Properties boxes and dialogs), you can type a calculation with \u003Ccode\u003E+\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E-\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E*\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E/\u003C/code\u003E and brackets instead of working it out first:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EType\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EGives\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E4+7/16\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E4.4375\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;6&quot;+3/4&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E12&#039;-6 3/4&quot; (in a feet-and-inch drawing)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E(24&#039;-0&quot;)/3\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E8&#039;-0&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E2*450mm\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E900 mm, in the drawing&#039;s units (when its units are set)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E@4+7/16,0\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ea point 4.4375 to the right of the last one\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E360/7\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ean angle of 51.43 degrees\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA dash written into a length is still part of it: \u003Ccode\u003E4-7/16\u003C/code\u003E is 4 7/16 and \u003Ccode\u003E24&#039;-6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E is 24 feet 6 inches. To subtract after a foot mark, finish the length first, \u003Ccode\u003E24&#039;-0&quot;-6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, or put the calculation in brackets with spaces round the dash, \u003Ccode\u003E(24&#039; - 6&quot;)\u003C/code\u003E (inside brackets a space is a space; elsewhere it presses \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA plain number added to a length is in drawing units; multiplying or dividing by one scales the length. Two lengths multiplied make an area, so \u003Ccode\u003E2&#039;*3&#039;\u003C/code\u003E is refused.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eabs\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eround\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eceil\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003Efloor\u003C/code\u003E work too: \u003Ccode\u003Eround(10/3)\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA calculation that cannot be read is refused with where it went wrong, for example &quot;The calculation ends too soon (character 3): a value is missing.&quot; Nothing is ever read as 0. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECAL\u003C/code\u003E prints a calculation&#039;s value.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"dynamic-input\"\u003EDynamic input\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDyn\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F12\"\u003EF12\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF12\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, in the status bar) puts the values by the crosshair while a command asks for a point.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith no base point yet (a line&#039;s first point), the two fields are \u003Cstrong\u003EX\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EY\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAfter that, they are the \u003Cstrong\u003Edistance\u003C/strong\u003E and the \u003Cstrong\u003Eangle\u003C/strong\u003E from the last point.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETyping still goes to the command line; the field by the crosshair shows it, and the rubber band follows it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType a value: it goes into the highlighted field.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (or a comma after X). The value is locked (shown bold) and the next field is highlighted. A locked distance keeps the rubber band that long while you aim with the mouse.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the second value, or just aim, and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003E50\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E90\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E draws 50 straight up from the last point.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPressing \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E after one value without \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E works as direct distance entry. Feet and inches work in the fields too (\u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E). Distance and angle prompts (a circle&#039;s radius, a rotation) show their value by the crosshair as well.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"options-and-defaults\"\u003EOptions and defaults\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOptions appear in square brackets. Type the capital letters of an option (\u003Ccode\u003ED\u003C/code\u003E for \u003Ccode\u003E[Diameter]\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ECL\u003C/code\u003E for \u003Ccode\u003ECLose\u003C/code\u003E) and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Longer beginnings of the word also work (\u003Ccode\u003EDIA\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA default appears in angle brackets, such as \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;500&gt;\u003C/code\u003E. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or right-click to use it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-coordinate-display\"\u003EThe coordinate display\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe status bar shows the crosshair&#039;s X and Y (in the UCS when one is set), after snaps and Ortho. While a command has a base point it adds \u003Ccode\u003EΔ\u003C/code\u003E with the distance and angle from that point, for example \u003Ccode\u003EΔ 1500 &lt; 30°\u003C/code\u003E: the numbers you would type as \u003Ccode\u003E@1500&lt;30\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUse \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EID\u003C/code\u003E to print a point&#039;s coordinates and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIST\u003C/code\u003E to measure between two points.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Points When the prompt asks for a point, type one of these and press Enter: You type Meaning Example x,y Absolute: measured from the origin 0,0 2500,1200 #x,y Also absolute (accepted for habit) #2500,1200 @dx,dy Relative: across and up from the last point @600,0 @d\u003Ca Relative polar: distance and angle from the last point @1500\u003C45 d\u003Ca Absolute polar: distance and angle from the origin 3000\u003C90 a single number Direct distance: that far from the base point, toward the crosshair 1200 *x,y, @*dx,dy World coordinates while a UCS is set *0,0 With a UCS set (UCS), x,y, @dx,dy and the angle in d\u003Ca are measured along the UCS axes. See Turn the view and use your own coordinates. Or click in the drawing; object snaps and Ortho apply to clicks. Rules: A comma separates X from Y. Decimals use a period: 1250.75,300. Negative values go left and down: @-600,0. Do not type spaces: Space sends the line, like Enter. Numbers may use exponents: 1e3 is 1000. No units are typed, and no feet-and-inch notation. Type plain numbers in drawing units. The last point @ counts from the last point you gave at any point prompt, in any command: a click, a snap or a typed point. It is remembered between commands, so you can start a new line @0,500 away from where the last one ended. Before you have given any point, @ counts from 0,0. Some places where this matters: In COPY, after the first copy the last point is that copy's point. @600,0 twice places copies 600 and 1200 from the original. After you place a dimension, the last point is where you placed the dimension line, not the dimension's end. When in doubt, use an absolute point, a snap, or a direct distance. Direct distance entry A bare number is a distance from the command's base point (the point the rubber band starts from) toward the crosshair: Move the crosshair in the direction you want. Type the distance and press Enter. With Ortho on, the direction snaps to straight across or straight up and down, which makes this the fastest way to draw square walls. If there is no base point, or the crosshair sits exactly on it, the number is refused with \"Point or option keyword required.\" Angles In degrees, as a plain number: 30, -45, 112.5. Measured counterclockwise from the positive X direction (pointing right): 0 is right, 90 up, 180 left, 270 or -90 down. Where an angle is asked for (rotation, text angle), you can also click: the angle is measured from the base point to the click. Before a base point exists, the first click sets it and the prompt asks for a second point. Distances Where a distance is asked for (radius, offset, text height, scale factor): type a positive number (in a feet-and-inch drawing also 12'6\" or 6-1/2: see Feet and inches), or click a point: the distance from the command's base point to it, or click two points when there is no base point: the prompt changes to Specify second point. \"Requires a positive number or two points.\" means the value was not a number, or was negative. Arithmetic in a typed value Anywhere a length, a coordinate, an angle or a number is typed (the command line, dynamic input, Properties boxes and dialogs), you can type a calculation with +, -, *, / and brackets instead of working it out first: Type Gives 4+7/16 4.4375 12'6\"+3/4\" 12'-6 3/4\" (in a feet-and-inch drawing) (24'-0\")/3 8'-0\" 2*450mm 900 mm, in the drawing's units (when its units are set) @4+7/16,0 a point 4.4375 to the right of the last one 360/7 an angle of 51.43 degrees A dash written into a length is still part of it: 4-7/16 is 4 7/16 and 24'-6\" is 24 feet 6 inches. To subtract after a foot mark, finish the length first, 24'-0\"-6\", or put the calculation in brackets with spaces round the dash, (24' - 6\") (inside brackets a space is a space; elsewhere it presses Enter). A plain number added to a length is in drawing units; multiplying or dividing by one scales the length. Two lengths multiplied make an area, so 2'*3' is refused. abs, round, ceil and floor work too: round(10/3). A calculation that cannot be read is refused with where it went wrong, for example \"The calculation ends too soon (character 3): a value is missing.\" Nothing is ever read as 0. CAL prints a calculation's value. Dynamic input Dyn (F12, in the status bar) puts the values by the crosshair while a command asks for a point. With no base point yet (a line's first point), the two fields are X and Y. After that, they are the distance and the angle from the last point. Typing still goes to the command line; the field by the crosshair shows it, and the rubber band follows it. Type a value: it goes into the highlighted field. Press Tab (or a comma after X). The value is locked (shown bold) and the next field is highlighted. A locked distance keeps the rubber band that long while you aim with the mouse. Type the second value, or just aim, and press Enter. 50, Tab, 90, Enter draws 50 straight up from the last point. Pressing Enter after one value without Tab works as direct distance entry. Feet and inches work in the fields too (12'6\"). Distance and angle prompts (a circle's radius, a rotation) show their value by the crosshair as well. Options and defaults Options appear in square brackets. Type the capital letters of an option (D for [Diameter], CL for CLose) and press Enter. Longer beginnings of the word also work (DIA). A default appears in angle brackets, such as \u003C500\u003E. Press Enter or right-click to use it. The coordinate display The status bar shows the crosshair's X and Y (in the UCS when one is set), after snaps and Ortho. While a command has a base point it adds Δ with the distance and angle from that point, for example Δ 1500 \u003C 30°: the numbers you would type as @1500\u003C30. Use ID to print a point's coordinates and DIST to measure between two points.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"units-papers-scales","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Units, paper sizes and scales","summary":"What drawing units mean in CadCraft, the paper sizes for sheets and plots, every standard scale, and how text and dimension sizes relate to the plot scale.","section":"reference","order":40,"keywords":["units","millimeters","inches","feet","feet and inches","architectural","engineering","fractional","fractions","lunits","length format","precision","meters","paper size","a3","a1","arch d","ansi","tabloid","letter","scale","1:100","1/4 inch","architectural scale","engineering scale","insunits"],"headings":[{"id":"drawing-units","text":"Drawing units","level":2},{"id":"feet-and-inches-and-fractions","text":"Feet and inches, and fractions","level":2},{"id":"paper-sizes","text":"Paper sizes","level":2},{"id":"scales","text":"Scales","level":2},{"id":"sizes-on-paper-and-in-the-model","text":"Sizes on paper and in the model","level":2},{"id":"line-weights-and-linetypes","text":"Line weights and linetypes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"drawing-units\"\u003EDrawing units\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou draw at full size, in drawing units. What one unit stands for is the drawing&#039;s \u003Cstrong\u003EUnits\u003C/strong\u003E setting:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E so nothing is selected, on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECurrent settings\u003C/span\u003E, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnits\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnitless\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMillimetres\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECentimetres\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMetres\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInches\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFeet\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDimensions can write their lengths in millimetres, centimetres, metres or kilometres whatever the drawing unit: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/dimension-settings#dimensions-in-mm-cm-m-or-km\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/dimension-settings\" data-hash=\"dimensions-in-mm-cm-m-or-km\"\u003EDimensions in mm, cm, m or km\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChanging the units resizes nothing. It changes how scales are read on sheets and in plots, and which scales are offered. A drawing opened from a DWG or DXF keeps the units stored in the file; the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrawing\u003C/span\u003E section of the left panel shows them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnitless\u003C/span\u003E drawing is treated as being in the paper&#039;s unit: millimeters on ISO paper, inches on US paper.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"feet-and-inches-and-fractions\"\u003EFeet and inches, and fractions\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow lengths are \u003Cstrong\u003Ewritten and typed\u003C/strong\u003E is a separate setting, the length format. It changes no geometry, only how numbers look and what you can type.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E so nothing is selected, on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECurrent settings\u003C/span\u003E, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength format\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDecimal\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003E150.5\u003C/code\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArchitectural\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;-6 1/2&quot;\u003C/code\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEngineering\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;-6.5&quot;\u003C/code\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFractional\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003E150 1/2\u003C/code\u003E) or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScientific\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003E1.505E+02\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPrecision\u003C/span\u003E: decimal places, or the smallest fraction (1/16 is the usual choice for architectural).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOr type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EUNITS\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EUN\u003C/code\u003E) and answer its two questions.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArchitectural\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEngineering\u003C/span\u003E, one drawing unit is one inch, as in AutoCAD: set \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnits\u003C/span\u003E to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInches\u003C/span\u003E too. You can then type lengths in feet and inches anywhere a length is asked for, and in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E boxes:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EYou type\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeans\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;-6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E12 feet 6 inches (150)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;6-1/2&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E12 feet 6½ inches. Use a hyphen before the fraction: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E is \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E at the command line.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E12 feet\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E6\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E6 inches: a plain number is inches\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E1/2\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E6-1/2\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Efractions work in any format\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPoints take the same lengths: \u003Ccode\u003E@12&#039;6&quot;,0\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E@10&#039;&lt;90\u003C/code\u003E. Angles stay in degrees.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe format shows in the history (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIST\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EID\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELIST\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EAREA\u003C/code\u003E: areas as square inches and square feet), in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E, in the coordinates on the status bar, and in \u003Cstrong\u003Enew dimensions\u003C/strong\u003E. Sheets keep their paper units (millimeters or inches) as decimals.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe length format is saved in DWG and DXF files, and each dimension keeps its own: a saved drawing opens again as you left it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"paper-sizes\"\u003EPaper sizes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESheets and model plots use these sizes, in either orientation:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EPaper\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESize\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EUnit on the sheet\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EISO A4\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E297 × 210 mm\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Emillimeters\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EISO A3\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E420 × 297 mm\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Emillimeters\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EISO A2\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E594 × 420 mm\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Emillimeters\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EISO A1\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E841 × 594 mm\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Emillimeters\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EISO A0\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E1189 × 841 mm\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Emillimeters\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELetter\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E11 × 8.5 in\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Einches\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELegal\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E14 × 8.5 in\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Einches\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETabloid / ANSI B\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E17 × 11 in\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Einches\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EANSI C\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E22 × 17 in\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Einches\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EANSI D\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E34 × 22 in\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Einches\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EARCH C\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E24 × 18 in\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Einches\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EARCH D\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E36 × 24 in\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Einches\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EARCH E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E48 × 36 in\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Einches\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENew sheets start on ISO A3 for metric and unitless drawings, and on Tabloid for drawings in inches or feet. Model plots start on ISO A4 or Letter the same way, then remember your last choice. Sheets from opened files keep their own size, shown as &quot;Custom&quot; when it is not one of these.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"scales\"\u003EScales\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA scale \u003Ccode\u003E1:n\u003C/code\u003E means one unit on paper shows n of the same unit in the model, so 1:100 shows 1 m of building as 10 mm of paper. CadCraft works this out from the drawing units and the paper&#039;s unit.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMetric scales\u003C/strong\u003E, offered for every drawing: 10:1, 5:1, 2:1, 1:1, 1:2, 1:5, 1:10, 1:20, 1:25, 1:50, 1:100, 1:200, 1:250, 1:500, 1:1000, 1:1250, 1:2000, 1:2500, 1:5000, 1:10000.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EArchitectural scales\u003C/strong\u003E, offered when the units are inches or feet: 3&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;, 1-1/2&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;, 1&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;, 3/4&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;, 1/2&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;, 3/8&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;, 1/4&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;, 3/16&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;, 1/8&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;, 3/32&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;, 1/16&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEngineering scales\u003C/strong\u003E, offered when the units are inches or feet: 1&quot; = 10&#039;, 1&quot; = 20&#039;, 1&quot; = 30&#039;, 1&quot; = 40&#039;, 1&quot; = 50&#039;, 1&quot; = 100&#039;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EYour own scales.\u003C/strong\u003E Type \u003Ccode\u003ESCALELISTEDIT\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003ESCALELIST\u003C/code\u003E) to add a scale of your own, such as 1:75 or a site scale of 2 paper : 300 drawing. \u003Ccode\u003EAdd\u003C/code\u003E asks for a name, then the paper units and the drawing units (a name like \u003Ccode\u003E1:75\u003C/code\u003E offers its own numbers; press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to take them). \u003Ccode\u003EEdit\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDelete\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EList\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EReset\u003C/code\u003E work on the same list; \u003Ccode\u003EReset\u003C/code\u003E goes back to the standard list. Your scales appear after the standard ones in the viewport scale list of Properties and in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlot to PDF\u003C/span\u003E dialog, a viewport at that scale reads with its name, and they are saved in the drawing (DWG and DXF 2000) as the drawing&#039;s scale list. A drawing opened from another program brings its list in; scales it has that match a standard one are not shown twice.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen a viewport is aimed with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow the whole model\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPick a window on the model…\u003C/span\u003E, CadCraft picks the largest standard scale that fits. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit the whole model\u003C/span\u003E sets an exact scale instead, which reads as a plain ratio.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"sizes-on-paper-and-in-the-model\"\u003ESizes on paper and in the model\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EText and dimension sizes are in the units of the space you draw in.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EYou want on paper\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDrawn on a sheet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDrawn in the model at 1:50 (mm drawing)\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDrawn in the model at 1/4&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot; (inch drawing)\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2.5 mm text\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2.5\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E125\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E—\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E3/32&quot; text\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0.09375\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E—\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E4.5\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor dimensions, keep the sizes as paper sizes and set the overall scale (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMSCALE\u003C/code\u003E) to the plot scale: 50 for 1:50, 48 for 1/4&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/dimension-settings\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/dimension-settings\"\u003ESet dimension sizes\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"line-weights-and-linetypes\"\u003ELine weights and linetypes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA layer, or an object, can have a \u003Cstrong\u003Elineweight\u003C/strong\u003E (0.05 to 2.11 mm) and a \u003Cstrong\u003Elinetype\u003C/strong\u003E (dashed, hidden, center, phantom, dot…). Set them in the right panel: with nothing selected, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinetype\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELineweight\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECurrent settings\u003C/span\u003E are what new objects get (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBy layer\u003C/span\u003E to follow their layer), and the two under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayer …\u003C/span\u003E are the current layer&#039;s own. With objects selected, the same two rows and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinetype scale\u003C/span\u003E change those objects. The commands are \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELINETYPE\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003ELT\u003C/code\u003E), \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELWEIGHT\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003ELW\u003C/code\u003E) and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELTSCALE\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003ELTS\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELinetypes show on screen and in plots. Their dashes are in drawing units: a metric drawing uses the ISO sizes (DASHED: 12.7 mm dashes), an inch drawing the inch ones (0.5&quot;). If the dashes look too small or too large, change the drawing&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinetype scale\u003C/span\u003E (LTSCALE): every dash and gap is multiplied by it; an object&#039;s own scale multiplies it again. A pattern too fine to see at all is drawn as a solid line.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELineweights print in plots, in mm on the paper whatever the scale. Lines with the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDefault\u003C/span\u003E lineweight use the pen width chosen in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlot to PDF\u003C/span\u003E dialog (0.13 to 0.70 mm; 0.25 mm to start with); a lineweight of 0 prints as the thinnest line. On screen every line is one pixel wide until you press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELWT\u003C/span\u003E in the status bar (or type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELWDISPLAY\u003C/code\u003E): then a lineweight above 0.25 mm draws wider (0.5 mm = 2 pixels) in the model, and at its true width on the paper on a sheet. The choice is kept in this browser.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Drawing units You draw at full size, in drawing units. What one unit stands for is the drawing's Units setting: Press Esc so nothing is selected, on the Model tab. In Current settings, choose Units: Unitless, Millimetres, Centimetres, Metres, Inches or Feet. Dimensions can write their lengths in millimetres, centimetres, metres or kilometres whatever the drawing unit: see Dimensions in mm, cm, m or km. Changing the units resizes nothing. It changes how scales are read on sheets and in plots, and which scales are offered. A drawing opened from a DWG or DXF keeps the units stored in the file; the Drawing section of the left panel shows them. A Unitless drawing is treated as being in the paper's unit: millimeters on ISO paper, inches on US paper. Feet and inches, and fractions How lengths are written and typed is a separate setting, the length format. It changes no geometry, only how numbers look and what you can type. Press Esc so nothing is selected, on the Model tab. In Current settings, choose Length format: Decimal (150.5), Architectural (12'-6 1/2\"), Engineering (12'-6.5\"), Fractional (150 1/2) or Scientific (1.505E+02). Choose Precision: decimal places, or the smallest fraction (1/16 is the usual choice for architectural). Or type UNITS (UN) and answer its two questions. With Architectural or Engineering, one drawing unit is one inch, as in AutoCAD: set Units to Inches too. You can then type lengths in feet and inches anywhere a length is asked for, and in the Properties boxes: You type Means 12'6\" or 12'-6\" 12 feet 6 inches (150) 12'6-1/2\" 12 feet 6½ inches. Use a hyphen before the fraction: Space is Enter at the command line. 12' 12 feet 6\" or 6 6 inches: a plain number is inches 1/2, 6-1/2 fractions work in any format Points take the same lengths: @12'6\",0 or @10'\u003C90. Angles stay in degrees. The format shows in the history (DIST, ID, LIST, AREA: areas as square inches and square feet), in Properties, in the coordinates on the status bar, and in new dimensions. Sheets keep their paper units (millimeters or inches) as decimals. The length format is saved in DWG and DXF files, and each dimension keeps its own: a saved drawing opens again as you left it. Paper sizes Sheets and model plots use these sizes, in either orientation: Paper Size Unit on the sheet ISO A4 297 × 210 mm millimeters ISO A3 420 × 297 mm millimeters ISO A2 594 × 420 mm millimeters ISO A1 841 × 594 mm millimeters ISO A0 1189 × 841 mm millimeters Letter 11 × 8.5 in inches Legal 14 × 8.5 in inches Tabloid / ANSI B 17 × 11 in inches ANSI C 22 × 17 in inches ANSI D 34 × 22 in inches ARCH C 24 × 18 in inches ARCH D 36 × 24 in inches ARCH E 48 × 36 in inches New sheets start on ISO A3 for metric and unitless drawings, and on Tabloid for drawings in inches or feet. Model plots start on ISO A4 or Letter the same way, then remember your last choice. Sheets from opened files keep their own size, shown as \"Custom\" when it is not one of these. Scales A scale 1:n means one unit on paper shows n of the same unit in the model, so 1:100 shows 1 m of building as 10 mm of paper. CadCraft works this out from the drawing units and the paper's unit. Metric scales, offered for every drawing: 10:1, 5:1, 2:1, 1:1, 1:2, 1:5, 1:10, 1:20, 1:25, 1:50, 1:100, 1:200, 1:250, 1:500, 1:1000, 1:1250, 1:2000, 1:2500, 1:5000, 1:10000. Architectural scales, offered when the units are inches or feet: 3\" = 1'-0\", 1-1/2\" = 1'-0\", 1\" = 1'-0\", 3/4\" = 1'-0\", 1/2\" = 1'-0\", 3/8\" = 1'-0\", 1/4\" = 1'-0\", 3/16\" = 1'-0\", 1/8\" = 1'-0\", 3/32\" = 1'-0\", 1/16\" = 1'-0\". Engineering scales, offered when the units are inches or feet: 1\" = 10', 1\" = 20', 1\" = 30', 1\" = 40', 1\" = 50', 1\" = 100'. Your own scales. Type SCALELISTEDIT (or SCALELIST) to add a scale of your own, such as 1:75 or a site scale of 2 paper : 300 drawing. Add asks for a name, then the paper units and the drawing units (a name like 1:75 offers its own numbers; press Enter to take them). Edit, Delete, List and Reset work on the same list; Reset goes back to the standard list. Your scales appear after the standard ones in the viewport scale list of Properties and in the Plot to PDF dialog, a viewport at that scale reads with its name, and they are saved in the drawing (DWG and DXF 2000) as the drawing's scale list. A drawing opened from another program brings its list in; scales it has that match a standard one are not shown twice. When a viewport is aimed with Show the whole model or Pick a window on the model…, CadCraft picks the largest standard scale that fits. Fit the whole model sets an exact scale instead, which reads as a plain ratio. Sizes on paper and in the model Text and dimension sizes are in the units of the space you draw in. You want on paper Drawn on a sheet Drawn in the model at 1:50 (mm drawing) Drawn in the model at 1/4\" = 1'-0\" (inch drawing) 2.5 mm text 2.5 125 — 3/32\" text 0.09375 — 4.5 For dimensions, keep the sizes as paper sizes and set the overall scale (DIMSCALE) to the plot scale: 50 for 1:50, 48 for 1/4\" = 1'-0\". See Set dimension sizes. Line weights and linetypes A layer, or an object, can have a lineweight (0.05 to 2.11 mm) and a linetype (dashed, hidden, center, phantom, dot…). Set them in the right panel: with nothing selected, Linetype and Lineweight under Current settings are what new objects get (By layer to follow their layer), and the two under Layer … are the current layer's own. With objects selected, the same two rows and Linetype scale change those objects. The commands are LINETYPE (LT), LWEIGHT (LW) and LTSCALE (LTS). Linetypes show on screen and in plots. Their dashes are in drawing units: a metric drawing uses the ISO sizes (DASHED: 12.7 mm dashes), an inch drawing the inch ones (0.5\"). If the dashes look too small or too large, change the drawing's Linetype scale (LTSCALE): every dash and gap is multiplied by it; an object's own scale multiplies it again. A pattern too fine to see at all is drawn as a solid line. Lineweights print in plots, in mm on the paper whatever the scale. Lines with the Default lineweight use the pen width chosen in the Plot to PDF dialog (0.13 to 0.70 mm; 0.25 mm to start with); a lineweight of 0 prints as the thinnest line. On screen every line is one pixel wide until you press LWT in the status bar (or type LWDISPLAY): then a lineweight above 0.25 mm draws wider (0.5 mm = 2 pixels) in the model, and at its true width on the paper on a sheet. The choice is kept in this browser.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"run-lisp","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Run LISP routines","summary":"What CadCraft's LISP runs (functions, prompts, commands, objects, dialogs, vla objects), how to type LISP on the command line, and what it does not have yet.","section":"reference","order":48,"keywords":["lisp","routine","lsp","dcl","dialog","command","defun","entget","ssget","vla","vlax","vl","getpoint","error","compatibility","what works"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"type-lisp-on-the-command-line","text":"Type LISP on the command line","level":2},{"id":"what-runs","text":"What runs","level":2},{"id":"routines-tested","text":"Routines tested","level":2},{"id":"not-built-yet","text":"Not built yet","level":2},{"id":"mistakes-to-avoid","text":"Mistakes to avoid","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat to expect when you load a LISP routine written for another CAD program: which parts run in CadCraft, and what it does not have yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECadCraft has its own LISP. It was written from the public AutoLISP reference, not from anyone else&#039;s program, so most routines written for AutoCAD run unchanged. Where something is missing, the command line names the function, so you can tell a gap in CadCraft from a mistake in the routine.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"type-lisp-on-the-command-line\"\u003EType LISP on the command line\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType an expression that starts with \u003Ccode\u003E(\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003E(+ 1 2)\u003C/code\u003E prints \u003Ccode\u003E3\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003E!name\u003C/code\u003E prints the value of a variable. An expression that doesn&#039;t close yet waits for the next line.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAt a prompt, an expression answers it: at &quot;Specify radius&quot; type \u003Ccode\u003E(* 2 12.5)\u003C/code\u003E for 25.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-runs\"\u003EWhat runs\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe language:\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode\u003Edefun\u003C/code\u003E (with locals after \u003Ccode\u003E/\u003C/code\u003E), \u003Ccode\u003Esetq\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Elambda\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eif\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Econd\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ewhile\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Erepeat\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eforeach\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eprogn\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eand\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eor\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Equote\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eapply\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Emapcar\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eeval\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eread\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eload\u003C/code\u003E, and \u003Ccode\u003E*error*\u003C/code\u003E (a local \u003Ccode\u003E*error*\u003C/code\u003E works). Names are not case-sensitive; integers and reals differ, as you expect (\u003Ccode\u003E(/ 7 2)\u003C/code\u003E is 3).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBuilt-in functions:\u003C/strong\u003E maths, lists (\u003Ccode\u003Ecar\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ecdr\u003C/code\u003E and their family, \u003Ccode\u003Eassoc\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Enth\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Emember\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Esubst\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eappend\u003C/code\u003E…), strings (\u003Ccode\u003Estrcat\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Esubstr\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Estrcase\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ewcmatch\u003C/code\u003E…), conversions (\u003Ccode\u003Ertos\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eatof\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eitoa\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eangtos\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Edistof\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ecvunit\u003C/code\u003E), geometry (\u003Ccode\u003Epolar\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Edistance\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eangle\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Einters\u003C/code\u003E) and files (\u003Ccode\u003Eopen\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eread-line\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ewrite-line\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eclose\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Efindfile\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECommands:\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E(defun c:NAME …)\u003C/code\u003E makes a command. \u003Ccode\u003E(command …)\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E(command-s …)\u003C/code\u003E drive CadCraft&#039;s own commands, with \u003Ccode\u003Epause\u003C/code\u003E for your answer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPrompts:\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode\u003Egetpoint\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Egetcorner\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Egetdist\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Egetangle\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Egetreal\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Egetint\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Egetstring\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Egetkword\u003C/code\u003E with \u003Ccode\u003Einitget\u003C/code\u003E keywords, \u003Ccode\u003Eentsel\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Enentsel\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003Essget\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EW\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EF\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EWP\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ECP\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EX\u003C/code\u003E, with filter lists and \u003Ccode\u003E-4\u003C/code\u003E tests).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EObjects as lists:\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode\u003Eentget\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eentmod\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eentmake\u003C/code\u003E (blocks too), \u003Ccode\u003Eentdel\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eentlast\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eentnext\u003C/code\u003E (a block reference&#039;s attributes come after it as ATTRIBs, then a SEQEND, so attribute routines work), \u003Ccode\u003Ehandent\u003C/code\u003E, selection sets, \u003Ccode\u003Etblsearch\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003Etblnext\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Elayoutlist\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eregapp\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Egetvar\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003Esetvar\u003C/code\u003E for the settings CadCraft has.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDialogs:\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E.dcl\u003C/code\u003E files with \u003Ccode\u003Eload_dialog\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Enew_dialog\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eaction_tile\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eset_tile\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eget_tile\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Estart_list\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Estart_dialog\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Edone_dialog\u003C/code\u003E. Buttons, edit boxes, list boxes, popup lists, toggles, radio buttons, rows, columns and boxes all show. A routine that writes its dialog to a temporary file works too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EObjects:\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode\u003Evl-load-com\u003C/code\u003E (it does nothing; nothing needs loading), the \u003Ccode\u003Evl-\u003C/code\u003E list and string functions, \u003Ccode\u003Evl-catch-all-apply\u003C/code\u003E, and \u003Ccode\u003Evla-get-…\u003C/code\u003E / \u003Ccode\u003Evla-put-…\u003C/code\u003E properties, \u003Ccode\u003Evla-…\u003C/code\u003E methods and \u003Ccode\u003Evlax-for\u003C/code\u003E over the Blocks, Layers and Layouts collections, model space and block definitions, with variants and safe arrays.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"routines-tested\"\u003ERoutines tested\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECadCraft&#039;s tests run a set of routines written for these checks (selection filters, prompts, commands, objects, error handlers), and one popular routine people asked for by name: Lee Mac&#039;s DelBlocks, by name through its dialog and by picking a block. CadCraft also has its own \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDELBLOCKS\u003C/code\u003E, so you don&#039;t need a routine for that.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Egrread\u003C/code\u003E (tracking the mouse yourself), reactors (\u003Ccode\u003Evlr-…\u003C/code\u003E), curve functions (\u003Ccode\u003Evlax-curve-…\u003C/code\u003E), dictionaries and extended data (\u003Ccode\u003Edictsearch\u003C/code\u003E, xdata in \u003Ccode\u003Eentget\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003Eentmake\u003C/code\u003E), and the Express Tools&#039; \u003Ccode\u003Eacet-…\u003C/code\u003E functions.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EImages and slides in dialogs show as blank frames.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERunning another program, the Windows registry, and files outside the ones you load: a routine can write and read its own temporary files, which last until you close the page.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen a routine reaches one of these, the command line shows &quot;no function definition&quot; and the name, and says CadCraft does not have it yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mistakes-to-avoid\"\u003EMistakes to avoid\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA command with the same name as one of CadCraft&#039;s.\u003C/strong\u003E CadCraft&#039;s own command stays. Run the routine&#039;s as \u003Ccode\u003E(c:NAME)\u003C/code\u003E on the command line.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERunning LISP you don&#039;t trust.\u003C/strong\u003E A routine can change anything in the drawing, but nothing outside it: it can&#039;t reach your files, other sites or other apps.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will learn What to expect when you load a LISP routine written for another CAD program: which parts run in CadCraft, and what it does not have yet. CadCraft has its own LISP. It was written from the public AutoLISP reference, not from anyone else's program, so most routines written for AutoCAD run unchanged. Where something is missing, the command line names the function, so you can tell a gap in CadCraft from a mistake in the routine. Type LISP on the command line Type an expression that starts with ( and press Enter: (+ 1 2) prints 3. !name prints the value of a variable. An expression that doesn't close yet waits for the next line. At a prompt, an expression answers it: at \"Specify radius\" type (* 2 12.5) for 25. What runs The language: defun (with locals after /), setq, lambda, if, cond, while, repeat, foreach, progn, and, or, quote, apply, mapcar, eval, read, load, and *error* (a local *error* works). Names are not case-sensitive; integers and reals differ, as you expect ((/ 7 2) is 3). Built-in functions: maths, lists (car, cdr and their family, assoc, nth, member, subst, append…), strings (strcat, substr, strcase, wcmatch…), conversions (rtos, atof, itoa, angtos, distof, cvunit), geometry (polar, distance, angle, inters) and files (open, read-line, write-line, close, findfile). Commands: (defun c:NAME …) makes a command. (command …) and (command-s …) drive CadCraft's own commands, with pause for your answer. Prompts: getpoint, getcorner, getdist, getangle, getreal, getint, getstring, getkword with initget keywords, entsel, nentsel and ssget (W, C, F, WP, CP, P, L, X, with filter lists and -4 tests). Objects as lists: entget, entmod, entmake (blocks too), entdel, entlast, entnext (a block reference's attributes come after it as ATTRIBs, then a SEQEND, so attribute routines work), handent, selection sets, tblsearch and tblnext, layoutlist, regapp, getvar and setvar for the settings CadCraft has. Dialogs: .dcl files with load_dialog, new_dialog, action_tile, set_tile, get_tile, start_list, start_dialog, done_dialog. Buttons, edit boxes, list boxes, popup lists, toggles, radio buttons, rows, columns and boxes all show. A routine that writes its dialog to a temporary file works too. Objects: vl-load-com (it does nothing; nothing needs loading), the vl- list and string functions, vl-catch-all-apply, and vla-get-… / vla-put-… properties, vla-… methods and vlax-for over the Blocks, Layers and Layouts collections, model space and block definitions, with variants and safe arrays. Routines tested CadCraft's tests run a set of routines written for these checks (selection filters, prompts, commands, objects, error handlers), and one popular routine people asked for by name: Lee Mac's DelBlocks, by name through its dialog and by picking a block. CadCraft also has its own DELBLOCKS, so you don't need a routine for that. Not built yet grread (tracking the mouse yourself), reactors (vlr-…), curve functions (vlax-curve-…), dictionaries and extended data (dictsearch, xdata in entget and entmake), and the Express Tools' acet-… functions. Images and slides in dialogs show as blank frames. Running another program, the Windows registry, and files outside the ones you load: a routine can write and read its own temporary files, which last until you close the page. When a routine reaches one of these, the command line shows \"no function definition\" and the name, and says CadCraft does not have it yet. Mistakes to avoid A command with the same name as one of CadCraft's. CadCraft's own command stays. Run the routine's as (c:NAME) on the command line. Running LISP you don't trust. A routine can change anything in the drawing, but nothing outside it: it can't reach your files, other sites or other apps.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"mouse-trackpad-touch","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Mouse, trackpad and touch","summary":"How to zoom, pan, right-click and pick in CadCraft with a wheel mouse, a Mac or PC trackpad, or a touch screen, and what differs between browsers.","section":"reference","order":50,"keywords":["trackpad","touchpad","magic mouse","touch","tablet","ipad","pan","zoom","pinch","right click","two-finger","middle button","browser","chrome","firefox","safari","edge"],"headings":[{"id":"wheel-mouse","text":"Wheel mouse","level":2},{"id":"trackpad","text":"Trackpad","level":2},{"id":"touch-screens-and-tablets","text":"Touch screens and tablets","level":2},{"id":"browsers","text":"Browsers","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ECadCraft is designed for a mouse with a wheel. Everything also works with a trackpad, with a few habits to learn. Touch screens work for picking, with limits.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"wheel-mouse\"\u003EWheel mouse\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EZoom:\u003C/strong\u003E roll the wheel. The drawing zooms about the crosshair, so point at what you want to see.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPan:\u003C/strong\u003E hold the wheel down and drag, or drag with the right button.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EZoom to everything:\u003C/strong\u003E double-click the wheel, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExtents\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBack to the view before:\u003C/strong\u003E click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPrevious\u003C/span\u003E in the View group, or type \u003Ccode\u003EZ\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. A run of wheel steps counts as one view, so one \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPrevious\u003C/span\u003E undoes it. Save views you come back to with \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ENAMEDVIEW\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003ENV\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEnter:\u003C/strong\u003E right-click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"trackpad\"\u003ETrackpad\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPan:\u003C/strong\u003E move two fingers on the trackpad, in any direction: the drawing follows your fingers. You can also drag with a two-finger click held down (a right-button drag).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EZoom:\u003C/strong\u003E pinch with two fingers. Zooming follows the crosshair.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERight-click (Enter):\u003C/strong\u003E click with two fingers. Turn on &quot;Secondary click&quot; in \u003Cstrong\u003ESystem Settings\u003C/strong\u003E, then \u003Cstrong\u003ETrackpad\u003C/strong\u003E, if it does nothing. Control-click may count as an ordinary click in CadCraft rather than a right-click, so use the two-finger click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EZoom to an area:\u003C/strong\u003E click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWindow\u003C/span\u003E in the View group and click two corners.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:windows,linux\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EWindows or Linux\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPan:\u003C/strong\u003E move two fingers on the touchpad, in any direction: the drawing follows your fingers. You can also drag with the right button held down.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EZoom:\u003C/strong\u003E pinch with two fingers. Zooming follows the crosshair.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERight-click (Enter):\u003C/strong\u003E click with two fingers, or click the lower-right corner of the touchpad, depending on your touchpad settings.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EZoom to an area:\u003C/strong\u003E click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWindow\u003C/span\u003E in the View group and click two corners.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHolding \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and dragging does \u003Cstrong\u003Enot\u003C/strong\u003E pan in CadCraft: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E is Enter.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA mouse wheel still zooms, one step at a time. A touchpad that sends its scrolling in whole steps, as a wheel does, zooms with a two-finger scroll instead of panning: zoom out, point at the area you want and zoom back in. The 3D tab keeps its own trackpad moves (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/3d-solids#look-around\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/3d-solids\" data-hash=\"look-around\"\u003EModel in 3D\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"touch-screens-and-tablets\"\u003ETouch screens and tablets\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECadCraft reads a finger as a mouse click:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA tap picks a point or an object, or selects.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDragging a finger on empty space draws a lasso (moving right first a window, left first a crossing; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/lasso-selection\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/lasso-selection\"\u003ELasso selection\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ETRIM\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXTEND\u003C/code\u003E, dragging a finger through pieces trims or extends them all.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDragging a selected object&#039;s grip moves the grip (grips and the pick box are bigger for a finger).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPinch\u003C/strong\u003E with two fingers to zoom about the point between them; move two fingers together to \u003Cstrong\u003Epan\u003C/strong\u003E. A second finger cancels whatever the first one began, so a pinch never leaves a mark.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003Elong press\u003C/strong\u003E (hold still for about half a second) is a right click: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. It ends a command, finishes a selection, or repeats the last command.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnder the command line a \u003Cstrong\u003Ekey row\u003C/strong\u003E appears: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E, Undo, Redo, the characters a tablet keyboard hides (\u003Ccode\u003E@\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E,\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E&#039;\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E&quot;\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E-\u003C/code\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESnap\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrtho\u003C/span\u003E, a keyboard button, and a big \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E. Tap the keyboard button (or the command line) to type a command or a number.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThere is no hover, so snap markers only show where your finger is.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:touch\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ETouch\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou are using a touch screen. For anything more than a quick look, an attached keyboard and a mouse or trackpad make CadCraft much easier to use.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"browsers\"\u003EBrowsers\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECadCraft works the same in Chrome, Edge, Firefox and Safari, on Mac, Windows and Linux. The differences are small:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETopic\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat to know\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpening a DWG\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELarge drawings take a few seconds to read in every browser; the status line shows &quot;Reading …&quot; meanwhile\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDownloads\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEach browser decides where downloads go and whether to ask first; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/browsers\" data-topic=\"basics/browsers\"\u003EBrowsers and computers\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen to print\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpens the PDF in a new tab. If the browser blocks pop-ups, CadCraft downloads the PDF instead\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EF7\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFirefox may offer caret browsing; choose No\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELeaving with unsaved changes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEvery browser shows its own &quot;Leave site?&quot; question, which CadCraft cannot change\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ESafari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn Safari, a PDF opened with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen to print\u003C/span\u003E may show in the tab without printing: use \u003Cstrong\u003EFile\u003C/strong\u003E, then \u003Cstrong\u003EPrint\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:firefox\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EFirefox\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFirefox may ask what to do with each download. Choose to save the file, or change this once in Firefox&#039;s settings under Downloads.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E","text":"CadCraft is designed for a mouse with a wheel. Everything also works with a trackpad, with a few habits to learn. Touch screens work for picking, with limits. Wheel mouse Zoom: roll the wheel. The drawing zooms about the crosshair, so point at what you want to see. Pan: hold the wheel down and drag, or drag with the right button. Zoom to everything: double-click the wheel, or click Extents. Back to the view before: click Previous in the View group, or type Z, Enter, P, Enter. A run of wheel steps counts as one view, so one Previous undoes it. Save views you come back to with NAMEDVIEW (NV). Enter: right-click. Trackpad Pan: move two fingers on the trackpad, in any direction: the drawing follows your fingers. You can also drag with a two-finger click held down (a right-button drag). Zoom: pinch with two fingers. Zooming follows the crosshair. Right-click (Enter): click with two fingers. Turn on \"Secondary click\" in System Settings, then Trackpad, if it does nothing. Control-click may count as an ordinary click in CadCraft rather than a right-click, so use the two-finger click. Zoom to an area: click Window in the View group and click two corners. Pan: move two fingers on the touchpad, in any direction: the drawing follows your fingers. You can also drag with the right button held down. Zoom: pinch with two fingers. Zooming follows the crosshair. Right-click (Enter): click with two fingers, or click the lower-right corner of the touchpad, depending on your touchpad settings. Zoom to an area: click Window in the View group and click two corners. Holding Space and dragging does not pan in CadCraft: Space is Enter. A mouse wheel still zooms, one step at a time. A touchpad that sends its scrolling in whole steps, as a wheel does, zooms with a two-finger scroll instead of panning: zoom out, point at the area you want and zoom back in. The 3D tab keeps its own trackpad moves (see Model in 3D). Touch screens and tablets CadCraft reads a finger as a mouse click: A tap picks a point or an object, or selects. Dragging a finger on empty space draws a lasso (moving right first a window, left first a crossing; see Lasso selection). In TRIM and EXTEND, dragging a finger through pieces trims or extends them all. Dragging a selected object's grip moves the grip (grips and the pick box are bigger for a finger). Pinch with two fingers to zoom about the point between them; move two fingers together to pan. A second finger cancels whatever the first one began, so a pinch never leaves a mark. A long press (hold still for about half a second) is a right click: Enter. It ends a command, finishes a selection, or repeats the last command. Under the command line a key row appears: Esc, Undo, Redo, the characters a tablet keyboard hides (@ , \u003C ' \" -), Snap, Ortho, a keyboard button, and a big Enter. Tap the keyboard button (or the command line) to type a command or a number. There is no hover, so snap markers only show where your finger is. You are using a touch screen. For anything more than a quick look, an attached keyboard and a mouse or trackpad make CadCraft much easier to use. Browsers CadCraft works the same in Chrome, Edge, Firefox and Safari, on Mac, Windows and Linux. The differences are small: Topic What to know Opening a DWG Large drawings take a few seconds to read in every browser; the status line shows \"Reading …\" meanwhile Downloads Each browser decides where downloads go and whether to ask first; see Browsers and computers Open to print Opens the PDF in a new tab. If the browser blocks pop-ups, CadCraft downloads the PDF instead F7 Firefox may offer caret browsing; choose No Leaving with unsaved changes Every browser shows its own \"Leave site?\" question, which CadCraft cannot change In Safari, a PDF opened with Open to print may show in the tab without printing: use File, then Print. Firefox may ask what to do with each download. Choose to save the file, or change this once in Firefox's settings under Downloads.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"dynamic-blocks","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Dynamic blocks","summary":"What CadCraft does with dynamic blocks in a DWG: they keep their true names, show the state they were saved in, switch visibility states, show their other values, and stay dynamic when saved.","section":"reference","order":62,"keywords":["dynamic block","dynamic blocks","visibility","visibility state","flip","stretch","lookup","parameters","custom","block properties","*u blocks","dynamic"],"headings":[{"id":"what-works","text":"What works","level":2},{"id":"what-is-read-only-or-not-there-yet","text":"What is read only, or not there yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-works\"\u003EWhat works\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA DWG made with dynamic blocks opens with them working this far:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETheir true names.\u003C/strong\u003E A dynamic block is listed in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBlocks\u003C/span\u003E panel under its own name (&quot;Receptacle&quot;, &quot;Sink&quot;), not as an unnamed \u003Ccode\u003E*U\u003C/code\u003E block.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe state they were saved in.\u003C/strong\u003E Each reference shows exactly what the other program showed: the visibility state it was set to, stretched or flipped as it was saved.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESwitching the visibility state.\u003C/strong\u003E Select one reference (or several of the same block), open \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E, and in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECustom\u003C/span\u003E section choose another state from the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EVisibility\u003C/span\u003E list. The reference shows that state at once, its attributes too: those the state hides disappear, those it shows appear with their values. A reference keeps every value, so nothing comes back empty; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E lists the hidden ones marked &quot;hidden&quot;. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E puts the old state back in one step.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EGrips.\u003C/strong\u003E A selected dynamic block shows its own grips where its parameters are: a small triangle for the visibility state (click it for the list of states), arrows at the ends of a distance, a double arrow for a flip. Only the visibility grip acts today; the others say so in the status bar.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe other values, read only.\u003C/strong\u003E Under the visibility list, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECustom\u003C/span\u003E also shows the block&#039;s distances, alignment angles, flip states and points as the drawing holds them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEditing visibility states.\u003C/strong\u003E Open the block with \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EBEDIT\u003C/code\u003E: the editor shows one state, named in the bar above the drawing (pick another there). Type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EBVSTATE\u003C/code\u003E to switch, add (hiding everything, showing everything or keeping what shows), rename or delete a state; \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EBVHIDE\u003C/code\u003E hides selected objects in the state showing; \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EBVSHOW\u003C/code\u003E shows the hidden ones faded so you can click the ones to bring back. Save the block: references set to a renamed state keep it under its new name, and those set to a deleted state show the first state.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EKept dynamic.\u003C/strong\u003E Saved as DWG in the version the drawing came in, the blocks stay dynamic, with the state you chose: the other program opens them as the same dynamic blocks. Visibility states you edited are saved too: their new names, the states you added and what each one shows.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-read-only-or-not-there-yet\"\u003EWhat is read only, or not there yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot there yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChanging distances, angles or flips (stretch, move, flip actions)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThey show read only in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECustom\u003C/span\u003E; edit in the program that made the block.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELookup, XY, rotation and polar parameters\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot read yet: the reference shows as saved.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDragging the distance, flip and point grips\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThey show where the parameters are; the visibility grip and the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EVisibility\u003C/span\u003E list in Properties switch states.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMaking dynamic blocks (adding parameters, or visibility states to a block that has none)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBlocks you make in CadCraft are plain blocks; \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EBPARAMETER\u003C/code\u003E says so.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAdding or erasing objects of a block with visibility states in the block editor\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe block is then saved as a plain block (the editor says so when it closes).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EKeeping them dynamic in another DWG version or in DXF\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThey are saved as plain blocks showing the state you chose, and the save says so.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMoving or changing objects in \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EBEDIT\u003C/code\u003E keeps a block with visibility states dynamic. A block without states, or one whose objects were added or erased in the editor, becomes a plain block.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"What works A DWG made with dynamic blocks opens with them working this far: Their true names. A dynamic block is listed in the Blocks panel under its own name (\"Receptacle\", \"Sink\"), not as an unnamed *U block. The state they were saved in. Each reference shows exactly what the other program showed: the visibility state it was set to, stretched or flipped as it was saved. Switching the visibility state. Select one reference (or several of the same block), open Properties, and in the Custom section choose another state from the Visibility list. The reference shows that state at once, its attributes too: those the state hides disappear, those it shows appear with their values. A reference keeps every value, so nothing comes back empty; Properties lists the hidden ones marked \"hidden\". Ctrl+Z puts the old state back in one step. Grips. A selected dynamic block shows its own grips where its parameters are: a small triangle for the visibility state (click it for the list of states), arrows at the ends of a distance, a double arrow for a flip. Only the visibility grip acts today; the others say so in the status bar. The other values, read only. Under the visibility list, Custom also shows the block's distances, alignment angles, flip states and points as the drawing holds them. Editing visibility states. Open the block with BEDIT: the editor shows one state, named in the bar above the drawing (pick another there). Type BVSTATE to switch, add (hiding everything, showing everything or keeping what shows), rename or delete a state; BVHIDE hides selected objects in the state showing; BVSHOW shows the hidden ones faded so you can click the ones to bring back. Save the block: references set to a renamed state keep it under its new name, and those set to a deleted state show the first state. Kept dynamic. Saved as DWG in the version the drawing came in, the blocks stay dynamic, with the state you chose: the other program opens them as the same dynamic blocks. Visibility states you edited are saved too: their new names, the states you added and what each one shows. What is read only, or not there yet Not there yet Instead, today Changing distances, angles or flips (stretch, move, flip actions) They show read only in Custom; edit in the program that made the block. Lookup, XY, rotation and polar parameters Not read yet: the reference shows as saved. Dragging the distance, flip and point grips They show where the parameters are; the visibility grip and the Visibility list in Properties switch states. Making dynamic blocks (adding parameters, or visibility states to a block that has none) Blocks you make in CadCraft are plain blocks; BPARAMETER says so. Adding or erasing objects of a block with visibility states in the block editor The block is then saved as a plain block (the editor says so when it closes). Keeping them dynamic in another DWG version or in DXF They are saved as plain blocks showing the state you chose, and the save says so. Moving or changing objects in BEDIT keeps a block with visibility states dynamic. A block without states, or one whose objects were added or erased in the editor, becomes a plain block.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"point-cloud-formats","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Point cloud formats: what attaches, and what to export","summary":"Which laser scan files attach under the drawing, which are a scanner maker's own format, and what to export from the scanner's software so the scan attaches here.","section":"reference","order":89,"keywords":["point cloud","pointcloudattach","pcattach","laser scan","scan","formats","e57","las","laz","ply","pcd","ptx","pts","xyz","rcp","rcs","fls","lgs","tzf","zfs","export","can't open","not read"],"headings":[{"id":"what-attaches-here","text":"What attaches here","level":2},{"id":"files-that-only-the-scanner-s-software-reads","text":"Files that only the scanner's software reads","level":2},{"id":"why-these-files-are-not-read","text":"Why these files are not read","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-attaches-here\"\u003EWhat attaches here\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAttach these with \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPOINTCLOUDATTACH\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen\u003C/span\u003E or by dropping them on the page (see the topic on tracing as-built lines over a point cloud):\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFormat\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EExtension\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat is read\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELAS, LAZ\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.las\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.laz\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELAS 1.0–1.4: points, colors, intensity, LiDAR classes, the coordinate system and its units. LAZ up to 1 GB.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EE57\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.e57\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEvery scan in the file, each placed by its pose; colors, or intensity when the file has no colors; meters. Pictures in the file are left out (said). Up to 6 million points and 512 MB for now: export bigger scans as LAS or LAZ.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPLY\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.ply\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EText and binary (both byte orders): the vertices&#039; x y z, colors and intensity. Faces are left out (said).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPCD\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.pcd\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EText and binary: x y z, packed or separate colors, intensity. Compressed PCD files are not read yet: save them as binary or text.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPTX\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.ptx\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne or more scans, each placed by its own transform; points the scanner did not measure are left out. Meters.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPTS, XYZ\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.pts\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.xyz\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EText: x y z, with intensity and colors when the columns are there.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"files-that-only-the-scanner-s-software-reads\"\u003EFiles that only the scanner&#039;s software reads\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESome scan files are a scanner maker&#039;s own format, with no published description and no open reader. CadCraft can&#039;t read them: attaching one prints what it is and what to do on the command line. Export the scan from the maker&#039;s software as E57 or LAS (or PTX, PTS), then attach the exported file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFile\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EExtension\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EExport it from\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EReCap project\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.rcp\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EReCap: open the project and export it. The project only points to its scans, so copying the \u003Ccode\u003E.rcp\u003C/code\u003E alone gives no points.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EReCap scan\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.rcs\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EReCap: open the project the scan belongs to and export it.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFARO scan\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.fls\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFARO SCENE.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELeica scan\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.lgs\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELeica Cyclone REGISTER 360.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETrimble scan\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.tzf\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETrimble RealWorks or Trimble Business Center.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZ+F scan\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.zfs\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZ+F LaserControl.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen the export asks for a format, LAS or LAZ keeps everything CadCraft uses (colors, intensity, classes, the coordinate system and its units) in the smallest file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"why-these-files-are-not-read\"\u003EWhy these files are not read\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThose formats are not published, and the only way to read them is each maker&#039;s own software library, under its own terms. CadCraft only reads formats it can read with its own code on your computer, so a scan never has to be sent anywhere to attach.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What attaches here Attach these with POINTCLOUDATTACH, Open or by dropping them on the page (see the topic on tracing as-built lines over a point cloud): Format Extension What is read LAS, LAZ .las, .laz LAS 1.0–1.4: points, colors, intensity, LiDAR classes, the coordinate system and its units. LAZ up to 1 GB. E57 .e57 Every scan in the file, each placed by its pose; colors, or intensity when the file has no colors; meters. Pictures in the file are left out (said). Up to 6 million points and 512 MB for now: export bigger scans as LAS or LAZ. PLY .ply Text and binary (both byte orders): the vertices' x y z, colors and intensity. Faces are left out (said). PCD .pcd Text and binary: x y z, packed or separate colors, intensity. Compressed PCD files are not read yet: save them as binary or text. PTX .ptx One or more scans, each placed by its own transform; points the scanner did not measure are left out. Meters. PTS, XYZ .pts, .xyz Text: x y z, with intensity and colors when the columns are there. Files that only the scanner's software reads Some scan files are a scanner maker's own format, with no published description and no open reader. CadCraft can't read them: attaching one prints what it is and what to do on the command line. Export the scan from the maker's software as E57 or LAS (or PTX, PTS), then attach the exported file. File Extension Export it from ReCap project .rcp ReCap: open the project and export it. The project only points to its scans, so copying the .rcp alone gives no points. ReCap scan .rcs ReCap: open the project the scan belongs to and export it. FARO scan .fls FARO SCENE. Leica scan .lgs Leica Cyclone REGISTER 360. Trimble scan .tzf Trimble RealWorks or Trimble Business Center. Z+F scan .zfs Z+F LaserControl. When the export asks for a format, LAS or LAZ keeps everything CadCraft uses (colors, intensity, classes, the coordinate system and its units) in the smallest file. Why these files are not read Those formats are not published, and the only way to read them is each maker's own software library, under its own terms. CadCraft only reads formats it can read with its own code on your computer, so a scan never has to be sent anywhere to attach.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"troubleshooting","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Troubleshooting","summary":"Messages CadCraft shows and problems you may meet, each with the cause and the fix.","section":"trouble","order":10,"keywords":["problem","error","not working","message","cannot open","unknown command","nothing happens","stuck","keys not working","blank","missing","fix"],"headings":[{"id":"opening-files","text":"Opening files","level":2},{"id":"the-command-line","text":"The command line","level":2},{"id":"editing","text":"Editing","level":2},{"id":"dimensions-and-blocks","text":"Dimensions and blocks","level":2},{"id":"sheets-and-plotting","text":"Sheets and plotting","level":2},{"id":"keys-and-the-screen","text":"Keys and the screen","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"opening-files\"\u003EOpening files\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMessage or symptom\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECause\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFix\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;CadCraft opens DWG and DXF drawings. &quot;…&quot; is neither.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe file is not a \u003Ccode\u003E.dwg\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E.dxf\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen the drawing file itself. A PDF can be brought in (Open asks: import or attach, see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/pdf-underlay\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/pdf-underlay\"\u003EPDF underlays\u003C/a\u003E); pictures go in with \u003Ccode\u003EIMAGEATTACH\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;This is a binary DXF. Save it as ASCII DXF (or open the DWG) and try again.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBinary DXF is not read.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAsk for a text (ASCII) DXF, or the DWG.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;This file is not a readable DXF (bad group code on line …).&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe DXF is damaged or not really a DXF.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAsk for the file again, or for a DWG.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;This drawing could not be read. It may be damaged, or saved in a version the reader does not handle yet (…).&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe DWG reader could not read it.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAsk for the DWG saved in an older version, or a DXF.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;This drawing is from AutoCAD R11 / R12 (DWG AC1009), older than CadCraft reads …&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDWG files from Release 12 and earlier use a format CadCraft does not read.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIn the program that made it, save it as DXF (any version) or as a newer DWG, and open that. CadCraft&#039;s own Save as offers DXF R12 for programs that want that old format.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Could not open …&quot; followed by a network message, on the first DWG\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe DWG reader could not be downloaded: you are offline and CadCraft is not saved for offline use, or the connection dropped.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGo online and try again, or open a DXF. Click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E and wait for &quot;Offline ready&quot; so DWG files open offline next time. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/offline\" data-topic=\"basics/offline\"\u003EWorking offline\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe offline button says &quot;Could not save&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESome files did not download.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECheck the connection and click the button to try again.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe offline button says &quot;Offline not available&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPrivate windows and some managed computers cannot keep an offline copy.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUse a normal window, or stay online.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECadCraft does not open with no connection\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIt was not saved for offline use in this browser, or the browser cleared it.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENext time you are online, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E and wait for &quot;Offline ready&quot;.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe drawing opens but parts are missing\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EObjects CadCraft cannot draw yet, or layers that are off or frozen in the file.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERead &quot;About this file&quot; in the left panel. Check the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayers\u003C/span\u003E panel.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EText wraps onto two lines\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECadCraft draws text in a Helvetica-style font, wider than some CAD fonts.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShorten the text in Properties, or live with the extra line.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA circle shows as an ellipse, or mirrored text reads the right way round\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe object was drawn in a tilted plane, or seen from below, in the program that made the file. CadCraft shows it as seen from above, the way the plan view of that program does; text is kept readable.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENothing to fix. To see it the other program&#039;s way, ask for the drawing flattened, or with the objects drawn from above.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA block, circle or dimension sits somewhere other than in the program that made the file\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA drawing case CadCraft reads wrong.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETell us on Discord, with the file if you can share it.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-command-line\"\u003EThe command line\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMessage or symptom\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECause\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFix\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Unknown command &quot;…&quot;. Type HELP for the list.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo command or alias by that name.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EHELP\u003C/code\u003E, or see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/commands\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/commands\"\u003ECommand reference\u003C/a\u003E. Some familiar commands are \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003Enot built yet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;No such command&quot; next to the command line while typing\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWhat you typed matches nothing yet.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EKeep typing, or check the spelling.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Point or option keyword required.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWhat you typed is not a point or an option.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECheck the format: \u003Ccode\u003Ex,y\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E@dx,dy\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E@d&lt;a\u003C/code\u003E, or a single number. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/coordinate-entry\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/coordinate-entry\"\u003ECoordinate and distance entry\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Requires a positive number or two points.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA distance was negative or not a number.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EType a positive number, or click two points.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Requires an angle in degrees or two points.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe angle was not a number.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EType degrees as a plain number.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Requires a number.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA number was expected.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EType digits only.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Invalid option keyword.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe letters do not match an option.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EType the capital letters shown in the square brackets.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E*Cancel*\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYou pressed \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E where a point was required.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStart the command again.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETyping does not reach the command line\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA box in a panel or a dialog has the cursor.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or click on the drawing.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe command uses objects you did not pick\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThey were selected before you started it.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E before the command.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA line went somewhere unexpected\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Ex,y\u003C/code\u003E is absolute.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUse \u003Ccode\u003E@dx,dy\u003C/code\u003E for relative points.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Finish or cancel the current command first (Esc).&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYou pressed \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while a command was running.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, then save.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Could not save as DWG (AutoCAD 2000): … Try Save as, then DXF.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESomething in the drawing could not be written as DWG.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command S\"\u003E⇧⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDXF R12\u003C/span\u003E and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Not saved.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYou closed the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave a copy as\u003C/span\u003E dialog with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECancel\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command S\"\u003E⇧⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"editing\"\u003EEditing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMessage or symptom\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECause\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFix\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAn object will not select\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIts layer is locked, off or frozen; or you are on a sheet and it is the model inside a viewport.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUnlock the layer, or go to the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Nothing found there.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo object under the click.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom in and click on the line itself.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Nothing crosses that object there.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ETRIM\u003C/code\u003E found no object crossing the piece you picked.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECheck that the cutting line really crosses it.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Cannot trim that object.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETrim works on lines, arcs, circles and polylines.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExplode blocks first, or erase and redraw.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;No boundary in that direction.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXTEND\u003C/code\u003E found nothing to meet.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPick nearer the end that should grow.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Cannot offset that object.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOffset works on lines, arcs, circles and polylines.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExplode it first, or draw a parallel line.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Cannot offset that far.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe offset would turn the shape inside out.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUse a smaller distance.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Radius is too large.&quot; or &quot;Distance is too large.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe fillet or chamfer would run past the end of a line.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESet a smaller radius with \u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E, or smaller distances with \u003Ccode\u003ED\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Only the first or last segment of an open polyline can be cut back.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe corner would cut into the middle of a polyline.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPick two segments of the polyline next to each other, or use \u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E for every corner.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Pick a second, different line.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe second pick was the same line, or not a line.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPick another line.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Only polylines, block references and dimensions explode.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe selection had nothing to explode.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect a polyline, block reference or dimension.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;That is the current layer. Make another layer current first.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYou tried to turn off the current layer.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick another layer&#039;s name, then turn this one off.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;There is already a layer called …&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELayer names must be unique.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChoose another name.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Nothing to undo.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThere is nothing left to undo, or you opened or started a drawing (that clears the history).\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E—\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"dimensions-and-blocks\"\u003EDimensions and blocks\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMessage or symptom\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECause\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFix\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDimension text and arrows are tiny or huge\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESizes are in drawing units; the overall scale does not fit the drawing.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESet \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMSCALE\u003C/code\u003E to the plot scale; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/dimension-settings\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/dimension-settings\"\u003ESet dimension sizes\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Pick a point inside the angle you want to measure.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe angular dimension&#039;s location sits on a leg.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick inside the angle.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Those lines are parallel.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EParallel lines have no angle between them.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPick two lines that meet.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Pick a linear or aligned dimension.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMCONTINUE\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMBASELINE\u003C/code\u003E needs one to start from.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPick a linear or aligned dimension.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;This drawing has no blocks yet: F inserts one from a file, L from the library …&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe drawing has no blocks of its own.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EF\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E, or make a block with \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EBLOCK\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;No block called &quot;…&quot;.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe name is wrong.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe message lists the blocks there are.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;A block name cannot start with * or use …&quot; followed by a list of characters\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe name has a character blocks cannot use.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUse letters, digits, \u003Ccode\u003E-\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E_\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;A block cannot contain itself.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe selection holds a reference to the block being made.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELeave that reference out.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;No objects selected; nothing made.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYou pressed \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E before selecting.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERun \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EBLOCK\u003C/code\u003E again and select the objects.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"sheets-and-plotting\"\u003ESheets and plotting\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMessage or symptom\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECause\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFix\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Viewports go on a layout. Open a layout tab (or make one with LAYOUT) first.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMVIEW\u003C/code\u003E on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick a sheet tab first.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Open the layout with the viewport first.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EVPVIEW\u003C/code\u003E on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick the sheet tab first.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA viewport scale looks ten times wrong\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe drawing units are wrong.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESet \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnits\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECurrent settings\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;There is nothing to plot yet.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe model is empty.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDraw something, or open a drawing.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Pick the window to plot.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EA window…\u003C/span\u003E is chosen and no window was picked.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPick\u003C/span\u003E and pick two corners.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;… bigger than the paper: the edges are cut off.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe area does not fit at that scale.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChoose a smaller scale, a larger paper, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit to paper\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;The browser blocked the new tab, so the PDF was downloaded instead.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPop-ups are blocked for the site.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen the downloaded PDF, or allow pop-ups for the site.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECharacters print as &quot;?&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe PDF&#039;s fonts have no letter for them (Arabic, Hebrew, rare ideographs, …), or the CJK font could not be fetched (offline). European scripts, Cyrillic, Greek, Chinese, Japanese and Korean print as themselves.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFor Chinese, Japanese or Korean, plot again with a connection. Other scripts are not built yet.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EConstruction lines show in the PDF\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETheir layer plots.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick the layer&#039;s printer button.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAll lines are equally thick\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETheir lineweight is \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDefault\u003C/span\u003E, so they print at the dialog&#039;s pen width.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGive the layers or objects a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELineweight\u003C/span\u003E in the right panel, or choose a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELine weight\u003C/span\u003E in the dialog. To see lineweights on screen, press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELWT\u003C/span\u003E in the status bar.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"keys-and-the-screen\"\u003EKeys and the screen\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESymptom\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECause\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFix\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EF3, F7 or F8 changes volume or opens Mission Control\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMac function keys.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHold \u003Cstrong\u003Efn\u003C/strong\u003E, or use the status bar buttons. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/keys\" data-topic=\"basics/keys\"\u003EKeys, mice and trackpads\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EF7 asks about caret browsing\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFirefox&#039;s own use of F7.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChoose No; use the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGrid\u003C/span\u003E button.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command N\"\u003E⌘N\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+N\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E opened a browser window\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe browser keeps that key.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew\u003C/span\u003E or type \u003Ccode\u003ENEW\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E selected the command line&#039;s text\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe command line had the cursor.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick an object first, or type \u003Ccode\u003EALL\u003C/code\u003E at a \u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects\u003C/code\u003E prompt.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E does not erase\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe command line had the cursor.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EE\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERight-click does nothing on a Mac trackpad\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EControl-click may count as an ordinary click.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick with two fingers.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENothing pans on a trackpad\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThere is no middle button, and Space does not pan.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESwipe with two fingers: the 2D view pans (pinch and the mouse wheel zoom). Or drag with a right-click held.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe first \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E left full screen\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe browser uses Esc to leave full screen.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again to cancel the command.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E does nothing on a Mac\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EF1 is a brightness key.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHold \u003Cstrong\u003Efn\u003C/strong\u003E, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHelp\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETyping goes into the help&#039;s search box\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe help panel has the cursor.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick on the drawing.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe drawing went blank\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYou zoomed far away.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExtents\u003C/span\u003E, or double-click the wheel.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Leave site?&quot; when closing the tab\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThere are unsaved changes.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStay, save with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, then close.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E","text":"Opening files Message or symptom Cause Fix \"CadCraft opens DWG and DXF drawings. \"…\" is neither.\" The file is not a .dwg or .dxf. Open the drawing file itself. A PDF can be brought in (Open asks: import or attach, see PDF underlays); pictures go in with IMAGEATTACH. \"This is a binary DXF. Save it as ASCII DXF (or open the DWG) and try again.\" Binary DXF is not read. Ask for a text (ASCII) DXF, or the DWG. \"This file is not a readable DXF (bad group code on line …).\" The DXF is damaged or not really a DXF. Ask for the file again, or for a DWG. \"This drawing could not be read. It may be damaged, or saved in a version the reader does not handle yet (…).\" The DWG reader could not read it. Ask for the DWG saved in an older version, or a DXF. \"This drawing is from AutoCAD R11 / R12 (DWG AC1009), older than CadCraft reads …\" DWG files from Release 12 and earlier use a format CadCraft does not read. In the program that made it, save it as DXF (any version) or as a newer DWG, and open that. CadCraft's own Save as offers DXF R12 for programs that want that old format. \"Could not open …\" followed by a network message, on the first DWG The DWG reader could not be downloaded: you are offline and CadCraft is not saved for offline use, or the connection dropped. Go online and try again, or open a DXF. Click Save for offline and wait for \"Offline ready\" so DWG files open offline next time. See Working offline. The offline button says \"Could not save\" Some files did not download. Check the connection and click the button to try again. The offline button says \"Offline not available\" Private windows and some managed computers cannot keep an offline copy. Use a normal window, or stay online. CadCraft does not open with no connection It was not saved for offline use in this browser, or the browser cleared it. Next time you are online, click Save for offline and wait for \"Offline ready\". The drawing opens but parts are missing Objects CadCraft cannot draw yet, or layers that are off or frozen in the file. Read \"About this file\" in the left panel. Check the Layers panel. Text wraps onto two lines CadCraft draws text in a Helvetica-style font, wider than some CAD fonts. Shorten the text in Properties, or live with the extra line. A circle shows as an ellipse, or mirrored text reads the right way round The object was drawn in a tilted plane, or seen from below, in the program that made the file. CadCraft shows it as seen from above, the way the plan view of that program does; text is kept readable. Nothing to fix. To see it the other program's way, ask for the drawing flattened, or with the objects drawn from above. A block, circle or dimension sits somewhere other than in the program that made the file A drawing case CadCraft reads wrong. Tell us on Discord, with the file if you can share it. The command line Message or symptom Cause Fix \"Unknown command \"…\". Type HELP for the list.\" No command or alias by that name. Type HELP, or see Command reference. Some familiar commands are not built yet. \"No such command\" next to the command line while typing What you typed matches nothing yet. Keep typing, or check the spelling. \"Point or option keyword required.\" What you typed is not a point or an option. Check the format: x,y, @dx,dy, @d\u003Ca, or a single number. See Coordinate and distance entry. \"Requires a positive number or two points.\" A distance was negative or not a number. Type a positive number, or click two points. \"Requires an angle in degrees or two points.\" The angle was not a number. Type degrees as a plain number. \"Requires a number.\" A number was expected. Type digits only. \"Invalid option keyword.\" The letters do not match an option. Type the capital letters shown in the square brackets. *Cancel* You pressed Esc, or Enter where a point was required. Start the command again. Typing does not reach the command line A box in a panel or a dialog has the cursor. Press Enter or Esc, or click on the drawing. The command uses objects you did not pick They were selected before you started it. Press Esc before the command. A line went somewhere unexpected x,y is absolute. Use @dx,dy for relative points. \"Finish or cancel the current command first (Esc).\" You pressed Ctrl+S while a command was running. Press Esc, then save. \"Could not save as DWG (AutoCAD 2000): … Try Save as, then DXF.\" Something in the drawing could not be written as DWG. Press Ctrl+Shift+S, choose DXF R12 and click Save. \"Not saved.\" You closed the Save a copy as dialog with Cancel or Esc. Press Ctrl+Shift+S again and click Save. Editing Message or symptom Cause Fix An object will not select Its layer is locked, off or frozen; or you are on a sheet and it is the model inside a viewport. Unlock the layer, or go to the Model tab. \"Nothing found there.\" No object under the click. Zoom in and click on the line itself. \"Nothing crosses that object there.\" TRIM found no object crossing the piece you picked. Check that the cutting line really crosses it. \"Cannot trim that object.\" Trim works on lines, arcs, circles and polylines. Explode blocks first, or erase and redraw. \"No boundary in that direction.\" EXTEND found nothing to meet. Pick nearer the end that should grow. \"Cannot offset that object.\" Offset works on lines, arcs, circles and polylines. Explode it first, or draw a parallel line. \"Cannot offset that far.\" The offset would turn the shape inside out. Use a smaller distance. \"Radius is too large.\" or \"Distance is too large.\" The fillet or chamfer would run past the end of a line. Set a smaller radius with R, or smaller distances with D. \"Only the first or last segment of an open polyline can be cut back.\" The corner would cut into the middle of a polyline. Pick two segments of the polyline next to each other, or use P for every corner. \"Pick a second, different line.\" The second pick was the same line, or not a line. Pick another line. \"Only polylines, block references and dimensions explode.\" The selection had nothing to explode. Select a polyline, block reference or dimension. \"That is the current layer. Make another layer current first.\" You tried to turn off the current layer. Click another layer's name, then turn this one off. \"There is already a layer called …\" Layer names must be unique. Choose another name. \"Nothing to undo.\" There is nothing left to undo, or you opened or started a drawing (that clears the history). — Dimensions and blocks Message or symptom Cause Fix Dimension text and arrows are tiny or huge Sizes are in drawing units; the overall scale does not fit the drawing. Set DIMSCALE to the plot scale; see Set dimension sizes. \"Pick a point inside the angle you want to measure.\" The angular dimension's location sits on a leg. Click inside the angle. \"Those lines are parallel.\" Parallel lines have no angle between them. Pick two lines that meet. \"Pick a linear or aligned dimension.\" DIMCONTINUE or DIMBASELINE needs one to start from. Pick a linear or aligned dimension. \"This drawing has no blocks yet: F inserts one from a file, L from the library …\" The drawing has no blocks of its own. Type F or L, or make a block with BLOCK. \"No block called \"…\".\" The name is wrong. The message lists the blocks there are. \"A block name cannot start with * or use …\" followed by a list of characters The name has a character blocks cannot use. Use letters, digits, - and _. \"A block cannot contain itself.\" The selection holds a reference to the block being made. Leave that reference out. \"No objects selected; nothing made.\" You pressed Enter before selecting. Run BLOCK again and select the objects. Sheets and plotting Message or symptom Cause Fix \"Viewports go on a layout. Open a layout tab (or make one with LAYOUT) first.\" MVIEW on the Model tab. Click a sheet tab first. \"Open the layout with the viewport first.\" VPVIEW on the Model tab. Click the sheet tab first. A viewport scale looks ten times wrong The drawing units are wrong. Set Units in Current settings. \"There is nothing to plot yet.\" The model is empty. Draw something, or open a drawing. \"Pick the window to plot.\" A window… is chosen and no window was picked. Click Pick and pick two corners. \"… bigger than the paper: the edges are cut off.\" The area does not fit at that scale. Choose a smaller scale, a larger paper, or Fit to paper. \"The browser blocked the new tab, so the PDF was downloaded instead.\" Pop-ups are blocked for the site. Open the downloaded PDF, or allow pop-ups for the site. Characters print as \"?\" The PDF's fonts have no letter for them (Arabic, Hebrew, rare ideographs, …), or the CJK font could not be fetched (offline). European scripts, Cyrillic, Greek, Chinese, Japanese and Korean print as themselves. For Chinese, Japanese or Korean, plot again with a connection. Other scripts are not built yet. Construction lines show in the PDF Their layer plots. Click the layer's printer button. All lines are equally thick Their lineweight is Default, so they print at the dialog's pen width. Give the layers or objects a Lineweight in the right panel, or choose a Line weight in the dialog. To see lineweights on screen, press LWT in the status bar. Keys and the screen Symptom Cause Fix F3, F7 or F8 changes volume or opens Mission Control Mac function keys. Hold fn, or use the status bar buttons. See Keys, mice and trackpads. F7 asks about caret browsing Firefox's own use of F7. Choose No; use the Grid button. Ctrl+N opened a browser window The browser keeps that key. Click New or type NEW. Ctrl+A selected the command line's text The command line had the cursor. Click an object first, or type ALL at a Select objects prompt. Delete does not erase The command line had the cursor. Type E and press Enter. Right-click does nothing on a Mac trackpad Control-click may count as an ordinary click. Click with two fingers. Nothing pans on a trackpad There is no middle button, and Space does not pan. Swipe with two fingers: the 2D view pans (pinch and the mouse wheel zoom). Or drag with a right-click held. The first Esc left full screen The browser uses Esc to leave full screen. Press Esc again to cancel the command. F1 does nothing on a Mac F1 is a brightness key. Hold fn, or click Help. Typing goes into the help's search box The help panel has the cursor. Click on the drawing. The drawing went blank You zoomed far away. Click Extents, or double-click the wheel. \"Leave site?\" when closing the tab There are unsaved changes. Stay, save with Ctrl+S, then close.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"not-built-yet","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Not built yet","summary":"What this build of the CadCraft drafting app does not do yet, and what to do instead today.","section":"trouble","order":20,"keywords":["missing","limitations","ctb","stb","plot style","properties","ucs","annotative","dynamic block","spline","roadmap","not supported","dwg save","hatch","stretch","array","join","matchprop","break","lengthen","linetype","lineweight","xref","cloud","3d","solids","booleans","multileader","wipeout"],"headings":[{"id":"files-and-saving","text":"Files and saving","level":2},{"id":"drawing-and-editing","text":"Drawing and editing","level":2},{"id":"the-properties-panel","text":"The Properties panel","level":2},{"id":"precision-aids","text":"Precision aids","level":2},{"id":"sheets-dimensions-and-plotting","text":"Sheets, dimensions and plotting","level":2},{"id":"constraints-and-parameters","text":"Constraints and parameters","level":2},{"id":"3d","text":"3D","level":2},{"id":"routines-and-customizing","text":"Routines and customizing","level":2},{"id":"help","text":"Help","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ECadCraft is in development. This page lists what the current build does not do, so you do not hunt for it. Each item says what to do instead today.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"files-and-saving\"\u003EFiles and saving\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA CadCraft DWG checked in AutoCAD itself\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEvery saved version is read back by three other programs&#039; DWG readers and by CadCraft. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/files#what-a-dwg-keeps-and-changes\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/files\" data-hash=\"what-a-dwg-keeps-and-changes\"\u003EWhat a DWG keeps and changes\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESaving DXF newer than 2000\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESave as DXF 2000, or as DWG up to AutoCAD 2018.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESaving over the opened file in the browser\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEvery save downloads a new copy; replace the old file yourself if you want to. In CadCraft Studio, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E writes back to the file you opened.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECloud saving and sync between computers\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECadCraft does not send its saves to the cloud yet: keep your DWG and DXF files in your own folders. In CadCraft Studio, \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/history\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/history\"\u003EHistory\u003C/a\u003E lists the cloud versions of a drawing saved from elsewhere and copies of your own saves on this computer.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOverlays, and editing a linked drawing in place\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAttach the drawing as a link and reload it after changing the other drawing. A link stays a link when you save as DWG, and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EXCLIP\u003C/code\u003E clips it.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPictures in DXF; wipeouts that hide what is under them\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDXF keeps a picture as its frame with the file name; DWG keeps it as a picture — see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/pictures\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/pictures\"\u003EPictures in a drawing\u003C/a\u003E. A wipeout shows as its frame.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEditing multileaders that have a block, several leader lines or curved lines\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThey are drawn as the other program drew them and move as one. Text multileaders come in editable.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETables in a DXF\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECounted in &quot;About this file&quot;, not shown. Tables in a DWG are shown as the other program drew them.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBinary DXF\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESave it as text (ASCII) DXF in the program that made it.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECopy and paste with other programs\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECadCraft&#039;s clipboard works between CadCraft drawings and tabs in the same browser, not with other programs. Save as DWG or DXF and open it there. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/copy-paste\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/copy-paste\"\u003ECopy, paste and duplicate\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"drawing-and-editing\"\u003EDrawing and editing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHatches tied to their boundary (associative), gradient fills, and patterns beyond the standard set\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHatch again after changing the outline. Opened hatches with other patterns show as a tone. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/hatch\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/hatch\"\u003EHatch and fill areas\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinetypes with shapes or text in them, and lineweights in a saved DXF R12\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShapes and text in a file&#039;s linetype are left out of its dashes; save as DWG to keep lineweights.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPatterned hatches in a saved DXF\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ER12 has no hatch: they are saved as their outlines. Save as DWG to keep the pattern.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAssociative arrays (edited later as one object) and arrays along a path\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EARRAY\u003C/code\u003E makes plain copies: undo and run it again to change it.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESTRETCH\u003C/code\u003E with a crossing polygon\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUse one or more crossing windows.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPEDIT\u003C/code\u003E Edit vertex (one vertex at a time at the command line)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGrips, and the vertex browser in Properties (every vertex, its widths and bulge). The rest of PEDIT is built: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/splines\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/splines\"\u003ESplines and polylines\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EBREAK\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELENGTHEN\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EOFFSET\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ETRIM\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXTEND\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EFILLET\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EJOIN\u003C/code\u003E on ellipses and splines\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXPLODE\u003C/code\u003E a spline (or \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPEDIT\u003C/code\u003E it) to get a polyline these commands take.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFillet and chamfer between an arc segment of a polyline and the segment next to it; fillet joining a line into a polyline\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXPLODE\u003C/code\u003E the polyline, then fillet the pieces. The fillet arc is added as its own object.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETrim and extend: the Project option (3D) and extending a polyline&#039;s arc end\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETrim in the plane of the drawing; for an arc end, explode the polyline or draw the arc again.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOLE objects: inserting them, running or editing them; showing the ones in a DWG where the file puts them; metafile pictures\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/pictures#objects-from-other-programs-ole\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/pictures\" data-hash=\"objects-from-other-programs-ole\"\u003EPictures\u003C/a\u003E. A DXF&#039;s objects show as their bitmap or a labelled frame; a DWG&#039;s are kept, not drawn in place (OLELIST lists them and places a copy of a picture where you say).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETyping formatting codes (bold, stacked fractions, colors per word) in paragraph text; fonts other than the one Helvetica-style font; columns\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFormatting that came in a file is kept, drawn and saved (see Text); new paragraph text has one height and color.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EParagraph text edited at its rotation\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe editor opens level; the text keeps its rotation.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMultileaders saved as multileader objects in a DXF; several leader lines on one multileader; block content\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA DWG keeps them as multileaders; an R12 DXF keeps what they draw (lines, arrowhead and text).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETables\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELines and text.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAttribute definitions kept on their own in a saved file (outside a block), multiple-line attributes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPut attribute definitions into a block before saving: on their own they save as text showing the tag.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELayer states (saving and restoring which layers are on)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUse \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYISO\u003C/code\u003E / \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYUNISO\u003C/code\u003E, or turn layers on and off by hand.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWindow polygon and crossing polygon selection, and saved selection filters\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFence (\u003Ccode\u003EF\u003C/code\u003E), Window, Crossing, Remove (\u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E), \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EQSELECT\u003C/code\u003E for a rule.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-properties-panel\"\u003EThe Properties panel\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrawing the Fit choices, and the horizontal text position and text below the line on dimensions other than linear and aligned\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThey are kept on the object, shown in Properties and saved; CadCraft places the text by its own rule.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMaterials that other programs see\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA saved file keeps the material name for CadCraft; other programs show the layer&#039;s material.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETransparency in DWG 2000 and DXF files\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOther programs show those objects solid; CadCraft keeps the transparency. Save as DWG 2004 or later for everyone.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAn associative hatch in other programs\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe hatch is saved; CadCraft keeps the setting, other programs see a hatch that does not follow its boundary.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECoordinates in Properties in the UCS\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EProperties shows world coordinates; the status bar and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EID\u003C/code\u003E show UCS ones. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/ucs-and-turned-view\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/ucs-and-turned-view\"\u003ETurn the view and use your own coordinates\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAnnotative scaling\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe annotative setting is kept with text; the annotation scale is always 1:1. (The drawing&#039;s scale list is edited with SCALELISTEDIT and saved; it does not drive annotation yet.)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChanging a dynamic block&#039;s distances, angles or flips; dynamic blocks kept dynamic when you save in another DWG version or as DXF\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESwitch its visibility state in Properties › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECustom\u003C/span\u003E; its other values show there read only. It stays dynamic when you save it as DWG in the version the drawing came in. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/dynamic-blocks\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/dynamic-blocks\"\u003EDynamic blocks\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESpline fit tolerance, spline degree 6 and above, 3D splines\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFit and control points, degree 1 to 5 and weights are built: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/splines\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/splines\"\u003ESplines and polylines\u003C/a\u003E. A spline&#039;s height (Z) is not kept.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA hatch that follows its boundary (associative)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe associative setting is kept and saved; hatch again after changing the outline.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"precision-aids\"\u003EPrecision aids\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETracking from points across commands, and the tracking settings dialog (relative polar angles, extra angles)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPOLARANG\u003C/code\u003E sets the step; acquire the points again in the next command.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESnap to grid\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EType coordinates; the grid is a visual guide only.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArc and Length options while picking an area&#039;s corners, and MEASUREGEOM Volume\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDraw the outline with \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPLINE\u003C/code\u003E (arcs included), then \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EAREA\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003EO\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDynamic input for grips, and its options list (the down arrow)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe command line shows the options.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAngles in degrees-minutes-seconds, grads or surveyor&#039;s bearings\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EType decimal degrees: \u003Ccode\u003E30.5\u003C/code\u003E for 30°30&#039;.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA view manager dialog; named views of a sheet that come back on that sheet\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ENAMEDVIEW\u003C/code\u003E lists and restores views. A saved file keeps them, but a sheet&#039;s views come back on the first sheet.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"sheets-dimensions-and-plotting\"\u003ESheets, dimensions and plotting\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA title block that updates itself\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit its texts by hand; the SCALE field does not follow viewport changes.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESaving which dimension style is current; dimension text dragged to a free place; annotative dimension scales\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA drawing opens with Standard current. Text sits on the dimension line (DIMTEDIT Left / Right / Center). See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/dimension-settings\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/dimension-settings\"\u003ESet dimension sizes\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOrdinate dimensions, and editable two-line angular dimensions from opened files\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThey are shown as drawn in the file.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAnnotative scaling\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESet sizes for one plot scale with \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMSCALE\u003C/code\u003E and text heights.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChecking the CTB reader against a file from another program (it is built, from the published description), named plot styles (STB), dither, pen numbers, grayscale from a CTB and adaptive linetype adjustment in a plot style\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMake the table in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlot style tables\u003C/span\u003E (color, gray, ink, lineweight, linetype, line end and join, and fill style per color): see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/plot-pdf#plot-style-tables\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/plot-pdf\" data-hash=\"plot-style-tables\"\u003EPlot style tables\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPage setups\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChoose paper, scale and plot style in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlot to PDF\u003C/span\u003E dialog; a sheet keeps its plot style.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArabic, Hebrew, Thai, Hindi and other scripts in PDFs, and rare ideographs (European scripts, Cyrillic, Greek, Chinese, Japanese and Korean print)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThey print as &quot;?&quot;.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPictures inside an attached PDF page, in a plot\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe page&#039;s lines, fills and text print. A page that is only a picture goes in with \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESCANATTACH\u003C/code\u003E, and pictures print. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/pdf-underlay#plot-and-save\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/pdf-underlay\" data-hash=\"plot-and-save\"\u003EPDF underlays\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESending a job to a laser from CadCraft Studio (Studio has no Web Serial)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESave the G-code and send it with your laser&#039;s own program, or send it from Chrome or Edge. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/laser-cutting#send-from-the-browser\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/laser-cutting\" data-hash=\"send-from-the-browser\"\u003ESend a drawing to a laser\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"constraints-and-parameters\"\u003EConstraints and parameters\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EConstraints on sheets, in blocks, on ellipses, splines or text\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EConstrain on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab, on lines, polylines, circles, arcs and points.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESmooth (G2), and constraining a polyline&#039;s arc bulge directly\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETangent and equal; an arc segment follows its two ends.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EReference (driven) dimensional constraints, and \u003Ccode\u003E3Point\u003C/code\u003E in \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDCANGULAR\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA plain dimension (DIMLINEAR …) shows a value without driving it.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDimensional constraints of a fixed size on paper, and plotting them\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThey show on screen only; add plain dimensions for the plot.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EConstraints kept through EXPLODE, BREAK, JOIN and other commands that make new objects, and on trimmed polylines\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EConstrain them again (AUTOCONSTRAIN helps). \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMIRROR\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ETRIM\u003C/code\u003E of lines, arcs and circles keep them.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDesign tables (configurations) in CadCraft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChange parameters in the panel.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA tested round trip with SolidWorks\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECheck the sketch the macro builds before you rely on it.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"3d\"\u003E3D\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFillets, chamfers, shells and lofts; sweeps along a path that rises (3D paths)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExtrude outlines with arcs, push and pull faces, revolve and sweep along flat paths.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExtrude in another direction than up\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDraw the shape on the ground and extrude it up or down (with a taper or along a path if you like); rotate the solid afterwards.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EElevation and thickness of 2D objects\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe drawing lies on the ground (height 0) on the 3D tab.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEditing solids, regions and bodies from other programs; showing those inside blocks; their faces on swept, lofted or other freeform surfaces; polyface and polygon meshes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EModel-space solids show on the 3D tab as they are and are saved back unchanged in DWG; what is not drawn is listed when the 3D tab opens. Ask for meshes as 3D faces in model space. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/3d-solids#solids-from-other-programs\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/3d-solids\" data-hash=\"solids-from-other-programs\"\u003ESolids from other programs\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECopy and paste of solids\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOn the 3D tab, use \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECOPY\u003C/code\u003E. The clipboard holds 2D objects only.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESections, views that follow later changes to the solids, and shaded solids on plotted sheets\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EVIEWBASE\u003C/code\u003E places flat views (a snapshot); run it again after changing the solids.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMaterials, textures and lighting settings\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe visual styles: shaded, edges, hidden line, wireframe, X-ray.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMap layers plotted, on sheets&#039; viewports and in the 3D tab; pictures from files saved with the drawing; a drawing&#039;s location written for other programs in DWG 2000–2007 and DXF R12\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMap layers show under the model space on screen; the system and the layer list are saved with the drawing: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/map-layers\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/map-layers\"\u003EMap layers under the drawing\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EE57 scans over 6 million points or 512 MB, scanner makers&#039; own files (RCP, RCS, FLS, LGS, TZF, ZFS), a point cloud saved with the drawing or plotted, snapping to a cloud on the 3D tab, upright (section) slices on the Model tab\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExport big or makers&#039; own scans as LAS or LAZ (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/point-cloud-formats\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/point-cloud-formats\"\u003EPoint cloud formats\u003C/a\u003E); attach the cloud each time you open the drawing; trace from a plan slice on the Model tab: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/point-clouds\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/point-clouds\"\u003ETrace as-built lines over a point cloud\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERegistering scans: finding targets (spheres, checkerboards) in a scan by itself, solving all scans together (bundle adjustment), scale, registering against a model\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPick matching points or type target coordinates, then refine one scan at a time with ICP: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/register-scans\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/register-scans\"\u003ERegister several scans into one\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"routines-and-customizing\"\u003ERoutines and customizing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECompiled or protected LISP (.fas, .vlx), native programs (.arx, .crx, .dbx) and VBA (.dvb)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELoad a routine&#039;s .lsp source with \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EAPPLOAD\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELISP&#039;s \u003Ccode\u003Egrread\u003C/code\u003E, reactors (\u003Ccode\u003Evlr-…\u003C/code\u003E), curve functions (\u003Ccode\u003Evlax-curve-…\u003C/code\u003E), dictionaries and extended data, the Express Tools&#039; \u003Ccode\u003Eacet-…\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe rest of the language runs: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/run-lisp\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/run-lisp\"\u003ERun LISP routines\u003C/a\u003E. The command line names a missing function when a routine reaches one.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EImages and slides in a routine&#039;s dialog\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThey show as blank frames; the rest of the dialog works.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECraftScript (.craft), Lua and Python in CadCraft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERun them in PartCraft&#039;s Script workspace.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELayer, color and linetype lists inside the ribbon menu styles\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUse the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayers\u003C/span\u003E panel and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe Startup Suite and aliases kept on your computer or synced\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThey are kept in this browser; the desktop app and the cloud service will keep them for you. In Studio, \u003Ccode\u003Eacad.lsp\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003Eacaddoc.lsp\u003C/code\u003E are read from a support folder you choose (Load applications).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"help\"\u003EHelp\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe help describes this build. When something is added, it moves out of this page and into the walkthroughs.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"CadCraft is in development. This page lists what the current build does not do, so you do not hunt for it. Each item says what to do instead today. Files and saving Not built yet Instead, today A CadCraft DWG checked in AutoCAD itself Every saved version is read back by three other programs' DWG readers and by CadCraft. See What a DWG keeps and changes. Saving DXF newer than 2000 Save as DXF 2000, or as DWG up to AutoCAD 2018. Saving over the opened file in the browser Every save downloads a new copy; replace the old file yourself if you want to. In CadCraft Studio, Ctrl+S writes back to the file you opened. Cloud saving and sync between computers CadCraft does not send its saves to the cloud yet: keep your DWG and DXF files in your own folders. In CadCraft Studio, History lists the cloud versions of a drawing saved from elsewhere and copies of your own saves on this computer. Overlays, and editing a linked drawing in place Attach the drawing as a link and reload it after changing the other drawing. A link stays a link when you save as DWG, and XCLIP clips it. Pictures in DXF; wipeouts that hide what is under them DXF keeps a picture as its frame with the file name; DWG keeps it as a picture — see Pictures in a drawing. A wipeout shows as its frame. Editing multileaders that have a block, several leader lines or curved lines They are drawn as the other program drew them and move as one. Text multileaders come in editable. Tables in a DXF Counted in \"About this file\", not shown. Tables in a DWG are shown as the other program drew them. Binary DXF Save it as text (ASCII) DXF in the program that made it. Copy and paste with other programs CadCraft's clipboard works between CadCraft drawings and tabs in the same browser, not with other programs. Save as DWG or DXF and open it there. See Copy, paste and duplicate. Drawing and editing Not built yet Instead, today Hatches tied to their boundary (associative), gradient fills, and patterns beyond the standard set Hatch again after changing the outline. Opened hatches with other patterns show as a tone. See Hatch and fill areas. Linetypes with shapes or text in them, and lineweights in a saved DXF R12 Shapes and text in a file's linetype are left out of its dashes; save as DWG to keep lineweights. Patterned hatches in a saved DXF R12 has no hatch: they are saved as their outlines. Save as DWG to keep the pattern. Associative arrays (edited later as one object) and arrays along a path ARRAY makes plain copies: undo and run it again to change it. STRETCH with a crossing polygon Use one or more crossing windows. PEDIT Edit vertex (one vertex at a time at the command line) Grips, and the vertex browser in Properties (every vertex, its widths and bulge). The rest of PEDIT is built: see Splines and polylines. BREAK, LENGTHEN, OFFSET, TRIM, EXTEND, FILLET and JOIN on ellipses and splines EXPLODE a spline (or PEDIT it) to get a polyline these commands take. Fillet and chamfer between an arc segment of a polyline and the segment next to it; fillet joining a line into a polyline EXPLODE the polyline, then fillet the pieces. The fillet arc is added as its own object. Trim and extend: the Project option (3D) and extending a polyline's arc end Trim in the plane of the drawing; for an arc end, explode the polyline or draw the arc again. OLE objects: inserting them, running or editing them; showing the ones in a DWG where the file puts them; metafile pictures See Pictures. A DXF's objects show as their bitmap or a labelled frame; a DWG's are kept, not drawn in place (OLELIST lists them and places a copy of a picture where you say). Typing formatting codes (bold, stacked fractions, colors per word) in paragraph text; fonts other than the one Helvetica-style font; columns Formatting that came in a file is kept, drawn and saved (see Text); new paragraph text has one height and color. Paragraph text edited at its rotation The editor opens level; the text keeps its rotation. Multileaders saved as multileader objects in a DXF; several leader lines on one multileader; block content A DWG keeps them as multileaders; an R12 DXF keeps what they draw (lines, arrowhead and text). Tables Lines and text. Attribute definitions kept on their own in a saved file (outside a block), multiple-line attributes Put attribute definitions into a block before saving: on their own they save as text showing the tag. Layer states (saving and restoring which layers are on) Use LAYISO / LAYUNISO, or turn layers on and off by hand. Window polygon and crossing polygon selection, and saved selection filters Fence (F), Window, Crossing, Remove (R), QSELECT for a rule. The Properties panel Not built yet Instead, today Drawing the Fit choices, and the horizontal text position and text below the line on dimensions other than linear and aligned They are kept on the object, shown in Properties and saved; CadCraft places the text by its own rule. Materials that other programs see A saved file keeps the material name for CadCraft; other programs show the layer's material. Transparency in DWG 2000 and DXF files Other programs show those objects solid; CadCraft keeps the transparency. Save as DWG 2004 or later for everyone. An associative hatch in other programs The hatch is saved; CadCraft keeps the setting, other programs see a hatch that does not follow its boundary. Coordinates in Properties in the UCS Properties shows world coordinates; the status bar and ID show UCS ones. See Turn the view and use your own coordinates. Annotative scaling The annotative setting is kept with text; the annotation scale is always 1:1. (The drawing's scale list is edited with SCALELISTEDIT and saved; it does not drive annotation yet.) Changing a dynamic block's distances, angles or flips; dynamic blocks kept dynamic when you save in another DWG version or as DXF Switch its visibility state in Properties › Custom; its other values show there read only. It stays dynamic when you save it as DWG in the version the drawing came in. See Dynamic blocks. Spline fit tolerance, spline degree 6 and above, 3D splines Fit and control points, degree 1 to 5 and weights are built: see Splines and polylines. A spline's height (Z) is not kept. A hatch that follows its boundary (associative) The associative setting is kept and saved; hatch again after changing the outline. Precision aids Not built yet Instead, today Tracking from points across commands, and the tracking settings dialog (relative polar angles, extra angles) POLARANG sets the step; acquire the points again in the next command. Snap to grid Type coordinates; the grid is a visual guide only. Arc and Length options while picking an area's corners, and MEASUREGEOM Volume Draw the outline with PLINE (arcs included), then AREA O. Dynamic input for grips, and its options list (the down arrow) The command line shows the options. Angles in degrees-minutes-seconds, grads or surveyor's bearings Type decimal degrees: 30.5 for 30°30'. A view manager dialog; named views of a sheet that come back on that sheet NAMEDVIEW lists and restores views. A saved file keeps them, but a sheet's views come back on the first sheet. Sheets, dimensions and plotting Not built yet Instead, today A title block that updates itself Edit its texts by hand; the SCALE field does not follow viewport changes. Saving which dimension style is current; dimension text dragged to a free place; annotative dimension scales A drawing opens with Standard current. Text sits on the dimension line (DIMTEDIT Left / Right / Center). See Set dimension sizes. Ordinate dimensions, and editable two-line angular dimensions from opened files They are shown as drawn in the file. Annotative scaling Set sizes for one plot scale with DIMSCALE and text heights. Checking the CTB reader against a file from another program (it is built, from the published description), named plot styles (STB), dither, pen numbers, grayscale from a CTB and adaptive linetype adjustment in a plot style Make the table in Plot style tables (color, gray, ink, lineweight, linetype, line end and join, and fill style per color): see Plot style tables. Page setups Choose paper, scale and plot style in the Plot to PDF dialog; a sheet keeps its plot style. Arabic, Hebrew, Thai, Hindi and other scripts in PDFs, and rare ideographs (European scripts, Cyrillic, Greek, Chinese, Japanese and Korean print) They print as \"?\". Pictures inside an attached PDF page, in a plot The page's lines, fills and text print. A page that is only a picture goes in with SCANATTACH, and pictures print. See PDF underlays. Sending a job to a laser from CadCraft Studio (Studio has no Web Serial) Save the G-code and send it with your laser's own program, or send it from Chrome or Edge. See Send a drawing to a laser. Constraints and parameters Not built yet Instead, today Constraints on sheets, in blocks, on ellipses, splines or text Constrain on the Model tab, on lines, polylines, circles, arcs and points. Smooth (G2), and constraining a polyline's arc bulge directly Tangent and equal; an arc segment follows its two ends. Reference (driven) dimensional constraints, and 3Point in DCANGULAR A plain dimension (DIMLINEAR …) shows a value without driving it. Dimensional constraints of a fixed size on paper, and plotting them They show on screen only; add plain dimensions for the plot. Constraints kept through EXPLODE, BREAK, JOIN and other commands that make new objects, and on trimmed polylines Constrain them again (AUTOCONSTRAIN helps). MIRROR and TRIM of lines, arcs and circles keep them. Design tables (configurations) in CadCraft Change parameters in the panel. A tested round trip with SolidWorks Check the sketch the macro builds before you rely on it. 3D Not built yet Instead, today Fillets, chamfers, shells and lofts; sweeps along a path that rises (3D paths) Extrude outlines with arcs, push and pull faces, revolve and sweep along flat paths. Extrude in another direction than up Draw the shape on the ground and extrude it up or down (with a taper or along a path if you like); rotate the solid afterwards. Elevation and thickness of 2D objects The drawing lies on the ground (height 0) on the 3D tab. Editing solids, regions and bodies from other programs; showing those inside blocks; their faces on swept, lofted or other freeform surfaces; polyface and polygon meshes Model-space solids show on the 3D tab as they are and are saved back unchanged in DWG; what is not drawn is listed when the 3D tab opens. Ask for meshes as 3D faces in model space. See Solids from other programs. Copy and paste of solids On the 3D tab, use COPY. The clipboard holds 2D objects only. Sections, views that follow later changes to the solids, and shaded solids on plotted sheets VIEWBASE places flat views (a snapshot); run it again after changing the solids. Materials, textures and lighting settings The visual styles: shaded, edges, hidden line, wireframe, X-ray. Map layers plotted, on sheets' viewports and in the 3D tab; pictures from files saved with the drawing; a drawing's location written for other programs in DWG 2000–2007 and DXF R12 Map layers show under the model space on screen; the system and the layer list are saved with the drawing: see Map layers under the drawing E57 scans over 6 million points or 512 MB, scanner makers' own files (RCP, RCS, FLS, LGS, TZF, ZFS), a point cloud saved with the drawing or plotted, snapping to a cloud on the 3D tab, upright (section) slices on the Model tab Export big or makers' own scans as LAS or LAZ (Point cloud formats); attach the cloud each time you open the drawing; trace from a plan slice on the Model tab: see Trace as-built lines over a point cloud Registering scans: finding targets (spheres, checkerboards) in a scan by itself, solving all scans together (bundle adjustment), scale, registering against a model Pick matching points or type target coordinates, then refine one scan at a time with ICP: see Register several scans into one Routines and customizing Not built yet Instead, today Compiled or protected LISP (.fas, .vlx), native programs (.arx, .crx, .dbx) and VBA (.dvb) Load a routine's .lsp source with APPLOAD. LISP's grread, reactors (vlr-…), curve functions (vlax-curve-…), dictionaries and extended data, the Express Tools' acet-… The rest of the language runs: see Run LISP routines. The command line names a missing function when a routine reaches one. Images and slides in a routine's dialog They show as blank frames; the rest of the dialog works. CraftScript (.craft), Lua and Python in CadCraft Run them in PartCraft's Script workspace. Layer, color and linetype lists inside the ribbon menu styles Use the Layers panel and Properties. The Startup Suite and aliases kept on your computer or synced They are kept in this browser; the desktop app and the cloud service will keep them for you. In Studio, acad.lsp and acaddoc.lsp are read from a support folder you choose (Load applications). Help The help describes this build. When something is added, it moves out of this page and into the walkthroughs.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"dwg-in-other-programs","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Open a CadCraft DWG in other programs","summary":"Which programs a DWG saved by CadCraft has been opened in, what is checked, and what to do when one complains.","section":"trouble","order":30,"keywords":["dwg","open in other programs","compatibility","does it open","audit","trueview","other cad programs","dxf","check","save as dwg"],"headings":[{"id":"what-cadcraft-writes","text":"What CadCraft writes","level":2},{"id":"where-it-has-been-opened","text":"Where it has been opened","level":2},{"id":"when-another-program-complains","text":"When another program complains","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-cadcraft-writes\"\u003EWhat CadCraft writes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E writes a real DWG, in the version the drawing came in or the one you choose in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as\u003C/span\u003E: AutoCAD 2000, 2004, 2007, 2010, 2013 or 2018. Nothing is converted behind your back: the history lists anything that changed on the way (for example a true colour written as the nearest standard colour in a 2000 DWG).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"where-it-has-been-opened\"\u003EWhere it has been opened\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery DWG CadCraft writes is opened, in every version, by three programs that are not CadCraft: the ODA File Converter (which checks the file and repairs nothing in it), LibreDWG and libdxfrw. Their reading is compared with CadCraft&#039;s: objects, layers, blocks and attributes, dimensions, hatches, sheets and their viewports.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpening CadCraft&#039;s DWG files in AutoCAD and in the free DWG TrueView is being checked on Windows, object kind by object kind (AUDIT, layers, sheets, dimensions, hatches, attributes, dynamic blocks, clipped references, pictures). The results will be listed here when they are in.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-another-program-complains\"\u003EWhen another program complains\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESave again as DXF (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDXF 2000\u003C/span\u003E) and open that: most programs read DXF.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESave the DWG in an older version (2013 or 2010).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETell us on Discord which program and version, and what it said; the file helps most.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E","text":"What CadCraft writes Save writes a real DWG, in the version the drawing came in or the one you choose in Save as: AutoCAD 2000, 2004, 2007, 2010, 2013 or 2018. Nothing is converted behind your back: the history lists anything that changed on the way (for example a true colour written as the nearest standard colour in a 2000 DWG). Where it has been opened Every DWG CadCraft writes is opened, in every version, by three programs that are not CadCraft: the ODA File Converter (which checks the file and repairs nothing in it), LibreDWG and libdxfrw. Their reading is compared with CadCraft's: objects, layers, blocks and attributes, dimensions, hatches, sheets and their viewports. Opening CadCraft's DWG files in AutoCAD and in the free DWG TrueView is being checked on Windows, object kind by object kind (AUDIT, layers, sheets, dimensions, hatches, attributes, dynamic blocks, clipped references, pictures). The results will be listed here when they are in. When another program complains Save again as DXF (Save as › DXF 2000) and open that: most programs read DXF. Save the DWG in an older version (2013 or 2010). Tell us on Discord which program and version, and what it said; the file helps most.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"help","scope":"basics","title":"Slik bruker du hjelpen","summary":"Åpne hjelpen, søk i den og se trinnene for datamaskinen din.","section":"start","order":10,"keywords":["hjelp","f1","søk","veiledninger","håndbok"],"headings":[{"id":"pne-hjelpen","text":"Åpne hjelpen","level":2},{"id":"finn-et-emne","text":"Finn et emne","level":2},{"id":"trinn-for-datamaskinen-din","text":"Trinn for datamaskinen din","level":2},{"id":"samme-hjelp-p-nettstedet","text":"Samme hjelp på nettstedet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EHver app har sin egen hjelp. Den er en del av appen, så den åpnes uten tilkobling og beskriver alltid versjonen du kjører.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"pne-hjelpen\"\u003EÅpne hjelpen\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETrykk \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, eller velg \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHjelp\u003C/span\u003E i linjen øverst i appen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETrykk \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E eller velg \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELukk\u003C/span\u003E for å legge den bort. Tegningen din blir som den var.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPå de fleste Mac-tastaturer styrer den øverste raden lysstyrke og lyd. Trykk \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E sammen med \u003Cstrong\u003Efn\u003C/strong\u003E-tasten (eller globustasten), eller bruk knappen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHjelp\u003C/span\u003E. Se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/keys#function-keys-on-a-mac\" data-topic=\"basics/keys\" data-hash=\"function-keys-on-a-mac\"\u003ETaster på en Mac\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"finn-et-emne\"\u003EFinn et emne\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESkriv i søkefeltet øverst i hjelpen. Resultatene oppdateres mens du skriver og omfatter andre programmers kommandonavn, så skriver du det du ville skrevet andre steder, finner du som regel riktig side.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEmnene er gruppert på samme måte i alle apper: komme i gang, steg-for-steg-veiledninger, hvis du kommer fra annen programvare, referanse og feilsøking.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELenker i et emne åpner neste emne i samme panel. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETilbake\u003C/span\u003E tar deg tilbake dit du var.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"trinn-for-datamaskinen-din\"\u003ETrinn for datamaskinen din\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENoen trinn er ulike på Mac, Windows og Linux, mellom nettlesere og mellom å være tilkoblet og frakoblet. Hjelpen finner ut hva som gjelder deg og viser de trinnene. Linjen øverst i et emne sier hva den valgte, for eksempel «Viser trinn for Mac, Chrome eller Edge, tilkoblet».\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EVelg \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEndre\u003C/span\u003E på den linjen for å velge en annen datamaskin eller nettleser, for eksempel for å hjelpe en kollega.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EVelg \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EVis alle\u003C/span\u003E for å se alle versjoner av alle trinn, hver med etikett.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHurtigtaster skrives slik tastaturet ditt merker dem: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E leses som ⌘S på en Mac og Ctrl+S i Windows og Linux.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"samme-hjelp-p-nettstedet\"\u003ESamme hjelp på nettstedet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENettstedet viser de samme emnene på getcadcraft.com/tutorials, med trinnene for hver plattform. I appen åpner \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EÅpne på nettstedet\u003C/span\u003E nederst i et emne den siden når du er tilkoblet.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Hver app har sin egen hjelp. Den er en del av appen, så den åpnes uten tilkobling og beskriver alltid versjonen du kjører. Åpne hjelpen Trykk F1, eller velg Hjelp i linjen øverst i appen. Trykk Esc eller velg Lukk for å legge den bort. Tegningen din blir som den var. På de fleste Mac-tastaturer styrer den øverste raden lysstyrke og lyd. Trykk F1 sammen med fn-tasten (eller globustasten), eller bruk knappen Hjelp. Se Taster på en Mac. Finn et emne Skriv i søkefeltet øverst i hjelpen. Resultatene oppdateres mens du skriver og omfatter andre programmers kommandonavn, så skriver du det du ville skrevet andre steder, finner du som regel riktig side. Emnene er gruppert på samme måte i alle apper: komme i gang, steg-for-steg-veiledninger, hvis du kommer fra annen programvare, referanse og feilsøking. Lenker i et emne åpner neste emne i samme panel. Tilbake tar deg tilbake dit du var. Trinn for datamaskinen din Noen trinn er ulike på Mac, Windows og Linux, mellom nettlesere og mellom å være tilkoblet og frakoblet. Hjelpen finner ut hva som gjelder deg og viser de trinnene. Linjen øverst i et emne sier hva den valgte, for eksempel «Viser trinn for Mac, Chrome eller Edge, tilkoblet». Velg Endre på den linjen for å velge en annen datamaskin eller nettleser, for eksempel for å hjelpe en kollega. Velg Vis alle for å se alle versjoner av alle trinn, hver med etikett. Hurtigtaster skrives slik tastaturet ditt merker dem: Ctrl+S leses som ⌘S på en Mac og Ctrl+S i Windows og Linux. Samme hjelp på nettstedet Nettstedet viser de samme emnene på getcadcraft.com/tutorials, med trinnene for hver plattform. I appen åpner Åpne på nettstedet nederst i et emne den siden når du er tilkoblet.","lang":"nb"},{"slug":"browsers","scope":"basics","title":"Nettlesere og datamaskiner","summary":"Hvilke nettlesere appene kjører i, hva som skiller dem og hva datamaskinen din trenger.","section":"start","order":20,"keywords":["chrome","edge","firefox","safari","brave","nettleser","mac","windows","linux","ipad","nettbrett","webgl","krav","minne"],"headings":[{"id":"hvilken-nettleser","text":"Hvilken nettleser","level":2},{"id":"hva-som-skiller-nettleserne","text":"Hva som skiller nettleserne","level":2},{"id":"mac-windows-og-linux","text":"Mac, Windows og Linux","level":2},{"id":"nettbrett-og-ber-ringsskjermer","text":"Nettbrett og berøringsskjermer","level":2},{"id":"eldste-nettleser-og-system","text":"Eldste nettleser og system","level":2},{"id":"hva-datamaskinen-din-trenger","text":"Hva datamaskinen din trenger","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EAppene kjører i en nettleser. De gjør jobben på din egen datamaskin: filene dine åpnes der og sendes aldri til oss (se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/your-files\" data-topic=\"basics/your-files\"\u003EHvor filene dine havner\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"hvilken-nettleser\"\u003EHvilken nettleser\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBruk en oppdatert versjon av en av disse:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENettleser\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMotor\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMerknader\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChrome, Edge, Brave, Opera, Vivaldi\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChromium\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDen mest komplette: valg av mappe, lagring for frakoblet bruk, filhåndtering.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFirefox\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGecko\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFungerer. Å åpne en hel mappe bruker en enklere velger. Frakoblet lagring fungerer kanskje ikke i private vinduer.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESafari\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWebKit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFungerer på Mac og iPad. Å åpne en hel mappe bruker en enklere velger. Safari kan slette en frakoblet kopi du ikke har brukt på noen uker.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAlle tre motorene fungerer fint til hverdagsbruk. Der en app gjør noe annerledes i en av dem, viser hjelpen trinnene for nettleseren din.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"hva-som-skiller-nettleserne\"\u003EHva som skiller nettleserne\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EÅ åpne en mappe.\u003C/strong\u003E Chromium-nettlesere viser en ekte mappevelger og kan lese mappen direkte. Firefox og Safari ber deg velge en mappe og leser deretter hver fil i den, noe som tar lengre tid for store prosjektmapper; å dra mappen til appen fungerer i alle tre.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELagring.\u003C/strong\u003E Appene lagrer ved å laste ned en fil. Nettleseren din avgjør hvor nedlastinger havner og om den spør først.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:chromium\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EChrome or Edge\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EI Chrome eller Edge slår du på «Spør hvor hver fil skal lagres før nedlasting» (i Innstillinger, under Nedlastinger) for å velge mappe hver gang.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:firefox\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EFirefox\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EI Firefox åpner du Innstillinger, deretter Generelt, deretter Filer og programmer, og velger «Spør alltid hvor filer skal lagres» for å velge mappe hver gang.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ESafari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EI Safari åpner du Innstillinger, deretter Generelt, og setter «Filnedlastingsmappe» til «Spør for hver nedlasting» for å velge mappe hver gang.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mac-windows-og-linux\"\u003EMac, Windows og Linux\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAppene er like på alle datamaskiner. Det som endres, er tastaturet: hurtigtaster skrives automatisk for din datamaskin. Se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/keys\" data-topic=\"basics/keys\"\u003ETaster, mus og styreflater\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"nettbrett-og-ber-ringsskjermer\"\u003ENettbrett og berøringsskjermer\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENoen apper tar imot berøring (ett finger tegner eller velger, to fingre klyper for å zoome og panorere). Hver apps hjelp sier om den gjør det. På en iPad bruker du Safari eller Chrome. Et tastatur hjelper i apper som bruker skrevne kommandoer.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"eldste-nettleser-og-system\"\u003EEldste nettleser og system\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDette er lest ut av koden i 0.7-appene, ikke gjettet: hver fil ble analysert, og hver språkfunksjon den bruker, ble slått opp i nettleserprodusentenes publiserte kompatibilitetsdata. Under disse versjonene kan appene ikke engang starte, fordi nettleseren ikke forstår koden.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003E\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EEldste versjon som kan starte appene\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChrome, Edge, Brave, Opera, Vivaldi\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChromium 94\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFirefox\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E93\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESafari (Mac, iPad, iPhone)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E16.4 (macOS 13.3, iPadOS og iOS 16.4)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECadCraft Studio har sin egen nettmotor i Windows, så det krever Windows 10 eller 11; på en Mac bruker det systemets, så det krever macOS 13.3 eller nyere. Innstillinger › Oppdateringer i Studio viser hva din kopi kjører på.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDette er den eldste versjonen som \u003Cem\u003Estarter\u003C/em\u003E, lest ut av koden. Det er ingen liste over gamle nettlesere vi har kjørt appene i, så en eldre som starter, kan likevel mangle en funksjon.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"hva-datamaskinen-din-trenger\"\u003EHva datamaskinen din trenger\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDette er planlagte minstekrav til appene, ikke målte krav:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E8 GB minne (16 GB anbefales for store føderte modeller).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEn grafikkbrikke med WebGL 2 for 3D-appene. Hvis en 3D-visning forblir tom, sjekk at maskinvareakselerasjon er slått på i innstillingene til nettleseren.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEn skjerm som er minst 1280 piksler bred for de fullstendige verktøylinjene; mindre skjermer bryter dem over flere rader.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESvært store filer (store tegningssett, store modeller) bruker mye minne i en nettleserfane. Lukk andre tunge faner hvis en app blir treg.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Appene kjører i en nettleser. De gjør jobben på din egen datamaskin: filene dine åpnes der og sendes aldri til oss (se Hvor filene dine havner). Hvilken nettleser Bruk en oppdatert versjon av en av disse: Nettleser Motor Merknader Chrome, Edge, Brave, Opera, Vivaldi Chromium Den mest komplette: valg av mappe, lagring for frakoblet bruk, filhåndtering. Firefox Gecko Fungerer. Å åpne en hel mappe bruker en enklere velger. Frakoblet lagring fungerer kanskje ikke i private vinduer. Safari WebKit Fungerer på Mac og iPad. Å åpne en hel mappe bruker en enklere velger. Safari kan slette en frakoblet kopi du ikke har brukt på noen uker. Alle tre motorene fungerer fint til hverdagsbruk. Der en app gjør noe annerledes i en av dem, viser hjelpen trinnene for nettleseren din. Hva som skiller nettleserne Å åpne en mappe. Chromium-nettlesere viser en ekte mappevelger og kan lese mappen direkte. Firefox og Safari ber deg velge en mappe og leser deretter hver fil i den, noe som tar lengre tid for store prosjektmapper; å dra mappen til appen fungerer i alle tre. Lagring. Appene lagrer ved å laste ned en fil. Nettleseren din avgjør hvor nedlastinger havner og om den spør først. I Chrome eller Edge slår du på «Spør hvor hver fil skal lagres før nedlasting» (i Innstillinger, under Nedlastinger) for å velge mappe hver gang. I Firefox åpner du Innstillinger, deretter Generelt, deretter Filer og programmer, og velger «Spør alltid hvor filer skal lagres» for å velge mappe hver gang. I Safari åpner du Innstillinger, deretter Generelt, og setter «Filnedlastingsmappe» til «Spør for hver nedlasting» for å velge mappe hver gang. Mac, Windows og Linux Appene er like på alle datamaskiner. Det som endres, er tastaturet: hurtigtaster skrives automatisk for din datamaskin. Se Taster, mus og styreflater. Nettbrett og berøringsskjermer Noen apper tar imot berøring (ett finger tegner eller velger, to fingre klyper for å zoome og panorere). Hver apps hjelp sier om den gjør det. På en iPad bruker du Safari eller Chrome. Et tastatur hjelper i apper som bruker skrevne kommandoer. Eldste nettleser og system Dette er lest ut av koden i 0.7-appene, ikke gjettet: hver fil ble analysert, og hver språkfunksjon den bruker, ble slått opp i nettleserprodusentenes publiserte kompatibilitetsdata. Under disse versjonene kan appene ikke engang starte, fordi nettleseren ikke forstår koden. Eldste versjon som kan starte appene Chrome, Edge, Brave, Opera, Vivaldi Chromium 94 Firefox 93 Safari (Mac, iPad, iPhone) 16.4 (macOS 13.3, iPadOS og iOS 16.4) CadCraft Studio har sin egen nettmotor i Windows, så det krever Windows 10 eller 11; på en Mac bruker det systemets, så det krever macOS 13.3 eller nyere. Innstillinger › Oppdateringer i Studio viser hva din kopi kjører på. Dette er den eldste versjonen som starter, lest ut av koden. Det er ingen liste over gamle nettlesere vi har kjørt appene i, så en eldre som starter, kan likevel mangle en funksjon. Hva datamaskinen din trenger Dette er planlagte minstekrav til appene, ikke målte krav: 8 GB minne (16 GB anbefales for store føderte modeller). En grafikkbrikke med WebGL 2 for 3D-appene. Hvis en 3D-visning forblir tom, sjekk at maskinvareakselerasjon er slått på i innstillingene til nettleseren. En skjerm som er minst 1280 piksler bred for de fullstendige verktøylinjene; mindre skjermer bryter dem over flere rader. Svært store filer (store tegningssett, store modeller) bruker mye minne i en nettleserfane. Lukk andre tunge faner hvis en app blir treg.","lang":"nb"},{"slug":"keys","scope":"basics","title":"Taster, mus og styreflater","summary":"Hvordan hurtigtaster skrives, funksjonstaster på en Mac, høyreklikk og panorering uten mus.","section":"start","order":30,"keywords":["tastatur","hurtigtast","ctrl","command","cmd","option","fn","funksjonstaster","styreflate","høyreklikk","midtknapp","magic mouse"],"headings":[{"id":"hvordan-hurtigtaster-skrives","text":"Hvordan hurtigtaster skrives","level":2},{"id":"funksjonstaster-p-en-mac","text":"Funksjonstaster på en Mac","level":2},{"id":"hurtigtaster-nettleseren-beholder","text":"Hurtigtaster nettleseren beholder","level":2},{"id":"musknapper-p-en-styreflate","text":"Musknapper på en styreflate","level":2},{"id":"skrive-i-et-tekstfelt","text":"Å skrive i et tekstfelt","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"hvordan-hurtigtaster-skrives\"\u003EHvordan hurtigtaster skrives\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHjelpen skriver hver hurtigtast slik tastaturet ditt merker den.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESkrevet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EPå en Mac\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EI Windows og Linux\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECommand (⌘) og S\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECtrl og S\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EControl (⌃), Shift (⇧) og F\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECtrl, Shift og F\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option ←\"\u003E⌥←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOption (⌥) og ←\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAlt og ←\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDelete-tasten\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBackspace\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Efn og Delete (slett forover)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDelete\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EReturn\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEnter\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEt plusstegn betyr at tastene holdes inne samtidig. «Deretter» betyr at man trykker på én etter én.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDe fleste hurtigtastene i appene godtar enten Command eller Control på en Mac, så \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E fungerer begge veier. Noen, for eksempel fullskjerm (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), bruker Control-tasten på alle datamaskiner; hjelpen sier fra der det gjelder.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"funksjonstaster-p-en-mac\"\u003EFunksjonstaster på en Mac\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPå de fleste Mac-tastaturer endrer den øverste raden lysstyrke, volum og medier. Slik sender du F1 til F12:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ehold inne \u003Cstrong\u003Efn\u003C/strong\u003E (eller globustasten) mens du trykker på tasten, eller\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eåpne \u003Cstrong\u003ESysteminnstillinger\u003C/strong\u003E, deretter \u003Cstrong\u003ETastatur\u003C/strong\u003E, deretter \u003Cstrong\u003EHurtigtaster\u003C/strong\u003E, deretter \u003Cstrong\u003EFunksjonstaster\u003C/strong\u003E, og slå på «Bruk F1, F2 osv. som standard funksjonstaster».\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENoen F-taster er også opptatt av macOS selv (for eksempel Mission Control). Hvis en tast ikke gjør noe i appen, prøv den med fn, eller bruk knappen i verktøylinjen i stedet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"hurtigtaster-nettleseren-beholder\"\u003EHurtigtaster nettleseren beholder\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEn nettside kan ikke ta noen hurtigtaster fra nettleseren, uansett hva den gjør. I alle apper gjelder:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command N\"\u003E⌘N\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+N\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command T\"\u003E⌘T\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+T\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E og \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command W\"\u003E⌘W\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+W\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E åpner eller lukker alltid nettleservinduer og faner. Appene bruker knapper for Ny og Lukk i stedet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Q\"\u003E⌘Q\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Q\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E på en Mac og \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option F4\"\u003E⌥ F4\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+F4\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E i Windows avslutter nettleseren. Hvis du har ulagret arbeid, spør nettleseren først («Forlate nettstedet?»); velg å bli, og lagre deretter.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENettleserens egen fullskjerm (\u003Cspan class=\"hp\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:mac\" title=\"Mac\"\u003EControl-Command-F, or the green button on the window\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:windows,linux\" title=\"Windows or Linux\"\u003EF11\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/span\u003E) fungerer fortsatt. Appene har også egne skjermmodi på \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, som også kan skjule appens linjer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"musknapper-p-en-styreflate\"\u003EMusknapper på en styreflate\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAppene bruker høyre knapp og midtknappen. Uten en mus med tre knapper:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EHøyreklikk:\u003C/strong\u003E klikk med to fingre. Slå på «Sekundærklikk» i \u003Cstrong\u003ESysteminnstillinger\u003C/strong\u003E, deretter \u003Cstrong\u003EStyreflate\u003C/strong\u003E, hvis tofingerklikk ikke gjør noe. Hvis du holder inne \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E mens du klikker, åpnes nettleserens meny, men inne i en tegning tolker noen apper det som et vanlig klikk (med Control inne), så bruk helst tofingerklikket.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDra med midtknappen (panorer):\u003C/strong\u003E hver app tilbyr en annen måte å panorere på, for eksempel å holde inne \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E mens du drar eller å dra med høyre knapp. Appens tastaturside sier hvilken.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EZoom med hjulet:\u003C/strong\u003E rulling med to fingre på styreflaten zoomer i de fleste apper. Klyping zoomer også der appen støtter det.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:windows,linux\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EWindows or Linux\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EHøyreklikk:\u003C/strong\u003E klikk med to fingre, eller klikk i det nedre høyre hjørnet av pekeplaten. Det avhenger av innstillingene for pekeplaten din.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDra med midtknappen (panorer):\u003C/strong\u003E hver app tilbyr en annen måte å panorere på, for eksempel å holde inne \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E mens du drar eller å dra med høyre knapp. Appens tastaturside sier hvilken.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EZoom med hjulet:\u003C/strong\u003E rulling med to fingre på pekeplaten zoomer i de fleste apper.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"skrive-i-et-tekstfelt\"\u003EÅ skrive i et tekstfelt\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMens du skriver i et felt (et navn, et notat, et søk), er appens enbokstavs hurtigtaster slått av, så skriver du «L», blir det en L. Trykk \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E eller klikk på tegningen for å gi tastene tilbake til appen.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Hvordan hurtigtaster skrives Hjelpen skriver hver hurtigtast slik tastaturet ditt merker den. Skrevet På en Mac I Windows og Linux Ctrl+S Command (⌘) og S Ctrl og S Ctrl+Shift+F Control (⌃), Shift (⇧) og F Ctrl, Shift og F Alt+← Option (⌥) og ← Alt og ← Backspace Delete-tasten Backspace Delete fn og Delete (slett forover) Delete Enter Return Enter Et plusstegn betyr at tastene holdes inne samtidig. «Deretter» betyr at man trykker på én etter én. De fleste hurtigtastene i appene godtar enten Command eller Control på en Mac, så Ctrl+Z fungerer begge veier. Noen, for eksempel fullskjerm (Ctrl+Shift+F), bruker Control-tasten på alle datamaskiner; hjelpen sier fra der det gjelder. Funksjonstaster på en Mac På de fleste Mac-tastaturer endrer den øverste raden lysstyrke, volum og medier. Slik sender du F1 til F12: hold inne fn (eller globustasten) mens du trykker på tasten, eller åpne Systeminnstillinger, deretter Tastatur, deretter Hurtigtaster, deretter Funksjonstaster, og slå på «Bruk F1, F2 osv. som standard funksjonstaster». Noen F-taster er også opptatt av macOS selv (for eksempel Mission Control). Hvis en tast ikke gjør noe i appen, prøv den med fn, eller bruk knappen i verktøylinjen i stedet. Hurtigtaster nettleseren beholder En nettside kan ikke ta noen hurtigtaster fra nettleseren, uansett hva den gjør. I alle apper gjelder: Ctrl+N, Ctrl+T og Ctrl+W åpner eller lukker alltid nettleservinduer og faner. Appene bruker knapper for Ny og Lukk i stedet. Ctrl+Q på en Mac og Alt+F4 i Windows avslutter nettleseren. Hvis du har ulagret arbeid, spør nettleseren først («Forlate nettstedet?»); velg å bli, og lagre deretter. Nettleserens egen fullskjerm (Control-Command-F, or the green button on the window / F11) fungerer fortsatt. Appene har også egne skjermmodi på Ctrl+Shift+F, som også kan skjule appens linjer. Musknapper på en styreflate Appene bruker høyre knapp og midtknappen. Uten en mus med tre knapper: Høyreklikk: klikk med to fingre. Slå på «Sekundærklikk» i Systeminnstillinger, deretter Styreflate, hvis tofingerklikk ikke gjør noe. Hvis du holder inne Ctrl mens du klikker, åpnes nettleserens meny, men inne i en tegning tolker noen apper det som et vanlig klikk (med Control inne), så bruk helst tofingerklikket. Dra med midtknappen (panorer): hver app tilbyr en annen måte å panorere på, for eksempel å holde inne Space mens du drar eller å dra med høyre knapp. Appens tastaturside sier hvilken. Zoom med hjulet: rulling med to fingre på styreflaten zoomer i de fleste apper. Klyping zoomer også der appen støtter det. Høyreklikk: klikk med to fingre, eller klikk i det nedre høyre hjørnet av pekeplaten. Det avhenger av innstillingene for pekeplaten din. Dra med midtknappen (panorer): hver app tilbyr en annen måte å panorere på, for eksempel å holde inne Space mens du drar eller å dra med høyre knapp. Appens tastaturside sier hvilken. Zoom med hjulet: rulling med to fingre på pekeplaten zoomer i de fleste apper. Å skrive i et tekstfelt Mens du skriver i et felt (et navn, et notat, et søk), er appens enbokstavs hurtigtaster slått av, så skriver du «L», blir det en L. Trykk Esc eller klikk på tegningen for å gi tastene tilbake til appen.","lang":"nb"},{"slug":"find-tools","scope":"basics","title":"Finn et verktøy eller en innstilling","summary":"Trykk Ctrl+K og skriv noen bokstaver for å kjøre et verktøy, endre en innstilling eller åpne et hjelpeemne.","section":"start","order":32,"keywords":["finn","søk","kommandopalett","hurtigåpne","kjøre kommando","ctrl k","cmd k","verktøy","innstilling","hurtigtast"],"headings":[{"id":"finn-og-kj-r","text":"Finn og kjør","level":2},{"id":"greit-vite","text":"Greit å vite","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EHver app har et søkefelt for verktøyene sine, innstillingene sine og denne hjelpen. Du trenger ikke vite hvor et verktøy står i verktøylinjen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"finn-og-kj-r\"\u003EFinn og kjør\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETrykk \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command K\"\u003E⌘K\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+K\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, eller velg \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFinn\u003C/span\u003E i linjen øverst.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESkriv noen bokstaver av det du vil ha: «avrund», «paneltekst», «enheter». Hvert ord du skriver, må treffe, så legg til et andre ord for å snevre inn listen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETrykk \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for å kjøre det første treffet, eller flytt først med piltastene. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E lukker feltet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEt verktøy kjøres som om du hadde klikket på det i verktøylinjen. En innstilling åpner \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInnstillinger\u003C/span\u003E ved akkurat den innstillingen. Et hjelpeemne åpnes her, i hjelpepanelet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"greit-vite\"\u003EGreit å vite\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDet du brukte sist, kommer først neste gang, så et verktøy du bruker ofte, er ett tastetrykk unna.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EVerktøy som ikke kan kjøres akkurat nå, for eksempel før en fil er åpen, står i listen men er nedtonet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EVerktøyene i tilleggene er også med i listen, når tillegget er slått på.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Hver app har et søkefelt for verktøyene sine, innstillingene sine og denne hjelpen. Du trenger ikke vite hvor et verktøy står i verktøylinjen. Finn og kjør Trykk Ctrl+K, eller velg Finn i linjen øverst. Skriv noen bokstaver av det du vil ha: «avrund», «paneltekst», «enheter». Hvert ord du skriver, må treffe, så legg til et andre ord for å snevre inn listen. Trykk Enter for å kjøre det første treffet, eller flytt først med piltastene. Esc lukker feltet. Et verktøy kjøres som om du hadde klikket på det i verktøylinjen. En innstilling åpner Innstillinger ved akkurat den innstillingen. Et hjelpeemne åpnes her, i hjelpepanelet. Greit å vite Det du brukte sist, kommer først neste gang, så et verktøy du bruker ofte, er ett tastetrykk unna. Verktøy som ikke kan kjøres akkurat nå, for eksempel før en fil er åpen, står i listen men er nedtonet. Verktøyene i tilleggene er også med i listen, når tillegget er slått på.","lang":"nb"},{"slug":"arrange-panels","scope":"basics","title":"Ordne panelene og bruk en ekstra skjerm","summary":"Fest sidepanelene til venstre, høyre eller nederst, stable dem, gruppér dem som faner, fest dem til kanten eller skjul dem, flytt dem over tegningen, gjør layouten tettere, velg hvor mange linjer verktøylinjen bruker, eller flytt dem til eget vindu på en annen skjerm.","section":"start","order":35,"keywords":["panel","fest","dra","flytt panel","flytt","layout","faner","fanegruppe","stable","fest til kanten","auto-skjul","skjul panel","lukk panel","kompakt","layouttetthet","tett","verktøylinjerader","bryt","1 rad","2 rader","verktøylinjer","andre skjerm","to skjermer","dobbel skjerm","flere skjermer","vindu","sprettoppvindu","tilbakestill layout"],"headings":[{"id":"flytt-et-panel-i-vinduet","text":"Flytt et panel i vinduet","level":2},{"id":"grupp-r-paneler-som-faner","text":"Gruppér paneler som faner","level":2},{"id":"fest-til-kanten-skjul-og-hent-tilbake","text":"Fest til kanten, skjul og hent tilbake","level":2},{"id":"gj-r-layouten-tettere","text":"Gjør layouten tettere","level":2},{"id":"velg-hvor-mange-linjer-verkt-ylinjen-bruker","text":"Velg hvor mange linjer verktøylinjen bruker","level":2},{"id":"bruk-tastaturet","text":"Bruk tastaturet","level":2},{"id":"legg-panelene-p-en-ekstra-skjerm","text":"Legg panelene på en ekstra skjerm","level":2},{"id":"mer-enn-ett-panelvindu","text":"Mer enn ett panelvindu","level":2},{"id":"flytt-en-dialogboks","text":"Flytt en dialogboks","level":2},{"id":"sett-panelene-tilbake","text":"Sett panelene tilbake","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EPanelene ved siden av tegningen kan flyttes. Plasser dem der det passer deg, eller gi dem et eget vindu på en ekstra skjerm, slik at tegningen får hele den første skjermen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHvert panel har en tittellinje: en smal linje med panelets navn, en \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E-meny, en festeknapp og en lukkeknapp øverst på panelet. Versjoner før 0.7 har bare navnet og \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E-menyen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"flytt-et-panel-i-vinduet\"\u003EFlytt et panel i vinduet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDra panelet etter navnet øverst.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMens du drar, vises omrisser av områder på venstre, høyre og nederst i tegningen. Slipp panelet på ett av dem for å feste det der. Området du er over, blir mørkere.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESlipp det et annet sted over tegningen for at det skal flyte.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPaneler kan dele én side: de stables, og hver linje mellom to av dem kan dras for å dele plassen (når en linje har fokus, flytter piltastene den, og dobbeltklikk på den deler plassen likt). Dra den indre kanten av en festet side for å gjøre den bredere eller smalere; dobbeltklikk kanten for å sette den tilbake.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEt flytende panel blir værende over tegningen. Flytt det etter navnet, og endre størrelsen ved å dra nedre høyre hjørne (eller bruk \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E til hjørnet og piltastene). Dobbeltklikk på navnet for å feste det igjen der det var.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:touch\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ETouch\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDra med ett finger på panelets navn. Dra nedre høyre hjørne på et flytende panel med ett finger for å endre størrelsen, og kantene mellom festede paneler på samme måte. For å flyte eller feste uten å dra, bruk \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E-menyen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"grupp-r-paneler-som-faner\"\u003EGruppér paneler som faner\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo paneler kan dele én ramme og vise ett om gangen. Dra et panel etter navnet over tittellinjen til et annet panel (linjen markeres mens du er over den), og slipp: rammen får nå én fane for hvert panel, i tittellinjen. Klikk på en fane for å vise panelet. Når en fane har fokus, bytter \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"←\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E og \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"→\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E faner, og \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ←\"\u003Efn ←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EHome\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E og \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn →\"\u003Efn →\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnd\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E går til første og siste.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor å ta et panel ut av gruppen, velg \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFlytt ut av fanegruppen\u003C/span\u003E i panelets \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E-meny, eller dra det et annet sted. Velg \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELag fane med\u003C/span\u003E og et panels navn for å gruppere dem uten å dra.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"fest-til-kanten-skjul-og-hent-tilbake\"\u003EFest til kanten, skjul og hent tilbake\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFesteknappen i et panels tittellinje gjør det til \u003Cstrong\u003Eauto-skjul\u003C/strong\u003E: panelet forlater festet sitt og blir en liten fane på den kanten. Pek på fanen, gi den fokus eller klikk på den, så glir panelet ut over tegningen; flytt bort (eller trykk \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), så glir det tilbake. Trykk festeknappen igjen, eller velg \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELøsne\u003C/span\u003E, for å feste det for godt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELukkeknappen, eller \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESkjul dette panelet\u003C/span\u003E i menyen, skjuler panelet og lukker festet. En stiplet fane på kanten henter det frem igjen: klikk på den, eller gi den fokus og trykk \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"gj-r-layouten-tettere\"\u003EGjør layouten tettere\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInnstillinger\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGenerelt\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayouttetthet\u003C/span\u003E har to valg. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERomslig\u003C/span\u003E er standard. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKompakt\u003C/span\u003E strammer inn radhøyden, panelmarginene, verktøylinjeknappene og panelteksten i den appen, slik at mer får plass på skjermen. Valget lagres for hver app. En berøringsskjerm beholder de store knappene i begge, og en telefon beholder sitt eget oppsett. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETilbakestill layout\u003C/span\u003E beholder tettheten du har valgt.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"velg-hvor-mange-linjer-verkt-ylinjen-bruker\"\u003EVelg hvor mange linjer verktøylinjen bruker\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInnstillinger\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGenerelt\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EVerktøylinjerader\u003C/span\u003E har tre valg. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBryt\u003C/span\u003E er standard: alle verktøygrupper vises, på så mange linjer som det trengs. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E2 rader\u003C/span\u003E holder verktøylinjen på to linjer og \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E1 rad\u003C/span\u003E på én, slik at tegningen får høyden. Gruppene som ikke får plass, ruller sideveis inne i verktøylinjen (bruk musehjulet, et tofingersveip eller \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E til et verktøy). En verktøylinje som allerede får plass på to linjer, blir som den er under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E2 rader\u003C/span\u003E, fordi et rutenett bare ville gjort den høyere. Valget lagres for hver app. En berøringsskjerm og en telefon beholder sitt eget oppsett. CadCraft har en egen innstilling for verktøylinjen, under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInnstillinger\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"bruk-tastaturet\"\u003EBruk tastaturet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHvert panels \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E-knapp åpner en meny: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFest til venstre\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFest til høyre\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFest nederst\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFlyt over tegningen\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFest til kanten\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESkjul dette panelet\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELag fane med\u003C/span\u003E et annet panel, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFlytt til et nytt vindu\u003C/span\u003E og \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESett det tilbake\u003C/span\u003E. Bruk \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E til knappen, trykk \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, flytt med piltastene og trykk \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E igjen. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E lukker menyen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"legg-panelene-p-en-ekstra-skjerm\"\u003ELegg panelene på en ekstra skjerm\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EVelg \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E på et panel, og så \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFlytt alle paneler til et nytt vindu\u003C/span\u003E. Du kan også velge \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFlytt paneler til et nytt vindu\u003C/span\u003E i \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInnstillinger\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEt lite vindu åpnes med panelene i seg. Dra det til den andre skjermen og gjør det så stort du vil.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFortsett å arbeide: panelene virker på tegningen som før, og tegningen fyller hovedvinduet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESnarveier fungerer fortsatt når panelvinduet er foran: taster du trykker der, unntatt når du skriver i en boks, går til tegningen. Spørsmål og dialoger som appen stiller, åpnes fortsatt i hovedvinduet, så se der når en vises. Temaet og størrelsen på panelteksten følger hovedvinduet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:chromium\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EChrome or Edge\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChrome og Edge kan plassere vinduet på den andre skjermen for deg. Når en ekstra skjerm er koblet til, velg \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELegg det på den andre skjermen min\u003C/span\u003E i panelvinduet (eller \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELegg panelene på den andre skjermen min\u003C/span\u003E i et panels \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E-meny). Nettleseren spør én gang om dette nettstedet får se skjermene dine; når du har tillatt det, åpnes panelvinduet på den skjermen hver gang.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:firefox,safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EFirefox or Safari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDra panelvinduet til den andre skjermen selv. Det åpnes på samme sted og i samme størrelse neste gang.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mer-enn-ett-panelvindu\"\u003EMer enn ett panelvindu\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHvert panel kan ha et eget vindu: opptil fire panelvinduer, for eksempel ett per skjerm.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EI et panels \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E-meny åpner \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFlytt til et nytt vindu\u003C/span\u003E et nytt vindu for det panelet, og \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFlytt til panelvindu 2\u003C/span\u003E (og så videre) flytter det inn i et som er åpent. Det andre vinduet kalles «panelvindu 2».\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDra et panel etter navnet fra ett vindu til et annet: slipp over hovedvinduet for å feste det der (områdene med omriss vises mens du er over det) eller for å la det flyte, og over et annet panelvindu for å flytte det inn. Slipper du et annet sted, blir det der det var.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEt panelvindu uten flere paneler lukkes av seg selv.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEtter en omlasting tilbyr appen deg panelvinduene dine igjen. Nettlesere åpner ett vindu per klikk, så velg \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EÅpne den neste\u003C/span\u003E én gang for hvert vindu.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"flytt-en-dialogboks\"\u003EFlytt en dialogboks\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHver dialogboks kan flyttes unna tegningen: trykk og dra tittellinjen. Dialogen husker hvor du lot den stå, hver dialog for seg, og kommer tilbake til samme sted neste gang den åpnes. Dobbeltklikk tittellinjen for å sentrere den igjen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFra tastaturet, med fokus inne i dialogen, trykker du \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option\"\u003E⌥\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+en piltast for å flytte den 16 piksler om gangen; legg til \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for 64. En dialog som havner utenfor skjermen (etter et mindre vindu eller en mindre skjerm), hentes tilbake.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPå en telefon forblir dialoger fullbredde ark, og de flyttes ikke.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EI CadCraft Studio har CadCraft sin dialog \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlott til PDF\u003C/span\u003E, som du kan la stå åpen mens du arbeider, en knapp i tittellinjen: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EÅpne i eget vindu\u003C/span\u003E. Dialogen flyttes inn i et eget vindu som du kan plassere på en annen skjerm. Lukker du dialogen, lukkes vinduet, og lukker du vinduet, lukkes dialogen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"sett-panelene-tilbake\"\u003ESett panelene tilbake\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EVelg \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESett panelene tilbake\u003C/span\u003E i panelvinduet, eller lukk det bare. Panelene går tilbake dit de var.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EÅ lukke eller laste hovedvinduet på nytt lukker også panelvinduet. Når appen åpnes igjen, sier den at panelene dine var i eget vindu; velg \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EÅpne det\u003C/span\u003E for å hente vinduet tilbake, eller \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBehold dem her\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInnstillinger\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGenerelt\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETilbakestill layout\u003C/span\u003E setter hvert panel tilbake der det startet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHvis ingenting åpnes når du flytter paneler til et nytt vindu, har nettleseren blokkert sprettoppvinduet. Appen sier fra om det og lar panelene være der de var. Tillat sprettoppvinduer for getcadcraft.com, og prøv igjen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELayouten lagres for hver app i denne nettleseren, sammen med de andre innstillingene dine.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Panelene ved siden av tegningen kan flyttes. Plasser dem der det passer deg, eller gi dem et eget vindu på en ekstra skjerm, slik at tegningen får hele den første skjermen. Hvert panel har en tittellinje: en smal linje med panelets navn, en ⋯-meny, en festeknapp og en lukkeknapp øverst på panelet. Versjoner før 0.7 har bare navnet og ⋯-menyen. Flytt et panel i vinduet Dra panelet etter navnet øverst. Mens du drar, vises omrisser av områder på venstre, høyre og nederst i tegningen. Slipp panelet på ett av dem for å feste det der. Området du er over, blir mørkere. Slipp det et annet sted over tegningen for at det skal flyte. Paneler kan dele én side: de stables, og hver linje mellom to av dem kan dras for å dele plassen (når en linje har fokus, flytter piltastene den, og dobbeltklikk på den deler plassen likt). Dra den indre kanten av en festet side for å gjøre den bredere eller smalere; dobbeltklikk kanten for å sette den tilbake. Et flytende panel blir værende over tegningen. Flytt det etter navnet, og endre størrelsen ved å dra nedre høyre hjørne (eller bruk Tab til hjørnet og piltastene). Dobbeltklikk på navnet for å feste det igjen der det var. Dra med ett finger på panelets navn. Dra nedre høyre hjørne på et flytende panel med ett finger for å endre størrelsen, og kantene mellom festede paneler på samme måte. For å flyte eller feste uten å dra, bruk ⋯-menyen. Gruppér paneler som faner To paneler kan dele én ramme og vise ett om gangen. Dra et panel etter navnet over tittellinjen til et annet panel (linjen markeres mens du er over den), og slipp: rammen får nå én fane for hvert panel, i tittellinjen. Klikk på en fane for å vise panelet. Når en fane har fokus, bytter ← og → faner, og Home og End går til første og siste. For å ta et panel ut av gruppen, velg Flytt ut av fanegruppen i panelets ⋯-meny, eller dra det et annet sted. Velg Lag fane med og et panels navn for å gruppere dem uten å dra. Fest til kanten, skjul og hent tilbake Festeknappen i et panels tittellinje gjør det til auto-skjul: panelet forlater festet sitt og blir en liten fane på den kanten. Pek på fanen, gi den fokus eller klikk på den, så glir panelet ut over tegningen; flytt bort (eller trykk Esc), så glir det tilbake. Trykk festeknappen igjen, eller velg Løsne, for å feste det for godt. Lukkeknappen, eller Skjul dette panelet i menyen, skjuler panelet og lukker festet. En stiplet fane på kanten henter det frem igjen: klikk på den, eller gi den fokus og trykk Enter. Gjør layouten tettere Innstillinger › Generelt › Layouttetthet har to valg. Romslig er standard. Kompakt strammer inn radhøyden, panelmarginene, verktøylinjeknappene og panelteksten i den appen, slik at mer får plass på skjermen. Valget lagres for hver app. En berøringsskjerm beholder de store knappene i begge, og en telefon beholder sitt eget oppsett. Tilbakestill layout beholder tettheten du har valgt. Velg hvor mange linjer verktøylinjen bruker Innstillinger › Generelt › Verktøylinjerader har tre valg. Bryt er standard: alle verktøygrupper vises, på så mange linjer som det trengs. 2 rader holder verktøylinjen på to linjer og 1 rad på én, slik at tegningen får høyden. Gruppene som ikke får plass, ruller sideveis inne i verktøylinjen (bruk musehjulet, et tofingersveip eller Tab til et verktøy). En verktøylinje som allerede får plass på to linjer, blir som den er under 2 rader, fordi et rutenett bare ville gjort den høyere. Valget lagres for hver app. En berøringsskjerm og en telefon beholder sitt eget oppsett. CadCraft har en egen innstilling for verktøylinjen, under Innstillinger › CadCraft. Bruk tastaturet Hvert panels ⋯-knapp åpner en meny: Fest til venstre, Fest til høyre, Fest nederst, Flyt over tegningen, Fest til kanten, Skjul dette panelet, Lag fane med et annet panel, Flytt til et nytt vindu og Sett det tilbake. Bruk Tab til knappen, trykk Enter, flytt med piltastene og trykk Enter igjen. Esc lukker menyen. Legg panelene på en ekstra skjerm Velg ⋯ på et panel, og så Flytt alle paneler til et nytt vindu. Du kan også velge Flytt paneler til et nytt vindu i Innstillinger. Et lite vindu åpnes med panelene i seg. Dra det til den andre skjermen og gjør det så stort du vil. Fortsett å arbeide: panelene virker på tegningen som før, og tegningen fyller hovedvinduet. Snarveier fungerer fortsatt når panelvinduet er foran: taster du trykker der, unntatt når du skriver i en boks, går til tegningen. Spørsmål og dialoger som appen stiller, åpnes fortsatt i hovedvinduet, så se der når en vises. Temaet og størrelsen på panelteksten følger hovedvinduet. Chrome og Edge kan plassere vinduet på den andre skjermen for deg. Når en ekstra skjerm er koblet til, velg Legg det på den andre skjermen min i panelvinduet (eller Legg panelene på den andre skjermen min i et panels ⋯-meny). Nettleseren spør én gang om dette nettstedet får se skjermene dine; når du har tillatt det, åpnes panelvinduet på den skjermen hver gang. Dra panelvinduet til den andre skjermen selv. Det åpnes på samme sted og i samme størrelse neste gang. Mer enn ett panelvindu Hvert panel kan ha et eget vindu: opptil fire panelvinduer, for eksempel ett per skjerm. I et panels ⋯-meny åpner Flytt til et nytt vindu et nytt vindu for det panelet, og Flytt til panelvindu 2 (og så videre) flytter det inn i et som er åpent. Det andre vinduet kalles «panelvindu 2». Dra et panel etter navnet fra ett vindu til et annet: slipp over hovedvinduet for å feste det der (områdene med omriss vises mens du er over det) eller for å la det flyte, og over et annet panelvindu for å flytte det inn. Slipper du et annet sted, blir det der det var. Et panelvindu uten flere paneler lukkes av seg selv. Etter en omlasting tilbyr appen deg panelvinduene dine igjen. Nettlesere åpner ett vindu per klikk, så velg Åpne den neste én gang for hvert vindu. Flytt en dialogboks Hver dialogboks kan flyttes unna tegningen: trykk og dra tittellinjen. Dialogen husker hvor du lot den stå, hver dialog for seg, og kommer tilbake til samme sted neste gang den åpnes. Dobbeltklikk tittellinjen for å sentrere den igjen. Fra tastaturet, med fokus inne i dialogen, trykker du Alt+Shift+en piltast for å flytte den 16 piksler om gangen; legg til Ctrl for 64. En dialog som havner utenfor skjermen (etter et mindre vindu eller en mindre skjerm), hentes tilbake. På en telefon forblir dialoger fullbredde ark, og de flyttes ikke. I CadCraft Studio har CadCraft sin dialog Plott til PDF, som du kan la stå åpen mens du arbeider, en knapp i tittellinjen: Åpne i eget vindu. Dialogen flyttes inn i et eget vindu som du kan plassere på en annen skjerm. Lukker du dialogen, lukkes vinduet, og lukker du vinduet, lukkes dialogen. Sett panelene tilbake Velg Sett panelene tilbake i panelvinduet, eller lukk det bare. Panelene går tilbake dit de var. Å lukke eller laste hovedvinduet på nytt lukker også panelvinduet. Når appen åpnes igjen, sier den at panelene dine var i eget vindu; velg Åpne det for å hente vinduet tilbake, eller Behold dem her. Innstillinger › Generelt › Tilbakestill layout setter hvert panel tilbake der det startet. Hvis ingenting åpnes når du flytter paneler til et nytt vindu, har nettleseren blokkert sprettoppvinduet. Appen sier fra om det og lar panelene være der de var. Tillat sprettoppvinduer for getcadcraft.com, og prøv igjen. Layouten lagres for hver app i denne nettleseren, sammen med de andre innstillingene dine.","lang":"nb"},{"slug":"layout-ribbon","scope":"basics","title":"Jobb i layouten med bånd og prosjektleser","summary":"Bytt appen til et bånd med faner over tegningen, med Egenskaper og en prosjektleser til venstre og en fane for hver åpne visning, og bytt tilbake til den klassiske layouten når du vil.","section":"start","order":36,"keywords":["layout","arbeidsområde","bånd","båndfaner","prosjektleser","leser","paletter","egenskapspalett","visningsfaner","statuslinje","hurtigtilgang","skjul båndet","minimer båndet","båndvindu","klassisk layout","bytte layout","endre layout","festet"],"headings":[{"id":"velg-den","text":"Velg den","level":2},{"id":"hva-som-er-hvor","text":"Hva som er hvor","level":2},{"id":"bruk-b-ndet-fra-tastaturet","text":"Bruk båndet fra tastaturet","level":2},{"id":"skjul-b-ndet-eller-gi-det-et-eget-vindu","text":"Skjul båndet eller gi det et eget vindu","level":2},{"id":"panelene-dine-forblir-dine","text":"Panelene dine forblir dine","level":2},{"id":"g-tilbake-til-den-klassiske-layouten","text":"Gå tilbake til den klassiske layouten","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBånd og prosjektleser\u003C/strong\u003E er en av appens tre layouter for arbeidsområdet. Et bånd med faner går langs toppen, Egenskaper og en prosjektleser deler det venstre panelet, hver visning du åpner, får en fane over tegningen, og en statuslinje ligger nederst. Verktøyene er appens egne: alt du kan gjøre i den klassiske layouten, fungerer også her, og tegningen din, menystilen din og tastene dine forblir som de er.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"velg-den\"\u003EVelg den\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EÅpne \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInnstillinger\u003C/span\u003E (tannhjulet, eller \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EVelg fanen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGlobalt\u003C/span\u003E og gå deretter til \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayout\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArbeidsområde\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EVelg \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBånd og prosjektleser\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELayouten endres med en gang. Valget gjelder alle apper i denne nettleseren: hver app åpnes i den fra nå av, og den er på plass før siden tegnes, så du ser aldri den klassiske layouten først.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"hva-som-er-hvor\"\u003EHva som er hvor\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETittellinje:\u003C/strong\u003E appens navn, hurtigtilgangsknapper (åpne, lagre, angre, gjør om), navnet på den åpne filen, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHva hvert verktøy gjør her\u003C/span\u003E (en liste over alle verktøy med et søkefelt, som sier hvilke som er klare nå og hvorfor de andre ikke er det), fullskjerm, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHjelp\u003C/span\u003E og \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInnstillinger\u003C/span\u003E. I denne layouten tar tittellinjen plassen til applinjen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBånd:\u003C/strong\u003E appens verktøy i faner, hver fane i grupper. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFil\u003C/span\u003E lengst til venstre åpner, lagrer og så videre. Noen apper legger til en egen fane mens du jobber i en bestemt modus eller et bestemt arbeidsområde; den er tent mens den gjelder og forsvinner etterpå.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAlternativlinje:\u003C/strong\u003E under båndet, det verktøyet som kjører trenger av deg, eller det som er valgt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EVenstre panel:\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEgenskaper\u003C/span\u003E øverst og under det palettene (prosjektleseren og panelets eget innhold) som faner. Dra linjen mellom dem for å fordele plassen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EVisningsfaner:\u003C/strong\u003E én fane for hver visning du har åpnet (et ark, en plan, en scene …). Lukk en med lukkeknappen; filen forblir åpen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EVisningslinje og statuslinje:\u003C/strong\u003E under tegningen visningens egne kontroller (målestokk, zoom); nederst appens meldinger.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEt verktøy som ikke kan kjøres akkurat nå, er nedtonet, og verktøytipset sier hvorfor (for eksempel «Åpne en fil først», eller at appen ikke har det verktøyet ennå). Klikk på det, eller trykk \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E på det, for å lese årsaken i en boks.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"bruk-b-ndet-fra-tastaturet\"\u003EBruk båndet fra tastaturet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMed en båndfane i fokus flytter \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"←\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E og \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"→\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E mellom fanene, og \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ←\"\u003Efn ←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EHome\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E og \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn →\"\u003Efn →\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnd\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E går til den første og den siste. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E avslutter først verktøyet som kjører, som i den klassiske layouten, og alle hurtigtaster og \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (skjermmodi) fungerer som før.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"skjul-b-ndet-eller-gi-det-et-eget-vindu\"\u003ESkjul båndet eller gi det et eget vindu\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDobbeltklikk på en båndfane for å skjule båndet til bare fanene; et klikk på en fane viser da verktøyene dens til du går videre. Dobbeltklikk igjen for å holde det åpent. Knappen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFlytt båndet\u003C/span\u003E i høyre ende av båndet har også \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESkjul båndet\u003C/span\u003E og \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHold båndet åpent\u003C/span\u003E. Hver app husker valget.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFlytt båndet\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFlytt til et nytt vindu\u003C/span\u003E legger båndet i et eget vindu, for eksempel på en ekstra skjerm. Taster som trykkes der, når appen. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESett båndet tilbake\u003C/span\u003E, eller å lukke det vinduet, henter det hjem.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EInnstillingene for verktøylinjen gjelder båndet: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInnstillinger\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGlobalt\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EVerktøylinje\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIkoner og etiketter\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBare ikoner\u003C/span\u003E (båndet viser da to rader med små ikoner per gruppe) eller \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBare etiketter\u003C/span\u003E, og \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EVis gruppenavn (Fil, Tegn, …)\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"panelene-dine-forblir-dine\"\u003EPanelene dine forblir dine\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDet venstre og det høyre panelet beholder alt som er beskrevet i \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/arrange-panels\" data-topic=\"basics/arrange-panels\"\u003EOrdne panelene\u003C/a\u003E: feste, flyte, feste til kanten, skjule, faner, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFlytt til et nytt vindu\u003C/span\u003E og \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETilbakestill layout\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"g-tilbake-til-den-klassiske-layouten\"\u003EGå tilbake til den klassiske layouten\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EÅpne \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInnstillinger\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGlobalt\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayout\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArbeidsområde\u003C/span\u003E og velg \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft klassisk\u003C/span\u003E. Hver verktøylinje, fane og panel går tilbake dit det var.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPå en telefon, eller i et vindu som er 760 piksler bredt eller smalere, bruker appen telefonlayouten sin uansett hva du valgte. Et bredere vindu gir deg layouten din tilbake.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EVil du prøve et annet arrangement, se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/layout-toolbox\" data-topic=\"basics/layout-toolbox\"\u003Elayouten med verktøykasse og infoboks\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Bånd og prosjektleser er en av appens tre layouter for arbeidsområdet. Et bånd med faner går langs toppen, Egenskaper og en prosjektleser deler det venstre panelet, hver visning du åpner, får en fane over tegningen, og en statuslinje ligger nederst. Verktøyene er appens egne: alt du kan gjøre i den klassiske layouten, fungerer også her, og tegningen din, menystilen din og tastene dine forblir som de er. Velg den Åpne Innstillinger (tannhjulet, eller Ctrl+,). Velg fanen Globalt og gå deretter til Layout › Arbeidsområde. Velg Bånd og prosjektleser. Layouten endres med en gang. Valget gjelder alle apper i denne nettleseren: hver app åpnes i den fra nå av, og den er på plass før siden tegnes, så du ser aldri den klassiske layouten først. Hva som er hvor Tittellinje: appens navn, hurtigtilgangsknapper (åpne, lagre, angre, gjør om), navnet på den åpne filen, Hva hvert verktøy gjør her (en liste over alle verktøy med et søkefelt, som sier hvilke som er klare nå og hvorfor de andre ikke er det), fullskjerm, Hjelp og Innstillinger. I denne layouten tar tittellinjen plassen til applinjen. Bånd: appens verktøy i faner, hver fane i grupper. Fil lengst til venstre åpner, lagrer og så videre. Noen apper legger til en egen fane mens du jobber i en bestemt modus eller et bestemt arbeidsområde; den er tent mens den gjelder og forsvinner etterpå. Alternativlinje: under båndet, det verktøyet som kjører trenger av deg, eller det som er valgt. Venstre panel: Egenskaper øverst og under det palettene (prosjektleseren og panelets eget innhold) som faner. Dra linjen mellom dem for å fordele plassen. Visningsfaner: én fane for hver visning du har åpnet (et ark, en plan, en scene …). Lukk en med lukkeknappen; filen forblir åpen. Visningslinje og statuslinje: under tegningen visningens egne kontroller (målestokk, zoom); nederst appens meldinger. Et verktøy som ikke kan kjøres akkurat nå, er nedtonet, og verktøytipset sier hvorfor (for eksempel «Åpne en fil først», eller at appen ikke har det verktøyet ennå). Klikk på det, eller trykk Enter på det, for å lese årsaken i en boks. Bruk båndet fra tastaturet Med en båndfane i fokus flytter ← og → mellom fanene, og Home og End går til den første og den siste. Esc avslutter først verktøyet som kjører, som i den klassiske layouten, og alle hurtigtaster og Ctrl+Shift+F (skjermmodi) fungerer som før. Skjul båndet eller gi det et eget vindu Dobbeltklikk på en båndfane for å skjule båndet til bare fanene; et klikk på en fane viser da verktøyene dens til du går videre. Dobbeltklikk igjen for å holde det åpent. Knappen Flytt båndet i høyre ende av båndet har også Skjul båndet og Hold båndet åpent. Hver app husker valget. Flytt båndet › Flytt til et nytt vindu legger båndet i et eget vindu, for eksempel på en ekstra skjerm. Taster som trykkes der, når appen. Sett båndet tilbake, eller å lukke det vinduet, henter det hjem. Innstillingene for verktøylinjen gjelder båndet: Innstillinger › Globalt › Verktøylinje › Ikoner og etiketter, Bare ikoner (båndet viser da to rader med små ikoner per gruppe) eller Bare etiketter, og Vis gruppenavn (Fil, Tegn, …). Panelene dine forblir dine Det venstre og det høyre panelet beholder alt som er beskrevet i Ordne panelene: feste, flyte, feste til kanten, skjule, faner, Flytt til et nytt vindu og Tilbakestill layout. Gå tilbake til den klassiske layouten Åpne Innstillinger › Globalt › Layout › Arbeidsområde og velg CadCraft klassisk. Hver verktøylinje, fane og panel går tilbake dit det var. På en telefon, eller i et vindu som er 760 piksler bredt eller smalere, bruker appen telefonlayouten sin uansett hva du valgte. Et bredere vindu gir deg layouten din tilbake. Vil du prøve et annet arrangement, se layouten med verktøykasse og infoboks.","lang":"nb"},{"slug":"layout-toolbox","scope":"basics","title":"Jobb i layouten med verktøykasse og infoboks","summary":"Bytt appen til en kolonne med verktøy til venstre med plasser for dine egne, en infoboks over toppen som følger verktøyet som kjører eller det du har valgt, en navigator til høyre, en fane for hver visning, hurtigalternativer og en redigeringspalett, og bytt tilbake til den klassiske layouten når du vil.","section":"start","order":37,"keywords":["layout","arbeidsområde","verktøykasse","verktøykolonne","infoboks","infolinje","navigator","prosjektkart","hurtiglinje","hurtigalternativer","redigeringspalett","mine verktøy","favorittverktøy","visningsfaner","verktøykassevindu","klassisk layout","bytte layout","endre layout"],"headings":[{"id":"velg-den","text":"Velg den","level":2},{"id":"hva-som-er-hvor","text":"Hva som er hvor","level":2},{"id":"gi-verkt-ykassen-et-eget-vindu","text":"Gi verktøykassen et eget vindu","level":2},{"id":"panelene-dine-forblir-dine","text":"Panelene dine forblir dine","level":2},{"id":"g-tilbake-til-den-klassiske-layouten","text":"Gå tilbake til den klassiske layouten","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EVerktøykasse og infoboks\u003C/strong\u003E er en av appens tre layouter for arbeidsområdet. Tegneverktøyene står i en kolonne til venstre, en infoboks over toppen viser hva verktøyet som kjører trenger, eller hva du har valgt, hver visning har en fane, og en hurtiglinje under tegningen inneholder noen hverdagsalternativer. Verktøyene er appens egne: alt du kan gjøre i den klassiske layouten, fungerer også her, og tegningen din, menystilen din og tastene dine forblir som de er.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"velg-den\"\u003EVelg den\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EÅpne \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInnstillinger\u003C/span\u003E (tannhjulet, eller \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EVelg fanen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGlobalt\u003C/span\u003E og gå deretter til \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayout\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArbeidsområde\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EVelg \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EVerktøykasse og infoboks\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELayouten endres med en gang. Valget gjelder alle apper i denne nettleseren: hver app åpnes i den fra nå av, og den er på plass før siden tegnes, så du ser aldri den klassiske layouten først.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"hva-som-er-hvor\"\u003EHva som er hvor\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EVerktøylinje:\u003C/strong\u003E appens egen verktøylinje blir stående øverst i tegneflaten, med menyene sine.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EInfoboks:\u003C/strong\u003E under verktøylinjen. Mens et verktøy kjører, viser den verktøyet og hva det ber om. Når du velger noe, viser den de viktigste innstillingene (for en vegg: type, tykkelse, lengde, plasseringslinje og høyde), og du kan endre dem rett der: en endring der er det samme som i Egenskaper, og \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E angrer den. Når ingenting er valgt, står det «Ingenting valgt». Hver app legger til egne bokser her, for eksempel gjeldende layer eller nivå, eller det åpne dokumentet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EVerktøykasse:\u003C/strong\u003E kolonnen til venstre, appens verktøy i grupper. Klikk på en gruppes navn for å skjule eller åpne den igjen. Verktøyet som kjører, er tent.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMine verktøy:\u003C/strong\u003E verktøykassens første gruppe, åtte tomme plasser for verktøyene du bruker mest. Klikk på en tom plass for å velge et hvilket som helst verktøy eller en hvilken som helst kommando i appen; eller høyreklikk på et verktøy i verktøykassen og velg \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELegg til i Mine verktøy\u003C/span\u003E; eller dra et verktøy til en plass. Høyreklikk på en plass for å endre eller tømme den. Plassene dine lagres for hver app i denne nettleseren.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENavigator:\u003C/strong\u003E det høyre panelet. Appens prosjektleser ligger øverst (planer, snitt, fasader, 3D-visninger, tabeller, ark, slik appen har dem): klikk på en for å åpne den. Under den finnes \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEgenskaper\u003C/span\u003E og en fane med det øvrige innholdet i det høyre panelet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EVisningsfaner:\u003C/strong\u003E én fane for hver visning i den åpne filen (et ark, en plan, en scene …), over tegningen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EHurtiglinje:\u003C/strong\u003E under tegningen, visningsinnstillingene og hverdagsalternativene appen har, for eksempel målestokk, detaljnivå, visningsområde, tilpass, plan, delt visning eller 3D, fang og enheter.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERedigeringspalett:\u003C/strong\u003E klikk på noe i tegningen, så kommer en liten palett frem ved pekeren med appens redigeringskommandoer for det, for eksempel flytt, kopier, roter, speil, dupliser eller slett, samt \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEgenskaper\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, musehjulet, et nytt klikk eller lukkeknappen legger den bort.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEt verktøy som ikke kan kjøres akkurat nå, er nedtonet, og verktøytipset sier hvorfor. Klikk på det, eller trykk \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E på det, for å lese årsaken i en boks.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E avslutter først verktøyet som kjører, som i den klassiske layouten, og alle hurtigtaster og \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (skjermmodi) fungerer som før.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"gi-verkt-ykassen-et-eget-vindu\"\u003EGi verktøykassen et eget vindu\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EKnappen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFlytt verktøykassen\u003C/span\u003E øverst i verktøykassen har \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFlytt til et nytt vindu\u003C/span\u003E: verktøykassen åpnes i et smalt eget vindu som du kan plassere ved siden av appen eller på en ekstra skjerm. Taster som trykkes der, når appen. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESett verktøykassen tilbake\u003C/span\u003E, eller å lukke det vinduet, henter den hjem.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EInnstillingene for verktøylinjen gjelder også verktøykassen: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInnstillinger\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGlobalt\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EVerktøylinje\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIkoner og etiketter\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBare ikoner\u003C/span\u003E eller \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBare etiketter\u003C/span\u003E, og \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EVis gruppenavn (Fil, Tegn, …)\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"panelene-dine-forblir-dine\"\u003EPanelene dine forblir dine\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDet venstre og det høyre panelet beholder alt som er beskrevet i \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/arrange-panels\" data-topic=\"basics/arrange-panels\"\u003EOrdne panelene\u003C/a\u003E: feste, flyte, feste til kanten, skjule, faner, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFlytt til et nytt vindu\u003C/span\u003E og \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETilbakestill layout\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"g-tilbake-til-den-klassiske-layouten\"\u003EGå tilbake til den klassiske layouten\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EÅpne \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInnstillinger\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGlobalt\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayout\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArbeidsområde\u003C/span\u003E og velg \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft klassisk\u003C/span\u003E. Hver verktøylinje, fane og panel går tilbake dit det var.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPå en telefon, eller i et vindu som er 760 piksler bredt eller smalere, bruker appen telefonlayouten sin uansett hva du valgte. Et bredere vindu gir deg layouten din tilbake.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EVil du prøve et annet arrangement, se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/layout-ribbon\" data-topic=\"basics/layout-ribbon\"\u003Elayouten med bånd og prosjektleser\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Verktøykasse og infoboks er en av appens tre layouter for arbeidsområdet. Tegneverktøyene står i en kolonne til venstre, en infoboks over toppen viser hva verktøyet som kjører trenger, eller hva du har valgt, hver visning har en fane, og en hurtiglinje under tegningen inneholder noen hverdagsalternativer. Verktøyene er appens egne: alt du kan gjøre i den klassiske layouten, fungerer også her, og tegningen din, menystilen din og tastene dine forblir som de er. Velg den Åpne Innstillinger (tannhjulet, eller Ctrl+,). Velg fanen Globalt og gå deretter til Layout › Arbeidsområde. Velg Verktøykasse og infoboks. Layouten endres med en gang. Valget gjelder alle apper i denne nettleseren: hver app åpnes i den fra nå av, og den er på plass før siden tegnes, så du ser aldri den klassiske layouten først. Hva som er hvor Verktøylinje: appens egen verktøylinje blir stående øverst i tegneflaten, med menyene sine. Infoboks: under verktøylinjen. Mens et verktøy kjører, viser den verktøyet og hva det ber om. Når du velger noe, viser den de viktigste innstillingene (for en vegg: type, tykkelse, lengde, plasseringslinje og høyde), og du kan endre dem rett der: en endring der er det samme som i Egenskaper, og Ctrl+Z angrer den. Når ingenting er valgt, står det «Ingenting valgt». Hver app legger til egne bokser her, for eksempel gjeldende layer eller nivå, eller det åpne dokumentet. Verktøykasse: kolonnen til venstre, appens verktøy i grupper. Klikk på en gruppes navn for å skjule eller åpne den igjen. Verktøyet som kjører, er tent. Mine verktøy: verktøykassens første gruppe, åtte tomme plasser for verktøyene du bruker mest. Klikk på en tom plass for å velge et hvilket som helst verktøy eller en hvilken som helst kommando i appen; eller høyreklikk på et verktøy i verktøykassen og velg Legg til i Mine verktøy; eller dra et verktøy til en plass. Høyreklikk på en plass for å endre eller tømme den. Plassene dine lagres for hver app i denne nettleseren. Navigator: det høyre panelet. Appens prosjektleser ligger øverst (planer, snitt, fasader, 3D-visninger, tabeller, ark, slik appen har dem): klikk på en for å åpne den. Under den finnes Egenskaper og en fane med det øvrige innholdet i det høyre panelet. Visningsfaner: én fane for hver visning i den åpne filen (et ark, en plan, en scene …), over tegningen. Hurtiglinje: under tegningen, visningsinnstillingene og hverdagsalternativene appen har, for eksempel målestokk, detaljnivå, visningsområde, tilpass, plan, delt visning eller 3D, fang og enheter. Redigeringspalett: klikk på noe i tegningen, så kommer en liten palett frem ved pekeren med appens redigeringskommandoer for det, for eksempel flytt, kopier, roter, speil, dupliser eller slett, samt Egenskaper. Esc, musehjulet, et nytt klikk eller lukkeknappen legger den bort. Et verktøy som ikke kan kjøres akkurat nå, er nedtonet, og verktøytipset sier hvorfor. Klikk på det, eller trykk Enter på det, for å lese årsaken i en boks. Esc avslutter først verktøyet som kjører, som i den klassiske layouten, og alle hurtigtaster og Ctrl+Shift+F (skjermmodi) fungerer som før. Gi verktøykassen et eget vindu Knappen Flytt verktøykassen øverst i verktøykassen har Flytt til et nytt vindu: verktøykassen åpnes i et smalt eget vindu som du kan plassere ved siden av appen eller på en ekstra skjerm. Taster som trykkes der, når appen. Sett verktøykassen tilbake, eller å lukke det vinduet, henter den hjem. Innstillingene for verktøylinjen gjelder også verktøykassen: Innstillinger › Globalt › Verktøylinje › Ikoner og etiketter, Bare ikoner eller Bare etiketter, og Vis gruppenavn (Fil, Tegn, …). Panelene dine forblir dine Det venstre og det høyre panelet beholder alt som er beskrevet i Ordne panelene: feste, flyte, feste til kanten, skjule, faner, Flytt til et nytt vindu og Tilbakestill layout. Gå tilbake til den klassiske layouten Åpne Innstillinger › Globalt › Layout › Arbeidsområde og velg CadCraft klassisk. Hver verktøylinje, fane og panel går tilbake dit det var. På en telefon, eller i et vindu som er 760 piksler bredt eller smalere, bruker appen telefonlayouten sin uansett hva du valgte. Et bredere vindu gir deg layouten din tilbake. Vil du prøve et annet arrangement, se layouten med bånd og prosjektleser.","lang":"nb"},{"slug":"layout-own","scope":"basics","title":"Ordne din egen layout og lagre oppsettene dine","summary":"Velg hvilket panel som viser prosjektleseren, Egenskaper eller panelets eget innhold, hvor verktøykassen havner og hva linjene inneholder, lagre det som din egen layout, og behold hele oppsett under et navn for å bytte mellom dem.","section":"start","order":39,"keywords":["layout","arbeidsområde","ordne","tilpasse","min layout","egen layout","paneler","redigeringsverktøy","prosjektleser","egenskaper","verktøykasse","hurtiglinje","infoboks","arbeidsmiljø","oppsett","profil","lagre oppsett","bytte oppsett","eksportere oppsett","importere oppsett","ccenv"],"headings":[{"id":"ordne-din-egen-layout","text":"Ordne din egen layout","level":2},{"id":"lagre-oppsettet-ditt-som-et-arbeidsmilj","text":"Lagre oppsettet ditt som et arbeidsmiljø","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EVed siden av de tre innebygde layoutene kan du ordne en egen og lagre hele oppsettet ditt under et navn, slik at du kan bytte tilbake til det i ett enkelt steg.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"ordne-din-egen-layout\"\u003EOrdne din egen layout\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EÅpne \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInnstillinger\u003C/span\u003E (tannhjulet, eller \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), velg \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGlobalt\u003C/span\u003E og deretter \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayout\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EVelg \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrdne din egen layout…\u003C/span\u003E. Dialogboksen tar utgangspunkt i layouten du bruker nå.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGi den et navn, og velg deretter:\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOver tegningen:\u003C/strong\u003E appens verktøylinje, et bånd eller ingen av delene; en tittellinje; en infoboks og hvilke kontroller den inneholder.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EVed siden av tegningen:\u003C/strong\u003E verktøykassen til venstre, til høyre eller ikke i det hele tatt, med eller uten \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMine verktøy\u003C/span\u003E; hvilket panel som inneholder palettene, hvilket som ligger øverst (for eksempel prosjektleseren eller Egenskaper), og hvilke som er faner under det.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETegneflaten:\u003C/strong\u003E visningsfaner, en hurtiglinje og en visningslinje med kontrollene du haker av, redigeringspaletten, en statuslinje.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUtseende:\u003C/strong\u003E størrelse, knappeomriss og fanestil.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EVelg \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBruk denne layouten\u003C/span\u003E. Appen bytter til den med en gang, og alle apper som har layouter, bruker den også.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBare det denne appen har, tilbys. Layouten din står under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFra tillegg og filer\u003C/span\u003E; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEksporter denne layouten\u003C/span\u003E lagrer den som en fil du kan gi en kollega, og fjernknappen tar den bort. Panelene den fyller, kan fortsatt festes, flyte og flyttes til egne vinduer, som i \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/arrange-panels\" data-topic=\"basics/arrange-panels\"\u003EOrdne panelene\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"lagre-oppsettet-ditt-som-et-arbeidsmilj\"\u003ELagre oppsettet ditt som et arbeidsmiljø\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEt arbeidsmiljø er hele oppsettet ditt for alle apper i denne nettleseren: layouten, menystilen og tastene dine, verktøylinjens stil, panelene og størrelsene deres samt \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMine verktøy\u003C/span\u003E. Temaet og språket er ikke med.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELagre:\u003C/strong\u003E skriv et navn under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArbeidsmiljøer\u003C/span\u003E og velg \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELagre oppsettet mitt\u003C/span\u003E. Lagrer du under et navn som finnes fra før, erstattes det oppsettet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBytt:\u003C/strong\u003E velg et oppsett i listen og velg \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBytt til det\u003C/span\u003E. Appen lastes på nytt i det oppsettet; hvis du har ulagret arbeid, spør appen først, som ved enhver ny innlasting.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDel:\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEksporter\u003C/span\u003E lagrer det valgte oppsettet som en \u003Ccode\u003E.ccenv.json\u003C/code\u003E-fil; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImporter et oppsett…\u003C/span\u003E legger til et fra en fil. En fil stiller bare inn disse valgene, ingenting annet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFjern:\u003C/strong\u003E velg et oppsett og velg \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFjern\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDu kan beholde opptil tolv oppsett.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EVil du prøve de innebygde arrangementene først, se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/layout-toolbox\" data-topic=\"basics/layout-toolbox\"\u003Elayouten med verktøykasse og infoboks\u003C/a\u003E og \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/layout-ribbon\" data-topic=\"basics/layout-ribbon\"\u003Elayouten med bånd og prosjektleser\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Ved siden av de tre innebygde layoutene kan du ordne en egen og lagre hele oppsettet ditt under et navn, slik at du kan bytte tilbake til det i ett enkelt steg. Ordne din egen layout Åpne Innstillinger (tannhjulet, eller Ctrl+,), velg Globalt og deretter Layout. Velg Ordne din egen layout…. Dialogboksen tar utgangspunkt i layouten du bruker nå. Gi den et navn, og velg deretter: Over tegningen: appens verktøylinje, et bånd eller ingen av delene; en tittellinje; en infoboks og hvilke kontroller den inneholder. Ved siden av tegningen: verktøykassen til venstre, til høyre eller ikke i det hele tatt, med eller uten Mine verktøy; hvilket panel som inneholder palettene, hvilket som ligger øverst (for eksempel prosjektleseren eller Egenskaper), og hvilke som er faner under det. Tegneflaten: visningsfaner, en hurtiglinje og en visningslinje med kontrollene du haker av, redigeringspaletten, en statuslinje. Utseende: størrelse, knappeomriss og fanestil. Velg Bruk denne layouten. Appen bytter til den med en gang, og alle apper som har layouter, bruker den også. Bare det denne appen har, tilbys. Layouten din står under Fra tillegg og filer; Eksporter denne layouten lagrer den som en fil du kan gi en kollega, og fjernknappen tar den bort. Panelene den fyller, kan fortsatt festes, flyte og flyttes til egne vinduer, som i Ordne panelene. Lagre oppsettet ditt som et arbeidsmiljø Et arbeidsmiljø er hele oppsettet ditt for alle apper i denne nettleseren: layouten, menystilen og tastene dine, verktøylinjens stil, panelene og størrelsene deres samt Mine verktøy. Temaet og språket er ikke med. Lagre: skriv et navn under Arbeidsmiljøer og velg Lagre oppsettet mitt. Lagrer du under et navn som finnes fra før, erstattes det oppsettet. Bytt: velg et oppsett i listen og velg Bytt til det. Appen lastes på nytt i det oppsettet; hvis du har ulagret arbeid, spør appen først, som ved enhver ny innlasting. Del: Eksporter lagrer det valgte oppsettet som en .ccenv.json-fil; Importer et oppsett… legger til et fra en fil. En fil stiller bare inn disse valgene, ingenting annet. Fjern: velg et oppsett og velg Fjern. Du kan beholde opptil tolv oppsett. Vil du prøve de innebygde arrangementene først, se layouten med verktøykasse og infoboks og layouten med bånd og prosjektleser.","lang":"nb"},{"slug":"addons-basics","scope":"basics","title":"Add-ons and your account list","summary":"How add-ons work in every app and edition. Add one once in the Library and it follows your account, loads the first time an app needs it, and updates inside its major version. Sign in on a computer, pin a version, or keep working offline.","section":"start","order":40,"keywords":["add-ons","library","add","remove","update","pin","lab channel","your add-ons","sign in","offline","this file uses","engine","needs","orphan","team add-ons"],"headings":[{"id":"what-an-add-on-is","text":"What an add-on is","level":2},{"id":"your-list","text":"Your list","level":2},{"id":"needs","text":"Needs","level":2},{"id":"files-that-use-add-ons","text":"Files that use add-ons","level":2},{"id":"offline","text":"Offline","level":2},{"id":"ipad-and-iphone","text":"iPad and iPhone","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-an-add-on-is\"\u003EWhat an add-on is\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn add-on adds features (a new tool or panel) or data (regulation packs, materials, trade packs) to an app. You add it once in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELibrary\u003C/span\u003E and it is on your account list, so it follows you to every edition: the browser, Studio, the portable build and the apps on a tablet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"your-list\"\u003EYour list\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd-ons\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EYour add-ons\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESign in\u003C/strong\u003E connects this app to your account. Signed out, the app keeps a list for this device only, and offers to merge it into your account when you sign in. In Studio the sign-in shows a short code to approve in your browser.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERemove\u003C/strong\u003E takes an add-on off the list. It is refused while another add-on you keep needs it, and the screen names that add-on. When you remove something, anything that was brought in only for it is taken off too, and the screen says which.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECheck for updates\u003C/strong\u003E lists every change together. Updates inside the same major version arrive by themselves. A new major version, a changed permission, a pinned version or a lab version always asks you first, and an update is all or nothing: if one file fails its check, nothing changes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EKeep a version\u003C/strong\u003E pins it. \u003Cstrong\u003EFollow newest\u003C/strong\u003E unpins it. \u003Cstrong\u003ETry lab versions\u003C/strong\u003E moves one add-on to the lab channel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERoll back\u003C/strong\u003E returns to the version you had.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"needs\"\u003ENeeds\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn add-on can need other add-ons or a shared engine. Add brings them along and tells you what it also adds, how big it is and which permissions it asks for. Engines are shared: one copy serves everything that needs it, and a copy nothing needs is freed after 30 days.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"files-that-use-add-ons\"\u003EFiles that use add-ons\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA drawing remembers the add-ons whose data it holds. Open it on a computer that does not have them and the app says &quot;This file uses ...&quot; and offers to add them. Until you do, the data stays in the file untouched.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"offline\"\u003EOffline\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload everything for offline\u003C/span\u003E fetches every add-on on your list. Once fetched, Studio and the portable build work with no connection. Each download is checked against its checksum and its signature before it is used.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"ipad-and-iphone\"\u003EiPad and iPhone\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAdd-ons that are only data download as usual. Code add-ons made by CadCraft are already inside the app and are switched on from your list. Code add-ons from outside makers are not offered on the iPad and iPhone apps; the browser app (Add to Home Screen) has them.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What an add-on is An add-on adds features (a new tool or panel) or data (regulation packs, materials, trade packs) to an app. You add it once in the Library and it is on your account list, so it follows you to every edition: the browser, Studio, the portable build and the apps on a tablet. Your list Open Settings › Add-ons › Your add-ons. Sign in connects this app to your account. Signed out, the app keeps a list for this device only, and offers to merge it into your account when you sign in. In Studio the sign-in shows a short code to approve in your browser. Remove takes an add-on off the list. It is refused while another add-on you keep needs it, and the screen names that add-on. When you remove something, anything that was brought in only for it is taken off too, and the screen says which. Check for updates lists every change together. Updates inside the same major version arrive by themselves. A new major version, a changed permission, a pinned version or a lab version always asks you first, and an update is all or nothing: if one file fails its check, nothing changes. Keep a version pins it. Follow newest unpins it. Try lab versions moves one add-on to the lab channel. Roll back returns to the version you had. Needs An add-on can need other add-ons or a shared engine. Add brings them along and tells you what it also adds, how big it is and which permissions it asks for. Engines are shared: one copy serves everything that needs it, and a copy nothing needs is freed after 30 days. Files that use add-ons A drawing remembers the add-ons whose data it holds. Open it on a computer that does not have them and the app says \"This file uses ...\" and offers to add them. Until you do, the data stays in the file untouched. Offline Download everything for offline fetches every add-on on your list. Once fetched, Studio and the portable build work with no connection. Each download is checked against its checksum and its signature before it is used. iPad and iPhone Add-ons that are only data download as usual. Code add-ons made by CadCraft are already inside the app and are switched on from your list. Code add-ons from outside makers are not offered on the iPad and iPhone apps; the browser app (Add to Home Screen) has them.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"offline","scope":"basics","title":"Jobbe frakoblet","summary":"Lagre en app for frakoblet bruk, hva som fungerer uten tilkobling og hva som krever en.","section":"start","order":40,"keywords":["frakoblet","offline","uten internett","flymodus","befaring","hurtigbuffer","service worker","installere","laste ned","tilkobling"],"headings":[{"id":"lagre-en-app-for-frakoblet-bruk","text":"Lagre en app for frakoblet bruk","level":2},{"id":"hva-som-fungerer-frakoblet","text":"Hva som fungerer frakoblet","level":2},{"id":"hva-som-krever-tilkobling","text":"Hva som krever tilkobling","level":2},{"id":"greit-vite","text":"Greit å vite","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EAppene gjør jobben på datamaskinen din, så det meste fungerer uten tilkobling. Nettleseren trenger bare å ha lagret filene til appen først.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"lagre-en-app-for-frakoblet-bruk\"\u003ELagre en app for frakoblet bruk\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EÅpne appen mens du er tilkoblet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EVelg \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELagre for frakoblet bruk\u003C/span\u003E i linjen øverst.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EVent på «Klar frakoblet». Filene til appen, filleserne og denne hjelpen er nå lagret i nettleseren din. Apper som leser DWG- eller PDF-filer, lagrer også leserne sine, så første gang kan det ta et minutt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EGjør dette én gang per app, i hver nettleser du bruker. For å gå tilbake til appen senere uten tilkobling åpner du den samme adressen som før, eller et bokmerke til den.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"net:offline\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EOffline\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDu er frakoblet nå. Hvis denne appen ble lagret for frakoblet bruk, fungerer alt nedenfor. Hvis den ikke ble det, kan du fortsatt bruke det som allerede er lastet inn i denne fanen, men ikke last den på nytt før du er tilkoblet igjen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"hva-som-fungerer-frakoblet\"\u003EHva som fungerer frakoblet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EÅ åpne filer fra datamaskinen din, tegne, redigere, måle og kontrollere.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EÅ lagre og eksportere: hver lagring er en nedlasting til datamaskinen din.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDenne hjelpen, inkludert søk.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"hva-som-krever-tilkobling\"\u003EHva som krever tilkobling\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EÅ åpne appen for første gang, og å lagre den for frakoblet bruk.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EÅ hente en nyere versjon av appen. Når du er tilkoblet, henter appen den nyeste versjonen neste gang den åpnes, og \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELagre for frakoblet bruk\u003C/span\u003E oppdaterer den lagrede kopien.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELenker til nettstedet, for eksempel \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EÅpne på nettstedet\u003C/span\u003E, og lenker til andre sider.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESkylagring og synkronisering mellom datamaskiner, som ikke er tilgjengelig ennå.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"greit-vite\"\u003EGreit å vite\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEn lagret kopi tilhører én nettleser på én datamaskin. Hvis du sletter nettstedsdata i nettleseren, fjernes den.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPrivate vinduer i noen nettlesere, og noen låste jobbdatamaskiner, tillater ikke frakoblet lagring. Knappen sier fra når den ikke kan lagre.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ESafari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESafari fjerner lagrede nettstedsdata fra nettsteder du ikke har besøkt på flere uker. Åpne appen nå og da mens du er tilkoblet, eller lagre den på nytt før en reise.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECadCraft Studio, skrivebordsappen med alle de åtte appene i én installasjon, finnes på getcadcraft.com/download.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Appene gjør jobben på datamaskinen din, så det meste fungerer uten tilkobling. Nettleseren trenger bare å ha lagret filene til appen først. Lagre en app for frakoblet bruk Åpne appen mens du er tilkoblet. Velg Lagre for frakoblet bruk i linjen øverst. Vent på «Klar frakoblet». Filene til appen, filleserne og denne hjelpen er nå lagret i nettleseren din. Apper som leser DWG- eller PDF-filer, lagrer også leserne sine, så første gang kan det ta et minutt. Gjør dette én gang per app, i hver nettleser du bruker. For å gå tilbake til appen senere uten tilkobling åpner du den samme adressen som før, eller et bokmerke til den. Du er frakoblet nå. Hvis denne appen ble lagret for frakoblet bruk, fungerer alt nedenfor. Hvis den ikke ble det, kan du fortsatt bruke det som allerede er lastet inn i denne fanen, men ikke last den på nytt før du er tilkoblet igjen. Hva som fungerer frakoblet Å åpne filer fra datamaskinen din, tegne, redigere, måle og kontrollere. Å lagre og eksportere: hver lagring er en nedlasting til datamaskinen din. Denne hjelpen, inkludert søk. Hva som krever tilkobling Å åpne appen for første gang, og å lagre den for frakoblet bruk. Å hente en nyere versjon av appen. Når du er tilkoblet, henter appen den nyeste versjonen neste gang den åpnes, og Lagre for frakoblet bruk oppdaterer den lagrede kopien. Lenker til nettstedet, for eksempel Åpne på nettstedet, og lenker til andre sider. Skylagring og synkronisering mellom datamaskiner, som ikke er tilgjengelig ennå. Greit å vite En lagret kopi tilhører én nettleser på én datamaskin. Hvis du sletter nettstedsdata i nettleseren, fjernes den. Private vinduer i noen nettlesere, og noen låste jobbdatamaskiner, tillater ikke frakoblet lagring. Knappen sier fra når den ikke kan lagre. Safari fjerner lagrede nettstedsdata fra nettsteder du ikke har besøkt på flere uker. Åpne appen nå og da mens du er tilkoblet, eller lagre den på nytt før en reise. CadCraft Studio, skrivebordsappen med alle de åtte appene i én installasjon, finnes på getcadcraft.com/download.","lang":"nb"},{"slug":"your-files","scope":"basics","title":"Hvor filene dine havner","summary":"Filene blir på datamaskinen din, hvordan lagring fungerer og hva nettleseren husker.","section":"start","order":50,"keywords":["personvern","opplasting","sky","lagre","nedlasting","lagring","lokal","sikkerhet","konfidensiell"],"headings":[{"id":"filene-dine-blir-p-datamaskinen-din","text":"Filene dine blir på datamaskinen din","level":2},{"id":"slik-fungerer-lagring","text":"Slik fungerer lagring","level":2},{"id":"hva-nettleseren-husker","text":"Hva nettleseren husker","level":2},{"id":"skylagring","text":"Skylagring","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"filene-dine-blir-p-datamaskinen-din\"\u003EFilene dine blir på datamaskinen din\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENår du åpner en tegning, en modell eller en PDF, leser appen den inne i nettleseren din. \u003Cstrong\u003EFilen lastes ikke opp.\u003C/strong\u003E Vi ser den aldri, og det fungerer likt uten tilkobling.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"slik-fungerer-lagring\"\u003ESlik fungerer lagring\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEn nettside kan ikke overskrive filer på datamaskinen din på egen hånd, så appene lagrer ved å \u003Cstrong\u003Elaste ned\u003C/strong\u003E en ny fil:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENedlastingen havner der nettleseren legger nedlastinger, vanligvis mappen Nedlastinger, med mindre du har stilt inn nettleseren til å spørre hver gang (se \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/browsers#what-differs-between-browsers\" data-topic=\"basics/browsers\" data-hash=\"what-differs-between-browsers\"\u003ENettlesere og datamaskiner\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EApper som endrer en fil, lagrer en kopi med nytt navn, for eksempel «(redigert)» eller «(markert)», slik at originalen forblir urørt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHvis du lukker fanen med ulagret arbeid, spør nettleseren først («Forlate nettstedet?»).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"hva-nettleseren-husker\"\u003EHva nettleseren husker\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENoen apper husker små innstillinger i nettleseren din, for eksempel forfatternavnet ditt eller verktøystiler. De blir i den nettleseren på den datamaskinen, og hvis du sletter nettstedsdata, fjernes de. Hver apps hjelp sier hva den beholder.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"skylagring\"\u003ESkylagring\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESkylagring og synkronisering mellom datamaskiner er en del av den kommersielle lisensen. De er ikke tilgjengelige ennå. Til da beholder du filene dine der du har de andre prosjektfilene dine og tar sikkerhetskopi på vanlig måte.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Filene dine blir på datamaskinen din Når du åpner en tegning, en modell eller en PDF, leser appen den inne i nettleseren din. Filen lastes ikke opp. Vi ser den aldri, og det fungerer likt uten tilkobling. Slik fungerer lagring En nettside kan ikke overskrive filer på datamaskinen din på egen hånd, så appene lagrer ved å laste ned en ny fil: Nedlastingen havner der nettleseren legger nedlastinger, vanligvis mappen Nedlastinger, med mindre du har stilt inn nettleseren til å spørre hver gang (se Nettlesere og datamaskiner). Apper som endrer en fil, lagrer en kopi med nytt navn, for eksempel «(redigert)» eller «(markert)», slik at originalen forblir urørt. Hvis du lukker fanen med ulagret arbeid, spør nettleseren først («Forlate nettstedet?»). Hva nettleseren husker Noen apper husker små innstillinger i nettleseren din, for eksempel forfatternavnet ditt eller verktøystiler. De blir i den nettleseren på den datamaskinen, og hvis du sletter nettstedsdata, fjernes de. Hver apps hjelp sier hva den beholder. Skylagring Skylagring og synkronisering mellom datamaskiner er en del av den kommersielle lisensen. De er ikke tilgjengelige ennå. Til da beholder du filene dine der du har de andre prosjektfilene dine og tar sikkerhetskopi på vanlig måte.","lang":"nb"},{"slug":"connect-ai-windows","scope":"basics","title":"Connect your AI on Windows","summary":"Let your own AI app work with the CadCraft apps: one click in CadCraft Studio, or a short paste for each AI app. Includes the one-line test that proves it works.","section":"start","order":63,"keywords":["ai","assistant","connect","mcp","claude","cursor","vs code","windsurf","codex","gemini","set up","test","windows"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"the-easy-way","text":"The easy way","level":2},{"id":"prove-it-works","text":"Prove it works","level":2},{"id":"or-paste-it-yourself","text":"Or paste it yourself","level":2},{"id":"claude-desktop","text":"Claude Desktop","level":3},{"id":"claude-code","text":"Claude Code","level":3},{"id":"cursor","text":"Cursor","level":3},{"id":"windsurf","text":"Windsurf","level":3},{"id":"visual-studio-code","text":"Visual Studio Code","level":3},{"id":"codex-cli","text":"Codex CLI","level":3},{"id":"gemini-cli","text":"Gemini CLI","level":3},{"id":"browser-ai-and-phones","text":"Browser AI and phones","level":2},{"id":"if-it-does-not-work","text":"If it does not work","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to let the AI app you already use (Claude, Cursor, Visual Studio Code, Windsurf, Codex, Gemini…) reach your CadCraft apps, how to check that it works in under a minute, and how to take it out again. The apps contain no AI; your AI app asks them to do things, and you decide what it may do. (For those who search for it: this uses the Model Context Protocol, &quot;MCP&quot;.)\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-easy-way\"\u003EThe easy way\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cstrong\u003ECadCraft Studio › Connect your AI\u003C/strong\u003E. Studio looks for the AI apps on this computer, shows you in plain words what it would add to each one&#039;s settings, and writes it only after you press \u003Cstrong\u003EYes\u003C/strong\u003E. \u003Cstrong\u003EDisconnect\u003C/strong\u003E takes it out again. Nothing secret is written into the AI app&#039;s settings.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"prove-it-works\"\u003EProve it works\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn your AI app, paste this:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EUse the CadCraft tools: list the apps you can reach, then open the sample drawing in my CadCraft folder and tell me how many objects it has, by layer.\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt should list the CadCraft apps it can reach and then give you a count by layer. It only reads, so it needs no extra permission beyond the \u003Cstrong\u003ERead\u003C/strong\u003E that is on to begin with. In Studio, \u003Cstrong\u003ECheck the connection\u003C/strong\u003E tests each step without the AI and says which one failed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"or-paste-it-yourself\"\u003EOr paste it yourself\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOne card for each AI app. Each one adds a single entry named \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft\u003C/code\u003E and nothing else.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"claude-desktop\"\u003EClaude Desktop\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen %APPDATA%\\Claude\\claude_desktop_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERestart Claude Desktop completely, then ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from %APPDATA%\\Claude\\claude_desktop_config.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"claude-code\"\u003EClaude Code\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen a terminal.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERun the command below.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Claude Code and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eclaude mcp add cadcraft --scope user -- npx -y @getcadcraft/mcp connect\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E claude mcp remove cadcraft --scope user\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"cursor\"\u003ECursor\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen %USERPROFILE%\\.cursor\\mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Cursor and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from %USERPROFILE%\\.cursor\\mcp.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"windsurf\"\u003EWindsurf\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen %USERPROFILE%\\.codeium\\windsurf\\mcp_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Windsurf and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from %USERPROFILE%\\.codeium\\windsurf\\mcp_config.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"visual-studio-code\"\u003EVisual Studio Code\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen %APPDATA%\\Code\\User\\mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;servers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Visual Studio Code and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;servers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;type&quot;: &quot;stdio&quot;,\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from %APPDATA%\\Code\\User\\mcp.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"codex-cli\"\u003ECodex CLI\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen %USERPROFILE%\\.codex\\config.toml (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcp_servers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Codex CLI and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E[mcp_servers.cadcraft]\ncommand = &quot;npx&quot;\nargs = [&quot;-y&quot;,&quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,&quot;connect&quot;]\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from %USERPROFILE%\\.codex\\config.toml.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"gemini-cli\"\u003EGemini CLI\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen %USERPROFILE%\\.gemini\\settings.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Gemini CLI and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from %USERPROFILE%\\.gemini\\settings.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"browser-ai-and-phones\"\u003EBrowser AI and phones\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAI apps in a web browser, and on phones and tablets, cannot read a settings file on your computer. They use the \u003Cstrong\u003Ehosted connector\u003C/strong\u003E instead (needs CadCraft Studio open on a computer). Studio › Connect your AI shows the link.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"if-it-does-not-work\"\u003EIf it does not work\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe AI app does not list CadCraft:\u003C/strong\u003E restart it fully (quit, not just close the window), then check the entry is in its settings.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;Could not reach CadCraft Studio&quot;:\u003C/strong\u003E open Studio and the app you want; each app has Settings › Assistants to turn on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIt can look but not change:\u003C/strong\u003E Edit, Save and Export are separate permissions you turn on per app in Studio.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to let the AI app you already use (Claude, Cursor, Visual Studio Code, Windsurf, Codex, Gemini…) reach your CadCraft apps, how to check that it works in under a minute, and how to take it out again. The apps contain no AI; your AI app asks them to do things, and you decide what it may do. (For those who search for it: this uses the Model Context Protocol, \"MCP\".) The easy way Open CadCraft Studio › Connect your AI. Studio looks for the AI apps on this computer, shows you in plain words what it would add to each one's settings, and writes it only after you press Yes. Disconnect takes it out again. Nothing secret is written into the AI app's settings. Prove it works In your AI app, paste this: Use the CadCraft tools: list the apps you can reach, then open the sample drawing in my CadCraft folder and tell me how many objects it has, by layer. It should list the CadCraft apps it can reach and then give you a count by layer. It only reads, so it needs no extra permission beyond the Read that is on to begin with. In Studio, Check the connection tests each step without the AI and says which one failed. Or paste it yourself One card for each AI app. Each one adds a single entry named cadcraft and nothing else. Claude Desktop Open %APPDATA%\\Claude\\claude_desktop_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Restart Claude Desktop completely, then ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from %APPDATA%\\Claude\\claude_desktop_config.json. Claude Code Open a terminal. Run the command below. Start Claude Code and ask it the test prompt. claude mcp add cadcraft --scope user -- npx -y @getcadcraft/mcp connect Undo: claude mcp remove cadcraft --scope user Cursor Open %USERPROFILE%\\.cursor\\mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Start Cursor and ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from %USERPROFILE%\\.cursor\\mcp.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Windsurf Open %USERPROFILE%\\.codeium\\windsurf\\mcp_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Start Windsurf and ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from %USERPROFILE%\\.codeium\\windsurf\\mcp_config.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Visual Studio Code Open %APPDATA%\\Code\\User\\mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"servers\" if the file has one). Start Visual Studio Code and ask it the test prompt. { \"servers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"type\": \"stdio\", \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from %APPDATA%\\Code\\User\\mcp.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Codex CLI Open %USERPROFILE%\\.codex\\config.toml (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcp_servers\" if the file has one). Start Codex CLI and ask it the test prompt. [mcp_servers.cadcraft] command = \"npx\" args = [\"-y\",\"@getcadcraft/mcp\",\"connect\"] Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from %USERPROFILE%\\.codex\\config.toml. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Gemini CLI Open %USERPROFILE%\\.gemini\\settings.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Start Gemini CLI and ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from %USERPROFILE%\\.gemini\\settings.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Browser AI and phones AI apps in a web browser, and on phones and tablets, cannot read a settings file on your computer. They use the hosted connector instead (needs CadCraft Studio open on a computer). Studio › Connect your AI shows the link. If it does not work The AI app does not list CadCraft: restart it fully (quit, not just close the window), then check the entry is in its settings. \"Could not reach CadCraft Studio\": open Studio and the app you want; each app has Settings › Assistants to turn on. It can look but not change: Edit, Save and Export are separate permissions you turn on per app in Studio.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"connect-ai-macos","scope":"basics","title":"Connect your AI on a Mac","summary":"Let your own AI app work with the CadCraft apps: one click in CadCraft Studio, or a short paste for each AI app. Includes the one-line test that proves it works.","section":"start","order":64,"keywords":["ai","assistant","connect","mcp","claude","cursor","vs code","windsurf","codex","gemini","set up","test","macos"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"the-easy-way","text":"The easy way","level":2},{"id":"prove-it-works","text":"Prove it works","level":2},{"id":"or-paste-it-yourself","text":"Or paste it yourself","level":2},{"id":"claude-desktop","text":"Claude Desktop","level":3},{"id":"claude-code","text":"Claude Code","level":3},{"id":"cursor","text":"Cursor","level":3},{"id":"windsurf","text":"Windsurf","level":3},{"id":"visual-studio-code","text":"Visual Studio Code","level":3},{"id":"codex-cli","text":"Codex CLI","level":3},{"id":"gemini-cli","text":"Gemini CLI","level":3},{"id":"browser-ai-and-phones","text":"Browser AI and phones","level":2},{"id":"if-it-does-not-work","text":"If it does not work","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to let the AI app you already use (Claude, Cursor, Visual Studio Code, Windsurf, Codex, Gemini…) reach your CadCraft apps, how to check that it works in under a minute, and how to take it out again. The apps contain no AI; your AI app asks them to do things, and you decide what it may do. (For those who search for it: this uses the Model Context Protocol, &quot;MCP&quot;.)\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-easy-way\"\u003EThe easy way\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cstrong\u003ECadCraft Studio › Connect your AI\u003C/strong\u003E. Studio looks for the AI apps on this computer, shows you in plain words what it would add to each one&#039;s settings, and writes it only after you press \u003Cstrong\u003EYes\u003C/strong\u003E. \u003Cstrong\u003EDisconnect\u003C/strong\u003E takes it out again. Nothing secret is written into the AI app&#039;s settings.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"prove-it-works\"\u003EProve it works\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn your AI app, paste this:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EUse the CadCraft tools: list the apps you can reach, then open the sample drawing in my CadCraft folder and tell me how many objects it has, by layer.\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt should list the CadCraft apps it can reach and then give you a count by layer. It only reads, so it needs no extra permission beyond the \u003Cstrong\u003ERead\u003C/strong\u003E that is on to begin with. In Studio, \u003Cstrong\u003ECheck the connection\u003C/strong\u003E tests each step without the AI and says which one failed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"or-paste-it-yourself\"\u003EOr paste it yourself\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOne card for each AI app. Each one adds a single entry named \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft\u003C/code\u003E and nothing else.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"claude-desktop\"\u003EClaude Desktop\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/Library/Application Support/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERestart Claude Desktop completely, then ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/Library/Application Support/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"claude-code\"\u003EClaude Code\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen a terminal.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERun the command below.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Claude Code and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eclaude mcp add cadcraft --scope user -- npx -y @getcadcraft/mcp connect\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E claude mcp remove cadcraft --scope user\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"cursor\"\u003ECursor\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/.cursor/mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Cursor and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/.cursor/mcp.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"windsurf\"\u003EWindsurf\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/.codeium/windsurf/mcp_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Windsurf and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/.codeium/windsurf/mcp_config.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"visual-studio-code\"\u003EVisual Studio Code\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/Library/Application Support/Code/User/mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;servers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Visual Studio Code and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;servers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;type&quot;: &quot;stdio&quot;,\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/Library/Application Support/Code/User/mcp.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"codex-cli\"\u003ECodex CLI\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/.codex/config.toml (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcp_servers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Codex CLI and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E[mcp_servers.cadcraft]\ncommand = &quot;npx&quot;\nargs = [&quot;-y&quot;,&quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,&quot;connect&quot;]\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/.codex/config.toml.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"gemini-cli\"\u003EGemini CLI\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/.gemini/settings.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Gemini CLI and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/.gemini/settings.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"browser-ai-and-phones\"\u003EBrowser AI and phones\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAI apps in a web browser, and on phones and tablets, cannot read a settings file on your computer. They use the \u003Cstrong\u003Ehosted connector\u003C/strong\u003E instead (needs CadCraft Studio open on a computer). Studio › Connect your AI shows the link.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"if-it-does-not-work\"\u003EIf it does not work\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe AI app does not list CadCraft:\u003C/strong\u003E restart it fully (quit, not just close the window), then check the entry is in its settings.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;Could not reach CadCraft Studio&quot;:\u003C/strong\u003E open Studio and the app you want; each app has Settings › Assistants to turn on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIt can look but not change:\u003C/strong\u003E Edit, Save and Export are separate permissions you turn on per app in Studio.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to let the AI app you already use (Claude, Cursor, Visual Studio Code, Windsurf, Codex, Gemini…) reach your CadCraft apps, how to check that it works in under a minute, and how to take it out again. The apps contain no AI; your AI app asks them to do things, and you decide what it may do. (For those who search for it: this uses the Model Context Protocol, \"MCP\".) The easy way Open CadCraft Studio › Connect your AI. Studio looks for the AI apps on this computer, shows you in plain words what it would add to each one's settings, and writes it only after you press Yes. Disconnect takes it out again. Nothing secret is written into the AI app's settings. Prove it works In your AI app, paste this: Use the CadCraft tools: list the apps you can reach, then open the sample drawing in my CadCraft folder and tell me how many objects it has, by layer. It should list the CadCraft apps it can reach and then give you a count by layer. It only reads, so it needs no extra permission beyond the Read that is on to begin with. In Studio, Check the connection tests each step without the AI and says which one failed. Or paste it yourself One card for each AI app. Each one adds a single entry named cadcraft and nothing else. Claude Desktop Open ~/Library/Application Support/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Restart Claude Desktop completely, then ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/Library/Application Support/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json. Claude Code Open a terminal. Run the command below. Start Claude Code and ask it the test prompt. claude mcp add cadcraft --scope user -- npx -y @getcadcraft/mcp connect Undo: claude mcp remove cadcraft --scope user Cursor Open ~/.cursor/mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Start Cursor and ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/.cursor/mcp.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Windsurf Open ~/.codeium/windsurf/mcp_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Start Windsurf and ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/.codeium/windsurf/mcp_config.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Visual Studio Code Open ~/Library/Application Support/Code/User/mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"servers\" if the file has one). Start Visual Studio Code and ask it the test prompt. { \"servers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"type\": \"stdio\", \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/Library/Application Support/Code/User/mcp.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Codex CLI Open ~/.codex/config.toml (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcp_servers\" if the file has one). Start Codex CLI and ask it the test prompt. [mcp_servers.cadcraft] command = \"npx\" args = [\"-y\",\"@getcadcraft/mcp\",\"connect\"] Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/.codex/config.toml. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Gemini CLI Open ~/.gemini/settings.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Start Gemini CLI and ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/.gemini/settings.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Browser AI and phones AI apps in a web browser, and on phones and tablets, cannot read a settings file on your computer. They use the hosted connector instead (needs CadCraft Studio open on a computer). Studio › Connect your AI shows the link. If it does not work The AI app does not list CadCraft: restart it fully (quit, not just close the window), then check the entry is in its settings. \"Could not reach CadCraft Studio\": open Studio and the app you want; each app has Settings › Assistants to turn on. It can look but not change: Edit, Save and Export are separate permissions you turn on per app in Studio.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"connect-ai-linux","scope":"basics","title":"Connect your AI on Linux","summary":"Let your own AI app work with the CadCraft apps: one click in CadCraft Studio, or a short paste for each AI app. Includes the one-line test that proves it works.","section":"start","order":65,"keywords":["ai","assistant","connect","mcp","claude","cursor","vs code","windsurf","codex","gemini","set up","test","linux"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"the-easy-way","text":"The easy way","level":2},{"id":"prove-it-works","text":"Prove it works","level":2},{"id":"or-paste-it-yourself","text":"Or paste it yourself","level":2},{"id":"claude-desktop","text":"Claude Desktop","level":3},{"id":"claude-code","text":"Claude Code","level":3},{"id":"cursor","text":"Cursor","level":3},{"id":"windsurf","text":"Windsurf","level":3},{"id":"visual-studio-code","text":"Visual Studio Code","level":3},{"id":"codex-cli","text":"Codex CLI","level":3},{"id":"gemini-cli","text":"Gemini CLI","level":3},{"id":"browser-ai-and-phones","text":"Browser AI and phones","level":2},{"id":"if-it-does-not-work","text":"If it does not work","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to let the AI app you already use (Claude, Cursor, Visual Studio Code, Windsurf, Codex, Gemini…) reach your CadCraft apps, how to check that it works in under a minute, and how to take it out again. The apps contain no AI; your AI app asks them to do things, and you decide what it may do. (For those who search for it: this uses the Model Context Protocol, &quot;MCP&quot;.)\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-easy-way\"\u003EThe easy way\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cstrong\u003ECadCraft Studio › Connect your AI\u003C/strong\u003E. Studio looks for the AI apps on this computer, shows you in plain words what it would add to each one&#039;s settings, and writes it only after you press \u003Cstrong\u003EYes\u003C/strong\u003E. \u003Cstrong\u003EDisconnect\u003C/strong\u003E takes it out again. Nothing secret is written into the AI app&#039;s settings.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"prove-it-works\"\u003EProve it works\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn your AI app, paste this:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EUse the CadCraft tools: list the apps you can reach, then open the sample drawing in my CadCraft folder and tell me how many objects it has, by layer.\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt should list the CadCraft apps it can reach and then give you a count by layer. It only reads, so it needs no extra permission beyond the \u003Cstrong\u003ERead\u003C/strong\u003E that is on to begin with. In Studio, \u003Cstrong\u003ECheck the connection\u003C/strong\u003E tests each step without the AI and says which one failed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"or-paste-it-yourself\"\u003EOr paste it yourself\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOne card for each AI app. Each one adds a single entry named \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft\u003C/code\u003E and nothing else.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"claude-desktop\"\u003EClaude Desktop\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/.config/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERestart Claude Desktop completely, then ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/.config/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"claude-code\"\u003EClaude Code\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen a terminal.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERun the command below.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Claude Code and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eclaude mcp add cadcraft --scope user -- npx -y @getcadcraft/mcp connect\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E claude mcp remove cadcraft --scope user\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"cursor\"\u003ECursor\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/.cursor/mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Cursor and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/.cursor/mcp.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"windsurf\"\u003EWindsurf\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/.codeium/windsurf/mcp_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Windsurf and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/.codeium/windsurf/mcp_config.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"visual-studio-code\"\u003EVisual Studio Code\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/.config/Code/User/mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;servers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Visual Studio Code and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;servers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;type&quot;: &quot;stdio&quot;,\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/.config/Code/User/mcp.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"codex-cli\"\u003ECodex CLI\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/.codex/config.toml (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcp_servers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Codex CLI and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E[mcp_servers.cadcraft]\ncommand = &quot;npx&quot;\nargs = [&quot;-y&quot;,&quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,&quot;connect&quot;]\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/.codex/config.toml.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"gemini-cli\"\u003EGemini CLI\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/.gemini/settings.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Gemini CLI and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/.gemini/settings.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"browser-ai-and-phones\"\u003EBrowser AI and phones\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAI apps in a web browser, and on phones and tablets, cannot read a settings file on your computer. They use the \u003Cstrong\u003Ehosted connector\u003C/strong\u003E instead (needs CadCraft Studio open on a computer). Studio › Connect your AI shows the link.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"if-it-does-not-work\"\u003EIf it does not work\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe AI app does not list CadCraft:\u003C/strong\u003E restart it fully (quit, not just close the window), then check the entry is in its settings.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;Could not reach CadCraft Studio&quot;:\u003C/strong\u003E open Studio and the app you want; each app has Settings › Assistants to turn on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIt can look but not change:\u003C/strong\u003E Edit, Save and Export are separate permissions you turn on per app in Studio.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to let the AI app you already use (Claude, Cursor, Visual Studio Code, Windsurf, Codex, Gemini…) reach your CadCraft apps, how to check that it works in under a minute, and how to take it out again. The apps contain no AI; your AI app asks them to do things, and you decide what it may do. (For those who search for it: this uses the Model Context Protocol, \"MCP\".) The easy way Open CadCraft Studio › Connect your AI. Studio looks for the AI apps on this computer, shows you in plain words what it would add to each one's settings, and writes it only after you press Yes. Disconnect takes it out again. Nothing secret is written into the AI app's settings. Prove it works In your AI app, paste this: Use the CadCraft tools: list the apps you can reach, then open the sample drawing in my CadCraft folder and tell me how many objects it has, by layer. It should list the CadCraft apps it can reach and then give you a count by layer. It only reads, so it needs no extra permission beyond the Read that is on to begin with. In Studio, Check the connection tests each step without the AI and says which one failed. Or paste it yourself One card for each AI app. Each one adds a single entry named cadcraft and nothing else. Claude Desktop Open ~/.config/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Restart Claude Desktop completely, then ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/.config/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json. Claude Code Open a terminal. Run the command below. Start Claude Code and ask it the test prompt. claude mcp add cadcraft --scope user -- npx -y @getcadcraft/mcp connect Undo: claude mcp remove cadcraft --scope user Cursor Open ~/.cursor/mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Start Cursor and ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/.cursor/mcp.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Windsurf Open ~/.codeium/windsurf/mcp_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Start Windsurf and ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/.codeium/windsurf/mcp_config.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Visual Studio Code Open ~/.config/Code/User/mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"servers\" if the file has one). Start Visual Studio Code and ask it the test prompt. { \"servers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"type\": \"stdio\", \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/.config/Code/User/mcp.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Codex CLI Open ~/.codex/config.toml (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcp_servers\" if the file has one). Start Codex CLI and ask it the test prompt. [mcp_servers.cadcraft] command = \"npx\" args = [\"-y\",\"@getcadcraft/mcp\",\"connect\"] Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/.codex/config.toml. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Gemini CLI Open ~/.gemini/settings.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Start Gemini CLI and ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/.gemini/settings.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Browser AI and phones AI apps in a web browser, and on phones and tablets, cannot read a settings file on your computer. They use the hosted connector instead (needs CadCraft Studio open on a computer). Studio › Connect your AI shows the link. If it does not work The AI app does not list CadCraft: restart it fully (quit, not just close the window), then check the entry is in its settings. \"Could not reach CadCraft Studio\": open Studio and the app you want; each app has Settings › Assistants to turn on. It can look but not change: Edit, Save and Export are separate permissions you turn on per app in Studio.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"connect-ai-mobile","scope":"basics","title":"Connect your AI on a phone or tablet","summary":"How your own AI app reaches the CadCraft apps from a phone or tablet: through the hosted connector, with CadCraft Studio open on a computer. Includes the one-line test.","section":"start","order":66,"keywords":["ai","assistant","connect","android","ipad","phone","tablet","hosted connector","test"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"how","text":"How","level":2},{"id":"prove-it-works","text":"Prove it works","level":2},{"id":"what-it-can-and-cannot-do","text":"What it can and cannot do","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn AI app on a phone or tablet cannot read a settings file on your computer, so it does not use the paste cards. It connects to a \u003Cstrong\u003Ehosted connector\u003C/strong\u003E instead, and CadCraft Studio must be open on a computer you own to answer.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how\"\u003EHow\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn the computer, open \u003Cstrong\u003ECadCraft Studio › Connect your AI\u003C/strong\u003E and choose \u003Cstrong\u003EHosted connector\u003C/strong\u003E. Studio shows the address to add and asks you to sign in.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn your AI app, add a connector (a &quot;remote&quot; or &quot;custom&quot; connector) with that address and sign in when asked.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EKeep Studio open. When it is closed the AI app says CadCraft is not reachable.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"prove-it-works\"\u003EProve it works\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn your AI app, paste this:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EUse the CadCraft tools: list the apps you can reach, then open the sample drawing in my CadCraft folder and tell me how many objects it has, by layer.\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-can-and-cannot-do\"\u003EWhat it can and cannot do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe hosted connector carries only the tool calls and their answers for the apps and permissions you allowed. It never holds your files, and the answers are marked as data from your files, not as instructions. You can disconnect it in Studio at any time.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you will learn An AI app on a phone or tablet cannot read a settings file on your computer, so it does not use the paste cards. It connects to a hosted connector instead, and CadCraft Studio must be open on a computer you own to answer. How On the computer, open CadCraft Studio › Connect your AI and choose Hosted connector. Studio shows the address to add and asks you to sign in. In your AI app, add a connector (a \"remote\" or \"custom\" connector) with that address and sign in when asked. Keep Studio open. When it is closed the AI app says CadCraft is not reachable. Prove it works In your AI app, paste this: Use the CadCraft tools: list the apps you can reach, then open the sample drawing in my CadCraft folder and tell me how many objects it has, by layer. What it can and cannot do The hosted connector carries only the tool calls and their answers for the apps and permissions you allowed. It never holds your files, and the answers are marked as data from your files, not as instructions. You can disconnect it in Studio at any time.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"local-models","scope":"basics","title":"Use an AI that runs on your own computer","summary":"Work with a local AI model and no network: through an AI app that can use tools, .","section":"start","order":66,"keywords":["ai","assistant","local","offline","ollama","lm studio","llama.cpp","jan","air-gapped","private","model","ask box"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"how","text":"How","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to use an AI model that runs on your own computer, so nothing you ask leaves it. This suits firms that cannot send drawings to an outside service. The apps contain no AI; the model asks them to do things, and you decide what it may do.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how\"\u003EHow\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUse an AI app on this computer that can use tools. LM Studio, Jan and llama.cpp&#039;s server can each use tools through the Model Context Protocol (&quot;MCP&quot;). Add the single \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft\u003C/code\u003E entry shown in \u003Cstrong\u003EConnect your AI\u003C/strong\u003E, open CadCraft Studio, then ask your question in that app.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EProgram\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECan use tools through MCP\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELM Studio\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ellama.cpp (llama-server)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes, start it with \u003Ccode\u003E--jinja\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EJan\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes, turn on tool calling for the model\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOllama\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot on its own: use it as the model, with Jan or an editor in front\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis list is taken from each program&#039;s own documentation. It has not yet been tried with every program on every system.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERaise the model&#039;s context length before you start; the default in some programs is small and tool descriptions will not fit.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESmall models call tools less reliably than large hosted ones. If the answer says what it would do but nothing changes, try a larger model or a model marked as able to use tools.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEverything else stays the same: the permission levels, \u003Cstrong\u003ESuggest\u003C/strong\u003E mode, the folders you allowed and the activity list. A model on this computer sends nothing out.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to use an AI model that runs on your own computer, so nothing you ask leaves it. This suits firms that cannot send drawings to an outside service. The apps contain no AI; the model asks them to do things, and you decide what it may do. How Use an AI app on this computer that can use tools. LM Studio, Jan and llama.cpp's server can each use tools through the Model Context Protocol (\"MCP\"). Add the single cadcraft entry shown in Connect your AI, open CadCraft Studio, then ask your question in that app. Program Can use tools through MCP LM Studio Yes llama.cpp (llama-server) Yes, start it with --jinja Jan Yes, turn on tool calling for the model Ollama Not on its own: use it as the model, with Jan or an editor in front This list is taken from each program's own documentation. It has not yet been tried with every program on every system. Good to know Raise the model's context length before you start; the default in some programs is small and tool descriptions will not fit. Small models call tools less reliably than large hosted ones. If the answer says what it would do but nothing changes, try a larger model or a model marked as able to use tools. Everything else stays the same: the permission levels, Suggest mode, the folders you allowed and the activity list. A model on this computer sends nothing out.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"engines","scope":"basics","title":"Run an engine on a model","summary":"The Engines panel runs the shared calculation engines (schematics, pipe and power networks, motion, queues, schedule and cost, requirements, risk, live data, circuits, boards, systems, PLC) on a model you paste.","section":"tasks","order":40,"keywords":["engines","schematic","network","power","load flow","robot","kinematics","queue","simulation","schedule","critical path","requirements","risk","fault tree","modbus","circuit","spice","board","pcb","modelica","plc"],"headings":[{"id":"run-one","text":"Run one","level":2},{"id":"controls-on-a-model-or-on-the-3d-scene","text":"Controls on a model, or on the 3D scene","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ESome calculations are the same in every app, so they live in one panel instead of eight. Open \u003Cstrong\u003EEngines\u003C/strong\u003E from the button in the bar at the top, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command K\"\u003E⌘K\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+K\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and type &quot;engines&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"run-one\"\u003ERun one\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose an engine from the list: schematics, pipe networks and power systems, motion and robots, queues, schedule and cost, requirements, risk, live data, circuits, boards, systems or controls.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose a worked example, or replace the text with your own model. Most engines read JSON; circuits, systems and controls read their own text (a circuit deck, Modelica, structured text).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERun\u003C/span\u003E. The results come back as tables, with the engine&#039;s own problems above them and the limits of its method below.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset\u003C/span\u003E puts the example text back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"controls-on-a-model-or-on-the-3d-scene\"\u003EControls on a model, or on the 3D scene\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe controls engine also takes a JSON input that ties a structured-text program to something it can sense and command. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThe program bound to a flow model\u003C/span\u003E is the queues engine&#039;s model. In ModelCraft, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThe program bound to the 3D scene\u003C/span\u003E ties it to the placements (groups and components) of the open model: a sensor reads \u003Ccode\u003EName.x\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EName.y\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EName.z\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EName.rotZ\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003EName.hidden\u003C/code\u003E, a command writes one of them, and the program decides. Position sensors are whole numbers, so give each a \u003Ccode\u003Escale\u003C/code\u003E (1000 turns metres into millimetres); a command is on or off, and \u003Ccode\u003Evalues\u003C/code\u003E says what off and on mean (\u003Ccode\u003E[0, 2]\u003C/code\u003E puts the placement at 0 or 2 m). A run changes nothing in the model: the results table shows where each placement would end up. Add \u003Ccode\u003E&quot;keep&quot;: true\u003C/code\u003E to write the final state into the model as one undo step. A placement that is not in the model is reported by name.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEverything runs in this page. Nothing is uploaded, and nothing is saved in your file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EProblems the engine finds also appear in the Problems panel, under their own source.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe examples are small on purpose, with answers you can check by hand. They are a way to see what an engine does, not a design.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe results are calculation aids. A person who is responsible for the design checks them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Some calculations are the same in every app, so they live in one panel instead of eight. Open Engines from the button in the bar at the top, or press Ctrl+K and type \"engines\". Run one Choose an engine from the list: schematics, pipe networks and power systems, motion and robots, queues, schedule and cost, requirements, risk, live data, circuits, boards, systems or controls. Choose a worked example, or replace the text with your own model. Most engines read JSON; circuits, systems and controls read their own text (a circuit deck, Modelica, structured text). Press Run. The results come back as tables, with the engine's own problems above them and the limits of its method below. Reset puts the example text back. Controls on a model, or on the 3D scene The controls engine also takes a JSON input that ties a structured-text program to something it can sense and command. The program bound to a flow model is the queues engine's model. In ModelCraft, The program bound to the 3D scene ties it to the placements (groups and components) of the open model: a sensor reads Name.x, Name.y, Name.z, Name.rotZ or Name.hidden, a command writes one of them, and the program decides. Position sensors are whole numbers, so give each a scale (1000 turns metres into millimetres); a command is on or off, and values says what off and on mean ([0, 2] puts the placement at 0 or 2 m). A run changes nothing in the model: the results table shows where each placement would end up. Add \"keep\": true to write the final state into the model as one undo step. A placement that is not in the model is reported by name. Things to know Everything runs in this page. Nothing is uploaded, and nothing is saved in your file. Problems the engine finds also appear in the Problems panel, under their own source. The examples are small on purpose, with answers you can check by hand. They are a way to see what an engine does, not a design. The results are calculation aids. A person who is responsible for the design checks them.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"assistants-recipes","scope":"basics","title":"Recipes for AI assistants","summary":"Worked examples for using your own AI assistant with the CadCraft apps: what to ask, the tools it calls, and the permissions each needs. Every recipe here is run by our tests with the same calls.","section":"tasks","order":62,"keywords":["ai","assistant","mcp","recipes","examples","prompts","automation","batch","convert","plot","check","takeoff","cut","fill","parameters","script"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"recipes","text":"Recipes","level":2},{"id":"tidy-a-drawing","text":"Tidy a drawing","level":3},{"id":"plot-a-set-of-sheets","text":"Plot a set of sheets","level":3},{"id":"convert-drawings-for-a-consultant","text":"Convert drawings for a consultant","level":3},{"id":"check-drawings-before-sending-them","text":"Check drawings before sending them","level":3},{"id":"ask-a-building-model-questions","text":"Ask a building model questions","level":3},{"id":"draw-a-room-in-bimcraft","text":"Draw a room in BimCraft","level":3},{"id":"total-a-takeoff","text":"Total a takeoff","level":3},{"id":"site-numbers","text":"Site numbers","level":3},{"id":"try-a-part-variant","text":"Try a part variant","level":3},{"id":"fill-in-a-title-block","text":"Fill in a title block","level":3},{"id":"your-files-and-your-assistant","text":"Your files and your assistant","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EReady-made requests for your own AI assistant, and what it does with them. The apps contain no AI: your assistant reads the files and calls the apps&#039; tools, and you decide which permissions it has. Each recipe names the tools it calls, so you can check its work in the activity log, and each one is run by our tests with exactly these calls, so they keep working.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThere are two ways in. In \u003Cstrong\u003ECadCraft Studio\u003C/strong\u003E, an assistant works with the apps you have open (Settings › Assistants in each app). \u003Cstrong\u003EWithout a window\u003C/strong\u003E, the CadCraft file server for assistants (\u003Ccode\u003Enpx @getcadcraft/mcp --root &lt;folder&gt;\u003C/code\u003E) works on files in folders you name; the \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft\u003C/code\u003E command line does the same jobs for your own scripts. The recipes say which way they use; most work both ways.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EStart read-only. Ask the assistant to describe a file before it changes anything; its answers are better when it has looked first, and Read is the only permission that is on to begin with.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"recipes\"\u003ERecipes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"tidy-a-drawing\"\u003ETidy a drawing\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;List the layers with nothing on them and the blocks nobody uses, then purge the unused blocks.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe assistant opens the drawing and reads it, then runs PURGE:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Edrawing_open {path: &quot;Kitchen/kitchen-equipment.dwg&quot;}            → d1\ndrawing_describe {doc: &quot;d1&quot;}                                    layers with 0 objects, blocks with 0 uses\ncommand_run {doc: &quot;d1&quot;, input: &quot;PURGE&quot;}                         one undo step: &quot;Assistant: PURGE&quot;\ndrawing_save {doc: &quot;d1&quot;, path: &quot;Kitchen/kitchen-tidy.dwg&quot;}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Read and Edit, and Save to write the result. Dimension arrowheads count as used, so they stay.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"plot-a-set-of-sheets\"\u003EPlot a set of sheets\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;Plot every sheet of every drawing in the Kitchen folder to PDF.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Efiles_list {folder: &quot;Kitchen&quot;, extensions: [&quot;dwg&quot;, &quot;dxf&quot;]}\ndrawing_open {path: …}  ·  drawing_describe {doc}                the sheets of each drawing\ndrawing_plot {doc, layout: &quot;&lt;sheet&gt;&quot;, out: &quot;Plots/&lt;drawing&gt; &lt;sheet&gt;.pdf&quot;}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Read and Export. From the command line, one PDF per drawing with all its sheets: \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft plot Kitchen/*.dwg Plots --all\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"convert-drawings-for-a-consultant\"\u003EConvert drawings for a consultant\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;Save copies of the drawings in Kitchen as AutoCAD 2013 DWG in the Out folder.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Efiles_list {folder: &quot;Kitchen&quot;, extensions: [&quot;dwg&quot;, &quot;dxf&quot;]}\ndrawing_convert {path: &quot;Kitchen/&lt;file&gt;&quot;, out: &quot;Out/&lt;file&gt;.dwg&quot;, version: &quot;2013&quot;}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Read and Export; the originals are not changed. From the command line: \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft convert Kitchen/*.dwg Kitchen/*.dxf Out --to dwg --version 2013\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"check-drawings-before-sending-them\"\u003ECheck drawings before sending them\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;Check the drawings in Kitchen: nothing on layer 0, colours by layer, no unused blocks. Give me a table of what fails.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Edrawing_check {doc, rules: {layers: {noObjectsOn: [&quot;0&quot;]}, objects: {colorByLayer: true}, blocks: {noUnused: true}}}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Read. Keep your office&#039;s rules in a file and the command line checks a whole folder: \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft check Kitchen/*.dwg --rules office-standards.json\u003C/code\u003E (exit code 1 when something fails, so it fits in a script).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"ask-a-building-model-questions\"\u003EAsk a building model questions\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;How many doors does the house have on each level, and which are external?&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eifc_open {path: &quot;house.ifc&quot;}                                    → d1\nifc_query {doc: &quot;d1&quot;, class: &quot;IfcDoor&quot;}                         each door with its level\nifc_properties {doc: &quot;d1&quot;, id: &lt;door&gt;}                          Pset_DoorCommon.IsExternal\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Read. In Studio, BimCraft&#039;s own tools answer the same about the model you have open.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"draw-a-room-in-bimcraft\"\u003EDraw a room in BimCraft\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;Draw a 6 by 4 metre room on Level 1 with a single door in the south wall.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Ewalls_add {points: [[0, 0], [6000, 0], [6000, 4000], [0, 4000]], closed: true}     one undo step\nopening_add {wall: &quot;&lt;the first wall&#039;s GlobalId&gt;&quot;, kind: &quot;door&quot;}                    one undo step\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Edit. Walls join and the room follows, as with the Draw tools. Without a window, \u003Ccode\u003Eifc_new\u003C/code\u003E starts a building first and \u003Ccode\u003Eifc_save\u003C/code\u003E writes it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"total-a-takeoff\"\u003ETotal a takeoff\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;Give me the estimate by item and export it to XLSX.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Etakeoff_open {path: &quot;job.takeoff.json&quot;}  ·  takeoff_estimate {doc}  ·  takeoff_export {doc, out: &quot;job.xlsx&quot;}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Read, and Export for the file. Marks on sheets with no scale are listed and never counted as zero, so an incomplete estimate says so.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"site-numbers\"\u003ESite numbers\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;How much cut and fill does the pad need?&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Esite_open {path: &quot;site.civilcraft.json&quot;}  ·  surface_volume {doc}       cut, fill and net, per pad\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Read. With a level instead of a design: \u003Ccode\u003Esurface_volume {doc, datum: 100}\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"try-a-part-variant\"\u003ETry a part variant\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;Set the length to 40 mm, rebuild, and give me the mass.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Epart_open {path: &quot;screw.partcraft.json&quot;}  ·  params_list {doc}\nparam_set {doc, name: &quot;l&quot;, value: 40}                           one undo step, the part rebuilt\nmass_properties {doc}                                            volume, mass from the material, centre, inertia\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Read and Edit; Save (\u003Ccode\u003Epart_save\u003C/code\u003E) to keep it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"fill-in-a-title-block\"\u003EFill in a title block\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;Insert the title block at 0,0 with sheet number A-101 and the title Kitchen plan.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eblock_insert {doc, block: &quot;TITLEBLOCK-ARCHD&quot;, at: [0, 0], attributes: {&quot;SHEET-NO&quot;: &quot;A-101&quot;, &quot;SHEET-TITLE&quot;: &quot;Kitchen plan&quot;}}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Edit. One undo step; the attributes it does not name keep their defaults.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"your-files-and-your-assistant\"\u003EYour files and your assistant\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe apps never send your files anywhere. \u003Cstrong\u003EBut what your assistant reads goes to whoever runs that assistant:\u003C/strong\u003E a cloud assistant&#039;s maker sees the parts of the drawing it is shown. If that matters for a job, use an assistant that runs on your own computer, or keep that job&#039;s folders out of the ones you allow.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you will learn Ready-made requests for your own AI assistant, and what it does with them. The apps contain no AI: your assistant reads the files and calls the apps' tools, and you decide which permissions it has. Each recipe names the tools it calls, so you can check its work in the activity log, and each one is run by our tests with exactly these calls, so they keep working. There are two ways in. In CadCraft Studio, an assistant works with the apps you have open (Settings › Assistants in each app). Without a window, the CadCraft file server for assistants (npx @getcadcraft/mcp --root \u003Cfolder\u003E) works on files in folders you name; the cadcraft command line does the same jobs for your own scripts. The recipes say which way they use; most work both ways. Start read-only. Ask the assistant to describe a file before it changes anything; its answers are better when it has looked first, and Read is the only permission that is on to begin with. Recipes Tidy a drawing Ask: \"List the layers with nothing on them and the blocks nobody uses, then purge the unused blocks.\" The assistant opens the drawing and reads it, then runs PURGE: drawing_open {path: \"Kitchen/kitchen-equipment.dwg\"} → d1 drawing_describe {doc: \"d1\"} layers with 0 objects, blocks with 0 uses command_run {doc: \"d1\", input: \"PURGE\"} one undo step: \"Assistant: PURGE\" drawing_save {doc: \"d1\", path: \"Kitchen/kitchen-tidy.dwg\"} Needs Read and Edit, and Save to write the result. Dimension arrowheads count as used, so they stay. Plot a set of sheets Ask: \"Plot every sheet of every drawing in the Kitchen folder to PDF.\" files_list {folder: \"Kitchen\", extensions: [\"dwg\", \"dxf\"]} drawing_open {path: …} · drawing_describe {doc} the sheets of each drawing drawing_plot {doc, layout: \"\u003Csheet\u003E\", out: \"Plots/\u003Cdrawing\u003E \u003Csheet\u003E.pdf\"} Needs Read and Export. From the command line, one PDF per drawing with all its sheets: cadcraft plot Kitchen/*.dwg Plots --all. Convert drawings for a consultant Ask: \"Save copies of the drawings in Kitchen as AutoCAD 2013 DWG in the Out folder.\" files_list {folder: \"Kitchen\", extensions: [\"dwg\", \"dxf\"]} drawing_convert {path: \"Kitchen/\u003Cfile\u003E\", out: \"Out/\u003Cfile\u003E.dwg\", version: \"2013\"} Needs Read and Export; the originals are not changed. From the command line: cadcraft convert Kitchen/*.dwg Kitchen/*.dxf Out --to dwg --version 2013. Check drawings before sending them Ask: \"Check the drawings in Kitchen: nothing on layer 0, colours by layer, no unused blocks. Give me a table of what fails.\" drawing_check {doc, rules: {layers: {noObjectsOn: [\"0\"]}, objects: {colorByLayer: true}, blocks: {noUnused: true}}} Needs Read. Keep your office's rules in a file and the command line checks a whole folder: cadcraft check Kitchen/*.dwg --rules office-standards.json (exit code 1 when something fails, so it fits in a script). Ask a building model questions Ask: \"How many doors does the house have on each level, and which are external?\" ifc_open {path: \"house.ifc\"} → d1 ifc_query {doc: \"d1\", class: \"IfcDoor\"} each door with its level ifc_properties {doc: \"d1\", id: \u003Cdoor\u003E} Pset_DoorCommon.IsExternal Needs Read. In Studio, BimCraft's own tools answer the same about the model you have open. Draw a room in BimCraft Ask: \"Draw a 6 by 4 metre room on Level 1 with a single door in the south wall.\" walls_add {points: [[0, 0], [6000, 0], [6000, 4000], [0, 4000]], closed: true} one undo step opening_add {wall: \"\u003Cthe first wall's GlobalId\u003E\", kind: \"door\"} one undo step Needs Edit. Walls join and the room follows, as with the Draw tools. Without a window, ifc_new starts a building first and ifc_save writes it. Total a takeoff Ask: \"Give me the estimate by item and export it to XLSX.\" takeoff_open {path: \"job.takeoff.json\"} · takeoff_estimate {doc} · takeoff_export {doc, out: \"job.xlsx\"} Needs Read, and Export for the file. Marks on sheets with no scale are listed and never counted as zero, so an incomplete estimate says so. Site numbers Ask: \"How much cut and fill does the pad need?\" site_open {path: \"site.civilcraft.json\"} · surface_volume {doc} cut, fill and net, per pad Needs Read. With a level instead of a design: surface_volume {doc, datum: 100}. Try a part variant Ask: \"Set the length to 40 mm, rebuild, and give me the mass.\" part_open {path: \"screw.partcraft.json\"} · params_list {doc} param_set {doc, name: \"l\", value: 40} one undo step, the part rebuilt mass_properties {doc} volume, mass from the material, centre, inertia Needs Read and Edit; Save (part_save) to keep it. Fill in a title block Ask: \"Insert the title block at 0,0 with sheet number A-101 and the title Kitchen plan.\" block_insert {doc, block: \"TITLEBLOCK-ARCHD\", at: [0, 0], attributes: {\"SHEET-NO\": \"A-101\", \"SHEET-TITLE\": \"Kitchen plan\"}} Needs Edit. One undo step; the attributes it does not name keep their defaults. Your files and your assistant The apps never send your files anywhere. But what your assistant reads goes to whoever runs that assistant: a cloud assistant's maker sees the parts of the drawing it is shown. If that matters for a job, use an assistant that runs on your own computer, or keep that job's folders out of the ones you allow.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"community-testing","scope":"basics","title":"Community testing","summary":"How to try a test on the Community testing page and send your result.","section":"tasks","order":90,"keywords":["community","testing","test","results","report","feedback","files","passed","failing"],"headings":[{"id":"send-a-result","text":"Send a result","level":2},{"id":"sending-files","text":"Sending files","level":2},{"id":"your-privacy","text":"Your privacy","level":2},{"id":"what-counts-as-known-not-to-work","text":"What counts as \"known not to work\"","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ESome checks need a real computer, a real file or a real device. The Community testing page lists them so anyone can help.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"send-a-result\"\u003ESend a result\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the \u003Cstrong\u003ECommunity testing\u003C/strong\u003E page on the website and choose a test.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUse the public build named in \u003Cstrong\u003EBefore you start\u003C/strong\u003E, never a lab build.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFollow the \u003Cstrong\u003ESteps\u003C/strong\u003E and compare with the \u003Cstrong\u003EExpected result\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFill in \u003Cstrong\u003ESend a result\u003C/strong\u003E: passed, still failing, partly works, or could not run it. Say which system you used and what happened.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou do not need an account to send a result. A signed-in account is needed only to send files.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"sending-files\"\u003ESending files\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen a test asks for files, drag them into the framed area (or click it, or press Enter or Space on it to choose them). You can send several at once; each shows its name and size and a Remove button. The form says which file types and sizes we take. We never take programs, scripts or web pages.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"your-privacy\"\u003EYour privacy\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA result sent while you are signed in carries your account so we can write back. It is never shown on the page.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EYour first name is shown next to a result only if you tick \u003Cstrong\u003EShow my first name\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENotes are shown to others only if you tick \u003Cstrong\u003EAnyone may read my notes\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFiles are seen only by the CadCraft team, kept privately and deleted automatically after the number of days the form states. Remove anything private from a file first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-counts-as-known-not-to-work\"\u003EWhat counts as &quot;known not to work&quot;\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach test lists what is not built yet. Please do not report those as bugs.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Some checks need a real computer, a real file or a real device. The Community testing page lists them so anyone can help. Send a result Open the Community testing page on the website and choose a test. Use the public build named in Before you start, never a lab build. Follow the Steps and compare with the Expected result. Fill in Send a result: passed, still failing, partly works, or could not run it. Say which system you used and what happened. You do not need an account to send a result. A signed-in account is needed only to send files. Sending files When a test asks for files, drag them into the framed area (or click it, or press Enter or Space on it to choose them). You can send several at once; each shows its name and size and a Remove button. The form says which file types and sizes we take. We never take programs, scripts or web pages. Your privacy A result sent while you are signed in carries your account so we can write back. It is never shown on the page. Your first name is shown next to a result only if you tick Show my first name. Notes are shown to others only if you tick Anyone may read my notes. Files are seen only by the CadCraft team, kept privately and deleted automatically after the number of days the form states. Remove anything private from a file first. What counts as \"known not to work\" Each test lists what is not built yet. Please do not report those as bugs.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"work-together-live","scope":"basics","title":"Work together live","summary":"Share the file open in an app so teammates edit it with you on your own network with a short code, and join someone else's session. Invite links over the internet are not open yet.","section":"tasks","order":90,"keywords":["live","studio live","work together","collaborate","share live","join","code","qr","invite link","link","host","approve","guest","network","relay","license","many people","limit","allow anyway","slow","recent edits","edit log","who changed","history"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"share-the-file-you-have-open","text":"Share the file you have open","level":2},{"id":"let-people-in","text":"Let people in","level":2},{"id":"when-many-people-join","text":"When many people join","level":2},{"id":"codes-on-your-own-network","text":"Codes on your own network","level":2},{"id":"invite-links-over-the-internet-not-open-yet","text":"Invite links over the internet: not open yet","level":2},{"id":"join-someone-else-s-session","text":"Join someone else's session","level":2},{"id":"who-changed-what-recent-edits","text":"Who changed what: Recent edits","level":2},{"id":"assistants-in-a-session","text":"Assistants in a session","level":2},{"id":"when-the-session-ends","text":"When the session ends","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to share a file live from CadCraft Studio, let people in, and join a session someone else shares. What you see in the drawing itself while you work together is on the app&#039;s own page about live sessions.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"share-the-file-you-have-open\"\u003EShare the file you have open\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the file in its app inside CadCraft Studio on a computer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELive\u003C/span\u003E in the app&#039;s bar, type the name others should see, then choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShare live\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStudio shows a code like \u003Ccode\u003EK7-Q4M2X\u003C/code\u003E with a picture to scan and the computer&#039;s address. Give the code to the people you want to invite.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYour file stays on your computer. Only you save it, and everyone sees your saves.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"let-people-in\"\u003ELet people in\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENobody gets in without you. When someone knocks, a card shows their name and device and whether they came on this network or through the internet:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E lets them change the file with you.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView only\u003C/span\u003E lets them watch; anything they try to change is taken back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDeny\u003C/span\u003E turns them away.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe list under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIn the session\u003C/span\u003E shows everyone. Change a person&#039;s role there, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove\u003C/span\u003E them: they keep a copy of what they had, and they can&#039;t come back without a new invitation.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-many-people-join\"\u003EWhen many people join\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA session&#039;s usual size is up to 20 people besides you. It is not a hard limit: how many people can work well together depends on their computers, the file and the network, so Studio asks you before a session grows past it instead of deciding for you.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen someone knocks and would make more than 20, their card says so, and Studio explains what to expect: every change goes to everyone, so with many people each change takes longer to reach each person, and slower laptops and connections fall behind first. Then you choose:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAllow anyway\u003C/span\u003E lets more people in for this session. Each person still waits for your \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView only\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDeny\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeep it at 20\u003C/span\u003E turns away the people waiting over the limit; they are told you are keeping the session small.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo allow more before anyone knocks, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAllow more people…\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIn the session\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack to 20\u003C/span\u003E puts the usual size back. Your choice lasts for this session only; the next one starts at 20 again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf things slow down, fewer people, a quieter file (fewer people changing it at the same moment) or a better connection (a cable instead of Wi-Fi) helps.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"codes-on-your-own-network\"\u003ECodes on your own network\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA code works for 10 minutes. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew code\u003C/span\u003E makes another; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStop inviting\u003C/span\u003E closes it. People already in stay.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOne-time code\u003C/span\u003E lets one person in per code.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAfter 3 wrong codes in a row, Studio closes the code and makes a new one, and tells you.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESessions on your own network are free, for everyone.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"invite-links-over-the-internet-not-open-yet\"\u003EInvite links over the internet: not open yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EInvite links aren&#039;t open yet. Sessions on your own network are free. Today the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOver the internet\u003C/span\u003E part of the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELive\u003C/span\u003E panel says so in a plain sentence instead of offering a link.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen invite links open, this is how they will work:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOver the internet\u003C/span\u003E, choose how long the link works (1 hour, 8 hours, or until you end the session), then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInvite by link\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECopy the link and send it like a meeting link.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe link&#039;s secret part (after \u003Ccode\u003E#\u003C/code\u003E) never reaches getcadcraft.com: the site only passes on encrypted messages it can&#039;t read, and you still approve each person. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew link\u003C/span\u003E makes the old one stop working at once; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStop the link\u003C/span\u003E ends it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMaking an invite link will need you signed in, with a commercial license in force for the app. Guests will need nothing: no account, no license.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"join-someone-else-s-session\"\u003EJoin someone else&#039;s session\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn CadCraft Studio&#039;s home screen, find \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJoin a live session\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the code the host gave you, or type the address shown under their code followed by the code. (Once invite links open, you will paste the host&#039;s link here too.)\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType your name and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJoin\u003C/span\u003E. Studio opens the right app; it fills with the host&#039;s file once the host lets you in.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWithout Studio, scan the picture under the host&#039;s code with a device on the same network: the app opens in the browser.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn CadCraft, if you already have the same file open in a Studio window, you can join from that window instead: choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELive\u003C/span\u003E, then under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJoin with the drawing open here\u003C/span\u003E type the code or the address and code, and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJoin here\u003C/span\u003E. Only the changes made since the file was opened travel. If the host&#039;s drawing is a different one, it replaces the one in that window; your file on disk stays as it is.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EGuest, hotel and school Wi-Fi often keep devices apart, so a code can&#039;t find the host there. Use a network that lets devices see each other, such as the office network. Invite links, for when you are not on the same network, are not open yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"who-changed-what-recent-edits\"\u003EWho changed what: Recent edits\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe Live panel lists \u003Cstrong\u003ERecent edits\u003C/strong\u003E: who made each change and what it was (the step&#039;s name, such as Line or Move), newest first, with how long ago. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow more\u003C/span\u003E lists more of them. With something selected in CadCraft or MarkupCraft, the panel also says who changed it last, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOnly what is selected\u003C/span\u003E keeps just the edits that touched it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe list is made in your own window from the session&#039;s edits while the session runs. It is gone when the session ends: it is never saved in the file or sent anywhere. Everyone in the session can see who made each change. If you joined after people had started, or were away for a while, the edits from before are not in your list, and the panel says so.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"assistants-in-a-session\"\u003EAssistants in a session\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf someone turned on assistants in their app&#039;s settings, their AI assistant can ask to work in the session too. The host&#039;s own assistant joins at once; anyone else&#039;s waits for the host, with a card saying whose assistant it is: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAllow\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDeny\u003C/span\u003E. An assistant edits like its person, with its own undo, and leaves the session when its person leaves.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-the-session-ends\"\u003EWhen the session ends\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe host chooses \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEnd the session\u003C/span\u003E, or closes the file. Everyone else keeps an unsaved copy of the file as it was: save it if you want to keep it. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELeave\u003C/span\u003E does the same for you alone.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to share a file live from CadCraft Studio, let people in, and join a session someone else shares. What you see in the drawing itself while you work together is on the app's own page about live sessions. Share the file you have open Open the file in its app inside CadCraft Studio on a computer. Choose Live in the app's bar, type the name others should see, then choose Share live. Studio shows a code like K7-Q4M2X with a picture to scan and the computer's address. Give the code to the people you want to invite. Your file stays on your computer. Only you save it, and everyone sees your saves. Let people in Nobody gets in without you. When someone knocks, a card shows their name and device and whether they came on this network or through the internet: Edit lets them change the file with you. View only lets them watch; anything they try to change is taken back. Deny turns them away. The list under In the session shows everyone. Change a person's role there, or Remove them: they keep a copy of what they had, and they can't come back without a new invitation. When many people join A session's usual size is up to 20 people besides you. It is not a hard limit: how many people can work well together depends on their computers, the file and the network, so Studio asks you before a session grows past it instead of deciding for you. When someone knocks and would make more than 20, their card says so, and Studio explains what to expect: every change goes to everyone, so with many people each change takes longer to reach each person, and slower laptops and connections fall behind first. Then you choose: Allow anyway lets more people in for this session. Each person still waits for your Edit, View only or Deny. Keep it at 20 turns away the people waiting over the limit; they are told you are keeping the session small. To allow more before anyone knocks, choose Allow more people… under In the session. Back to 20 puts the usual size back. Your choice lasts for this session only; the next one starts at 20 again. If things slow down, fewer people, a quieter file (fewer people changing it at the same moment) or a better connection (a cable instead of Wi-Fi) helps. Codes on your own network A code works for 10 minutes. New code makes another; Stop inviting closes it. People already in stay. One-time code lets one person in per code. After 3 wrong codes in a row, Studio closes the code and makes a new one, and tells you. Sessions on your own network are free, for everyone. Invite links over the internet: not open yet Invite links aren't open yet. Sessions on your own network are free. Today the Over the internet part of the Live panel says so in a plain sentence instead of offering a link. When invite links open, this is how they will work: Under Over the internet, choose how long the link works (1 hour, 8 hours, or until you end the session), then Invite by link. Copy the link and send it like a meeting link. The link's secret part (after #) never reaches getcadcraft.com: the site only passes on encrypted messages it can't read, and you still approve each person. New link makes the old one stop working at once; Stop the link ends it. Making an invite link will need you signed in, with a commercial license in force for the app. Guests will need nothing: no account, no license. Join someone else's session In CadCraft Studio's home screen, find Join a live session. Type the code the host gave you, or type the address shown under their code followed by the code. (Once invite links open, you will paste the host's link here too.) Type your name and choose Join. Studio opens the right app; it fills with the host's file once the host lets you in. Without Studio, scan the picture under the host's code with a device on the same network: the app opens in the browser. In CadCraft, if you already have the same file open in a Studio window, you can join from that window instead: choose Live, then under Join with the drawing open here type the code or the address and code, and choose Join here. Only the changes made since the file was opened travel. If the host's drawing is a different one, it replaces the one in that window; your file on disk stays as it is. Guest, hotel and school Wi-Fi often keep devices apart, so a code can't find the host there. Use a network that lets devices see each other, such as the office network. Invite links, for when you are not on the same network, are not open yet. Who changed what: Recent edits The Live panel lists Recent edits: who made each change and what it was (the step's name, such as Line or Move), newest first, with how long ago. Show more lists more of them. With something selected in CadCraft or MarkupCraft, the panel also says who changed it last, and Only what is selected keeps just the edits that touched it. The list is made in your own window from the session's edits while the session runs. It is gone when the session ends: it is never saved in the file or sent anywhere. Everyone in the session can see who made each change. If you joined after people had started, or were away for a while, the edits from before are not in your list, and the panel says so. Assistants in a session If someone turned on assistants in their app's settings, their AI assistant can ask to work in the session too. The host's own assistant joins at once; anyone else's waits for the host, with a card saying whose assistant it is: Allow or Deny. An assistant edits like its person, with its own undo, and leaves the session when its person leaves. When the session ends The host chooses End the session, or closes the file. Everyone else keeps an unsaved copy of the file as it was: save it if you want to keep it. Leave does the same for you alone.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"pen-and-touch","scope":"basics","title":"Draw with a pen on a tablet","summary":"What the app does when you draw with a pen on a touch screen: it ignores your palm, can leave the drawing to the pen while your fingers move the view, and lets the pen's buttons and a two-finger tap undo.","section":"tasks","order":94,"keywords":["pen","stylus","tablet","touch","palm","palm rejection","finger","fingers","two fingers","tap","undo","redo","side button","barrel button","eraser","pressure","hover","phone"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"a-pen-is-noticed-the-first-time","text":"A pen is noticed the first time","level":2},{"id":"your-palm-is-ignored","text":"Your palm is ignored","level":2},{"id":"let-the-pen-draw-and-your-fingers-move-the-view","text":"Let the pen draw and your fingers move the view","level":2},{"id":"undo-with-a-tap","text":"Undo with a tap","level":2},{"id":"the-pen-s-buttons","text":"The pen's buttons","level":2},{"id":"these-are-the-device-s-settings","text":"These are the device's settings","level":2},{"id":"not-built-yet","text":"Not built yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow the app works with a pen on a tablet or a touch-screen laptop, and the settings that change it. They are in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPen and touch\u003C/span\u003E, which shows on a touch screen, or once you have used a pen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-pen-is-noticed-the-first-time\"\u003EA pen is noticed the first time\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENothing changes until you touch the drawing with a pen. From then on the app remembers, on this device, that you draw with one, and the choices below start to apply. Before that, your fingers draw and select as usual.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"your-palm-is-ignored\"\u003EYour palm is ignored\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIgnore my palm while I use a pen\u003C/span\u003E on (it starts on), a touch on the drawing is ignored while the pen touches the screen, for a moment after you lift it or while it hovers just above the screen, and whenever the contact is as big as a palm. Rest your hand on the screen and draw.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf a quick finger right after the pen does nothing, wait a moment: that moment is the palm rule at work.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"let-the-pen-draw-and-your-fingers-move-the-view\"\u003ELet the pen draw and your fingers move the view\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFingers on the drawing, once you use a pen here\u003C/span\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraw and select\u003C/span\u003E (the start): a finger does what the pen does.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOnly move the view\u003C/span\u003E: one finger on the drawing does nothing, so a stray touch never draws or picks. Two fingers still move and zoom the view as usual.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe toolbars, panels and dialogs work with your fingers either way.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"undo-with-a-tap\"\u003EUndo with a tap\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETap with two fingers to undo, three to redo\u003C/span\u003E on (it starts on), tap the drawing with two fingers together to undo your last step, and with three to redo it. It works with or without a pen. Moving the fingers is a pan or a zoom, never a tap.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-pen-s-buttons\"\u003EThe pen&#039;s buttons\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPen side button\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWhat the app does (a right click)\u003C/span\u003E at the start, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedo\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPen eraser end\u003C/span\u003E (on pens that have one): \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWhat the app does\u003C/span\u003E at the start, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedo\u003C/span\u003E. In ModelCraft, what the app does is to erase while the eraser end touches.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"these-are-the-device-s-settings\"\u003EThese are the device&#039;s settings\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe pen and touch choices are kept for this device and apply in every app on it, so you set them once. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset the layout\u003C/span\u003E and resetting an app&#039;s settings leave them as they are.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPen pressure and tilt don&#039;t change line weights yet, and no app has a handwriting or sketch-to-shape tool. The app reads the pen&#039;s pressure, tilt and hover, so these can come later per app.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you will learn How the app works with a pen on a tablet or a touch-screen laptop, and the settings that change it. They are in Settings › General › Pen and touch, which shows on a touch screen, or once you have used a pen. A pen is noticed the first time Nothing changes until you touch the drawing with a pen. From then on the app remembers, on this device, that you draw with one, and the choices below start to apply. Before that, your fingers draw and select as usual. Your palm is ignored With Ignore my palm while I use a pen on (it starts on), a touch on the drawing is ignored while the pen touches the screen, for a moment after you lift it or while it hovers just above the screen, and whenever the contact is as big as a palm. Rest your hand on the screen and draw. If a quick finger right after the pen does nothing, wait a moment: that moment is the palm rule at work. Let the pen draw and your fingers move the view Under Fingers on the drawing, once you use a pen here: Draw and select (the start): a finger does what the pen does. Only move the view: one finger on the drawing does nothing, so a stray touch never draws or picks. Two fingers still move and zoom the view as usual. The toolbars, panels and dialogs work with your fingers either way. Undo with a tap With Tap with two fingers to undo, three to redo on (it starts on), tap the drawing with two fingers together to undo your last step, and with three to redo it. It works with or without a pen. Moving the fingers is a pan or a zoom, never a tap. The pen's buttons Pen side button: What the app does (a right click) at the start, or Undo or Redo. Pen eraser end (on pens that have one): What the app does at the start, or Undo or Redo. In ModelCraft, what the app does is to erase while the eraser end touches. These are the device's settings The pen and touch choices are kept for this device and apply in every app on it, so you set them once. Reset the layout and resetting an app's settings leave them as they are. Not built yet Pen pressure and tilt don't change line weights yet, and no app has a handwriting or sketch-to-shape tool. The app reads the pen's pressure, tilt and hover, so these can come later per app.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"input-devices","scope":"basics","title":"Use a trackpad, 3D mouse or controller","summary":"Set what a trackpad, your mouse's side buttons, a pen, finger taps, a 3D mouse, a game controller or a MIDI controller does, for every app or for one app.","section":"tasks","order":95,"keywords":["trackpad","touchpad","two fingers","pinch","scroll","mouse","side button","back button","forward button","tilt wheel","pen","stylus","eraser","tap","3D mouse","space mouse","6 axis","game controller","gamepad","joystick","MIDI","knob","fader","pad","input device","customize","assign"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"every-app-or-only-this-app","text":"Every app, or only this app","level":2},{"id":"trackpad","text":"Trackpad","level":2},{"id":"mouse-buttons","text":"Mouse buttons","level":2},{"id":"pen-and-finger-taps","text":"Pen and finger taps","level":2},{"id":"3d-mouse","text":"3D mouse","level":2},{"id":"game-controller","text":"Game controller","level":2},{"id":"midi-controller","text":"MIDI controller","level":2},{"id":"share-your-settings","text":"Share your settings","level":2},{"id":"not-built-yet","text":"Not built yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to choose what each of your input devices does in the app. The settings are in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGlobal\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInput devices\u003C/span\u003E, one card per device. Until you change something, every device works as it did before.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"every-app-or-only-this-app\"\u003EEvery app, or only this app\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAt the top, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThese settings are for\u003C/span\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEvery app\u003C/span\u003E (the start): what you set applies in every app that has no settings of its own.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOnly\u003C/span\u003E this app: the app takes a copy of the shared settings and keeps its own from then on. Change them here without touching the other apps.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEvery app\u003C/span\u003E again to drop this app&#039;s own settings and go back to the shared ones. The settings are kept on this device, in every app window and in the desktop app&#039;s apps.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"trackpad\"\u003ETrackpad\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETwo-finger scroll\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWhat the app does\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPan the view\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EZoom\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrbit (3D views)\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPinch\u003C/span\u003E: zoom, as the app does, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENothing\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpeed\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReverse the direction\u003C/span\u003E apply when you have chosen pan, zoom or orbit.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe app tells a trackpad from a mouse wheel by the way it scrolls, so a mouse wheel keeps doing what it does.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mouse-buttons\"\u003EMouse buttons\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack side button\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EForward side button\u003C/span\u003E: run any command, such as Undo and Redo, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit everything in view\u003C/span\u003E. A side button set to a command no longer takes the browser back a page.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWheel tilted sideways\u003C/span\u003E: what the app does, pan the view, or nothing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe wheel&#039;s zoom direction and which buttons pan or orbit are in the app&#039;s own tab, under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMenus, keys and mouse\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"pen-and-finger-taps\"\u003EPen and finger taps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESide button\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEraser end\u003C/span\u003E: what the app does, or any command.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETwo-finger tap\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThree-finger tap\u003C/span\u003E (they start as Undo and Redo): any command, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENothing\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPalm rejection and what your fingers do once you use a pen stay under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPen and touch\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"3d-mouse\"\u003E3D mouse\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConnect a 3D mouse\u003C/span\u003E and pick it in the browser&#039;s list; the browser remembers it for this site. Then push, lift, tilt and twist the cap to move the view under the pointer:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach movement (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPush left and right\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPush forward and back\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELift and press\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETilt forward and back\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETilt left and right\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETwist\u003C/span\u003E) can pan, zoom, orbit or do nothing, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReverse\u003C/span\u003E turns it round.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpeed\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDead zone\u003C/span\u003E (how far the cap moves before anything happens) and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOnly the strongest movement at a time\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach button runs a command or fits the view.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA 3D mouse needs Chrome or Edge in the browser.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"game-controller\"\u003EGame controller\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPlug in a controller and press any button so the browser shows it to the app. The sticks pan, orbit or zoom; the triggers zoom in and out; every button can run a command. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpeed\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDead zone\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReverse up and down on the sticks\u003C/span\u003E are there too.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"midi-controller\"\u003EMIDI controller\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConnect MIDI\u003C/span\u003E. Then, for each control: pick what it should do under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a control for\u003C/span\u003E (a knob or fader pans, zooms or orbits; a pad or key runs a command), choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELearn it\u003C/span\u003E, and turn the knob or press the pad. Each learned control is listed, with a button to remove it. MIDI needs Chrome or Edge in the browser.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"share-your-settings\"\u003EShare your settings\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E saves the settings shown as a file; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport\u003C/span\u003E reads one back (settings this version doesn&#039;t know are left out, and you&#039;re told how many). \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack to the defaults\u003C/span\u003E undoes every change for the settings shown.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA wheel dial (such as the ones on Windows) and the desktop app&#039;s own drivers for 3D mice are not supported yet. A 3D mouse, controller or knob moves the view only in apps that have handed their views over; their buttons work everywhere.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to choose what each of your input devices does in the app. The settings are in Settings › Global › Input devices, one card per device. Until you change something, every device works as it did before. Every app, or only this app At the top, These settings are for: Every app (the start): what you set applies in every app that has no settings of its own. Only this app: the app takes a copy of the shared settings and keeps its own from then on. Change them here without touching the other apps. Choose Every app again to drop this app's own settings and go back to the shared ones. The settings are kept on this device, in every app window and in the desktop app's apps. Trackpad Two-finger scroll: What the app does, Pan the view, Zoom or Orbit (3D views). Pinch: zoom, as the app does, or Nothing. Speed and Reverse the direction apply when you have chosen pan, zoom or orbit. The app tells a trackpad from a mouse wheel by the way it scrolls, so a mouse wheel keeps doing what it does. Mouse buttons Back side button and Forward side button: run any command, such as Undo and Redo, or Fit everything in view. A side button set to a command no longer takes the browser back a page. Wheel tilted sideways: what the app does, pan the view, or nothing. The wheel's zoom direction and which buttons pan or orbit are in the app's own tab, under Menus, keys and mouse. Pen and finger taps Side button and Eraser end: what the app does, or any command. Two-finger tap and Three-finger tap (they start as Undo and Redo): any command, or Nothing. Palm rejection and what your fingers do once you use a pen stay under Pen and touch. 3D mouse Choose Connect a 3D mouse and pick it in the browser's list; the browser remembers it for this site. Then push, lift, tilt and twist the cap to move the view under the pointer: Each movement (Push left and right, Push forward and back, Lift and press, Tilt forward and back, Tilt left and right, Twist) can pan, zoom, orbit or do nothing, and Reverse turns it round. Speed, Dead zone (how far the cap moves before anything happens) and Only the strongest movement at a time. Each button runs a command or fits the view. A 3D mouse needs Chrome or Edge in the browser. Game controller Plug in a controller and press any button so the browser shows it to the app. The sticks pan, orbit or zoom; the triggers zoom in and out; every button can run a command. Speed, Dead zone and Reverse up and down on the sticks are there too. MIDI controller Choose Connect MIDI. Then, for each control: pick what it should do under Add a control for (a knob or fader pans, zooms or orbits; a pad or key runs a command), choose Learn it, and turn the knob or press the pad. Each learned control is listed, with a button to remove it. MIDI needs Chrome or Edge in the browser. Share your settings Export saves the settings shown as a file; Import reads one back (settings this version doesn't know are left out, and you're told how many). Back to the defaults undoes every change for the settings shown. Not built yet A wheel dial (such as the ones on Windows) and the desktop app's own drivers for 3D mice are not supported yet. A 3D mouse, controller or knob moves the view only in apps that have handed their views over; their buttons work everywhere.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"full-screen-crosshair","scope":"basics","title":"Full-screen crosshair","summary":"Two thin lines through the pointer, edge to edge across the drawing or view, to check by eye that things line up. One switch turns it on in every app.","section":"reference","order":30,"keywords":["full screen crosshair","full-screen cursor","crosshair","cursor","cursorsize","cursorsize 100","edge to edge","line up","alignment","align","guide lines","cross hairs"],"headings":[{"id":"turn-it-on","text":"Turn it on","level":2},{"id":"what-it-does","text":"What it does","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe full-screen crosshair draws two thin lines through the pointer: one across the whole width of the drawing or view under it, one down its whole height. Move the pointer to an edge or a corner and you can see at once whether other things sit on the same line. It is the same as AutoCAD&#039;s \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECURSORSIZE\u003C/code\u003E 100.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"turn-it-on\"\u003ETurn it on\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E (the gear in the app bar, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), go to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E and tick \u003Cstrong\u003ECrosshair from edge to edge in every app\u003C/strong\u003E under \u003Cstrong\u003EFull-screen crosshair\u003C/strong\u003E. It is one switch for all eight apps on this computer: turn it on in one app and the others have it too, also in tabs that are already open.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-does\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe lines show while the pointer is over a drawing or a view, also when no tool is running.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EToolbars, cards and hints that sit over the view stay on top of the lines; a dialog never gets them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith a finger nothing changes: the lines follow a mouse, a trackpad or a pen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn CadCraft the crosshair is drawn by the drawing itself: it turns with the coordinate system (UCS), and with the view split it stays in the pane you are working in. CadCraft also has it as a cursor size of its own: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cstrong\u003ECursor\u003C/strong\u003E › \u003Cstrong\u003EFull screen\u003C/strong\u003E, or type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECURSORSIZE\u003C/code\u003E and 100.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"The full-screen crosshair draws two thin lines through the pointer: one across the whole width of the drawing or view under it, one down its whole height. Move the pointer to an edge or a corner and you can see at once whether other things sit on the same line. It is the same as AutoCAD's CURSORSIZE 100. Turn it on Open Settings (the gear in the app bar, or Ctrl+,), go to General and tick Crosshair from edge to edge in every app under Full-screen crosshair. It is one switch for all eight apps on this computer: turn it on in one app and the others have it too, also in tabs that are already open. What it does The lines show while the pointer is over a drawing or a view, also when no tool is running. Toolbars, cards and hints that sit over the view stay on top of the lines; a dialog never gets them. With a finger nothing changes: the lines follow a mouse, a trackpad or a pen. In CadCraft the crosshair is drawn by the drawing itself: it turns with the coordinate system (UCS), and with the view split it stays in the pane you are working in. CadCraft also has it as a cursor size of its own: Settings › CadCraft › Cursor › Full screen, or type CURSORSIZE and 100.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"unit-lists","scope":"basics","title":"Units, typed values and the unit lists","summary":"Type a length in any unit with its mark, choose which units the unit lists show, and star the unit new work starts in. A file always keeps its own unit.","section":"reference","order":60,"keywords":["units","unit list","metric","imperial","feet","inches","feet and inches","meters","millimeters","US survey foot","survey feet","international foot","yards","miles","nautical mile","chain","rod","link","furlong","fathom","point","pica","acre","hectare","square","roofing square","acre-foot","board foot","gallon","gon","grad","radian","degrees minutes seconds","slope","percent","H:V","rise run","default unit","star","INSUNITS"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"type-a-length-in-any-unit","text":"Type a length in any unit","level":2},{"id":"choose-the-units-the-lists-show","text":"Choose the units the lists show","level":2},{"id":"where-the-lists-and-the-star-are-used","text":"Where the lists and the star are used","level":2},{"id":"a-file-keeps-its-own-unit","text":"A file keeps its own unit","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to type a value in any unit, and how to choose which units the unit lists in the apps show and which one new work starts in. Every app shares one table of units, with the exact sizes their legal definitions give.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"type-a-length-in-any-unit\"\u003EType a length in any unit\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn a box that takes a length, type the number and the unit&#039;s mark after it. The app turns it into the unit the box is in.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EType\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeans\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039; 6 1/2&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;-6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E3 ft 6 in\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Efeet and inches\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E450 mm\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E3.2 m\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1.5 km\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E2 dm\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E400 nm\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Emetric lengths (\u003Ccode\u003Enm\u003C/code\u003E is the nanometer)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E3 yd\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E2.5 mi\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E250 mil\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eyards, miles, thousandths of an inch\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E10 US ft\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1 US mi\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ethe US survey foot and mile\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E2 rd\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E100 li\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E3 ch\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1 fur\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Erods, links, chains, furlongs\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E1 nmi\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E1 NM\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E6 ftm\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Enautical miles, fathoms\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E12 pt\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E3 pc\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Epoints and picas (1/72 and 1/6 of an inch)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Enm\u003C/code\u003E is always the nanometer. For the nautical mile type \u003Ccode\u003Enmi\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003ENM\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003ENm\u003C/code\u003E is refused, because it could be either.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eft\u003C/code\u003E is the international foot (0.3048 m). The US survey foot (1200/3937 m) is a different unit: type \u003Ccode\u003EUS ft\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eusft\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003Esurvey ft\u003C/code\u003E. Rods, links, chains, furlongs and acres are the international-foot ones.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESums work as before where the app takes them, with any of these marks: \u003Ccode\u003E2*3 yd\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;6&quot; + 3 ch\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn CadCraft a mark is read when the drawing has units (its $INSUNITS). A drawing with no units reads one only when its length format is Engineering or Architectural, where one drawing unit is an inch.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn CivilCraft a project in feet is in US survey feet, as it always was, so \u003Ccode\u003E10 US ft\u003C/code\u003E is 10 and \u003Ccode\u003E10 ft\u003C/code\u003E is 10 project feet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"choose-the-units-the-lists-show\"\u003EChoose the units the lists show\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E (the gear on the app bar, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E, and find \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnits\u003C/span\u003E. There is one list for each kind: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArea\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EVolume\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAngle\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESlope\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach unit has a switch. A unit that is off is left out of the unit lists in the apps.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the star beside a unit to make it the default for new work. Click it again to take the star away. With no star, each app keeps its own default (the file&#039;s unit, or the one for your region).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset the unit lists\u003C/span\u003E puts the starting lists back, with no stars.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe choices are the same in every app in this browser, and they are saved as you make them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"where-the-lists-and-the-star-are-used\"\u003EWhere the lists and the star are used\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EApp\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat follows the switches\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat the star sets\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECadCraft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe units ($INSUNITS) of a new drawing, when it is a unit a drawing can carry\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECivilCraft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe project&#039;s units list\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELength: a new project in meters (a metric star) or feet. Area and volume: the unit areas and volumes are reported in\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EModelCraft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe units list beside the value box\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe unit of a new model\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBimCraft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe units list on the plan toolbar\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe display units, until you pick them on the toolbar (your earlier pick is kept)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMarkupCraft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe measuring and position units in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E, and the unit in the calibrate dialog\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe measuring unit, until MarkupCraft&#039;s own settings have been saved\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETakeoffCraft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe unit lists of an item\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELength and area: the unit of a new item\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe angle and slope lists are saved, but no app&#039;s box follows them yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-file-keeps-its-own-unit\"\u003EA file keeps its own unit\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe settings never change a file. A drawing keeps its units, a CivilCraft project its feet or meters, a model its unit. The unit a file uses is always in its list, even when it is turned off in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to type a value in any unit, and how to choose which units the unit lists in the apps show and which one new work starts in. Every app shares one table of units, with the exact sizes their legal definitions give. Type a length in any unit In a box that takes a length, type the number and the unit's mark after it. The app turns it into the unit the box is in. Type Means 12'6\", 12' 6 1/2\", 12'-6\", 3 ft 6 in feet and inches 450 mm, 3.2 m, 1.5 km, 2 dm, 400 nm metric lengths (nm is the nanometer) 3 yd, 2.5 mi, 250 mil yards, miles, thousandths of an inch 10 US ft, 1 US mi the US survey foot and mile 2 rd, 100 li, 3 ch, 1 fur rods, links, chains, furlongs 1 nmi or 1 NM, 6 ftm nautical miles, fathoms 12 pt, 3 pc points and picas (1/72 and 1/6 of an inch) nm is always the nanometer. For the nautical mile type nmi or NM. Nm is refused, because it could be either. ft is the international foot (0.3048 m). The US survey foot (1200/3937 m) is a different unit: type US ft, usft or survey ft. Rods, links, chains, furlongs and acres are the international-foot ones. Sums work as before where the app takes them, with any of these marks: 2*3 yd, 12'6\" + 3 ch. In CadCraft a mark is read when the drawing has units (its $INSUNITS). A drawing with no units reads one only when its length format is Engineering or Architectural, where one drawing unit is an inch. In CivilCraft a project in feet is in US survey feet, as it always was, so 10 US ft is 10 and 10 ft is 10 project feet. Choose the units the lists show Open Settings (the gear on the app bar, or Ctrl+,), choose General, and find Units. There is one list for each kind: Length, Area, Volume, Angle and Slope. Each unit has a switch. A unit that is off is left out of the unit lists in the apps. Click the star beside a unit to make it the default for new work. Click it again to take the star away. With no star, each app keeps its own default (the file's unit, or the one for your region). Reset the unit lists puts the starting lists back, with no stars. The choices are the same in every app in this browser, and they are saved as you make them. Where the lists and the star are used App What follows the switches What the star sets CadCraft The units ($INSUNITS) of a new drawing, when it is a unit a drawing can carry CivilCraft The project's units list Length: a new project in meters (a metric star) or feet. Area and volume: the unit areas and volumes are reported in ModelCraft The units list beside the value box The unit of a new model BimCraft The units list on the plan toolbar The display units, until you pick them on the toolbar (your earlier pick is kept) MarkupCraft The measuring and position units in Settings, and the unit in the calibrate dialog The measuring unit, until MarkupCraft's own settings have been saved TakeoffCraft The unit lists of an item Length and area: the unit of a new item The angle and slope lists are saved, but no app's box follows them yet. A file keeps its own unit The settings never change a file. A drawing keeps its units, a CivilCraft project its feet or meters, a model its unit. The unit a file uses is always in its list, even when it is turned off in Settings.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"lost-toolbar","scope":"basics","title":"Where did my toolbar go?","summary":"The bars, menus or panels went missing. Here is what hides them on purpose, the keys that bring them back, and one button that puts the whole layout back.","section":"trouble","order":5,"keywords":["toolbar gone","toolbar missing","lost toolbar","lost menu","menu disappeared","ribbon disappeared","bars hidden","show bars","full screen","panels gone","reset layout","layout","ribbon","collapsed","locked toolbar"],"headings":[{"id":"the-quickest-fix-reset-the-layout","text":"The quickest fix: reset the layout","level":2},{"id":"the-bars-are-hidden-full-screen","text":"The bars are hidden (full screen)","level":2},{"id":"the-toolbar-looks-different","text":"The toolbar looks different","level":2},{"id":"the-toolbar-s-own-menu","text":"The toolbar's own menu","level":2},{"id":"everything-is-too-big-or-too-small","text":"Everything is too big or too small","level":2},{"id":"the-panels-are-gone","text":"The panels are gone","level":2},{"id":"on-a-phone","text":"On a phone","level":2},{"id":"still-missing","text":"Still missing?","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ENothing is deleted when a bar or a panel goes away: the app only hides or moves it. Each of these ways is on purpose, and each has a way back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-quickest-fix-reset-the-layout\"\u003EThe quickest fix: reset the layout\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E with the gear button in the app bar, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayout\u003C/span\u003E, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset the layout\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe toolbar goes back to its first order and buttons, the panels go back to their sides, the screen mode goes back to the normal view and the menu style goes back to the one the app starts with. Your theme, language, sizes and the app&#039;s own settings stay as they are, and so do any menu styles you made yourself (choose them again under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMenus, keys and mouse\u003C/span\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn CadCraft Studio, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E in Studio&#039;s own window also has \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset the layout in every app\u003C/span\u003E. Each app&#039;s windows follow at once, and an app you open later starts from its first layout.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-bars-are-hidden-full-screen\"\u003EThe bars are hidden (full screen)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe screen-mode button in the app bar, and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, go from the normal view to full screen, then to full screen with the bars hidden, then back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile the bars are hidden, a small \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow bars\u003C/span\u003E tab stays at the top middle of the window. Choose it, or point at the top edge of the window, and the bars slide down; they hide again a moment after you move away. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to leave this mode, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to leave full screen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf the app opens with its bars hidden every time, look at \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScreen\u003C/span\u003E: the app does what is chosen there when it opens.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:touch\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ETouch\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETap the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow bars\u003C/span\u003E tab, or tap the top edge of the screen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-toolbar-looks-different\"\u003EThe toolbar looks different\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETools in another order, or tools you can&#039;t find:\u003C/strong\u003E someone arranged the toolbar. Choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOriginal order\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EToolbar\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOnly icons, or only words:\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EToolbar buttons\u003C/span\u003E in the same place picks icons and labels, icons only or labels only. Hold the pointer over an icon to see its name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETools won&#039;t move:\u003C/strong\u003E the toolbar is locked. Right-click the toolbar (or press and hold it) and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnlock the toolbar\u003C/span\u003E, or clear \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELock the toolbar\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EToolbar\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA ribbon that shows only its tab names:\u003C/strong\u003E it is collapsed. Choose a tab name to open its tools for one command, or double-click a tab name (or choose the pin at the end of the tab row) to keep the ribbon open.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMenus or a ribbon instead of the toolbar:\u003C/strong\u003E a menu style is on. Pick the app&#039;s own style under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › the app&#039;s tab › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMenus, keys and mouse\u003C/span\u003E. Every command is in every style; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command K\"\u003E⌘K\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+K\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E finds any of them by name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-toolbar-s-own-menu\"\u003EThe toolbar&#039;s own menu\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERight-click the toolbar, or press and hold it on a touch screen, for a short menu:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArrange the toolbar\u003C/span\u003E: drag tools within their group and groups by their name, or move the focused one with the arrow keys. Choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDone arranging\u003C/span\u003E in the same menu, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, when it looks right.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELock the toolbar\u003C/span\u003E / \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnlock the toolbar\u003C/span\u003E: a locked toolbar can&#039;t be arranged or reset by accident.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset the toolbar\u003C/span\u003E: tools back in their first order.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith a ribbon menu style: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECollapse the ribbon\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPin the ribbon open\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a keyboard, the menu key (or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift F10\"\u003E⇧ F10\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+F10\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) opens it for the tool that has the focus.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"everything-is-too-big-or-too-small\"\u003EEverything is too big or too small\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInterface size\u003C/span\u003E makes the bars, toolbars, panels and dialogs of this app bigger or smaller, from 80 % to 140 %. The drawing keeps its own zoom, so a smaller interface leaves more room for it, for example with two apps side by side on one monitor. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPanel text size\u003C/span\u003E beside it changes only the text in the side panels, on top of the interface size. Each app keeps its own size; CadCraft Studio&#039;s own window has one in its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E too.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-panels-are-gone\"\u003EThe panels are gone\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPanels can float over the drawing, dock on another side, or move to a window of their own (often on a second monitor, or behind this window).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELook for a second window called after the app with &quot;panels&quot;, and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPut the panels back\u003C/span\u003E in it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOr choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset layout\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPanels\u003C/span\u003E, which brings every panel home.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"on-a-phone\"\u003EOn a phone\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a phone the panels are sheets: open one from the bar at the bottom of the screen. The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETools\u003C/span\u003E button in that bar hides and shows the toolbar, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove\u003C/span\u003E puts it at the top or the bottom of the screen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"still-missing\"\u003EStill missing?\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFind any tool by name with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command K\"\u003E⌘K\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+K\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, even while its bar is hidden. If something is still not where it should be after resetting the layout, tell us on the support page with the app&#039;s name and what you were doing.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Nothing is deleted when a bar or a panel goes away: the app only hides or moves it. Each of these ways is on purpose, and each has a way back. The quickest fix: reset the layout Open Settings with the gear button in the app bar, or press Ctrl+,. Choose the General tab. Under Layout, choose Reset the layout. The toolbar goes back to its first order and buttons, the panels go back to their sides, the screen mode goes back to the normal view and the menu style goes back to the one the app starts with. Your theme, language, sizes and the app's own settings stay as they are, and so do any menu styles you made yourself (choose them again under Menus, keys and mouse). In CadCraft Studio, Settings in Studio's own window also has Reset the layout in every app. Each app's windows follow at once, and an app you open later starts from its first layout. The bars are hidden (full screen) The screen-mode button in the app bar, and Ctrl+Shift+F, go from the normal view to full screen, then to full screen with the bars hidden, then back. While the bars are hidden, a small Show bars tab stays at the top middle of the window. Choose it, or point at the top edge of the window, and the bars slide down; they hide again a moment after you move away. Press Ctrl+Shift+F to leave this mode, or Esc to leave full screen. If the app opens with its bars hidden every time, look at Settings › General › Screen: the app does what is chosen there when it opens. Tap the Show bars tab, or tap the top edge of the screen. The toolbar looks different Tools in another order, or tools you can't find: someone arranged the toolbar. Choose Original order under Settings › General › Toolbar. Only icons, or only words: Toolbar buttons in the same place picks icons and labels, icons only or labels only. Hold the pointer over an icon to see its name. Tools won't move: the toolbar is locked. Right-click the toolbar (or press and hold it) and choose Unlock the toolbar, or clear Lock the toolbar under Settings › General › Toolbar. A ribbon that shows only its tab names: it is collapsed. Choose a tab name to open its tools for one command, or double-click a tab name (or choose the pin at the end of the tab row) to keep the ribbon open. Menus or a ribbon instead of the toolbar: a menu style is on. Pick the app's own style under Settings › the app's tab › Menus, keys and mouse. Every command is in every style; Ctrl+K finds any of them by name. The toolbar's own menu Right-click the toolbar, or press and hold it on a touch screen, for a short menu: Arrange the toolbar: drag tools within their group and groups by their name, or move the focused one with the arrow keys. Choose Done arranging in the same menu, or press Esc, when it looks right. Lock the toolbar / Unlock the toolbar: a locked toolbar can't be arranged or reset by accident. Reset the toolbar: tools back in their first order. With a ribbon menu style: Collapse the ribbon or Pin the ribbon open. On a keyboard, the menu key (or Shift+F10) opens it for the tool that has the focus. Everything is too big or too small Settings › General › Interface size makes the bars, toolbars, panels and dialogs of this app bigger or smaller, from 80 % to 140 %. The drawing keeps its own zoom, so a smaller interface leaves more room for it, for example with two apps side by side on one monitor. Panel text size beside it changes only the text in the side panels, on top of the interface size. Each app keeps its own size; CadCraft Studio's own window has one in its Settings too. The panels are gone Panels can float over the drawing, dock on another side, or move to a window of their own (often on a second monitor, or behind this window). Look for a second window called after the app with \"panels\", and choose Put the panels back in it. Or choose Reset layout under Settings › General › Panels, which brings every panel home. On a phone On a phone the panels are sheets: open one from the bar at the bottom of the screen. The Tools button in that bar hides and shows the toolbar, and Move puts it at the top or the bottom of the screen. Still missing? Find any tool by name with Ctrl+K, even while its bar is hidden. If something is still not where it should be after resetting the layout, tell us on the support page with the app's name and what you were doing.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"recover","scope":"basics","title":"Recover unsaved work","summary":"How the apps keep a recovery copy while you work, and how to get your work back after a crash or a closed tab.","section":"trouble","order":55,"keywords":["autosave","recover","recovery","crash","lost work","closed tab","unsaved","restore","backup"],"headings":[{"id":"when-the-copy-is-kept","text":"When the copy is kept","level":2},{"id":"get-your-work-back","text":"Get your work back","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EWhile you work, each app keeps a recovery copy of what you have not saved yet. It is not a save: your file on your computer only changes when you save it. The copy is there so a crash, a closed tab or a computer that turns off does not cost you your work.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-the-copy-is-kept\"\u003EWhen the copy is kept\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA few seconds after you stop editing, and at least every 30 seconds while you keep going.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhen you switch to another tab or window.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe copy stays in this browser, on this computer. Nothing is uploaded.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"get-your-work-back\"\u003EGet your work back\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the same app again, in the same browser.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf it closed before your work was saved, it asks \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERecover your work?\u003C/span\u003E and lists what it kept, with the time of the last copy.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERecover\u003C/span\u003E to open it, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDiscard\u003C/span\u003E to throw it away.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESave it to a file as soon as it opens.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENot now\u003C/span\u003E keeps the copies for 30 days, and the app asks again the next time it opens. A reload counts too: reload a tab with unsaved work and the app offers it back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhen you save, or close a file on purpose, its recovery copy is removed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAnother tab of the same app that is still open never offers your copies. It only offers work from a tab that has gone.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPrivate windows in some browsers, and a browser with no space left, cannot keep a copy. The app tells you when that happens, so save to a file more often.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClearing your browser&#039;s site data removes the copies.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENot every app keeps a recovery copy yet. Each app&#039;s own help says whether it does.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"While you work, each app keeps a recovery copy of what you have not saved yet. It is not a save: your file on your computer only changes when you save it. The copy is there so a crash, a closed tab or a computer that turns off does not cost you your work. When the copy is kept A few seconds after you stop editing, and at least every 30 seconds while you keep going. When you switch to another tab or window. The copy stays in this browser, on this computer. Nothing is uploaded. Get your work back Open the same app again, in the same browser. If it closed before your work was saved, it asks Recover your work? and lists what it kept, with the time of the last copy. Choose Recover to open it, or Discard to throw it away. Save it to a file as soon as it opens. Not now keeps the copies for 30 days, and the app asks again the next time it opens. A reload counts too: reload a tab with unsaved work and the app offers it back. Things to know When you save, or close a file on purpose, its recovery copy is removed. Another tab of the same app that is still open never offers your copies. It only offers work from a tab that has gone. Private windows in some browsers, and a browser with no space left, cannot keep a copy. The app tells you when that happens, so save to a file more often. Clearing your browser's site data removes the copies. Not every app keeps a recovery copy yet. Each app's own help says whether it does.","lang":"en"}],"locale":"nb","chain":["nb","en"]};
